var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/review/#Shot-review","page":"Shot review","text":"Every check is judged on the worst case over pointing, target positioning, hardware tolerance and focus errors, never on the nominal beams. clearance gives the worst-case beam-to-hardware clearance, power and intensity, with intensity-contour envelopes, clearance-only spot wings (nif_wings) and pass, review and fail tiers; its thresholds and wing models are engineering defaults, which the review requirements and measured far fields of a facility should replace. talis_check applies NIF's published target-configuration rules (3ω capture and incidence, 3ω and 1ω clearance, unconverted-light management, the TAS stay-in zone), and unconverted_light_check covers the 1ω and 2ω footprints and the 32 mm zone, and unconverted_deposition puts their power on the target (a uniform square beam per beamlet and color, with shadowing; the unconverted fraction is an input). unconverted_history follows that light through the pulses, with a fraction and a prepulse that may vary in time, and irradiance_history the 3ω light; specular_check and preplasma_check compare them with the NIF User Guide's 10⁸ W/cm² plasma-onset guideline for metals. ApertureKeepOut puts the worst-case clearance of each beam from its entrance hole into an optimization objective, SphereKeepOut its clearance from the capsule and HardwareKeepOut its clearance from named hardware; each clearance is also a separate quantity (keepout_clearance) for a hand-written objective or constraint, as are the fractions of the laser power on named parts (clipped_fraction beside power_fraction). The TaLIS rules that carry a margin are such quantities too, each checked against talis_check: the 3ω clearance is a HardwareKeepOut with the rule tolerance, CaptureKeepIn gives the 3ω capture margin (capture_margin), IncidenceRule the 3ω incidence (incidence_angle) and Omega1KeepOut the 1ω clearance (omega1_clearance). backlighter_foil and line_of_sight place a foil on a diagnostic line of sight and check it against shields and beams, and backlighter_signal follows the deposition through a He-α conversion efficiency fitted to published foil data (kshell_ce) to the photons at the detector.","title":"Shot review"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.DEFAULT_CLEARANCE_THRESHOLDS","page":"Shot review","text":"DEFAULT_CLEARANCE_THRESHOLDS\n\nThe status tiers of clearance. They are engineering defaults, and no facility criterion sets them:\n\n:fail above 1e12 W/cm², for plasma formation, or above 1e-3 of the beam power on a component;\n:review above 1e9 W/cm², for the onset of ablation and debris, or above 1e-5.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DEFAULT_CLEARANCE_THRESHOLDS"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.BeamEnvelope","page":"Shot review","text":"BeamEnvelope(beam; intensity, peak_fraction, enclosed, margin = 0.0)\n\nA geometric beam envelope. At axial distance s from best focus, the envelope is the best-focus contour ellipse (see envelope_radius) grown by the f-cone radius |s|/(2F) and a uniform margin (mm). In geometric optics with a phase plate, every lens point fills the whole spot. The intensity at a point is then the mean of the focal intensity over a disk of radius |s|/(2F), so outside the envelope it is below the contour level at every s. Clearance is the signed distance to the envelope; negative means inside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.BeamEnvelope"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.ClearanceReport","page":"Shot review","text":"ClearanceReport\n\nThe result of clearance. It holds one ClearanceRow per beam and hardware component and the envelope, error-set and threshold settings; see violations and min_clearance. canceled is true when a clearance callback stopped the check early. rows then hold only the beams completed before the cancel, which are the leading beams in input order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ClearanceReport"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.ClearanceRow","page":"Shot review","text":"ClearanceRow\n\nOne pair of a beam and a hardware component. \"Worst\" values are taken over the error set of pointing, target offset, hardware tolerance and focus:\n\nclearance — worst-case signed distance to the beam envelope (mm), negative for overlap\nnominal — the same at nominal pointing and focus\npoint, s — hardware point of minimum clearance and its axial position from best focus (mm)\npower, intercepted — worst-case power (TW) on the component (core + wings) and its fraction of the beam power\npeak_intensity — worst-case beam intensity (W/cm², normal to the beam, core + wings) at the upstream surface points of the component, ignoring shadowing\nstatus — :fail, :review or :pass against the thresholds of the report\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ClearanceRow"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.PowerLawWing","page":"Shot review","text":"PowerLawWing(fraction; rh = :r95, rmax = 1.5)\n\nHalo with I ∝ [1 + (r/rh)²]^(-3/2) for r ≤ rmax, carrying fraction of the beam power. rh = :r95 uses the radius enclosing 95% of the core, the geometric mean of the axes. rmax is the spatial-filter cutoff, ≈ ±200 µrad × 7.7 m ≈ 1.5 mm on NIF.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PowerLawWing"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.SpotWing","page":"Shot review","text":"SpotWing\n\nSupertype of low-level circular far-field components outside the phase-plate core of a beam. Only clearance checks use them, since they carry ≲1% of the energy and do not matter for drive symmetry. Each wing is centered on the beam axis at best focus, and radii are in mm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SpotWing"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.UniformScatter","page":"Shot review","text":"UniformScatter(fraction; radius = 23.0)\n\nA far-angle scatter floor, with fraction of the beam power spread uniformly over a disk of radius mm at the target plane. The default covers NIF final-optics scatter up to ≈3 mrad × 7.7 m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.UniformScatter"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.beam_component_power-Tuple{DepositionOperator, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"beam_component_power(op, beams) -> Matrix\n\nReturns the absorbed power (TW) per beam (rows) and component (columns), on both sides of each component, from the deposition operator. Beams may differ from the operator's in pointing, focus, spot and power; each is matched to the operator by its port.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.beam_component_power"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.clearance-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"clearance(target, beams; targets, hardware = all other surfaces,\n          intensity = nothing, peak_fraction = 1e-4, enclosed = nothing,\n          wings = nif_wings(), pointing = 0.05, target_offset = 0.025,\n          hardware_tolerance = 0.0, focus = 1.0, margin = 0.0, ssd = 0.0,\n          thresholds = DEFAULT_CLEARANCE_THRESHOLDS, nangles = 16, level = 2,\n          resolution = 1e-4, rtol = 0.01,\n          op = nothing, nspot = 61, callback = nothing,\n          batch = Threads.nthreads()) -> ClearanceReport\n\nChecks beam-to-hardware clearance, judged on the worst case over an error set. targets and hardware name parts or components (see elements). A part split for computation stands for all its pieces and gets one row per beam, the worst case over its pieces.\n\nSpot. The spot is the SuperGaussianSpot core of each beam, with its optional super-Gaussian halo, plus clearance-only circular wings. The default wings, nif_wings(), are a 1% power-law halo and a 10⁻³ scatter floor. They are engineering defaults, to be overridden with measured far fields. ssd (mm) is the half-width of the SSD smear along the dispersion axis, added isotropically to the envelope as a conservative choice.\n\nEnvelope. The envelope is the best-focus contour grown by the f-cone, ssd and margin. The contour is set by one of an absolute intensity (W/cm²), a peak_fraction of the core peak, or an enclosed core fraction. With wings, the contour of each spot component is taken at the threshold divided by the number of components, and the envelope is their union, so the total intensity outside it is below the threshold.\n\nError set. Transverse errors add linearly, not in quadrature, as R = pointing + target_offset + hardware_tolerance (mm). The three terms are:\n\nthe per-beam pointing, with a NIF spec of 50 µm rms; pass ≈ 0.12 for a 3σ statistical radius;\nthe rigid target offset, with a NIF mean beams-to-target offset of ≈ 25 µm;\nthe as-built hardware tolerance, from metrology, or 0.1–0.25 mm without it.\n\nfocus is the ± focal-position error in mm, ≤ 1 mm in the 1994 NIF spec. The worst-case clearance is exact, nominal − R − focus/(2F). Worst-case power and intensity are maxima over aims displaced to nangles points on the radius-R circle, plus the nominal aim, at focus offsets 0, ±focus.\n\nStatus. thresholds are in W/cm² and in fractions of the beam power. A pair is :fail above fail_intensity (1e12, plasma formation) or fail_fraction (1e-3). It is :review above review_intensity (1e9, onset of ablation and debris) or review_fraction (1e-5). These are engineering defaults, and no facility review criterion sets them; talis_check applies the NIF rules.\n\nReach. Only hardware upstream of the landing point of each beam is checked. The landing point is where the beam axis first meets one of the targets components, the surfaces that the beam should hit, or the aim plane if the axis misses them. Hardware is split into 4^level sub-triangles per triangle. A point counts only if beam light can reach it past the opaque surfaces of the target. Apertures are transparent, and the triangles of the component itself never block it. Beam light reaches a point in one of three cases:\n\nthe straight segment to the point from the beam axis at the same axial position is unobstructed;\nthe point is outside the envelope, and the segment to it from the nearest envelope point along the same transverse ray is unobstructed, provided that envelope point is lit. An envelope point is lit when some f-cone direction reaches it from upstream, so envelope behind a surface that the beam edge already struck is dark.\nthe point is inside the envelope and is itself lit.\n\nHardware outside a hohlraum wall is therefore not judged against a beam inside it, while hardware beside the beam path before an entrance hole still is. A sub-triangle counts whole when any of its vertices or its centroid is reached. This choice is conservative, since a sub-triangle is dropped only when every path to each of those points is blocked.\n\nMinimum. The clearance is minimized over the whole of each counted sub-triangle, and not only at its vertices. The envelope distance is Lipschitz in the point, with constant √(1 + cone²), so a branch-and-bound subdivision bounds the minimum from below. The bound is within resolution (mm) or rtol times its distance from zero worst-case clearance, whichever is larger. A plate crossed by a beam between its vertices is therefore found. The reported clearance is that lower bound. The peak intensity is the largest over the reached vertices and the point of least clearance. The power on hardware comes from the reference ray trace, with an nspot grid per core component and equal-power polar grids for the wings. When op, a DepositionOperator for these ports, is given, the core power comes from it.\n\nProgress and cancel. With a callback, beams are checked in batches of batch in input order. callback((; done, total)) is called with the number of beams completed, once with done = 0 before any work and then after each batch. Returning true cancels the check. clearance then returns at once with the rows of the completed beams and canceled = true in the report. A true returned after the last batch is ignored. The callback always runs on the calling task, never on a worker thread, so it may touch task-local state; it must not block for long, since no work runs while it does. Rows do not depend on callback or batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.clearance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.enclosed_radius-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"Shot review","text":"enclosed_radius(order, fraction)\n\nReturns the radius, in units of the 1/e semi-axes, of the super-Gaussian contour that encloses fraction of the power. The fraction inside ρ is P(2/n, ρⁿ), the regularized lower incomplete gamma function. For spots with a halo use envelope_radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.enclosed_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.envelope_distance-Tuple{BeamEnvelope, StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64}}","page":"Shot review","text":"envelope_distance(env, x) -> (distance, s)\n\nReturns the signed distance (mm) from point x to the beam envelope in the plane through x normal to the beam, and the axial position s of that plane relative to best focus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.envelope_distance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.envelope_radius-Tuple{SuperGaussianSpot, Any}","page":"Shot review","text":"envelope_radius(spot, power; intensity = nothing, peak_fraction = nothing,\n                enclosed = nothing) -> ρ\n\nReturns the best-focus contour radius in units of the core 1/e semi-axes, with the halo included. Exactly one of three keywords sets the contour: an absolute intensity (W/cm²), a fraction peak_fraction of the peak focal intensity, or the enclosed power fraction. Returns 0 if the whole spot is below an intensity threshold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.envelope_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.min_clearance-Tuple{ClearanceReport}","page":"Shot review","text":"min_clearance(report) -> Dict{Symbol,ClearanceRow}\n\nReturns the closest beam, by worst-case clearance, for each hardware component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.min_clearance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.nif_wings-Tuple{}","page":"Shot review","text":"nif_wings(; halo = 0.01, rmax = 1.5, scatter = 1e-3, scatter_radius = 23.0)\n\nReturns the default NIF clearance wing model. Its values are engineering defaults (see reference/research-notes.md), and no facility criterion sets them; override them with measured far-field data. The model has two wings:\n\na 1% power-law halo, truncated at the spatial-filter angle. OMEGA measured < 1% outside the super-Gaussian, and the NIF/LMJ design specs allowed 2% outside the nominal spot.\na 10⁻³ uniform scatter floor out to 3 mrad.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_wings"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.violations-Tuple{ClearanceReport}","page":"Shot review","text":"violations(report; status = (:fail, :review), clearance = -Inf) -> Vector{ClearanceRow}\n\nReturns the rows whose status is in status, or whose worst-case clearance is below clearance mm, with failures first and then sorted by clearance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.violations"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.write_clearance_csv-Tuple{AbstractString, ClearanceReport}","page":"Shot review","text":"write_clearance_csv(path, report)\n\nWrites every row of a clearance report as CSV.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_clearance_csv"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.LineOfSightReport","page":"Shot review","text":"LineOfSightReport\n\nThe result of line_of_sight, with fields:\n\nclear, true when no ray is blocked and the foil covers the object disk;\nrays and blocked, the rays in the bundle and the rays obstructed;\nblocked_by, pairs of hardware part => rays it obstructs, in target order, where a ray may count for several parts and rays that would leave the foil through its back count under the foil name;\ncovered, the fraction of the disk sample points whose image ray meets the foil front;\nsamples, the foil and disk sample counts;\nlos, the unit vector toward the detector;\ncenter and radius, the object disk;\ndetector, nothing or the detector distance used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.LineOfSightReport"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.backlighter_foil-Tuple{Any}","page":"Shot review","text":"backlighter_foil(los; standoff, width = 2.0, height = width, tilt = 0.0,\n                 tilt_toward = 0.0, rotation = 0.0, center = (0, 0, 0), n = 8,\n                 name = :backlighter, material = foil_material(),\n                 two_sided = true) -> Component\n\nBuilds a rectangular backlighter foil for the diagnostic line of sight los = (θ, φ) in degrees, the direction from the object toward the detector. The object is at center, TCC by default. The foil is placed on the far side, centered standoff mm from center at center − standoff · port_vector(θ, φ). Its front face is heated by the backlighter beams, and its x rays cross the object to the detector, which the front face is turned toward. The placement is foil_frame with the same keywords. The mesh lies in the x–y plane of that frame, with width along its x axis.\n\nBy default the foil normal lies along the line of sight. tilt (degrees) rotates the whole foil about its center, off the line of sight toward cos(ψ) θ̂ + sin(ψ) φ̂. The angle ψ = tilt_toward is in degrees from θ̂ of the line of sight toward φ̂, like the angle of a spot. Before the tilt, the width edge of the foil lies along θ̂ and height along φ̂, and rotation (degrees) turns both about the normal from θ̂ toward φ̂. A foil tilted by β about its height axis (tilt_toward = 0) has a projected width width · cos β across the line of sight.\n\nThe defaults (a 2 mm square gold foil) are generic engineering values that are not taken from a facility backlighter design, and real backlighter dimensions and standoffs are shot-specific. n sets the mesh (n × n patches for a square foil). The default material is foil_material(), directly irradiated gold (albedo 0.8, conversion 0.8); foil_material(:Ti) and the other mid-Z elements give a suggested point inside a suggested range, listed in the table of foil_material, and none of these is a measurement. The x-ray emission of the foil set by this conversion is broadband radiosity; the He-α line signal comes from kshell_ce, fitted to separate data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.backlighter_foil"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.line_of_sight-Tuple{Target, Any}","page":"Shot review","text":"line_of_sight(target, los; foil = :backlighter, object = :capsule,\n              center = (0, 0, 0), radius = the object's projected radius,\n              hardware = nothing, level = 0, nrings = 4,\n              detector = nothing) -> LineOfSightReport\n\nChecks whether the diagnostic line of sight los is unobstructed from the backlighter foil through the object to the detector. los is θ, φ in degrees from center toward the detector, or a direction vector. The object is represented by its disk normal to the line of sight through center, of radius radius. The default radius is the projected radius of the object, the largest distance of its vertices from the line of sight; give radius when there is no object (object = nothing). The foil and object parts never obstruct.\n\nhardware names the parts or components that may obstruct, and nothing takes every opaque part of the target except the foil and object. Apertures such as entrance holes and open windows are transparent, as for rays everywhere; a closed window is opaque.\n\nThe bundle. With detector = nothing (the default), the bundle consists of every ray from a foil sample point through a disk sample point, continued without end past the disk. These rays are the x-ray paths from the whole emitting area of the foil through the object toward any detector in their cone, a conservative superset of what one imager accepts. The samples are taken as follows:\n\nFoil samples are the vertices of the foil mesh plus 4^level sub-triangle points per triangle. The vertices include the corners and edges, where the extreme rays of the bundle start.\nDisk samples are the center and nrings rings of 8k points, starting along θ̂ of the line of sight.\n\nA ray counts as obstructed if it meets an opaque triangle of the hardware anywhere past the foil. A ray that would leave the foil through its back face also counts as obstructed, under the foil name.\n\ndetector = L takes the imaging rays instead, with L in mm from center along the line of sight, or Inf for a distant imager. Each imaging ray is traced from the detector point through a disk point back to the foil, or along the line of sight for Inf. The segment from the foil to the detector is then checked, unbounded for Inf.\n\nCoverage. covered is the fraction of disk points whose image ray meets the foil front. The image ray is parallel to the line of sight, or directed toward the detector point when detector is finite. Below 1 the foil does not backlight the whole object. clear requires that no ray is obstructed and that covered == 1.\n\nThe result is exact for obstructions whose edges meet the bundle at its sampled extremes, such as straight edges parallel to a foil edge and rims crossing the θ̂ or φ̂ extreme of the bundle. Elsewhere it is a sampled answer, and raising level and nrings resolves a near-grazing obstruction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.line_of_sight"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.BacklighterSignal","page":"Shot review","text":"BacklighterSignal\n\nThe result of backlighter_signal.\n\nSource fields:\n\nline (keV) and laser_energy (J incident on all the foil elements).\nirradiance (W/cm², the power-weighted mean over the foil).\nce, sigma (1σ of ln CE) and extrapolated.\nxray_energy (J of He-α into 4π).\nfacing (fraction of the x-ray energy from foil elements that face the detector).\nper_sr (J/sr toward a distant detector along the line of sight, from unobstructed elements only).\n\nDetector fields:\n\nmode (:projection or :pinhole).\nsolid_angle (sr), seen from the foil center, of 1 mm² of detector or of the pinhole.\ntransmission (through the filters).\nfluence (J/mm² of He-α at the detector, after the filters). For a pinhole it is the mean over the image of the object.\nphotons (per mm² of detector), with photons_lo and photons_hi (the 1σ range of the CE).\nphotons_range (the minimum and maximum per mm² over the image of the object, pinhole mode only).\npixel (mm, or nothing) and photons_pixel.\n\nAttenuation by the object is not included. These values are open-field (bright) levels, to be multiplied by the transmission of the object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.BacklighterSignal"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.KShellCEModel","page":"Shot review","text":"KShellCEModel\n\nThe fitted He-α conversion-efficiency model returned by kshell_ce_model, with fields:\n\ncoef, the eight parameters (a_foil, a_prepulsed, b, c_foil, c_prepulsed, d, s, Lₛ) of the form described in kshell_ce_model, where the saturation energy is hνₛ = 10 keV · exp(Lₛ);\ncov, their covariance;\ntau, the scatter of ln CE beyond the stated measurement errors;\nscatter, the predictive scatter of a measured ln CE, √(tau² + s̄²) with s̄ the rms stated error of the fitted points;\nn, the number of data points fitted;\nhnu (keV), irradiance (W/cm²) and pulse (ns), the ranges of the data per class, each a Dict from :foil and :prepulsed to (min, max).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.KShellCEModel"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.backlighter_signal-Tuple{Target, Deposition, Any}","page":"Shot review","text":"backlighter_signal(target, deposition, los; line, pulse, distance, foil = :backlighter,\n                   prepulse = false, pinhole = nothing, pinhole_distance = nothing,\n                   pixel = nothing, filters = (), angular = :isotropic,\n                   ce = nothing, irradiance = :mean, object = :capsule,\n                   center = (0, 0, 0), radius = nothing, nrings = 4,\n                   model = kshell_ce_model()) -> BacklighterSignal\n\nComputes the photometric chain from the laser power on a backlighter foil to He-α photons per unit area at a detector. The detector is distance mm from center on the line of sight los, given as (θ, φ) in degrees or as a direction vector from center toward the detector. The chain has six steps:\n\nLaser energy. The energy of each foil element is its power in deposition (TW) over a flat-top pulse of pulse ns. Briareus deposits all of the incident power.\nConversion. The CE comes from kshell_ce for line (an element Symbol or keV). With irradiance = :mean, it is evaluated at the power-weighted mean irradiance of the foil. With :element, each element gets the CE at its irradiance, but σ is still that of the mean. ce = value overrides the model, with σ = 0. The He-α energy into 4π is E = CE · E_laser.\nAngular distribution. The distribution is an assumption:\n:isotropic (the default): the CE data were converted to 4π this way, each from one view angle.\n:lambertian: an optically thick emitting face, 4 cos θ, toward the front only.\nA function f(cos θ) normalized to ∫ f dΩ = 4π.\nEach element radiates E_e f(cos θ)/4π per steradian. An element contributes nothing if its front faces away from the detector or if its path to the detector crosses hardware. Every surface part except the foil and object counts as hardware.\nGeometry.\nWith pinhole = nothing, a point projection, the fluence at the detector point is Σ_e E_e f(cos θ_e) / (4π r_e²), where r_e is the element–detector distance.\nWith a pinhole of that diameter (mm) at pinhole_distance p mm from center, the setup is an imager of the object. The detector is at distance, so q = distance − p behind the pinhole. The fluence at each image point is R · A_pinhole / q², where R = E_e f / (4π A_e cos θ) is the radiance of the foil element seen through the pinhole and the object disk. The disk has radius radius, by default the projected radius of object, and is sampled as in line_of_sight. fluence is the mean over the image points of the disk.\nFilters. The fluence is multiplied by the filter_transmission of filters, given as material => mm pairs, at the line energy and normal incidence.\nPhotons. The photon count is fluence / hν per mm², and per pixel (mm, in the detector plane) if given.\n\nAttenuation by the object is not included, so the result is the open-field level. Only the He-α line is counted, without the continuum, Ly-α or a detector response. The lognormal 1σ of the CE propagates to photons_lo and photons_hi; filter and geometry uncertainties are not included.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.backlighter_signal"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.filter_transmission-Tuple{Any, Real}","page":"Shot review","text":"filter_transmission(filters, hν; table = :auto) -> Real\n\nReturns the transmission exp(−Σ (μ/ρ) ρ t) at normal incidence through filters at photon energy hν (keV). filters is a collection of material => thickness pairs, with thickness in mm. The materials and μ/ρ are those of mass_attenuation, with table passed on. The densities in g/cm³ are:\n\nfor elements, Be 1.848, Al 2.699, Ti 4.54, Fe 7.874, Cu 8.96, Mo 10.22, Ta 16.65, Mg 1.738, Si 2.329, V 6.11, Cr 7.19, Co 8.90, Ni 8.908, Zn 7.134, Ag 10.49, Au 19.32, B 2.34, Sc 2.985, Mn 7.3, Ge 5.323, Y 4.472 and C 2.2 (graphite, assumed);\nMylar 1.38, Kapton 1.42 and polyethylene 0.93;\nfor the other compounds of henke_mass_attenuation, CH 1.05, Lexan 1.20, parylene (N) 1.11, Saran 1.70, SiO₂ 2.2, CaF₂ 3.18 and B₄C 2.52, with the CH, parylene and Saran densities assumed.\n\nPass material => (thickness, density) to override a density.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.filter_transmission"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.kshell_ce-Tuple{Any}","page":"Shot review","text":"kshell_ce(line; irradiance, prepulse = false, pulse = nothing,\n          model = kshell_ce_model()) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturns the He-α conversion efficiency of a planar foil from the fit kshell_ce_model. The CE is the fraction of the incident laser energy emitted in the line into 4π. line is an element Symbol (see kshell_line_energy) or a line energy in keV, and irradiance (W/cm²) is the spot-average incident value.\n\nReturns (ce, sigma, lo, hi, optimum, peak, extrapolated):\n\nce: the median CE.\nsigma: the 1σ uncertainty of ln CE, which combines the model scatter and the coefficient uncertainty of the fit at this point in quadrature. CE is lognormal in this model, uncertain by a factor e^σ either way.\nlo = ce·e^-σ and hi = ce·e^σ.\noptimum and peak: the irradiance at which the CE of this line peaks, and that CE.\nextrapolated: whether the point lies outside the data of its class in line energy or irradiance, or in pulse length when pulse (ns) is given. pulse changes nothing else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.kshell_ce"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.kshell_ce_data-Tuple{}","page":"Shot review","text":"kshell_ce_data() -> NamedTuple\n\nReturns the He-α conversion-efficiency compilation behind kshell_ce_model, read from data/kshell_ce.csv, with the sources in its header. The columns are element, Z, hnu (keV), irradiance (10¹⁵ W/cm²), pulse (ns), class (\"foil\" or \"prepulsed\"), ce and u (%), fit (1 if fitted), facility, source and note. u is NaN where no error is published. The sources are:\n\nWorkman & Kyrala (2001), OMEGA, 1 ns, digitized from the figures;\nBarrios et al. (2013, 2014), NIF, a picket ahead of a 2–5 ns drive, from the tables and text;\nCoppari et al. (2019), Fe, Cu and Ge foils on OMEGA and OMEGA EP, 0.6–2 ns with or without a pre-pulse, digitized from their Fig. 16;\none OMEGA Ti foil (Girard et al. 2005, as recalibrated in the 2016 review), held out with fit = 0 as a check below the fitted lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.kshell_ce_data"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.kshell_ce_model-Tuple{}","page":"Shot review","text":"kshell_ce_model() -> KShellCEModel\n\nReturns the weighted least-squares fit of ln CE to the rows of kshell_ce_data with fit = 1 (68 points). CE is the He-α line with its satellites, into 4π, over the incident laser energy, for planar foils driven at 351 nm:\n\nln mathrmCE = a_c + bmax(L - L_s 0) + c_cY + dY^2qquad Y = X - sL\n\nwith L = ln(hnu10 mathrmkeV) and X = ln(I10^15 mathrmWcm^2). The CE of each line is a parabola in ln I of shared curvature d. It peaks at the optimum irradiance I^* propto (hnu)^s, where Y* = −c_c/2d. The peak CE falls as (hnu)^b above the saturation energy hnu_s = 10 mathrmkeV e^L_s and is flat below it. The class c is :foil for one main pulse or :prepulsed for a pre-pulse or picket ahead of the main drive. The amplitude a_c and the linear coefficient c_c differ by class, so each class has a separate peak and optimum; b, d, s and hνₛ are shared. The fitted values are:\n\nb = −3.33 ± 0.25, where Workman & Kyrala report hν^-(2.5–4) and Barrios et al. report hν^-(3–4);\ns = 2.5 ± 0.4;\nhνₛ = 6.1 ± 0.4 keV, between Cr and Fe;\na foil optimum of 1.2e15 W/cm² for Cr, 1.8e15 for Fe and 5.2e15 for Ge, with a peak of 3.2% at and below hνₛ;\na prepulsed optimum of 2.0e15 for Ge.\n\nWhy this form. The reasons are recorded in the research notes, \"He-α CE: saturating peak (2026-10-26)\". The earlier form, a_c + bL + c_c X + dX² + eXL, has a peak a_c + bL + (c_c + eL)²/(4|d|) that is convex in L. Its peak therefore rose steeply toward low hν, to 4.1% for Cr and 9.2% for Ti, against the Cr data (3.0% at 0.93e15) and the held-out Ti foil. Writing the parabola about its optimum Y removes that convexity at the same number of parameters. It also lowers the cross-validated error, from 0.55 to 0.44 rms in ln CE with one source left out at a time. The saturation adds one parameter. The Cr points require it, since they lie 0.24 below the power law through Fe–Mo, and with it the fit predicts the held-out Ti foil within 1.1σ. Inside the data the saturation is weakly determined, since the likelihood changes by only 1.1 in −2 ln L with it. With Cr left out, hνₛ is not identified. Two plausible reasons for a plateau below Fe are not modeled. Absorption, and the fraction of it radiated, cannot grow without bound. At the lower temperatures that suffice for low-Z lines, the energy also goes increasingly into lower ionization stages and the continuum.\n\nWeights. Each point has the variance σᵢ² = sᵢ² + τ². The term sᵢ = u/ce is the published error bar as a relative error in ln CE. Where a row has none, as for the digitized points of Workman & Kyrala, sᵢ = √(0.1² + 0.2²) combines their stated relative error and shot-to-shot reproducibility. τ is the scatter that the stated errors do not cover, from calibrations, spot definitions, bands and the model form. It is set to 0.23, so that the weighted χ² per degree of freedom is 1 (random effects). For given (s, Lₛ) the other six parameters are linear, and (s, Lₛ) maximize the likelihood on a refined grid. The covariance of all eight is (JᵀWJ)⁻¹, with J the gradient of ln CE at the data. The scatter of the model is the spread of a measured CE about the fit, √(τ² + s̄²) with s̄ the rms sᵢ. It is 0.35 in ln CE, a factor of 1.43. Outside the data the parameter covariance adds to it (see kshell_ce).\n\nPulse length and spot size do not enter. The foil data are 1 ns (OMEGA), except the Fe shots of Coppari et al. at 0.6 and 2 ns. A ln τ term on the foil class fits CE ∝ τ^(0.6 ± 0.2), but only through the 2 ns Fe of Coppari against the 1 ns Fe of Workman. Within the shots of Coppari, the 1 ns Fe point lies above the 2 ns ones. The pulse length therefore stays confounded with the source and, in the prepulsed class, with the picket. Every source varies the irradiance through the spot size, so the spot size is confounded with the irradiance.\n\nBelow Cr the plateau is an extrapolation. The measured CE of the one held-out Ti foil (OMEGA, 1 ns, 1.3e15 W/cm², fit = 0) is 1.6%, where the fit gives 3.0% ×/÷ 1.48. That 1.6% is the He-α share of a 4–6 keV band, inferred; the whole band has 2.3%. The difference is −0.63 in ln CE, 1.1σ with the 42% error bar of that point. On Gekko XII and Shenguang II, Ti foils had CEs of 1.4–1.8% in the band, so the CE may fall below Cr, where the fitted plateau may not hold. The fit does not separate the two cases. Ti is flagged extrapolated; pass a measured ce for Ti backlighters when one exists. The fitted Fe peak (2.3%) lies below the two-sided Fe of Coppari et al. (2.8–3.25%) and the 3.0% of Workman at 3.5e15. It agrees with the Fe of Workman near 1e15 (2.4%), so the sources disagree by about the two-sided factor.\n\nEngineering model: confidence medium inside the data, low beyond it. Facilities, spectrometer calibrations, spot definitions, one- and two-sided irradiation and bands are mixed in the compilation; the 550 eV band of Workman may include part of Ly-α. The fit runs once per session, from the CSV.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.kshell_ce_model"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.kshell_line_energy-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Shot review","text":"kshell_line_energy(element) -> keV\n\nReturns the photon energy of the He-α resonance line of a backlighter element given as a Symbol. The line is the He-like 1s²–1s2p ¹P₁ transition, the \"w\" line. The elements are :Ti, :V, :Cr, :Mn, :Fe, :Co, :Ni, :Cu, :Zn, :Ga, :Ge, :Br, :Kr, :Zr, :Nb, :Mo and :Ag. The energies of Ti–Ge and Kr are the commonly quoted line energies, to ~0.01 keV. Those of Br, Zr, Nb, Mo and Ag come from a screened-hydrogenic fit anchored on the others, hν ≈ 10.204 eV · (Z − 0.42)² · [1 + 0.0011 (Z − 22)], good to ~0.5%. For Zr the fit gives 16.30 keV, and Barrios et al. 2013 quote 16.3. The values for Z ≥ 35 are derived.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.kshell_line_energy"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.mass_attenuation-Tuple{Symbol, Real}","page":"Shot review","text":"mass_attenuation(material, hν; table = :auto) -> cm²/g\n\nMass attenuation coefficient μ/ρ of a filter material at photon energy hν (keV).\n\ntable = :nist takes the NIST X-Ray Mass Attenuation Coefficients (Hubbell & Seltzer), with coherent scattering included, over 3–40 keV. The table covers :Be, :Al, :Ti, :Fe, :Cu, :Mo, :Ta, :mylar, :kapton and :polyethylene; :kapton is given the μ/ρ of Mylar, a few-percent approximation. Values are interpolated log-log with the absorption edges exact, and an energy exactly on an edge is given the value above it. Between the table energies, spaced 1–5 keV, the values are good to a few percent away from edges.\ntable = :henke takes photoabsorption from Henke's f₂ (henke_mass_attenuation) over 0.01–30 keV. It covers the elements H, B, Be, C, N, O, F, Mg, Al, Si, Cl, Ca, Sc, Ti, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Ge, Y, Mo, Ag and Au and the compounds :CH, :lexan, :parylene, :parylene_c, :saran, :polyimide, :mylar, :polyethylene, :SiO2, :CaF2 and :B4C, where :polyimide is the same as :kapton.\ntable = :auto (default) takes NIST where it covers the material and energy and Henke elsewhere, so the backlighter filters keep the NIST values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mass_attenuation"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.ApertureKeepOut","page":"Shot review","text":"ApertureKeepOut(target; apertures = planar aperture components, peak_fraction = 0.01,\n                intensity = nothing, pointing = 0.05, target_offset = 0.025,\n                hardware_tolerance = 0.0, focus = 1.0, margin = 0.0,\n                minimum = 0.0, weight = 100.0, nrim = 96, softness = 1e-4)\n\nKeep-out constraint for beams entering through planar apertures of any shape, such as laser entrance holes and slots. aperture_clearance(keepout, beams) gives the worst-case clearance (mm) of each beam from the rim of its entrance aperture, which is the aperture on the port side of the beam. The clearance is judged as clearance judges it. The beam envelope is the best-focus contour at peak_fraction of the peak (or at intensity, W/cm²), grown by the f-cone radius |s|/(2F) and margin. The clearance is the smallest distance from the rim to the envelope, less the transverse error radius pointing + target_offset + hardware_tolerance and focus/(2F) for the focus error.\n\nThe rim is the boundary of the hole. It consists of the edges of the aperture triangles that no other triangle of the aperture continues across; a closing lip in the plane of the aperture about the same center is part of the union. On a DiskSurface aperture, an edge whose ends lie at one radius from the center of the surface is the circular arc between them, the exact shape of the element. The rim of a circular hole meshed as a polygon is therefore its circle. Every other edge is straight, as for rectangular, slotted or elliptical openings and CAD apertures, where the mesh is the geometry. The rim is sampled at about nrim points, with every straight edge sampled at both ends. The least distance on each sample interval is found exactly, at an end or where the slope of the distance along the rim rises through zero. That point is located from the slopes at the samples to 1e-12 of the interval. An interval is first split at these points:\n\nwhere the distance has a kink, which is where the rim crosses the plane of best focus and the f-cone term turns;\nwhere the rim crosses an axis of an elongated envelope, since the first-order distance below turns sharply there;\ninside the envelope, where the rim passes nearest the beam axis, since the first-order distance dips steeply there;\nwhere the cubic through the distances and slopes at the ends turns twice.\n\nThe rim of a dual beam is searched on its values. The partials of the result follow from its sensitivities to the focus and the semi-axes of the envelope, so their cost does not grow with the number of partials.\n\nThe clearance is a smooth minimum of these interval minima, −softness · log Σ σₖ exp(−dₖ/softness) (softness in mm). The weight σₖ of each interval is 1 within 10 softness of the least and falls smoothly to 0 at 20 softness, where a term weighs at most e⁻¹⁰ of the least. The exact minimum over the rim has kinks where the worst rim point jumps between separate local minima of the distance, and the smooth minimum rounds them off. It never exceeds the exact minimum, lies below it by at most softness times the log of the number of intervals counted, and is the exact minimum for softness = 0. The default, 1e-4 mm, is an engineering default well below the agreement of the keep-out with clearance, which is a few micrometers. On NIF quads through the entrance holes of a hohlraum, the smooth minimum lies 0 to 0.18 µm below the exact one. Over 144 scans of the aim, the largest change of slope in a step falls from 0.21 (a kink) to 0.002, which remains where one interval contains two local minima (research notes, \"Defaults changed (2026-11-19)\").\n\nThe distance to the envelope ellipse is first order (Sampson). It is exact on the envelope and for round spots, and smooth in aim, defocus and spot size. A design should be confirmed with clearance, which also accounts for wings and power on hardware. The keep-out is passed as keepout to symmetry_objective or asymmetry_objective, which then add weight × Σᵢ max(0, minimum − cᵢ)².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ApertureKeepOut"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.SphereKeepOut","page":"Shot review","text":"SphereKeepOut(target; spheres = parts on a `SphereSurface`, peak_fraction = 0.01,\n              intensity = nothing, pointing = 0.05, target_offset = 0.025,\n              hardware_tolerance = 0.0, focus = 1.0, margin = 0.0, minimum = 0.0,\n              weight = 100.0)\n\nKeep-out constraint for spherical obstacles inside a target, such as the capsule of a hohlraum. sphere_clearance(keepout, beams) gives the worst-case clearance (mm) of each beam from the nearest of the spheres that it passes before landing, with the envelope and error set of ApertureKeepOut. The clearance is the distance from the center of the sphere to the beam envelope, less the radius of the sphere, the transverse error radius pointing + target_offset + hardware_tolerance and focus/(2F). The envelope is the best-focus contour at peak_fraction of the peak, or at intensity, grown by the f-cone at the axial position of the center and by margin. The distance to the envelope is first order (Sampson), as for apertures, and the f-cone tilt of the envelope surface (the cosine of the cone half-angle) is neglected.\n\nBy default the spheres are the parts that are spheres, which are those whose largest spherical component covers at least 90% of the part area. Examples are a primitive capsule, or a CAD capsule fitted with curved_components with its fill tube a separate piece. The small spherical fillets fitted on hohlraum CAD are excluded. spheres names parts or components. A part stands for its largest sphere, and its other pieces are not landing surfaces.\n\nA sphere counts only when its center lies upstream of the point where the beam axis first meets another opaque component, such as the wall on which a hohlraum beam lands. That point is found on the nominal beam, and a sphere behind that surface is shadowed and ignored. The penalty added by the objectives is weight × Σᵢ max(0, minimum − cᵢ)². The deposition penalty direct (the squared fraction of power on the capsule) vanishes as the spot edge starts to clip, so it cannot hold a margin. This keep-out serves when the capsule must stay clear, and a design is confirmed with clearance on the capsule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SphereKeepOut"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.aperture_clearance-Tuple{ApertureKeepOut, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"aperture_clearance(keepout, beams) -> Vector\n\nWorst-case clearance (mm) of each beam from the rim of its entrance aperture (see ApertureKeepOut), and Inf for a beam with no aperture on its port side. Differentiable in the aim, defocus and spot size of the beams.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.aperture_clearance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.keepout_clearance-Tuple{ApertureKeepOut, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"keepout_clearance(keepout, beams) -> Vector\nkeepout_clearance(keepout, ev::Evaluation) -> Vector\n\nThe worst-case clearance (mm) of each beam from a keep-out, which is the quantity that its penalty holds at or above the minimum of the keep-out. It is aperture_clearance for an ApertureKeepOut, sphere_clearance for a SphereKeepOut and hardware_clearance for a HardwareKeepOut, and Inf for a beam to which the keep-out does not apply. It is a pure function of the beams, or of the setup beams of an Evaluation, in their number type. A constraint keepout_clearance(keepout, ev) .≥ minimum can therefore be handed to an optimizer as it stands.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.keepout_clearance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.keepout_penalty-Tuple{Nothing, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"keepout_penalty(keepout, beams) -> Real\nkeepout_penalty(keepout, ev::Evaluation) -> Real\n\nThe penalty that a keep-out adds to an objective, weight × Σᵢ max(0, minimum − cᵢ)² over the clearances cᵢ = keepout_clearance(keepout, beams), with the weight and minimum of the keep-out. For a tuple or vector of keep-outs it is the sum of their penalties, and for nothing it is 0. The keepout keyword of symmetry_objective, asymmetry_objective and face_flux_objective adds this penalty, so a hand-written objective adds keepout_penalty(keepout, ev) to the same effect. In the number type of the beams (ForwardDiff duals) and thread-safe. A keep-out of another type takes part through a keepout_clearance method and weight and minimum fields.\n\nA clearance of +Inf, for a beam to which the keep-out does not apply, adds nothing. A clearance of -Inf or NaN throws an ArgumentError naming the keep-out and the beam. -Inf marks a beam that fails a rule with no finite margin to continue, such as a CaptureKeepIn beam whose axis misses every part when no part has a free edge. Either value would give the optimizer a non-finite objective, and a large finite penalty in its place would hide the cause.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.keepout_penalty"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.sphere_clearance-Tuple{SphereKeepOut, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"sphere_clearance(keepout, beams) -> Vector\n\nWorst-case clearance (mm) of each beam from the spheres of a SphereKeepOut that it passes before landing, and Inf for a beam that passes none. Differentiable in the aim, defocus and spot size of the beams.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.sphere_clearance"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.IrradianceHistory","page":"Shot review","text":"IrradianceHistory\n\nIncident laser irradiance on a target over time, per color. It is the result of unconverted_history (colors :omega1, :omega2) and of irradiance_history (3ω, :omega3).\n\ntarget, time (ns), colors (the color names, in order)\nelement — per color, a nelements × length(time) matrix of the irradiance incident on each element (W/cm²), which is the power reaching it over its area, at its incidence\nbackside — per color, a ncomponents × length(time) matrix of the irradiance on the unmodeled back side of each one-sided component (W/cm²). It is the power over the area of the component, a mean that lies below the local peak of the back side where it is lit unevenly.\nabsorption — per color, the fraction of the incident light that a surface absorbs (the absorption of the UnconvertedColor, 1 for 3ω). The absorbed irradiance is absorption[c] .* element[c].\nskipped — the beams whose unconverted light cannot be placed (see unconverted_deposition)\n\nBetween its times the history is taken to be linear, and crossings of a threshold (threshold_crossing) are interpolated within a step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.IrradianceHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.UnconvertedColor","page":"Shot review","text":"UnconvertedColor(name; footprint, edge, focal_shift, absorption = 1.0, conversion = 1.0)\n\nUnconverted-light footprint of one color at the 3ω best-focus plane. The footprint is a square of side footprint (mm) whose nearest edge lies edge mm from the 3ω aim point, displaced in a port-dependent direction. Its focus lies focal_shift mm beyond the 3ω focus.\n\nabsorption is the fraction of the incident light of this color that a surface absorbs, relative to the 3ω light, which the deposition takes as fully absorbed. conversion is the x-ray conversion efficiency of the absorbed light relative to the 3ω conversion of a material. unconverted_deposition deposits absorption times the incident power, and solve with unconverted multiplies the conversion of a material, at the absorbed flux of this color, by conversion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.UnconvertedColor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.UnconvertedReport","page":"Shot review","text":"UnconvertedReport\n\nResult of unconverted_light_check. It contains rows per beam, color and component, and per-component zone and flat flags. zone marks a component whose nearest distance to TCC is within zone_radius. flat marks a planar component, which violates the NIF curvature rule inside a 1ω footprint unless dimpled; the rule requires a 1D curvature radius ≤ 2 mm or a 2D radius ≤ 5 mm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.UnconvertedReport"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.UnconvertedRow","page":"Shot review","text":"UnconvertedRow\n\nOne row of an unconverted-light check, with fields beam, color, component, exposure and distance. exposure is :exposed for hardware inside the footprint for a known offset direction, :possible for hardware inside the footprint for some offset direction when the direction is unknown, and :clear otherwise. distance is the smallest transverse distance (mm) from the footprint region to the component, negative inside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.UnconvertedRow"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.irradiance_history-Tuple{DepositionOperator, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any, Any}","page":"Shot review","text":"irradiance_history(op, beams, pulses, times; by = :group) -> IrradianceHistory\n\nIrradiance of the 3ω light (color :omega3) on the target of the DepositionOperator op at each of times (ns), with the beams following pulses. pulses is one function of time for all beams, or keyed by by as in with_pulses. With the geometry fixed, deposition is linear in the power of each beam. The history therefore costs one deposit per distinct pulse, with the beams of that pulse at unit power and the others at zero, scaled by the pulse at each time. The irradiance is the incident light (every ray deposits all its power), before any laser–plasma model. preplasma_check uses it for the time at which the backlighter beams arrive on their foil.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.irradiance_history"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.nif_unconverted-Tuple{}","page":"Shot review","text":"nif_unconverted()\n\nNIF 1ω and 2ω footprints (NIF User Guide §4.5, §5.6.1). The 1ω footprint is a square of about 22 mm with its nearest edge about 4.8 mm from the 3ω aim point, and the 2ω footprint a square of about 12 mm with its nearest edge about 2.9 mm away. The focal shift is +460 mm for 1ω (MacFarlane 2003) and about +270 mm for 2ω, derived from fused-silica dispersion (see reference/research-notes.md).\n\nThe absorption and x-ray conversion relative to 3ω are an engineering default with low confidence. They are the ratios on gold disks at the lowest intensity measured, about 3 × 10¹³ W/cm² with pulses of about 1 ns, held constant below that intensity.\n\n1ω: absorption 0.82 against 0.93 at 3ω (Ze et al., UCRL-86414 (1981), Fig. 4) gives 0.88; conversion of the absorbed energy 0.50 against 0.57 (Mead et al., LA-UR-86-300 (1985), Fig. 16; same laboratory) gives 0.88.\n2ω: absorption equal to that at 3ω (Ze, Fig. 4) gives 1.0. The conversion is 0.72 (small spots) to 1.16 (large spots) of that at 3ω (Mead, Fig. 16), and the geometric mean, 0.9, is used.\n\nUnconverted light reaches hardware at ~10¹⁰–10¹² W/cm², 1.5–3.5 decades below these data. The ratios rise toward 1 as the intensity falls, and inverse-bremsstrahlung absorption is near complete in that regime. Before a plasma forms, cold metals reflect 1ω almost entirely, and cold silicon transmits it (research notes, \"Unconverted light: absorption and conversion vs. wavelength\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_unconverted"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.preplasma_check-Tuple{IrradianceHistory, IrradianceHistory}","page":"Shot review","text":"preplasma_check(history, arrival; foil = :backlighter, threshold = 1e8, colors = nothing)\n    -> NamedTuple (onset, arrival, lead, preplasma)\n\nWhether the foil foil forms a pre-plasma before its backlighter beams arrive. onset is the first time (ns) at which the irradiance of history, the unconverted light of unconverted_history summed over colors, reaches threshold (W/cm²) on the foil (threshold_crossing). arrival is a time (ns), or the 3ω irradiance_history of the backlighter beams, whose first crossing of the same threshold on the foil is then the arrival. lead = arrival - onset (ns; -Inf when the unconverted light never reaches the threshold), and preplasma = onset < arrival.\n\nThe default 10⁸ W/cm² is the general guideline of the NIF User Guide for the onset of weakly ionized plasma on a metal surface (§4.4, citing Spaeth et al., Fusion Sci. Technol. 69, 25 (2016)). The guide puts the 1ω prepulse above it 3–5 ns before the nominal start of the pulse. It is a guideline for metals, and no facility criterion for foils sets it. A foil of another material needs a separate value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.preplasma_check"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.specular_check-Tuple{IrradianceHistory}","page":"Shot review","text":"specular_check(history; threshold = 1e8, parts = nothing, colors = nothing)\n    -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nWhether each surface part stays below the irradiance threshold (W/cm²) at which it is taken to stop being a specular reflector, under the light of history summed over colors. history is an IrradianceHistory, e.g. of unconverted_history. The result has one row per part of parts (by default every surface part), in target order, with the fields part, peak (the highest irradiance on any of its elements or back sides, W/cm²), peak_time (ns), crossing and specular. crossing is the first time at which the part reaches threshold (threshold_crossing, Inf if never), and specular is crossing == Inf.\n\nA surface below the threshold stays a cold mirror, and the NIF curvature rule disperses its specular 1ω reflection. The rule requires a curvature radius ≤ 2 mm in one dimension, ≤ 5 mm in two, or dimpling (User Guide §5.6.1). Above the threshold a plasma forms, and the cold-mirror reflection no longer applies. The default 10⁸ W/cm² is an engineering default with low confidence. It is the general guideline of the User Guide for the onset of weakly ionized plasma on a metal surface (§4.4, citing Spaeth et al., Fusion Sci. Technol. 69, 25 (2016)), taken as the end of specular reflection. No facility criterion for specularity sets it. It applies only to metals, and silicon and plastics need separate values. A fluence-driven change of the surface (melting, roughening) is not modeled. See the research notes, \"Unconverted light over time\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.specular_check"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.threshold_crossing-Tuple{IrradianceHistory, Real}","page":"Shot review","text":"threshold_crossing(history, threshold; parts = nothing, colors = nothing)\n    -> NamedTuple (time, part, element)\n\nThe first time (ns) at which the irradiance of history (an IrradianceHistory), summed over colors, reaches threshold (W/cm²) on any element or one-sided back side of the surface components of parts. By default colors are all the colors of the history and parts are all parts, and the history is linear between its times. part is the part where the threshold is reached, and element the element (0 for a back side, whose irradiance is the component mean). When the threshold is never reached, time = Inf, part = :none and element = 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.threshold_crossing"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.unconverted_deposition-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"unconverted_deposition(target, beams; ratio, colors = nif_unconverted(), offsets = nothing,\n                       lens_distance = 7700.0, coarse = 0.5, resolution = 0.02)\n    -> NamedTuple\n\nLaser power of the unconverted (1ω, 2ω) light absorbed by the target. The result contains one Deposition per color of colors, keyed by its name (:omega1, :omega2). It also contains skipped, the beams whose footprints cannot be placed (no NIF quad name and no offsets entry), and colors, the UnconvertedColors themselves. solve and flux_response then use the x-ray conversions of the result with its light.\n\nThe light of one color from each beamlet is a uniform square beam converging to its focus. At the 3ω best-focus plane it is a square of side footprint whose nearest edge lies edge mm from the 3ω axis (UnconvertedColor). nif_unconverted gives the values of the NIF User Guide, 22 mm and 4.8 mm for 1ω and 12 mm and 2.9 mm for 2ω (§4.5 and Fig. 4-12). The beam converges to a point focal_shift beyond the 3ω focus on the chief ray from the lens center, lens_distance upstream. This is the geometry of the 1ω footprint of talis_check, at the nominal size of the footprint where TaLIS uses its 10% contour. The square is displaced along φ̂ to the side of the quad axis on which the port of the beamlet lies (Fig. 4-12). A NIF quad-level beam is expanded into its four beamlets, and offsets sets the displacement direction of other beams, as in talis_check. The light at a target is the image of the near-field aperture, so its intensity is taken to be uniform across the square, as a flat top. The blur of the edge by the phase plate, about 0.1 mm, is not modeled. The geometry is derived (confidence medium; research notes, \"Unconverted light (NIF)\").\n\nratio gives, per color, the unconverted power as a fraction of the 3ω power of each beam, e.g. (omega1 = 0.05, omega2 = 0.01), as a number or as a vector with one entry per beam. It has no default. According to the NIF User Guide (§4.5), most of the unconverted light is 1ω and shaped pulses leave more of it, but the guide does not give a fraction. VisRad also takes the fraction as an input, in its \"Fraction of Total Beam Power\" and \"Relative to Blue Cone Power\" models.\n\nWithout shadowing, the power that a beam puts on a flat triangle is exact. Every ray of the beam passes through its focus, so the share of the power on the triangle is the area of its projection from the focus onto the 3ω focal plane, clipped to the footprint square, over the area of the square. This holds at any tilt and distance from focus. Shadowing is resolved by rays from the triangle toward the lens. The clipped projection is divided into pieces of at most coarse mm in the focal plane and, where a shadow edge crosses a piece, down to resolution mm. The side of a surface facing the lens absorbs absorption times the power of the color reaching it. The power goes to its element or, for an unmodeled back side, to the backside of its component. The rest is dropped without reflection, and delivered - sum(per_beam) includes it. Light beyond its focus (hundreds of mm past the target) is not followed. Values are Float64, since this is a fixed source and lies outside the differentiable deposition path (deposit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.unconverted_deposition"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.unconverted_history-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any, Any}","page":"Shot review","text":"unconverted_history(target, beams, pulses, times; ratio, prepulse = (;), by = :group,\n                    colors = nif_unconverted(), offsets = nothing, lens_distance = 7700.0,\n                    coarse = 0.5, resolution = 0.02) -> IrradianceHistory\n\nIrradiance of the unconverted (1ω, 2ω) light on the target at each of times (ns), from the 3ω pulse of each beam and an unconverted fraction that may vary in time.\n\npulses gives the 3ω power history of each beam (TW per beam), keyed as in with_pulses. It is one function of time (a PulseShape) for all beams, or a Dict or NamedTuple keyed by the group of each beam. With by = :quad the key is the quad, with by = :beam the name, and otherwise by(beam). For each color c of colors, the light of one beam is\n\nP_c(t) = ratio[c](t) · P₃ω(t) + prepulse[c](t)    (TW per beam)\n\nratio — per color, the unconverted power as a fraction of the 3ω power of the beam, as a number or a function of time (ns), e.g. a PulseShape(times, fractions) table (zero outside its times). It has no default, since the fraction and its dependence on the pulse shape are not public. According to the NIF User Guide (§4.5), most of the unconverted light is 1ω and shaped pulses leave more of it. Every color needs an entry (0 for none), keyed by its name as a Symbol; any other key, such as a String or an unknown name, is an error. The entry of a color applies to every beam, or gives each beam a separate fraction (a number or a function of time). A separate fraction is a vector with one entry per beam of beams, in order, or is keyed as pulses by by. A beam without an entry is a KeyError, as for pulses. An example for 1ω on the outer cones only is (omega1 = Dict(:outer44 => 0.05, :outer50 => 0.05, :inner23 => 0.0, :inner30 => 0.0), omega2 = 0.01).\nprepulse — per color (optional), light not proportional to the 3ω pulse, in TW per beam. It is one function of time (a PulseShape) for all beams or is keyed as pulses, and it is keyed by color as ratio is. An example is the 1ω prepulse, which the User Guide (§4.4) puts above 10⁸ W/cm² 3–5 ns before the nominal start of the pulse, with no level given. As a derived scale, the 22 mm 1ω footprint (4.84 cm² in the 3ω focal plane) is at 10⁸ W/cm² when a beamlet carries 0.48 GW (4.8e-4 TW) of 1ω.\n\nThe geometry is fixed, with the aims, spots and ports of beams, and only the power varies. The light is linear in the power of each beam, so the whole history costs one unconverted_deposition. The beams are grouped by their (pulse, prepulse, fraction) schedule (equal numbers or the same function), and each group is deposited once at unit power. The history is that pattern times the power of the group at each time. A pointing or a target that changes in time needs a deposition per configuration, which this function does not cover.\n\nThe irradiance is the light incident on each surface, with the absorption of each color set to 1, since the threshold checks (specular_check, preplasma_check) compare incident intensities. Multiplied by the area of each element (and divided by 10¹⁴ for W/cm² to TW/mm²), absorption[c] .* element[c] is the power that unconverted_deposition absorbs at the beam powers of each time. For a constant fraction the two agree within rounding. offsets, lens_distance, coarse and resolution are those of unconverted_deposition. The history is sampled at times and is linear between them, so the knots of the pulses and fractions should be included in times to follow them exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.unconverted_history"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.unconverted_light_check-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"unconverted_light_check(target, beams; colors = nif_unconverted(), offsets = nothing,\n                        hardware = all surfaces, zone_radius = 32.0, level = 1)\n\nCheck target hardware against unconverted-light footprints. Footprints are prisms along each beam through its square footprint at the 3ω focal plane. Near TCC the converging 1ω and 2ω beams change size by ≲ 2% per cm, so the prism is a close approximation. Shadowing is ignored, so the exposure is conservative.\n\noffsets maps beam names to the footprint offset direction, given as an angle (degrees, from θ̂ of the port toward φ̂, in the focal plane) or as a 3-vector. The per-port directions are not public. Without them every azimuth is considered, and hardware is reported as :possible when any offset direction would expose it.\n\nAlso reports components within zone_radius mm of TCC (where unconverted light reaches) and planar components (flagged against the NIF curvature rule).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.unconverted_light_check"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.TALIS_OUT_OF_SCOPE","page":"Shot review","text":"TALIS_OUT_OF_SCOPE\n\nRequirements of the TCC Configuration Requirements document that talis_check does not evaluate, because Briareus has no model of the hardware involved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TALIS_OUT_OF_SCOPE"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.TALIS_SOURCE","page":"Shot review","text":"Source of the rules implemented by talis_check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TALIS_SOURCE"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.TaLISReport","page":"Shot review","text":"TaLISReport\n\nResult of talis_check, with rows (one TaLISRow per beam, rule and component), the settings used (thresholds, error set, tolerance interpretation) and notes on checks that were not evaluated. See violations, write_talis_csv.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TaLISReport"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.TaLISRow","page":"Shot review","text":"TaLISRow\n\nOne check of talis_check, for a (beam, rule, component) triple.\n\nbeam — beam name (\"upper beams\"/\"lower beams\" for :tas_stay_in)\nrule — :capture_3w, :incidence_3w, :clearance_3w, :clearance_1w, :management_1w, :diagnostic_1w or :tas_stay_in (see talis_check)\ncomponent — target component (:none when a beam misses every capture surface)\nstatus — :pass; :fail (the criterion is violated in the worst case); or :review (a TaLIS \"evaluate further\" outcome, or not decidable automatically)\nmargin — worst-case value less the requirement, in unit (:mm, or :deg for angle rules); positive passes\nworst, nominal — the measured quantity in the worst case (errors and the tolerance applied) and at nominal (neither applied)\nrequired — the TaLIS threshold, in unit\npoint — worst location (mm)\nnote — consequence or explanation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TaLISRow"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.talis_check-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"talis_check(target, beams; capture, hardware = all surfaces, leh = (),\n            omega1_components = all surfaces, diagnostics = (), tolerance = 0.5, pivot = :tcc,\n            pointing = 0.05, target_offset = 0.025, hardware_tolerance = 0.0,\n            focus = 1.0, ssd = 0.0, offsets = nothing, open_ports = :nif,\n            curvature = Dict(), level = 2, resolution = 1e-4, rtol = 0.01, ...) -> TaLISReport\n\nNIF TaLIS target-configuration checks from the \"TCC Configuration Requirements\" (NIF User Forum, 27 Oct 2018, LMF Shot RI Resources). Every distance is measured orthogonal to the beam, as the document states, and judged on the worst case over the error set (and, except for incidence, the rule tolerance), never at nominal.\n\ncapture lists the components that beams are meant to land on, such as backlighter foils, capture surfaces and hohlraum walls. The landing component of a beam is the first of them its nominal axis meets. leh lists the components that form the edge of a laser entrance hole the beams pass through (the end caps or LEH lips of a hohlraum, e.g. (:cap_top, :cap_bottom) for hohlraum); they are checked for 3ω clearance like hardware, whether or not hardware names them. Here and in hardware, leh, omega1_components, diagnostics and curvature, a component may be named by its part: a part split for computation (curved_components) is checked as one, and its rows are listed under the part name.\n\nRules\n\n:capture_3w — the capture_contour (10%)-of-peak contour must lie ≥ capture_margin (0.5 mm) inside the substrate edge of the landing component: the minimum over the edge of the transverse distance to the beam envelope (best-focus contour grown by the f-cone at the axial position of each point, see BeamEnvelope). Substrate edges are the free edges of the opaque mesh (edges with a single triangle; edges shared with another component are not edges). A beam whose axis misses every capture component fails with component :none.\n:incidence_3w — incidence < max_incidence (70°) to the target normal, the maximum over the surface of the landing component inside the worst-case 10% footprint (visible from the beam; exact normals on SphereSurface/CylinderSurface components). The angle is that of the steepest ray of the f-cone, the angle of the beam axis plus the cone half-angle atan(1/(2f#)) (1.38° at NIF), as VisRad's capture report measures it (VisRad 21 reports tilt + 1.384–1.390° on plates tilted 68–69.7° toward either spot axis). The incidence check includes the error set but not the rule tolerance; the capture margin and the clearances include both.\n:clearance_3w — the clearance_contour (1%)-of-peak contour must clear each hardware component other than the landing component by > clearance_margin (0.5 mm); hardware downstream of the landing point is skipped, and everything along the beam is checked if the beam misses every capture component. Capture and clearance failures have the TaLIS consequence, an assessment of blow-by by the backscatter working group. On an leh component the rule is an LEH-clearance assessment: not clearing it is a :review (on other hardware, a :fail; see \"Entrance-hole edges\").\n:clearance_1w — the 10% contour of the 1ω footprint must clear each omega1_components component by > omega1_margin (0.2 mm). Not clearing is a :review (the TaLIS outcome is to evaluate unconverted-light management) and is resolved by the next rule.\n:management_1w — for each surface the 1ω footprint does not clear: pass if its radius of curvature is < cylinder_radius (2 mm, cylindrical) or < sphere_radius (5 mm, spherical); otherwise the specular 1ω reflection from every uncleared facet must stay > reflection_angle (20°) from every open quad (open_ports), less twice the tolerance angle (a tilt by α turns a reflection by 2α). Curvature comes from curvature[name] when given, and otherwise from the CylinderSurface/SphereSurface of the component or a planar mesh. The entry is (:cylinder, r), (:sphere, r), :flat, or (:dimpled, r) / (:dimpled, r, half_side) for a surface covered with spherical dimples of radius of curvature r, e.g. the dimpled 1ω \"sombrero\" shield around the LEH of an ignition hohlraum, modeled as a flat annulus_mesh. A dimpled surface is judged like a sphere against sphere_radius. The NIF User Guide (§5.6.2) also requires a dimple half-side/radius ≥ 0.25, so a dimpled surface passes only with half_side given and that ratio met, and is a :review otherwise. For other meshes the curvature is unknown and a failing reflection check is a :review. A flat component without a curvature entry is given the reflection check, which a horizontal plate usually fails (it mirrors each beam into the conjugate port of the same hemisphere).\n:diagnostic_1w — the diagnostics components (diagnostic hardware modeled in the target) must stay ≥ 10 mm from 1ω light at TCC, growing linearly to 25 mm at 1 m (10 + 0.015 r mm, r the distance from TCC; interpretation of \"a cone of 10 mm at TCC extruded to 25 mm at 1 m\"). With no diagnostics the rule is listed in the notes of the report as not evaluated. Diagnostic hardware given only as hardware is checked for the 3ω and 0.2 mm 1ω clearances but not for this rule.\n:tas_stay_in — (NIF beams) no component may extend more than stay_in (19 mm) above the lowest aim point of an upper beam or below the highest aim point of a lower beam (TAS alignment stay-in zone; violating it may require a different alignment sequence or beam selection). hardware_tolerance is added to the extent.\n\nThe remaining requirements of the document are listed in TALIS_OUT_OF_SCOPE.\n\n1ω footprint\n\nThe 1ω rules apply to beams whose quad is a NIF quad name (or that have an offsets entry). A NIF quad-level beam (port within 0.1° of its quad port, as from nif_quads) is expanded into its four beamlets (LMF beam table) sharing its aim and defocus. The 1ω footprint of each beamlet at its 3ω best-focus plane is a square of side omega1_size whose nearest edge is omega1_edge = 4.45 mm (the TaLIS 10% contour) from the 3ω axis, displaced in φ̂ toward the beamlet side of its quad (the side of the quad axis its port lies on; NIF User Guide Fig. 4-12 — derived, confidence medium). The default side is the User Guide's 22 mm footprint grown by 4.8 − 4.45 = 0.35 mm per side, so its near edge is the TaLIS 10% contour (22.7 mm). Away from that plane the footprint is the cone converging to the 1ω focus omega1_focus (default 460 mm, from nif_unconverted) beyond the 3ω focus, with its center on the chief ray from the lens center lens_distance (7.7 m) upstream: at axial position s the near edge is 4.45 + 0.027 s mm, i.e. closer to the 3ω axis upstream of focus (derived). Pass omega1_focus = Inf, lens_distance = Inf for a prism (the footprint fixed in the transverse plane). offsets maps beam names to a footprint direction (degrees from the port θ̂ toward φ̂, or a vector) and overrides the NIF convention for that beam. The focus error moves the footprint with the 3ω focal plane; shadowing is ignored (conservative).\n\nEntrance-hole edges\n\nThe TaLIS document states 3ω clearance \"to target features\" without distinguishing the entrance holes of a target from hardware, and gives every 3ω violation the same consequence: the configuration \"must be assessed for blow-by by the backscatter working group\". The consequence is an assessment and does not prohibit the configuration. Reporting LEH edges as :review while hardware proper, such as shields, stalks, diagnostics and closed windows, stays :fail is an assumption of Briareus; the TaLIS document does not state it. It is based on the fact that no NIF hohlraum with 3.1 mm-diameter LEHs meets the rule on its LEH edge. The best-focus 1% contour of an inner (23.5°) quad has a semi-axis of 1.145 mm along φ̂, which is horizontal and orthogonal to the beam. With the axis through the LEH center, 1.55 − 1.145 = 0.405 mm remains to the lip before the f-cone, any error or the tolerance is counted; the rule requires 0.5 mm. Light that reaches the lip is LEH clipping, which the deposition computes as such.\n\nTolerance and error set\n\nThe document adds \"up to 0.5 degrees metrology/alignment/positioning tolerance\" to the 3ω and 1ω clearances; Briareus also applies it to the 3ω capture margin. The angle is an insertion or assembly error of the target component: the beams must still clear with the component rotated by up to 0.5° off nominal in the least favorable direction, whether the whole target is inserted rotated or the component is assembled off-square (user, from facility practice, 2026-10-27; the document does not give a formula). It enters as a lateral allowance r·tan(tolerance), with r the distance of the hardware point from the pivot of the rotation: the largest displacement of the point that such a rotation causes, taken in full toward the beam (conservative). The default pivot = :tcc is the target inserted rotated about TCC; a component assembled off-square rotates about its mount, given as a point (pivot = (0, 0, 12.0)). :aim (rotation about the beam aim point) and :max (the larger of the TCC and aim distances) are alternatives; with tolerance = 0 the allowance is dropped.\n\nIt is combined with the error set of clearance by linear addition (no RSS):\n\nworst = nominal − (pointing + target_offset + hardware_tolerance) − focus/(2F) − r·tan(tolerance)\n\nevaluated at each point and minimized over the component (for the 1ω footprint the focus term is the footprint shift over ±focus instead of the f-cone). The target_offset and hardware_tolerance partly overlap what the tolerance is meant to cover, so this double counts conservatively; set them to 0 to rely only on the tolerance. ssd (mm) widens the 3ω envelopes as in clearance.\n\nDistances are minimized over the whole of each triangle (and each substrate edge), not at sample points. Every distance in these rules is Lipschitz in the point, so a branch-and-bound subdivision bounds the minimum from below. The Lipschitz constant is √(1\n\ncone²) for the beam envelope, 1 plus its taper for the 1ω footprint and tan(tolerance)\n\nfor the tolerance allowance. The reported worst and nominal values are those lower bounds, never above the exact minimum and within resolution (mm) or rtol times the distance of the value from the rule threshold, whichever is larger: a bound never crosses the threshold, so the status is exact (up to resolution), and only large margins are reported coarser (1% by default). A plate crossed by a beam between its vertices fails. The incidence and specular-reflection rules include each of the 4^level sub-triangles of a component (with its normal) that has any point inside the footprint, and whether a sub-triangle reaches the footprint is determined in the same way. Clearance-only far-field wings (nif_wings) are not used: the TaLIS rules refer to contours of the beam spot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.talis_check"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.talis_requirement-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Shot review","text":"talis_requirement(rule, settings) -> String\n\nHuman-readable requirement of a talis_check rule (:capture_3w, :incidence_3w, :clearance_3w, :clearance_1w, :management_1w, :diagnostic_1w, :tas_stay_in) under the thresholds in settings (a TaLISReport's settings).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.talis_requirement"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.violations-Tuple{TaLISReport}","page":"Shot review","text":"violations(report::TaLISReport; status = (:fail, :review)) -> Vector{TaLISRow}\n\nRows of a TaLIS report whose status is in status, sorted with failures first and then by margin.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.violations"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.write_talis_csv-Tuple{AbstractString, TaLISReport}","page":"Shot review","text":"write_talis_csv(path, report)\n\nWrite every row of a TaLIS report as CSV, with margins in the unit of each row. A header of # comment lines makes the file stand alone. The header contains the source of the rules, every setting in settings, the requirement of each rule, the checks not evaluated (notes) and the requirements out of scope (TALIS_OUT_OF_SCOPE). The settings are the thresholds, the error set, the rule tolerance and pivot, the capture, LEH and diagnostic components, and the resolution. CSV readers skip the header with a comment option, e.g. CSV.File(path; comment = \"#\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_talis_csv"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.CaptureKeepIn","page":"Shot review","text":"CaptureKeepIn(target, parts; peak_fraction = 0.1, intensity = nothing, pointing = 0.05,\n              target_offset = 0.025, hardware_tolerance = 0.0, focus = 1.0,\n              margin = 0.0, tolerance = 0.5, pivot = :tcc, minimum = 0.5,\n              weight = 100.0)\n\nCapture constraint for the parts meant to be struck by beams, such as backlighter foils and capture plates. capture_margin(keepin, beams) gives the worst-case margin (mm) by which the envelope of each beam stays inside the substrate edge of the struck part. The margin is the TaLIS 3ω capture rule of talis_check (:capture_3w) as a quantity, with parts in the role of the capture of talis_check. The struck part is the first of parts (a name or a collection of part or component names) that the nominal beam axis meets; its substrate edge is its free edges (edges of a single opaque triangle, as talis_check takes them). The margin is the smallest signed distance from that edge to the beam envelope (the best-focus contour at peak_fraction of the peak, or at intensity W/cm², grown by the f-cone and margin; negative inside it), less the transverse error radius pointing + target_offset + hardware_tolerance, focus/(2F) for the focus error and the rule tolerance r·tan(tolerance) (r the distance from pivot, as in HardwareKeepOut), point by point. It is minimized over the edge as HardwareKeepOut minimizes over the segments of its parts: exactly, continuous in the aim, defocus and spot size of the beams, with the derivatives of the distance at the closest point. A part without a free edge (a closed surface) gives Inf, as talis_check passes it.\n\nA beam whose axis meets none of parts fails the rule (talis_check reports -Inf). The quantity continues the margin there, to keep the penalty of a stray beam finite and its gradient pointing back: the margin from the outline of the part nearest the axis, less twice the transverse distance d of the axis from that outline. Where the axis crosses the outline d vanishes, so the margin is continuous there (on a straight edge it continues linearly), and it falls at least as fast as the axis moves away. The margin is -Inf when no part has a free edge. The margin jumps where the landing moves from one part to another.\n\nThe defaults are the TaLIS values (the 10% contour at least 0.5 mm inside the edge with a 0.5° tolerance about TCC; talis_check passes a margin at or above 0.5 mm). The quantity is a surrogate checked against talis_check in this package (see the research notes), not against the facility tool. Pass it as keepout to the objectives, which add weight × Σᵢ max(0, minimum − mᵢ)² (keepout_penalty).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CaptureKeepIn"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.HardwareKeepOut","page":"Shot review","text":"HardwareKeepOut(target, parts; targets = every other opaque surface,\n                peak_fraction = 0.01, intensity = nothing, pointing = 0.05,\n                target_offset = 0.025, hardware_tolerance = 0.0, focus = 1.0,\n                margin = 0.0, tolerance = 0.0, pivot = :tcc, miss = :aim,\n                minimum = 0.0, weight = 100.0)\n\nKeep-out constraint for named hardware such as shields, stalks, diagnostic snouts and closed windows. hardware_clearance(keepout, beams) gives the worst-case clearance (mm) of each beam from parts, judged as clearance judges it with wings = []. parts is a name or a collection of part or component names, and a part stands for all its pieces. The beam envelope is the best-focus contour at peak_fraction of the peak, or at intensity (W/cm²), grown by the f-cone radius |s|/(2F) and margin. The clearance is the smallest signed distance from the surface of the parts to the envelope, transverse to the beam and negative inside it, less the transverse error radius pointing + target_offset + hardware_tolerance and focus/(2F) for the focus error.\n\nOnly the surface upstream of the landing of the beam counts. As in clearance, the landing is the plane normal to the beam where its nominal axis first meets one of targets, by default every opaque surface not among parts. A beam whose axis meets none of them is judged up to its aim plane, or along its whole length with miss = :all. A beam whose landing is on one of parts itself (when targets names it) gets Inf. The defaults are those of ApertureKeepOut. They are engineering defaults and are not set by a facility criterion; the 1% contour is the level of the TaLIS 3ω clearance rule.\n\ntolerance (degrees) is the rule tolerance of talis_check: the parts may sit rotated off nominal by up to that angle about pivot (:tcc, :aim the beam aim point, :max the farther of the two, or a point), which costs each point r·tan α, r its distance from the pivot, subtracted at each point inside the minimization (so the clearance stays continuous where the closest point jumps). With targets the capture components, miss = :all, tolerance = 0.5 and minimum = 0.5 (all other defaults equal) the keep-out is the TaLIS 3ω clearance rule of talis_check for each beam:\n\nHardwareKeepOut(t, hardware; targets = capture, tolerance = 0.5, miss = :all,\n                minimum = 0.5)\n\na surrogate checked against talis_check in this package (its :clearance_3w worst values; see the research notes), not against the facility tool. talis_check reports each hardware part on a separate row, where the keep-out takes the minimum over its parts (one keep-out per part gives the rows), and it passes a clearance strictly above 0.5 mm; on an leh component its failure is a review, which a keep-out does not distinguish.\n\nThe distance to the envelope ellipse is exact (the point–ellipse distance clearance uses), and the minimum over the parts is found, not sampled. On a triangle not crossing best focus the distance is convex (a convex function of the transverse position less a linear one of the axial), and it has no stationary point inside the triangle except on the medial strip, where it is not differentiable: the segment of the major axis between ±(A² − B²)/A (the axis itself for a round spot) swept along the beam, at least B²/A inside the envelope. So the minimum lies on the triangle edges, their crossings with the medial strip, or their cuts by the landing plane, each split at best focus: line segments on which the distance is convex. The triangles are grouped in spatially compact clusters of up to 16; clusters and segments are taken best first by a lower bound (the circumscribed circle and the homothetic ellipse through the center of a cluster or the midpoint of a segment, less the Lipschitz constant √(1 + 1/(2F)²) times its radius or half length), and each segment is minimized by a safeguarded root search on the derivative, until the bound reaches the smallest value found. The result is the exact minimum to about 1e-12 mm.\n\nThe tolerance term −r·tan α is concave in the point. With it the minimum still lies off the triangle interiors, because off the medial strip the Hessian of the distance has rank one, so in every plane the sum curves down along some direction. The term does spoil the convexity along a segment. Each segment is therefore minimized with the term replaced by its chord, a lower bound equal to it at the ends, and split at the minimizer of the chord until the two agree to 1e-14 mm; the bounds of clusters and segments subtract tan α (r + radius).\n\nThe clearance is continuous in the aim, defocus and spot size of the beams (a minimum over a fixed surface), and its derivatives are those of the distance at the closest point (Danskin's theorem); on the medial strip, where two points of the ellipse are equally near, they are the mixture of theirs that leaves the distance stationary at the minimum, as at the saddle of a minimax. They are one-sided only where two separate points of the parts are equally close (a kink of the minimum itself). The landing is found on the nominal beam: the clearance jumps where the landing of the axis moves from one component to another while the parts reach past the landing plane, and the cut by the landing plane does not move with the beam in the derivatives.\n\nThe keep-out departs from clearance in two ways, both conservative. Every point of the parts upstream of the landing counts, also where opaque surfaces hide it from the beam; clearance skips points the beam cannot reach, and talis_check judges 3ω clearance as this keep-out does. The clearance-only far-field wings are not modeled. Pass the keep-out as keepout to the objectives, by itself or in a tuple with other keep-outs; the objectives then add weight × Σᵢ max(0, minimum − cᵢ)² (keepout_penalty).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.HardwareKeepOut"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.IncidenceRule","page":"Shot review","text":"IncidenceRule(target, parts; peak_fraction = 0.1, intensity = nothing, pointing = 0.05,\n              target_offset = 0.025, hardware_tolerance = 0.0, focus = 1.0, margin = 0.0,\n              max_incidence = 70.0, sharpness = 100.0, level = 2, minimum = 0.0,\n              weight = 100.0)\n\nIncidence limit on the parts struck by beams. incidence_angle(rule, beams) gives the worst-case incidence (degrees) of each beam on the struck part, the first of parts that the nominal beam axis meets. The quantity is the TaLIS 3ω incidence rule of talis_check (:incidence_3w, at most max_incidence). As in talis_check, the part is cut into 4^level sub-triangles per mesh triangle, each with its normal, which is exact on sphere, cylinder, cone and torus surfaces. The incidence is the largest angle between the beam axis and the normal of a sub-triangle that is visible from upstream and within reach of the worst-case footprint, plus the f-cone half-angle atan(1/(2F)), at most 90°. The worst-case footprint is the envelope at peak_fraction of the peak, grown by the f-cone and margin and enlarged by the transverse error radius pointing + target_offset + hardware_tolerance and focus/(2F). The rule tolerance does not enter.\n\nThe set of sub-triangles within reach of the footprint changes as a beam moves, so the largest angle over it steps. The quantity smooths the step. Each sub-triangle f enters with its angle less sharpness × max(0, d_f) in degrees, where d_f (mm) is the clearance of the sub-triangle from the footprint. d_f is the exact minimum over the sub-triangle, found as in HardwareKeepOut. A sub-triangle thus counts in full once the footprint reaches it, and its weight fades over Δθ/sharpness mm outside it. The incidence is continuous in the aim, defocus and spot size of the beams, and it is never below the incidence of talis_check, to its resolution. It equals that incidence where no sub-triangle outside the footprint lies within Δθ/sharpness of it, with Δθ the amount by which the angle of that sub-triangle exceeds those of the reached sub-triangles. On a flat part the incidence is the tilt of the part to the beam plus the half-angle, independent of the pointing. sharpness (100°/mm) is an engineering default and is not set by a facility criterion. The other defaults are those of talis_check. A beam whose axis meets none of parts has no incidence (-Inf); CaptureKeepIn gives the capture rule that such a beam fails.\n\nAs a keep-out (keepout_clearance, keepout_penalty) the quantity of the rule is the margin max_incidence − incidence in degrees, held at or above minimum. The quantity is a surrogate checked against talis_check in this package (see the research notes), not against the facility tool.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.IncidenceRule"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.Omega1KeepOut","page":"Shot review","text":"Omega1KeepOut(target, parts; pointing = 0.05, target_offset = 0.025,\n              hardware_tolerance = 0.0, focus = 1.0, tolerance = 0.5, pivot = :tcc,\n              omega1_edge = 4.45, omega1_size = nothing, omega1_focus = nothing,\n              lens_distance = 7700.0, offsets = nothing, resolution = 1e-9,\n              minimum = 0.2, weight = 100.0)\n\nKeep-out of named parts from the 1ω footprints of the beams. omega1_clearance(keepout, beams) gives the worst-case clearance (mm) of parts from the 10% contour of the unconverted 1ω light of each beam, where parts is a name or a collection of part or component names. The quantity is the TaLIS 1ω clearance rule of talis_check (:clearance_1w, more than 0.2 mm). The footprint and its keywords are those of talis_check, described there under \"1ω footprint\". Per beamlet of a NIF quad, the footprint is a square whose near edge lies omega1_edge from the 3ω axis. It tapers to the 1ω focus omega1_focus beyond the 3ω focus and moves by ±focus with the 3ω focal plane. The clearance is the smallest transverse distance from the parts to the footprint, less the transverse error radius pointing + target_offset + hardware_tolerance and the rule tolerance r·tan(tolerance) about pivot at each point. Beams without a NIF quad name or an offsets entry have no 1ω footprint and get Inf.\n\nThe minimum over the parts is found by talis_check's branch and bound on the Lipschitz constant of the footprint distance, to resolution (mm), not by an exact search: the clearance is the smallest value found, within resolution of the minimum and continuous in the aim and defocus of the beams to that accuracy. Its derivatives are those of the footprint distance at that point (Danskin's theorem; one-sided at the corners of the footprint and where the footprints of two beamlets are equally near). Not clearing calls for unconverted-light management (:management_1w), a review outside the scope of this quantity. The defaults are talis_check's; the quantity is a surrogate checked against talis_check in this package (see the research notes), not against the facility tool.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Omega1KeepOut"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.capture_margin-Tuple{CaptureKeepIn, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"capture_margin(keepin, beams) -> Vector\n\nWorst-case capture margin (mm) of each beam on the part of a CaptureKeepIn struck by the beam, the rule tolerance included; continued past the outline of the part for a beam whose axis misses every part, Inf on a part without a free edge. The margin is differentiable in the aim, defocus and spot size of the beams and has their number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.capture_margin"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.clipped_fraction-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Shot review","text":"clipped_fraction(ev, names...) -> Real\n\nFraction of the delivered laser power striking the outside (back side) of the named parts or components (a part stands for all its pieces, see part_pieces); with no names, of every component. Beside power_fraction(ev, names...), the power absorbed on their elements, the sum\n\npower_fraction(ev, names...) + clipped_fraction(ev, names...)\n\nis all the laser light the parts receive, from either side: the quantity of a ceiling on hardware meant to stay dark, such as closed windows, shields and stalks (at most the value of an accepted design), or of a floor on capture by a part meant to be hit. Both are totals over the beams, in the number type of the evaluation (differentiable), and relative to the effective (post-LPI) power, as component_power reports the same powers in TW (name and Symbol(name, \"_outside\")). An open window is an aperture: light passes through it, so it receives none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.clipped_fraction"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.direct_fraction","page":"Shot review","text":"direct_fraction(ev) -> Real\ndirect_fraction(ev, names) -> Real\n\nFraction of the delivered laser power absorbed by the observed elements of the scenario (the capsule whose drive is measured), or with names (a collection of part or component names) by those parts instead. The direct penalty of symmetry_objective, asymmetry_objective and face_flux_objective weighs the square of this fraction, and the direct_names of those objectives corresponds to names. A name that is not in the target is an ArgumentError. The fraction is relative to the effective (post-LPI) power, as every deposition fraction is; see clipped_fraction and missed_fraction for the other two penalized fractions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.direct_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.hardware_clearance-Tuple{HardwareKeepOut, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"hardware_clearance(keepout, beams) -> Vector\n\nWorst-case clearance (mm) of each beam from the parts of a HardwareKeepOut, the rule tolerance included; Inf for a beam whose landing lies upstream of all of them or on one of them. The clearance is differentiable in the aim, defocus and spot size of the beams and has their number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.hardware_clearance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.incidence_angle-Tuple{IncidenceRule, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"incidence_angle(rule, beams) -> Vector\n\nWorst-case incidence (degrees) of each beam on the part of an IncidenceRule struck by the beam, the f-cone half-angle included; -Inf when no part is struck. The incidence is continuous in the aim, defocus and spot size of the beams and has their number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.incidence_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/review/#Briareus.omega1_clearance-Tuple{Omega1KeepOut, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Shot review","text":"omega1_clearance(keepout, beams) -> Vector\n\nWorst-case clearance (mm) of the parts of an Omega1KeepOut from the 1ω footprint of each beam, the rule tolerance included; Inf for a beam without a 1ω footprint. The clearance is differentiable in the aim and defocus of the beams and has their number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.omega1_clearance"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/optimization/#Optimization","page":"Optimization","text":"GroupPointing maps parameters to beams, per cone, quad or beam. The parameters are the axial, radial and tangential aim (in the port azimuth of the quad by default), the split of a quad about its aim point, defocus, power, spot scale and Δλ, with top–bottom mirroring. parameters builds its parameter vector from values named by group and field, and Scenario precomputes everything an evaluation needs. The objectives are:\n\nsymmetry_objective and asymmetry_objective, for capsule drive symmetry (axisymmetric and three-dimensional);\nface_flux_objective, for the uniformity of the x-ray drive on a flat observed face, such as a halfraum package or an opacity sample;\nirradiance_objective, for foil uniformity.\n\noptimize_pointing runs bounded L-BFGS-B on forward-mode automatic-differentiation gradients, with a callback for progress and cancellation. influence_matrix gives the mode response of each beam. HeldParameters holds some parameters of a parameterization at given values, and PowerScales adds a power scale per group of beams (for example per cone) beside its parameters; both compose with GroupPointing, FramePointing and each other.\n\nLimits that a design must meet go to optimize_pointing as Constraints, which an augmented Lagrangian around the same L-BFGS-B handles. check_constraints reports them on any evaluation. The PointingResult records the value of each constraint against its limit and what ended the run, and, for each parameter held by the box, the derivative of the optimum with respect to that bound. from = result continues a run with its multipliers and penalty, and time_limit bounds its wall time. On VisRadProfile with its hard cut, a run optimizes the profile tapered below the cut and reports the design under the hard cut (smooth_cut). Phase 1 (phase1, on by default) first searches for a design within the limits and ends with :infeasible when the search finds none; relaxation = true then reports how far each conflicting constraint must be relaxed by itself (Relaxation).\n\nThe ready-made constraints are:\n\nirradiance_ceiling, a ceiling on the laser irradiance over named parts. By default the exact value, peak_irradiance, is constrained; with exact = false only the smooth stand-in irradiance_norm is.\nmode_gate, a gate per degree on the mode_departure from a reference drive shape, which goes with drive_objective for the largest drive within the gates. Keywords of mode_gate set the gates at a fraction of the pointing floor, set physical gates with the floor as their lower bound, or shift the target shape from that of the reference by shift_modes.\ndrive_floor, a lower bound on the capsule drive as a fraction of that of a reference, and drive_ceiling, the matching upper bound, for a band.\nclearance_floor, a lower bound on the worst-case clearance of each beam from a keep-out at its minimum.\nlight_ceiling and light_floor, an upper and a lower bound on the laser light on named parts (light_fraction).\npicket_ceiling (Laser–plasma models), a dedicated ceiling on the irradiance of the outer cones in a phase such as the picket, trough or foot, the quantity of the published gold-bubble scalings.\n\nmode_sensitivity gives the exact sensitivity of the drive shape to the pointing and power errors of each beam. mode_uncertainty gives the asymmetry that independent errors leave by linear propagation (sample_modes is the Monte Carlo check), with its floor, the spread per degree in the units of mode_departure and the lower bound of a gate. Errors can be shared by a group of beams or be the positioning error of the target. The uncertainty keyword of the objectives optimizes the expected symmetry over them, and infer_errors finds the most probable errors behind a measured asymmetry.\n\npointing_summary reports a pointing against reference beams, such as a re-pointing result against its start. It lists the aim shifts, power and cone fraction, keep-out clearances, and the clipped and missed fractions, and, against the drive of a reference design, the drive ratio and the departure per degree. shift_objective, the mean squared aim shift from reference beams, gives with gates as constraints the least re-pointing that meets them.","title":"Optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/optimization/#Several-phases-under-one-pointing","page":"Optimization","text":"A PhasedScenario holds one pointing over several phases of a pulse, such as the picket, the foot, and the peak before and after windows burn through. Each Phase has a separate target, radiation model, options and beam powers, given per beam or from pulses at a time. The parameter vector (PhasedPointing) is that of the shared pointing, which sets the aims of every phase, followed by a power scale per (group, phase) for the phases not fixed. evaluate gives one evaluation per phase in a PerPhase, which optimize_pointing passes to the objective and to every constraint. in_phase applies any constraint or objective of one evaluation to one phase, with the phase in its reported name, while the clearance_floor of a keep-out takes every phase at once. The albedos and time of a phase come from a drive history with Phase(name, scenario; history, time), and a reference at the same time from remake(scenario; history, time).\n\nFurther tools for phased designs are:\n\nbounds(scenario.param; power_max) caps every beam in every phase at the limit of the laser, and pulse_ceiling constrains the pulses written for a design (scaled_pulse) to stay within it between the phase samples.\nphase_beams puts reference beams at the powers of each phase.\npower_scales returns the scales inside an objective (differentiable), power_scale_objective penalizes their departure from a reference drive (each scale weighted by the beams it governs), and power_scale_table tabulates them per key and phase.\nremake(scenario; deposition_kw) rebuilds the deposition operator of every phase, for example to check a finished design on a finer one.\npointing_summary reports each phase against its gates and pointing floor, with the departure per degree split into m = 0 and m ≠ 0 and the quads moved past given shifts.","title":"Several phases under one pointing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/optimization/#Pulse-shapes","page":"Optimization","text":"Each distinct pulse shape needs facility validation. pulse_shape_groups groups per-key power multipliers over the phases of a pulse (one per quad and phase, from power scales) into a given number of shapes. It runs k-means on the log multipliers per class (for example the inner and the outer cones) and splits the shapes between the classes by fit. shape_pulses and scaled_pulse write the shapes out as pulses for write_pulse_table, and the assignment of the grouping ties the power scales of a re-optimization per shape (PowerScales).","title":"Pulse shapes"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.ConstraintRow","page":"Optimization","text":"ConstraintRow\n\nOne component of a Constraint at a design, from check_constraints or in PointingResult.constraints. Fields:\n\nname — the name of the constraint, with the label of the component for a vector constraint, e.g. \"mode_gate[l = 2]\".\nvalue, limit — in the units of the constraint.\nupper — true for a ceiling value ≤ limit, false for a floor.\nviolation — the normalized excess ±(value - limit)/scale, positive when violated and negative inside.\nmultiplier — the Lagrange multiplier of the component at the design, in the normalized units. In the result of a constrained run, it is the nonnegative least-squares solution of the stationarity condition on the parameters inside the box (PointingResult). The fit covers the components within feasibility of their limits or beyond them, and the other components get zero. It is 0 when not computed, as in check_constraints.\nstatus — :violated (violation > feasibility), :active (within feasibility of the limit) or :satisfied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ConstraintRow"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.Evaluation","page":"Optimization","text":"Evaluation\n\nResult of evaluate: the setup beams, the effective beams after the laser–plasma model of the scenario, their deposition, and flux — incident x-ray flux on the observed elements of the scenario (TW/mm²; nothing without radiation). Deposition fractions are relative to effective (post-LPI) power. The flux is computed when first read; flux_moments and flux_ylm do not need it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Evaluation"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.GroupPointing","page":"Optimization","text":"GroupPointing(beams; by = :group, fields = (:z, :r, :defocus), mirror = true,\n              relative = false, unit = :quad, radius = nothing, center = (0, 0, 0))\n\nMaps a parameter vector to beams. Beams are grouped by by: :group (e.g. a cone), :quad, :beam, or a function beam -> key. Each group gets one value per field:\n\n:z — axial aim coordinate (mm). With mirror, beams from the lower hemisphere (port z < 0) aim at -z, so one value describes a top/bottom-symmetric cone.\n:r — radial aim offset (mm) toward the quad port azimuth. With unit = :beam (below), the offset is toward the beam port azimuth.\n:phi — tangential aim offset (mm), counterclockwise about z.\n:split — tangential split of the beams of a quad about its aim point (mm), along the quad φ̂. Each beam aim moves by ±split, with the sign of the beam port azimuth measured from the mean port of the quad; beams counterclockwise of it take +split. A positive split therefore opens the aims of a quad in the sense of its ports, and a negative one crosses them. The s of hybride_mock_pointing is split = -s. The field sets the tangential offset of each beam from the mean template aim of its quad, or shifts that offset with relative. A quad held as one beam is not split. The field needs unit = :quad and quad names.\n:defocus — focal-plane offset along the beam (mm, see Beam).\n:power — power per beam (TW).\n:spot — multiplier on the best-focus spot semi-axes.\n:dlambda — wavelength detuning (Å), used by laser–plasma models.\n\nFields not listed keep the template value of each beam.\n\nPointing on a sphere (polar direct drive). With radius = R (mm), the aim can instead be a point on the sphere of radius R about center. Direct-drive pointings are published on this target surface (Barlow et al., PRL 133, 175101, 2024, Table I; Yeamans et al., Nucl. Fusion 61, 046031, 2021, Table 1; Hohenberger et al., PoP 22, 056308, 2015, Table I). The spherical fields are:\n\n:theta_s — polar angle of the aim point (degrees from +z). With mirror, beams from the lower hemisphere aim at 180° - theta_s, so one value describes a top/bottom-symmetric cone pair, and a larger value moves both toward the equator.\n:dphi_s — azimuth of the aim point relative to the beam port azimuth, in degrees counterclockwise about z and the same for both hemispheres.\n:split_theta, :split_phi — split of the beams of a quad (degrees). Each beam aim moves by ±split_theta in polar angle and ±split_phi in azimuth, with the sign of the beam port offset from the mean port of the quad. Beams at a larger polar angle than the quad port take +split_theta, with the angle counted from the pole of their hemisphere when mirrored. Beams counterclockwise of the quad port take +split_phi. Positive values therefore open the aims of a quad in the sense of its ports. This is the split-quad pointing of Cok et al. (PoP 15, 082705, 2008) and Yeamans et al. 2021 (left/right Δφ) as angles on the sphere. A quad given as one beam, or a beam level with the mean port of its quad, is not split. The fields need quad names; other per-beam aims come from by, e.g. one group per sub-ring.\n\nThe aim is the surface point itself, aim = center + R * port_vector(θ, φ), which the axis meets first when it faces the port (Beam). Best focus is then at aim + defocus * k, so defocus is measured from the surface aim point, as VisRad measures the focus offset from its reference point (research notes, \"Pointing and focus conventions\"). The angles of each template beam are those of the first point where its axis meets the sphere; an ArgumentError is thrown if the axis misses. Listed spherical values are shared by the members of a group. For an angle not set by a listed field, the value of each beam is kept. The aim moves to the surface along the template axis, so the focus moves with it unless the template aim was already on the sphere. The spherical fields and the hohlraum fields :z, :r, :phi exclude each other.\n\nBy default (unit = :quad), the unit of pointing for the hohlraum fields is a quad. :r, :phi and :split are then measured in the azimuth of the quad port, which is the mean port of the beams that share its quad name, instead of the port of each beam. The beams of a quad that share their values then share one aim point, whether the table contains the quad as one beam or as four. A NIF pointing given per cone or per quad thus stays one aim per quad while the deposition is computed per beam. The beams of a quad are pointed apart about its aim point as follows:\n\np = GroupPointing(nif_beams(); by = :quad, fields = (:z, :r, :phi, :split))\nbeams = apply(p, parameters(p, \"Q31T\" => (z = 5.8, split = 0.5)))\n\nby = :beam gives each beam a separate aim, with its offsets still in the quad azimuth. With relative = true (below), the differences between the beams of a per-beam table are kept under one shift per quad. An as-shot SST export, read_visrad_sst(path; template = nif_beams(quads)), is such a table. unit = :beam measures :r and :phi of each beam in the beam port azimuth instead. The four beams of a quad with the same r then aim r sin Δφ apart, where Δφ is the azimuth of a beam from that of its quad, up to 5.7° on the NIF 23.5° cone. A quad held as one beam, and a beam without a quad name, give identical results either way.\n\nrelative = true makes the aim fields (:z, :r, :phi, :split, or the spherical ones) shifts added to the template aim of each beam, instead of values shared by the beams of a group. A group then moves rigidly, and the differences between its members are kept. This re-points an as-shot table, whose beams within a quad aim at different points (the per-beam offsets of a facility), by one shift per quad. The initial parameters are then zero; the other fields stay absolute.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.GroupPointing"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.PointingResult","page":"Optimization","text":"PointingResult\n\nResult of optimize_pointing. Fields:\n\nx — the best parameters.\nbeams — apply(scenario.param, x), a PerPhase of beam vectors for a phased scenario.\nvalue, initial_value — the objective at x and at the clamped x0.\niterations — the L-BFGS-B iterations completed.\nevaluations — the objective evaluations, each gradient counting as one.\nreason — :converged, :iterations, :evaluations, :time_limit or :callback.\nstopped — true for the last three reasons, which return the best design evaluated before the stop.\nresult — the result of Optim, nothing when stopped.\nnames — the parameter_names of the parameterization, or x[i] for one without a method.\nlower, upper — the box.\nat_bounds — the names of the parameters at a bound, within 1e-8 of the box width. Parameters fixed by lower == upper are excluded.\n\nThe display lists the parameters at a bound with their values and bounds.\n\nWhat ended the run is recorded in two more fields. constraints holds one ConstraintRow per constraint component at x, and it is empty without constraints. The display shows a constraint of more than eight components, such as a keep-out per beam, as its worst row and a count by status. outcome is one of:\n\n:interior: the run converged with every constraint met and no parameter at a bound;\n:box: the run converged with every constraint met and parameters at a bound. The box is active, and a wider box may lower the objective;\n:infeasible_box: a constraint is still violated and parameters are at a bound, so the constraint cannot be met within the box;\n:infeasible: a constraint is still violated and no parameter is at a bound. The design is a local point where the constraints cannot be met, and another start may help;\n:iterations, :evaluations, :time_limit or :callback: the budget, the time limit or the callback ended the run first. Its constraints, if any, are in constraints.\n\nreason is :converged for the first four. outer is the number of outer (multiplier) iterations of a constrained run, and 0 without constraints. iterations and evaluations are then totals over them, and value and initial_value are the values of the objective without the constraint terms.\n\nstate is what optimize_pointing(...; from = result) continues from. It is nothing without constraints. For a constrained run, it is a named tuple of:\n\nthe multipliers λ, in normalized units, one per constraint component in the order of constraints;\nthe penalty parameter μ and its ceiling μmax;\nthe tolerance tol of the inner runs;\nthe last infeasibility–complementarity measure Vold;\nthe working limits limits, which contain the lowered stand-in limit of an exact ceiling (see irradiance_ceiling);\nthe iterate x they belong to. It is the x of the result unless the run returned its iterate of least violation (below).\n\nFor a run ended by its budget, time limit or callback, the state of the interrupted inner problem is kept, so that the restart resumes it.\n\nMultipliers. The multipliers in constraints are those that best satisfy stationarity at x, which differ from the iterated state.λ. They are the nonnegative least-squares solution of ∂f/∂xᵢ + Σⱼ λⱼ ∂gⱼ/∂xᵢ = 0 over the parameters inside the box. Here gⱼ are the normalized violations, with the stand-in of an exact ceiling at its working limit. The fit covers the components within feasibility of their limits or beyond them, and the other components get zero (complementarity). The iterated estimate λ ← max(0, λ + μ g) after each inner run is exact only when the inner run ended at a stationary point. The inner runs of a drive objective end on their value tolerance instead. At a large penalty μ, the update then zeroes the multipliers of active limits at which the design ends a few 1e-5 inside (research notes, \"Multipliers from the converged active set\").\n\nThe fit costs one Jacobian of the objective and the constraints after the run, the cost of a gradient, which is not counted in evaluations. Several sets of multipliers can fit equally, as for components with the same gradient, such as the clearances of beams related by the port symmetry. The fit then returns the one of least norm. stationarity is the residual of the fit: the largest |∂L/∂xᵢ| over the parameters inside the box, with L = f + Σⱼ λⱼ gⱼ. It is in objective units per parameter unit, the measure that the inner runs compare with g_tol, and it is NaN without constraints or when phase 1 ended the run. Near zero, the design is a Karush–Kuhn–Tucker point with these multipliers. A drive run whose inner runs ended on their value tolerance can end with a stationarity that is a sizable fraction of the objective gradient. The multipliers and bound sensitivities are then estimates at a design short of stationarity. On the case of the dropped-bundle tutorial, the stationarity is 1.2e-3, against 3.0e-3 per mm for the largest derivative of the drive objective.\n\nAn exact ceiling, such as irradiance_ceiling or picket_ceiling with exact = true, reports the exact value in its row. It is included in the fit through its smooth stand-in at the working limit. Its row can therefore show :satisfied, with the exact value inside the limit by more than feasibility, together with a positive multiplier from the active stand-in. The display notes such rows; the multiplier is that of the stand-in.\n\nBound sensitivities. For each parameter in at_bounds, in the same order, bound_gradient contains the derivative ∂f/∂xᵢ of the objective at x. bound_sensitivity contains that of the Lagrangian, ∂L/∂xᵢ with L = f + Σⱼ λⱼ gⱼ, using the multipliers of constraints above. Without constraints the two are equal. By the envelope theorem, bound_sensitivity is the derivative of the optimal objective with respect to the bound value, with the active set held. When the run has converged, it is positive at a lower bound and negative at an upper bound. Widening the box by δ in that parameter, by lowering a lower bound or raising an upper one, then lowers the optimum by about |bound_sensitivity| δ, to first order. The unit is objective units per parameter unit. bound_gradient is the gain of the objective without the constraint terms, before the constraints take their share.\n\nWithout constraints, the sensitivities cost one gradient after the run. With constraints, they come from the Jacobian of the multiplier fit. Neither cost is counted in evaluations. The sensitivities are meaningful at convergence. The active constraints and bounds can leave a flat family of designs, as for a drive objective under several active gates, and the run can end short of stationarity when its inner runs end on their value tolerance. In both cases the derivative can differ from the gain of a wider box by its whole size. A sign that would move the parameter inward marks a bound that does not hold it (research notes, \"Multipliers from the converged active set\"). A restart with from in the wider box measures the gain.\n\nVisRad's hard cut. When optimize_pointing optimized a scenario with the hard cut on a profile tapered below its cut (smooth_cut, its default), tapered_value is the objective that the tapered run reached. value, initial_value, constraints and outcome are then those of the scenario under its hard cut. Otherwise tapered_value is NaN.\n\nPhase 1 and relaxations (phase1 = true, relaxation = true). phase1 is nothing when phase 1 did not run. Otherwise it is a named tuple of:\n\nevaluations and iterations, which are included in the totals;\nfeasible, true when phase 1 reached a design within the limits, where the main run started;\nverdict, true when phase 1 converged or stalled above feasibility. The run then ended at that design, with outer 0;\nits design x;\nreason, the cause of the end of phase 1. It is :feasible, :converged or :stalled for a verdict or a design within the limits. Otherwise it is the stop that came first, :evaluations, :time_limit or :callback. No verdict is reached on such a stop, and the run ends with its design of least violation.\n\nWith relaxation = true, relaxations contains one Relaxation per constraint violated or active at the design of a verdict; otherwise it is empty. Their evaluations count in evaluations. The display lists them under the constraints.\n\nLeast violation and the last design within the limits. These fields apply to constrained runs and are nothing without constraints. The run records the largest normalized violation of each iterate over the reported rows, max(0, maxⱼ violation) of ConstraintRow. For an exact ceiling, the row contains the exact value against its limit, and the violation is zero when the constraint is met for every component. The record is taken at the start, at the design of phase 1 and after every inner run. least_violation is the iterate of least violation, with violations within feasibility counted as equal and the lower objective then preferred. last_feasible is the latest iterate with every violation within feasibility, or nothing when none had. Each is a named tuple (; x, violation, value, outer) that contains the parameters, the largest violation, the objective and the outer iteration of that iterate. The outer iteration is 0 for the start and for the design of phase 1.\n\nA run can end with a violation above feasibility: with outcome :infeasible or :infeasible_box, or after a budget, time-limit or callback stop above the limits. It then returns least_violation as x, with its beams, value and rows. The last iterate is not returned, since it can be worse than the start on the limits that the start met. state.x is then the last iterate, to which the multipliers of state belong and from which a restart with from continues. When an earlier iterate met the limits, the limits are met at the returned design, and a run that ended at the penalty ceiling has reason and outcome :iterations. Under VisRad's hard cut (tapered_value finite), the records are those of the tapered run.\n\ncapped counts the outer iterations whose inner run ended on its cap (iterations or inner_evaluations) instead of converging. After each such run that ended above the limits, the multipliers were updated, and the penalty, the inner tolerance and the reference of the penalty test were held (optimize_pointing). The count is shown in the display.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PointingResult"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.Relaxation","page":"Optimization","text":"Relaxation\n\nHow far a single constraint must be relaxed for the others to be met. A relaxation is an entry of PointingResult.relaxations after a run that optimize_pointing found infeasible with relaxation = true. Fields:\n\nname — the name of the constraint.\nviolation — the largest normalized violation of its components at a design where every other constraint is met. This is the relaxation in units of its scale; where the scale is the limit (the default), the relaxed limit is 1 + violation times the limit.\nlimit, relaxed_limit — one per component, in the units of the constraint. The relaxed limit is a ceiling raised, or a floor lowered, by violation × scale.\nupper — true for a ceiling.\nstatus — :relaxed, :others_conflict or :stopped (below).\nx — that design.\nconstraints — the ConstraintRow of every constraint at x.\nevaluations — the objective evaluations of the search.\n\nstatus is :relaxed when the others are met at x. It is :others_conflict when the other constraints cannot all be met either, so relaxing only this one does not suffice; violation is then NaN. It is :stopped when the budget, the time limit or the callback ended the search first; violation is then that of the best design, and the others are possibly unmet.\n\nThe relaxation is achievable, since the others are met at the design x with this one at relaxed_limit. It is the least one only locally. The run that finds it minimizes a smooth stand-in for the largest violation, a 64-norm of the positive violations, from the infeasible design, which is a local search.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Relaxation"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.Scenario","page":"Optimization","text":"Scenario(target, pointing::GroupPointing; observe = nothing,\n         radiation = observe !== nothing, time = 0.0, albedo = nothing, lpi = nothing,\n         deposition_kw = (;), viewfactor_kw = (;), model = nothing)\nScenario(target, beams; kwargs...)\nScenario(target, parameterization; kwargs...)\n\nA scenario contains everything precomputed for fast repeated evaluation of a pointing design. Its parameterization (scenario.param) maps the parameter vectors of evaluate and optimize_pointing to beams. The three forms differ in the parameterization:\n\nThe first form builds the deposition operator for the template beams of pointing, which may also be a FramePointing.\nThe second form is Scenario(target, GroupPointing(beams)), with fields :z, :r and :defocus per group.\nThe third form takes any other parameterization with methods of apply(parameterization, x) and initial_parameters(parameterization), and builds the operator for the beams at its initial parameters.\n\nThe operator resolves the beam footprints where those beams aim (reach, DepositionOperator). Besides the DepositionOperator, the scenario contains the view factors and the linear flux response of the observed elements when radiation and observe are given. observe names the components whose incident x-ray flux is the figure of merit, e.g. :capsule. lpi is a laser–plasma model (Backscatter, CalibratedTransfer, CBET, or a tuple of them), applied at time between the setup beams and deposition.\n\nThe flux response is linear in the laser source at fixed albedos. By default these are the albedos of the materials at time, with temperature-dependent ones at their reference temperatures and thin foils intact. An explicit per-element albedo replaces them, such as the converged sol.albedo of a solution at the design point (e.g. the peak of a drive history, with windows burned through). For a time partway through a shaped pulse (a phase), the material albedos at time differ from those of the wall. The albedo of a WallLossAlbedo depends on the temperature history of each element, and neither the reference temperature nor a standalone solve at time includes that history. Pass the history albedos as st.time, st.albedo, with st = history_state(target, history, τ) (history_state).\n\nA scenario with a response (observe and a model) warns at construction when it is built without albedo on a target whose materials include a WallLossAlbedo, or a BurnthroughAlbedo whose before is one. The loss is then taken at the reference temperature (250 eV by default). Mid-pulse, this overstated the drive against a drive history by up to 1.64 times on a public mock-up (research notes, \"Drive histories: sliver steps and the albedo of a phase\"). albedo = :reference marks the reference temperature as intended and builds the same scenario without the warning.\n\nObjectives need only the moments of the observed flux, so the response is stored contracted with the Legendre (lmax = 12) and real-harmonic (lmax = 6) weights. Each evaluation then costs a 13 × n and a 49 × n product, and the setup costs a 62-column solve. The full observed × n response scenario.response, and with it the flux of an evaluation, is computed when first read. On a large (iterative) model, the flux of an evaluation is one solve instead, until the response has been read.\n\nOptions of the operators pass through two named tuples:\n\ndeposition_kw is passed to DepositionOperator. Its options are:\nprofile, a VisRadProfile that selects the VisRad beam model instead of the f-cone superposition;\nncone, the number of f-cone directions (13);\nreach, the aim motion in mm over which the operator stays accurate (2.0);\nresolution, level, maxlevel and shadow, the quadrature refinement.\nviewfactor_kw is passed to RadiationModel and through it to view_factors. Its options are:\nclosed, the closure correction, by default the declaration of the target that hohlraum makes;\nF, given view factors, e.g. those of a target that differs only in its materials;\nstorage, one of Float64, Float32 or :hmatrix;\nsolver, visibility (:centroid or :sampled), visibility_grid, level and near.\n\nmodel, a RadiationModel of the target, is used instead of building one. It excludes viewfactor_kw, and giving both throws an ArgumentError.\n\nremake(scenario; ...) replaces the laser–plasma model, the parameterization, the deposition options or the observed elements, time and albedos of the response, and rebuilds only what depends on them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Scenario"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.apply-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{GroupPointing, AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Optimization","text":"apply(p::GroupPointing, x) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nBeams for parameter vector x (element type follows x, so this is differentiable).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.apply"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.asymmetry_objective-Tuple{}","page":"Optimization","text":"asymmetry_objective(; modes = (1 => 1.0, 2 => 1.0, 3 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0), components = (),\n                    clip = 10.0, missed = 10.0, direct = 10.0, direct_names = nothing,\n                    drive = nothing, drive_weight = 1.0, keepout = nothing,\n                    reference = nothing, uncertainty = nothing)\n\nObjective for three-dimensional drive symmetry. For each l => w in modes it adds w × relative_mode_power(c, l) — the rotation-invariant power of all 2l+1 real harmonics of degree l over the mean ((aₗ/a₀)²/(2l+1) for an axisymmetric field) — and for each (l, m) => w in components adds w × (c_lm/c_00)², so a particular azimuthal mode (e.g. the l = 1, m = ±1 drift of a dropped bundle) can be weighted separately. Penalties for clipping, missing and direct capsule illumination and the drive target and keepout are as in symmetry_objective, with drive compared to the mean flux c_00/√(4π).\n\nWith reference (a coefficient vector from flux_ylm, e.g. of a tuned design before a change to the target or the beams), the modes and components measure the departure of c_lm/c_00 from c_lm/c_00 of the reference instead of from zero, so the objective restores a known-good drive shape.\n\nWith uncertainty = (; pointing, power) the mode and component terms are their expected values over independent beam errors of those rms sizes (linearized; see mode_uncertainty and symmetry_objective): a robust design. Shared errors (group_pointing, group_power, groups, positioning) are accepted as in mode_uncertainty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.asymmetry_objective"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.bounds","page":"Optimization","text":"bounds(p::GroupPointing, x0; z = 1.0, r = 1.0, phi = 1.0, split = 1.0, defocus = 2.0,\n       power = 0.5, spot = 0.5, dlambda = 2.0, theta_s = 5.0, dphi_s = 10.0,\n       split_theta = 5.0, split_phi = 5.0, power_min = 0.0, power_max = Inf)\n       -> (lower, upper)\n\nBox bounds around x0: ±z, ±r, ±phi, ±split, ±defocus mm, relative ±power, ±spot fractions, ±dlambda Å, and ±theta_s, ±dphi_s, ±split_theta, ±split_phi degrees (engineering defaults that are not facility limits). Power bounds are further clipped to the absolute limits power_min ≤ P ≤ power_max (TW per beam, e.g. the per-quad capability of a facility). A group at zero power in x0 (a dropped quad) gets ±power of the mean of the groups' nonzero powers, [0, power · P̄] before the clipping, so that it can come back; with every group at zero the upper bound is power_max, which must then be finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.clipped_fraction-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"Fraction of delivered laser power striking the outside (back side) of any component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.clipped_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.evaluate-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Optimization","text":"evaluate(scenario, x) / evaluate(scenario, beams) -> Evaluation\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.evaluate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.face_flux-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"face_flux(ev) -> Vector\n\nIncident x-ray flux H_e (TW/mm²) on each observed element of the scenario: the full linear flux response (scenario.response, computed when first read) times the absorbed laser power, generic in the number type (the duals of the optimizer). On a dense model, the same numbers as the flux of the evaluation; on a large (iterative) model it builds the response (a solve per observed element) instead of one iterative solve, so meant for an observed face of moderate size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.face_flux"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.face_flux_objective-Tuple{}","page":"Optimization","text":"face_flux_objective(; uniformity = 1.0, rms_scale = nothing, flux = nothing,\n                    flux_weight = 1.0, clip = 10.0, missed = 10.0, direct = 10.0,\n                    direct_names = nothing, keepout = nothing)\n\nObjective for the x-ray drive of a flat observed face, such as a package face in a halfraum or an opacity sample, which has no capsule modes. The shape term is uniformity × (σ/σ₀)². Here σ = rms_nonuniformity(H, A) is the area-weighted RMS nonuniformity of the incident flux on the observed elements of the scenario (face_nonuniformity). It is computed through the linear flux response of the scenario, so it is differentiable, and it is the shape term of FaceFlux for a uniform target. σ₀ = rms_scale, and nothing sets it to 1, which gives the absolute σ². With flux (TW/mm²) given, the objective adds flux_weight × (H̄/flux - 1)², with H̄ the face_mean_flux. The clip, missed, direct and keepout penalties are as in symmetry_objective. Here direct counts laser light on the face itself, or with direct_names on the named parts or components.\n\nrms_scale = face_nonuniformity(evaluate(scenario, x0)) makes the term equal to uniformity at the design x0, so uniformity = 1 balances the shape against the penalties on any face. Without it, the term is the absolute σ², which is as small as the face is uniform. On a uniform 1 mm sample, σ ~ 1e-4 and σ² ~ 1e-8, so the penalties (10 × a squared light fraction, ~1e-2) set the optimum. The modes of a capsule, by contrast, have (Pₗ/P₀)² ~ 1e-2 before tuning, the same size as the penalties. The scale is a number fixed at construction, so the objective remains a function of only the beams, thread-safe and differentiable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.face_flux_objective"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.face_mean_flux-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"face_mean_flux(ev) -> Real\n\nArea mean H̄ = Σ A_e H_e / Σ A_e (TW/mm²) of the incident x-ray flux on the observed elements of the scenario (face_flux): the level of FaceFlux and the level that the flux keyword of face_flux_objective holds, so With flux = face_mean_flux(evaluate(scenario, x0)), the drive of the design x0 is held.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.face_mean_flux"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.face_nonuniformity-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"face_nonuniformity(ev) -> Real\n\nArea-weighted RMS nonuniformity σ = rms_nonuniformity(H, A) (relative to the mean) of the incident x-ray flux on the observed elements of the scenario (face_flux): the shape term of face_flux_objective, and the scale of its rms_scale keyword, so rms_scale = face_nonuniformity(evaluate(scenario, x0)) weighs the nonuniformity at the start as 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.face_nonuniformity"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.flux_moments-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"flux_moments(ev; lmax = 8, axis = ẑ, center = mode_center(target, observed)) -> Vector\n\nLegendre moments aₗ of the observed incident flux (index l+1), about the center of the observed capsule by default (mode_center).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.flux_moments"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.flux_ylm-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"flux_ylm(ev; lmax = 6) -> Vector\n\nReal spherical-harmonic coefficients of the observed incident flux in the flat mode_index order, from the cached ylm_weights of the scenario. The polar axis is ẑ, and the azimuth is measured from +x about the center of the observed capsule (mode_center, which is TCC unless the capsule is offset). Use ylm_matrix(c, lmax) for the C[l+1, lmax+1+m] layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.flux_ylm"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.influence_matrix-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Optimization","text":"influence_matrix(scenario, beams; lmax = 6) -> Matrix\n\nMode response of the observed flux to each beam: column b is the real-harmonic coefficient vector (mode_index order) produced by beam b by itself at 1 TW, with the pointing, spot and focus of the beam. Without a laser–plasma model the flux is linear in beam powers, so the coefficients of any power assignment P are M * P — the basis of power-rebalancing solves and of tables of what losing each beam costs. beams are the effective beams (after apply_lpi); a laser–plasma model that deposits light itself (the ridge stop of BubbleAbsorption, linear in the power of each beam) is included through lpi_deposit at the scenario time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.influence_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.initial_parameters-Tuple{GroupPointing}","page":"Optimization","text":"initial_parameters(p::GroupPointing) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nParameters reproducing the template beams (group means where members differ; zero shifts for the aim fields of a relative parameterization). Spherical fields take the angles where each template axis first meets the sphere (see GroupPointing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.initial_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.irradiance_objective-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Optimization","text":"irradiance_objective(name; center, radius, intensity = nothing, uniformity = 1.0,\n                     spill = 1.0, intensity_weight = 1.0, side = 1)\n\nObjective for a laser-irradiated foil such as a backlighter. The region is the set of elements of component name within radius of center, on side side (1 front, -1 back). The objective is the RMS nonuniformity of the absorbed laser intensity over the region, plus spill × the squared fraction of delivered power landing outside it. With intensity (TW/mm²) given, the objective adds intensity_weight × (mean/intensity - 1)².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.irradiance_objective"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.mean_flux-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"mean_flux(ev) -> Real\n\nMean incident x-ray flux on the observed elements (TW/mm²): the solid-angle mean seen from the mode center, flux_moments(ev)[1]. It is the drive level that the drive keyword of symmetry_objective and asymmetry_objective holds, so With drive = mean_flux(evaluate(scenario, x0)), the drive of the starting design is held.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mean_flux"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.missed_fraction-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"Fraction of delivered power that strikes no surface at all.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.missed_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.nparams-Tuple{GroupPointing}","page":"Optimization","text":"Number of parameters of a GroupPointing (groups × fields).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nparams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.optimize_pointing-Tuple{Briareus._AbstractScenario, Any}","page":"Optimization","text":"optimize_pointing(scenario, objective; from = nothing,\n                  x0 = initial_parameters(scenario.param),\n                  lower = bounds(scenario.param, x0)[1], upper = bounds(scenario.param, x0)[2],\n                  iterations = 200, evaluations = 20 * iterations, show_trace = false,\n                  g_tol = 1e-8, callback = nothing, constraints = (),\n                  feasibility = 1e-3, outer = 30, time_limit = Inf, phase1 = true,\n                  relaxation = false, inner_reltol = 1e-10,\n                  inner_evaluations = nothing, smooth_cut = true) -> PointingResult\n\nMinimize objective(evaluate(scenario, x)) within the box lower ≤ x ≤ upper by L-BFGS-B with forward-mode automatic-differentiation gradients, from x0 (clamped to the box). Returns a PointingResult, whose display lists the parameters that end at a bound.\n\nThe default box is bounds(scenario.param, x0) with its default widths. These are aims within ±1 mm of x0 (z, r, phi), defocus ±2 mm, power and spot scale ±50%, Δλ ±2 Å and the spherical angles ±5–10°. They are engineering defaults and are not facility limits. A parameter at its bound usually means that the box set its value and the objective without the box would have moved it further. To widen the box, pass keywords of bounds, e.g. lower, upper = bounds(pointing, x0; z = 1.5, power = 0.4, power_max = 12.0), or pass any lower and upper vectors. A parameterization other than GroupPointing and FramePointing has no bounds method and needs lower and upper.\n\nscenario may be a PhasedScenario, which contains one pointing over several phases of a pulse with power scales per (group, phase). The objective and every constraint then receive the PerPhase of the evaluations of the phases at each parameter vector, evaluated once. The beams of the result are a PerPhase of beam vectors. Everything else is the same, including constraints, from, phase1, relaxation and the time limit.\n\nThe run ends after iterations L-BFGS-B iterations or evaluations objective evaluations, whichever comes first; each gradient counts as one evaluation. A line search may take many evaluations in one iteration, and dozens on a kinked objective such as keep-out hinges or a coarse mesh, so the iteration limit alone does not bound the run time. time_limit, in seconds of wall time (default none), ends the run at the first evaluation after it has passed. reason is one of:\n\n:converged: the projected gradient fell below g_tol, or the run made no further progress;\n:iterations, :evaluations, :time_limit or :callback: that limit or the callback ended the run.\n\nOn :evaluations, :time_limit and :callback, the result is the best design evaluated before the stop, with stopped = true and result = nothing. iterations is the number of iterations completed in every case.\n\ncallback, if given, is called after every (non-derivative) objective evaluation with (; evaluations, iteration, value, best_value, best_x); returning true stops the optimization. Use it for progress reports and cancellation.\n\nConstraints. constraints is a tuple or vector of Constraints: ceilings f(ev) ≤ limit and floors f(ev) ≥ limit, such as irradiance_ceiling and mode_gate. They are handled as constraints by an augmented Lagrangian; they are not penalty terms of the objective. Its inner problems are box-constrained L-BFGS-B runs on objective + Σ μ/2 [max(0, gᵢ + λᵢ/μ)² - (λᵢ/μ)²], where gᵢ are the normalized violations ±(value - limit)/scale. Each inner run starts from the last, and the multipliers λ and the penalty μ are updated between them by the rules of ALGENCAN (Andreani et al., SIAM J. Optim. 18, 1286, 2008).\n\nThe run converges when three conditions hold:\n\nevery violation is at most feasibility, in the scaled units of the constraints (1e-3, or 0.1% of a limit, by default);\nthe multipliers are complementary to the same tolerance;\nthe last inner run has converged at the final tolerance.\n\nThe tolerance of the inner runs on the projected gradient tightens tenfold per outer iteration, from 1e-3 to g_tol. The run ends only once an inner run at g_tol has converged. An inner run converges when its projected gradient falls below the tolerance, or when its value changes by less than inner_reltol relative per iteration, since a drive objective is flat along the limits. A start already within the limits, as on a restart or a hand-off to a finer parameterization, is therefore still optimized. A constraint that cannot be met ends the run at the verdict of phase 1 (below). With phase1 = false, it ends the run once the penalty has grown by 10⁸ without progress.\n\nouter caps the outer iterations, iterations caps each inner run, and evaluations is the budget of the whole run. The iterations and evaluations of the result count over all inner runs. The multipliers change only between inner runs, so the cap sets the number of iterations spent on each set of multipliers. An inner run ended by its cap has not solved its subproblem, and the penalty test (has the infeasibility–complementarity measure halved?) requires a solved one. After a capped run that ends above the limits, the multipliers are updated, while the penalty, the inner tolerance and the reference of the test are held. A cap therefore never grows the penalty toward the infeasible verdict. The usual test is applied to a capped run that ends within the limits. capped in the result counts both kinds (research notes, \"Least violation, capped inner runs and phase 1's budget\"). A run that ends above its limits, infeasible or stopped by a budget, the time limit or the callback, returns its iterate of least violation instead of its last (the least_violation and last_feasible fields of PointingResult).\n\nThe public cases of up to about a hundred parameters converge with a large cap in about a thousand evaluations; the dropped-bundle tutorial passes 1,000. Small early caps were measured as an alternative: they saved evaluations on some of these cases and cost drive on others. A problem with hundreds of parameters, whose evaluations take seconds and whose start is far from the limits, gains from a cap of about 100, which updates the multipliers sooner. A 276-parameter study completed one outer iteration in an hour with iterations = 1000, and six in 40 minutes with 100 (research notes, \"The inner budget\").\n\nThe cap on iterations does not bound the evaluations of an inner run, which line searches consume. An inner run can spend hundreds of them on 1e-4 of the objective while limits stay violated. inner_evaluations (default none) caps the evaluations of each inner run. An inner run that reaches it ends at its best design, as one ended by iterations does, and the outer loop updates the multipliers and the penalty from there. Phase 1 is not capped; the runs of the relaxations are. A capped inner run never counts as converged, so the run still ends only after an inner run converges within the cap at the final tolerance, and a small cap needs a larger outer. A coarse version of the run within limits in the dropped-bundle tutorial has 92 parameters, and its default run spent 795 of its 1,044 evaluations in one inner run. With a cap of 100, it converged in 10 outer iterations and 766 evaluations at 0.06% less drive. With a cap of 30, it ended at outer = 30 unconverged (research notes, \"Inner-run options, a wall-loss scenario warning and laser delivery figures\").\n\ninner_reltol (default 1e-10; 0 turns it off) is the relative value tolerance of the inner runs above, also used by phase 1. The default ends the inner runs of a drive objective where the objective is flat along the limits, short of stationarity; the distance is recorded in the stationarity of the result. A tighter tolerance costs evaluations. On the run within limits in the dropped-bundle tutorial, a tolerance of 1e-12 gives a stationarity of 1.9e-5 against 1.2e-3 for 2.1 times the evaluations, with the drive the same within the 1e-4 spread of the flat family (research notes, \"Multipliers from the converged active set\"). When the multipliers or bound sensitivities of a result matter, continue it tighter through from with a smaller inner_reltol.\n\nThe constraints and outcome of the result report each constraint against its limit, with the multipliers that best satisfy stationarity at the final design. They also report whether the box, the constraints or the budget ended the run (PointingResult). The named tuple of the callback then also contains outer, and its value is that of the inner problem, the objective with the constraint terms. Without constraints, the run is the plain L-BFGS-B above.\n\nInfeasible constraints. With phase1 = true (the default), a start that violates a constraint first goes through phase 1. Phase 1 minimizes the infeasibility ½ Σ max(0, gᵢ)² over the normalized violation of every component, without the objective, by the same L-BFGS-B. This infeasibility is the limit of the augmented Lagrangian as its penalty grows. Phase 1 stops at the first design where every violation is within feasibility, and the main run starts at that design. Phase 1 can instead converge or stall with a violation above feasibility; it stalls when its least infeasibility falls by less than 1% over 100 evaluations, an engineering default. The run then ends there with outcome :infeasible, or :infeasible_box when parameters are at a bound, and no outer iteration. That design is the least infeasible one the search reached from this start, a local verdict, and its constraints rows show the violation of each constraint at that design.\n\nrelaxation = true implies phase1. After such a verdict, it adds one constrained run per constraint violated or active at that design. Each run computes the smallest largest violation of the components of its constraint with every other constraint met, which is how far that one constraint must be relaxed (Relaxation, in PointingResult.relaxations). These runs share the budget and the time limit.\n\nphase1 is on by default and relaxation off. A run whose start violates a constraint starts its main run from the design of phase 1, and it can end at another design of a flat family than a run started at x0. phase1 = false starts the main run at x0. A start at which every constraint is met, and a restart with from, skip phase 1. The outer of the callback is 0 during phase 1. Phase 1's L-BFGS-B run is not capped by iterations, which caps the subproblems of the augmented Lagrangian. It ends on its convergence, its stall rule, the evaluations of the run, the time limit or the callback. A stop before a verdict is named in the result (phase1.reason) and its display.\n\nOn the dropped-bundle test case made infeasible, phase 1 reached the verdict in 577 evaluations, where the augmented Lagrangian had none after 2,195. On feasible starts, phase 1 cost 8% fewer to 29% more evaluations (research notes, \"A verdict on conflicting constraints\"). On the constrained cases of the test suite that start outside their limits, it cost −23% to +54%, with drive designs 3e-4 apart on a flat family (research notes, \"Defaults changed (2026-11-19)\").\n\nVisRad's hard cut. A scenario can deposit with a VisRadProfile at its hard cut (taper = 0, its default). Every deposition-derived quantity then jumps where an operator point crosses the cut, and line searches stall on the jumps. With smooth_cut = true (the default), the run optimizes the same scenario with the profile tapered over one e-fold below the cut (taper = 1). The points and screens of the operator and the responses of the scenario are shared, and nothing is rebuilt. The run then reports the design under the hard cut of the scenario. The value, initial_value, constraints and outcome of the result are those of the hard cut at the design and start of the run, and tapered_value contains the objective the tapered run reached.\n\nThe taper moves wall elements by at most 7e-5 of the wall peak on NIF hohlraums, well inside the default feasibility of 1e-3. A design within its limits under the taper is therefore normally within them under the cut. Where it is not, its row shows :violated and outcome is :infeasible. The multipliers (fitted under the taper), stationarity, bound sensitivities, phase 1, relaxations and state are those of the tapered run, and a restart with from continues it. smooth_cut = false optimizes the scenario profile as it is. Other scenarios, such as the f-cone model or a profile already tapered, are optimized as they are. Each phase of a PhasedScenario is tapered separately.\n\nRestarts. from, the PointingResult of a previous run, continues that run. x0, lower and upper default to its last iterate and box. The last iterate is state.x of a constrained run, and x otherwise or whenever the run returned its last iterate. A constrained run starts from the multipliers, penalty parameter, inner tolerance and working limits of the previous run (result.state) instead of starting them again from zero. A run cut short by evaluations or by the callback and continued with from resumes the interrupted inner problem. The counts of the new result (iterations, evaluations, outer) cover only that run, and its initial_value is the objective at its start. The constraints must have the same components as those of the previous run, or a DimensionMismatch is thrown. An unconstrained from result only sets the start and the box.\n\nCompared with penalty terms at fixed weights, the weights of the constraints are carried by the multipliers from one inner run to the next. The limits are met without stiff weights, which stall the line search, and without stages of loosened limits set by hand. The design also ends at its limits. With penalty terms, it ends where the terms balance the objective. On the dropped-bundle tutorial, this gave 0.35% more drive in a comparable number of evaluations, 997 against 1,072 for the penalty form (research notes, \"Constrained re-pointing\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.optimize_pointing"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.parameter_names-Tuple{GroupPointing}","page":"Optimization","text":"Names \"<group>.<field>\" for each parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.parameters-Tuple{GroupPointing, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Optimization","text":"parameters(p::GroupPointing, beams::AbstractVector{<:Beam}) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe parameter vector of p that reproduces beams, such as the result of an optimization in another parameterization of the same beams. Each template beam of p is matched to the beam of its name in beams. An ArgumentError is thrown for a missing name, and extra beams are ignored. The value of a field for each group is the mean over its members of what apply would need for that beam:\n\nFor the aim fields, it is the axial coordinate of the aim (:z, mirrored as in apply) and the components of the aim along the radial and tangential directions of the beam (:r, :phi, in the frame of unit). For a relative parameterization, it is the same components of the shift from the template aim.\nFor :split, it is the group mean of the signed tangential offset of each beam from the mean of its quad, as in initial_parameters.\nFor :defocus, :power and :dlambda, it is the value of the beam. For :spot, it is the ratio of the spot semi-axis a of the beam to that of the template.\n\nSpherical fields are not supported and throw an ArgumentError.\n\napply(p, parameters(p, beams)) reproduces the beams exactly when p can express them, as when the groups of p split those of the parameterization that produced beams and share its aim frame: a re-pointing by quad handed to a per-beam polish on the same template,\n\nquad = GroupPointing(beams; by = :quad, fields = (:z, :phi), relative = true)\ns = remake(scenario; parameterization = quad)\nr = optimize_pointing(s, objective; ...)\nper_beam = GroupPointing(beams; by = :beam, fields = (:z, :phi), relative = true)\nx0 = parameters(per_beam, r.beams)       # the box `bounds(per_beam)` stays about `beams`\npolish = optimize_pointing(remake(s; parameterization = per_beam), objective; x0, ...)\n\nOtherwise the result is the group means of those values: per-beam aims read by quad give each quad its mean shift.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.parameters-Tuple{GroupPointing, Vararg{Pair}}","page":"Optimization","text":"parameters(p::GroupPointing, values::Pair...; group = (field = value, ...), ...)\n    -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe parameter vector of p with named values: each keyword (for Symbol group keys, such as NIF's cones :inner23 … :outer50) or key => (; field = value, ...) pair (for any key, such as a quad name) sets those fields of that group. Fields and groups not named keep initial_parameters, so only the values that differ from the template beams need to be given. An unknown group or a field that is not a parameter of p is an ArgumentError.\n\npointing = GroupPointing(nif_quads(); fields = (:z, :r, :power))\nx0 = parameters(pointing; inner23 = (z = 3.09, r = -1.09), outer50 = (z = 5.01,))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.power_fraction-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Optimization","text":"Fraction of delivered power absorbed by the named parts or components (see elements).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.power_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.remake-Tuple{Scenario}","page":"Optimization","text":"remake(scenario; parameterization, lpi, deposition_kw, observe, time, albedo, model,\n       history) -> Scenario\n\nCopy of scenario with the given parts replaced. What depends on a replaced part is rebuilt, and everything else is shared:\n\nlpi (a laser–plasma model or nothing): nothing is rebuilt.\nparameterization: the deposition operator is kept when the beams of the new parameterization enter through the ports of the operator in the same order with the same f-cone directions, and, under a VisRadProfile, with the same envelope ratios. These beams are the template of a GroupPointing or FramePointing, or the beams of any other parameterization at its initial parameters. Otherwise the change is refused with an ArgumentError, unless deposition_kw is given too. A kept operator resolves the footprints where the original beams aimed (its reach), as for subset.\ndeposition_kw: the operator is rebuilt for the beams of the parameterization with these options. Options not given take their defaults, because a scenario does not record the options its operator was built with. Pass all options that differ from the defaults, such as profile, reach and resolution.\nobserve, time, albedo and model (a RadiationModel of the same target, or nothing for none): the mode weights and moment responses are recomputed with one flux-response solve, and the full response is computed again when read. With albedo = nothing or :reference, the material albedos at time are used without a warning; any warning was issued when the scenario was constructed.\n\nThe target, radiation and viewfactor_kw do not change through remake, and giving them throws an ArgumentError. They need a new Scenario, or a model built for the purpose.\n\nhistory, a drive_history of the scenario target run with keep_albedo = true, sets with time the albedos and time of the history state at time. remake(scenario; history = h, time = τ) is exactly st =history_state(scenario.target, h, τ) followed by remake(scenario; st.time, st.albedo). Remade so, a phase of a design and its reference at the same time have the same time, which the reference checks of mode_gate and drive_floor compare.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.remake"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.report-Tuple{IO, Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"report([io = stdout,] ev; lmax = 6, minor = 1e-3) -> nothing\n\nPrint a power accounting and flux-moment summary of an evaluation. The summary lists:\n\nthe setup and effective power per group, when the scenario has a laser–plasma model;\nthe delivered laser power and its split over the parts (component_power). The parts below minor × the delivered power are summed on one line;\nthe residual, unless it is below that fraction;\nthe mean of the observed flux (mean_flux), its radiation temperature and its Legendre moments Pₗ/P₀ up to lmax.\n\nThe residual is the delivered power minus the power absorbed and clipped. It is the light that does not strike a surface plus the error of the deposition quadrature; DepositionOperator conserves the power of each beam to 1e-4 when nothing clips. The residual prints as missed when positive and as quadrature excess when negative, where the quadrature counts more power than the beams deliver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.report"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.subset-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Optimization","text":"subset(scenario, keep; parameterization = restricted to the kept beams)\n\nScenario for the beams keep (indices into the scenario beams), e.g. after dropping a bundle. The subset reuses the view factors, flux response and mode weights, and only the deposition operator is restricted. The template values (pointing, power) of the kept beams come from the scenario parameterization.\n\nBy default, the new parameterization has the groups of the scenario. For a GroupPointing, the keys of the groups that still contain a beam are kept, so the parameter names of the subset are those of the full pointing. The fields, mirroring, relative, unit and sphere carry over. With unit = :quad, the default, the azimuth and split of a quad are those of its kept beams. Pass a parameterization to group the kept beams otherwise. For a FramePointing scenario, the frames, groups, fields and relative of the kept beams are kept by default.\n\nFor a HeldParameters or PowerScales scenario, the default wraps the subset of the inner parameterization, so the kept parameters keep their names. The result equals the wrappers built by hand on the kept beams:\n\nFor a parameter whose group still contains some of its beams, the value and the held or free state are kept. The value applies to the kept beams as it applied to all, as an absolute aim or a shift.\nA parameter whose group contains none of the kept beams is dropped, held or free.\nThe power scale of a group with no kept beam is dropped too.\n\nAny other parameterization needs an explicit parameterization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.subset"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.symmetry_objective-Tuple{}","page":"Optimization","text":"symmetry_objective(; modes = (2 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0), odd = 1.0, lmax = 6,\n                   clip = 10.0, missed = 10.0, direct = 10.0, direct_names = nothing,\n                   drive = nothing, drive_weight = 1.0, center = nothing, keepout = nothing,\n                   uncertainty = nothing)\n\nObjective for indirect-drive symmetry. The objective is the sum of these terms:\n\nΣ wₗ (aₗ/a₀)² over modes;\nodd × the sum of squared odd moments, which measures the top/bottom imbalance;\nclip, missed and direct times the squared fractions of laser power clipped on the outside of the target, missing it, or hitting the observed elements directly. The observed elements are those named by the scenario observe, the capsule whose drive is measured. With direct_names, the direct term counts the named parts or components instead, and a name that is not in the target is an error;\nwith drive (TW/mm²) given, drive_weight × (a₀/drive - 1)², with a₀ the mean_flux. With drive = mean_flux(evaluate(scenario, x0)), the objective holds the drive of the design x0;\nwith a keepout, its penalty on the worst-case clearance of the beams. A keep-out is an ApertureKeepOut for the entrance-hole rims, a SphereKeepOut for the capsule, or a tuple of them.\n\nThe direct penalty counts only power already on the capsule, so keep a margin with a SphereKeepOut.\n\nWith uncertainty = (; pointing, power), the mode terms are their expected values over those errors, linearized. The errors are the rms beam-to-beam pointing in mm and the relative power, as in mode_uncertainty. Each (aₗ/a₀)² gains the variance due to the errors, so the optimum also makes the drive shape insensitive to them (robust design). This costs about three gradients per evaluation. The shared errors of mode_uncertainty (group_pointing, group_power, groups, positioning) are accepted too. They add a fourth per-beam sensitivity, for about four gradients per evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.symmetry_objective"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.Constraint","page":"Optimization","text":"Constraint(name, f; upper = nothing, lower = nothing, scale = nothing, labels = nothing)\n\nAn inequality constraint for optimize_pointing: f(ev) ≤ upper (a ceiling) or f(ev) ≥ lower (a floor), with f any function of an Evaluation (its beams ev.beams included), generic in the number type so that the automatic-differentiation gradients of the optimizer pass through it. Give exactly one of upper and lower.\n\nf may return a number or a vector; a vector constraint has one component per entry, each with a separate multiplier and row in the report, against a limit that is a number (shared) or a vector of the same length. labels name the components in reports (default \"1\", \"2\", …): a collection of names, or a function of the evaluation that returns them, for a constraint whose components follow the evaluation (one per beam, as in clearance_floor). Within one run the number of components is fixed; a constraint with a shared limit and labels from the evaluation can be used for scenarios with different numbers of beams, such as a stage on quads and then one on their beams.\n\nscale (a number or one per component, positive) sets the units of the violation: the optimizer works on ±(f(ev) - limit)/scale and its feasibility tolerance is in those units. By default scale = |limit|, so a violation is relative (1e-3: 0.1% of the limit); a zero limit needs a scale.\n\nReady-made constraints: irradiance_ceiling, mode_gate, clearance_floor (the clearance from a keep-out per beam), light_ceiling and light_floor (the laser light on named parts), pulse_ceiling (the limit of the laser on the pulses of a phased design). Any other quantity of the beams only is Constraint(:name, ev -> quantity(ev.beams); lower = minimum).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Constraint"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.check_constraints-Tuple{Evaluation, Constraint}","page":"Optimization","text":"check_constraints(ev, constraints; feasibility = 1e-3) -> Vector{ConstraintRow}\n\nThe value of each component of constraints (a Constraint, or a tuple or vector of them) at the evaluation ev, against its limit, as ConstraintRows: the report of PointingResult.constraints, for any design (a reference, the result of a staged run). feasibility is the tolerance on the normalized violation that separates :violated, :active and :satisfied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.check_constraints"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.clearance_floor-Tuple{Any}","page":"Optimization","text":"clearance_floor(keepout; minimum = keepout.minimum, scale = 1.0, name = :clearance) -> Constraint\n\nA floor on the worst-case clearance (mm) of each beam from a keep-out: the Constraint keepout_clearance(keepout, ev) .≥ minimum, one component per beam of the evaluation, labeled by the beam name. The keep-out is any with a keepout_clearance method and a minimum field: an ApertureKeepOut, a SphereKeepOut, a HardwareKeepOut. A beam the keep-out does not apply to (clearance Inf) gives a component that is always met; a clearance of -Inf or NaN throws an ArgumentError naming the keep-out and the beam, as in keepout_penalty. The weight of the keep-out is not used: the constraint replaces its penalty (keepout_penalty), so pass the keep-out to the keepout keyword of the objective or as a constraint, not both.\n\nscale (mm) is the unit of the violation: with the default 1 mm and feasibility = 1e-3, a clearance within 1 µm of minimum is :active (an engineering default, finer than the keep-out accuracy against clearance, a few micrometers). Give each of several keep-outs a distinct name.\n\nWhere the limits conflict, scale is also the weight of the keep-out against the other constraints, because phase 1, the augmented Lagrangian and the least-violation design of PointingResult all trade normalized violations. At 1 mm, a beam 0.1 mm inside the keep-out weighs as much as a mode gate exceeded by 10% of itself or a ceiling 10% over its limit, with one component per beam. A smaller scale, e.g. a pointing tolerance of 0.05 mm, makes the same shortfall weigh twenty times more. On a re-pointing study whose gates conflicted with the keep-out and a ceiling, that change moved the violation from the keep-out onto the gates and the ceiling, and each of them ended several times further over (research notes, \"Least violation, capped inner runs and phase 1's budget\", § 6). The scale should be chosen for the trade the design accepts.\n\nThe floor applies to each beam separately. In the augmented Lagrangian each beam component has a separate multiplier, which stays zero for a beam clear of its minimum, and the term of that beam then vanishes from the inner problem. Each term is as smooth as the clearance of its beam. The smallest clearance over the beams would instead have a kink wherever the closest beam changes (research notes, \"Constrained re-pointing\"). The components follow the beams of the evaluation, so the same constraint can be used for a stage on quads and a stage on their beams.\n\nOn a PhasedScenario the constraint takes the PerPhase evaluation directly, without in_phase: the component of each beam is its least clearance over the phases, and its rows carry no phase in their names (leh[B211]). The phases share the aims, so a keep-out that does not read the beam power (an ApertureKeepOut at a peak_fraction) gives every phase the same clearance, identical to that of any one phase; a keep-out at an absolute intensity widens with the power, and the brightest phase sets the clearance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.clearance_floor"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.drive_ceiling-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"drive_ceiling(reference; fraction, scale = limit, name = :drive_ceiling) -> Constraint\n\nA ceiling on the capsule drive: the Constraint mean_flux(ev) ≤ fraction × mean_flux(reference), the upper side of drive_floor, with the same reference, scale and check of the laser–plasma model and time of the reference (an ArgumentError on an evaluation with another; the reference may be on another target). With a floor the drive is held in a band about the reference drive: drive_floor(reference; fraction = 1.0) and drive_ceiling(reference; fraction = 1.02) keep it within 0–2% above, so that power scales among the parameters cannot buy shape with drive beyond the band. fraction (positive, no default) is a choice of the study and is not a facility limit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.drive_ceiling"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.drive_floor-Tuple{Evaluation}","page":"Optimization","text":"drive_floor(reference; fraction, scale = limit, name = :drive) -> Constraint\n\nA floor on the capsule drive, the Constraint mean_flux(ev) ≥ fraction × mean_flux(reference). mean_flux is the solid-angle mean of the incident flux on the observed capsule (TW/mm²). reference is an Evaluation of a reference design, such as the pointing before a bundle was dropped or the start, in a scenario that observes the capsule. fraction (positive, no default) sets how much of the reference drive the design must keep: no value suits every study, as a reference with more beams than the design delivers more drive.\n\nThis floor is the constraint for a design whose power can fall. With power scales (PowerScales) or powers among the parameters, a ceiling on an absolute quantity, such as an irradiance_ceiling or a light_ceiling, is met by cutting power, and the drive falls with it. The default scale is the limit, so the default feasibility = 1e-3 meets the floor to 0.1% of the limit. As for mode_gate, the floor records the laser–plasma model and time of the reference scenario, and evaluating it with another throws an ArgumentError; the reference may be on another target. drive_ceiling is the upper side, for a band.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.drive_floor"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.drive_objective-Tuple{}","page":"Optimization","text":"drive_objective(; drive, clip = 10.0, missed = 10.0, direct = 10.0,\n                direct_names = nothing, keepout = nothing)\n\nObjective for the largest drive: -a₀/drive, with a₀ the mean_flux on the capsule and drive (TW/mm²) its scale (e.g. mean_flux(evaluate(scenario, x0)), or a that of a reference design), plus the clip, missed, direct and keepout penalties of symmetry_objective. The objective is meant to run with constraints that hold the shape, such as mode_gate. Without them it rewards drive at any cost to symmetry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.drive_objective"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.irradiance_ceiling-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Optimization","text":"irradiance_ceiling(reference, names...; limit = peak_irradiance(reference, names...),\n                   p = 64, weight = nothing, scale = limit, exact = true) -> Constraint\n\nA ceiling on the absorbed laser irradiance over the elements of the named parts or components. The Constraint is named :irradiance, and by default it bounds the peak_irradiance by limit (see exact below). Its function is the smooth stand-in the optimizer works on, the irradiance_norm scaled to equal the peak at the reference evaluation, ‖I‖ₚ × peak_ref/‖I‖ₚ,ref. With the default limit, the peak of the reference, the irradiance is held at that of the reference design, for example the pointing before a bundle was dropped; a number in TW/mm² sets another, measured on the same scale. The reference evaluation must be on the same target as the optimized scenario (a subset of it shares the target), with the same laser–plasma model (lpi, compared on every field, so that a model built alike passes) at the same time; otherwise evaluating the ceiling throws an ArgumentError. A reference evaluated without the CBET model of the scenario gives the peak and the stand-in scale of another quantity, and the ceiling would hold the design to a limit on that quantity, so the mismatch throws an error. Evaluate the reference in a scenario with the same model, e.g. evaluate(remake(reference_scenario; lpi = scenario.lpi), beams). weight selects and weighs the beams that count, as in peak_irradiance (the outer cones only, or picket energies for a fluence ceiling), for the reference and the design alike.\n\nThe ceiling is on an absolute irradiance: where the power can fall (power scales, PowerScales, or powers among the parameters) the optimizer can meet it by cutting power, at a loss of drive (on a re-pointing study with a power scale per cone at most 1, a large one). With a drive_floor beside it, the drive is held.\n\nA ceiling on the peak wall irradiance at the value of the reference is an engineering limit and is not a published criterion. The published scalings of the growth of the gold bubble are written in terms of the picket fluence of the outer cones on the wall (research notes), and a weight computes that fluence on the same machinery.\n\nWith exact = true (the default) the peak itself is held at the limit. The optimizer works on the norm, but once the constrained run has converged it compares the peak_irradiance with the limit; while the peak exceeds it by more than the feasibility of the run (in units of scale), the working limit of the norm is scaled by limit/peak and the run continues from the same design, multipliers and penalty. The reported value (check_constraints, PointingResult.constraints) is the peak against the limit, and a converged run ends with the peak at or below the limit within feasibility. The cost is the further outer iterations, when the peak ends above the limit (research notes, \"Constrained re-pointing\").\n\nexact = false bounds only the norm, and the norm is the reported value. At the limit the hot spots of the field are as hot, in the p-norm mean, as those of the reference, but the peak over the elements may exceed the limit by a margin that depends on where the run ends. On the tutorial mesh the margin is 0.1% at p = 64 and 3% at p = 16. On the dropped-bundle test case (0.45 mm wall elements) it is 3% at p = 64 for one of two designs that meet the same gates, under another floating-point rounding of the same run (research notes, \"Constrained re-pointing\").\n\nAcross meshes the ceiling is a ratio to the reference on the same mesh, which changes less with the element size than the ratio of the peaks does (rms difference 1.0% against 1.3% between 0.2 and 0.45 mm elements near the limit), while the peak itself falls on a coarser mesh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.irradiance_ceiling"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.irradiance_norm-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Optimization","text":"irradiance_norm(ev, names...; p = 64, weight = nothing) -> Real\n\nThe area-weighted p-norm of the absorbed laser irradiance I (TW/mm²) over the elements of the named parts or components, (Σ Aₑ Iₑᵖ / Σ Aₑ)^(1/p). It is a smooth stand-in for the peak_irradiance. It lies below the peak and approaches it as p grows (the power-mean inequality), and its gradient comes from every element near the peak instead of only the hottest one. As an area mean it is a property of the irradiance field and not of the elements, so it depends less on the mesh than the peak does. weight is as in peak_irradiance. Generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.irradiance_norm"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.light_ceiling-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Optimization","text":"light_ceiling(reference, names...; limit = light_fraction(reference, names...),\n              scale = max(limit, 1e-3), name = :light) -> Constraint\nlight_ceiling(limit::Real, names...; scale = max(limit, 1e-3), name = :light) -> Constraint\n\nA ceiling on the laser light the named parts or components receive, the Constraint light_fraction(ev, names...) ≤ limit, labeled by the names. light_fraction is the light absorbed on the parts plus the light striking their outside, as a fraction of the delivered power. With a reference evaluation the limit is the light of the reference on the parts, so that a window or a shield receives no more light than in an accepted design. A number sets the fraction directly.\n\nscale sets the units of the violation: relative to the limit (the default) when the limit is above 1e-3 of the delivered power, otherwise absolute in units of 1e-3, so that a ceiling at or near zero (a part meant to stay dark) is met to 1e-6 of the delivered power at feasibility = 1e-3 (an engineering default). The fraction comes from the deposition operator, which samples the spot edges: compare a ray trace (raytrace_deposition) where the light on hardware must be known to better than the sampling of the operator (research notes, \"Capture and clearance rules as optimization quantities\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.light_ceiling"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.light_floor-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Optimization","text":"light_floor(reference, names...; limit = light_fraction(reference, names...),\n            scale = max(limit, 1e-3), name = :light) -> Constraint\nlight_floor(limit::Real, names...; scale = max(limit, 1e-3), name = :light) -> Constraint\n\nA floor on the laser light the named parts or components receive, light_fraction(ev, names...) ≥ limit: the capture by a part meant to be hit, such as a backlighter foil or a landing surface, at least that of the reference design or a given fraction. Otherwise as light_ceiling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.light_floor"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.light_fraction-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Optimization","text":"light_fraction(ev, names...) -> Real\n\nAll the laser light the named parts or components receive, from either side, as a fraction of the delivered (effective, post-LPI) power: power_fraction(ev, names...) +clipped_fraction(ev, names...), the absorbed power on their elements and the power striking their outside (back side). The value of light_ceiling and light_floor; differentiable, in the number type of the evaluation. A name not in the target is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.light_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.mode_departure-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Optimization","text":"mode_departure(c, reference; lmax = 4, m = :all) -> Vector\nmode_departure(ev, reference; lmax = 4, m = :all) -> Vector\n\nThe departure of a drive shape from a reference shape per degree l = 1:lmax: δₗ = √((2l+1) Σₘ (c_lm/c_00 - r_lm/r_00)²), the mode_amplitude of the difference of the two normalized shapes (c and reference are coefficient vectors from flux_ylm; with an evaluation, those of the evaluation). For an axisymmetric difference this is |aₗ/a₀ - aₗ'/a₀'|. Generic in the number type: the value of mode_gate.\n\nm = :zero sums only the zonal order m = 0, √(2l+1) |Δ(c_l0/c_00)|, which is the change of aₗ/a₀ about the polar axis of the coefficients. m = :nonzero sums the other orders, so that δₗ² = δₗ(:zero)² + δₗ(:nonzero)², as in the split of mode_rms's m. A gate per degree bounds δₗ, and the split shows whether the departure in a degree is axisymmetric. For example, l = 1 with m = ±1 is a sideways shift of the drive from a dropped bundle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_departure"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.mode_gate-Tuple{Evaluation, Any}","page":"Optimization","text":"mode_gate(reference::Evaluation, gates; fraction = 1, floor = nothing, shift = nothing)\n    -> Constraint\nmode_gate(reference::AbstractVector, gates; fraction = 1, floor = nothing, shift = nothing)\n    -> Constraint\n\nA gate per degree on the departure of the capsule drive shape from a reference shape. The Constraint mode_departure(ev, reference; lmax) ≤ gates is named :mode_gate and has one component per degree l = 1:lmax, labeled l = 1, …, each in units of its gate.\n\ngates is either a vector of departures or the result of mode_uncertainty. In a vector, gates[l] is the gate for degree l in the units of mode_departure, which for an axisymmetric difference are |Δaₗ/a₀|; so 0.005 at l = 2 is 0.5% in P2/P0. For a mode_uncertainty result the gate is its floor, √(2l+1) × rms_mode[l], the departure that random errors of that size (e.g. NIF's 50 µm rms pointing) give each degree. That gate is a lower bound, because it is what pointing error alone produces. Laser delivery and the hohlraum plasma move the shape from shot to shot by more, in ways a view-factor model does not carry. A tolerance per degree in physical units, chosen for the design, is therefore the usual gate. At the pointing floor the gates can cost re-pointing and drive that a tolerance within that shot-to-shot spread does not (research notes, \"Mode gates in physical units\"). The share of laser delivery can be included in the floor through mode_uncertainty's power, an rms fraction per pointing unit. On the case of the dropped-bundle tutorial, 3% per quad raises the floor by 40% at l = 1 and 21% at l = 2, and 5% raises it by 92% and 51%. NIF's published beam-to-beam balance is better than 5% (research notes, \"Inner-run options, a wall-loss scenario warning and laser delivery figures\", § 7). With drive_objective the optimizer finds the largest drive within the gates; with shift_objective it finds the least re-pointing that meets them.\n\nWith a reference Evaluation (e.g. the pointing before a bundle was dropped, in a scenario that observes the capsule), the shape is its flux_ylm coefficients, and the gate records the laser–plasma model (lpi) and time of the reference scenario: evaluating the gate on a scenario with another model, or the same model at another time, throws an ArgumentError, as for irradiance_ceiling (the shape of a drive without the CBET model of the scenario is another shape, and the gates would hold the design to it). The reference may be on another target (a hohlraum without the windows of the design). A coefficient vector does not record a model, so that method cannot check it: the vector must come from an evaluation with the lpi and time of the scenario.\n\nKeywords.\n\nfraction (positive, default 1) scales a pointing floor. With gates a mode_uncertainty result, the gate is fraction × √(2l+1) × rms_mode[l], that is fraction × u.floor[l]; fraction = 0.8 gives 0.8 of the floor. A vector as long as the floor gives one multiple per degree; fraction = [1, 2, 2, 2] gives the floor at l = 1 and twice the floor at l = 2–4.\nfloor (a mode_uncertainty result, or a vector of departures per degree) makes the floor a lower bound of physical gates: the gate per degree is the larger of gates[l] and fraction × floor[l], so that a tolerance in physical units is never tighter than the departure pointing error alone produces (research notes, \"Mode gates in physical units\"). gates and floor cover the same degrees.\nshift moves the target shape away from the reference shape: the departure is measured from the reference coefficients with each listed mode shifted, the laser–plasma model and time of the reference still checked. A shift is a pair l => Δ (the axisymmetric mode (l, 0)) or (l, m) => Δ, or a collection of them, with Δ in the units of mode_departure: for m = 0, Δ is the change of aₗ/a₀ in the Legendre expansion of the capsule flux Σ aₗ Pₗ(cos θ) (2 => 0.024 sets the target P2/P0 0.024 above that of the reference; with the orthonormal coefficients of flux_ylm, c_l0 → c_l0 + Δ c_00/√(2l+1)), and for m ≠ 0 the same change of the real coefficient c_lm, so that the shifted shape departs from the reference shape by |Δ| in degree l (shift_modes). The drive c_00 is unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_gate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.peak_irradiance-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Optimization","text":"peak_irradiance(ev, names...; weight = nothing) -> Real\n\nThe largest absorbed laser irradiance (TW/mm²; to_Wcm2 converts) over the elements of the named parts or components. The irradiance of an element is its power over its area, as in laser_flux. The peak is exact over the elements, so it has a kink wherever it moves to another element. It is the reported quantity of irradiance_ceiling, whose optimizer works on the smooth irradiance_norm. The element average resolves a peak only as finely as the mesh: a coarser mesh averages the hottest spot over more area and reports a lower peak (on the dropped-bundle hohlraum, 8% lower at 0.45 mm than at 0.2 mm).\n\nweight, a function of a beam, counts the deposition of each beam times weight(beam) instead of all beams at their own powers: 0 leaves a beam out, so weight = b -> b.group in (:outer44, :outer50) ? 1.0 : 0.0 gives the irradiance of the outer cones, and a picket energy over the beam power gives a fluence map (the deposition is linear in the power of each beam). The weighted deposition is one more deposit per evaluation, of the effective beams of the evaluation through the laser–plasma model of the scenario. The function is called with the setup beams of the evaluation (their names, groups, quads and ports).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.peak_irradiance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.shift_modes-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, Any}","page":"Optimization","text":"shift_modes(c, shift) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe coefficient vector c with each mode of shift moved, the target shape of mode_gate(...; shift). The coefficients are those of flux_ylm, orthonormal real harmonics in mode_index order. A shift is a pair l => Δ for the mode (l, 0), a pair (l, m) => Δ, or a collection of them, with Δ in the units of mode_departure: c_lm → c_lm + Δ c_00/√(2l+1). For m = 0, Δ is the change of aₗ/a₀ in the Legendre expansion Σ aₗ Pₗ(cos θ) (since aₗ/a₀ = √(2l+1) c_l0/c_00); a single shift departs from c by |Δ| in its degree. c_00 (the drive) is unchanged, and the vector does not record a laser–plasma model: mode_gate(reference::Evaluation, gates; shift) still checks the reference model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.shift_modes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.infer_errors-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any}","page":"Optimization","text":"infer_errors(scenario, beams, measured; sigma, pointing = 0.05, power = 0.0,\n             group_pointing = 0.0, group_power = 0.0, groups = :quad,\n             positioning = 0.0) -> NamedTuple\n\nInfers the most probable beam errors behind a measured drive-shape departure. This function is the inverse of mode_uncertainty and takes the same error priors. A measured low mode can trend from shot to shot; MacGowan et al. (HEDP 2021) trend mode 1 of NIF implosions against beam and target data. Such a trend raises the question of which errors could have caused it, and infer_errors gives a linear answer for one shot.\n\nmeasured contains the departures Δc = (c_lm/c_00)_measured − (c_lm/c_00)_nominal as pairs. A pair (l, m) => Δc gives one component, and l => [Δc_l,-l, …, Δc_l,l] gives a whole degree in mode_index order. sigma is the rms error of the measurement, one number or one per component in the order that measured expands to. The nominal shape is that of the scenario for beams. Components are allowed through l = 6, the limit of the cached Y_lm weights.\n\nThe model is Δc = J x + ε, with x the errors and J their exact sensitivities (mode_sensitivity, with the shared columns). The errors are zero-mean normal with the variances of mode_uncertainty: per beam, its aim along the spot axes a, b and its relative power; per group, a shared displacement and power; and the target positioning. The posterior mean, which is also the most probable x, and its covariance come from the data-space (Woodbury) form, a solve the size of the measurement:\n\nx̂ = Σₚ Jᵀ (J Σₚ Jᵀ + Σₘ)⁻¹ Δc,   Σ = Σₚ − Σₚ Jᵀ (J Σₚ Jᵀ + Σₘ)⁻¹ J Σₚ.\n\nReturns (; beam, group, positioning, x, sigma, prior_sigma, covariance, names, components, measured, predicted, residual, sources, resolved, beams, J, c):\n\nbeam: nb × 3, the inferred a, b displacement (mm) and relative power of each beam;\ngroup: a Dict from group key to (; displacement, power), with the displacement a Vec3 in mm;\npositioning: the inferred common displacement of every aim relative to the target. The target therefore lies at minus this vector from where the beams are aimed.\nx, sigma, prior_sigma, covariance, names: all errors as one vector, with their posterior and prior rms, the posterior covariance and labels. The vector holds the beam a, b and power, then the group errors, then x, y and z.\npredicted = J x̂ and residual = Δc − J x̂, over the (l, m) list components;\nsources: the fraction of Δc reproduced by the share of J x̂ from each error source, dot(J_s x̂_s, Δc)/|Δc|², sorted descending. A source is the pointing or power of a beam or of a group, or the positioning. The fractions sum to dot(predicted, Δc)/|Δc|², and the first entries are the sources that best explain the measurement.\nresolved: Σⱼ (1 − Σⱼⱼ/σⱼ²), the number of error combinations that the data determine, at most the number of components;\nbeams: beams with x̂ applied, for a full evaluate as the nonlinear check. The errors are applied before a laser–plasma model, as in the sensitivities.\n\nThe name describes the result: the errors that are most probable under a prior, which are not a unique explanation. Many errors map to a few modes, so a handful of combinations are determined and every other direction stays at its prior. sigma ./ prior_sigma therefore stays near 1 for most errors, and resolved gives the number of determined combinations. The result is only as good as the prior. An error left out of it, such as group_pointing = 0, cannot be blamed, and its effect is attributed to the sources that are allowed. The inference is linear in the errors. infer_velocity_errors is the same inverse for a measured hot-spot velocity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.infer_errors"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.mode_sensitivity-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Optimization","text":"mode_sensitivity(scenario, beams; lmax = 4) -> NamedTuple\n\nComputes the sensitivity of the real spherical-harmonic shape c_lm/c_00 of the observed flux to the errors of each beam. The coefficients are in mode_index order, through lmax ≤ 6. Each beam has two kinds of error. One is a pointing displacement of its aim transverse to the beam, in mm along the spot a and b axes. The other is a relative power error. Returns (; J, c, names):\n\nJ is (lmax+1)² × 3nb, and column 3(b-1) + k is the derivative with respect to error k of beam b. Errors k = 1 and 2 are the two transverse directions, and k = 3 is the relative power.\nc is the nominal c_lm/c_00.\nnames contains the column labels.\n\nThe deposition of each beam depends on its errors only, so the derivatives come from one forward-mode pass per beam with three dual partials: exact (within round-off), and about the cost of one gradient of an objective. The beams are the setup beams of the scenario. A laser–plasma model is applied before the errors, since the errors are in the delivered beams. Errors move the aim, and the beam direction, set by the port, is fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_sensitivity"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.mode_uncertainty-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Optimization","text":"mode_uncertainty(scenario, beams; pointing = 0.05, power = 0.0, lmax = 4,\n                 group_pointing = 0.0, group_power = 0.0, groups = :quad,\n                 positioning = 0.0) -> NamedTuple\n\nComputes the expected spread of the observed drive shape from beam errors. The errors are propagated linearly through exact per-beam sensitivities (mode_sensitivity).\n\nIndependent beam-to-beam errors displace the aim of each beam at random transverse to the beam, with rms pointing mm. That rms is of the displacement length, so each axis gets pointing/√2; NIF specifies 50 µm. The power of each beam is scaled by an rms fraction power.\n\nCorrelated errors are zero by default:\n\ngroup_pointing displaces every beam of a group by one shared vector transverse to the mean beam direction of the group, with rms length group_pointing mm. groups is a Beam field such as :quad or a function of the beam, which can select bundles or the cone in one hemisphere, b -> (b.group, b.port[3] > 0). Beams at an angle to that mean also move along their axes, with the focus moving with the aim. A group whose beams have no common direction, such as :group for a cone over both hemispheres, is refused.\ngroup_power gives one shared relative power error per group, with rms group_power.\npositioning displaces the target from chamber center, which moves every aim by the same vector relative to the target. It is an isotropic rms length in mm or a 3-tuple of rms values along x, y and z.\n\nEach source adds to the covariance independently. Returns (; c, sigma, covariance, rms_mode, floor, rms_by, J):\n\nc is the nominal c_lm/c_00, sigma the rms of each coefficient and covariance their covariance.\nrms_mode[l], for each degree l = 1:lmax, is the rms of the mode amplitude over the errors, √E[Σ_m (δc_lm/c_00)²]. It is the expected mode-l budget used by the errors. mode_amplitude gives √(2l+1) times the nominal amplitude for comparison.\nfloor[l] is √(2l+1) × rms_mode[l], the same spread in the units of mode_departure, which is |Δaₗ/a₀| for an axisymmetric change. It is the noise floor of a drive shape under these errors. mode_gate takes its gate from this result, with fraction a multiple of the floor, one number or one per degree.\nrms_by splits rms_mode by source into beam, group and positioning, which add in quadrature.\nJ is the matrix of mode_sensitivity.\n\nThe propagation is linear in the errors. Large errors can be checked with sample_modes, which takes the same keywords.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_uncertainty"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.sample_modes-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Optimization","text":"sample_modes(scenario, beams; pointing = 0.05, power = 0.0, n = 100,\n             rng = Random.default_rng(), lmax = 4, group_pointing = 0.0,\n             group_power = 0.0, groups = :quad, positioning = 0.0) -> Matrix\n\nRuns a Monte Carlo of the drive shape under the errors of mode_uncertainty, with the same keywords. Returns n samples of c_lm/c_00 as columns. Each sample comes from a full evaluation with the shared group and target-positioning errors and with the independent errors of every beam. The aim of each beam is displaced along its spot axes by normal errors of rms pointing/√2 each, and its power is scaled by a normal fraction of rms power. The samples check the linear propagation independently. They also give the answer when the errors are large enough for the drive to respond nonlinearly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.sample_modes"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.HeldParameters","page":"Optimization","text":"HeldParameters(p, held; values = initial_parameters(p))\n\nThe parameterization p with the parameters held fixed at values and the rest free. Its parameter vector contains the free parameters of p in their order. apply(h, y) is apply(p, x) with x = values except at the free indices, where x = y. held selects parameters of p by\n\nindices (a vector of integers) or a mask (a Bool per parameter);\nnames (a vector of parameter_names(p) entries, e.g. \"Q31B.z\");\na predicate on the name, e.g. n -> startswith(n, \"inner\") for the inner cones of a GroupPointing grouped by cone, or n -> first(split(n, '.')) in inner_quads for a set of quad names when grouped by quad.\n\nAn unknown name throws an ArgumentError. p may itself be a composition, such as a PowerScales; p needs a parameter_names method to select by name.\n\nThe full parameterization interface follows p:\n\ninitial_parameters are values at the free indices;\nparameter_names and nparams are those of the free parameters;\nbounds(h, y0; kwargs...) is the box of p about the held values and y0, restricted to the free parameters, with kwargs the keywords of the bounds of p.\n\napply is generic in the number type. The held values enter as constants of the element type of y, so ForwardDiff duals pass only through the free parameters. Named values pass through to p (parameters), and a subset of a scenario contains the names, values and holds of the kept parameters.\n\npointing = GroupPointing(quads; by = :quad, fields = (:z, :phi), relative = true)\ninner = Set(b.quad for b in quads if b.group in (:inner23, :inner30))\nouters_only = HeldParameters(pointing, n -> first(split(n, '.')) in inner)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.HeldParameters"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.PowerScales","page":"Optimization","text":"PowerScales(p; by = :group)\n\nThe parameterization p with a power scale per group of beams after its parameters. The parameter vector is [x; s], and apply(ps, [x; s]) is apply(p, x) with the power of each beam multiplied by the scale s of its group. Groups are keyed by by on the beams of p at its initial parameters. by is :group, which keys cones, or :quad, :beam or a function beam -> key, such as one with two keys for the inner and outer cones,\n\ncones = PowerScales(pointing; by = b -> b.group in (:inner23, :inner30) ? :inner : :outer)\n\nWith this grouping, the cone fraction and the total power vary beside the pointing. Beams that share a key share a scale, so a grouping ties them. With the shapes of pulse_shape_groups, keyed by quad, PowerScales(pointing; by = b -> g.assignment[b.quad]) gives one scale per pulse shape for a re-optimization. The scales start at 1 and are named \"<key>.power_scale\". They multiply whatever power p gives, including a :power field of p, so the two are redundant together. Holding one of them (HeldParameters) removes the redundancy.\n\nbounds(ps, y0; scale = 0.5, scale_min = 0.0, scale_max = Inf, kwargs...) puts each scale within y0 (1 ± scale), clipped to [scale_min, scale_max], and the other parameters in the box of p with kwargs. The defaults are engineering defaults, and no facility limit sets them; with scale_max = 1, each group stays at or below the power of p. apply is generic in the number type. A scale is set by name as the field power_scale of its key (parameters), and a subset of a scenario contains the scales of the groups that still have a beam.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PowerScales"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.apply-Tuple{HeldParameters, AbstractVector}","page":"Optimization","text":"apply(h::HeldParameters, y) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nReturns the beams of the inner parameterization at the held values, with y at the free parameters. The element type follows y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.apply"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.apply-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{PowerScales, AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Optimization","text":"apply(ps::PowerScales, y) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nReturns the beams of the inner parameterization at y[1:n], with the power of each beam multiplied by the scale of its group in y[n+1:end]. The element type follows y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.apply"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.bounds-2","page":"Optimization","text":"bounds(h::HeldParameters, y0 = initial_parameters(h); kwargs...) -> (lower, upper)\n\nReturns the box of the inner parameterization, its bounds with kwargs, about the held values and y0, at the free parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bounds"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.bounds-3","page":"Optimization","text":"bounds(ps::PowerScales, y0 = initial_parameters(ps); scale = 0.5, scale_min = 0.0,\n       scale_max = Inf, kwargs...) -> (lower, upper)\n\nReturns the box of the inner parameterization, its bounds with kwargs, for the first parameters, and each power scale within y0 (1 ± scale), clipped to [scale_min, scale_max].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.initial_parameters-Tuple{HeldParameters}","page":"Optimization","text":"initial_parameters(h::HeldParameters) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturns the held values at the free indices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.initial_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.initial_parameters-Tuple{PowerScales}","page":"Optimization","text":"initial_parameters(ps::PowerScales) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturns the initial parameters of the inner parameterization, then a scale of 1 per group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.initial_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.nparams-Tuple{HeldParameters}","page":"Optimization","text":"Number of free parameters of a HeldParameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nparams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.nparams-Tuple{PowerScales}","page":"Optimization","text":"Number of parameters of a PowerScales: those of the inner parameterization, plus one per group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nparams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.parameter_names-Tuple{HeldParameters}","page":"Optimization","text":"Names of the free parameters of a HeldParameters (those of its inner parameterization).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.parameter_names-Tuple{PowerScales}","page":"Optimization","text":"Returns the names of the parameters of a PowerScales. These are the names of the inner parameterization, or \"x[i]\" for one without names, then \"<key>.power_scale\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.parameters-Tuple{HeldParameters, Vararg{Pair}}","page":"Optimization","text":"parameters(h::HeldParameters, values::Pair...; group = (field = value, ...), ...)\nparameters(ps::PowerScales, values::Pair...; group = (field = value, ...), ...)\n    -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturns the parameter vector of a composed parameterization with named values, given as for a GroupPointing. The values are key => (; field = value, ...) pairs, or keywords for Symbol keys. The fields pass through to the wrapped GroupPointing or FramePointing. The scale of a PowerScales is the field power_scale of its key, so one pair can set the aim of a quad and its scale:\n\np = HeldParameters(PowerScales(GroupPointing(beams; by = :quad, fields = (:z, :phi)); by = :quad),\n                   n -> startswith(n, \"Q31\"))\ny0 = parameters(p, \"Q15T\" => (z = 5.2, power_scale = 0.9))\n\nValues not named keep initial_parameters, which are the values of a HeldParameters at its free parameters and scales of 1. A held parameter, a scale key with no scale, an unknown group or a field that is not a parameter throws an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameters"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.PointingSummary","page":"Optimization","text":"PointingSummary\n\nA pointing (e.g. the beams of a re-pointing result) against reference beams, from pointing_summary. Fields:\n\nnbeams; shift_mean, shift_rms and shift_max (mm): the mean, root-mean-square and largest aim shift |aim - reference aim| over the beams, matched by name (the rms is the square root of shift_objective with unit weights);\npower (TW): the total setup power, and cone_fraction: the share of it in the inner groups;\nclearance and reference_clearance (mm): per keep-out, the least clearance of the beams and of the reference beams. +Inf, for a beam to which the keep-out does not apply, is left out, and the value is Inf when every beam is of that kind. -Inf and NaN are kept, so that they are the least and show as such. -Inf marks a beam that the rule cannot place, such as a CaptureKeepIn beam whose axis misses every capture part. margin (mm) is the clearance less the minimum of the keep-out, and it is negative inside the keep-out;\nclipped and missed: clipped_fraction and missed_fraction of the beams in the scenario;\nwith a drive_reference: drive_ratio, the mean capsule drive over that of the reference (c₀₀/r₀₀ of the flux_ylm coefficients), and departure, the mode_departure per degree l = 1:lmax from the shape of the reference. The departure is split by order into departure_zero (m = 0, mode_departure(...; m = :zero)) and departure_nonzero (m ≠ 0), whose squares add to the square of departure. Without a reference these are NaN and empty;\nwith gates: gate, the gate per degree, and gate_departure, the departure that it bounds. That is the value of a mode_gate, from the shape of its reference, shifted if the gate is shifted, and otherwise departure. With floor: floor, the pointing floor per degree, against which departure is shown. Without them these are empty;\nwith moved thresholds (mm): moved_thresholds, and moved, the number of groups (by moved_by, e.g. quads, with ngroups groups) whose largest aim shift exceeds each threshold. Without thresholds these are empty, and ngroups is 0.\n\nThe display is one line, suited to a log of the runs of a study. Where given, it shows the departure over the gate and over the floor per degree (1.00 at the gate) and the groups moved past each threshold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PointingSummary"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.pointing_summary-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Optimization","text":"pointing_summary(scenario, beams; reference = starting beams of the scenario,\n                 keepout = nothing, inner = (:inner23, :inner30),\n                 drive_reference = nothing, lmax = 4, gates = nothing, floor = nothing,\n                 moved = (), moved_by = :quad) -> PointingSummary\npointing_summary(scenario, result::PointingResult; kwargs...)\n\nSummary of beams (or the beams of a result) in scenario against the reference beams. By default the reference is the beams of the parameterization of the scenario at its initial parameters, the start of optimize_pointing. Each beam is matched to the reference beam of its name, with an ArgumentError when there is none, so the beams of a subset compare with those of the full set. keepout is an ApertureKeepOut, a SphereKeepOut, a HardwareKeepOut (any keep-out with a keepout_clearance method), a tuple or vector of them, or nothing. inner names the groups whose power is the cone fraction, as for cone_fraction_pulses. The clipped and missed fractions come from evaluate(scenario, beams), after the laser–plasma model of the scenario.\n\ndrive_reference adds the capsule drive against a reference design, as the drive ratio and the departure per degree, the quantities of drive_objective and mode_gate. It is an Evaluation of the reference design (e.g. the pointing before a bundle was dropped, in the full scenario) or its flux_ylm coefficients through l = lmax. It needs a scenario that observes the capsule.\n\ngates adds the departure against gates per degree. It is a vector of gates (in the units of mode_departure, against the departure from drive_reference), a mode_uncertainty result (its floor, √(2l+1) × rms_mode), or a mode_gate constraint. For a constraint, its value and limits are shown, where the value is the departure from the shape of its reference, shifted if the gate is shifted, with its check of the laser–plasma model and time. floor (a vector, or a mode_uncertainty result) adds the departure from drive_reference against the pointing floor. Both need degrees 1:lmax.\n\nmoved, aim shifts in mm (e.g. (0.1, 0.2, 0.3)), counts the groups of beams whose largest shift exceeds each threshold. Groups are formed by moved_by, a Beam field (:quad, the default; :group for cones, :name for beams) or a function of a beam. The beams of a quad re-aimed as a unit move alike, so the count per quad is the number of quads whose aim point changes by more than the threshold in a review.\n\nr = optimize_pointing(scenario, objective)\npointing_summary(scenario, r; keepout = ApertureKeepOut(target), drive_reference = ref,\n                 moved = (0.1, 0.2, 0.3))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pointing_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.shift_objective-Tuple{Scenario}","page":"Optimization","text":"shift_objective(scenario; reference = starting beams of the scenario, weight = b -> 1.0)\nshift_objective(reference::AbstractVector{<:Beam}; weight = b -> 1.0)\n\nAn objective for optimize_pointing, the weighted mean squared aim shift (mm²) of the beams of an evaluation from the reference beams, matched by name, Σ w_b |aim_b - aim_ref(b)|² / Σ w_b. By default the reference is the beams of the parameterization of the scenario at its initial parameters, the start of optimize_pointing. With gates as constraints the objective gives the least re-pointing that meets them, for example to cost each entrance-hole size against a fixed reference drive:\n\nr = optimize_pointing(scenario, shift_objective(scenario); constraints = (gates, keepout))\nsqrt(r.value)            # the rms aim shift (mm), as pointing_summary(scenario, r).shift_rms\n\nweight, a function of a reference beam (of its group, quad or name), is evaluated once on the reference beams. For example, b -> b.group in (:inner23, :inner30) ? 4.0 : 1.0 makes shifts of the inner cones costlier. The weights must be finite and non-negative with a positive sum over the beams evaluated. A beam without a reference beam of its name is an ArgumentError at evaluation. Reference beams outside the evaluation are ignored, so the beams of a subset compare with those of the full set. Only the aim enters; the defocus does not. The objective is generic in the number type and thread-safe, since it does not modify its tables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.shift_objective"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.PerPhase","page":"Optimization","text":"PerPhase{V}\nPerPhase(:picket => a, :peak => b, ...)\n\nOne value per phase of a PhasedScenario, in the order of the phases. The values are the beams (apply of a PhasedPointing, PointingResult.beams), the evaluations (evaluate) or the summaries (pointing_summary) of a phased design. A PerPhase is indexed by phase name (v[:picket]) or position (v[1]). keys gives the names, values the values and pairs the name => value pairs. Iteration, length, haskey and map (which applies a function to each value and keeps the names) work as for an ordered dictionary whose iteration yields the values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PerPhase"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.Phase","page":"Optimization","text":"Phase(name, target; power = nothing, pulses = nothing, time = nothing, pulse_by = :group,\n      scaled = true, history = nothing, kwargs...)\nPhase(name, scenario::Scenario; power = nothing, pulses = nothing, time = scenario.time,\n      pulse_by = :group, scaled = true, history = nothing)\n\nOne phase of a PhasedScenario. A phase consists of its name, the target that its beams deposit in and radiate from, and the beam powers in the phase. With a target, the phased scenario builds the Scenario of the phase for the shared pointing. The build uses the Scenario keywords of the phased scenario, overridden by the kwargs of this phase (observe, deposition_kw, viewfactor_kw, lpi, albedo, model, radiation, time). A phase with separate wall albedos is a target with separate materials. It can share the view factors of the model of another phase through viewfactor_kw = (; F = model.F). A ready scenario is used with its operators, radiation model and options, and its parameterization is replaced by the shared pointing. Its deposition operator must then serve the beams of the pointing, as for remake(scenario; parameterization).\n\nOne target, view-factor matrix and deposition operator can be used for every phase, with windows intact or burned through, as remakes of a shared scenario with the albedos and time of each phase. history, a drive_history of the target of the phase run with keep_albedo = true, gives the albedos at the time of the phase. Phase(:rise, shared; history = h, time = τ, pulses) is Phase(:rise, remake(shared; history = h, time = τ); pulses), with the albedos of history_state(target, h, τ). With a target, they are the albedo of the built scenario. A reference scenario at the same time is remake(reference_scenario; history = reference_history, time = τ), so that the reference checks of mode_gate and drive_floor use the time of the phase.\n\nThe beam powers of the phase (TW per beam) replace those of the pointing:\n\npower: a number for every beam, a vector (one per beam of the pointing, in its order) or a function of a beam. With nothing, the powers given by the pointing are kept, which are those of its template or of a :power field.\npulses at time (ns): the power of each beam is the power of its pulse at that time, as set by with_pulses(beams, pulses, time; by = pulse_by).\n\ntime is also the time of the built scenario, which sets the albedos of the materials and the laser–plasma model, unless kwargs give another. The time of a ready scenario is kept. scaled = false fixes the powers of the phase, for example for a peak at the laser limit. The phase then has no power scales among the parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Phase"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.PhasedPointing","page":"Optimization","text":"PhasedPointing(pointing, phases, power; scaled = phases, by = :group)\n\nThe parameterization of a PhasedScenario, its param. The parameters of pointing set the aims of every phase. pointing is any parameterization with apply and initial_parameters, such as a GroupPointing by cone, quad or beam (relative or not), a FramePointing or a HeldParameters of either. A power scale per (group, phase) follows for the phases in scaled. phases are the names of the phases in order, and power[k] the powers of phase k, a vector per beam of the pointing or nothing for the powers of the pointing. Groups are keyed by by on the beams of the pointing as for PowerScales (:group, :quad, :beam or a function of a beam). A function that gives several quads one key ties their scales, with one pulse shape for them and a multiplier per phase. The keys of the scales, keys, are (key, phase) tuples, phase by phase in order of first appearance. The scales are named \"<key>.power_scale.<phase>\" and follow the parameter names of the pointing.\n\napply(p, x) gives a PerPhase of beam vectors. They are the beams of the pointing at x[1:n] with the power of each phase, multiplied by the scale of the group in a scaled phase. Generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PhasedPointing"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.PhasedScenario","page":"Optimization","text":"PhasedScenario(pointing, phases; by = :group, kwargs...)\nPhasedScenario(pointing, phase::Phase...; by = :group, kwargs...)\n\nSeveral phases of one pulse under one pointing, such as a picket, a foot, and the peak before and after the windows burn through. Each Phase has a Scenario with its target (wall albedos, windows), radiation model, deposition options, observed elements and laser–plasma model, and its beam powers. The ports and spots of the beams of pointing are shared, and its parameters set the aims of every phase. The parameterization (scenario.param, a PhasedPointing) adds a power scale per (group, phase) for the phases not fixed with scaled = false. Groups are keyed by by (:group, :quad, :beam or a function of a beam), and one key for several quads ties their scales. kwargs are Scenario keywords shared by the phases built from a target (observe, deposition_kw, viewfactor_kw, …), and the keywords of a phase override them.\n\nevaluate(scenario, x) gives a PerPhase of Evaluations, computed once per parameter vector, and optimize_pointing passes it to the objective and every constraint. A builder of an objective or constraint of one evaluation is applied to one phase through in_phase. Such builders are mode_gate, drive_floor, irradiance_ceiling, picket_ceiling, light_ceiling, drive_ceiling, drive_objective and user Constraints. A function of the whole PerPhase combines phases (ev -> w1 * f1(ev[:intact]) + w2 * f2(ev[:burned])). The clearance_floor of a keep-out depends only on the beams and takes every phase at once. power_scales extracts the power scales inside such a function. The beams of the result are a PerPhase of beam vectors, and pointing_summary reports every phase.\n\nscenario[:picket] is the Scenario of a phase, and keys(scenario) gives the names of the phases. remake(scenario; pointing, by, scaled) replaces the pointing (staging per quad, then per beam), the grouping of the scales (tied scales) or the scaled phases; the operators of every phase are kept. subset(scenario, keep) keeps some beams in every phase. For the box of a run, bounds(scenario.param, x0; power_max) caps every beam in every phase at the laser limit. A scenario of one phase whose powers are the pointing powers and not scaled (Phase(name, scenario; scaled = false)) evaluates and optimizes exactly as that Scenario does.\n\npointing = GroupPointing(beams; by = :beam, fields = (:z, :phi), relative = true)\nps = PhasedScenario(pointing,\n                    Phase(:picket, target_picket; pulses, time = 1.0),\n                    Phase(:peak, target_peak; power = 2.0, scaled = false);\n                    by = :quad, observe = :capsule)\ngates = [in_phase(:picket, mode_gate(ref[:picket], g1)), in_phase(:peak, mode_gate(ref[:peak], g2))]\nlower, upper = bounds(ps.param; power_max = 2.0)\nr = optimize_pointing(ps, in_phase(:peak, drive_objective(; drive)); constraints = gates, lower, upper)\nr.beams[:picket]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PhasedScenario"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.PowerScaleTable","page":"Optimization","text":"PowerScaleTable{K}\n\nThe power scales of a phased design per key and phase, from power_scale_table. The table has a row per key (keys, the group keys of the scales in order of first appearance) and a column per scaled phase (phases). scales[i, j] is the scale of keys[i] in phases[j], and NaN where that key has no scale in the phase. The text display prints the table and the least, mean and largest scale of each phase for the log of a study; other reports use the fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PowerScaleTable"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.apply-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{PhasedPointing, AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Optimization","text":"apply(p::PhasedPointing, x) -> PerPhase\n\nThe beams of every phase at x. They are the beams of the pointing at x[1:n], one set of aims, with the powers of each phase. In a scaled phase each power is multiplied by the scale of its group in x[n+1:end]. The element type follows x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.apply"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.bounds-4","page":"Optimization","text":"bounds(p::PhasedPointing, x0 = initial_parameters(p); scale = 0.5, scale_min = 0.0,\n       scale_max = Inf, power_max = Inf, pulses = nothing, times = nothing,\n       peak = nothing, kwargs...) -> (lower, upper)\n\nThe box of the pointing (its bounds about x0[1:n] with kwargs) for the first parameters, and a range for each power scale. Each scale lies within x0 (1 ± scale), where scale = Inf removes the relative limit, and is clipped to [scale_min, scale_max] and to the laser limit. power_max (TW per beam, a number or a function of a beam) caps every beam in every scaled phase, so a scale is at most the least power_max / P over the beams of its group in its phase. P is the phase power, or the pointing power at x0 in a phase without powers. A fixed phase with a beam above power_max is an ArgumentError, and so is an empty box (scale_min above the cap of a scale). The defaults are engineering defaults; no facility limit sets them.\n\nBetween the phases. The scale bounds limit the beams only at the times of the phases. A design is delivered as the pulse scaled_pulse (or shape_pulses) of the base pulse of each beam, with the scales of its key as multipliers at the times of the scaled phases. The multipliers are interpolated between those times, so a base pulse that rises between two phase samples can exceed power_max where neither sample does. Given pulses, times and peak, the box also includes every condition of pulse_ceiling that bounds only one scale. pulses is the base pulse of each beam, a PulseShape for every beam or a function of a beam. times is the time of each phase in ns by name, as a named tuple or dictionary, or a vector in the order of the phases. peak is the value given to scaled_pulse. The conditions on one scale are:\n\nthe pulse before the first scaled phase, with its multiplier held at the first scale;\nthe pulse after the last scaled phase, on the peak ramp to 1, or held at the last scale without peak;\non each interval between two scaled phases, the cap that follows with the neighboring scale at its least value in the box.\n\nNo design whose pulses stay within power_max is excluded by these caps. The conditions that couple two neighboring scales, for the multiplier between their times, are linear in both, and no box can express them; pulse_ceiling imposes them as a constraint. The power of each scaled phase must be the base pulse at the phase time. Otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown, since the multipliers would scale another pulse. Without pulses the caps are those at the phase times, and scaled_pulse(...; power_max) checks a pulse table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.evaluate-Tuple{PhasedScenario, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Optimization","text":"evaluate(scenario::PhasedScenario, x) / evaluate(scenario::PhasedScenario, beams::PerPhase)\n    -> PerPhase\n\nOne Evaluation per phase, of the beams of each phase in the scenario of that phase. The beams are apply(scenario.param, x), or beams, a PerPhase of beam vectors with the phases of the scenario in order. Generic in the number type. No mutable state is shared between the evaluations, so concurrent gradient chunks can evaluate them independently.\n\nSome phases are deposited in one pass: those whose deposition operators have the same points (one operator, or operators built alike on targets that differ only in their materials) and whose effective beams differ only in power. The beam profile is then evaluated once per point and scaled by the beam powers of each phase, and the deposition of each phase equals a separate deposit of that phase within rounding. A phase is deposited separately when its effective beams differ from those of the other phases in aim, spot or focus, when the points of its operator differ, or when its laser–plasma model deposits light itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.evaluate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.in_phase-Tuple{Symbol, Constraint}","page":"Optimization","text":"in_phase(phase, constraint::Constraint) -> Constraint\nin_phase(phase, f) -> function\n\nA constraint or an objective of one Evaluation, applied to the phase phase (by name) of the evaluations of a PhasedScenario. The function and labels of the constraint use ev[phase] of the PerPhase evaluation, and its name becomes \"<phase>.<name>\" (e.g. picket.mode_gate[l = 2] in the reports). The exact value of an exact ceiling (irradiance_ceiling, picket_ceiling) is kept. Any other function f of an evaluation (an objective) becomes ev -> f(ev[phase]).\n\ngate = in_phase(:picket, mode_gate(reference[:picket], gates))\nobjective = in_phase(:peak, drive_objective(; drive))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.in_phase"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.initial_parameters-Tuple{PhasedPointing}","page":"Optimization","text":"initial_parameters(p::PhasedPointing) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe initial parameters of the pointing, then a scale of 1 per (group, phase).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.initial_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.nparams-Tuple{PhasedPointing}","page":"Optimization","text":"Number of parameters of a PhasedPointing: the pointing's plus one per power scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nparams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.parameter_names-Tuple{PhasedPointing}","page":"Optimization","text":"Names of the parameters of a PhasedPointing. The names of the pointing come first (\"x[i]\" for a pointing without names), then \"<key>.power_scale.<phase>\" per scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.parameters-Tuple{PhasedPointing, PerPhase}","page":"Optimization","text":"parameters(p::PhasedPointing, beams::PerPhase) -> Vector{Float64}\nparameters(p::PhasedPointing, values::Pair...; group = (field = value, ...), ...)\n    -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe parameter vector of p that reproduces beams, a PerPhase of beam vectors. An example is the beams of a phased result in another parameterization, when staging from quads to beams or to tied scales. The parameters of the pointing come from the beams of the first phase (parameters(pointing, beams), matched by name), since the aims are the same in every phase. Each scale is the mean, over the beams of its group, of the beam power over the phase power. Beams at zero phase power are left out, and a scale with no other beams is 1. Every scaled phase must be among beams.\n\nWith named values, as for PowerScales, the fields of the pointing are passed to the pointing, and the field power_scale of a key sets its scales. Its value is a number for every scaled phase or a named tuple by phase, \"Q31T\" => (z = 0.1, power_scale = (picket = 1.1, rise = 0.95)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.phase_beams-Tuple{PhasedPointing, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Optimization","text":"phase_beams(p, beams; scale = 1) -> PerPhase\n\nThe beams beams in every phase of p, a PhasedScenario or its PhasedPointing. beams are beams of the pointing in its order, which set the aims, spots and focus. Their powers are those of each phase, multiplied by scale in the scaled phases; scale is a number or a function (beam, phase) -> scale. A reference pointing is evaluated over the same phases with evaluate(scenario, phase_beams(scenario, reference_beams)). In a phase whose powers are those of the pointing, the beam powers are kept and multiplied by the scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.phase_beams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.phase_powers-Tuple{PhasedPointing, Symbol}","page":"Optimization","text":"phase_powers(p, phase) -> Union{Vector{Float64},Nothing}\n\nThe powers of the phase named phase of p before its power scales, in TW per beam of the pointing and in its order. p is a PhasedScenario or its PhasedPointing. The powers are the power of the Phase, or its pulses at its time, and nothing for a phase whose powers are those of the pointing. The result is a copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.phase_powers"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.pointing_summary-Tuple{PhasedScenario, PerPhase}","page":"Optimization","text":"pointing_summary(scenario::PhasedScenario, beams::PerPhase; reference = the beams of the start,\n                 keepout = nothing, inner = (:inner23, :inner30), drive_reference = nothing,\n                 lmax = 4) -> PerPhase\npointing_summary(scenario::PhasedScenario, result::PointingResult; kwargs...)\n\nA PointingSummary per phase, of the beams of each phase in its scenario against the reference beams. reference is a PerPhase of beam vectors, by default the start of the scenario apply(scenario.param, initial_parameters(scenario.param)), or one beam vector for every phase. The drive is compared with drive_reference[phase] where given, as a PerPhase, a named tuple or a dictionary by phase of evaluations or coefficient vectors; a phase without one has no drive ratio. The display is one line per phase.\n\ngates and floor show the departure of each phase against its gates and its pointing floor, as for one Scenario. They are given per phase (a PerPhase, a named tuple or a dictionary by phase), or as one value for every phase (a vector, a mode_uncertainty result, a mode_gate). Nothing is shown for a phase without an entry. gates may also be the constraints of the run, a vector or tuple such as the constraints passed to optimize_pointing. The summary of each phase then shows its in_phase(phase, mode_gate(...)), from the reference shape of that gate. moved and moved_by count the groups moved past each shift threshold, as for one scenario. The phases share the aims, so the counts are the same in every phase.\n\npointing_summary(ps, r; drive_reference = refs, gates = constraints, floor = floors)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pointing_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.power_scale_objective-Tuple{PhasedScenario}","page":"Optimization","text":"power_scale_objective(scenario::PhasedScenario; reference = 1, weight = b -> 1.0)\n\nAn objective term for optimize_pointing on a phased scenario. The term is the beam-weighted mean squared relative change of the power scales from reference,\n\nΣ_j W_j (s_j / s0_j - 1)² / Σ_j W_j,\n\nover the scales s_j of power_scales, one per (key, phase) of scenario.param.keys. W_j is the summed weight of the beams that the scale applies to, by default their number. With unit weights the term is the mean, over every beam and scaled phase, of the squared relative change of the beam scale. A scale shared by a cone therefore counts as many times as the cone has beams, and a scale per quad as many times as the quad has beams. The term regularizes a re-pointing whose powers should stay near a reference drive. It is added to an objective, ev -> J(ev) + w * reg(ev) with reg = power_scale_objective(ps; reference = s0).\n\nreference is one scale for every key, a vector in the order of the keys (for example power_scales(ps, x0) of a start), or a function (key, phase) -> scale. Its values must be positive and finite. weight is a function of a beam of the pointing (of its group, quad or name), evaluated once at construction on the beams of the pointing. The weights must be finite and non-negative with a positive sum. The scales are taken from the evaluations as in power_scales, so every scaled phase needs explicit powers (a power or pulses in its Phase). Generic in the number type and thread-safe, since it does not modify its tables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.power_scale_objective"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.power_scale_table-Tuple{PhasedPointing, PointingResult}","page":"Optimization","text":"power_scale_table(p, result::PointingResult) -> PowerScaleTable\npower_scale_table(p, x::AbstractVector) / power_scale_table(p, beams_or_ev::PerPhase)\n\nThe power scales of a phased design as a PowerScaleTable in Float64, with the values of any duals. p is a PhasedScenario or its PhasedPointing. The design is given as a result, a parameter vector, or the evaluations or beams that power_scales accepts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.power_scale_table"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.power_scales-Tuple{PhasedPointing, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Optimization","text":"power_scales(p, ev::PerPhase) -> Vector\npower_scales(p, beams::PerPhase) -> Vector\npower_scales(p, x::AbstractVector) -> Vector\n\nThe power scales of a phased design, one per (key, phase) of p.param.keys in that order, where p is a PhasedScenario or its PhasedPointing. They are the parameters x[n+1:end] by which apply multiplies the powers of the phases. From a PerPhase of evaluations (their setup beams, ev[phase].beams) or of beam vectors (in the order of the pointing, as apply and evaluate give them), each scale is the power of a beam over its phase power (phase_powers). The beam used is the first beam of the key with positive phase power. A key whose beams are all at zero power in the phase has no scale to recover and gives 1, as in parameters. A scaled phase whose powers are the pointing powers (no power or pulses in its Phase) has no phase power to divide by, and an ArgumentError is thrown; the scales of such a phase come from x.\n\nGeneric in the number type and thread-safe. Inside an objective of the PerPhase evaluation the scales are duals of the optimizer, for example in a regularization of the scales toward s0 (a vector in the order of the keys), ev -> J(ev) + w * sum(abs2, power_scales(ps, ev) ./ s0 .- 1) / length(s0). power_scale_table gives them per key and phase for a report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.power_scales"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.pulse_ceiling-Tuple{PhasedPointing}","page":"Optimization","text":"pulse_ceiling(p; pulses, times, power_max, peak = nothing, name = :pulse) -> Constraint\n\nA ceiling on the pulses of a phased design between the phase samples as well as at them. The Constraint requires every point of the scaled_pulse of each beam to stay at or below power_max. That pulse is the base pulse of the beam, with the power scales of its key as multipliers at the times of the scaled phases, ramped to 1 at peak. p is a PhasedScenario or its PhasedPointing. pulses is the base pulse of each beam, a PulseShape for every beam or a function of a beam. times is the time of each phase in ns by name, as a named tuple or dictionary, or a vector in the order of the phases. power_max is the limit in TW per beam (a number, or a function of a beam), and peak the time given to scaled_pulse. The power of each scaled phase must be the base pulse at the phase time, or an ArgumentError is thrown. Every scaled phase needs powers, a power or pulses in the Phase, since the constraint takes the scales from the beams of the evaluation (power_scales). The constraint is generic in the number type.\n\nThe written pulse is linear between its points, which are the points of the base pulse and the phase times, so its largest power is at a point. At a point τ between the scaled phases at tᵢ and tᵢ₊₁, the multiplier is (1 − w) sᵢ + w sᵢ₊₁ with w = (τ − tᵢ)/(tᵢ₊₁ − tᵢ). The condition P(τ) ((1 − w) sᵢ + w sᵢ₊₁) ≤ power_max is therefore linear in the two scales. Before the first phase and after the last, the condition bounds one scale; on the peak ramp the multiplier runs to 1. Of the points between two phases, only those on the lower convex hull of (w, power_max/P(τ)) are kept, since their conditions imply those at the other points. The constraint is therefore exact, with a few components per key and interval: a design meets it if and only if its written pulses stay within power_max. The pulse from peak on is the base pulse, which lies outside the constraint; scaled_pulse(...; power_max) checks it.\n\nThe value of a component is the written power at its point (TW), and its limit is power_max. The label gives the key and the time (pulse[outer, 4.35 ns]), and the default scale makes the violation relative to the limit. bounds(p.param; power_max, pulses, times, peak) imposes exactly, as a box, the components that bound only one scale. With both, the augmented Lagrangian includes only the coupled components, each to within the feasibility of the run (1e-3 of the limit by default), so a written pulse can end that far over the limit. With a limit lowered by the feasibility, power_max = (1 - 1e-3) * L, the pulse stays within L.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pulse_ceiling"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.remake-Tuple{PhasedScenario}","page":"Optimization","text":"remake(scenario::PhasedScenario; pointing, by, scaled, deposition_kw) -> PhasedScenario\n\nCopy of a phased scenario with another pointing, another grouping by of the power scales, or other scaled phases (by name). The new pointing is a parameterization of the same beams, whose ports the deposition operators of the phases serve, as when staging per quad, then per beam. A function that gives several quads one key ties their scales. The operators, radiation model and response of every phase are kept, and the powers of the phases are set again on the beams of the new pointing.\n\nWith deposition_kw, the deposition operator of every phase is rebuilt with these options for the beams of the pointing, as remake(scenario; deposition_kw) does for one Scenario. Options not given take their defaults, including those in the deposition_kw of a phase. The target, radiation model, response, laser–plasma model and time of each phase are kept. An example is a finished design checked on a finer operator, evaluate(remake(ps; deposition_kw = (; resolution = 0.3)), r.x). With pointing as well, the operators are built for the beams of the new pointing, so its ports need not be the old ones. Phases that shared one operator share the rebuilt one. A phase on a target with the same mesh as an earlier phase (a target that differs only in its materials) is given the rebuilt operator of that phase on its target, so that phases on one mesh are still deposited in one pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.remake"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.subset-Tuple{PhasedScenario, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Optimization","text":"subset(scenario::PhasedScenario, keep; pointing = the pointing subset with the same groups)\n\nThe phased scenario of the beams keep (indices into the beams of the pointing) in every phase. The scenario of each phase is restricted as by subset, with its view factors and response shared. The powers of the phases are restricted to the kept beams, and the grouping of the scales is kept. The default pointing has the same groups, and so the same parameter names, as the pointing of the scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.subset"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.PulseShapeGroups","page":"Optimization","text":"PulseShapeGroups\n\nMultipliers per key and phase grouped into shapes, from pulse_shape_groups. Fields:\n\nkeys: the keys (e.g. quad names), in the order of the input;\nnames: the shape names, Symbol(class, i) per class (:shape1, … without classes), numbered within a class by increasing mean log multiplier;\nclass: the class of each shape;\nmultipliers: shapes × phases, the multiplier of each shape per phase, the geometric mean of those of its members;\nmembers: the keys of each shape, in input order;\nassignment: a Dict from each key to the name of its shape;\nrms: the root-mean-square residual of the fit in the natural log of the multipliers, over every key and phase;\nsplit: the number of shapes per class, as class => n pairs in order of first appearance; splits: every admissible split of the chosen number of shapes (counts per class in that order) and its rms;\ncounts: each number of shapes tried and the rms of its best split, in increasing order (only shapes when no tolerance is given);\nexact: true when the grouping of every class is the exact least-squares optimum (by enumeration or, with one phase, by dynamic programming), false where seeded restarts found it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PulseShapeGroups"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.pulse_shape_groups-Tuple{AbstractMatrix{<:Real}, AbstractVector}","page":"Optimization","text":"pulse_shape_groups(multipliers::AbstractMatrix, keys; shapes, tolerance = nothing,\n                   class = nothing, seed = 1, restarts = 100,\n                   exact_limit = 10^6) -> PulseShapeGroups\npulse_shape_groups(multipliers::AbstractDict; phases = nothing, kwargs...)\n\nGroup per-key power multipliers over the phases of a pulse into shapes distinct pulse shapes. The grouping is k-means on the natural log of the multipliers, with one coordinate per phase for the point of each key, so that the multiplier of a shape per phase is the geometric mean of those of its members and the fit is least squares in the relative error. Rows of the matrix are keys (e.g. quad names) and columns phases; a dictionary maps each key to its vector of multipliers per phase, or, with phases (the phases in order), each (key, phase) pair to a multiplier, the layout of power scales keyed per quad and phase. Keys of a dictionary are sorted (by their string) for a deterministic order. Every multiplier must be positive.\n\nclass is a function of the key, a dictionary or a vector aligned with the keys, and nothing means one class. It separates keys that never share a shape, such as the quads of the inner and the outer cones, which follow different base pulses. Each class is grouped separately, with at least one shape and at most one per key, and the shapes are split between the classes by the smallest total squared error over every admissible split (ties to the first in order).\n\ntolerance (an rms in the natural log of the multipliers; nothing, the default, for a fixed count) makes shapes an upper bound: the result is the smallest number of shapes, from one per class up to shapes, whose best split fits with an rms at or below tolerance, or shapes when none does (counts lists the rms of each number tried). Shapes that differ by less than the laser delivers distinctly need not be separate pulses. The following figures give a scale only; the default tolerance is nothing. NIF's laser performance model predicts total energies \"within 2–3 percent\" and gives \"beam-to-beam energy balance better than 5 percent for full system shots\" (NIF User Guide, section 4.8, Laser Performance Operations Model, https://nifuserguide.llnl.gov/home/4-laser-system/48-laser-performance-operations-model). The page does not give a statistic for the balance. A relative error ε is about ε in the log, so 0.02–0.05 spans those figures. The tolerance is left to the study.\n\nEach class of n keys is grouped into k groups exactly where the size allows. Every partition into k groups is enumerated when there are at most exact_limit of them (the Stirling number S(n, k)), and dynamic programming over the sorted values is used when there is a single phase (an optimal one-dimensional grouping is contiguous). Otherwise the best of restarts k-means++ seedings is taken, each refined by single-key transfers until no transfer lowers the squared error (Hartigan's rule, which reaches a local optimum at least as good as Lloyd's). The seedings are drawn from a SplitMix64 stream started at seed, so the result is the same on every Julia version and machine. Restarts are a heuristic: the result is the global optimum only with high probability (it matched exhaustive enumeration on every small case tested).\n\nshape_pulses writes the shapes out as pulses, and the assignment is used to tie the power scales of a re-optimization per shape (PowerScales with by = b -> groups.assignment[b.quad]).\n\ninner = Set(b.quad for b in beams if b.group in (:inner23, :inner30))\ng = pulse_shape_groups(m, quad_names; shapes = 6, class = q -> q in inner ? :inner : :outer)\ng.split, g.rms, g.assignment[\"Q15T\"]\n# the fewest shapes, up to 8, that fit within 3% rms\ng3 = pulse_shape_groups(m, quad_names; shapes = 8, tolerance = 0.03,\n                        class = q -> q in inner ? :inner : :outer)\nlength(g3.names), g3.counts\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pulse_shape_groups"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.scaled_pulse-Tuple{PulseShape, Any, Any}","page":"Optimization","text":"scaled_pulse(base::PulseShape, times, multipliers; peak = nothing, power_max = nothing)\n    -> PulseShape\n\nThe pulse base with its power multiplied by a multiplier given per phase. The multiplier is multipliers[i] at times[i] (ns, increasing), interpolated linearly between the phase times and held at the first and last values outside them. The result is tabulated at the union of base's times and the phase times (inside base's span), and each tabulated power is the power of the base times the multiplier at that point. Between those points the result is linear, as every PulseShape is. The exact product of two linear pieces is quadratic, so the table contains the multiplier applied at the points; the exact product is not tabulated.\n\nWith peak, a time after the last phase, the pulse is left unscaled from that time on: the multiplier then varies linearly from its value at the last phase to 1 between the last phase time and peak, and is 1 after it. This is the treatment for a peak at the laser limit, which is left unscaled. The pulse is sampled only at the phase times, and the ramp between the last phase and the peak is an assumption of this function (it is not fitted).\n\npower_max (TW per beam) checks the result against the laser limit: a pulse above it anywhere (at the table points, where its maximum lies) is an ArgumentError naming the time, the power and the excess. Multipliers within the limit at the phase times can still exceed it between them, where the base pulse rises. pulse_ceiling(scenario; pulses, times, power_max, peak) holds this pulse within the limit as a constraint of a phased design, and bounds(scenario.param; power_max, pulses, times, peak) gives the conditions on a single scale as a box. A table is not clipped: clipping would change the energy of the pulse and the drive the design was optimized for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.scaled_pulse"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/optimization/#Briareus.shape_pulses-Tuple{PulseShapeGroups, Any, Any}","page":"Optimization","text":"shape_pulses(groups::PulseShapeGroups, base, times; peak = nothing, power_max = nothing)\n    -> Dict{Symbol,PulseShape}\n\nThe pulse of each shape of groups (pulse_shape_groups), keyed by the shape name. Each pulse is scaled_pulse of the base pulse of its class with the multipliers of the shape at the phase times (and peak). base is one PulseShape for every class, or a dictionary or function from class to pulse (e.g. the pulses of the inner and the outer cones). write_pulse_table writes the result as a table, one column per shape, and with_pulses applies it to beams keyed by groups.assignment. power_max (a number, or like base one per class) checks every shape against the laser limit as scaled_pulse does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.shape_pulses"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#From-drive-asymmetry-to-implosion-metrics","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"Briareus computes the x-ray flux on the capsule. What NIF measures is the implosion: the velocity of the hot spot from the Doppler shift of its neutrons (nToF), its shape in x-ray and neutron images, and the yield. Published transfer functions connect the two for the low modes. This tutorial uses them through a thin layer of reduced models:\n\nthe mode 1 of the flux as a hot-spot velocity (hotspot_velocity), statically or weighted over a drive_history with a Green's function (ResponseKernel);\nthe yield factors of the piston model (implosion_metrics, mode1_tolerance);\nP2 swing metrics over a pulse (swing_metrics);\nbeam errors as a velocity budget (velocity_uncertainty), the inverse from a measured velocity (infer_velocity_errors), and a velocity penalty for objectives (VelocityPenalty).\n\nEach model is a published fit for particular NIF platforms. The sensitivities are engineering defaults, to be replaced with design-specific numbers. Sources and confidence are in reference/research-notes.md (\"Implosion metrics\").\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nusing Random\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\nset_theme!(fontsize = 15, linewidth = 2.5)","title":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#The-hohlraum","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"The hohlraum and pointing are those of the Pointing and power errors tutorial, on a coarser mesh: 10.1 × 5.75 mm, with 3.1 mm entrance holes, a 0.9 mm capsule and 48 quads at 8 TW.\n\ntarget = hohlraum(length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9,\n                  wall = gold(albedo = 0.85, conversion = 0.8), h = 0.6, capsule_h = 0.2)\np = GroupPointing(nif_quads(power = 8.0); fields = (:z, :r))\nexample = Dict(:outer50 => (5.01, 0.0), :outer44 => (5.20, 0.0),\n               :inner30 => (3.63, -0.60), :inner23 => (3.09, -1.09))\nquads = apply(p, reduce(vcat, [[example[k]...] for k in p.keys]))\nscenario = Scenario(target, quads; observe = :capsule, viewfactor_kw = (; closed = true))\n@printf(\"%d elements, %d quads\\n\", nelements(target), length(quads))\n1428 elements, 48 quads","title":"The hohlraum"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#Mode-1-as-a-hot-spot-velocity","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"A dipole drive 1 + varepsiloncosgamma pushes the shell harder on its bright side, so the hot spot moves away from it (Casey et al., PRL 126, 025002, 2021). The published sensitivity is about 100 km/s per percent of varepsilon over the rise and peak. Young et al. (Phys. Plasmas 27, 082702, 2020) found 0.44% of rise-and-peak mode 1 on N170601. They predicted 44.7 km/s from view factors and their Green's function, while 3D HYDRA gave 37.6 km/s. Kritcher et al. (2024) put about 0.7% on N230729, for 70–75 km/s without burn. hotspot_velocity uses HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT = 100 km/s per % by default. Its input is mode1_vector, sqrt3(c_11 c_1-1 c_10)c_00, whose norm is mode_amplitude(c, 1) (the varepsilon of the papers). It is tested here on a sphere lit with a known dipole:\n\nsphere = Target(Component(:s, icosphere_mesh(1.0; n = 8), gold()))\nW = ylm_weights(sphere, 1:nelements(sphere); lmax = 1)\nn̂ = normalize([0.3, -0.5, 0.8])\ndipole(ε) = W * [1 + ε * dot(n̂, normalize(sphere.elem_centroid[e])) for e in 1:nelements(sphere)]\nfor (case, ε, published_v) in ((\"Young N170601\", 0.0044, \"37.6 (3D HYDRA), 44.7 (VF + G)\"),\n                               (\"Kritcher N230729\", 0.007, \"70–75 (burn-off)\"),\n                               (\"Spears 2014, peak\", 0.02, \"120 (saturated)\"))\n    v = hotspot_velocity(dipole(ε))\n    @printf(\"%-18s %.2f%%: %5.1f km/s, direction · n̂ = %+.4f; published %s\\n\", case,\n            100ε, norm(v), dot(normalize(v), n̂), published_v)\nend\nYoung N170601      0.44%:  44.0 km/s, direction · n̂ = -1.0000; published 37.6 (3D HYDRA), 44.7 (VF + G)\nKritcher N230729   0.70%:  69.9 km/s, direction · n̂ = -1.0000; published 70–75 (burn-off)\nSpears 2014, peak  2.00%: 199.8 km/s, direction · n̂ = -1.0000; published 120 (saturated)\n\nThe first two land within the published ranges. Spears et al. (Phys. Plasmas 21, 042702, 2014) applied 2% in 2D and got 120 km/s. The linear rule gives 200 km/s there, beyond its range: Young found the response linear only below about 0.75%. Treat velocities above ~80 km/s from this rule as upper bounds.","title":"Mode 1 as a hot-spot velocity"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#A-dropped-quad-and-a-displaced-target","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"Losing the power of one quad leaves a mode 1 of a few percent, past the linear range, but the comparison between cones still holds. Young et al. found that a dropped inner quad gives about twice the velocity of a dropped outer one on N170827:\n\nfor g in (:inner23, :inner30, :outer44, :outer50)\n    vs = [norm(hotspot_velocity(evaluate(scenario, [j == i ? remake(b; power = 0.0) : b\n                                                    for (j, b) in enumerate(quads)])))\n          for (i, q) in enumerate(quads) if q.group === g]\n    @printf(\"%-8s quad dropped: %4.0f km/s (%.1f%% mode 1)\\n\", g, sum(vs) / length(vs),\n            sum(vs) / length(vs) / 100)\nend\ninner23  quad dropped:  190 km/s (1.9% mode 1)\ninner30  quad dropped:  217 km/s (2.2% mode 1)\nouter44  quad dropped:  150 km/s (1.5% mode 1)\nouter50  quad dropped:  139 km/s (1.4% mode 1)\n\nHere the inner quads give 1.3–1.6 times the velocity of the outer quads, against Young's\n\nThe difference comes from this hohlraum and not from the model. Young's setup is the\n\nstatic model of N170827: an 11.3 × 5.27 mm hohlraum with radii at 85%, albedo 0.8, the requested pulses and the digitized Green's function young2020_kernel. On it Briareus gives 336, 349, 161 and 152 km/s for the four cones against Young's 317, 337, 170 and 146, a ratio of 2.2 against 2.07 (test suite; research notes, \"Young 2020 validation\"). The same paper gives the sensitivity to a whole-target offset from chamber center: 1.3 km/s per µm vertical and 0.1 km/s per µm horizontal. The positioning error of mode_uncertainty, carried to a velocity, gives the same derivative:\n\nfor (axis, d) in ((\"vertical (z)\", (0.0, 0.0, 1.0)), (\"horizontal (x)\", (1.0, 0.0, 0.0)),\n                  (\"horizontal (y)\", (0.0, 1.0, 0.0)))\n    u = velocity_uncertainty(scenario, quads; pointing = 0.0, positioning = d)\n    @printf(\"target offset %-15s %.2f km/s per µm\\n\", axis, u.rms / 1000)\nend\ntarget offset vertical (z)    0.99 km/s per µm\ntarget offset horizontal (x)  0.60 km/s per µm\ntarget offset horizontal (y)  0.61 km/s per µm\n\nThe vertical number is within 25% of Young's; the horizontal ones are six times larger. A horizontal offset moves the inner and outer footprints so that their mode 1 opposes, and how well they cancel depends on the hohlraum and pointing: on Young's N170827 setup Briareus gets 0.09 and 1.30 km/s per µm (Young 0.1 and 1.3), and 0.10 and 1.07 for the capsule moved inside the hohlraum (Young 0.1 and 1.1). The derivative for another platform should be computed before it is used in a tolerance.","title":"A dropped quad and a displaced target"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#The-piston-model","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"Hurricane et al. (Phys. Plasmas 27, 062704, 2020) treat mode 1 as two pistons of unequal areal density. The velocity of the hot spot over the implosion velocity, f = v_HSv_imp, is also the ρR asymmetry of the shell. They give:\n\nthe residual kinetic energy mathrmRKE = c f^2mathrmKE, with c approx 066;\nthe no-α yield factor (1-f^2)^a, with a = 33;\nan α-heating factor exp(-(a-1)chi^12 f^2), where chi is the 1D generalized Lawson parameter.\n\nTheir Table I lists the f and v_HS allowed for 5–20% yield loss at v_imp = 380 km/s, \"near ignition\" (chi approx 1–2). mode1_tolerance inverts the loss. At chi = 2 it reproduces the table:\n\nprintln(\"ΔY/Y   f (Table I)   v_HS (Table I)\")\nfor (loss, f, v) in ((0.05, 0.08, 30), (0.10, 0.11, 40), (0.15, 0.14, 50), (0.20, 0.16, 60))\n    tol = mode1_tolerance(loss; vimp = 380, chi = 2.0)\n    @printf(\"%.2f   %.3f (%.2f)   %4.1f km/s (%d)\\n\", loss, tol.f, f, tol.velocity, v)\nend\nΔY/Y   f (Table I)   v_HS (Table I)\n0.05   0.077 (0.08)   29.4 km/s (30)\n0.10   0.111 (0.11)   42.0 km/s (40)\n0.15   0.137 (0.14)   52.2 km/s (50)\n0.20   0.161 (0.16)   61.1 km/s (60)\n\nAt chi = 15 the 5% row would allow f = 0.09. The chi of the table is about 2. implosion_metrics collects the factors for one velocity:\n\nm = implosion_metrics([0.0, 0.0, 75.0]; vimp = 380, chi = 1.0)\n@printf(\"75 km/s: f = %.3f, RKE/KE = %.3f, no-α yield ×%.3f, α ×%.3f, total ×%.3f\\n\",\n        m.f, m.rke_fraction, m.yield_factor, m.alpha_factor, m.total)\n75 km/s: f = 0.197, RKE/KE = 0.026, no-α yield ×0.877, α ×0.914, total ×0.802\n\nα heating also amplifies the measured velocity. Nora et al. (Phys. Plasmas 30, 092705, 2023) fit this as burn_amplification(Y_amp), about 1.8 at Y_amp = 30 (their Table IV divides N210808's measured speed by 1.75). Kritcher et al.'s burn-on/burn-off simulations give 1.27–1.39 for the 2022–23 shots, so the fit is best read as an upper estimate. The stagnated shape changes it too: shape_amplification(P2, θ) is Nora's 3D table, about 0.8–1.2 for P_2  04. Their product is passed as amplification for a comparison with an nToF measurement.","title":"The piston model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#A-velocity-budget-for-the-errors","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"velocity_uncertainty carries mode_uncertainty to the hot-spot velocity: its 3 × 3 covariance, its rms by error source, and the expected yield factor over the errors. For NIF's 50 µm pointing, 3% power imbalance, and 30 µm target positioning:\n\nub = velocity_uncertainty(scenario, quads; pointing = 0.05, power = 0.03, positioning = 0.03,\n                          vimp = 380, chi = 1.0)\n@printf(\"velocity rms %.0f km/s (beams %.0f, positioning %.0f); per axis %s km/s\\n\", ub.rms,\n        ub.rms_by.beam, ub.rms_by.positioning, join((@sprintf(\"%.0f\", x) for x in ub.sigma), \", \"))\n@printf(\"expected yield factor: no α %.3f, with α (χ = 1) %.3f\\n\", ub.expected_yield, ub.expected_total)\nvelocity rms 53 km/s (beams 48, positioning 23); per axis 27, 27, 38 km/s\nexpected yield factor: no α 0.939, with α (χ = 1) 0.900\n\nMacGowan et al. (HEDP 40, 100944, 2021) found each source to be about 0.5% of mode 1 (~50 km/s), and the sources together up to 100 km/s. The expectation uses (1-f^2)^a approx e^-a f^2, whose Gaussian average has a closed form. A Monte Carlo of full evaluations checks it (the test suite does the same at a smaller v_imp, where the factor is far from 1):\n\nsamples = sample_modes(scenario, quads; pointing = 0.05, power = 0.03, positioning = 0.03,\n                       n = 200, lmax = 1, rng = MersenneTwister(1))\nvmc = [hotspot_velocity(samples[:, k]) for k in axes(samples, 2)]\n@printf(\"Monte Carlo: velocity rms %.0f km/s, mean yield factor %.3f (exact (1 − f²)^3.3: %.3f)\\n\",\n        sqrt(sum(v -> sum(abs2, v), vmc) / length(vmc)),\n        sum(v -> exp(-3.3 * sum(abs2, v) / 380^2), vmc) / length(vmc),\n        sum(v -> yield_factor(norm(v) / 380), vmc) / length(vmc))\nMonte Carlo: velocity rms 53 km/s, mean yield factor 0.939 (exact (1 − f²)^3.3: 0.938)","title":"A velocity budget for the errors"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#Weighting-the-drive-over-the-pulse","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"The static rule weights mode 1 equally at all times. Young et al. instead convolved the mode-1 history with a Green's function G_1(t) (km/s per % per ns) computed for their platform: v = -int G_1(t)100vec p_1(t)dt. Their G_1 is negative in the foot and dominated by the rise and peak. They published it only as a figure, so Briareus does not include a G_1. The default is flat_kernel: a flat weight over a window that gives 100 km/s for a constant 1%.\n\nThe test pulse has a picket, foot and peak. Each inner quad carries 0.8 of the nominal quad power in the picket and 0.3 after it; each outer quad carries 1.2 and then 1.7. In this hohlraum, equal quad powers drive the waist hot (P2/P0 ≈ −14%), and the split at the peak brings P2 near −1%. One inner quad loses a quarter of its power at 7 ns, during the peak:\n\npulse_times = [0.0, 0.5, 1.5, 2.0, 5.0, 6.5, 9.0, 9.5]\npulse(picket, rest) = PulseShape(pulse_times, [1.5picket, 2.0picket, 2.0picket,\n                                               0.6rest, 0.6rest, 8.0rest, 8.0rest, 4.0rest])\npulses = Dict(:inner23 => pulse(0.8, 0.3), :inner30 => pulse(0.8, 0.3),\n              :outer44 => pulse(1.2, 1.7), :outer50 => pulse(1.2, 1.7))\nlost = quads[3].name\nbeams_at(t) = [b.name == lost && t >= 7.0 ? remake(b; power = 0.75b.power) : b\n               for b in with_pulses(quads, pulses, t)]\ntimes = collect(0.25:0.25:9.25)\nhist = drive_history(scenario.model, scenario.deposition, beams_at, times; ylm = true, lmax = 4)\np1 = [norm(mode1_vector(hist.ylm[:, k])) for k in eachindex(hist.time)]\nP2 = hist.moments[3, :] ./ hist.moments[1, :]\n\nfig = Figure(size = (680, 340))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (ns)\", ylabel = \"% of the mean flux\",\n          title = \"Capsule flux modes through the pulse\")\nlines!(ax, hist.time, 100 .* P2; label = \"P₂/P₀\")\nlines!(ax, hist.time, 100 .* p1; label = \"mode 1 (ε₁)\")\naxislegend(ax; position = :lt)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nWith the flat kernel over the rise and peak (5–9.25 ns), the power lost at 7 ns counts for the part of the window it covers. An illustrative kernel that is negative in the foot, like Young's, changes nothing here, because the foot has no mode 1. The kernel table below is invented for illustration and is not Young's:\n\nv_flat = hotspot_velocity(hist; window = (5.0, 9.25))\nyoung_like = ResponseKernel([0.0, 4.5, 4.5, 6.0, 9.25], [-3.0, -3.0, 5.0, 40.0, 20.0])\nv_kernel = hotspot_velocity(hist; kernel = young_like)\nv_peak = hotspot_velocity(evaluate(scenario, beams_at(8.0)))\n@printf(\"static at 8 ns %.0f km/s; flat over 5–9.25 ns %.0f km/s; illustrative kernel %.0f km/s\\n\",\n        norm(v_peak), norm(v_flat), norm(v_kernel))\nstatic at 8 ns 12 km/s; flat over 5–9.25 ns 7 km/s; illustrative kernel 8 km/s\n\nThe same convolution, with a kernel for an observable, applies to P2. swing_metrics reports the time-averaged P2 over a window and its swing (max − min). It compares the average over the picket with the 7.5% of Milovich et al. (Phys. Plasmas 23, 032701, 2016), the early-time P2 that keeps margin loss under 10%:\n\nsw = swing_metrics(hist; window = (5.0, 9.25), picket = (0.25, 2.0))\n@printf(\"P2/P0 over the rise and peak: mean %+.2f%%, swing %.2f%%; picket mean %+.2f%% (within 7.5%%: %s)\\n\",\n        100sw.mean, 100sw.swing, 100sw.picket_mean, sw.picket_ok)\nwhole = swing_metrics(hist)\n@printf(\"over the whole pulse: mean %+.2f%%, swing %.2f%%, mean |P2 − mean| %.2f%%\\n\",\n        100whole.mean, 100whole.swing, 100whole.deviation)\nP2/P0 over the rise and peak: mean -1.13%, swing 0.05%; picket mean -9.26% (within 7.5%: false)\nover the whole pulse: mean -2.72%, swing 9.12%, mean |P2 − mean| 2.55%\n\nThe peak is close to round, but the picket is outside Milovich's limit. Over the whole pulse, the time average hides a swing of several percent.\n\nWithout kernels response is empty. A flux P2 history does not give a hot-spot shape in µm. Masse et al. (Phys. Plasmas 26, 2019) found that the response of the hot spot changes sign before about 4.5 ns while that of the shell ρR does not. The same flux history can therefore make an oblate hot spot and a prolate ρR. Kernels for another platform are passed as kernels = (; hotspot = …, rhoR = …).","title":"Weighting the drive over the pulse"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#Reading-the-errors-back-from-a-measured-velocity","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"infer_velocity_errors is infer_errors for a measured velocity (km/s). Its default measurement error is NIF's nToF: ±14 km/s in magnitude, 13° in θ, and 29° in φ (ntof_covariance). It adds a capsule-thickness mode 1 as a nuisance error, which moves the hot spot toward the thicker side at about 110 km/s per % of thickness (Casey et al. 2021), the opposite sign to flux. In a synthetic shot the target sits 40 µm low, and the shell is 0.3% thicker toward +x:\n\nshifted = [remake(b; aim = b.aim + [0.0, 0.0, 0.04]) for b in quads]\nv_meas = hotspot_velocity(evaluate(scenario, shifted)) + 100 * 110 * [0.003, 0.0, 0.0]\nr = infer_velocity_errors(scenario, quads, v_meas; pointing = 0.05, power = 0.03,\n                          positioning = 0.03, thickness = 0.003)\n@printf(\"measured v = (%.0f, %.0f, %.0f) km/s; χ²_ν of the nominal %.1f, against the prior spread %.2f\\n\",\n        v_meas..., r.velocity.chi2, r.velocity.chi2_prior)\n@printf(\"inferred: thickness (%+.2f, %+.2f, %+.2f)%%, target offset z %+.0f µm\\n\",\n        (100 .* r.thickness)..., 1000r.positioning[3])\nfor s in r.sources[1:3]\n    @printf(\"  %-22s explains %5.1f%%\\n\", s.source, 100s.fraction)\nend\nmeasured v = (33, 0, -39) km/s; χ²_ν of the nominal 4.5, against the prior spread 0.37\ninferred: thickness (+0.17, +0.00, -0.14)%, target offset z +4 µm\n  capsule thickness      explains  46.3%\n  positioning            explains   8.5%\n  Q33B pointing          explains   2.2%\n\nA χ^2_ν of 4.5 shows that the nominal design does not explain the shot. Against the spread the priors allow, 0.37, the shot is unremarkable. The inverse then shares the blame by prior size. Three numbers do not separate a thicker shell from a brighter opposite side. Here it puts about half the true thickness on x, a spurious thickness on z that stands in for the offset, and only 4 µm of the 40 µm offset. With the errors of the beams known to be small (zero prior), the offset and the thickness still compete along z. The separation has to come from capsule metrology (a tighter thickness prior):\n\nfor σt in (0.003, 0.0005)\n    rk = infer_velocity_errors(scenario, quads, v_meas; pointing = 0.0, positioning = 0.05, thickness = σt)\n    @printf(\"beams known, thickness prior %.2f%%: thickness (%+.2f, %+.2f, %+.2f)%%, offset z %+.0f µm\\n\",\n            100σt, (100 .* rk.thickness)..., 1000rk.positioning[3])\nend\nbeams known, thickness prior 0.30%: thickness (+0.21, +0.00, -0.19)%, offset z +15 µm\nbeams known, thickness prior 0.05%: thickness (+0.02, +0.00, -0.01)%, offset z +30 µm\n\nA tight prior about zero recovers 30 of the 40 µm, but it also hides the real 0.3% thickness along x, which then shows up in the residual. The prior is centered on zero, so a measured thickness mode belongs in the nominal. Subtract its velocity, 100 * 110 * t, from the measurement before the inverse.","title":"Reading the errors back from a measured velocity"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#A-velocity-penalty-for-objectives","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"VelocityPenalty adds wv^2s^2 to an objective. Mode 1 is then weighted by its velocity, with a weight that differs from that of the other modes. With uncertainty, it also adds the expected delta v^2 of the errors. It is generic in the number type, so optimize_pointing differentiates it:\n\npenalty = VelocityPenalty(; scale = 20.0, uncertainty = (; pointing = 0.05, power = 0.03))\nsymmetry = asymmetry_objective(; modes = (2 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0))\nobjective(ev) = symmetry(ev) + penalty(ev)\nev = evaluate(scenario, quads)\n@printf(\"objective %.4f = modes %.2e + velocity %.4f (rms %.0f km/s over 20 km/s, squared)\\n\",\n        objective(ev), symmetry(ev), penalty(ev), sqrt(penalty(ev)) * 20)\nobjective 5.8240 = modes 4.18e-03 + velocity 5.8199 (rms 48 km/s over 20 km/s, squared)","title":"A velocity penalty for objectives"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/implosion_metrics/#What-this-checks","page":"From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics","text":"A dipole drive of 0.44% gives 44 km/s, between Young et al.'s 37.6 km/s (3D HYDRA) and 44.7 km/s (view factors and Green's function) for N170601. 0.7% gives 70 km/s against Kritcher et al.'s 70–75 km/s burn-off for N230729. The hot spot moves away from the bright side to 1e-6 in direction. Spears et al.'s saturated 120 km/s at 2% is over-predicted (200 km/s), as the linear range requires (test suite).\nmode1_tolerance at chi = 2 reproduces Hurricane et al.'s Table I: f to two digits, and v_HS within the 10 km/s rounding of the table (29.4/42.0/52.2/61.1 against 30/40/50/60). Casey et al.'s a = 6 - 15ln T gives Hurricane's 3.3 at 6 keV (test suite).\nOn this hohlraum the dropped inner/outer quad ratio is 1.3–1.6 and the horizontal target offset ~0.6 km/s/µm, against Young's ~2 and 0.1 for N170827. On Young's static N170827 setup with his digitized pulses and Green's function, Briareus reproduces Fig. 10a's mode-1 histories to 0.05–0.21% rms, the dropped-quad velocities within 6% (ratio 2.2 against 2.07), and the four offset sensitivities (0.09/1.30 target, 0.10/1.07 capsule, against 0.1/1.3 and 0.1/1.1 km/s/µm) (test suite).\nKernel integrals are exact for tabulated kernels against hand integration (a step kernel on steps that miss its breakpoints), and match a 2×10⁵-point midpoint rule for a function kernel. A constant drive history reproduces the static velocity to 1e-6. Swing metrics match dense sampling to 1e-6 (test suite).\nThe velocity covariance matches a Monte Carlo of full evaluations to 8%. Its closed-form expected yield factor matches the Monte Carlo mean to 3% (5% with α) at a v_imp where the factor is far from 1 (test suite).\nThe velocity inverse recovers a 20 µm vertical target offset from the velocity of a full evaluation to 5%, and the thickness of a thickness-only shot to 1%. The dual-number gradient of the penalty matches central differences to 1e-5, and its uncertainty term equals the propagated variance to 1e-8 (test suite).\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/analysis/#Mode-analysis","page":"Mode analysis","text":"Legendre moments (legendre_moments) and real spherical harmonics (ylm_moments, flux_ylm) are integrated over the exact solid angle of each facet; mode_amplitude gives the fractional amplitude of each mode (the mode-1 drive asymmetry, for example). energy_balance accounts for the source, wall and capsule absorption, LEH loss and leakage, and radiation_temperature converts flux to temperature.","title":"Mode analysis"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.legendre-Tuple{Integer, Any}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"legendre(l, μ)\n\nLegendre polynomial Pₗ(μ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.legendre"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.legendre_moments-Tuple{AbstractVector, Target, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"legendre_moments(values, target, elems; lmax = 8, axis = ẑ, center = mode_center(target, elems), level = 3) -> Vector\n\nLegendre coefficients aₗ (index l+1) of the azimuthally averaged field, aₗ = (2l+1)/(4π) ∫ f Pₗ(cosθ) dΩ, where θ is measured from axis about center and values[e] is indexed by element (taken constant over each element). aₗ/a₀ is the usual \"Pₗ/P₀\" asymmetry. legendre_weights precomputes the linear operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.legendre_moments"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.legendre_weights-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"legendre_weights(target, elems; lmax = 8, axis = ẑ, center = mode_center(target, elems), level = 3) -> Matrix\n\nMatrix W with legendre_moments(values, …) == W * values[elems]: W[l+1, k] = (2l+1)/Ω ∫ Pₗ(cosθ) dΩ over element elems[k], integrated over 4^level sub-triangles per facet with exact solid angles, and Ω the total solid angle of elems (4π for a closed surface around center). Treating the field as constant on each element makes a uniform field have no l > 0 moments, however the facets tile the sphere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.legendre_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.mode_amplitude-Tuple{AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"mode_amplitude(c, l) -> Real\n\nRotation-invariant amplitude of degree l relative to the mean, in the normalization of Legendre moments, √((2l+1) Σₘ (c_lm/c_00)²). It equals |aₗ/a₀| for a field axisymmetric about any axis. For l = 1 it is therefore the fractional amplitude ε of a dipole drive 1 + ε cos γ, the quantity to which hot-spot velocity sensitivities refer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_amplitude"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.mode_center-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"mode_center(target, elems) -> Vec3\n\nCenter about which the mode analysis of elems (legendre_weights, ylm_weights and the scenario caches built from them) measures directions by default. It is the center of their SphereSurface when every element lies on sphere components with one center, as for a capsule, also when the capsule is offset from chamber center. Otherwise it is the origin. Analyzing an offset capsule about the origin mixes its P2 into a spurious mode 1 at first order in the offset, by 0.74% of mode 1 per 100 µm on a 0.83 mm capsule with P2/P0 ≈ −0.1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_center"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.mode_index-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"mode_index(l, m) -> Int\n\nPosition of the real spherical harmonic (l, m) in the flat ordering used by ylm_weights: l² + l + m + 1, i.e. (0,0), (1,-1), (1,0), (1,1), (2,-2), ….\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_index"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.mode_power-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"mode_power(C; m = :all) -> Vector\n\nPer-l amplitude σₗ = √(Σₘ cₗₘ²) / c₀₀ from ylm_moments (index l+1; ylm_matrix turns flat coefficients into C), a rotation-invariant nonuniformity spectrum. The rms of the field over the sphere relative to its mean is √(Σ_{l≥1} σₗ²) (mode_rms). The amplitude equals |aₗ/a₀|/√(2l+1) for an axisymmetric field. With m = :zero, only the zonal harmonic m = 0 of each degree is kept, |c_l0|/c₀₀. This is the spectrum of the azimuthally averaged field, |aₗ/a₀|/√(2l+1) from its legendre_moments. With m = :nonzero, the rest is kept, and the squares of the two add to those of the :all spectrum. The zonal part depends on the polar axis about which the coefficients were taken, and :all does not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_power"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.mode_rms-Tuple{AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"mode_rms(c, lmax; m = :all) -> Real\n\nRMS nonuniformity over degrees 1 ≤ l ≤ lmax, σ = √(Σₗ Σₘ c_lm²) / |c₀₀|, from flat real-harmonic coefficients c (mode_index order, from ylm_weights(…) * values or flux_ylm). It is the rms deviation of the field over the sphere relative to its mean, with the degrees above lmax filtered out (σ² = Σₗ σₗ² with σₗ from mode_power). With m = :zero only the zonal harmonics m = 0 are summed, which gives the rms of the azimuthally averaged field about the polar axis of the coefficients. With m = :nonzero only the rest are summed, and mode_rms(c, l; m = :zero)^2 + mode_rms(c, l; m = :nonzero)^2 == mode_rms(c, l)^2 up to rounding. The split is the m = 0 / m ≠ 0 decomposition of polar-direct-drive designs (Yeamans et al., Nucl. Fusion 61, 046031, 2021). The filtered σ over l ≤ 30 is the illumination nonuniformity of Barlow et al. (PRL 133, 175101, 2024).\n\nGeneric in the number type, since the function lies on the optimization path. It has a square root, so its derivative is singular for an exactly uniform field. Objectives can instead sum relative_mode_power over l, which is σ² without the root.\n\nFor hard-sphere laser illumination, without plasma, every ray is straight and the incident I cos ψ is absorbed where it lands. The field is then the absorbed intensity of a deposit on a bare sphere, t = Target(Component(:capsule, icosphere_mesh(R; n), material; surface = SphereSurface(R))). Its dep.element ./ t.elem_area is projected by W = ylm_weights(t, 1:nelements(t); lmax = 30), built once, with DepositionOperator(t, beams; ncone = 1) for collimated beams. The element values average the illumination over each facet, which lowers σₗ at second order in the element size. On the 60 OMEGA beams with SG4 spots on R = 0.5 mm, σ (l ≤ 30) is 3.5% low at n = 20 (edges 0.055 R) and 1.7% low at n = 30, and a Richardson extrapolation of the two is 0.3% low (reference/research-notes.md, \"Hard-sphere direct-drive illumination\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode_rms"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.relative_mode_power-Tuple{AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"relative_mode_power(c, l) -> Real\n\nΣₘ (c_lm / c_00)² from flat coefficients c (mode_index order), the squared rotation-invariant amplitude of degree l relative to the mean. It is smooth, with no square root, so it suits objectives, and mode_power is its square root. For an axisymmetric field it equals (aₗ/a₀)² / (2l+1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.relative_mode_power"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.rms_nonuniformity-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"rms_nonuniformity(values, weights) -> Real\n\nWeighted RMS deviation over weighted mean.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.rms_nonuniformity"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.solid_angle","page":"Mode analysis","text":"solid_angle(target, e, center) -> Float64\n\nExact solid angle subtended by element e at center (Van Oosterom–Strackee formula summed over its triangles). Mode decompositions use it as the quadrature weight, so the weights of a closed surface around center sum to exactly 4π.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.solid_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.ylm_frame-Tuple{Any}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"ylm_frame(axis) -> (ẑ, x̂, ŷ)\n\nRight-handed frame for spherical harmonics with the polar axis along axis. For the default axis = ẑ this is the chamber frame, so azimuthal mode phases are relative to +x, which at NIF is the direction of Switchyard 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ylm_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.ylm_matrix-Tuple{AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"ylm_matrix(c, lmax) -> Matrix\n\nFlat coefficients (mode_index order) as the (lmax+1) × (2lmax+1) matrix C[l+1, lmax+1+m] used by ylm_moments and mode_power (unused entries zero).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ylm_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.ylm_moments-Tuple{AbstractVector, Target, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"ylm_moments(values, target, elems; lmax = 8, axis = ẑ, center = mode_center(target, elems), level = 2) -> Matrix\n\nCoefficients of real orthonormal spherical harmonics, c[l+1, l+1+m] for -l ≤ m ≤ l (cos mφ for m > 0, sin |m|φ for m < 0), with the polar axis along axis. The field is constant on each element, and the harmonics are integrated over each facet (see ylm_weights). mode_power gives rotation-invariant per-l amplitudes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ylm_moments"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/analysis/#Briareus.ylm_weights-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Mode analysis","text":"ylm_weights(target, elems; lmax = 8, axis = ẑ, center = mode_center(target, elems), level = 2) -> Matrix\n\nMatrix W with W * values[elems] the coefficients of real orthonormal spherical harmonics in the flat mode_index ordering: Y_l0 = P̄_l0, Y_lm ∝ cos mφ for m > 0 and ∝ sin |m|φ for m < 0, without the Condon–Shortley phase, so Y_11 ∝ +x and Y_1,-1 ∝ +y. The field of each element is taken as constant over the element, and the harmonics are integrated over its facets with exact sub-triangle solid angles (4^level per triangle). The covered solid angle is normalized to 4π.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ylm_weights"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/geometry/#Geometry-and-targets","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Targets are assembled from meshed primitives (cylinder, annulus or disk, sphere, rectangle, frustum) with per-component materials; hohlraum and foil build the common ones. Surfaces are one- or two-sided, and laser entrance holes are transparent apertures that measure the escaping x-ray flux. A component can carry the exact surface its mesh approximates (SphereSurface, CylinderSurface, DiskSurface), and the radiation model then integrates over the true curved surface; hohlraums do this by default. A HohlraumProfile gives a hohlraum a radius per break instead of one radius. It describes a frustraum (frustraum_profile) or a multi-section wall whose sections are cylinders, cones (ConeSurface) or flat steps.\n\nWallWindow cuts rectangular or round diagnostic windows, with optional assembly gaps, into a hohlraum wall at exact areas, and the mesh conforms to their edges. Closed windows re-emit with their own material; open ones are apertures. A thin-foil window burns through when the Marshak heat front reaches its back (BurnthroughAlbedo) and then transmits the gray Eddington fraction 4(3tau + 2) of the flux on it (Lester et al., Phys. Rev. E 114, L023201 (2026)), optionally with the optical depth following the radiation temperature (RosselandTransmission), or also the temperature profile in the foil (DiffusionTransmission). Gaps can close during the pulse (GapClosure). Sombrero adds LEH shields.\n\nCAD parts (for example the solids of a multi-solid STL file) become a radiation-ready target in one call, cad_target: windings repaired into closed solids facing out of their material (orient_mesh), the cavity around a point kept (enclosure), the surface remeshed into compact, near-planar elements of a chosen size (remesh_mesh, which also cuts the long slivers CAD tessellates curved walls into; coarsen_mesh only merges whole facets), and cylinders, spheres, cones and tori fitted (fit_surfaces). Primitive components can be passed with the parts. Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum walks through each step.","title":"Geometry and targets"},{"category":"module","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Briareus\n\nView-factor radiation and laser-pointing design for high-energy-density experiments: indirect-drive hohlraums, backlighter foils, and other laser-irradiated targets.\n\nUnits: lengths mm, time ns, power TW, flux TW/mm² (to_Wcm2 converts), radiation temperature eV, angles in degrees at the user interface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.ConeSurface","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"ConeSurface(half_angle; apex = (0, 0, 0), axis = (0, 0, 1))\n\nOne nappe of a right circular cone. Its points are seen from apex at half_angle degrees (0 < θ < 90) from axis, at distance z tan θ from the axis a distance z along it. Entrance-hole cones, conical shields and tapered bores have this shape. As on a cylinder, points are projected onto the cone radially, keeping their height along the axis. The chords of a circle about the axis, such as the rims of a faceted cone, therefore land on the circle, and a faceted frustum covers the true one exactly. The facet patches have exact areas.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ConeSurface"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.CurvedSurface","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"CurvedSurface\n\nThe exact surface that a faceted component approximates, one of SphereSurface, CylinderSurface, ConeSurface, TorusSurface and DiskSurface. When a component has one, the radiation model integrates over the true surface, using the points, normals and areas of the curved patches. Ray casting and visibility still use the flat facets. On a curved concave surface the view-factor kernel stays bounded, and flat facets would misrepresent the exchange between nearby elements.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CurvedSurface"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.CylinderSurface","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"CylinderSurface(radius; origin = (0, 0, 0), axis = (0, 0, 1))\n\nInfinite circular cylinder through origin along axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CylinderSurface"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.DiskSurface","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"DiskSurface(; center = (0, 0, 0), normal = (0, 0, 1))\n\nPlane through center whose elements are polar sectors about center, as produced by annulus_mesh and disk_mesh: the exact shape of each element is the annular sector spanned by its vertices, with circular arcs where the mesh has chords. Giving the end caps and laser entrance holes of a hohlraum this surface makes them meet a CylinderSurface wall exactly at the rim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DiskSurface"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.Mesh","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Mesh(vertices, triangles, patches)\n\nTriangle mesh whose triangles are grouped into planar patches. patches[p] is the contiguous range of triangle indices forming patch p. Lengths are in mm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Mesh"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.SphereSurface","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"SphereSurface(radius; center = (0, 0, 0))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SphereSurface"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.TorusSurface","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"TorusSurface(major_radius, minor_radius; center = (0, 0, 0), axis = (0, 0, 1))\n\nA torus, the points at minor_radius r from its spine. The spine is the circle of major_radius R about axis through center. A CAD fillet is a quarter of the tube, where a wall meets a cap. Points are projected in their meridian half-plane:\n\nA point inside the tube circle is projected to the nearest point of the circle. For a ring torus, with R > r, this is the nearest point of the torus.\nA point outside the tube circle is projected as on a cone, radially from the axis at its height, onto the side of the tube it is on. Beyond the top or bottom of the tube it goes to the pole.\n\nNormals point away from the spine. The rims of a faceted fillet are chords of circles about the axis, which lie at the rim height toward the axis. The chords lie outside the tube for rims on the poles, where a quarter fillet meets a cap, and on the inner half. They lie inside the tube, along the radius, on the outer equator, where a fillet meets a wall. On all of these rims the chords land on the rim circle, and the elements of a fillet cover the torus band exactly. Elsewhere on the outer half, where a fillet meets a cone, the rim chords stay off the circle by their sag. The band is then covered to second order in the facet size. A projection of points cannot both cover those rims exactly and keep facets near the poles second order. The facet patches have exact areas under the projection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TorusSurface"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.Vec3","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Vec3\n\nA point or direction in 3D, in mm for positions, stored as SVector{3,Float64}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Vec3"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.add_polygon!-Tuple{Mesh, AbstractVector}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"add_polygon!(mesh, poly; facing = nothing)\n\nAppends the convex planar polygon poly as one fan-triangulated patch. If facing is a vector, the winding is reversed when needed so the front normal of the patch points along facing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.add_polygon!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.annulus_mesh-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"annulus_mesh(r_inner, r_outer, z; nr, nphi, up = true, phi0 = 0.0, phibreaks = ())\n\nBuilds a flat annulus in the plane z, or a disk when r_inner == 0, with nr radial by nphi azimuthal patches. Front faces point along +ẑ when up, -ẑ otherwise. Rings are equal-area so patch areas are uniform. phibreaks (radians) are azimuths the mesh must contain, as in cylinder_mesh; giving both the same nphi and phibreaks makes an end cap meet a wall at shared vertices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.annulus_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.conforming_grid-Tuple{Any, Any, Integer, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"conforming_grid(a, b, n, breaks; periodic = false) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturns grid lines from a to b with about n cells that include every break line in breaks. Each interval between consecutive breaks is divided uniformly into max(1, round(len / h)) cells, with h = (b - a)/n. Breaks closer than 1e-9 (b - a) are merged. periodic is for an azimuth with b - a one period. The breaks are then wrapped into the period, and the grid extends from the first break to the first break plus the period, so the end points a and b are not grid lines themselves. Without breaks, use range(a, b; length = n + 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.conforming_grid"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.cubed_sphere_mesh-Tuple{Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"cubed_sphere_mesh(radius; n, outward = true)\n\nBuilds a sphere about the origin from the six faces of a cube, each divided into n × n equiangular cells projected onto the sphere. The four corners of a projected cell are not coplanar. Each cell is therefore split into two triangular patches along alternating diagonals, chosen to keep the faceted sphere convex, for 12n² planar patches. The mesh has no poles and no slivers, which suits radiation problems where every part of the sphere matters equally.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cubed_sphere_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.cylinder_mesh-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"cylinder_mesh(radius, z0, z1; nz, nphi, inward = true, phi0 = 0.0, zbreaks = (),\n              phibreaks = ())\n\nBuilds an open cylinder of the given radius about the z axis spanning z0 ≤ z ≤ z1, with nz axial by nphi azimuthal quadrilateral patches. Front faces point toward the axis when inward, as for a hohlraum wall, and away from it otherwise.\n\nzbreaks (mm) and phibreaks (radians) are lines that the mesh must contain, such as the edges of a window cut into the wall. The grid is then uniform between consecutive breaks with about the default spacing (conforming_grid), so whole patches tile a region bounded by break lines exactly. Without breaks the grid is uniform from phi0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cylinder_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.disk_mesh-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"disk_mesh(radius, z; nr, nphi, up = true)\n\nBuilds a flat disk in the plane z, an annulus_mesh with zero inner radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.disk_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.frustum_mesh-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"frustum_mesh(r0, z0, r1, z1; nz, nphi, inward = true)\n\nBuilds a conical frustum about the z axis from radius r0 at z0 to r1 at z1, or a cone when one radius is zero. It is used for shields, cones and tapered hohlraum sections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.frustum_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.icosphere_frequency-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"icosphere_frequency(radius, h) -> n\n\nReturns the subdivision of icosphere_mesh for triangles with sides of about h (mm). An icosahedron edge spans 1.107 radians of arc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.icosphere_frequency"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.icosphere_mesh-Tuple{Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"icosphere_mesh(radius; n, outward = true)\n\nBuilds a geodesic sphere about the origin. Each face of an icosahedron is divided into n × n triangles whose vertices are projected onto the sphere, for 20n² triangular patches. The icosahedron is in its standard frame, with vertices at the cyclic permutations of (0, ±1, ±ϕ), and is mirror-symmetric in x, y and z. The triangles are nearly equilateral everywhere, with edges within about ±20% of 1.107 radius / n, and the mesh has no poles and no slivers. On a capsule the high Legendre moments (P4) of the flux converge with far fewer elements than on sphere_mesh, whose polar cells are slivers. icosphere_frequency gives n for an element size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.icosphere_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.merge_meshes-Tuple{Vararg{Mesh}}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"merge_meshes(meshes...)\n\nConcatenates meshes into one, preserving patch structure and order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.merge_meshes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.npatches-Tuple{Mesh}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Number of patches of a mesh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.npatches"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.rectangle_mesh-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"rectangle_mesh(width, height; nu, nv)\n\nBuilds the rectangle |x| ≤ width/2, |y| ≤ height/2 in the plane z = 0, with its front face along +ẑ, divided into nu × nv patches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.rectangle_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.rotation_to-Tuple{Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"rotation_to(n)\n\nReturns the proper rotation matrix that takes ẑ to the unit vector along n by the minimal rotation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.rotation_to"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.sphere_mesh-Tuple{Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"sphere_mesh(radius; ntheta, nphi, outward = true)\n\nBuilds a latitude–longitude sphere about the origin with ntheta bands uniform in cos θ, so all ntheta × nphi patches have equal area. Polar bands are triangle fans.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.sphere_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.split_mesh-Tuple{Mesh, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"split_mesh(mesh, keep) -> (selected, rest)\n\nSplits a mesh by patch. keep(centroid) is called with the area-weighted centroid of each patch, and the patch goes to selected when it returns true and to rest otherwise. Use it to cut a port or a window out of a surface and make it an aperture.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.split_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.surface_point-Tuple{SphereSurface, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"surface_point(surface, p) -> (point, normal)\n\nReturns the projection of p onto the surface and the outward unit normal at that point. The projection is radial from the center or the axis. On a torus it is radial from the spine inside the tube and from the axis outside it. The normal points away from the center, the axis or the spine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.surface_point"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.transform","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"transform(mesh, R, t)\n\nApplies the rigid map x ↦ R*x + t to every vertex. R must be a proper rotation for the patch orientations to be preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.transform"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.translate-Tuple{Mesh, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"translate(mesh_or_component, t)\n\nReturns a copy shifted by the vector t (mm).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.translate"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.coarsen_mesh-Tuple{Mesh}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"coarsen_mesh(mesh; size, angle = 10.0, tol = 1e-9) -> Mesh\n\nThe mesh with neighboring patches merged into larger ones, the radiosity elements of a target. Each new patch grows from a seed patch over shared edges. It takes patches whose normals are within angle degrees of the seed normal and whose vertices lie within size / 2 mm of the seed centroid. Triangles are unchanged, and only their grouping into patches and their order change. Imported CAD often has hundreds of thousands of facets, from fine tessellation of curved faces and slivers along edges, far more than view factors need. Coarsening to a few hundred micrometers makes such parts usable for radiation.\n\nPatches are merged only across edges where both are oriented consistently (see orient_mesh), so the front side of a merged patch is well defined. An element stays within angle of planar, so the flat-patch error of a merged element on a curved surface is that of a facet of the same size. Seeds are taken by decreasing area, so large flat faces are kept whole up to size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.coarsen_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.connected_pieces-Tuple{Mesh}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"connected_pieces(mesh; tol = 1e-9) -> Vector{Mesh}\n\nThe connected pieces of a mesh, largest area first, where patches that share a vertex belong to one piece. Vertices are first welded by position, to tol times the extent of the mesh, as orient_mesh welds them. A piece whose file duplicates the vertices along a seam therefore stays whole. The patches of each piece are kept, in their order. A CAD assembly stored as one mesh, such as a target holder with its shields, sombreros and arms, splits into its parts this way, where split_mesh needs a position test.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.connected_pieces"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.orient_mesh-Tuple{Mesh}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"orient_mesh(mesh; toward = nothing, tol = 1e-9, strict = true) -> Mesh\n\nThe mesh with the windings of its patches made consistent. Across every edge that two patches share, the patches traverse it in opposite directions, so their front sides lie on the same side of the surface. Vertices are welded by position to tol times the extent of the mesh. Imported CAD is often stitched from faces of either orientation, and the front of a radiating element is the side that its winding defines.\n\nEach connected surface then gets one orientation as a whole:\n\na closed surface (every edge shared by exactly two patches) faces out of the volume it encloses (positive signed volume), so a thick hohlraum wall, meshed as a closed solid, faces into the cavity on its inner side;\nan open surface faces the point toward (e.g. the target center for a thin hohlraum wall) by area-weighted majority. Without toward, it takes the orientation of the larger part of its area.\n\nEdges shared by more than two patches (non-manifold) do not constrain the orientation. Where such edges only join separate surfaces, each with two patches on the edge, as for closed solids touching along an edge in a CAD assembly, each surface still counts as closed and faces out of its volume. Other non-manifold edges, such as three patches of one surface or a sheet meeting a solid, leave their surfaces open and are reported in a warning. A surface that cannot be oriented consistently, such as a Möbius-like stitching, has no correct winding. Every orientation leaves some front side facing the wrong way, and enclosure and the radiosity take the windings as given. Such a surface throws an ArgumentError. With strict = false, the first orientation found for each patch is kept instead, with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.orient_mesh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.remesh_mesh-Tuple{Mesh}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"remesh_mesh(mesh; size, angle = 10.0, tol = 1e-9) -> Mesh\n\nThe surface of the mesh divided into compact, near-planar radiosity elements of about size mm, whatever the shape of its facets. On a smooth surface the elements average size² in area, as a square element of side size does, and every vertex lies within size of the centroid of its element. Imported CAD tessellates curved walls as long slivers, such as a hohlraum wall as axial strips spanning its length. coarsen_mesh cannot shorten such strips, since it only groups whole facets. The steps are:\n\nVertices are welded by position, to tol times the extent of the mesh. One-sided edges with the vertices of other one-sided edges on them, the T-junctions between CAD faces, are split there, so faces that meet along an edge are connected.\nEvery facet is split, in its plane, into triangles with edges of at most size / 3. An edge gets the same points from both facets that share it, so the refined triangulation is conforming. Parallel edges longer than that are the sides of the strips of a wall, the generators of a cylinder or an extrusion to within 0.25°. They and the diagonals of their strips are cut where they cross a common family of planes across them. Neighboring strips are therefore cut at the same places, and a cut across a strip is one straight chord. Other edges are divided equally.\nThe refined triangles are clustered. Seeds 0.85 size apart (a Poisson-disk sample) collect triangles nearest first across shared, consistently wound edges (see orient_mesh), and take those whose normals are within angle degrees of the seed normal. Lloyd iterations move each seed to the centroid and mean normal of its element. Strips are facets whose edges longer than size are all cut by one family of planes. On strips, every few planes bound a band about size long, and elements grow within one band. Their cuts across the strips then lie on the planes, straight and shared by the elements on both sides. The piece of a sliver strip in a band goes whole to one element, so the element sides run along strip edges. An element w wide crosses about w/(strip width) strips, and its outline has about two edges per strip. An element is halved until none of its facets lies more than angle from its mean normal, no vertex lies farther than size from its centroid, and its centroid projects along the normal onto the element.\nWithin each element, the pieces of facets in one plane are joined and re-triangulated from their outline. A point is dropped only where it lies on a straight outline in every element that uses it, so the mesh stays conforming.\n\nThe surface is unchanged. Every output triangle lies in the plane of an input facet, and the elements tile the input facets exactly, with areas that agree within round-off. Facets of zero area (height below 1e-12 of their longest edge) and triangles outside every patch are dropped. Slivers thinner than 1e-6 of their length have no orientation to keep and join any neighboring element. The patches of the input do not constrain the elements, but its windings do, so the input should be oriented first. Faces narrower than size, such as the end of a wall or a fillet, give elements as narrow. Elements on a faceted curved wall keep its faceting, and a facet of width w on radius R lies up to w²/8R off the true surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.remesh_mesh"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.BLACK","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Perfect absorber that emits nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.BLACK"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.AlbedoModel","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"AlbedoModel\n\nSupertype of x-ray albedo models: ConstantAlbedo, TabulatedAlbedo, WallLossAlbedo, BurnthroughAlbedo, GapClosure, LipClosure. Evaluate with albedo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.AlbedoModel"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.BurnthroughAlbedo","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"BurnthroughAlbedo(; thickness, density = 19.3, before = WallLossAlbedo(),\n                  transmission = eddington_transmission(τ), after = nothing,\n                  width = 0.4, burnthrough = nothing, front = HeatFront())\n\nAlbedo of a thin wall layer, such as a thin-foil diagnostic window, that re-emits like the wall until the Marshak heat front (front, a HeatFront) has heated its whole areal mass ρ × thickness, and then transmits part of the flux that reaches it. The layer is thickness µm of a material of density g/cm³, gold by default.\n\nOnce burned through, a fraction f = transmission of the incident flux passes through the foil and leaves the hohlraum (a number, RosselandTransmission() for a fraction that follows the radiation temperature, or DiffusionTransmission() for one that also follows the temperature profile of the foil). The rest meets the foil as before and is re-emitted with the albedo of before. Across the transition\n\nα(t) = α_before (1 - s f),    transmitted fraction s f,\n\nwith s = s((t - t_b)/width) the smooth step 3x² - 2x³ on [0, 1] and t_b the burnthrough time, so the burned-through albedo is (1 - f) α_before. Of the flux H A on the foil, s f H A is transmitted and (1 - s f)(1 - α_before) H A absorbed (energy_balance, drive_history). before is an albedo model or a number, by default the Marshak loss of the wall (WallLossAlbedo).\n\nThe default f is the gray-foil estimate of R. S. Lester et al., Phys. Rev. E 114, L023201 (2026), doi:10.1103/k8dz-pjdx: f = 4/(3τ + 2) (their eq. 2, eddington_transmission). The optical depth is their eq. 1, τ = L/λ_Au(T_h) at a fixed Rosseland mean free path λ_Au, scaled linearly in areal mass (optical_depth) from their point τ ∼ 5 at L = 2.5 µm:\n\nτ = 5 × ρ thickness / (19.3 g/cm³ × 2.5 µm),\n\nso f ≈ 0.33, 0.235 and 0.17 for 1.7, 2.5 and 3.6 µm of gold. Limitations:\n\nλ_Au is held at their evaluation, for a hohlraum radiation temperature T_h of typically 300–350 eV and an assumed gold density of about 1 g/cm³ (the expanded foil), so f is constant once burned through. With transmission = RosselandTransmission(), λ_Au follows the temperature of the flux on the foil instead; its density dependence is not modeled.\nPairing that density with the 2.5 µm as-built thickness is theirs. Taken literally, eq. 1 at 1 g/cm³ describes a layer of 0.25 mg/cm², against 4.8 mg/cm² in the foil. The scaling with thickness (areal mass) is derived in Briareus from eq. 1.\nThe estimate is gray, planar and quasi-static, and for gold: pass transmission for another material. Below τ = 2/3 (0.33 µm of gold) it is capped at 1.\n\ntransmission = 1 makes a burned-through foil an open hole whose whole incident flux is transmitted.\n\nAn explicit after (an albedo model or number; exclusive with transmission) sets the burned-through albedo directly instead,\n\nα(t) = α_before + s (α_after - α_before),\n\nand counts as transmitted only the loss beyond that of the intact foil, (α_before - α) H A; the (1 - α_before) H A of the intact foil stays absorbed. after = 0 is the open-hole bound on the loss with this split (the default before transmission existed).\n\nThe burnthrough time t_b is, in order:\n\nthe per-element time passed to solve/albedos (burnthrough = ...), which drive_history integrates from the incident-flux temperature history of each element (heat_front);\nthe explicit burnthrough (ns), when given;\notherwise the foil is intact (t_b = ∞): a single solve has no history to estimate it from. burnthrough_time gives the estimate for a temperature history.\n\nbefore is evaluated at t; in a standalone solve or albedos, t is the time since the pulse start, as for a wall without history. In a drive_history with wall_history on, a WallLossAlbedo before instead follows the temperature history of the element, as the wall elements do: an intact foil equals the wall around it, and across and after the transition α_before follows that history (with the re-emission of stored energy from the wall scaled by α/α_before).\n\nBefore t_b the foil loses exactly what a thick wall loses and transmits nothing: in the diffusion picture the cold material ahead of a nonlinear heat front does not change the flux into it, so no early loss is modeled. Measured burnthrough of 0.8–3 µm gold foils on a 260 eV hohlraum wall (Nova; Lindl et al., Phys. Plasmas 11, 339 (2004), Fig. 4-6) reaches half signal 0.1–0.25 ns after the computed front arrival, 0.19 ns on average, which sets the default width (half transmission at t_b + width/2; see the diagnostic-windows tutorial).\n\nCaution on an ignition-scale drive. The onset, the width and the constant gray f come from bare Nova foils and the Letter's estimate. examples/tamped_foil.jl models a 1-D gold foil with Hammer & Rosen's power laws, flux-limited diffusion and hydrodynamics. On an ignition-scale drive (N221204's Dante temperature) that foil starts transmitting about 0.6 ns after the arrival of the front, and its transmission rises slowly. Over 4–9 ns it passes 0.49–0.54 of the energy this default gives it (1.5–3 µm). On Nova's drive the modeled onset comes 0.2–0.45 ns after the arrival, and the measured half signal 0.09–0.23 ns after it. The one ignition-scale measurement, the window flux Lester et al. extracted from Dante (its solid-angle calibration pending), corresponds to about 0.18 of the flux on a 2.5 µm window near 8.5 ns, between 0.16 for the 1-D foil and 0.235 for this default. A study should bracket the transmission and start time of a window accordingly: transmission = DiffusionTransmission() (lower, temperature-following) against the default, and the arrival of the front against a start up to 0.6 ns later. Research notes, \"Backed windows: what delays the emergent flux, and eq. 3 (2026-11-18)\" and \"A diffusion transmission for burned-through windows (2026-11-18)\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.BurnthroughAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.CapsuleAlbedo","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"CapsuleAlbedo(; albedo = 0.2, radius = nothing, outside_absorptance = 0.0)\n\nAlbedo of a capsule that absorbs as a sphere of effective radius R(t) inside its fixed mesh of radius R₀, plus a share outside_absorptance of what crosses the mesh outside that sphere:\n\nα_eff(t) = 1 - (1 - α_c) s² - a_out (1 - s²),    s = R(t)/R₀,\n\nso that the capsule mesh absorbs (1 - α_c) H 4πR(t)², the absorption of the smaller sphere at the same incident flux, plus a_out H 4π(R₀² - R(t)²). The mesh re-emits the rest, which approximates light crossing the surroundings of the shrunken capsule. albedo α_c is a number or an AlbedoModel, such as TabulatedAlbedo in time or TemperatureAlbedo in the incident radiation temperature. radius is nothing (R = R₀), a RadiusSchedule such as TabulatedRadius or ScaledRadius, a (times, R/R₀) table, or a function t -> R/R₀. A table is in ns, linear and held at its ends, and it is stored as a TabulatedRadius. outside_absorptance a_out (0  a_out  1, default 0) is the absorptance of the blow-off between the ablation front and R₀ for the flux that misses the front. This explicit term for absorption outside the front, as by W-doped ablator plasma, is unpublished, so it is 0 unless a study sets it. With the default the model is only the absorption of the front, and without a radius the term vanishes. energy_balance then books the absorbed capsule power at the effective radius plus the outside term, and energy_budget books its integral E_cap.\n\nThe default α_c = 0.2 is the 0-D value f_cap = 1 - α_cap  08 of Swadling et al., PoP 32, 052707 (2025). For a NIF HDC capsule in a drive history, Young et al.'s quasi-1D HYDRA ablator albedo (PoP 27, 082702 (2020) Fig. 3a, young2020_albedos()) is the published α_c(t); examples/n221204.jl uses it on N221204's time axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CapsuleAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.Component","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Component(name, mesh, material; kind = :surface, two_sided = false, surface = nothing,\n          part = name)\n\nA named piece of a target. kind = :surface components are opaque: they block laser rays and lines of sight, absorb laser light, and radiate from their front side (and back side when two_sided). kind = :aperture components (e.g. laser entrance holes) are transparent diagnostic surfaces that record the x-ray flux crossing them and re-emit nothing, or, with a GapClosure or LipClosure albedo, re-emit what a closing lip of plasma would (see aperture). surface is the exact CurvedSurface the mesh approximates, if any; the radiation model then uses the curved geometry (see view_factors).\n\npart names the user's part the component belongs to: the component name, unless it is a piece of a part split for computation (curved_components names the pieces of a part p p/cyl1, …, p/rest, each with part = p). Name-taking functions (elements, Scenario(...; observe), keep-outs, clearance hardware) accept the name of a part for all its pieces, and reports (energy_balance, component_power, write_results, report) are keyed by part; see parts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Component"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.ConstantAlbedo","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"ConstantAlbedo(α)\n\nTime-independent x-ray albedo (re-emitted / incident flux).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ConstantAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.Conversion","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Conversion(; efficiency = 0.9, time_exponent = 0.0, reference_time = 1.0, start = 0.0,\n           t0 = 0.1, intensities = nothing, efficiencies = nothing, range = (0.0, 1.0))\n\nLaser-to-x-ray conversion efficiency that varies in time and with the absorbed laser intensity, for Material(...; conversion = Conversion(...)):\n\nη(t, I) = clamp(η₀(I) ((t - start)/reference_time)^s, range...),\n\nwith s = time_exponent, t - start clamped below at t0 (ns), and η₀(I) either the constant efficiency or, with intensities (W/cm²) and efficiencies, the table interpolated linearly in log₁₀ I and held at its ends. The default efficiency 0.9 is the conversion of gold, the input of a published static VisRad model of a NIF hohlraum (Young et al., PoP 27, 082702 (2020)); 0.75 suits 1 ns Nova and Omega hohlraums. Research notes, \"Material defaults and their sources (2026-11-18)\". I is the absorbed laser flux P/A of each element (×1e14 W/cm²): a property of the element mesh too (coarse elements average a spot over more area). Evaluate with conversion_efficiency.\n\nPublished forms (see nif_conversion for the preset):\n\nin time, η ∝ t^0.12–0.2: 0.7 t^0.12 for Nova's 1 ns hohlraums, which makes the measured T ∝ t^0.15 (Lindl et al., PoP 11, 339 (2004), Fig. 4-4 caption); 0.7 t^0.2 (Rosen 2005, Nova LASNEX); 0.85 t^0.2 (Schein 2007, Omega);\nin intensity, the backscatter-corrected plateau on Au, U and cocktail spheres: 0.95 at 10¹⁴ and 0.80 at 10¹⁵ W/cm² (Dewald et al., PoP 15, 072706 (2008), abstract), Conversion(intensities = (1e14, 1e15), efficiencies = (0.95, 0.80)). The η of a hohlraum exceeds that of a disk at the same intensity (Lindl 2004, Fig. 4-10).\n\nGeneric in the number type of the laser power (the intensity table is differentiable); for a material with a plain number, that number is used exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Conversion"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.DiffusionTransmission","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"DiffusionTransmission(; reference_temperature = 325.0, exponent = 1.5)\n\nPost-burnthrough transmission of a gold foil whose Rosseland opacity follows its temperature through its thickness, for BurnthroughAlbedo(; transmission = DiffusionTransmission()) (opt-in; the default is the constant gray estimate). The optical depth at the temperature T (eV) of the flux on the foil is that of RosselandTransmission, τ = τ_ref (T / reference_temperature)^(-exponent); the transmitted fraction t is the steady planar diffusion flux through a foil whose opacity varies as T^(-α) (α = exponent) across it, its front at T and its rear radiating into vacuum (T_rear⁴ = t T⁴/2, the boundaries of Lester et al.'s eq. 2):\n\n(3τ/4) t = (4/(4 + α)) [1 - (t/2)^((4 + α)/4)].\n\nAt α = 0 this is eddington_transmission exactly; for τ ≫ 1 it tends to 4/(4 + α) of it, 8/11 for Hammer & Rosen's α = 1.5 (Phys. Plasmas 10, 1829 (2003), eq. 31): the rear of the foil, colder than its front, is more opaque. It is derived, with no parameter fitted to a measurement, and capped at 1 like the gray estimate.\n\nThe arrival of the front and the width of the transition are unchanged by this choice. examples/tamped_foil.jl models 1-D gold foils with Hammer & Rosen's power laws, flux-limited diffusion and hydrodynamics. On a public ignition-scale drive, N221204's Dante temperature at ×1 and ×0.95, those foils transmit over 4–9 ns 0.76–0.79 of the energy this model gives for 1.5–3 µm of gold. They transmit 0.49–0.54 of the energy the constant default gives and 0.59–0.62 of that of RosselandTransmission. The remainder of the gap is the later onset of the 1-D foil. The research notes section \"A diffusion transmission for burned-through windows (2026-11-18)\" gives the comparison. Generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DiffusionTransmission"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.GapClosure","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"GapClosure(; closure, width = 0.4, after = WallLossAlbedo())\n\nAlbedo of an opening that closes during the pulse, such as the assembly gap around a diagnostic window, WallWindow(; gap, gap_material = Material(:Au; albedo = GapClosure(closure = 3.0))). Until closure (ns) the gap is open: all the flux that reaches it leaves the hohlraum, as through an aperture. Across the next width ns it closes, and then it re-emits like the wall with the albedo of after (a model or a number):\n\nα(t) = s α_after,    transmitted fraction 1 - s,\n\nwith s = s((t - closure)/width) the smooth step 3x² - 2x³ on [0, 1], as for BurnthroughAlbedo. energy_balance books (1 - s) H A as transmitted, and drive_history counts it in window_loss. The closure time is an input: a gap tens of micrometers wide closes as plasma blown off its walls fills it, which this model does not compute. Unlike an aperture, the gap is opaque to laser light and to view-factor visibility rays, which matters only for gaps a beam would reach.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.GapClosure"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.HeatFront","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"HeatFront(; coefficient = 9.51e-4, temperature_exponent = 1.914, time_exponent = 0.5156)\n\nAreal mass (g/cm²) behind the ablative (subsonic) Marshak heat front that radiation of temperature T drives into a gold wall: at constant T,\n\nm_F = c (T/100 eV)^a (t/1 ns)^b,\n\nwith the defaults from the self-similar solution of Hammer & Rosen, Phys. Plasmas 10, 1829 (2003), eq. 112 for a constant drive (q₀ = 0), built on their power-law fits to the opacity and equation of state of gold. For a drive T(t) the front is integrated as\n\nm_F(t) = c [∫₀ᵗ (T(t′)/100 eV)^(a/b) dt′]^b,\n\nwhich is exact at constant T, depends only on the drive history, and for a ramp T ∝ tᵏ gives m_F = c (1 + k a/b)^(-b) T(t)^a t^b: within 2% of Hammer & Rosen's exact coefficient for k = 0.182 and within 1% of Shussman & Heizler's (Phys. Plasmas 22, 082109 (2015)) for k = 0.123 (see heat_front and the diagnostic-windows tutorial). Lindl et al. (Phys. Plasmas 11, 339 (2004), eq. 4-24) give c = 1.20e-3, a = 1.86, b = 0.54, the scaling the WallLossAlbedo defaults come from; it heats about 20% more mass at 300 eV, 1 ns. T is the incident-flux radiation temperature (Lindl's convention; Hammer & Rosen drive with the surface temperature, a few percent lower for gold late in a pulse).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.HeatFront"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.LipClosure","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"LipClosure(; speed = 0.022, exponent = 0.5, depth = 0.24, hold_time = 0.4,\n           hold = (6.0, 17.0), after = WallLossAlbedo())\n\nAn entrance-hole lip whose closure is computed from the local conditions (hohlraum(; leh_closure = :computed)). The gold blown off the lip fills the hole from its rim inward; the plasma edge (the radius at which the blow-off turns opaque) sits a depth d(t) inside the initial radius r₀ and moves as\n\ndd/dt = (1 − h) v(T) + h (depth − d)/hold_time,    v(T) = speed (T/100 eV)^exponent.\n\nHere T is the radiation temperature incident on the lip; the sound speed of x-ray-ablated gold, c_s ∝ T^½, sets the free expansion. The laser hold h ∈ [0, 1] is the smooth step 3x² − 2x³ of the laser intensity through the hole between hold[1] and hold[2]. That intensity is the power of the beams entering the hole over its initial area (TW/mm²). While the beams pass at high power, the blow-off stagnates against the laser-heated plasma in a dense ridge depth inside the rim. Chen et al., PoP 27, 022702 (2020), observe the ridge forming on the rise to peak where the outer beams cross the lip blow-off, after which it persists. They attribute the open LEH to the laser heating of the plasma inside the LEH region. A lip that closed further in a long foot is pushed back to the ridge; Schneider et al. 2015 observe closure in the low-power part and reopening at peak. Below hold[1], in the foot and after the laser turns off, the edge moves in freely at v(T).\n\ndrive_history integrates d(t) per lip from times[1] (open there) and maps it onto the lip elements: a lip meshed in rings between its inner radius and r₀ (the floor of the closure) has each ring the closed fraction s of its area outside the edge, so the open area is exact at any radial resolution. A closed part re-emits with the albedo of after, α = s α_after, and 1 − s escapes (as for GapClosure). A single solve treats the lip as open unless given its closed fraction per element as burnthrough. lip_depth integrates the closure for given histories.\n\nThe defaults are calibrated, and the research notes section \"Computed LEH closure (2026-10-24)\" records the fit. speed, depth and hold_time are fitted to the measured effective LEH radius of five HDC basecamp shots (Chen 2020 Fig. 27, 3.37 mm LEHs, chen2020_leh()), with the lip temperature that the model computes for the basecamp history. The hold intensities bracket the rise, with free closure at 110 TW and the hold from about 300 TW of 400. The fit has an rms of 0.033 mm over 31 points, about the shot-to-shot scatter. depth 0.24 mm matches the closure at peak of Young et al. 2020 (SXI, from 1.83 to 1.57 mm, 0.26) and Schneider et al. 2015 (0.84 of 1.55 mm, 0.25). On 3.1–3.64 mm LEHs a common depth and a common fraction of r₀ are indistinguishable, and the depth is used because the ridge stands where the beams meet the blow-off. After the laser turns off the measured edge moves in ≤ 0.02 mm/ns over 1.7 ns (LASNEX 0.07–0.11), as the fitted free speed gives at the falling temperature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.LipClosure"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.Material","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Material(name; albedo = 0.0, conversion = 0.0)\n\nSurface properties for the radiation model. albedo (a number or AlbedoModel) is the x-ray re-emission fraction; conversion (a number or a Conversion) is the fraction of absorbed laser power re-emitted as x-rays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Material"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.RadiusSchedule","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"RadiusSchedule\n\nA capsule radius history, R(t)/R₀ against time (ns), for CapsuleAlbedo's radius and moving_hohlraum's capsule_shrink; radius_fraction(s, t) evaluates it. Concrete schedules: TabulatedRadius (a table, linear, held at its ends), ScaledRadius (another schedule with its time and radius axes scaled) and TrajectoryRadius (a kinematic trajectory through published anchors). A new kind (a rocket model, say) subtypes this and defines radius_fraction. Both keywords also take a (times, R/R₀) table, which becomes a TabulatedRadius, or a function t -> R/R₀.\n\nWhich radius a published history follows (ablation front, ablator–fuel interface, fuel center of mass) and which shot it belongs to matters: research notes, \"Capsule radius schedules: published histories and the API (2026-10-26)\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.RadiusSchedule"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.RosselandTransmission","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"RosselandTransmission(; reference_temperature = 325.0, exponent = 1.5)\n\nPost-burnthrough transmission of a gold foil that follows the radiation temperature T (eV) of the flux on it, for BurnthroughAlbedo(; transmission = RosselandTransmission()). The optical depth of the foil scales with the Rosseland opacity of gold,\n\nτ(T) = τ_ref (T / reference_temperature)^(-exponent),\n\nand the transmission is eddington_transmission(τ(T)), where τ_ref is the optical_depth of the foil (5 at 2.5 µm). The exponent is that of Hammer & Rosen's power-law fit to the Rosseland opacity of gold, 1/K = g T^1.5 ρ^-0.2 (Phys. Plasmas 10, 1829 (2003), eq. 31, fitted over 100–200 eV), so the mean free path grows as T^1.5. The reference temperature is the middle of the 300–350 eV at which Lester et al. (Phys. Rev. E 114, L023201 (2026)) evaluate τ ∼ 5: at the reference the transmission equals the constant default. Hammer & Rosen's fit also reproduces that point: with the as-built areal mass of 2.5 µm of gold at Lester's expanded density of 1 g/cm³ it gives τ = 5.9 at 325 eV (derived, extrapolating the fit). The density is held at that expansion; since K ∝ ρ^0.2, solid gold would double τ. Where no flux is known (T = nothing) the reference temperature is used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.RosselandTransmission"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.ScaledRadius","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"ScaledRadius(base; time_scale = 1.0, radius_scale = 1.0, source = \"\")\n\nThe RadiusSchedule base stretched in time and radius:\n\nR(t)/R₀ = radius_scale · base(t / time_scale),\n\nso a feature of base at time t₁ (its minimum, say) falls at time_scale · t₁. For a TabulatedRadius base the table times are scaled once (base.t .* time_scale, interpolated as a separate table). A scaled schedule is derived: it stands in for a shot whose own history is unpublished (e.g. Zylstra's LEH pair on Kritcher 2024's N210808 capsule, its minimum at 9.18 ns moved to the bang time of each shot). source defaults to the source of the base with the factors appended.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ScaledRadius"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.TabulatedAlbedo","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"TabulatedAlbedo(times, values)\n\nAlbedo interpolated linearly in time (ns) and held constant outside the table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TabulatedAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.TabulatedRadius","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"TabulatedRadius(t, r; source = \"\")\n\nA RadiusSchedule from a table: r = R/R₀ at the times t (ns, strictly increasing), linear between them and held at the ends. source records where the table comes from (paper and figure, and whether it is published or derived). kritcher2024_radius gives the published N221204 and N210808 tables.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TabulatedRadius"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.Target","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Target(components; closed = false, visibility = :centroid)\n\nAssembled target geometry. Each patch side that radiates becomes an element (the unknowns of the radiation model): one element per patch for one-sided surfaces and apertures, two for two-sided surfaces. The back side of a one-sided surface absorbs laser light as a loss but is not modeled radiatively.\n\nclosed = true declares that the elements (apertures included) form a closed enclosure, so RadiationModel balances its view factors by default (enforce_closure!). hohlraum sets it; a target assembled from its components does not, since added parts such as foils and sombreros may lie outside the enclosure. Declare it (here, or cad_target(...; closed = true)) when apertures cover every opening (open_fraction shows where facets leak). On bored entrance holes, waist gaps, windows and CAD cavities so closed, balancing brings the loss through the apertures within 0.2% of a continuum Monte Carlo with :sampled visibility (raw factors up to 0.8% off) and the capsule drive within 0.07%, and moves the capsule a₂/a₀ by at most 0.08 pp (toward the reference where it moves it most). With :centroid, rims leave the raw loss 2–3% high and balancing brings it within 0.4%, but moves a₂/a₀ by 0.1–0.3 pp, toward the reference or away from it. Flat facets must be watertight for it: balancing a cracked mesh adds the exchange the cracks lose (research notes, \"Closure on bored, windowed and CAD cavities\").\n\nvisibility is the view_factors visibility rule RadiationModel uses by default: :centroid (one ray per pair) or :sampled (the visible fraction of pairs partly shadowed by a rim). Targets with rims set :sampled: cad_target and visrad_target (bored entrance holes, gaps, CAD walls), where one centroid ray leaves row sums up to 1% off on walls and 6% on LEH apertures; hohlraum and assembled targets keep :centroid: on flush entrance holes the largest row-sum error is on the wall, 0.3–0.7%, and after balancing :sampled moves the drive by less than 3e-5 relative and a₂/a₀ by less than 0.03 pp for 3–4 times the build (research notes, \"Partial visibility\" and \"Visibility default on the GUI presets\").\n\nA Target is immutable in all its fields but has identity (a mutable struct of const fields), so that caches of derived data (the footprint of a Dante view, the pixels of an imager) hold it weakly and release their entries once the target is unreachable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Target"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.TemperatureAlbedo","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"TemperatureAlbedo(temperatures, values; reference_temperature = 250.0)\n\nAlbedo interpolated linearly in the incident-flux radiation temperature (eV) and held constant outside the table; reference_temperature is used where no flux is known. solve iterates it to self-consistency, like WallLossAlbedo.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TemperatureAlbedo"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.TrajectoryRadius","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"TrajectoryRadius(; start, peak_time, peak_radius, velocity, bang, minimum, radius,\n                 source = \"\")\n\nA kinematic RadiusSchedule through the published anchors of an implosion, for a shot without a published radius history. The surface stays at its initial radius radius (mm) until start (ns), when the first shock reaches it, as at the shock-1 breakout of a keyhole from the ablator. It then falls along a cubic Hermite segment to peak_radius (mm) at peak_time, at rest at start and with the inward speed velocity (km/s) at peak_time. A second segment takes it to minimum (R/R₀) at bang, at rest there, and the radius stays at that value after bang; the rebound is not modeled. R/R₀ = R/radius. The anchors are a keyhole breakout, the bang time and the values that HYDRA tables give: the peak implosion velocity, its time and the limb-minimum radius at peak velocity. Kritcher et al., PRE 106, 025201 (2022), list these values in Table I. Both segments must stay monotone, with the speed at most 3× the segment mean; otherwise the constructor throws an ArgumentError.\n\nThe schedule is derived, an interpolant that does not model ablation physics. It is checked against Kritcher 2024 Fig. 1, which was not used to build it. N221204's anchors are a keyhole start at 3.864 ns, bang at 9.53 ns and 383 km/s, with peak velocity 0.44 ns before bang at 197 µm; these two values come from the N210207/N210307/N210808 HYDRA rows. With these anchors, R/R₀ lies within 0.019 of the published history (0.029 in R²) from 3 ns to peak velocity. Research notes, \"Capsule radius schedules: published histories and the API (2026-10-26)\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TrajectoryRadius"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.WallLossAlbedo","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"WallLossAlbedo(; coefficient = 0.32, temperature_exponent = -0.7, time_exponent = -0.38,\n               reference_temperature = 250.0, t0 = 0.1, range = (0.0, 0.95),\n               factor = 1.0, factor_exponent = 0.0, factor_temperature = 300.0,\n               liner = 0.0, front = HeatFront(), release = 1.0)\n\nMarshak-wave wall-loss albedo, 1 - α = c (T/100 eV)^p (t/1 ns)^q, with defaults for gold from Lindl's scaling (T in heV, t in ns). t is clamped below at t0, and α to range. The radiation temperature is the incident-flux temperature of each element, so solve iterates it to self-consistency; reference_temperature (eV) is used where no flux is known. The form is Lindl's for a wall held at T since t = 0; drive_history evaluates it instead at the effective time of each element, from the temperature history of the element (wall_history).\n\nWall material. factor f and factor_exponent δ scale the gold loss to that of another wall material:\n\n1 - α = r(T) c (T/100 eV)^p (t/1 ns)^q,    r(T) = f (T / factor_temperature)^δ.\n\nThe factor r(T) is the ratio of the Marshak wall losses of the two materials. Rosen (2005) gives E/A ∝ T^3.1 t^0.57 for U₀.₆Dy₀.₂Au₀.₂ and T^3.3 t^0.6 for Au, a ratio ∝ T^-0.22 t^-0.05. The factor applies to the gold loss after its range clamp, so the albedo of a DU wall may exceed the gold cap. du_wall_loss() is the preset for an Au-lined depleted-uranium wall. A loss above 1 leaves albedo 0, and the wall then absorbs all it receives; such a loss arises for a lossier material (r > 1) where the gold loss is already large. A range reaching below 0, as for the unclamped power law of a closure study, lowers that floor to the lower end of the range. The constructor throws an ArgumentError for a negative coefficient, factor, liner or release, a nonpositive t0, reference_temperature or factor_temperature, and a range with lo > hi or hi > 1.\n\nLiner. liner is the areal mass (g/cm², 0 for none) of a gold liner on the inside of such a wall. Until the heat front has crossed the liner, the wall loses like gold. The areal mass of the front is the constant-temperature similarity form of front (a HeatFront) at the T and t of the element, m_F = c_F (T/100 eV)^a t^b. This form does not depend on the temperature history. It is exact at constant T and within a few percent of the drive integral for slow ramps. The ratio becomes\n\nr_eff = w + (1 - w) r(T),    w = min(1, liner / m_F),\n\nwhere w is the share of the heated mass in the gold liner. This energy-content blend is an engineering choice. It is continuous in T and t, so the albedo iteration stays smooth; a sharp front would switch to the loss rate of the substrate as soon as m_F passes the liner. With the defaults (factor = 1, no liner) the albedo is identical to that of gold.\n\nRe-emission. release scales the stored energy that the wall gives back under a falling drive in a drive_history (wall_release). The released energy is the linear (Duhamel) response of the self-similar loss of the wall, times release. The default 1.0 is calibrated against 1-D Lagrangian radiation hydrodynamics of an ablating gold wall in examples/late_time_wall.jl. That model uses the Hammer & Rosen 2003 power laws, P = 0.25 e/V, flux-limited diffusion and a Marshak boundary, and the closure is given the constant-T scaling of that wall. Drops of 10, 30 and 50% in the drive temperature after a NIF-like peak need 1.07, 0.99 and 0.91 for the energy the wall takes in over the next 1.5 ns. On the pulses of three NIF shots, 0-D hohlraums need 0.92–1.06 (range 0.9–1.07). That wall reproduces Hammer & Rosen's ablative self-similar solution (Rosen, OSTI 885380 (2005), Lecture 1) to 1–3% in stored energy and 3–7% in heated mass at constant and ramped temperature, within the first-order accuracy of that solution. A static (non-ablating) wall needs 0.77 (the value before 2026-10-25): its cooled surface throttles the release more, while Lindl's loss, which this model uses, is the ablative one. Research notes, \"Wall re-emission and the capsule radius (2026-10-24)\" and \"Capsule absorption, conversion and the ablating-wall release (2026-10-25)\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.WallLossAlbedo"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.ablator-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Capsule ablator (defaults: albedo 0.3, conversion 0.3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ablator"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.albedo","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"albedo(model, t, T = nothing)\nalbedo(material, t = 0.0)\n\nX-ray albedo at time t (ns); temperature-dependent models use the radiation temperature T (eV) when given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.albedo"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.aperture-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"aperture(name, mesh; surface = nothing, closure = nothing)\n\nTransparent diagnostic surface (e.g. a laser entrance hole): rays pass through it and its incident x-ray flux is the flux escaping through it.\n\nWith closure, a GapClosure, the aperture is an opening that plasma closes to x-rays during the pulse while laser light and lines of sight still pass. An example is the lip of a closing laser entrance hole, hohlraum(; leh_closure). The aperture re-emits the fraction α(t) = s α_after of its incident flux, and the fraction 1 - s escapes and is booked as escaped by energy_balance. The aperture does not convert laser light. In the Monte Carlo reference (montecarlo_incident), paths escape through open apertures and are re-emitted diffusely from a lip with the albedo of the lip (given albedo = albedos(target, time)). With a LipClosure, the closed fraction of each element is drive_history's state instead of a function of time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.aperture"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.areal_mass-Tuple{BurnthroughAlbedo}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Areal mass (g/cm²) of a burnthrough layer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.areal_mass"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.burnthrough_time-Tuple{BurnthroughAlbedo, Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"burnthrough_time(model::BurnthroughAlbedo, times, Tr) -> Float64\n\nTime (ns) at which the heat front driven by the radiation temperature history Tr (eV, at times, piecewise linear; heat_front) reaches the back of the layer, or Inf if it does not within times. The function returns only this estimate and does not use an explicit burnthrough of the model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.burnthrough_time"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.component_index-Tuple{Target, Symbol}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"component_index(target, name) -> Int\n\nIndex of the component named name (the name of a piece; part_pieces gives the pieces of a part).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.component_index"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.conversion_efficiency","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"conversion_efficiency(model, t = 0.0, I = nothing)\n\nLaser-to-x-ray conversion efficiency of model (a Conversion or a number) at time t (ns) and absorbed laser intensity I (W/cm²; required by an intensity table).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.conversion_efficiency"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.diffusion_transmission","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"diffusion_transmission(τ, α = 1.5)\n\nSteady transmitted fraction of a planar foil of optical depth τ at its front, whose opacity varies as T^(-α) through it (front at the drive temperature, rear radiating into vacuum; see DiffusionTransmission): the root of (3τ/4) t = (4/(4 + α)) [1 - (t/2)^((4 + α)/4)], capped at 1. α = 0 gives eddington_transmission(τ). Newton's method from the gray value, which bounds the root from above (the root is at most the gray value for α ≥ 0); generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diffusion_transmission"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.du_wall_loss-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"du_wall_loss(; factor = 0.836, factor_exponent = -0.2, liner = 19.3 × 0.7e-4, kwargs...)\n\nWallLossAlbedo for an Au-lined depleted-uranium hohlraum wall: the gold loss times r(T) = factor (T/300 eV)^factor_exponent, gold until the heat front crosses the liner (g/cm²). kwargs pass through (the Lindl coefficients for gold by default).\n\nfactor_exponent −0.2: the temperature scaling of the cocktail/Au loss ratio, T^-0.22 from Rosen's Hammer–Rosen fits (M. D. Rosen, \"Fundamentals of ICF hohlraums\", UCRL-JRNL- 211678 / OSTI 885380 (2005), §VI) and T^-0.19 between LASNEX's 97.6 / 90.8 / 88.6% at 185 / 275 / 305 eV (Schein et al., PRL 98, 175003 (2007)).\nfactor 0.836 at 300 eV is calibrated in this model. With it the DU wall is worth 6.5% of laser power against gold at the same capsule radiation temperature, the measured equivalence of the first DU shots (Döppner 2015, citing Kline 2013 and Callahan 2012). Callahan et al., PoP 27, 072704 (2020), report a drive benefit of about 6.5% in both simulations and experiments. The calibration case is the NIF high-foot hohlraum of Döppner et al. 2015 at peak, with 5.75 × 9.43 mm, 3.1 mm LEHs, a 1.125 mm capsule, 390 TW × 0.86 coupling, conversion 0.9 and the wall source in inner and outer bands. The wall loss is taken at the effective time 2.5 ns, which the wall history of drive_history gives a wall about 3 ns into a NIF peak. Over effective times 2–4 ns the factor ranges over 0.827–0.838. At equal power it raises the capsule Tr by 4.8 eV against the measured +5 ± 6 eV of Döppner's T-1 pair (N140311 Au 317 eV, N140520 DU 322 eV, 391/393 TW). It agrees with the ratio for the U:Dy:Au cocktail, Rosen's \"about 0.83\" and Schein's 17.5% (0.825). Before 2026-10-24 the factor was calibrated at 13 ns since the pulse start with the constant-temperature evaluation, and it was 0.785. That wall was too reflective, and the loss reduction was worth too little.\nliner is the 0.7 µm gold of Döppner 2015's DU hohlraums (7 µm DU, 22.3 µm gold backing); HYBRID-E's liner thickness is unpublished. The front crosses it in about 0.4 ns at 150 eV, so it matters only in the picket.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.du_wall_loss"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.eddington_transmission-Tuple{Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"eddington_transmission(τ)\n\nFraction of the incident flux that a gray, planar foil of optical depth τ transmits in the Eddington approximation with a Marshak boundary condition,\n\nF_trans / F_inc = (4/3) / (τ + 2/3) = 4 / (3τ + 2),\n\neq. 2 of R. S. Lester et al., Phys. Rev. E 114, L023201 (2026), doi:10.1103/k8dz-pjdx. It is a diffusion result for optically thick foils: it exceeds 1 below τ = 2/3, where it is capped at 1. BurnthroughAlbedo takes its default transmission from it (see optical_depth). Generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.eddington_transmission"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.element_quadrature-Tuple{Target, Integer, Integer}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"element_quadrature(target, e, level) -> (points, weights, normals)\n\nQuadrature over element e from 4^level sub-triangles per triangle: sub-triangle centroids weighted by area, with the element normal. On a component with a CurvedSurface, the points are projected onto the surface and carry its normals (on the radiating side of the element) and curved-patch areas; an element of more than two facets (imported CAD) instead gets as many points as a two-triangle element, from the pieces of its facets lumped into cells of the element tangent plane. Weights sum to the element area.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.element_quadrature"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.element_sector-Tuple{Target, Integer}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"element_sector(target, e) -> (r0, r1, φa, φb)\n\nPolar sector of an element of a component with a DiskSurface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.element_sector"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.elements-Tuple{Target, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"elements(target, names...) -> Vector{Int}\n\nElement indices of the named parts or components (both sides of two-sided components); the name of a part stands for all its pieces (see part_pieces).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.elements"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.foil_transmission","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"foil_transmission(model::BurnthroughAlbedo, T = nothing)\n\nFraction of the incident flux that a burned-through foil transmits when the flux on it has radiation temperature T (eV): the transmission of the model, evaluated at T for a RosselandTransmission or DiffusionTransmission (at its reference temperature when T is nothing). NaN for a model with an explicit after.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.foil_transmission"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.gold-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"gold(; albedo = 0.8, conversion = 0.9)\n\nGold hohlraum wall for static solves. Its laser-to-x-ray conversion is a constant 0.9 whatever the albedo. Both defaults are the inputs of a published static VisRad model of a NIF hohlraum (Young et al., PoP 27, 082702 (2020)). The conversion lies within the 0.85–0.95 published for NIF hohlraums near peak power, above the 0.68–0.85 of 1 ns Nova and Omega hohlraums, which gold(conversion = 0.75) suits. The research notes section \"Material defaults and their sources (2026-11-18)\" gives the sources. For a drive history on NIF, use nif_gold, whose albedo and conversion follow the drive. For a directly irradiated foil, use foil_material.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.gold"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.heat_front-Tuple{HeatFront, Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"heat_front(front::HeatFront, times, Tr) -> Vector\n\nAreal mass (g/cm²) behind the heat front at each of times (ns) for the radiation temperature history Tr (eV) sampled at those times, piecewise linear between them (trapezoidal drive integral), from zero before times[1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.heat_front"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.lip_depth-Tuple{LipClosure, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"lip_depth(model::LipClosure, times, Tr, intensity; depth = 0.0, floor = Inf) -> Vector\n\nClosure depth d (mm inside the initial LEH radius) of a LipClosure lip at times (ns), given the radiation temperature on the lip Tr (eV) and the laser intensity through the hole intensity (TW/mm² over its initial area) at those times: the trapezoidal mean of v(T) and of the hold over each step, integrated exactly for the relaxation of the hold (as drive_history does). The integration starts from depth at times[1], and the depth is capped at floor (the radial extent of the lip).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.lip_depth"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.nelements-Tuple{Target}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Number of radiating elements (patch sides) of a target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nelements"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.nif_conversion-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"nif_conversion(; efficiency = 0.7, time_exponent = 0.12, range = (0.0, 0.95), kwargs...)\n\nThe conversion preset for NIF hohlraums: Lindl's Nova 0.7 t^0.12 (t from the start of the pulse, ns) capped at 0.95. Extrapolated to a NIF pulse it gives 0.80 at 3 ns, 0.85 at 5 ns and 0.90 at 8 ns, the 85–90% inferred from NIF vacuum hohlraums at 235–345 eV (Olson et al., PoP 19, 053301 (2012), abstract); the cap is the 10¹⁴ W/cm² plateau of Dewald 2008 and the value of the only published VisRad energetics calibration (η 0.95 with albedo 0.77, Au, 400 TW; Swadling et al., PoP 32, 052707 (2025)). The preset is derived, and the research notes section \"Wall material, conversion and capsule energetics (2026-10-24)\" gives the derivation. It is the conversion of the drive-history walls nif_gold and nif_uranium; gold and uranium keep the constant 0.9.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_conversion"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.nif_gold-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"nif_gold(; albedo = WallLossAlbedo(), conversion = nif_conversion())\n\nThe gold wall of a NIF drive history: Lindl's Marshak wall loss, evaluated by drive_history at the temperature history of each element, and the rising nif_conversion(), 0.7 t^0.12 ≤ 0.95 with t the time since the pulse start (at solve's default time = 0 it is 0.53, so a static solve needs gold).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_gold"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.nif_uranium-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"nif_uranium(; albedo = du_wall_loss(), conversion = nif_conversion())\n\nThe Au-lined depleted-uranium wall of a NIF drive history (HYBRID-E's): du_wall_loss() and the rising nif_conversion() of its gold liner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_uranium"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.optical_depth-Tuple{BurnthroughAlbedo}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"optical_depth(model::BurnthroughAlbedo)\n\nGray optical depth of a burned-through gold foil, scaled linearly in areal mass from τ ∼ 5 at 2.5 µm (Lester et al., Phys. Rev. E 114, L023201 (2026), eq. 1 at a fixed Rosseland mean free path): 5 × areal_mass(model) / (19.3 g/cm³ × 2.5 µm). The default transmission is eddington_transmission(optical_depth(model)). An explicit transmission or after does not change this estimate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.optical_depth"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.part_pieces-Tuple{Target, Symbol}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"part_pieces(target, name) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIndices of the components that make up name: the pieces of the part name, or else the one component of that name, so a piece stays reachable by its name. A KeyError for a name that is neither.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.part_pieces"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.parts-Tuple{Target}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"parts(target) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe user's parts of a target, in the order of their first components: the components' parts. The parts of a primitive target are its component names; a CAD part that curved_components split into p/cyl1, …, p/rest is the one part p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parts"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.radius_fraction-Tuple{CapsuleAlbedo, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"radius_fraction(m::CapsuleAlbedo, t) -> R(t)/R₀\n\nThe effective radius over the mesh radius of a CapsuleAlbedo at time t (ns): that of its radius schedule, 1 without one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.radius_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.radius_fraction-Tuple{TabulatedRadius, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"radius_fraction(s, t) -> R(t)/R₀\n\nThe capsule radius over its initial radius at time t (ns) on the schedule s: a RadiusSchedule, a (times, R/R₀) table, a function t -> R/R₀, or nothing (R = R₀, 1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.radius_fraction"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.transform-2","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Component with its mesh rigidly moved (x ↦ R*x + t).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.transform"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.uranium-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"uranium(; albedo = du_wall_loss(), conversion = 0.9)\n\nAu-lined depleted-uranium hohlraum wall: du_wall_loss() and the constant conversion 0.9 of gold (the conversion happens in the gold liner). For a drive history on NIF use nif_uranium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.uranium"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.visible-Tuple{Target, StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64}, StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64}}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"visible(target, a, b) -> Bool\n\nWhether the open segment between points a and b is free of opaque surfaces (apertures are transparent): visible(t, Vec3(0, 0, 0), Vec3(0, 0, 8)) is true when no opaque surface lies between the target center and a point 8 mm up the axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visible"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.wall_loss_factor-Tuple{WallLossAlbedo, Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"wall_loss_factor(model::WallLossAlbedo, t, T)\n\nThe ratio r_eff of the wall loss of the model to that of gold at time t (ns) and radiation temperature T (eV): 1 for gold, f (T/T_f)^δ for another material, blended toward 1 while the heat front is inside a gold liner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.wall_loss_factor"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.HohlraumProfile","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"HohlraumProfile(z, r)\n\nWall profile of a hohlraum of revolution about z (hohlraum(; profile)). The profile has break points at heights z (mm, nondecreasing, from the bottom end z[1] = −L/2 to the top end z[end] = L/2) with wall radii r (mm, positive). Between consecutive breaks the wall is a section. The section is a cylinder where the two radii are equal, a conical frustum where they differ, and a flat ring (a step in radius) where two breaks share a height. A frustraum is two frusta meeting at the waist (frustraum_profile), and a multi-section hohlraum has a radius per break.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.HohlraumProfile"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.Sombrero","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"Sombrero(; inner_radius = 4.0, outer_radius = 9.2, offset = 0.623, material = BLACK,\n         nr = 4, nphi = 96)\n\nEntrance-hole shield (\"sombrero\") for hohlraum(; sombrero). The shield is a flat annular sheet around each end of the hohlraum, from inner_radius to outer_radius (mm). Its mid-plane lies offset mm from the entrance-hole plane (length/2) toward the waist, or beyond the hole when negative. hohlraum meshes it as an annulus_mesh of nr × nphi patches in equal-area rings, with its exact DiskSurface. The polygon edges lie within r (1 − cos(π/nphi)) of the circles, 5 µm at 9.2 mm with 96 sides.\n\nThe defaults are representative of a NIF symcap shield, relative to the inner face of the entrance-hole washer. They are a bore of 4.0 mm and a rim of 9.2 mm radius, with a 39 µm sheet whose mid-plane lies 0.623 mm inside that face. Both shields are identical mirror images. A real sheet is dimpled, within about ±0.12 mm of its mean plane, and extends past the rim where it is mounted; the model uses the flat mean plane.\n\nmaterial is an engineering default, BLACK, which absorbs laser light and emits nothing. The shield lies outside the cavity and has no view into it, since beside the wall the wall hides the hole from the shield. Its albedo and conversion therefore do not change the radiation of the hohlraum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Sombrero"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.WallWindow","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"WallWindow(; phi, z = 0.0, width, height, material = nothing, open = false, name = nothing,\n           gap = 0.0, gap_material = nothing)\nWallWindow(; phi, z = 0.0, diameter, segments = 0, ...)\n\nDiagnostic window cut into a hohlraum wall (see hohlraum), centered at azimuth phi (degrees from +x) and axial position z (mm). Its shape is drawn on the unrolled wall, with arc length along the wall against z. The unrolling is an isometry of the cylinder, so areas are those of the flat shape. The shape is:\n\na rectangle width mm of arc by height mm along the axis, or\na disk of diameter mm, area π d²/4. Its edge is a regular polygon of segments sides, enlarged to the area of the disk. By default there is about one side per wall cell, with a multiple of 8 and at least 32 sides. The area of the window is then exact, while its edge stays within π² d/6 segments² of the circle (3 µm for a 2 mm window with 32 sides).\nA closed window (open = false) is an opaque patch of wall that re-emits with a separate material (albedo and laser conversion), e.g. a thin gold foil whose albedo follows BurnthroughAlbedo. With material = nothing, the wall material is used.\nAn open window (open = true) is a hole: an aperture that laser light passes through and through which its incident x-ray flux escapes.\n\ngap > 0 (mm) surrounds the window with an assembly gap of that width, a ring around a disk or a frame around a rectangle. The gap is a separate component Symbol(name, :_gap), open by default or a closed patch of gap_material. name defaults to :window_<k> for the k-th window.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.WallWindow"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.capsule_counts-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"capsule_counts(capsule_radius, capsule_h) -> (; capsule_ntheta, capsule_nphi)\n\nCapsule mesh counts of hohlraum for elements of at most capsule_h (mm) along a meridian or the equator, with capsule_nphi a multiple of 4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.capsule_counts"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.equal_area_radius-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"equal_area_radius(r, n)\n\nCircumradius of the regular n-gon with the area of a circle of radius r: r √(2π / (n sin(2π/n))). Its edge runs from r π²/3n² outside the circle at the vertices to r π²/6n² inside at mid-side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.equal_area_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.foil-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"foil(name, center, normal; width, height = width, n = 20, material = foil_material(),\n     two_sided = true, rotation = 0.0)\n\nRectangular foil (e.g. a backlighter) with front face along normal. rotation (radians) spins it about its normal. The default material is a directly irradiated gold foil (albedo 0.8, conversion 0.8); foil_material lists the other foil elements with their suggested ranges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.foil"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.frustraum_profile-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"frustraum_profile(; length, waist_radius, end_radius, bottom_end_radius = end_radius)\n\nHohlraumProfile of a frustraum: two conical frusta joined at the waist z = 0 (radius waist_radius, mm), narrowing to end_radius at the top end (z = length/2) and bottom_end_radius at the bottom. The wall angle from the axis is atand((waist_radius − end_radius) / (length/2)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.frustraum_profile"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.hohlraum-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"hohlraum(; length = 9.4, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9,\n         nz = 36, nphi = 48, nr_cap = 6, nr_leh = 4, capsule_ntheta = 24, capsule_nphi = 32,\n         h = nothing, capsule_h = nothing, capsule_mesh = :compact, wall = gold(),\n         capsule = ablator(), capsule_center = (0,0,0), windows = (), corner_radius = 0,\n         nr_corner = nothing, leh_closure = nothing, leh_open_radius = nothing,\n         nr_lip = nothing, sombrero = nothing, top_end = nothing, bottom_end = nothing,\n         profile = nothing)\n\nCylindrical hohlraum along z (or, with profile, a hohlraum of revolution of that profile), centered on TCC (dimensions in mm), with components\n\n:wall — inner cylinder wall (radiates inward)\n:cap_top, :cap_bottom — end-cap washers between the LEH and the wall\n:leh_top, :leh_bottom — laser entrance holes (apertures; their incident flux is the x-ray loss through the LEH)\n:leh_top_lip, :leh_bottom_lip — with leh_closure, the closing lip of each hole (after its hole)\n:capsule — spherical capsule (omitted with capsule_radius = 0)\n:window_1, … — diagnostic windows in the wall, one component per WallWindow in windows (or the window name). Closed windows are opaque wall patches with a separate material, and open windows are apertures. A window with a gap is followed by its gap component :window_1_gap, ….\n:sombrero_top, :sombrero_bottom — with sombrero, the entrance-hole shields (last)\n\nLaser light that strikes the outside of the wall or caps, by clipping on the LEH edge, is reported as the _outside back-side loss of those components. Light that leaves through an open window is :missed. The wall, caps, LEHs, windows and capsule carry their exact CylinderSurface, DiskSurface and SphereSurface, so the radiation model uses the true geometry. The target is declared closed, so RadiationModel balances its view factors (enforce_closure!) unless given closed = false. Windows, their gaps and closing lips are apertures, and the sombreros have no view of the cavity. With open windows and gaps, the balanced loss and capsule drive agree with a continuum Monte Carlo within 0.1% (research notes, \"Closure on bored, windowed and CAD cavities\").\n\nEach end (top_end at +length/2, bottom_end at −length/2) is set as follows:\n\nby default, an entrance hole of radius leh_radius;\na number gives an entrance hole of that radius. The ring counts of its cap and hole come from h when given, and otherwise from nr_cap and nr_leh. With leh_closure, its open radius is leh_open_radius scaled by the ratio of the radii;\n:closed gives a cap with no hole. The mesh of the hole and of its lip are merged into the cap, with the wall material;\n:open gives neither cap nor hole. The cavity is open there, to a physics package placed against it or to nothing (a halfraum).\n\nA target with an open end is not declared closed, so its view factors are not balanced, and the light that leaves through the open end and strikes nothing is lost. Such a target has no fillet (corner_radius = 0) or sombrero. The defaults leave the hohlraum exactly as without these keywords.\n\nWith windows, the wall and end-cap meshes contain the window edges as grid lines (conforming_grid). The axial grid of the wall includes their z edges, and the wall and caps share the azimuthal grid with their φ edges. Each rectangular window (and gap frame) is therefore tiled exactly by whole patches, and the caps still meet the wall at shared vertices. A circular window is cut in the same way, as a square box around it and its gap, half a wall cell wider. The box is re-meshed to conform to the circles (_circular_window) and stitched to the surrounding grid at the box vertices. Without windows the mesh is the uniform nz × nphi grid.\n\nMesh size: h (mm) sets the wall, cap and LEH counts from an element size instead (hohlraum_counts), and capsule_h sets the counts for the capsule (capsule_counts). These replace the corresponding counts.\n\ncorner_radius (mm) rounds the wall–cap corners into quarter-circle fillets of that radius, as for a hohlraum machined with curved corners. The wall stops corner_radius short of each end, and the caps stop corner_radius short of the wall radius. Each fillet is nr_corner bands, by default about one wall cell of arc each and at least 2. The bands are the components :corner_top_1, … (from the wall to the cap) and :corner_bottom_1, …, of the wall material. Each band is the exact frustum through its two rim circles (ConeSurface). The fillet is therefore the chord polygon of the arc, with sagitta corner_radius (1 − cos(π/4k)) (20 µm for 1 mm in 4 bands), and the bands, wall and caps meet at shared vertices.\n\nleh_closure, a GapClosure, closes each entrance hole to x-rays from leh_radius down to leh_open_radius during the pulse. The annulus between them is a closing lip (aperture(...; closure)), transparent to laser light and lines of sight. Its incident flux escapes until closure.closure (ns), and across the next closure.width ns the lip turns into plasma that re-emits with the albedo of closure.after, the wall albedo, e.g. WallLossAlbedo(). In the engineering default for low-fill NIF hohlraums, the hole stays open through the foot and closes over about 0.5 ns at the rise to peak, to leh_open_radius = 0.84 leh_radius. Schneider et al. 2015 find about 0.84 r₀ through the peak, and Young et al. 2020 use 85% static radii (research notes, \"Closing entrance holes\"). Beams pass the lip unclipped, since the path of the laser stays open (Chen et al. 2020).\n\nleh_closure = :computed (or a LipClosure) computes the closure instead. The plasma edge of each lip moves in from leh_radius at the sound speed of the gold blown off the lip, and stays at a fixed depth inside the rim while the beams pass at high power. The LipClosure is calibrated on measured LEH radii, and drive_history integrates it per hole from the lip temperature and the beams entering that hole. The lip then extends down to leh_open_radius (default 0.6 leh_radius, the floor of the closure) in nr_lip rings (default 6; 1 for a GapClosure).\n\nsombrero (true for the measured Sombrero(), or a Sombrero) adds an annular shield around each end, one-sided and facing away from the waist. The shield is opaque to beams and lines of sight, and it is hardware for clearance. For clipping and deposition, the power on its waist side is booked as sombrero_top_outside. The shield lies outside the cavity, and its elements have no view of the elements of the hohlraum, so their view-factor rows and columns are zero. The enclosure therefore stays closed, closed = true balancing leaves the shield elements unchanged, and the solution of the hohlraum does not change. Off by default.\n\nprofile, a HohlraumProfile, gives the wall a radius per break instead of length and radius, which must then be omitted. The profile describes a frustraum (frustraum_profile) or a multi-section hohlraum. Each section between two breaks is a separate wall component, :wall_1, :wall_2, … from the bottom, each with part = :wall. :wall therefore names the whole wall in elements, drive_history(; wall) and every name-taking analysis. Each section has its exact surface: a CylinderSurface, a ConeSurface (a frustum, meshed as planar trapezoids between parallel chords) or a DiskSurface (a flat ring where the radius steps). All sections share the azimuthal grid, so they meet at shared vertices, and the cap at each end runs from its hole to the radius of that end. With h, the azimuthal count comes from the widest radius, and the axial count of each section from its meridian length. Otherwise nz is shared out among the sections by length. Windows are cut only into cylindrical sections, each window within one section. A window on a frustum or across a break is refused, as are corner fillets. The ends, closing lips, sombreros and the capsule work as for the cylinder, and the bore of a sombrero must clear the widest radius. A profile of one cylindrical section gives exactly the cylindrical hohlraum, including the :wall component.\n\ncapsule_mesh selects the capsule mesh. :compact (the default) is a geodesic icosphere_mesh of nearly equilateral triangles with sides of about capsule_h (icosphere_frequency). Without capsule_h, the side is the lat-long meridional size π capsule_radius / capsule_ntheta. :latlong is a sphere_mesh of capsule_ntheta × capsule_nphi. The capsule mesh sets the P4 of the flux. Lat-long cells are slivers near the poles, and the compact mesh reaches the same P4 error with several times fewer elements (research notes, \"Compact capsule mesh\"). :latlong is the comparison mesh of that convergence study (examples/capsule_mesh.jl, test/capsule_mesh.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.hohlraum"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.hohlraum_counts-Tuple{}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"hohlraum_counts(; length, radius, leh_radius, h) -> (; nz, nphi, nr_cap, nr_leh)\n\nMesh counts of hohlraum for wall and end-cap elements of at most h (mm) along either side. The counts are nz axial and nphi azimuthal wall cells, nr_cap radial cap rings and nr_leh LEH rings, with sides ≤ h. nphi is a multiple of 8, so that the NIF and OMEGA port symmetries map the mesh onto itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.hohlraum_counts"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.SurfaceRegion","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"SurfaceRegion\n\nPatches of a mesh lying on one analytic surface, as found by fit_surfaces:\n\nkind: :plane, :cylinder, :cone, :sphere or :torus\nsurface: the fitted CylinderSurface, ConeSurface, SphereSurface or TorusSurface (nothing for a plane)\ncentroid: area-weighted centroid of the patches (mm)\nnormal: the unit normal of a plane, on the side that most of its area faces (zero for curved surfaces)\npatches: indices of the patches in the mesh\narea: total area of the patches (mm², flat facets)\nrms, maxdev: rms and largest distance of the vertices of the patches from the surface (mm)\nfacing: for curved surfaces, -1 when every patch faces the axis, center or spine, as on the inside of a hohlraum wall or of a cone or on a fillet in the corner of a cavity; +1 when every patch faces away from it; 0 when mixed. For planes, +1, or 0 when some patches face the other way\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SurfaceRegion"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.curved_components-Tuple{Component}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"curved_components(component; kinds = (:cylinder, :cone, :sphere, :torus), regions = nothing,\n                  maxradius = Inf, minfraction = 1e-3, kwargs...) -> Vector{Component}\ncurved_components(components; kwargs...) -> Vector{Component}\ncurved_components(target; kwargs...) -> Target\n\nThe component split into one component per cylinder, cone, sphere or torus that fit_surfaces finds in its mesh, and the rest. Other keywords are passed on to fit_surfaces, or regions gives the regions directly. Each new component has its fitted CylinderSurface, ConeSurface, SphereSurface or TorusSurface. The components are named <name>/cyl1, <name>/cyl2, …, <name>/cone1, …, <name>/sph1, …, <name>/tor1, …, by decreasing area, then <name>/rest with the other patches (planes and unrecognized faces). Material, kind, sidedness and part are those of the component. A component that one fitted surface covers whole is returned with its name and its mesh, and only gains the surface. For a remeshed CAD part, the facets from which it was remeshed should be given as reference (see fit_surfaces). A component that already has a surface, or in which no curved region is found, is returned unchanged.\n\nWith maxradius and minfraction, some regions are left in the rest. These are the regions whose radius of curvature (R/cos θ on a cone of half-angle θ, the minor radius on a torus) exceeds maxradius times the extent of the part (its bounding-box diagonal), and those of less than minfraction of the part area. Otherwise, gently curved CAD faces that are neither planar nor free-form within the tolerance of the fit each become a separate component, with radii of tens to hundreds of mm on a hohlraum half. Their flat elements are close to exact, since the sagitta of an element of size h is h²/8R. minfraction defaults to 1e-3, since small holes and edge faces of CAD fit as tiny cylinders, cones and spheres, as did the fillet pieces before tori. On a NIF CAD cavity (remeshed at 0.6 and 0.3 mm), the default keeps 68 of 158–159 components, with the incident flux on the capsule within 3e-4 and its Legendre moments within 0.04 percentage points of fitting everything (research notes, \"Fitting CAD by default\"). minfraction = 0 fits every region. maxradius is off by default.\n\nThe radiation model then integrates over the true curved surface, as it does for the primitive hohlraum. The sub-triangle points of each element are projected onto the surface with exact curved-patch areas, whatever the shape of the element, including the compact, many-triangle elements of remesh_mesh. The facets of a cylinder, cone or sphere no longer occlude pairs of points inside it. A torus is not convex, so its facets still occlude. Ray casting uses the facets. Planar regions are not given a surface, since flat elements are already exact there. DiskSurface describes the polar-sector elements of primitive end caps and does not apply to general planar CAD faces.\n\nThe pieces record their part (part), so the split is computational only. elements(target, :part), the other name-taking functions and the reports (energy_balance, component_power, write_results, report) use the part, and each piece stays reachable by its name (see parts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.curved_components"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/geometry/#Briareus.fit_surfaces-Tuple{Mesh}","page":"Geometry and targets","text":"fit_surfaces(mesh; reference = nothing, kinds = (:plane, :cylinder, :cone, :sphere, :torus),\n             rtol = 1e-3, atol = 1e-4, angle = 30.0, minpatches = 6, minarea = 0.0,\n             tol = 1e-9) -> Vector{SurfaceRegion}\n\nRegions of mesh whose patches lie on one plane, cylinder (any axis), cone, sphere or torus, by decreasing area. Patches in no region lie on none of these surfaces, such as free-form faces and patches that straddle two faces. The function applies to imported CAD, as raw facets or after remesh_mesh, and curved_components then gives the curved regions their exact surface for the radiation model.\n\nA patch belongs to a surface when every one of its vertices lies within max(atol, rtol min(R, L)) mm of it, and its normal is within angle degrees of the surface normal at its centroid, on either side, since windings do not matter. R is the radius of curvature (R/cos θ on a cone of half-angle θ), and L the extent of the region. For a plane the tolerance is atol, since CAD planes are flat within round-off. Regions grow from seed patches, by decreasing area, over patches that share a vertex, nearest first. Vertices are welded by position to tol times the extent of the mesh. Each region is refitted by least squares as it grows:\n\nplanes by principal components;\nspheres by an algebraic fit;\ncylinders by the axis to which the patch normals are perpendicular and a circle fit across it;\ncones by the axis with which the normals make a constant angle, or the cylinder axis for a small patch, and an algebraic fit of a radius linear along it;\ntori by the axis that all the normals meet, or on a small patch by axes through the centers of curvature of the patch, and a circle fit in the meridian plane.\n\nTori are refined by Levenberg–Marquardt, and the other kinds by Gauss–Newton on the vertex distances. A cone whose apex lies over 1000 extents away is left to the cylinder, as a surface whose radius of curvature does is left to the plane. From each seed all kinds are grown, and the largest region is kept. Among regions within 1% of the largest, the kind that comes first in the order plane, cylinder, cone, sphere, torus is kept. A narrow band with vertices only on its two rims fits a sphere as well, and also a cone when its rims differ in radius. The bore of an entrance hole is a cylinder, and a conical shield tessellated as one band is a cone, while a tessellator puts rings of vertices inside a spherical zone that bulges beyond its chord tolerance. Only regions with at least minpatches patches and minarea mm² whose rms distance is under a quarter of the tolerance count. On a true surface the residual is the faceting, well inside the tolerance. A fit to a free-form face, such as a variable-radius fillet or a torus without :torus, grows until the tolerance stops it, with an rms of about 0.4 of the tolerance, so such patches stay unfitted. Regions of one kind whose patches all lie on the fits of each other, such as one surface interrupted by another face, are then merged.\n\nTori are CAD fillets, a quarter of the tube where a wall meets a cap. They are held to CAD round-off, since with three more parameters than a sphere, a torus also follows free-form faces and spans neighboring faces within the relative tolerance. The conditions for a torus are:\n\nits tolerance is atol, and its rms must be under atol/16;\nits vertices must fall on at least four evenly spaced points of the tube circle, since bands of strips with vertices only on their rims lie on a circle through any three of them;\npatches that a plane, cylinder, cone or sphere fits are left to that kind.\n\nA fillet then becomes one region in place of a patchwork of small cones and spheres. kinds without :torus gives the fits as they were before tori (2026-10-24). On the NIF CAD parts that change affects only fillet regions (research notes, \"Tori\").\n\nOn a finely faceted face of none of the kinds, such as a tapered tube without :cone or a fine fillet without :torus, every patch would seed such a growth, at a cost quadratic in the facet count. A rejected growth of at least 512 patches therefore covers the patches near its seed, within half its radius, while all its patches stay available. A growth of the same kind from a covered seed is given up once its rms, at 128, 256, … members, reaches half that of the covering growth at the same size, as it does when it retraces the same face. A true surface fits its patches to the faceting and keeps growing.\n\nThe tolerance must exceed the faceting several times over. Facet vertices of exported CAD lie on the true surface, but points that remesh_mesh adds on a facet lie up to w²/8R inside a curved surface. With w the width of the facet, this is 0.1 µm for 50 µm strips on a 2.9 mm wall and 5 µm for 0.3 mm strips, more than the tolerance allows once w exceeds about 0.08 R. The facets from which the mesh was remeshed should therefore be given as reference. The regions are then fitted on them, and each patch of mesh joins the region whose surface all its vertices lie within, to that tolerance plus the largest distance of the facets of the region from it. The patch must also lie within the bounding box of the region and have its normal within angle. The regions returned index the patches of mesh, with their rms and largest distances from the (reference) fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.fit_surfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/beams/#Beams-and-facilities","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"Beams enter from facility ports: nif_quads and nif_beams (the LMF beam table, with measured phase-plate fits), NIF bundles (nif_bundle), OMEGA, or any port table (beams_from_table). Each beam has an elliptical super-Gaussian spot, an f-number cone, pointing and defocus. A quad model splits into its facility beams, nif_beams(quads), and beams join into quads at their mean aim, nif_quads(beams).\n\nThe direction of a beam is fixed by its port (mathbfk = -hatmathbfp), and pointing translates its axis through aim. Best focus is at aim + defocus·k; positive defocus moves the focus along the beam, as in VisRad and the NIF CMT. The spot axes are given in the (hattheta, hatphi) frame of the port. VisRadProfile reproduces VisRad's beam away from focus, by the law that the parameters exported by VisRad follow (research notes, \"VisRad's beam profile law\").","title":"Beams and facilities"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.Beam","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"Beam(name; theta, phi, power = 1.0, spot, fnumber = 20.0, aim = (0,0,0),\n     defocus = 0.0, group = :none, quad = \"\")\n\nOne laser beam entering from the port at polar angle theta and azimuth phi (degrees, target-chamber frame, z up). power in TW. group labels beams that share pointing parameters (e.g. a cone). wavelength is the nominal vacuum wavelength (µm) and dlambda the detuning from it (Å), used by laser–plasma models.\n\nThe axis has the direction of the port and passes through aim, so direct-drive pointings are aims:\n\na point s on a target sphere about c is aim = s. The axis first meets the sphere there when s faces the port, port · (s - c) > 0. For a point on the sphere at polar angle θ and azimuth φ, s = c + R * port_vector(θ, φ);\na shift Δ normal to the beam is aim = c + Δ * u with u ⟂ port, for example toward the equator for a northern port with u = θ̂ of port_basis;\nan axis crossing the polar axis at height z is aim = (0, 0, z).\n\nBest focus is at aim + defocus * k in each case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Beam"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.SuperGaussianSpot","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"SuperGaussianSpot(a, b = a; order = 2.0, angle = 0.0, halo = 0.0, halo_scale = 3.0,\n                  halo_order = 2.0)\n\nElliptical super-Gaussian best-focus intensity I ∝ exp(-[(u/a)² + (v/b)²]^(order/2)) with 1/e semi-axes a, b (mm). angle (radians) rotates the a axis from θ̂ of the port toward φ̂.\n\nMeasured phase-plate spots have low-level wings beyond the super-Gaussian core fit. A fraction halo of the power can be put in a second, broader component with semi-axes halo_scale·(a, b) and order halo_order. The halo matters for clearance and clipping checks; drive symmetry is insensitive to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SuperGaussianSpot"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.VisRadProfile","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"VisRadProfile(; envelope = nothing, taper = 0)\n\nThe beam model of VisRad 21, for deposition that reproduces the VisRad deposition (DepositionOperator(...; profile)). The beam is the analytic profile of the manual (VR/power_sources/set_laser_profile_props.html), a super-Gaussian blended toward a square away from best focus. The default model of Briareus instead convolves the focal spot over the f-cone. At a distance z from best focus, with (u, v) along the spot axes,\n\nI = I₀(z) exp(−α),   α = f [(|u|/ΔL)^M + (|v|/ΔL)^M] + (1 − f) [(u/a)² + (v/b)²]^(n/2).\n\nFor focal 1/e semi-axes a₀ ≥ b₀, exponent n₀, f-number F and envelope ratio ρ, with z_A = sqrt2 a_0 F and z_B = sqrt2 b_0 F:\n\na(z)² = a₀² + z²/(2F²), and likewise b(z);\nn(z) = n_0 (1 + 02 zz_A);\nthe weight of the square, f(z) = z  sqrtz^2 + z_A z_B;\nits half-width ΔL(z) = ρ a₀/√2 + 0.99605 |z|/(2F);\nits exponent M(z) = 10 (1 + zsqrtz_A z_B).\n\nThe profile is cut where α reaches ln 1000 (10⁻³ of its local peak), and I₀(z) puts the power of the beam in every transverse plane. A surface absorbs I |n·k| of the beam axis, where the axis reaches it unobstructed.\n\nThe forms of a, b, n, f and ΔL are those of the VisRad parameters, which VisRad exports with the beam–target intersection points. These were exported at 299 beam hits (|z| ≤ 15 mm), and the forms reproduce them to 5 × 10⁻⁵ in a and b, 1.4 × 10⁻⁴ in n, 5 × 10⁻⁴ in f, and 1.3 × 10⁻³ in ΔL. Only M and the cut were fitted, to the VisRad deposition on 48 plates normal to single NIF beams (0.06–0.22% rms per element). On 32 tilted plates and on normal plates up to 15 mm past focus, the model is then within 0.12–0.27% rms of VisRad, against 1.3–4.6% for the tabulated profile that it replaces (reference/research-notes.md, \"VisRad's beam profile law\").\n\nenvelope sets ρ for every beam (a number), per beam name (a Dict, where beams not in it take the default), or nothing for the VisRad default (ln 1000)^(1/n₀). The default puts the focal profile at 10⁻³ of its peak, and it reproduces the ratios 1.53645, 1.49576, 1.27326 and 1.28955 of the NIF plates. VisRad 21 replaced larger ratios (2 and\n\nwith the default, so ρ is capped there. ρ enters only ΔL: with a smaller ratio, 1.2,\n\nthe VisRad deposition does not show a cut-off at q = ρ. visrad_profile takes the envelopes from a workspace.\n\ntaper is an option for optimization and is not part of the VisRad model. The default, 0, is the hard cut of VisRad. At the cut the profile drops from 10⁻³ of its local peak to zero. As an aim moves, the deposition, and every objective or constraint computed from it, therefore jumps wherever an operator point crosses the cut, and finite differences and line searches encounter steps that derivatives miss. A positive taper multiplies exp(−α) by the quintic smoothstep S(t) = 10t³ − 15t⁴ + 6t⁵ of t = (ln 1000 − α)/taper (S = 1 for t ≥ 1), and I₀(z) is renormalized so that each plane still contains the power of the beam. The profile is then twice continuously differentiable across the cut, and its support, which the sampling and screens of the operator use, is unchanged. The band lies where the law is least constrained, since the VisRad deposition fits a cut at α = ln 1000 or none equally well. taper = 1 (one e-fold, intensities between 10⁻³ and e × 10⁻³ of the local peak) changes the element rms against VisRad on 59 probe plates by at most 0.011 percentage points, and NIF wall deposition by at most 7 × 10⁻⁵ of its peak (reference/research-notes.md, \"VisRad's beam profile law\").\n\nSome dependences were not identified:\n\nany dependence on the final-optic distance (every run had 769.5 cm) or on the f-number beyond F itself (every run had f/20.755, where the ΔL slope was measured);\nthe orientation of the square for a beam whose spot VisRad rotates, which is taken on the spot axes;\noccultation by the ray grid of VisRad, which here is set by the beam axis through each point.\n\nVisRad also renormalizes the deposited total of each beam, which per-plane normalization misses on steep surfaces (+0.2% at 40° incidence, +2.2% at 78°).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.VisRadProfile"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.direction-Tuple{Beam}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"Unit propagation direction of a beam, -port.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.direction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.enclosed_fraction-Tuple{SuperGaussianSpot, Any}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"enclosed_fraction(spot, ρ)\n\nFraction of the beam power inside elliptical radius ρ (core 1/e units) at best focus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.enclosed_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.focal_profile-Tuple{SuperGaussianSpot, Any, Any}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"focal_profile(spot, power, ρ)\n\nBest-focus intensity (TW/mm²) at elliptical radius ρ (in units of the core 1/e semi-axes) for a beam of power TW.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.focal_profile"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.intensity-Tuple{Briareus.BeamFrame, Any}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"intensity(frame_or_beam, x)\n\nBeam intensity (TW/mm², per unit area normal to the beam) at point x. Away from best focus the spot is the focal profile convolved with the geometric f-cone disk; this is approximated by widening the semi-axes so second moments add exactly (a(s)² = a² + s²/(16 F² m_n) with m_n = Γ(4/n)/(2Γ(2/n))) at fixed order n, for the core and halo components separately. This smooth profile is used for envelope and clearance estimates. Far from focus its shape departs from the convolution, so deposition (DepositionOperator, raytrace_deposition) sums over the cone directions instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.intensity-Tuple{VisRadProfile, Beam, Any}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"intensity(profile::VisRadProfile, beam, x)\n\nIntensity (TW/mm², per unit area normal to the beam) of beam at point x under the VisRad beam model (VisRadProfile).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.intensity"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.port_angles-Tuple{Beam}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"Polar and azimuthal angles (degrees) of a beam's port.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.port_angles"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.port_basis-Tuple{StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64}}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"port_basis(port) -> (θ̂, φ̂)\n\nSpherical unit vectors at the port, which are the transverse frame for spot orientation. The spot a axis of a beam at angle is cos(angle) θ̂ + sin(angle) φ̂ (spot_axes). An aim displaced along θ̂ of the port by d mm is:\n\neθ, eφ = port_basis(b.port)\nmoved = remake(b; aim = Tuple(b.aim + d * eθ))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.port_basis"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.port_vector-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"port_vector(theta, phi)\n\nUnit vector from TCC to a port at polar angle theta, azimuth phi (degrees).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.port_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.remake-Tuple{Beam}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"remake(beam; aim, power, defocus, spot, dlambda, port, fnumber, group, name)\n\nCopy of beam with the given fields replaced. The element type widens as needed, so that ForwardDiff duals can flow through pointing parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.remake"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.spot_axes-Tuple{Beam}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"spot_axes(beam) -> (a, b)\n\nUnit vectors of the a and b axes of the spot, transverse to the beam. They are θ̂ and φ̂ of the port turned by the angle of the spot toward φ̂.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.spot_axes"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.visrad_envelope-Tuple{Any}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"visrad_envelope(order) -> Float64\n\nThe default envelope ratio of VisRad for a super-Gaussian of exponent order. It is the radius, in 1/e units, where the focal profile falls to 10⁻³ of its peak, (ln 1000)^(1/n). The value is inferred, and it reproduces the ratio of every NIF plate in VisRad 21 workspaces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_envelope"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.wavelength-Tuple{Beam}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"Wavelength (µm) including the detuning dlambda.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.wavelength"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.with_axes-Tuple{SuperGaussianSpot, Any, Any}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"Same spot profile with semi-axes a, b (number type follows them).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.with_axes"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.NIF_DIAGNOSTIC_PORTS","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"NIF_DIAGNOSTIC_PORTS\n\nThe 110 diagnostic ports of the NIF target chamber, as (name, θ, φ, size). The fields are the name, such as \"P143-274\", the polar angle and azimuth in degrees, and the port diameter in mm. They come from the NIF User Guide (2024-09), Table 5-1, transcribed from the page images as published. A name rounds its angles: \"P116-250\" is at θ 116.8°, φ 250.5°, and \"P121-83\" at θ 120.5°. port_angles(:nif, \"P143-274\") looks a port up by name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.NIF_DIAGNOSTIC_PORTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.NIF_QUAD_NAMES","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"NIF_QUAD_NAMES\n\nNIF quad name of each indirect-drive chamber port (Dict(port => \"Q11T\", …)), taken from the labeled LMF chamber-geometry figures; see nif_port, nif_bundle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.NIF_QUAD_NAMES"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.beams_from_table-Tuple{Any}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"beams_from_table(rows; spot, fnumber, power) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nBuild beams from rows (NamedTuples or Dicts) with keys name, theta, phi (degrees) and optionally group, quad, power, aim, defocus, fnumber, spot. The keyword arguments supply defaults for rows that lack them; each may be a value or a function row -> value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.beams_from_table"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.hybride_mock_pointing","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"hybride_mock_pointing(beams = nif_beams(); table = HYBRIDE_MOCK_POINTING) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nA mock of the HYBRID-E (N221204/N230729-class) NIF pointing. beams (NIF beams or quads, with group the cone) are re-aimed by cone with defocus 0, and power, spot and dlambda are kept. The quads of each cone share one rule in the frame of each quad, mirrored top to bottom. The aim lies at ±z (mm, the bottom hemisphere at -z), at r along the port azimuth of the quad (negative across the axis, toward the opposite wall) and, for the outer cones, at ±s along φ̂ of the quad. Each beam aims to the side opposite its port (-sign(φ_beam − φ_quad) s), so the beams of the quad cross. The quad azimuth is that of the mean port of its beams in beams, so a quad given as one beam (nif_quads()) is not split.\n\ncone z r s\n23.5° 3.3 −1.3 0\n30° 4.2 −0.8 0\n44.5° 5.7 0 0.5\n50° 5.8 0 0.5\n\nDerived; the rule is an illustrative HYBRID-E-like rule on a 0.1 mm grid, for the 11.24 × 6.4 mm hohlraum with 3.1 mm LEHs, and does not reproduce a facility pointing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.hybride_mock_pointing"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.nif_beams-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"nif_beams(quads; fnumber = 20.7, spot = nothing, spot_frame = :beam) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nThe facility beams of NIF quads, such as a re-pointed quad model, with the beams named and quad-named as by nif_quads. Each quad gives its four beams, in its order, from the LMF beam table. The beams have the aim, defocus, dlambda, wavelength and group of the quad and a quarter of its power. spot is nothing, in which case each beam takes the spot of its quad, or a function of the cone, as for nif_beams(). spot_frame is the frame of its angle (:beam or :quad, see nif_beams()). nif_beams(nif_quads()) contains the beams of nif_beams().\n\nThe beams of a quad model then serve as a template for a reader of a per-beam pointing table. An SST export (aims and defocus per beam) applied to the beams of the quads is read_visrad_sst(path; template = nif_beams(quads)), which keeps the quarter of the quad power and the dlambda of each beam. nif_quads(beams) converts back to quads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_beams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.nif_beams-Tuple{}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"nif_beams(; power = 2.0, fnumber = 20.7, spot = nif_spot, spot_frame = :beam)\n\nAll 192 indirect-drive NIF beams with their individual directions from the LMF beam table (data/nif_beams.csv, transcribed from beam-port-locations-updated-10-15-2020.xlsx of the LMF Shot RI Resources). Names are the facility beam names, \"B111\"–\"B468\", with bundle xy and beam z; beams 1–4 form quad QxyT and beams 5–8 quad QxyB. quad is the quad name and group the cone. The mean direction of the four beams of each quad is its port direction to within 0.004°.\n\nspot maps a cone to its spot, whose angle is measured in the frame spot_frame. With :beam the frame is the port θ̂ and φ̂ of the beam. With :quad it is that of the quad port, and the spot is turned about the beam axis by the angle between φ̂ of the quad port and φ̂ of the beam (1.6–5.3°, with a sign that varies by beam). This is how VisRad orients the phase-plate spot of a NIF beam. The VisRad beams are nif_beams(; spot = visrad_nif_spot, spot_frame = :quad) (visrad_nif_spot). Whether the facility spots follow the quad frame is not established.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_beams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.nif_bundle-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"nif_bundle(beam_or_quad) -> String\n\nBundle of a NIF quad or beam, given by the cluster and bundle digits of its quad name, e.g. \"31\" for Q31T and Q31B. The two quads of a bundle lie in opposite hemispheres and need not share a cone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_bundle"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.nif_bundles-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"nif_bundles(beams) -> Dict{String,Vector{Int}}\n\nIndices of beams (NIF quads or beams) belonging to each bundle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_bundles"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.nif_port-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"nif_port(quad) -> Int\n\nChamber port number of a NIF quad name such as \"Q31B\" (see NIF_QUAD_NAMES).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_port"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.nif_quads-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"nif_quads(beams; fnumber = 7.9, spot = nothing, spot_frame = :beam) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nNIF quads from their facility beams, named by nif_beams, with any subset of the four beams of a quad. The result has one quad per quad name, in the order of the first beams of the quads, at its port (that of the NIF User Guide, as for nif_quads()). Each quad has the mean aim, defocus and dlambda of its beams, their summed power, and the group and wavelength that they share. The mean aim of each quad in a beam list is therefore Dict(q.quad => q.aim for q in nif_quads(beams)). A split quad, with beams pointed apart as in an as-shot table, becomes one aim point at the mean.\n\nspot is nothing, in which case each quad takes the spot of its first beam, or a function of the cone. With spot = nothing, spot_frame = :quad takes the angle of that spot in the quad frame and turns it back to the quad port, the inverse of nif_beams(quads; spot_frame = :quad). Beams of one quad in different groups are an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_quads"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.nif_quads-Tuple{}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"nif_quads(; power = 8.0, fnumber = 7.9, spot = nif_spot)\n\nThe 48 indirect-drive NIF quads as single beams (power in TW per quad, i.e. four beamlines). Port angles are the NIF User Guide table; group is the cone (:inner23, :inner30, :outer44, :outer50, shared by top and bottom), and name and quad are the quad name (e.g. \"Q31B\", see NIF_QUAD_NAMES; nif_port, nif_bundle). spot maps a cone to its SuperGaussianSpot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_quads"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.nif_spot-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"nif_spot(cone; scale = 1.0)\n\nBest-focus CPP spot for a NIF cone (:inner23, :inner30, :outer44, :outer50), with the major axis along φ̂ of the port and the minor axis along θ̂.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_spot"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.omega_beams-Tuple{}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"omega_beams(; cones = (:cone1, :cone2, :cone3), power = 0.5, fnumber = 6.7,\n            spot = omega_spot(:EIDI300))\n\nOMEGA beams in the hohlraum (P6–P7) frame (see omega_ports), restricted to cones (the 40 indirect-drive beams by default; pass cones = :all for all 60).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.omega_beams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.omega_ports-Tuple{}","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"omega_ports() -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nThe 60 OMEGA beam directions in a hohlraum frame whose z axis is a pentagon (P6–P7) axis. They are the vertices of a truncated icosahedron with hexagon:pentagon edge ratio 1.2 (cut at 1.2/3.4 of each icosahedron edge), which gives cones at 21.415°, 42.020°, 58.852° and 81.249° and their supplements (Sci. Rep. 13, 9883, 2023). The azimuthal origin puts cone 1 at φ = 0°, 72°, …; it is not tied to chamber coordinates. Groups are :cone1–:cone4; lower-hemisphere beams use the same groups.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.omega_ports"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/beams/#Briareus.omega_spot","page":"Beams and facilities","text":"omega_spot(name = :EIDI300)\n\nOMEGA phase-plate spots (1/e semi-axes, mm): :EIDI300 hohlraum elliptical plate (n = 4.1, 1/e diameters 290 × 212 µm), :SG4 (r₀ = 0.36 mm, n = 4.2 with SSD+PS), :SG8 (r₀ = 0.412 mm, n = 4.7). OMEGA Users' Guide (2014).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.omega_spot"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/lpi/#Laser–plasma-models","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"Corrections applied between the beam setup and the deposition, differentiable in power and Δλ: per-cone, time-dependent Backscatter; an empirical CalibratedTransfer (transfer as a function of Δλ); a reduced kinetic CBET model at the LEHs (multi-species Maxwellian ion-acoustic response, flow Doppler shift, polarization smoothing and a smooth δn/n saturation clamp), whose multiplier, flow and clamp calibrate_cbet fits to transfer data; TwoZoneCBET, which separates the flowing plasma outside the LEH from the stagnant one inside, with calibrations to published HYDRA transfer for HYBRID-E shots; and inner-beam absorption in the gold bubble (BubbleAbsorption), whose picket fluence ratio picket_ratio can take from the outer picket fluence of a pointing on the wall (picket_fluence). PicketLinkedBubble takes it from the beams inside every evaluation, so that the bubble follows a re-pointed design, and bubble_prediction reports what a re-pointing does to the picket, the bubble and the P2 swing. picket_irradiance gives the irradiance of the outer cones in a phase, and picket_ceiling bounds it in a constrained optimization. The irradiance is the peak, the energy-weighted mean, the spatially averaged value of the published scalings, or the mean near the centerlines of the inner beams. picket_hot_elements lists the hottest elements. lpi_deposit applies them together. The CBET model assumes uniform LEH plasma and full pairwise overlap, and its unsaturated coupling overpredicts transfer: its magnitudes hold only after calibration against shot data or reference transfer points.","title":"Laser–plasma models"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.Backscatter","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"Backscatter(fractions)\n\nPer-group backscattered power fraction (SRS + SBS), removed before deposition. fractions maps a beam group to a number, a function t -> fraction, or a (times, values) table (ns); groups not listed are unchanged. The fractions are typically set from measured FABS/NBI backscatter per cone. A fraction lies in [0, 1]. Numbers and tables outside this range throw an ArgumentError at construction, and a function value outside it throws when applied.\n\nBackscatter(:inner23 => 0.10, :inner30 => ([0, 5, 8], [0.0, 0.05, 0.12]))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Backscatter"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.CalibratedTransfer","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"CalibratedTransfer(donors, receivers, fraction)\n\nEmpirical cross-beam transfer. A fraction of the total power of the donors groups is moved to the receivers groups and shared in proportion to the power of each receiving beam. fraction(Δλ, t) is a calibration, e.g. a fit to shot data, where Δλ = ⟨dlambda⟩_receivers − ⟨dlambda⟩_donors (Å, power-weighted means), so a positive Δλ means the receivers are red-shifted relative to the donors, the direction that drives transfer toward them. Total power is conserved.\n\nAt zero power the transfer is evaluated at its limit. Nothing is transferred from donors without power (f · 0), and their ⟨dlambda⟩ is then the unweighted mean, so the derivatives with respect to their powers are finite. Nothing is transferred to receivers without power, since the light is shared in proportion to their powers, and the beams are then returned unchanged.\n\nBuild fraction with linear_transfer or tabulated_transfer, or pass any differentiable function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CalibratedTransfer"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.LaserPlasmaModel","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"LaserPlasmaModel\n\nSupertype of laser–plasma corrections. Implement apply_lpi(model, beams, time) returning beams with modified powers (same order, ports and pointing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.LaserPlasmaModel"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.apply_lpi","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"apply_lpi(model, beams, time = 0.0) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nEffective beams after the laser–plasma model (nothing = no correction). A tuple or vector of models is applied in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.apply_lpi"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.kemp2026_sbs-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"kemp2026_sbs() -> NamedTuple\n\nMeasured inner-cone SBS backscatter of N210808 per quad against time, digitized from Kemp et al., PoP 33, 082705 (2026) Fig. 2 into data/kemp2026_fig2.csv; the sources are listed in its header. The columns are t (ns, 4.4–7.1 every 0.05), inner23 and inner30. Each column contains all backscattered SBS power of one 23° or 30° quad in TW, cone-averaged over both hemispheres; it is the in-lens signal × 3.4. The energies in the paper are 2.8 and 0.9 kJ per quad, with eight quads per cone. Single-shot errors reach 100%, and N210808 repeats vary by 30–50%.\n\nSBS starts as the inner power reaches its peak. On this axis the pre-CBET quad power is at half its 9 TW peak at 4.74 ns. SBS peaks at 5.3 ns (23°) and 5.75 ns (30°) and ends by 6.6–6.9 ns. The 30° cone ends first, which the paper attributes to refraction and absorption by an on-axis density spike (§III C). examples/n221204.jl uses this table for backscatter=history. Research notes, \"Inner-cone SBS time histories and the late-time refraction (2026-10-25)\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.kemp2026_sbs"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.linear_transfer-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"linear_transfer(f0, slope; range = (0, 1))\n\nfraction(Δλ, t) = clamp(f0 + slope·Δλ, range...) (slope per Å).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.linear_transfer"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.lpi_deposit","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"lpi_deposit(model, op::DepositionOperator, beams, time = 0.0) -> Deposition\n\nDeposition of the effective beams (from apply_lpi) under the laser–plasma model. It is deposit(op, beams), unless the model deposits part of the light outside every beam footprint, as the ridge stop of BubbleAbsorption does. Of a tuple or vector of models, at most one may do so.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.lpi_deposit"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.lpi_summary-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Beam}, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"lpi_summary(setup, effective) -> Dict{Symbol,NamedTuple}\n\nPer-group setup and effective power (TW) and their ratio. The ratio is 1 for a group without power in either, which is unchanged, and Inf for one that gains power from none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.lpi_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.tabulated_transfer-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"tabulated_transfer(Δλs, fractions)\n\nPiecewise-linear fraction(Δλ, t) through the table, constant beyond its ends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.tabulated_transfer"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.BubbleAbsorption","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"BubbleAbsorption(; radius, turn_on = 0.0, fill = 0.3, picket_ratio = 1.0, kwargs...)\n\nInner-beam absorption in the wall bubble (a LaserPlasmaModel). The picket of the outer cones launches a bubble of wall plasma from the wall under their footprint. Its tip has moved\n\nx_bal(t) = v_b · max(0, t − t_L),   t_L = turn_on + delay,\nv_b = speed · E^0.2 · (fill / 0.3)^-0.2\n\nin from the initial wall radius R (mm, ns) while it is ballistic. E = picket_ratio is the picket fluence of the outer cones over that of the calibration platform, the HDC basecamp of Izumi 2021, and is dimensionless. fill is the gas fill in mg/cc. E is set by hand by default. picket_ratio computes it from the picket fluence of a pointing on the wall (picket_fluence), for remake(m; picket_ratio), and PicketLinkedBubble computes it for the beams of every evaluation; both are opt-in. The tip stagnates smoothly at the depth stagnation x_max (bubble_depth). It is ballistic up to x_max - w (w = stagnation_width), then at x_max - w e^-(x_bal - x_max + w)w. In the plane z_b of the outer footprint, at the axial position where the beam axes of the footprint cones reach the wall, the bubble fills the annulus from R in to radius R − x_bub. Each inner beam loses\n\nabsorbed = f · (1 − T_b),\nT_b = exp(−τ_b (fill/0.3)^q (x_bub/x_ref)^p (R/(R − x_bub))²),\n\nwhere f is the fraction of its power crossing z_b inside the bubble and T_b the transmission of the bubble. f is computed from the beam itself, with its best-focus spot carried along its ncone f-cone directions, as in the deposition. T_b uses the opacity τ_b, the exponent q = density_exponent of the fill density and the exponent p = depth_exponent of the depth. The absorbed power is conducted to the wall under the outer footprint (Ralph et al. 2018). It is shared equally by the footprint beams in the hemisphere of the inner beam, those whose port is on the same side, so it is deposited where those beams land. Beam powers are conserved, since the model moves power between beams and loses none. The fraction of the footprint beams that misses the wall, a few 1e-4 through the entrance holes, applies to the moved power as well.\n\nRidge stop. After the ablator–wall collision at collision t_c, the inner power that leaves the bubble stops at the stagnation ridge, at radius ridge·R about the axis (Izumi 2023), and does not reach the wall. A fraction g(t) of it (ridge_fraction, rising over ±ridge_rise ns about t_c) is deposited by lpi_deposit on an axisymmetric band of the ridge_components (the hohlraum wall). The band is centered on the height where the beam axis crosses that radius, Gaussian in z with rms ridge_width (mm), and area-weighted. The heated ridge radiates at the conversion of the wall, from the wall just outside it. The rest of the beam power is deposited as usual. Power is conserved, and the ridge power is booked to the per_beam of the beam. The ridge stop acts only where the deposition goes through lpi_deposit: in drive_history, in Scenario evaluations, in the beam-error sensitivities (mode_sensitivity and the rest of uncertainty.jl) and in influence_matrix.\n\nThe edge of the bubble is smoothed over edge (mm, a logistic step), which keeps f smooth in time, in the bubble depth and in the pointing of the beams. Everything is generic in the number type. speed, opacity, stagnation, collision and picket_ratio, and the beams, can carry ForwardDiff duals (remake), so the model can be used in drive_history(...; lpi), Scenario(...; lpi) and calibrations. The model composes with CBET as lpi = (cbet, bubble). The transfer acts first, and the bubble then takes its share of the power in the inner beams.\n\nKeywords, by role (defaults in parentheses):\n\nShot and geometry: radius R (required; the initial wall radius, mm), turn_on (0.0; the turn-on of the outer cones, ns), fill (0.3 mg/cc), picket_ratio E (1.0), capsule_radius r_cap (0.908 mm), inner ((:inner23, :inner30)) and footprint ((:outer50,)), the beam groups that lose and receive the power; wall (nothing), a WallMotion that moves the footprint plane with the radiating wall. The axes of the outer beams reach the moved wall nearer the entrance hole, and the bubble tip and the ridge are measured from the initial wall in either case. footprint_z (nothing) fixes |z_b| instead.\nCalibration: speed (0.36 mm/ns), delay (0.25 ns), opacity τ_b (0.111), density_exponent q (1.8), depth_exponent p (0.5) and its reference depth x_ref (1.0 mm).\nTip stagnation: stagnation (0.80 R) and stagnation_width (0.08 R), both defaulting to fractions of radius; with stagnation = Inf the tip stays ballistic.\nRidge sub-model: collision t_c (derived from the other keywords, below; collision = Inf turns the ridge stop off), ridge (0.73), ridge_width (0.5 mm), ridge_rise (0.2 ns), ridge_components ((:wall,)).\nNumerics: edge (0.05 mm); spot points, nr (12) equal-power radii by nangle (24) angles per f-cone direction, over ncone (13) directions.\n\nDefaults (research notes, \"Hohlraum dynamics calibrated to published data (2026-10-24)\"; the data are izumi2021_bubble and izumi2021_absorption):\n\nspeed 0.36 mm/ns at 0.3 mg/cc and the basecamp picket, ∝ E^0.2 ρ₀^-0.2, and delay 0.25 ns after the outer turn-on are fitted to the 28 measured tip positions of Izumi et al., PoP 28, 022706 (2021) Fig. 8a. The data cover seven HDC SymCap shots at 0.15–0.3 mg/cc with pickets of 0.73–1.5×, and the fit has χ²/dof 0.89 with the ±40 µm of the paper. It gives 0.360 (0.347–0.372) mm/ns and a delay of 0.25 (0.07–0.42) ns, with the two correlated. Meezan et al. 2020 measured 321–461 µm/ns on the same shots.\ndensity_exponent q = 1.8: inverse-bremsstrahlung absorption goes as ρ_bub^2, and Eq. 7 of Izumi has ρ_bub = S ρ₀ R/(R − x) with the shock compression S ∝ ρ₀^-0.1, so ρ_bub^2 ∝ ρ₀^1.8 (R/(R − x))². depth_exponent 0.5 is the chord (x_bub/1 mm)^0.5 of Eq. 6.\nopacity τ_b 0.111 is fitted to the inner-power fraction absorbed in the bubble in the 2D HYDRA simulation by Izumi of the 0.3 mg/cc basecamp (Fig. 10a: 0.02, 0.23, 0.55 at 4, 5, 6 ns). The fit uses the clipping that this model computes for NIF beams pointed for a 5.75 mm hohlraum, with the bubble plane at z = ±3 mm as in Izumi (footprint_z = 3.0). The fitted model absorbs 0.03, 0.20, 0.36 and 0.56 at 4, 5, 5.5 and 6 ns, with χ² 1.1 at ±0.03. The statistical range is 0.100–0.124. The plane (2.8–3.2 mm) and the launch move τ_b over 0.085–0.158 but leave the absorbed fraction, the calibrated quantity, within 0.01. T_b at 5.5 ns is then 0.36 at 0.3 mg/cc and 0.58 at 0.15 mg/cc; the model of Izumi gives 0.29 and 0.54, with an annulus for f.\nstagnation 0.80 R and stagnation_width 0.08 R are engineering values. The tip is exactly ballistic to 0.72 R (2.07 mm in a 2.88 mm hohlraum), past the measured depths at 0.3 mg/cc (2.07 mm at 6.2 ns) and near those at 0.15 mg/cc (up to 2.28 mm, 0.79 R). In Izumi 2023 the tip slows on back-pressure near the axis. A depth of R − 0.73 R, the depth of the ridge at the waist, would stop the tip before it reaches the inner beams at z_b, 1.3 mm from the wall.\ncollision t_c = turn_on + 5.5 ns · (R − r_cap)/(2.875 − 0.908 mm) · E^-0.16 is fitted to Izumi et al., RSI 89, 10K111, 2018. There the inner spots separate at 5.8 ns for the 0.3 ns turn-on of the basecamp, at 5.45 ns with a 1.5× picket, and about 0.4 ns earlier with the 1.18× capsule. The gap between the outer radius of the capsule capsule_radius r_cap and the wall gives 0.44 ns for that capsule, so a constant closing speed of ablator and wall plasma, 0.36 mm/ns, accounts for the capsule shot. ridge 0.73 corresponds to the ridge of Izumi 2023 at r ≈ 2.1 mm in a 2.88 mm hohlraum. ridge_width 0.5 mm and ridge_rise 0.2 ns are engineering choices and were not measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.BubbleAbsorption"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.bubble_depth-Tuple{BubbleAbsorption, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"bubble_depth(m::BubbleAbsorption, t)\n\nBubble tip displacement from the initial wall (mm) at time t (ns). It is the ballistic depth v_b (t - t_L) up to the knee stagnation − stagnation_width, and beyond the knee stagnation − w·exp(−(x − knee)/w), which stops at stagnation (C¹ at the knee).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bubble_depth"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.bubble_fractions-Union{Tuple{S}, Tuple{BubbleAbsorption, AbstractArray{Beam{S}, 1}, Any}} where S<:Real","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"bubble_fractions(m::BubbleAbsorption, beams, t) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe bubble at time t (ns) and its effect on beams. The fields are depth x_bub (mm), transmission T_b, footprint_z (z_b of the upper and lower hemispheres, nothing where there are no footprint beams), ridge (the ridge_fraction g), and per beam clipped (f, zero for beams that are not inner) and absorbed (f (1 - T_b)). Of the power of an inner beam, absorbed goes to the footprint beams, and g(1 − absorbed) stops at the ridge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bubble_fractions"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.bubble_speed-Tuple{BubbleAbsorption}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"Bubble tip speed (mm/ns): speed · picket_ratio^0.2 · (fill/0.3)^-0.2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bubble_speed"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.bubble_transmission-Tuple{BubbleAbsorption, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"bubble_transmission(m::BubbleAbsorption, t) -> T_b\n\nTransmission of the bubble for the inner-beam light it clips, at time t (ns); see BubbleAbsorption. 1 before the launch, 0 once the tip reaches the axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bubble_transmission"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.iris_fraction-Tuple{Beam, Any, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"iris_fraction(beam, z, ρ; edge = 0.05, nr = 12, nangle = 24, ncone = 13)\n\nFraction of the power of beam that crosses the plane at height z (mm) outside the cylinder of radius ρ about the hohlraum axis. The best-focus spot of the beam (core and halo) is carried along ncone f-cone directions, and the edge of the cylinder is smoothed over edge mm. Generic in the number type of the beam, z and ρ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.iris_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.izumi2021_absorption-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"izumi2021_absorption() -> NamedTuple\n\nThe fraction of the inner-cone power absorbed in the bubble against time for the 0.3 mg/cc basecamp of Izumi et al., PoP 28, 022706 (2021), Fig. 10a, from the vector graphics of the figure (data/izumi2021_fig10a.csv). The columns are source, which is \"hydra\" for the 2D simulation (0, 0.02, 0.23, 0.55 at 3, 4, 5, 6 ns) or \"model\" for Eq. 6 of the paper (every 0.1 ns), t (ns), absorbed and u. The table is the calibration target of the opacity of BubbleAbsorption.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.izumi2021_absorption"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.izumi2021_bubble-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"izumi2021_bubble() -> NamedTuple\n\nAu bubble tip displacement from the initial wall against time on the HDC SymCap series of Izumi et al., PoP 28, 022706 (2021), Fig. 8 (5.75 mm Au hohlraum, outer turn-on 0.3 ns), extracted from the vector graphics of the figure (data/izumi2021_fig8.csv, sources in its header). The columns are shot, source (\"data\" for measured values, Fig. 8a, error bars ±40 µm; \"hydra\" for 2D HYDRA, Fig. 8b), energy (outer picket fluence over that of the basecamp), fill (mg/cc), capsule (radius over that of the basecamp), t (ns), x_bub (mm) and u (mm). The table is the calibration target of the speed and delay of BubbleAbsorption.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.izumi2021_bubble"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.remake-Tuple{BubbleAbsorption}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"remake(m::BubbleAbsorption; speed = m.speed, opacity = m.opacity,\n       stagnation = m.stagnation, collision, picket_ratio = m.picket_ratio)\n\nThe model with new calibration parameters (e.g. ForwardDiff duals in a fit), or a new picket fluence ratio E (picket_ratio, e.g. from picket_ratio for a re-pointed design). With a new E, the picket dependence of the model enters the ridge stop as in the constructor, with t_c − turn_on ∝ E^-0.16. collision defaults to turn_on + (m.collision − turn_on)·(E/m.picket_ratio)^-0.16. That is the stored value when E is unchanged, whether the constructor derived it or it was given, and Inf stays Inf. An explicit collision sets the value. Every argument may be a ForwardDiff dual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.remake"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.ridge_fraction-Tuple{BubbleAbsorption, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"ridge_fraction(m::BubbleAbsorption, t) -> g\n\nThe fraction of the remaining power of the inner beams that stops at the ablator–wall ridge at time t (ns). It is a C¹ smoothstep from 0 at collision − ridge_rise to 1 at collision + ridge_rise, and 0 throughout with collision = Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ridge_fraction"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.PicketFluence","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"PicketFluence{T}\n\nThe picket fluence of a set of beams on the wall of a target, from picket_fluence:\n\nfluence: per element of the target (kJ/mm²; ×1e5 → J/cm²), zero outside elements;\nelements: the elements counted (those of the named components);\nenergy: the picket energy deposited on them, ∫F dA (kJ). Over a fixed spot area it is the spatially averaged fluence of the published bubble scalings, whose ratio between designs is the energy ratio (the :average of picket_irradiance);\nmean: the energy-weighted mean fluence ∫F² dA / ∫F dA (kJ/mm²), the summary that picket_ratio uses;\npeak: the largest element fluence (kJ/mm²), for information;\narea: the effective footprint area energy / mean = (∫F dA)² / ∫F² dA (mm²), over all counted elements. These include both hemispheres of a hohlraum, so a symmetric pointing has the mean of either side and twice its area.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PicketFluence"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.PicketLinkedBubble","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"PicketLinkedBubble(bubble, target, reference; pulses = nothing, window = nothing,\n                   by = :group, power = nothing, groups = (:outer44, :outer50),\n                   components = (:wall,), deposition_kw = (;))\n\nA BubbleAbsorption whose picket fluence ratio E follows the beams it is applied to. The model is an opt-in LaserPlasmaModel. For beams bs,\n\nE(bs) = bubble.picket_ratio × F̄(bs) / F̄(reference),\n\nwith F̄ the energy-weighted mean outer picket fluence on the components of target (picket_fluence). The bubble is remake(bubble; picket_ratio = E(bs)), with tip speed ∝ E^0.2 and collision time − turn_on ∝ E^-0.16. bubble.picket_ratio is the E of the reference pointing, set by hand or from picket_ratio against the calibration platform. At the reference the model is therefore identical to bubble. A re-pointed design is given the bubble that its picket grows, inside evaluate(scenario, beams), the constraints and objectives built on it (mode_gate) and drive_history(...; lpi). The model composes as bubble does, for example lpi = (cbet, linked).\n\nThe picket map is computed on target. It is the hohlraum on a mesh fine enough for the map (h ≤ 0.2 mm), a separate target used only for the deposition; the radiation mesh of a drive history is usually too coarse (research notes). A deposition operator is built on it for the reference beams in groups (with deposition_kw). The beams that the model is applied to are matched to those beams by name, and they must enter through the same ports. A beam of groups not among them is an error, and a missing one, as in a dropped bundle, deposits nothing. The picket powers are those of picket_irradiance: pulses over a window, for example the mean power over the first ns after the outer turn-on, or power, a function of the beam. One of the two is required. It must not use the power of the beam, which at the time of the model is not the picket power; in a chain after CBET it is not even the setup power. E depends only on the pointing, spots and focus of the beams, so it is constant through a drive history.\n\nCost: two picket depositions per evaluation, one in apply_lpi and one in lpi_deposit. Generic in the number type. E and the bubble carry the ForwardDiff duals of the beams, so a pointing gradient includes the response of the bubble to the picket fluence. Thread-safe, since the model has no state. The per-beam sensitivities of the uncertainty analysis (mode_sensitivity, mode_uncertainty) deposit beams one at a time and cannot resolve E, so they throw an ArgumentError. The resolved bubble linked_bubble(model, beams) is passed to them instead.\n\nDerived (confidence low). The exponents are published for picket energy and quad splitting on one platform: E^0.2 from Izumi et al., PoP 28, 022706 (2021), on seven shots, and E^-0.16 from Izumi et al., RSI 89, 10K111 (2018). Their use for a fluence changed by re-pointing at fixed energy has no published test. F̄ counts the overlap of the spots, as the split-quad shot of Izumi 2021 does. The energy over a fixed area used in the papers (the :average of picket_irradiance) does not, and it differs from F̄ on uneven powers. See the research notes, \"The swing and the bubble without new data (2026-11-18)\" §5, and \"The published picket fluence, the trough and the inner-beam corridor (2026-11-18)\" §5, which measures the link through the fixed area instead. No facility criterion sets the model, and no default uses it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PicketLinkedBubble"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.basecamp_picket_fluence-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"basecamp_picket_fluence(; energy = 26.0, h = 0.2, capsule_h = 0.4) -> PicketFluence\n\nThe outer picket fluence of the calibration platform of BubbleAbsorption, where the picket_ratio is E = 1. The platform is the HDC SymCap basecamp of Izumi et al., PoP 28, 022706 (2021), a 5.75 × 10.13 mm Au hohlraum with 3.37 mm entrance holes and a 0.908 mm capsule. The mesh size is h (mm), and capsule_h is that of the capsule. The 128 outer beams (nif_beams, with the NIF outer phase-plate spots) carry equal shares of energy, the outer picket energy in kJ over all outer beams. The value is about 26 kJ in the first ns after the outer turn-on, read by eye from Izumi 2021 Fig. 3a (L). The quads are unsplit and aimed on the axis at z = ±5.20 mm (44.5°) and ±5.01 mm (50°). This is the proxy pointing of the package for this hohlraum, the one with which the bubble opacity was calibrated; the basecamp pointing of the facility is unpublished (L). The result is the reference of picket_ratio for an absolute E, and the map of the design should be computed on the same h.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.basecamp_picket_fluence"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.bubble_prediction-Tuple{PicketLinkedBubble, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"bubble_prediction(model::PicketLinkedBubble, design, reference; history = nothing,\n                  reference_history = nothing, fixed = false, swing = (4.5, 9.0))\n    -> NamedTuple\n\nThe prediction of the gold bubble model for a re-pointed design (beams) against a reference pointing. Both are mapped by model, a PicketLinkedBubble with its picket powers, target and bubble. Usually reference is the pointing on which the model was built. The fields are:\n\npeak_ratio: the maximum picket fluence (equivalently irradiance) over the elements of the model, design over reference (the peak of picket_fluence);\nfluence_ratio: the energy-weighted mean picket fluence, design over reference;\npicket_ratio, reference_picket_ratio: E of each (linked_bubble);\nspeed_ratio: the tip speed of the bubble, design over reference, fluence_ratio^0.2;\ncollision, reference_collision: the ablator–wall collision times (ns);\nbubble, reference_bubble: the two BubbleAbsorptions; fluence, reference_fluence: the two PicketFluence maps;\nhistory: nothing, or, with history, the P2/P0 of the capsule drive through time with the bubble linked.\n\nhistory(beams, bubble) runs a drive history of beams (the result of drive_history, with lmax ≥ 2) with the laser–plasma chain built on bubble, for example (bs, bub) -> drive_history(model, τ -> with_pulses(bs, pulses, τ), times; lpi = (cbet, bub)). It is run for the design with its bubble and for the reference with a separate bubble. reference_history, a result of the same call for the reference, replaces that second run. With fixed = true it is also run for the design with the bubble of the reference, which splits the effect of the pointing into the parts through the deposition and through the bubble. history then contains time, p2, reference_p2 and fixed_p2 (or nothing). For each run (design, reference, fixed) it gives the minimum of P2/P0 in the swing over the swing window (ns), the time at which it is reached (at), and the upward zero crossing after it (linear between steps; NaN if none). It also gives shift, the value of the design minus that of the reference, and with fixed, through_bubble (design − fixed) and through_deposition (fixed − reference).\n\nDerived (confidence low). The response of the bubble to the pointing rests on the published exponents (tip speed ∝ E^0.2, Izumi 2021; collision ∝ E^-0.16, Izumi 2018) applied to a fluence changed by re-pointing, which has no published test (PicketLinkedBubble). Ratios between pointings on one mesh converge to < 1% (research notes, \"The swing and the bubble without new data (2026-11-18)\" §5).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bubble_prediction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.linked_bubble-Tuple{PicketLinkedBubble, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"linked_bubble(m::PicketLinkedBubble, beams) -> BubbleAbsorption\n\nThe bubble of a PicketLinkedBubble for beams, which is remake(m.bubble; picket_ratio = E) with E from the picket fluence of the beams relative to that of the reference. At the reference it is m.bubble itself. The result is in the number type of the beams.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.linked_bubble"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.picket_ceiling-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"picket_ceiling(reference, names...; pulses = nothing, window = nothing, by = :group,\n               power = nothing, groups = (:outer44, :outer50), lpi = false,\n               measure = :peak, area = 53.0, corridor = 1.3, edge = 0.3,\n               inner = (:inner23, :inner30),\n               limit = picket_irradiance(reference, reference_names...; ...), p = 64,\n               scale = limit, exact = true, reference_names = names) -> Constraint\n\nA ceiling on the laser irradiance of the beams in groups over the elements of the named parts or components, as a Constraint for optimize_pointing named :picket_<measure>. The irradiance is that of picket_irradiance, whose keywords set the powers, the beams and the measure, the same for the reference and the design. By default the limit is the value of the reference evaluation; a number in TW/mm² sets another limit.\n\nmeasure = :peak: the maximum irradiance over the elements. The optimizer works on the smooth area-weighted p-norm of the irradiance, scaled to the peak of the reference, as irradiance_ceiling does at the time of the scenario. With exact = true (the default) the run enforces the peak itself. The working limit of the stand-in is lowered while the peak exceeds the limit, and the peak is reported against the limit (check_constraints, PointingResult.constraints).\nmeasure = :weighted: the energy-weighted mean irradiance, the quantity that picket_ratio carries into the tip speed of BubbleAbsorption (∝ ratio^0.2). It is smooth and enforced directly.\nmeasure = :average: the spatially averaged irradiance over a fixed area, the quantity of the published bubble scalings. Against the reference it is the absorbed power on the parts over that of the reference. It is linear in the powers and enforced directly.\nmeasure = :corridor: the mean irradiance near the centerlines of the inner beams of the design, which move with its aims. It is smooth and enforced directly.\n\nA reference on another target. For :peak the reference evaluation must be on the same target as the optimized scenario; a subset of the scenario shares the target. Otherwise evaluating the constraint throws an ArgumentError. The reason is that the peak follows one element and the mesh, and the scale of the stand-in is the ratio of peak to norm on the reference mesh. The three integral measures are valid across meshes and accept a reference on another target, such as a hohlraum without the windows of the design or a separate mesh for the reference. They are:\n\n:average, the absorbed power over a fixed area, which is a quantity of the field and does not depend on the mesh;\n:weighted, an integral that converges as O(h²) with the element size h. Targets should be meshed at the same h, where the bias of about 1% at 0.2 mm cancels in the ratio;\n:corridor, an area mean.\n\nOn an evaluation of another target, the constraint measures the parts names of that target, and the limit is the value of the reference over reference_names (by default names). A design with windows in its mesh can then hold the light on its wall and windows at the light of the reference on its wall, with picket_ceiling(reference, :wall, :window; reference_names = (:wall,), measure = :average).\n\nThe laser–plasma model is used only with lpi = true. The reference, on the same target or another, must then have the model and time of the scenario, as for irradiance_ceiling and mode_gate. Evaluating the constraint with another model, or at another time with a model, throws an ArgumentError. The constraint performs one deposition per evaluation, in the number type of the evaluation. Inside optimize_pointing, with the beams at their present powers (no pulses or power), no deposition is needed. The element powers of the groups are then kept from the same pass of the deposition in the evaluation, with identical sums. The exceptions are a laser–plasma model of the scenario that deposits light itself, and one that changes the beams with lpi = false. For a phase other than that of the optimized scenario, such as a trough or a foot, the powers of the phase are given by pulses and window or by power, or the constraint is built on an evaluation of that phase.\n\nDerived; no facility criterion sets the limit. The picket fluence is the measure of the published bubble scalings, and holding it at the value of a reference was chosen for a re-pointing study (user, 2026-11-18). That study limits first its peak, as an engineering limit on the hottest element of the picket, then the published :average in the picket, the trough and the second step, with the :corridor reported. The model predicts the bubble of a design from the ratio of the means to the 0.2 power (PicketLinkedBubble, bubble_prediction), with low confidence (research notes, \"The swing and the bubble without new data (2026-11-18)\" §5, and \"The published picket fluence, the trough and the inner-beam corridor (2026-11-18)\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.picket_ceiling"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.picket_fluence-Tuple{DepositionOperator, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"picket_fluence(op, beams; groups = (:outer44, :outer50), components = (:wall,),\n               duration = 1.0) -> PicketFluence\npicket_fluence(op, beams, pulses, window; by = :group, groups, components)\n\nThe picket fluence F (energy per area, kJ/mm²) of the beams in groups (the outer cones) on the elements of components, and its summaries (PicketFluence). components are part or component names, as for elements. The first form takes the power of each beam as its picket power (TW) over a picket of duration ns, or, with duration = 1, as its picket energy (kJ). The second form integrates the pulse of each beam (pulses keyed as for with_pulses, with by) over window = (t0, t1) ns. An example window is the first ns after the outer turn-on, the convention of the research notes for the pickets of the basecamp and N221204; the same window should be used in a design and its reference. The other beams are deposited at zero power. Deposition is linear in power, so F is the deposition of those beams at their picket energies over the area of each element. It is the fluence on the wall, with the incidence and the overlap of the spots. Backscatter or transfer in the picket can be taken off the beams first; Ralph et al. 2018 use the picket energy net of outer backscatter.\n\nThe summaries. Every published bubble scaling is written in the picket fluence of the outer cones, as a nominal energy over a nominal area:\n\nRalph et al., PoP 25, 082701 (2018) use the outer picket energy net of backscatter over the outer phase-plate spot area (speed ∝ I^0.36).\nCallahan et al., PoP 25, 056305 (2018) use the outer picket energy over the one-side outer spot area, 53 mm² (size ∝ (E/A)^0.5).\nIzumi et al., PoP 28, 022706 (2021) use the spatially averaged outer picket fluence over the first ns (tip position ∝ E^0.2). It is 27% lower when the quads and cones are split at the same energy.\n\nOver a fixed area that quantity is energy, whose ratios between designs are the energy ratios (the :average of picket_irradiance). mean = ∫F² dA / ∫F dA is the energy over the effective area (∫F dA)²/∫F² dA, and it also counts the overlap of the spots. One isolated spot gives its energy over its effective area. For a flat top that is exactly E/A; for a super-Gaussian of order n it is 2^(-2/n) of the peak fluence, and half the peak for a Gaussian. k coincident spots give k times the value of one, and separated spots the same value as one. mean therefore includes the split-quad change of Izumi 2021, which a fixed area does not. As an integral, it is smooth in the pointing and nearly independent of the mesh. The two measures differ when the picket energies of the beams are uneven or their spots move onto one another (research notes, \"The published picket fluence, the trough and the inner-beam corridor (2026-11-18)\"). peak follows one element and the mesh, and it measures neither quantity.\n\nMesh. An element average lowers ∫F² dA by about h²/(6a²) for a Gaussian spot of 1/e radius a on elements of size h. mean therefore converges as O(h²) and is low on a coarse mesh. With the NIF outer spots (0.67 × 0.39 mm 1/e semi-axes) on NIF hohlraums, it is about 1% low at h = 0.2 mm and 0.3% low at 0.1 mm, and its ratio between two hohlraums is 0.35% high at 0.2 mm (research notes). A design and its reference should be compared on the same mesh size, where most of the error cancels in the ratio. A radiation mesh of 0.8 mm is too coarse for the map, and a separate target used only for the deposition is cheap.\n\nGeneric in the number type of the beams (ForwardDiff duals through the pointing). Derived: a link built on the published definitions and exponents. No facility criterion sets it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.picket_fluence"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.picket_hot_elements-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"picket_hot_elements(ev, names...; n = 8, pulses = nothing, window = nothing, by = :group,\n                    power = nothing, groups = (:outer44, :outer50), lpi = false,\n                    inner = (:inner23, :inner30)) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nThe n elements of the named parts or components with the largest irradiance from the beams in groups, hottest first. The powers and beams are as in picket_irradiance. Each entry gives the index of the element (element), its irradiance (TW/mm²), centroid z (mm), azimuth phi (degrees, 0–360) and distance (mm). The distance runs from the centroid to the nearest centerline of a beam in inner with power (Inf without one), and it is the distance weighted by the :corridor measure of picket_irradiance. The report is in Float64, with the values of any duals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.picket_hot_elements"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.picket_irradiance-Tuple{Evaluation, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"picket_irradiance(ev, names...; pulses = nothing, window = nothing, by = :group,\n                  power = nothing, groups = (:outer44, :outer50), lpi = false,\n                  measure = :peak, area = 53.0, corridor = 1.3, edge = 0.3,\n                  inner = (:inner23, :inner30)) -> Real\n\nThe laser irradiance (TW/mm²) of the beams of an Evaluation in groups (the outer cones by default) on the elements of the named parts or components. measure selects the summary:\n\n:peak: the largest over the elements;\n:weighted: the energy-weighted mean ∫I² dA / ∫I dA, the summary that picket_ratio and PicketLinkedBubble use (picket_fluence);\n:average: the spatially averaged irradiance over a fixed spot area, which is the power absorbed on the elements over area (mm²) and the quantity of the published bubble scalings (below);\n:corridor: the mean irradiance on the elements near the centerlines of the beams in inner, ∫w I dA / ∫w dA with a weight w that is 1 within corridor − edge/2 of a centerline and 0 beyond corridor + edge/2 (below).\n\nThe irradiance times the length of the phase is its fluence, so the ratios between designs are the fluence ratios. The powers are those of whatever phase the evaluation represents: the picket, the trough or the foot.\n\nThe powers. With pulses (keyed as for with_pulses, with by) and window = (t0, t1) (ns), the power of each beam is its mean power over the window. With power, a function of the beam that returns its power (TW), the power can be set, for example, by cone from a picket table. With neither, the power of each beam is used, as in an evaluation run at the powers of a phase, or with power = b -> r * b.power to scale them. The other beams are deposited at zero power, by the deposition operator of the scenario with the setup beams of the evaluation (ev.beams, which set the pointing, spots and focus). With lpi = true the beams are instead the effective beams of the evaluation (ev.effective), after the laser–plasma model of the scenario at its time, for example net of a Backscatter and after a transfer. They are used at their present powers, and pulses and power are then an error. Otherwise the laser–plasma model is not used, and backscatter or transfer is taken off the powers given. Each call performs one deposition. On an evaluation inside optimize_pointing that already contains the element powers of the groups at their present powers (picket_ceiling), no deposition is needed. Generic in the number type (ForwardDiff duals through the pointing and powers).\n\nThe published quantity (:average). The published scalings use these definitions:\n\nCallahan et al., PoP 25, 056305 (2018) divide the picket energy of the outer cones by the one-side outer spot area of the NIF phase plates, 53 mm². That area is the default area, so that the irradiance times the picket length in ns is their E/A in kJ/mm².\nRalph et al., PoP 25, 082701 (2018) take the outer picket energy net of backscatter over the outer spot area.\nIzumi et al., PoP 28, 022706 (2021) take the spatially averaged outer picket fluence over the first ns.\n\nIn Briareus terms, the quantity is the power absorbed on the named parts, over a fixed area. For the wall, light clipped on the outside of the entrance holes, light landing on the capsule and light leaving the target do not grow the bubble on the wall (L). The power is net of backscatter when the powers are, either with lpi = true and a backscatter model or with powers net of backscatter. A ratio to a reference does not depend on the area, and an absolute value uses the convention of Callahan. A fixed area does not count the overlap of the spots, which :weighted does. The two measures differ for uneven powers between beams and for spots moved onto one another (research notes, \"The published picket fluence, the trough and the inner-beam corridor (2026-11-18)\").\n\nThe inner-beam corridor (:corridor). Izumi et al., PoP 30, 032706 (2023) found that outer bubble spikes in line with the 23.5° beams clip those beams and make the core more oblate. The corridor measures the outer light where the inner beams pass. A centerline is the line through the aim of a beam along its direction, for each beam of inner with nonzero power in the evaluation. The distance is that from the element centroid, in 3-D, to the nearest centerline. The weight is one minus the product over the centerlines of (1 − s), where s rises from 0 to 1 across the edge as a quintic smoothstep. The weight is therefore smooth (C²) in the aims of the inner beams as they move, and exactly 0 or 1 away from the edge; elements beyond every edge are skipped. corridor = 1.3 mm and edge = 0.3 mm are engineering defaults. The value 1.3 mm was chosen for a re-pointing study (user, 2026-11-18). The edge is wider than an element of a 0.2 mm deposition map, so that the mean changes smoothly as centroids cross it. Neither value is a published criterion.\n\nThe peak follows one element and the mesh, since on a coarser mesh the hottest spot is averaged over more area. The other three measures are integrals, nearly independent of the mesh (picket_fluence). picket_hot_elements lists the hottest elements with their distance to the nearest inner centerline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.picket_irradiance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.picket_ratio-Tuple{PicketFluence, PicketFluence}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"picket_ratio(design::PicketFluence, reference::PicketFluence; at = 1.0) -> E\n\nThe picket_ratio E of BubbleAbsorption for design. E is at times the ratio of the energy-weighted mean picket fluences of the two designs (mean, picket_fluence), so that E follows the pointing (overlap, incidence, spot sizes) and the picket energy.\n\nRelative to what. The tip speed of the bubble (0.36 mm/ns, ∝ E^0.2, Izumi 2021) was fitted at E = 1, on the HDC basecamp of Izumi et al., PoP 28, 022706 (2021). That platform is a 5.75 mm Au hohlraum with unsplit outer quads (research notes, \"Hohlraum dynamics calibrated to published data (2026-10-24)\"). With reference = basecamp_picket_fluence() and at = 1, E is relative to that calibration. Its pointing is a proxy, since the facility pointing is unpublished, and its picket energy was read from a figure by eye. The absolute E is therefore uncertain by more than its changes between designs. A re-pointing study is better referred to a design whose E is already set: picket_ratio(new, old; at = E_old) changes E through the pointing only. E_old is 1.1 in examples/n221204.jl, from the picket energy and the split quads. Both maps should be computed on the same mesh size.\n\nWith remake(bubble; picket_ratio = E), the design then enters the tip speed (∝ E^0.2) and the collision time (t_c − turn_on ∝ E^-0.16). The link is opt-in, and no default uses it; the exponents and calibrated constants are those of the model. Generic in the number type. Derived; no facility criterion sets it. The exponents are published (Izumi 2021: 0.2 ± 0.03 on seven shots), but their application to a re-pointing at fixed energy is not. No re-pointing study is published, and the nearest test is the split-quad shot of Izumi 2021 (research notes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.picket_ratio"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.CH_IONS","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"Fully ionized CH (1:1): [(0.5, 6, 12.011), (0.5, 1, 1.008)].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CH_IONS"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.CBET","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"CBET(; leh_z, ne_nc = 0.06, Te = 5000, Ti = 1500, ions = CH_IONS, mach = 1.0,\n     flow = nothing, length = 1.5, polarization = :smoothed, multiplier = 1.0,\n     nsteps = 20)\n\nReduced steady-state CBET among the beams that share each laser entrance hole, applied as a laser–plasma model (apply_lpi, Scenario(...; lpi)).\n\nleh_z — z (mm) of the LEH planes; beams whose ports have z > 0 cross at +leh_z, the rest at -leh_z.\nPlasma in the crossing region: ne_nc (electron density over the critical density at the nominal wavelength), Te, Ti (eV), and ion species ions ((fraction, Z, A) number fractions; default fully ionized CH). Defaults are the NIF LEH conditions of Michel et al. 2009; Ti is not given in that paper and is an assumption.\nflow — outflow speed along the hohlraum axis (km/s, away from the hohlraum), or mach times the ion sound speed √((Z̄ Te + 3 Ti)/(Ā m_p)) when flow is nothing. The value is signed: a negative mach or flow points into the hohlraum. With outflow the inner (steeper) cones get the larger blue shift, so flow alone moves power from the inner to the outer cones; red-detuned inner cones (dlambda > 0) take power back. TwoZoneCBET puts a supersonic zone outside the LEH and a slow one inside.\nlength — maximum interaction length (mm). A pair crossing at angle θ interacts over min(length, w/sin θ), with w the mean beam width at the LEH plane.\npolarization — :smoothed for NIF polarization smoothing with uncorrelated speckle, which multiplies the gain by (1+cos²θ)/4, or :parallel for co-polarized beams, which leave the gain unchanged.\nmultiplier — calibration factor on all gains. Fit it to shot data: published NIF designs tune CBET with saturation clamps and power multipliers.\nclamp — ion-wave saturation level δn/n (nothing = linear theory, which grossly overpredicts transfer at NIF intensities). The coupling of each pair is multiplied by 1/√(1 + (δn/clamp)²), a smooth version of capping the linear δn/n at clamp. Published NIF calibrations range from 3×10⁻⁴ (2011) to 0.002 (foot) / 0.0075 (peak) for N221204 (Kritcher et al., PRE 109, 025204, 2024); the default is 0.002. cbet_clamp_schedule builds the foot/peak schedule from a PulseShape.\nsaturation — how clamp acts: :smooth (default, the factor above) or :min, the hard limit δn = min(clamp, δn_linear) of Strozzi et al., PRL 118, 025002 (2017), i.e. a factor min(1, clamp/δn), as in HYDRA's inline model, so its clamp values carry over one to one. At δn = clamp the smooth factor is √2 lower.\n\nAny plasma parameter and multiplier may be a number, a function of time (ns), or a (times, values) table. The exchange of each pair uses the geometric-mean beam area at the LEH, so total power is conserved. The coupled exchange is integrated with nsteps RK4 steps. Beam detuning comes from Beam.dlambda (Å at the beam wavelength — NIF quotes Δλ at 1ω, so divide those values by 3 for 3ω beams).\n\nThe pair coefficients and the exchanges at the two LEHs are computed on threads; the results are identical for every thread count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CBET"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.TwoZoneCBET","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"TwoZoneCBET(outside::CBET, inside::CBET)\nTwoZoneCBET(; leh_z, offset = 0.5, outside = (;), inside = (;))\n\nCBET in two crossing zones per entrance hole, applied in the order in which the beams meet them. The outside zone lies outside the LEH, in the supersonic outflow, where the Doppler shift of the flow moves power from the inner cones to the outer ones even without detuning. The inside zone lies inside the LEH, in slow flow, where the transfer is set by the wavelength detuning; red-detuned inner cones take power from the outer cones. Each zone is a full CBET with a separate crossing plane, flow (signed mach or flow), plasma state (ne_nc, Te, Ti, ions, as numbers, functions of time or (times, values) tables), clamp, multiplier and length, and stays generic in the number type.\n\nThe keyword form places the zones offset (mm) outside and inside the LEH planes ±leh_z and passes outside/inside (NamedTuples) to CBET; their defaults are Mach 2 outflow outside (the near-sonic LEH flow turns supersonic outside, Michel et al.\n\nand Mach 0 inside. Both are engineering defaults; neither is measured. In a low-fill\n\nhohlraum the net transfer is the balance of the two. In Pickworth et al. 2020's HYDRA runs, power is transferred from the inner to the outer cones outside the LEH and from the outer to the inner cones inside. For this reason Δλ = 0 does not give zero transfer in such a hohlraum; I-Raum needed ~0.5 Å to cancel flow-driven CBET (NC24). n221204_cbet is a version calibrated to the N221204 HYDRA transfer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TwoZoneCBET"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.calibrate_cbet-Tuple{Union{CBET, TwoZoneCBET}, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"calibrate_cbet(model, beams, data; receivers, fit = (:multiplier,), time = 0.0,\n               bounds = Dict(:multiplier => (1e-3, 10.0), :mach => (0.0, 3.0),\n                             :clamp => (1e-4, 0.05))) -> (model, result)\n\nFit CBET calibration parameters to measured or reference transfer data. data is a list of (dlambda, ratio) pairs: with the receivers groups (e.g. the inner cones) detuned by dlambda (Å, beam wavelength — 1ω-quoted NIF values ÷ 3), their total power should be multiplied by ratio (e.g. 1.6 for \"inner +60%\"). The constant-valued fields in fit (any of :multiplier, :mach, :clamp) are fitted by bounded L-BFGS-B on squared log-ratio residuals with automatic-differentiation gradients. Returns the calibrated model and the Optim result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.calibrate_cbet"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.calibrate_cbet-Tuple{Union{CBET, TwoZoneCBET}, AbstractVector}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"calibrate_cbet(model, shots::AbstractVector; receivers, fit = (:multiplier,), bounds,\n               penalty = nothing, iterations = 200) -> (model, result)\n\nJoint multi-shot calibration, in which one model (shared plasma physics) is fitted to the post-transfer power fractions of several shots at once. Each entry of shots is a NamedTuple (; beams_at, data, delay = 0.0): beams_at(t) gives the setup beams of that shot (its powers and detuning) and data its (t, fraction[, weight]) rows, as in the multi-epoch method. delay (ns) shifts the shot against the time tables of the model: the model is evaluated at t - delay, so a shot whose rise to peak comes 0.2 ns earlier than that of the reference pulse has delay = -0.2 and is evaluated with the plasma history (and clamp schedule) of the reference 0.2 ns early. penalty(model) (optional) is added to the loss, e.g. a constraint on the Δλ null; it must be generic in the number type. fit and bounds are as in the multi-epoch method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.calibrate_cbet"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.calibrate_cbet-Tuple{Union{CBET, TwoZoneCBET}, Function, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"calibrate_cbet(model, beams_at, data; receivers, fit = (:multiplier,), bounds,\n               iterations = 200) -> (model, result)\n\nMulti-epoch calibration, which fits model (a CBET or TwoZoneCBET) to a time series of post-transfer power fractions. beams_at(t) gives the setup beams at time t (ns); data lists (t, fraction) or (t, fraction, weight) rows, fraction being the receivers groups' share of the total power after model (e.g. the inner cone fraction of PRE 109 Fig. 9b). The fit minimizes the weighted sum of squared fraction residuals.\n\nfit names the parameters: fields of a CBET (:multiplier, :mach, :flow, :clamp, :Te, :Ti, :ne_nc), or for a TwoZoneCBET (zone, field) pairs with zone :outside, :inside or :both (one value shared by the two zones; a bare field name means :both). A field holding a number is one parameter; a field holding a (times, values) table fits every value at its fixed time, so plasma-state histories are calibration inputs. bounds maps fields to (lo, hi); parameters with lo > 0 are fitted in log space. The optimizer is bounded L-BFGS-B with forward-mode gradients.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.calibrate_cbet"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.cbet_clamp_schedule-Tuple{Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"cbet_clamp_schedule(pulse; foot = 0.002, peak = 0.0075, width = 0.2) -> (times, values)\n\nCBET saturation clamp δn/n stepping from foot to peak over width (ns) centered on the start of the rise of pulse to peak power (pulse_rise), as a two-knot (times, values) table for CBET's clamp (constant beyond the knots, so fitting :clamp fits the foot and peak values). The defaults are PRE 109's HYDRA setting for N221204 (0.002 until the rise, 0.0075 after; PoP 31, 070502 quotes 0.008), which on PRE 109 Fig. 9's input powers puts the switch at 3.9–4.1 ns as in the deck (4.0–4.2 ns on the delivered pulse). The schedule is a HYDRA-equivalent setting; it is not derived from physics. With saturation = :min the values mean what they mean in HYDRA.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cbet_clamp_schedule"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.cbet_coupling-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"cbet_coupling(Ω, k, kdotu; ne, Te, Ti, ions) -> Γ\n\nIon-acoustic response Γ = χe(1+χi)/(1+χe+χi) for beat frequency Ω (rad/s), beat wavenumber k (1/m), Doppler shift k·u (rad/s), electron density ne (m⁻³), temperatures Te, Ti (eV), and ion species ions — (fraction, Z, A) tuples with number fractions summing to one. χi sums over species, each with its Landau damping; the damping matters for CH, where the protons dominate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cbet_coupling"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.cbet_gain","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"cbet_gain(m, b, c, t) -> g\n\nLinear (unsaturated) spatial gain rate (1/mm per TW/mm² of beam c) of beam b due to beam c crossing at the LEH, at time t, including the polarization factor and multiplier. The gain is antisymmetric: cbet_gain(m, c, b, t) == -cbet_gain(m, b, c, t).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cbet_gain"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.cbet_null-Tuple{LaserPlasmaModel, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"cbet_null(model, beams, t; receivers, lo = 0.0, hi = 1.5, tol = 1e-4) -> dlambda\n\nThe detuning (Å at the beam wavelength; × 3 for NIF's 1ω convention) of the receivers groups, all other beams at zero, for which model leaves the total power of the receivers unchanged at time t. This Δλ cancels flow-driven transfer (TwoZoneCBET). NC24's I-Raum needed ~0.5 Å at 1ω \"mostly to negate\" it. Their 1–1.55 Å for HYBRID-E is the Δλ of round hot spots, which also compensates bubble absorption, so it lies above the null. The null is found by bisection on receiver_ratio over [lo, hi] and is NaN when the ratio does not cross 1 on that interval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cbet_null"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.cbet_plasma-Tuple{CBET, Any, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"Plasma parameters of m at time t as a NamedTuple (SI densities, eV, m/s flow).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cbet_plasma"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.hybride_cbet-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"hybride_cbet(; leh_z = 5.62, offset = 0.5, delay = 0.0) -> TwoZoneCBET\n\nTwo-zone CBET fitted jointly to HYDRA's post-transfer cone fraction of three HYBRID-E shots at three detunings: N210808 (1.8 Å, PRE 109 Fig. 1e with PRE 106 Fig. 1d's incident powers, pre109_fig1, n210808_fig1), N220919 (2.5 Å, n220919_fig9) and N221204 (2.75 Å, n221204_fig9), with one plasma history for all, under two published constraints: Te ≤ 5.5 keV (the LEH values of Michel 2009 and Barrios 2016) and the Δλ null inside NC24's 1–1.55 Å for HYBRID-E. That band is the Δλ of round hot spots in the 2020–21 shots and lies above the transfer null; in hybride_cbet_iraum the null is held at I-Raum's ≤ 0.5 Å instead. The model has HYDRA's hard-minimum saturation (saturation = :min), with the clamp schedule switched at the rise (cbet_clamp_schedule) and both clamp values fitted: 1.4×10⁻³ in the foot (not binding, unconstrained) and 4.8×10⁻⁴ at peak (binding; ±27%). The outside zone has Mach 1.54 and multiplier 0.14 (×/÷ 2), and the inside zone Mach 0 and multiplier 0.73 (±18%). The fitted Te(t) is 2.1 keV at 1 ns, 2.8 at 4, 3.4–3.9 in the peak and 5.5 (the bound) at 8 ns (±6–14%).\n\nThe fit (107 rows, 0.8–8.4 ns) has a cone-fraction rms of 0.027 (N221204), 0.016 (N220919) and 0.047 (N210808), against 0.016 / 0.030 / 0.068 for n221204_cbet; the null is 0.97 ± 0.10 Å at 5.6 ns. The model does not reproduce HYDRA's flat cross-shot response. Its dCF/dΔλ at peak is 0.17–0.20 per Å, whereas HYDRA's N220919 and N221204 differ by 0.03–0.06 per Å, so N210808's peak is 0.07–0.10 low and N221204's 0.03 high. delay (ns) shifts the time tables for a pulse whose rise comes delay later than N221204's (a negative delay for an earlier rise); leh_z is as in n221204_cbet.\n\nDerived (research notes, \"Joint multi-shot CBET calibration (2026-10-24)\"): the parameters are effective values fitted to HYDRA and do not describe a measured plasma state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.hybride_cbet"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.hybride_cbet_iraum-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"hybride_cbet_iraum(; leh_z = 5.62, offset = 0.5, delay = 0.0) -> TwoZoneCBET\n\nThe joint three-shot fit of hybride_cbet (same data, model, Te ≤ 5.5 keV bound and fitted hard-minimum clamps) with the Δλ null constraint corrected. NC24's 1–1.55 Å for HYBRID-E is the detuning that minimized hot-spot P2 in the 2020–21 shots, which also compensates the bubble absorption of the inner beams, so it lies above the transfer null. The published statement on the null itself is I-Raum's (0.5 Å \"mostly to negate the flow-induced CBET\", NC24). Here the null at N221204's peak (5.6 and 7.0 ns) is held in 0.25–0.5 Å. The lower edge was chosen in Briareus so that a flow-driven transfer remains at Δλ = 0.\n\nThe outside zone has Mach 1.49 (± 0.8) and multiplier 0.072 (×/÷ 5), and the inside zone Mach 0 and multiplier 1.60 (± 31%). The fitted clamps are 9.1×10⁻⁴ (foot, not binding, unconstrained) and 1.4×10⁻⁴ (peak, binding, ± 29%). The peak clamp lies below the 5×10⁻⁴–7.5×10⁻³ of HYDRA decks and is correlated with the inside multiplier and Te (correlations −0.92 to −0.97). The fitted Te(t) is 3.2 keV at 1 ns, 4.5 at 4, 2.5–3.8 in the peak and 5.5 (the bound) at 8 ns (± 7–15%).\n\nThe fit (107 rows, 0.8–8.4 ns) has a cone-fraction rms of 0.024 (N221204), 0.016 (N220919) and 0.028 (N210808), and 0.024 over all shots, against 0.027 / 0.016 / 0.047 and 0.033 over all shots for hybride_cbet. Its loss is 0.059, against 0.120 with the null in 1–1.55 Å. The null is 0.24 ± 0.20 Å at 5.6 ns (0.29 at 7 ns), and dCF/dΔλ is 0.137 ± 0.011 per Å at peak (2.5–2.75 Å), linear from 1 Å. HYDRA's cross-shot response stays 2–3× flatter, which is not supported by the measured hot-spot P2 sensitivity (research notes). delay and leh_z are as in hybride_cbet.\n\nDerived (research notes, \"Joint multi-shot CBET calibration: the null\"): the parameters are effective values fitted to HYDRA and do not describe a measured plasma state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.hybride_cbet_iraum"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.hybride_shots-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"hybride_shots() -> NamedTuple\n\nScalar per-shot observables of N210808, N220919, N221204 and N230729 (data/hybride_shots.csv, sources in its header). The columns are shot (strings), dlambda (Å at 1ω, with values 1.8, 2.5, 2.75 and 2.9), laser_energy (MJ), peak_power (TW), hot-spot P2 hs_p2 and its error u_hs_p2 (µm, NaN = not quoted), yield (MJ) and HYDRA's published clamps clamp_foot, clamp_peak (NaN where not published).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.hybride_shots"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.ion_wave_amplitude","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"ion_wave_amplitude(m::CBET, b, c, t = 0.0) -> δn/n\n\nLinear ion-acoustic density perturbation driven by beams b and c at their LEH plane intensities, for comparison with m.clamp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ion_wave_amplitude"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.n210808_fig1-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"n210808_fig1() -> NamedTuple\n\nKritcher et al., PRE 106, 025201 (2022) Fig. 1(d, e) for N210808 (HYDRA, Δλ 1.8 Å): incident and post-CBET power per cone and inner cone fraction, digitized (data/pre106_fig1_n210808.csv, sources in its header). The columns are those of n221204_fig9. The incident powers carry HYDRA's multipliers (Fig. 1 caption), as Fig. 9's do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.n210808_fig1"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.n220919_fig9-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"n220919_fig9() -> NamedTuple\n\nPRE 109, 025204 (2024) Fig. 9 for N220919 (HYDRA; the black curves, Δλ 2.5 Å), digitized (data/pre109_fig9_n220919.csv, sources in its header). The columns are those of n221204_fig9 (t, cf_in, cf_post, p23_in, …, p50_post; 0.7–7.4 ns, where the black curves end), each with a 1σ reading error u_* and a reading flag f_* (\"v\" seen, \"p\" partly covered, \"h\" hidden under N221204's red curve, value N221204's; see the header); NaN where not readable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.n220919_fig9"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.n221204_cbet-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"n221204_cbet(; leh_z = 5.62, offset = 0.5) -> TwoZoneCBET\n\nTwo-zone CBET calibrated to the N221204 HYDRA transfer (PRE 109, 025204 Fig. 9b, as digitized in n221204_fig9), on hybride_mock_pointing beams with Fig. 9's input powers (n221204_fig9_beams) and Δλ 2.75 Å (1ω, 0.917 Å at 3ω) on the inner cones. Both zones have the clamp schedule of the deck (δn/n 0.002 until 3.9 ns, 0.0075 from 4.1 ns), CH at ne/nc 0.06 and Ti 1.5 keV (repository defaults), and one fitted electron-temperature history Te(t) (2.8 keV at 1 ns, 3.7 at 4, 5.5 at 5.5, 7.4 at 8 ns; constant outside). The outside zone has a fitted outflow Mach number of 1.23 and a fitted multiplier of 0.30; the inside zone is at Mach 0 with a fitted multiplier of 1.23. Over 0.8–8.4 ns the cone-fraction rms of the fit is 0.016 and its worst residual is 0.041 (8.4 ns); the outer-cone post/input ratios (not fitted) match Fig. 9a to 0.03. leh_z (mm) sets the LEH planes (TwoZoneCBET). Its default is half the 11.24 mm length of the HYBRID-E hohlraum fitted, and a caller with a target passes its half-length, as examples/n221204.jl does with geometry.length / 2.\n\nDerived (research notes, \"Two-zone CBET calibrated to PRE 109 Fig. 9\"): the model is an effective calibration to one HYDRA simulation of one shot and does not describe a measured plasma state. Times are shot times, so the model applies to pulses timed like N221204's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.n221204_cbet"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.n221204_fig10-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"n221204_fig10() -> NamedTuple\n\nCapsule radiation-flux P2/P0 against time from HYDRA (post-CBET) in PRE 109, 025204 (2024) Fig. 10, for N210808, N220919 and N221204, digitized (data/pre109_fig10.csv, sources in its header). The columns are t (ns, 0.8–8.9 in 0.1 ns steps plus the extrema of each curve), n210808, n220919, n221204 (P2/P0; negative is waist-hot) and their reading errors u210808, u220919, u221204. The error is ±0.002 where a curve is visible and 0.004–0.010 where another curve covers it. N221204 lies under the other two for much of 6.6–8.9 ns. Along the late spikes the error is 0.006, and 0.015 for N221204's spike, which is hidden under N220919's except its left flank. N221204's minimum is −0.173 at 5.58 ns. The curves are HYDRA calculations; none is measured.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.n221204_fig10"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.n221204_fig9-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"n221204_fig9() -> NamedTuple\n\nPRE 109, 025204 (2024) Fig. 9 for N221204 (HYDRA), digitized (data/pre109_fig9_n221204.csv, sources in its header). The columns are t (ns, 0.7–9.0 in 0.1 ns steps), inner cone fraction cf_in/cf_post before and after CBET (Fig. 9b), and per-cone powers p23_in, p23_post, …, p50_post (TW, Fig. 9a). The reading error is about ±0.01 in cone fraction and ±2 TW per cone; the foot inner-cone powers (< 8 TW) are below the resolution of the figure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.n221204_fig9"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.n221204_fig9_beams-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"n221204_fig9_beams(beams, fig9, t) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nbeams (NIF cones :inner23, :inner30, :outer44, :outer50) with the powers HYDRA fed to CBET at time t in PRE 109 Fig. 9 (fig9 = n221204_fig9(), nearest sample). The outer-cone powers come from Fig. 9a and the inner-cone total from the input cone fraction of Fig. 9b, because Fig. 9a's foot inner powers are below its resolution. The inner total is split evenly between 23.5° and 30°, as Fig. 9a shows at peak, and the power of each cone is shared equally by its beams. These are HYDRA's degraded powers (~370 TW at peak, against ~440 TW delivered).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.n221204_fig9_beams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.n221204_pulse-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"n221204_pulse() -> NamedTuple\n\nN221204's delivered laser power and measured Dante 1 radiation temperature, Pak et al., PRE 109, 025203 (2024) Fig. 1, read from the vector paths of the figure (data/pak2024_fig1_n221204.csv, sources in its header). The columns are t (ns, 0–9.6 in 0.05 ns steps), power (TW, total of the 192 beams; 2.051 MJ) and dante1 (eV; NaN outside 3.3–9.4 ns, the plotted range). Dante 1 peaks at 311.9 eV at 8.30 ns. Only the total power is published in the paper; the inner/outer split is not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.n221204_pulse"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.plasma_dispersion-Tuple{Real}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"plasma_dispersion(ξ) -> Complex\n\nFried–Conte plasma dispersion function for real argument, Z(ξ) = −2 D(ξ) + i√π exp(−ξ²), where D is Dawson's integral. The function is differentiable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.plasma_dispersion"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.pre109_fig1-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"pre109_fig1() -> NamedTuple\n\nPRE 109, 025204 (2024) Fig. 1(d, e), digitized (data/pre109_fig1_n210808.csv, sources in its header). The columns are the inner cone fraction before and after CBET (cf_in_n210808, cf_post_n210808, cf_in_n221204, cf_post_n221204) and the power of the 23° cone (p23_in_*, p23_post_*, TW) against t (ns), each with reading errors u_* and flags f_*. N210808's post-CBET curve in this table is that of the 2024 HYDRA model (the one of Fig. 9 and Fig. 10); it differs from PRE 106's (n210808_fig1) by +0.04 on average (PRE 106 higher; 0.27 against 0.17 at 3.4 ns, +0.03 in the peak) while the incident curves agree to 0.004.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pre109_fig1"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.pulse_rise-Tuple{PulseShape}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"pulse_rise(pulse; lookback = 1.0, delta = 0.15) -> t\n\nStart of the rise to peak power (ns) of pulse, a PulseShape or (times, powers). The start is the knee where the tangent at the half-maximum crossing t½ (slope over t½ ± delta) meets the power lookback ns before t½, the step the rise leaves. It is invariant to a scale factor on the power, so delivered and HYDRA-degraded inputs give nearly the same time: 4.11 ns on N221204's delivered pulse (n221204_pulse), 4.0 on PRE 109 Fig. 9's degraded input powers, where the HYDRA deck switches its CBET clamp (\"until the beginning of the rise to peak power\", 3.9–4.1 ns). This is an engineering definition for pulses with one main rise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pulse_rise"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.receiver_ratio-Tuple{LaserPlasmaModel, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"receiver_ratio(model, beams, dlambda; receivers, time = 0.0)\n\nRatio of the receivers groups' total power after/before model when those groups are detuned by dlambda (Å, beam wavelength) and all other beams by zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.receiver_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/lpi/#Briareus.zylstra2022_fig2-Tuple{}","page":"Laser–plasma models","text":"zylstra2022_fig2() -> NamedTuple\n\nThe HYBRID-E LEH-size pair of Zylstra et al., PRE 106, 025202 (2022) Fig. 2, read from the vector paths of the figure (data/zylstra2022_fig2.csv, sources in its header): the symcaps N191118 (3.64 mm LEH) and N210601 (3.1 mm LEH). The fields are t (ns, 0–10.85 in 0.05 ns steps), power_n191118, power_n210601 (TW, total delivered; 1.623 and 1.663 MJ, peaks 470.6 and 398.9 TW), dante_n191118, dante_n210601 (eV, measured with Dante; NaN before 3.50 and 1.60 ns, where the curves start) and bang, the SPIDER bang times of 9.37 and 9.48 ns in the figure. Only the total power is published; the inner/outer split is not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.zylstra2022_fig2"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Freeing-a-bundle-for-radiography","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"A radiograph of an imploding capsule needs a backlighter, and on NIF the backlighter lasers come out of the drive. Freeing one bundle (two quads of four beams, one quad in each hemisphere, sharing an amplifier chain) leaves 46 quads to drive the hohlraum, and the missing ports leave an asymmetry in the capsule drive. On a layered DT ignition shot there is little margin for it. A mode-1 asymmetry of the drive, a flux difference between opposite sides of the capsule, pushes the hot spot sideways at about 100 km/s for each percent of mode-1 flux (MacGowan et al., High Energy Density Physics 40, 100944, 2021), and the drive itself is the scarcest resource of the shot.\n\nWhich bundle can be freed is set first by the radiograph: its quads must reach a backlighter foil placed across the target from the detector, perpendicular to the line of sight, without their light touching the hohlraum or its entrance-hole shields on the way, and the x rays from the foil must reach the detector unobstructed. This tutorial finds the bundles that can serve each of two equatorial lines of sight, ranks them by the symmetry they cost, and compares three ways to win that symmetry back: freeing the symmetric partner of the bundle as well, rebalancing the powers of the remaining quads, and re-pointing them within the clearance the entrance holes allow. It introduces three-dimensional mode analysis with real spherical harmonics, the symmetry of the NIF ports, scenarios on a subset of the beams, the influence matrix, optimization with automatic-differentiation gradients and under constraints, beam-clearance checks, and a re-pointing study in two stages, from quads to their beams.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Briareus), \"docs\", \"literate\", \"_plotting.jl\"))   # figure helpers\ncutaway! (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Freeing a bundle for radiography"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Modes-on-the-capsule","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"Expand the incident flux on the capsule in real spherical harmonics,\n\nH(theta phi) = sum_l m c_lm Y_lm(theta phi)\n\nwhere Y_lm with m  0 varies as cos mphi and with m  0 as sin mphi; Y_11 points along +x and Y_1-1 along +y. flux_ylm returns the coefficients, in the order of mode_index. The size of mode l relative to the mean is\n\nepsilon_l = frac1c_00sqrt(2l + 1)sum_m c_lm^2\n\ncomputed by mode_amplitude. For a field with azimuthal symmetry about any axis this is the familiar Legendre ratio a_la_0; for l = 1 it is the fractional amplitude epsilon of a flux 1 + epsiloncosgamma, where gamma is the angle from the direction of the asymmetry.","title":"Modes on the capsule"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#The-hohlraum-and-its-pointing","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The hohlraum is 10.1 mm long with a radius of 2.875 mm and 3.1 mm laser entrance holes, around a capsule of radius 0.9 mm. Its wall, end caps, entrance holes and capsule carry their exact cylindrical, planar and spherical surfaces (see the Integrating sphere tutorial). nif_quads gives the 48 quads, at 8 TW each, with their measured phase-plate spots. The outer quads are aimed on the hohlraum axis near the entrance-hole planes, at z = pm 501 mm (50° cone) and pm 520 mm (44.5°); the inner quads at z = pm 363 mm (30°) and pm 309 mm (23.5°), displaced 0.60 and 1.09 mm from the axis away from their own port, across the hohlraum. This pointing is an illustrative example and was not fielded on any shot.\n\ntarget = hohlraum(length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9,\n                  wall = gold(albedo = 0.85, conversion = 0.8))\npointing = Dict(:outer50 => (0.0, 5.01), :outer44 => (0.0, 5.20),\n                :inner30 => (0.60, 3.63), :inner23 => (1.09, 3.09))\nfunction aimed(b)\n    r, z = pointing[b.group]\n    φ = port_angles(b)[2]\n    return remake(b; aim = (-r * cosd(φ), -r * sind(φ), sign(b.port[3]) * z))\nend\nquads = aimed.(nif_quads(power = 8.0))\n48-element Vector{Beam{Float64}}:\n Beam(Q15T, θ=23.50°, φ=78.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.2126, -1.069, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q42T, θ=23.50°, φ=168.75°, 8 TW, aim=(1.069, -0.2126, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q33T, θ=23.50°, φ=258.75°, 8 TW, aim=(0.2126, 1.069, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q24T, θ=23.50°, φ=348.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-1.069, 0.2126, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q13T, θ=30.58°, φ=34.33°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.4955, -0.3384, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q41T, θ=30.00°, φ=123.75°, 8 TW, aim=(0.3333, -0.4989, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q36T, θ=30.58°, φ=214.33°, 8 TW, aim=(0.4955, 0.3384, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q26T, θ=30.00°, φ=303.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.3333, 0.4989, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q14T, θ=44.50°, φ=16.29°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, -0, 5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q12T, θ=44.50°, φ=62.46°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, -0, 5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n ⋮\n Beam(Q22B, θ=135.50°, φ=343.71°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, 0, -5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q16B, θ=150.00°, φ=56.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.3333, -0.4989, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q44B, θ=149.42°, φ=145.67°, 8 TW, aim=(0.4955, -0.3384, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q31B, θ=150.00°, φ=236.25°, 8 TW, aim=(0.3333, 0.4989, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q21B, θ=149.42°, φ=325.67°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.4955, 0.3384, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q15B, θ=156.50°, φ=11.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-1.069, -0.2126, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q42B, θ=156.50°, φ=101.25°, 8 TW, aim=(0.2126, -1.069, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q33B, θ=156.50°, φ=191.25°, 8 TW, aim=(1.069, 0.2126, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q24B, θ=156.50°, φ=281.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.2126, 1.069, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n\nA Scenario holds what does not change while beams are re-aimed and their powers adjusted: the target, the deposition operator for these ports, the view factors, the linear response of the incident flux on the capsule to the power each element absorbs, and the mode weights of the capsule. Its parameterization here gives each quad a separate power.\n\nThe deposition operator is sampled more coarsely than its defaults, to keep the optimizations below quick. Its points lie at most 0.6 of the smaller spot semi-axis apart (the default is 0.3) and are refined only within 0.5 mm of the footprint of each beam (the default is 2 mm; no aim moves by more than 0.3 mm here); elsewhere each triangle gets one point. That cuts the operator to a fifth of its points and changes the modes below in the fourth digit.\n\nscenario = Scenario(target, GroupPointing(quads; by = :quad, fields = (:power,));\n                    observe = :capsule, deposition_kw = (; resolution = 0.6, reach = 0.5, level = 0))\nfull = flux_ylm(evaluate(scenario, quads); lmax = 4)\nshow_modes(label, c) = @printf(\"%-24s ε₁ %6.3f%%  ε₂ %6.3f%%  ε₃ %6.3f%%  ε₄ %6.3f%%  drive %.4f\\n\",\n                               label, (100 * mode_amplitude(c, l) for l in 1:4)..., c[1] / full[1])\nshow_modes(\"48 quads\", full)\n48 quads                 ε₁  0.000%  ε₂ 14.562%  ε₃  0.211%  ε₄  0.503%  drive 1.0000\n\nWith all 48 quads the drive has the large l = 2 asymmetry of this untuned pointing (the cone powers and pointing would tune it away) and no mode 1.","title":"The hohlraum and its pointing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#The-symmetry-of-the-ports","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The NIF ports are not symmetric under every rotation that leaves the hohlraum unchanged. Rotating every port by 180° about the x, y or z axis maps the set of ports onto itself, but rotating by 90° about z, inverting through the center, or reflecting through the equatorial plane does not:\n\nports = [b.port for b in quads]\nmaps_onto_itself(f) = all(any(norm(f(p) - q) < 1e-6 for q in ports) for p in ports)\noperations = [\"180° about x\" => p -> [p[1], -p[2], -p[3]],\n              \"180° about y\" => p -> [-p[1], p[2], -p[3]],\n              \"180° about z\" => p -> [-p[1], -p[2], p[3]],\n              \"90° about z\" => p -> [-p[2], p[1], p[3]],\n              \"inversion\" => p -> -p,\n              \"reflection in z = 0\" => p -> [p[1], p[2], -p[3]]]\nfor (name, f) in operations\n    @printf(\"%-20s %s\\n\", name, maps_onto_itself(f) ? \"symmetry\" : \"not a symmetry\")\nend\n180° about x         symmetry\n180° about y         symmetry\n180° about z         symmetry\n90° about z          not a symmetry\ninversion            not a symmetry\nreflection in z = 0  not a symmetry\n\nThe three rotations form the group D_2. A harmonic survives the average over D_2 only if each rotation leaves it unchanged. A rotation by 180° about z multiplies Y_lm by (-1)^m, and one about x multiplies it by (-1)^l+m for m ge 0 and by (-1)^l+m+1 for m  0. A drive with the symmetry of the ports can therefore contain only even m, with l even for the cosine harmonics and l odd for the sine harmonics. Every coefficient the rule forbids is zero within round-off:\n\nallowed(l, m) = iseven(m) && (m >= 0 ? iseven(l) : isodd(l))\nforbidden = [abs(full[mode_index(l, m)]) for l in 0:4 for m in -l:l if !allowed(l, m)]\n@printf(\"largest forbidden |c_lm|/c₀₀ %.1e\\n\", maximum(forbidden) / full[1])\nfor l in 1:4, m in -l:l\n    allowed(l, m) && @printf(\"c(%d,%2d)/c₀₀ = %+.5f\\n\", l, m, full[mode_index(l, m)] / full[1])\nend\nlargest forbidden |c_lm|/c₀₀ 1.6e-16\nc(2, 0)/c₀₀ = -0.06512\nc(2, 2)/c₀₀ = -0.00047\nc(3,-2)/c₀₀ = +0.00080\nc(4, 0)/c₀₀ = +0.00057\nc(4, 2)/c₀₀ = -0.00004\nc(4, 4)/c₀₀ = -0.00157\n\nThe cosine l = 2 m = 2 and sine l = 3 m = -2 harmonics are allowed by the symmetry of the ports, and the ports excite them weakly.","title":"The symmetry of the ports"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Which-bundles-can-drive-the-backlighter","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"Two lines of sight at the equator (theta = 90) suit radiography of the capsule, at phi = 78 and phi = 348. For each, backlighter_foil places a 2 mm square foil on the far side of the target, a distance d (the standoff) from its center, facing the detector across the capsule; the radiography beams strike its face toward the target, so the x rays they generate cross the capsule to the detector. The size and standoff of the foil here are generic engineering values: real backlighter dimensions are shot-specific. The radiograph looks through a pair of 3 mm windows in the hohlraum wall, one on each side of the capsule along the line of sight.\n\nThe scene around the foil is the hohlraum with its entrance-hole shields (sombrero = true), the dimpled annular sheets around each end that keep unconverted 1ω light off the hohlraum and its thermo-mechanical package. Each reaches 9.2 mm from the axis, 0.62 mm inside the entrance-hole plane (see Sombrero), so it stands across much of the space between the ports and a foil near the waist. The shields lie outside the cavity and do not change its radiation, and the cost of the windows to the drive is the subject of the Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall tutorial, so the scenario above uses the closed hohlraum without them; the scene is for the geometry only.\n\nshielded = Dict(φ => hohlraum(length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9,\n                              wall = gold(albedo = 0.85, conversion = 0.8), sombrero = true,\n                              windows = (WallWindow(; phi = φ, diameter = 3.0, open = true, name = :detector_window),\n                                         WallWindow(; phi = mod(φ + 180, 360), diameter = 3.0, open = true,\n                                                    name = :foil_window)))\n                for φ in (78.0, 348.0))\nscene(φ_los, d; hohl = shielded[φ_los]) =\n    Target([hohl.components..., backlighter_foil((90.0, φ_los); standoff = d)])\nscene (generic function with 1 method)\n\nA quad can drive the foil if it strikes the foil face at less than 70° from its normal, the LMF limit for laser capture on a target surface, and if its beam, aimed at the foil center, clears the hardware on the way: the hohlraum and capsule, and the shields. The clearance is judged as a shot review judges it, on the worst case: clearance grows the envelope of each beam (its contour at 1% of the peak, spread by the f-cone) by 50 µm of pointing error and 25 µm of target positioning, adds ±1 mm of focus error, and reports the smallest distance to each piece of hardware. That distance is exact; only the power on the hardware, not used here, depends on how finely the error set and the spot are sampled, so both are sampled coarsely. The standoff is a design choice: for each bundle, the table gives the worst-case clearance from the hohlraum and from the shields at 8 and 12 mm, and the smallest standoff at which both are positive, found by bisection to 0.05 mm.\n\nhohlraum_parts, shields = (:wall, :cap_top, :cap_bottom, :capsule), (:sombrero_top, :sombrero_bottom)\nfunction worst_clearance(φ_los, k, d)\n    n = port_vector(90.0, φ_los)                           # toward the detector\n    report = clearance(scene(φ_los, d), [remake(b; aim = Tuple(-d .* n)) for b in quads[bundles[k]]];\n                       targets = (:backlighter,), hardware = (hohlraum_parts..., shields...),\n                       peak_fraction = 0.01, wings = [], nangles = 4, nspot = 15)\n    worst(parts) = minimum(r -> r.clearance, filter(r -> r.component in parts, report.rows))\n    return (hohlraum = worst(hohlraum_parts), shield = worst(shields))\nend\nclears(φ_los, k, d) = (c = worst_clearance(φ_los, k, d); min(c.hohlraum, c.shield) > 0)\nfunction min_standoff(φ_los, k; lo = 8.0, hi = 30.0, tol = 0.05)\n    @assert !clears(φ_los, k, lo) && clears(φ_los, k, hi)\n    while hi - lo > tol\n        mid = (lo + hi) / 2\n        clears(φ_los, k, mid) ? (hi = mid) : (lo = mid)\n    end\n    return hi\nend\nmin_standoff (generic function with 1 method)\n\nA closed form bounds the bisection from below. The axis of a quad runs from the foil center -dhat n back toward its port hat p, crossing the shield plane of the same hemisphere, z = z_s (4.427 mm), a distance s = z_sp_z along hat p. It passes outside the rim, rho = 92 mm, once -dhat n + shat p_perp  rho, that is beyond the larger root\n\nd_mathrmaxis = shat ncdothat p_perp\n  + sqrtrho^2 - s^2left(hat p_perp^2 - (hat ncdothat p_perp)^2right)\n\ntaken here as the larger over the two quads of the bundle. The beam envelope and the error set add to it.\n\nz_shield = 10.1 / 2 - Sombrero().offset\nfunction axis_standoff(φ_los, k)\n    n = port_vector(90.0, φ_los)\n    return maximum(quads[bundles[k]]) do b\n        s = z_shield / abs(b.port[3])\n        p⊥ = [b.port[1], b.port[2], 0.0]\n        s * dot(n, p⊥) + sqrt(Sombrero().outer_radius^2 - s^2 * (dot(p⊥, p⊥) - dot(n, p⊥)^2))\n    end\nend\n\nbundles = nif_bundles(quads)\nstandoff = Dict{Tuple{Float64,String},Float64}()\nfor φ_los in (78.0, 348.0)\n    n = port_vector(90.0, φ_los)\n    candidates = [k for k in sort(collect(keys(bundles)))\n                  if all(b -> acosd(dot(b.port, n)) < 70, quads[bundles[k]])]\n    @printf(\"line of sight 90-%g: %d bundles within 70°\\n\", φ_los, length(candidates))\n    @printf(\"  %-6s %-16s %-13s %-34s %s\\n\", \"bundle\", \"cones\", \"incidence\",\n            \"worst case, hohlraum / shields (mm)\", \"standoff: axis, worst case (mm)\")\n    for k in candidates\n        cones = join(sort(unique(string(quads[i].group) for i in bundles[k])), \"+\")\n        incidence = [acosd(dot(b.port, n)) for b in quads[bundles[k]]]\n        c8, c12 = worst_clearance(φ_los, k, 8.0), worst_clearance(φ_los, k, 12.0)\n        standoff[(φ_los, k)] = min_standoff(φ_los, k)\n        @printf(\"  %-6s %-16s %4.1f°, %4.1f°  d = 8: %5.2f / %5.2f, 12: %5.2f / %5.2f   %5.2f, %5.2f\\n\",\n                k, cones, incidence..., c8.hohlraum, c8.shield, c12.hohlraum, c12.shield,\n                axis_standoff(φ_los, k), standoff[(φ_los, k)])\n        @assert standoff[(φ_los, k)] > axis_standoff(φ_los, k)\n    end\nend\nline of sight 90-78: 6 bundles within 70°\n  bundle cones            incidence     worst case, hohlraum / shields (mm) standoff: axis, worst case (mm)\n  11     outer50          53.1°, 47.1°  d = 8: -0.63 / -1.14, 12:  2.03 / -1.14   13.56, 15.39\n  12     outer44          47.5°, 45.7°  d = 8: -1.02 / -1.09, 12:  1.66 / -1.09   13.53, 15.18\n  16     inner30+outer50  40.4°, 62.3°  d = 8: -1.20 / -1.24, 12:  0.72 / -1.14   14.42, 16.34\n  41     inner30+outer50  69.6°, 43.1°  d = 8: -1.20 / -1.24, 12:  1.20 / -1.14   14.09, 15.95\n  43     outer44          51.9°, 57.3°  d = 8: -0.13 / -1.09, 12:  2.79 / -1.09   12.80, 14.45\n  46     outer50          61.4°, 69.2°  d = 8:  1.85 / -1.14, 12:  4.88 / -1.02   11.52, 13.24\nline of sight 90-348: 6 bundles within 70°\n  bundle cones            incidence     worst case, hohlraum / shields (mm) standoff: axis, worst case (mm)\n  11     outer50          61.6°, 69.4°  d = 8:  1.88 / -1.14, 12:  4.92 / -1.01   11.49, 13.24\n  13     inner30+outer50  69.4°, 43.1°  d = 8: -1.20 / -1.24, 12:  1.20 / -1.14   14.09, 15.95\n  14     outer44          51.9°, 57.3°  d = 8: -0.13 / -1.09, 12:  2.79 / -1.09   12.80, 14.45\n  21     inner30+outer50  40.4°, 61.9°  d = 8: -1.20 / -1.24, 12:  0.72 / -1.14   14.42, 16.34\n  22     outer44          47.5°, 45.7°  d = 8: -1.02 / -1.09, 12:  1.66 / -1.09   13.53, 15.18\n  23     outer50          53.2°, 47.3°  d = 8: -0.60 / -1.14, 12:  2.06 / -1.14   13.54, 15.39\n\nEach line of sight can be served by six bundles. Without the shields, a foil 12 mm from the center would let all six clear the hohlraum, and at 8 mm the hohlraum would leave only bundle 46 for 90-78 and bundle 11 for 90-348, whose quads pass above and below it at steep incidence. The shields rule out both designs: at 8 and 12 mm the beam of every candidate quad reaches a shield (a worst-case clearance near −1 mm is the whole envelope on it), the beam of the upper quad on the upper shield and that of the lower quad on the lower. Bundles 46 and 11 are still the first to clear, at a standoff of 13.2 mm; the other candidates need 14.5–16.3 mm. Each worst-case standoff lies 1.6–1.9 mm beyond the closed-form one at which the axes of the bundle graze the rim. The difference is the half-width of the envelope plus the errors, stretched by the shallow angle at which the beams cross the shield plane (40° for the 50° cone).\n\nThe beams are half of it; the x rays must also reach the detector, and the same hardware may stand between the foil and the detector. line_of_sight traces a bundle of rays from every sample point of the emitting face of the foil through every sample point of the projected disk of the capsule, on to the detector, and reports any that hardware stops. The traced paths include every x-ray path from the foil through the capsule toward a detector in the cone of the bundle, more paths than one imager accepts. Here the foil is placed at the standoff that bundle 11 needs, rounded up to a millimeter:\n\nd_los = ceil(standoff[(348.0, \"11\")])\nlos = line_of_sight(scene(348.0, d_los), (90.0, 348.0))\nLineOfSightReport: clear, 0 of 6561 rays blocked, foil covers 100.0% of the disk (radius 0.9 mm)\n\nEvery one of the 81 × 81 rays passes: the shields stand more than 3 mm above and below the equatorial line of sight, the windows open the wall, and the foil covers the capsule disk. Without the windows the hohlraum wall stops every ray:\n\nclosed_wall = hohlraum(length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9,\n                       wall = gold(albedo = 0.85, conversion = 0.8), sombrero = true)\nline_of_sight(scene(348.0, d_los; hohl = closed_wall), (90.0, 348.0))\nLineOfSightReport: obstructed, 6561 of 6561 rays blocked, foil covers 100.0% of the disk (radius 0.9 mm)\n  wall: 6561 rays\n\nand a foil closer in spreads the bundle more beyond the capsule. At 8 mm the rays from the corners of the foil through the rim of the capsule reach about 1.7 mm from the line of sight at the far wall, past the 1.5 mm radius of the detector-side window, which stops a twentieth of them:\n\ns_far = (8.0 + 2.875) / 8.0           # distance from the foil to the far wall, in standoffs\n@printf(\"bundle radius at the far wall %.2f mm\n\", s_far * 0.9 + (s_far - 1) * sqrt(2))\nline_of_sight(scene(348.0, 8.0), (90.0, 348.0))\nLineOfSightReport: obstructed, 356 of 6561 rays blocked, foil covers 100.0% of the disk (radius 0.9 mm)\n  wall: 356 rays","title":"Which bundles can drive the backlighter"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#The-radiograph-signal","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"A clear line of sight still needs enough photons. The freed quads heat the foil. A fraction of their energy, the conversion efficiency (CE), comes out in the He-α line of the foil material, and the imager records what passes its pinhole and filters. kshell_ce estimates the CE from published measurements on planar foils (kshell_ce_data): OMEGA foils driven by a single 1 ns pulse (Workman and Kyrala, 2001), NIF foils with a picket 3 ns ahead of a 2–5 ns drive (Barrios et al., 2013 and 2014), and OMEGA foils with and without a pre-pulse (Coppari et al., 2019). kshell_ce_model fits ln CE, weighting each point by its stated error, as a quadratic in ln I for each line, with an optimum irradiance that rises with photon energy and a peak that falls as (hnu)^-33 above about 6 keV and levels off below. The scatter of the data about the fit, a factor of 1.43, is the CE's uncertainty. The data do not separate the pulse length from the picket or the source.\n\nbacklighter_signal computes the chain in six steps:\n\nthe laser energy on each foil element, from a deposition calculation;\nthe CE at the mean irradiance of the foil;\nisotropic emission (the assumption the CE measurements were converted with);\na pinhole imager on the line of sight;\nthe attenuation of the filters (filter_transmission, from NIST mass attenuation coefficients);\nthe photons per detector pixel, with the CE's uncertainty.\n\nThe capsule itself does not attenuate in this calculation: the result is the open-field level that its transmission multiplies. Here bundle 11's two quads, 16 TW for 2 ns with a picket, are aimed at the center of a germanium foil (He-α at 10.3 keV) at the 14 mm standoff. The imager is generic: a 20 µm pinhole 200 mm from the capsule, the detector 2 m away (magnification 9), behind 250 µm of beryllium and 50 µm of Kapton.\n\nfoil_center = Tuple(-d_los .* port_vector(90.0, 348.0))\nradiography = [remake(b; aim = foil_center) for b in quads[bundles[\"11\"]]]\nbl_scene = scene(348.0, d_los)\nbl_deposition = raytrace_deposition(bl_scene, radiography; nspot = 61)\nimager = (; distance = 2000.0, pinhole = 0.02, pinhole_distance = 200.0, pixel = 0.05,\n          filters = (:Be => 0.25, :kapton => 0.05))\nsignal = backlighter_signal(bl_scene, bl_deposition, (90.0, 348.0); line = :Ge, pulse = 2.0,\n                            prepulse = true, imager...)\nBacklighterSignal (pinhole): 10.28 keV line, 2.73e+04 J of laser at 5.1e+14 W/cm²\n  CE 0.538% (×1.47 at 1σ), extrapolated: 147 J into 4π, 11.7 J/sr toward the detector\n  filter transmission 0.95: 1.82e+05 photons/mm² (1σ 1.24e+05–2.68e+05), 455 per 0.05 mm pixel\n\nThe phase-plate spots at best focus spread the light of the quads over most of the foil, so the mean irradiance, about 5times10^14 W/cm², lies just below the lowest measured NIF point, which flags the result as extrapolated. It is also well below the fitted optimum for germanium, about 2times10^15 W/cm². About 450 photons per 50 µm pixel (6 µm at the capsule), averaged over the capsule image, is uncertain by a factor of 1.5 either way. Across the image the level varies a hundredfold (signal.photons_range), because the radiance of the foil follows the spots of the quads: the rim of the capsule is backlit by the weakly irradiated edges of the foil. All the emitting area of the foil faces the detector, and through the open windows every element sees it, so the power per steradian toward the detector is the isotropic E_x4pi. Lower-Z lines convert better at this irradiance; the transmission of the capsule at each energy determines the choice between them:\n\n@assert signal.facing ≈ 1 && signal.per_sr ≈ signal.xray_energy / 4π\nprintln(\"line   hν (keV)  CE (%, 1σ range)      filters  photons per pixel (1σ range)\")\nfor line in (:Cu, :Zn, :Ge, :Zr)\n    s = backlighter_signal(bl_scene, bl_deposition, (90.0, 348.0); line, pulse = 2.0,\n                           prepulse = true, imager...)\n    @printf(\"%-4s   %6.2f   %5.2f (%4.2f–%4.2f)%s   %5.3f    %6.0f (%.0f–%.0f)\\n\", line, s.line,\n            100s.ce, 100s.ce * exp(-s.sigma), 100s.ce * exp(s.sigma), s.extrapolated ? \"*\" : \" \",\n            s.transmission, s.photons_pixel, s.photons_lo * 0.05^2, s.photons_hi * 0.05^2)\nend\nline   hν (keV)  CE (%, 1σ range)      filters  photons per pixel (1σ range)\nCu       8.39    1.33 (0.92–1.93)*   0.916      1332 (919–1930)\nZn       9.00    0.99 (0.68–1.43)*   0.930       933 (642–1356)\nGe      10.28    0.54 (0.37–0.79)*   0.950       455 (310–669)\nZr      16.30    0.05 (0.03–0.08)*   0.981        26 (15–43)\n\n(A * marks a value outside the measured range of its class, here in irradiance.)","title":"The radiograph signal"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#What-each-bundle-costs","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"Without a laser–plasma model the capsule flux is linear in the beam powers. influence_matrix returns the harmonic coefficients produced by each quad alone at 1 TW, so the coefficients for any assignment of powers P are M P. nif_bundles groups the quads into bundles.\n\nM = influence_matrix(scenario, quads; lmax = 4)\nP_full = [b.power for b in quads]\nwithout(k) = setdiff(eachindex(quads), bundles[k])\nwithout (generic function with 1 method)\n\nThe rotations of D_2 carry each bundle onto another, so the 24 bundles fall into sets of equivalent bundles, and freeing any member of a set costs the same. Each quad maps under a rotation onto a quad of the image bundle:\n\nimage(i, f) = findfirst(q -> norm(f(quads[i].port) - q.port) < 1e-6, quads)\nrotations = last.(operations[1:3])\nimage_bundle(k, f) = only(unique(nif_bundle(quads[image(i, f)]) for i in bundles[k]))\norbits = unique(sort(unique([k; [image_bundle(k, f) for f in rotations]])) for k in keys(bundles))\n\ncost(k) = M[:, without(k)] * P_full[without(k)]\nsort!(orbits; by = o -> mode_amplitude(cost(o[1]), 1))\n@printf(\"%-13s %-15s %7s %7s %7s %7s %7s\\n\", \"bundles\", \"cones\", \"ε₁ (%)\", \"ε₂ (%)\",\n        \"ε₃ (%)\", \"ε₄ (%)\", \"drive\")\nfor o in orbits\n    cones = join(sort(unique(string(quads[i].group) for i in bundles[o[1]])), \"+\")\n    ε = [[mode_amplitude(cost(k), l) for l in 1:4] for k in o]\n    @assert all(e -> isapprox(e, ε[1]; atol = 1e-9), ε)\n    @printf(\"%-13s %-15s %7.3f %7.3f %7.3f %7.3f %7.4f\\n\", join(o, \" \"), cones,\n            (100 .* ε[1])..., cost(o[1])[1] / full[1])\nend\nbundles       cones            ε₁ (%)  ε₂ (%)  ε₃ (%)  ε₄ (%)   drive\n11 23 34 46   outer50           1.091  15.413   0.799   0.552  0.9625\n14 22 35 45   outer44           1.485  15.236   0.885   0.589  0.9606\n12 25 32 43   outer44           1.485  15.226   0.822   0.585  0.9606\n15 24 33 42   inner23           2.998  13.508   1.035   0.779  0.9533\n13 21 36 44   inner30+outer50   3.094  14.219   1.266   0.386  0.9565\n16 26 31 41   inner30+outer50   3.118  14.231   1.204   0.396  0.9566\n\nThe two quads of a bundle need not share a cone. The bundles whose quads both sit in the 50° cone cost least, in mode 1 and in drive, and those of the 44.5° cone next. A bundle with an inner quad costs three times as much mode 1: the inner quads deposit their power around the waist of the hohlraum, closest to the capsule. Among the bundles that can drive a backlighter, the 50° bundles are therefore the ones to free: 11 or 23 for the line of sight at 90-348, 46 or 11 for 90-78. Of these, 11 (for 90-348) and 46 (for 90-78) are also the ones the shields let through closest in, at 13.2 mm; the others need 15.4 mm. This tutorial frees bundle 11, the pair Q11T and Q11B, for the line of sight at 90-348; bundle 46 is its image under a rotation of D_2 and costs the same.\n\nfreed = \"11\"\nkeep = without(freed)\nremaining = quads[keep]\n46-element Vector{Beam{Float64}}:\n Beam(Q15T, θ=23.50°, φ=78.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.2126, -1.069, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q42T, θ=23.50°, φ=168.75°, 8 TW, aim=(1.069, -0.2126, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q33T, θ=23.50°, φ=258.75°, 8 TW, aim=(0.2126, 1.069, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q24T, θ=23.50°, φ=348.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-1.069, 0.2126, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q13T, θ=30.58°, φ=34.33°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.4955, -0.3384, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q41T, θ=30.00°, φ=123.75°, 8 TW, aim=(0.3333, -0.4989, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q36T, θ=30.58°, φ=214.33°, 8 TW, aim=(0.4955, 0.3384, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q26T, θ=30.00°, φ=303.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.3333, 0.4989, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q14T, θ=44.50°, φ=16.29°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, -0, 5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q12T, θ=44.50°, φ=62.46°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, -0, 5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n ⋮\n Beam(Q22B, θ=135.50°, φ=343.71°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, 0, -5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q16B, θ=150.00°, φ=56.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.3333, -0.4989, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q44B, θ=149.42°, φ=145.67°, 8 TW, aim=(0.4955, -0.3384, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q31B, θ=150.00°, φ=236.25°, 8 TW, aim=(0.3333, 0.4989, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q21B, θ=149.42°, φ=325.67°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.4955, 0.3384, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q15B, θ=156.50°, φ=11.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-1.069, -0.2126, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q42B, θ=156.50°, φ=101.25°, 8 TW, aim=(0.2126, -1.069, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q33B, θ=156.50°, φ=191.25°, 8 TW, aim=(1.069, 0.2126, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q24B, θ=156.50°, φ=281.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.2126, 1.069, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)","title":"What each bundle costs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Freeing-bundle-11","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"A subset of the scenario contains the 46 remaining quads and reuses everything except the deposition operator, which it restricts to their ports. The same coefficients follow from the full scenario with the quads of the bundle at zero power, and from the influence matrix:\n\nc_free = flux_ylm(evaluate(subset(scenario, keep), remaining); lmax = 4)\nzeroed = [i in bundles[freed] ? remake(b; power = 0.0) : b for (i, b) in enumerate(quads)]\nc_zeroed = flux_ylm(evaluate(scenario, zeroed); lmax = 4)\nc_matrix = M[:, keep] * P_full[keep]\n@printf(\"subset vs zero power %.1e   subset vs influence matrix %.1e\\n\",\n        norm(c_free - c_zeroed) / norm(c_free), norm(c_free - c_matrix) / norm(c_free))\nshow_modes(\"bundle 11 freed\", c_free)\nsubset vs zero power 0.0e+00   subset vs influence matrix 8.5e-16\nbundle 11 freed          ε₁  1.091%  ε₂ 15.413%  ε₃  0.799%  ε₄  0.552%  drive 0.9625\n\nThe drive falls by 4% and a mode-1 asymmetry of 1.1% appears, about 110 km/s of hot-spot velocity. Its direction lies between the azimuths of the freed quads' ports:\n\ndipole = normalize([c_free[mode_index(1, 1)], c_free[mode_index(1, -1)], c_free[mode_index(1, 0)]])\n@printf(\"mode-1 direction θ = %.1f°, φ = %.1f°\\n\", acosd(dipole[3]),\n        mod(atand(dipole[2], dipole[1]), 360))\nfor i in bundles[freed]\n    @printf(\"%s port θ = %.2f°, φ = %.2f°\\n\", quads[i].name, port_angles(quads[i])...)\nend\nmode-1 direction θ = 90.0°, φ = 45.1°\nQ11T port θ = 50.00°, φ = 39.67°\nQ11B port θ = 130.00°, φ = 50.62°\n\nAn outer quad crosses the hohlraum axis near its entrance hole and heats the wall on the far side, so without it the side of the capsule facing away from its port is colder.\n\nThe maps below show the flux on the capsule as seen from outside, in an equal-area (Hammer) projection, so that equal areas of the map are equal solid angles of the capsule. Polar angle θ is marked at the left edge and azimuth φ along the equator, which runs across the middle. The flux is computed per element, on the 1,620 triangles of the geodesic capsule mesh; the maps interpolate it linearly from the element values averaged onto their corners. The left map shows the flux relative to the mean flux, Hlangle Hrangle - 1, where langle Hrangle = c_00sqrt4pi. The right map subtracts the same quantity for all 48 quads, which isolates what freeing the bundle changed. The figure helpers sphere_axis, sphere_map and mark_directions! come from docs/literate/_plotting.jl.\n\ncapsule = elements(target, :capsule)\ndeviation(H, c) = 100 .* (H ./ (c[1] / sqrt(4π)) .- 1)       # percent of ⟨H⟩\nH_full = evaluate(scenario, quads).flux\nH_free = evaluate(subset(scenario, keep), remaining).flux\nport_marker = (marker = :xcross, markersize = 12, color = :black)\nlos_marker = (marker = :circle, markersize = 11, color = :transparent, strokecolor = :black,\n              strokewidth = 1.5)\nfunction capsule_panel(pos, field, range; title = \"\")\n    ax = sphere_axis(pos; title)\n    hm = sphere_heatmap!(ax, sphere_map(target, field, :capsule); colormap = :balance,\n                         colorrange = (-range, range))\n    mark_directions!(ax, [port_angles(quads[i]) for i in bundles[freed]]; port_marker...)\n    mark_directions!(ax, [(90, 348)]; los_marker...)\n    return hm\nend\ncapsule_legend(pos) = Legend(pos, [MarkerElement(; port_marker...), MarkerElement(; los_marker...)],\n                             [\"ports of the freed quads\", \"line of sight 90-348\"];\n                             orientation = :horizontal, framevisible = false)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 330))\nhm = capsule_panel(fig[1, 1], deviation(H_free, c_free), 15; title = \"bundle 11 freed\")\nColorbar(fig[2, 1], hm; vertical = false, flipaxis = false, width = Relative(0.7),\n         label = \"H/⟨H⟩ − 1 (%)\")\nhm = capsule_panel(fig[1, 2], deviation(H_free, c_free) - deviation(H_full, full), 2;\n                   title = \"change from 48 quads\")\nColorbar(fig[2, 2], hm; vertical = false, flipaxis = false, width = Relative(0.7),\n         label = \"difference in H/⟨H⟩ − 1 (%)\")\ncapsule_legend(fig[3, 1:2])\nrowgap!(fig.layout, 6)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe untuned l = 2 asymmetry, a hot waist and cold poles, dominates the flux and barely changes. What freeing the bundle changed is mostly mode 1: the side facing the freed ports, around phi = 45, is warmer relative to the mean by about 1%, and the far side, where the freed quads deposited their power, colder by up to 2%.","title":"Freeing bundle 11"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Remedy-1:-free-the-partner-as-well","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The rotation by 180° about z carries bundle 11 onto bundle 34. Freeing both leaves ports with that symmetry, which removes every harmonic of odd m, among them the two transverse components of mode 1. The axial component c_10 vanishes for another reason: the hohlraum is axisymmetric, so the contribution of a quad to c_10 depends only on its cone and hemisphere, and each freed bundle takes one quad from each hemisphere of the same cone.\n\npartner = image_bundle(freed, rotations[3])\nkeep_pair = setdiff(keep, bundles[partner])\nc_pair = M[:, keep_pair] * P_full[keep_pair]\nshow_modes(\"bundles 11 and $partner freed\", c_pair)\nbundles 11 and 34 freed  ε₁  0.000%  ε₂ 16.360%  ε₃  0.338%  ε₄  0.626%  drive 0.9249\n\nMode 1 is gone, but so is another 4% of the drive, which an ignition shot cannot spare.","title":"Remedy 1: free the partner as well"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Remedy-2:-rebalance-the-powers","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"Keeping the other 46 quads at their pointing, find the smallest change delta P of their powers that restores the full-set coefficients of every mode up to degree L. With M_L the rows of M through degree L, this is the minimum-norm solution\n\ndelta P = M_L^+left(c^mathrmfull_L - M_L Pright)\n\nwhere M_L^+ is the pseudoinverse:\n\nMk = M[:, keep]\nP_keep = P_full[keep]\nrebalanced = Dict{Int,Vector{Float64}}()\nfor L in 1:3\n    rows = 1:(L+1)^2\n    rebalanced[L] = P_keep + pinv(Mk[rows, :]) * (full[rows] - Mk[rows, :] * P_keep)\n    show_modes(\"rebalanced through l = $L\", Mk * rebalanced[L])\n    @printf(\"%24s quad powers %.2f – %.2f TW\\n\", \"\", extrema(rebalanced[L])...)\nend\nrebalanced through l = 1 ε₁  0.000%  ε₂ 15.530%  ε₃  0.931%  ε₄  0.561%  drive 1.0000\n                         quad powers 7.86 – 8.88 TW\nrebalanced through l = 2 ε₁  0.000%  ε₂ 14.562%  ε₃  0.965%  ε₄  0.489%  drive 1.0000\n                         quad powers 7.37 – 8.81 TW\nrebalanced through l = 3 ε₁  0.000%  ε₂ 14.562%  ε₃  0.211%  ε₄  0.491%  drive 1.0000\n                         quad powers 7.06 – 9.91 TW\n\nRestoring the drive and mode 1 needs some quads 11% above the nominal 8 TW, and restoring l = 3 as well needs 24%. An ignition pulse runs the laser close to its energy and peak-power limits, so this headroom is rarely available. The quads near the freed bundle carry the extra power. Each hemisphere is shown separately, with the nominal 8 TW as a gray line and the azimuth of its freed quad dashed; the three solutions are offset by ±1.5° in azimuth so that they do not hide one another:\n\nφk = [port_angles(b)[2] for b in remaining]\nupper = [b.port[3] > 0 for b in remaining]\nfunction rebalance_panel(pos, hemisphere, sel; bottom)\n    ax = Axis(pos; ylabel = \"power (TW)\", title = \"quads in the $hemisphere hemisphere\",\n              xticks = 0:45:360, limits = ((0, 360), (6.8, 10.3)),\n              xlabel = \"port azimuth φ (degrees)\", xlabelvisible = bottom,\n              xticklabelsvisible = bottom)\n    hlines!(ax, [8.0]; color = :gray, linewidth = 1)\n    for i in bundles[freed]\n        b = quads[i]\n        (b.port[3] > 0) == (hemisphere == \"upper\") || continue\n        vlines!(ax, [port_angles(b)[2]]; color = :gray, linestyle = :dash)\n        text!(ax, port_angles(b)[2] + 2, 10.2; text = \"$(b.name) freed\", align = (:left, :top),\n              fontsize = 12, color = :gray30)\n    end\n    for (L, marker, dx) in ((1, :circle, -1.5), (2, :utriangle, 0.0), (3, :rect, 1.5))\n        scatter!(ax, φk[sel] .+ dx, rebalanced[L][sel]; marker, markersize = 9,\n                 label = \"restored through l = $L\")\n    end\n    return ax\nend\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 470))\nrebalance_panel(fig[1, 1], \"upper\", upper; bottom = false)\nax = rebalance_panel(fig[2, 1], \"lower\", .!upper; bottom = true)\nLegend(fig[3, 1], ax; orientation = :horizontal, framevisible = false)\nrowgap!(fig.layout, 8)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe same answer comes from the optimizer. optimize_pointing minimizes an objective of an Evaluation over the parameters of the scenario, here the 46 quad powers, with gradients from forward-mode automatic differentiation. With the misfit of the l le 2 coefficients as the objective and the bounds out of reach, L-BFGS-B starts from P and moves only within the span of the gradients, which is the row space of M_2; it therefore converges to the minimum-norm solution:\n\npower_only = subset(scenario, keep; parameterization = GroupPointing(remaining; by = :quad,\n                                                                     fields = (:power,)))\nmisfit(ev) = sum(abs2, flux_ylm(ev; lmax = 2) .- full[1:9]) / full[1]^2\nP0 = initial_parameters(power_only.param)\nfit = optimize_pointing(power_only, misfit; x0 = P0, lower = fill(0.0, length(P0)),\n                        upper = fill(20.0, length(P0)), g_tol = 1e-14)\n@printf(\"misfit %.1e → %.1e,  max |P_optimizer − P_pseudoinverse| %.1e TW\\n\",\n        fit.initial_value, fit.value, maximum(abs.(fit.x - rebalanced[2])))\nmisfit 1.5e-03 → 2.6e-23,  max |P_optimizer − P_pseudoinverse| 6.0e-10 TW","title":"Remedy 2: rebalance the powers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Remedy-3:-re-point","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"Re-aiming quads moves drive around the hohlraum at no cost in power. Let each of the remaining quads move its aim by up to 0.3 mm along the hohlraum axis and sideways around it, and minimize mode 1 strongly and modes 2–4 weakly while holding the drive at its value after the bundle is freed. asymmetry_objective builds that objective and adds penalties for light clipped by the entrance holes and light striking the capsule. Each optimization here stops after a fixed number of L-BFGS-B iterations, 40 and then 20, short of full convergence but well past the point where mode 1 is gone.\n\nrepoint = subset(scenario, keep; parameterization = GroupPointing(remaining; by = :quad,\n                                                                  fields = (:phi, :z)))\nx0 = initial_parameters(repoint.param)\nlower, upper = bounds(repoint.param, x0; phi = 0.3, z = 0.3)\ngoals = (modes = (1 => 200.0, 2 => 1.0, 3 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0), drive = c_free[1] / sqrt(4π),\n         drive_weight = 10.0)\nfirst_try = optimize_pointing(repoint, asymmetry_objective(; goals...); x0, lower, upper,\n                              iterations = 40)\nshow_modes(\"bundle 11 freed\", c_free)\nshow_modes(\"re-pointed\", flux_ylm(evaluate(repoint, first_try.x); lmax = 4))\n@printf(\"clipped %.1e of the delivered power\\n\", clipped_fraction(evaluate(repoint, first_try.x)))\nbundle 11 freed          ε₁  1.091%  ε₂ 15.413%  ε₃  0.799%  ε₄  0.552%  drive 0.9625\nre-pointed               ε₁  0.001%  ε₂ 13.817%  ε₃  0.752%  ε₄  0.546%  drive 0.9597\nclipped 1.2e-07 of the delivered power\n\nMode 1 is gone and nothing is clipped. But the clipping penalty sees only the nominal beams, and a shot review judges a pointing on its worst case. clearance grows the envelope of each beam (here its contour at 1% of the peak, the level of LMF's target-configuration rules, spread by the f-cone) by 50 µm of pointing error and 25 µm of target positioning, adds ±1 mm of focus error, and reports the smallest distance to the end caps, together with the worst-case power and intensity the caps receive. The scattered-light wings, which reach the entrance-hole lip in any design, are left out:\n\nfunction cap_review(beams)\n    report = clearance(target, beams; targets = (:wall, :capsule),\n                       hardware = (:cap_top, :cap_bottom), peak_fraction = 0.01, wings = [],\n                       nangles = 8)\n    return (nominal = minimum(r -> r.nominal, report.rows),\n            worst = minimum(r -> r.clearance, report.rows),\n            power = maximum(r -> r.intercepted, report.rows),\n            fail = count(r -> r.status === :fail, report.rows))\nend\nreview_line(label, r) = @printf(\"%-22s nominal %6.3f mm   worst case %6.3f mm   cap power ≤ %.1e   %d fail\\n\",\n                                label, r.nominal, r.worst, r.power, r.fail)\nreview_48 = cap_review(quads)\nreview_line(\"48 quads\", review_48)\nreview_line(\"re-pointed\", cap_review(first_try.beams))\n48 quads               nominal  0.204 mm   worst case  0.066 mm   cap power ≤ 6.0e-08   0 fail\nre-pointed             nominal -0.010 mm   worst case -0.149 mm   cap power ≤ 5.7e-04   12 fail\n\nThe original pointing clears the caps by 66 µm in the worst case. The re-pointed quads have used up that margin: in the worst case their 1% contours overlap the entrance-hole lips, several of them fail, and up to several times 10⁻⁴ of the power of a beam is deposited on a cap.\n\nThe optimizer has to see the same worst case. An ApertureKeepOut computes the worst-case clearance of each beam from the rim of its entrance hole with the same envelope and error set, smoothly enough to differentiate, and adds a penalty when it falls below a minimum, here 10 µm. On the original pointing it agrees with clearance:\n\nkeepout = ApertureKeepOut(target; peak_fraction = 0.01, minimum = 0.01, weight = 1e3)\nfrom_keepout = aperture_clearance(keepout, quads)\n@printf(\"worst-case clearance from the keep-out %.3f mm, from clearance %.3f mm\\n\",\n        minimum(from_keepout), review_48.worst)\n\nsecond_try = optimize_pointing(repoint, asymmetry_objective(; goals..., keepout); x0,\n                               lower, upper, iterations = 20)\nrepointed = evaluate(repoint, second_try.x)\nc_repointed = flux_ylm(repointed; lmax = 4)\nshow_modes(\"re-pointed, keep-out\", c_repointed)\nreview_line(\"re-pointed, keep-out\", cap_review(second_try.beams))\nworst-case clearance from the keep-out 0.066 mm, from clearance 0.066 mm\nre-pointed, keep-out     ε₁  0.022%  ε₂ 14.433%  ε₃  1.055%  ε₄  0.613%  drive 0.9598\nre-pointed, keep-out   nominal  0.146 mm   worst case  0.008 mm   cap power ≤ 1.6e-06   0 fail\n\nRe-pointing within the clearance still removes nearly all of mode 1, to a few kilometers per second of hot-spot velocity, and the drive stays within 0.3% of its value after the bundle is freed. The worst-case clearance comes out at 8 µm, short of the 10 µm minimum: the penalty is soft, and the distance from the keep-out differs from that of the clearance check by a few micrometers. The maps below show the flux on the capsule before and after re-pointing, on the same color scales as above:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 470))\nfor (j, (title, H, c)) in enumerate(((\"bundle 11 freed\", H_free, c_free),\n                                     (\"re-pointed within clearance\", repointed.flux, c_repointed)))\n    capsule_panel(fig[1, j], deviation(H, c), 15; title)\n    capsule_panel(fig[2, j], deviation(H, c) - deviation(H_full, full), 2)\nend\nColorbar(fig[1, 3]; colormap = :balance, colorrange = (-15, 15), height = Relative(0.8),\n         label = \"H/⟨H⟩ − 1 (%)\")\nColorbar(fig[2, 3]; colormap = :balance, colorrange = (-2, 2), height = Relative(0.8),\n         label = \"change from 48 quads (%)\")\ncapsule_legend(fig[3, 1:2])\nrowgap!(fig.layout, 6)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nRe-pointing removes the warm side around the freed ports. What remains of the change from the 48-quad drive is in the higher modes, which the objective weighted two hundred times less than mode 1: the re-pointed drive trades the dipole for a stronger l = 3, while l = 2 is as it was with all 48 quads.","title":"Remedy 3: re-point"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Independent-check","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The optimizer worked through the deposition operator and the linear flux response. raytrace_deposition recomputes the deposition of the re-pointed quads by tracing rays from each spot through its f-cone, and solve recomputes the radiosity directly:\n\ntraced = raytrace_deposition(target, second_try.beams; nspot = 61)\nH = solve(scenario.model, traced).H[capsule]\nshow_modes(\"re-pointed, ray-traced\", ylm_weights(target, capsule; lmax = 4) * H)\nre-pointed, ray-traced   ε₁  0.031%  ε₂ 14.440%  ε₃  1.050%  ε₄  0.612%  drive 0.9596","title":"Independent check"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#Re-pointing-within-limits","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The objective above weighs mode 1 against the other modes and the drive by hand-set weights. A design review states limits instead: every mode within a tolerance of the 48-quad shape, the laser irradiance on the wall no higher than under the 48-quad pointing, and within those limits as much drive as possible.\n\nThe first limit is a gate per degree on the departure from the 48-quad shape, delta_l = sqrt(2l+1)sum_m (c_lmc_00 - c_lmc_00)^2 (mode_departure), for an axisymmetric difference the change in the Legendre ratio a_la_0 (P_2P_0 at l = 2). mode_uncertainty gives the rms amplitude that random pointing errors of 50 µm per quad leave in each degree, and mode_gate turns it into the gate sqrt2l+1 times that rms. That gate is a lower bound: it is what pointing error alone produces, while the delivery of the laser and the hohlraum plasma move the shape from shot to shot by more, in ways a view-factor model does not quantify. The usual gate is a tolerance per degree in physical units, a choice of the design; the floor is used here as the strictest case, and the research notes (\"Mode gates in physical units\") measure what relaxed gates buy on this case in aim shift and drive. The second limit is a ceiling on the laser irradiance over the wall elements (irradiance_ceiling): a hot spot on the gold drives the wall plasma inward. The peak under the 48-quad pointing serves as the limit, an engineering choice; no published criterion sets it. The peak over the elements (peak_irradiance) has a kink wherever the hottest element changes, so the optimizer works on a smooth stand-in, the area-weighted 64-norm of the irradiance (irradiance_norm), scaled to equal the peak at the reference. Once a run has converged on the stand-in, the ceiling compares the peak itself with the limit and lowers the working limit of the stand-in while the peak exceeds it (exact = true, its default).\n\nref48 = evaluate(scenario, quads)\ngates = mode_gate(full, mode_uncertainty(scenario, quads; pointing = 0.05, lmax = 4))\nceiling = irradiance_ceiling(ref48, :wall)\ncheck_constraints(evaluate(repoint, x0), (ceiling, gates))\n    constraint              value        limit  violation multiplier  status\n    irradiance              8.273 ≤      8.273   0.00e+00          0  active\n    mode_gate[l = 1]     0.010908 ≤  0.0034556   2.16e+00          0  violated\n    mode_gate[l = 2]      0.01075 ≤  0.0039498   1.72e+00          0  violated\n    mode_gate[l = 3]    0.0076876 ≤  0.0031252   1.46e+00          0  violated\n    mode_gate[l = 4]     0.001208 ≤ 0.00094983   2.72e-01          0  violated\n\nFreeing the bundle puts every degree outside its gate, l = 1 by a factor of three, while the hottest wall element, lit by quads that stay, remains at the 48-quad peak: the ceiling is active from the start.\n\noptimize_pointing takes the limits as constraints. It handles them by an augmented Lagrangian: a sequence of L-BFGS-B runs on the objective plus multiplier-weighted terms, each run started where the last ended, with the multipliers updated in between until every limit is met. The objective is the drive (drive_objective), with the usual penalties on light clipped by the entrance holes.\n\nmost_drive = drive_objective(; drive = mean_flux(ref48))\nlimited = optimize_pointing(repoint, most_drive; x0, lower, upper, constraints = (ceiling, gates),\n                            iterations = 1000, evaluations = 3000)\nPointing optimization converged: objective -0.9625 → -0.9625\n  526 iterations, 1541 evaluations, 92 parameters; at a bound (widen the box if the objective should set them):\n    Q12T.phi = 0.3  (upper bound; box -0.3 to 0.3)  d objective/d bound -0.000297\n  phase 1: a design within the limits in 15 evaluations\n  9 outer iterations: constraints met, parameters at a bound\n  stationarity: largest |∂L/∂x| inside the box 7.88e-04 (objective units per parameter unit)\n    constraint              value        limit  violation multiplier  status\n    irradiance              8.277 ≤      8.273   4.83e-04     0.0253  active\n    mode_gate[l = 1]    0.0030904 ≤  0.0034556  -1.06e-01          0  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 2]    0.0039472 ≤  0.0039498  -6.54e-04    0.00244  active\n    mode_gate[l = 3]    0.0030812 ≤  0.0031252  -1.41e-02          0  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 4]   0.00094975 ≤ 0.00094983  -7.87e-05   0.000942  active\n\nOne call from the start meets every gate and the ceiling. The start violates the gates, so the run first searches for a design within the limits by minimizing only the violations (phase 1, phase1 on by default) and starts its main run there. The table lists the value of each limit, its normalized excess and its multiplier; the outcome records that the run converged with every constraint met inside the box. The multipliers are those that best satisfy stationarity at the design, and the stationarity line gives the residual they leave. Here the residual is not small against the drive gradient: the drive is flat along the limits and the run ends on its value tolerance, so the multipliers rank the cost of the limits only roughly.\n\nThe same limits as penalty terms are what a hand-written objective would add: 100 times the squared excess of each degree over its gate and of the irradiance over the ceiling, with a small reward, 0.01 times the squared ratio of each degree to its gate, for symmetry inside the gates.\n\nfunction penalized(ev)\n    J = most_drive(ev)\n    ratio = mode_departure(ev, full) ./ gates.limit\n    J += sum(100 * max(0, r^2 - 1)^2 + 0.01 * r^2 for r in ratio)\n    excess = ceiling.f(ev) / only(ceiling.limit) - 1\n    return J + 100 * max(0, excess)^2\nend\nby_penalty = optimize_pointing(repoint, penalized; x0, lower, upper, iterations = 1000, evaluations = 3000)\nfor (label, r) in ((\"within limits\", limited), (\"penalty terms\", by_penalty))\n    ev = evaluate(repoint, r.x)\n    @printf(\"%-14s drive %.4f  δ/gate %s  peak/limit %.4f  %4d evaluations\\n\", label,\n            flux_ylm(ev; lmax = 0)[1] / full[1],\n            join((@sprintf(\"%.3f\", d) for d in mode_departure(ev, full) ./ gates.limit), \" \"),\n            peak_irradiance(ev, :wall) / peak_irradiance(ref48, :wall), r.evaluations)\nend\n@assert limited.outcome in (:interior, :box) && all(r -> r.status !== :violated, limited.constraints)\n@assert peak_irradiance(evaluate(repoint, limited.x), :wall) <= only(ceiling.limit) * (1 + 1e-3)\nwithin limits  drive 0.9625  δ/gate 0.894 0.999 0.986 1.000  peak/limit 1.0005  1541 evaluations\npenalty terms  drive 0.9592  δ/gate 0.002 0.120 0.005 0.066  peak/limit 1.0093  1082 evaluations\n\nBoth designs meet the gates. The penalty terms keep rewarding symmetry inside the gates, beyond what the limits require, and pay for it with drive: modes 1 and 3 end far inside their gates and three tenths of a percent of the drive is gone. With the limits as constraints the gates end at or near their limits, with more drive left over. The drive is nearly flat along the limits, so where the run ends in that family of designs depends on its path, down to rounding: runs started 10⁻¹² to 10⁻⁹ mm from this one end with other gates active, after 840 to 1,500 evaluations, at 0.9616 to 0.9625 of the 48-quad drive (research notes, \"The dropped-bundle tutorial's long runs under rounding (2026-11-20)\"). The printed departures of this run are one member of the family; its drive is determined to about 0.1%. The penalty design is determined: the same perturbed starts reproduce its drive and departures to the printed digits and its peak to 0.1%, and only its evaluation count varies. Staging the penalties by hand (gates loosened four-, two- and 1.4-fold, then the final ones) reaches the penalty design again, in more evaluations (research notes, \"Constrained re-pointing\").\n\nThe constrained run ends with the peak over the elements at the ceiling or under it (from 0.8% under to 0.06% over, within the run tolerance of 0.1%). The penalty form bounds the 64-norm, a mean over the hot spots, and leaves the hottest element a percent over the ceiling. Without the ceiling the same gates are met with the wall peak 27–38% above the peak under the 48-quad pointing (research notes), because the re-aimed quads are stacked on the same spots.","title":"Re-pointing within limits"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#A-re-pointing-study,-end-to-end","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The design within limits meets the shape and wall-irradiance limits, but no limit in its run covered the entrance holes. The clearance check of the end caps shows what that run left unconstrained:\n\nreview_line(\"within limits\", cap_review(limited.beams))\nwithin limits          nominal  0.098 mm   worst case -0.040 mm   cap power ≤ 5.4e-05   2 fail\n\nIn the worst case the 1% contours of the quads end within a few tens of micrometers of the caps, on either side: no limit in the run held them, and over the perturbed starts above, the worst case ranges from 38 µm into a cap to 4 µm clear of it, with up to two beams failing the check. A study repeats the run with every limit a design review checks, on the parameters the design may change, in two stages: the quads first, then each of their beams.","title":"A re-pointing study, end to end"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#The-parameters","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"Here the inner quads keep their pointing (their spots set the drive at the waist and pass close to the capsule), the outer quads move by up to 0.3 mm as before, and the power of each cone may fall by up to 20% but not rise: an ignition pulse runs the laser at its limit. PowerScales adds a power scale per cone after the pointing parameters, and HeldParameters fixes at their values the parameters that a predicate on their names selects, here those of the inner quads. With relative = true the parameters of each quad are shifts from its present aim. remake puts the composed parameterization in the scenario and reuses its deposition operator, since the beams enter through the same ports.\n\ninner_quads = Set(b.quad for b in remaining if b.group in (:inner23, :inner30))\nby_quad = GroupPointing(remaining; by = :quad, fields = (:phi, :z), relative = true)\nstudy = remake(repoint; parameterization = HeldParameters(PowerScales(by_quad),\n                                                          n -> first(split(n, '.')) in inner_quads))\nlower_s, upper_s = bounds(study.param; phi = 0.3, z = 0.3, scale = 0.2, scale_max = 1.0)\nprintln(nparams(study.param), \" free parameters, ending \", join(parameter_names(study.param)[end-4:end], \", \"))\n64 free parameters, ending Q22B.z, inner23.power_scale, inner30.power_scale, outer44.power_scale, outer50.power_scale","title":"The parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#The-limits","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The study has four limits: the gates and the wall-irradiance ceiling above, a keep-out from the end caps, and a ceiling on the light the caps receive. HardwareKeepOut gives the worst-case clearance of each beam from named parts, here the caps, exactly and with the envelope and error set of the clearance check, and clearance_floor makes the minimum of the keep-out, 10 µm, a lower bound on the clearance of each beam, as a constraint with one component and one multiplier per beam. The keep-out checks the 1% contour; light_ceiling bounds all the nominal light on the caps, the wings of the spots included (light_fraction, absorbed plus striking their outside), here at 10⁻⁵ of the delivered power. On the 48-quad pointing the keep-out and the clearance check agree:\n\ncap_keepout = HardwareKeepOut(target, (:cap_top, :cap_bottom); targets = (:wall, :capsule),\n                              peak_fraction = 0.01, minimum = 0.01)\n@printf(\"worst-case clearance from the caps: keep-out %.4f mm, clearance check %.4f mm\\n\",\n        minimum(hardware_clearance(cap_keepout, quads)), review_48.worst)\nlimits = (ceiling, gates, clearance_floor(cap_keepout), light_ceiling(1e-5, :cap_top, :cap_bottom))\nstage1 = optimize_pointing(study, most_drive; lower = lower_s, upper = upper_s, constraints = limits,\n                           iterations = 1000, evaluations = 3000)\nPointing optimization converged: objective -0.9625 → -0.9506\n  280 iterations, 711 evaluations, 64 parameters; at a bound (widen the box if the objective should set them):\n    Q14B.phi            = -0.3  (lower bound; box -0.3 to 0.3)  d objective/d bound 0.0016\n    outer44.power_scale = 1  (upper bound; box 0.8 to 1)  d objective/d bound -0.357\n  phase 1: a design within the limits in 12 evaluations\n  8 outer iterations: constraints met, parameters at a bound\n  stationarity: largest |∂L/∂x| inside the box 6.79e-02 (objective units per parameter unit)\n    constraint                       value        limit  violation multiplier  status\n    irradiance                      8.1133 ≤      8.273  -1.93e-02      0.531  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 1]             0.0034548 ≤  0.0034556  -2.20e-04      0.005  active\n    mode_gate[l = 2]             0.0039498 ≤  0.0039498   2.59e-06     0.0106  active\n    mode_gate[l = 3]             0.0031188 ≤  0.0031252  -2.05e-03          0  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 4]            0.00089154 ≤ 0.00094983  -6.14e-02          0  satisfied\n    clearance[Q13B]               0.042951 ≥       0.01  -3.30e-02          0  satisfied (the worst of 46: 0 violated, 0 active)\n    light[cap_top+cap_bottom]   1.7731e-10 ≤      1e-05  -1.00e-02          0  satisfied\n  irradiance: :satisfied with a positive multiplier, the smooth stand-in's at its working limit, not the reported value's (an exact ceiling; PointingResult, \"Multipliers\")\n\nThe run ends with every limit met and the box active: the 44.5° cone at full power, the 30° cone 1.5–3.3% below it and the other two within 1%, and one to three of the four gates active. Every quad stays above the 10 µm minimum of the keep-out (the display shows the worst of the 46 rows, one per quad, each with a separate multiplier, and counts the active ones), and the light ceiling is met with room to spare: the gates and the box are the binding limits in this run, and the cap limits are not. This run too ends in a flat family: from the perturbed starts above it ends at 0.950 to 0.957 of the 48-quad drive, with the least keep-out clearance at 13 to 66 µm, after 650 to 1,650 evaluations.\n\nev1 = evaluate(study, stage1.x)\n@printf(\"%s after %d evaluations; cone powers %s of nominal\\n\", stage1.outcome, stage1.evaluations,\n        join((@sprintf(\"%s %.3f\", n[1:7], x) for (n, x) in zip(stage1.names[end-3:end], stage1.x[end-3:end])), \", \"))\ngate_line(label, ev) = @printf(\"%-22s drive %.4f, departure / gate for l = 1–4: %s\\n\", label,\n                               mean_flux(ev) / mean_flux(ref48),\n                               join((@sprintf(\"%.3f\", d) for d in mode_departure(ev, full) ./ gates.limit), \" \"))\ngate_line(\"stage 1 (quads)\", ev1)\nreview_line(\"stage 1 (quads)\", cap_review(stage1.beams))\nbox after 711 evaluations; cone powers inner23 0.989, inner30 0.977, outer44 1.000, outer50 0.988 of nominal\nstage 1 (quads)        drive 0.9506, departure / gate for l = 1–4: 1.000 1.000 0.998 0.939\nstage 1 (quads)        nominal  0.181 mm   worst case  0.043 mm   cap power ≤ 1.9e-06   0 fail","title":"The limits"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#From-quads-to-beams","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"So far each quad has been one beam: one aim, one power and the deposition of its four beams as a single f/7.9 cone. nif_beams gives the four beams of each quad, each through its port at f/20.7, at the aim of the quad and with a quarter of its power. A scenario on them needs a deposition operator for their ports: remake builds one, given the options of the first. The polish lets each outer beam move independently by up to 0.1 mm, along the hohlraum axis and along the tangent of its quad (a quad is the unit of GroupPointing's frame by default); the beams of the inner quads are held as before.\n\nbeams = nif_beams(stage1.beams)\ninner_beams = Set(b.name for b in beams if b.quad in inner_quads)\nby_beam = GroupPointing(beams; by = :beam, fields = (:phi, :z), relative = true)\npolish = remake(study; parameterization = HeldParameters(by_beam, n -> first(split(n, '.')) in inner_beams),\n                deposition_kw = (; resolution = 0.6, reach = 0.5, level = 0))\npolish_start = evaluate(polish, initial_parameters(polish.param))\ngate_line(\"stage 1 on beams\", polish_start)\ncheck_constraints(polish_start, limits)\n    constraint                       value        limit  violation multiplier  status\n    irradiance                      7.7318 ≤      8.273  -6.54e-02          0  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 1]             0.0035124 ≤  0.0034556   1.64e-02          0  violated\n    mode_gate[l = 2]              0.002495 ≤  0.0039498  -3.68e-01          0  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 3]             0.0031341 ≤  0.0031252   2.86e-03          0  violated\n    mode_gate[l = 4]            0.00028408 ≤ 0.00094983  -7.01e-01          0  satisfied\n    clearance[B248]               0.049697 ≥       0.01  -3.97e-02          0  satisfied (the worst of 184: 0 violated, 0 active)\n    light[cap_top+cap_bottom]   3.0152e-10 ≤      1e-05  -1.00e-02          0  satisfied\n\nThe same aims, deposited per beam, give a slightly different drive: the four beams of a quad enter at angles up to a few degrees apart, each with a separate, narrower f-cone, and a departure that was at its gate can now exceed it by up to a couple of percent (l = 1 or l = 3, depending on where the first stage ended). The limits are the same objects as in the first stage. The components of the keep-out follow the beams of the evaluation, 184 of them now, so the constraints carry over unchanged. The polish starts from the aims of the first stage (relative = true, all shifts zero) with a capped budget:\n\nlower_p, upper_p = bounds(polish.param; phi = 0.1, z = 0.1)\nstage2 = optimize_pointing(polish, most_drive; lower = lower_p, upper = upper_p, constraints = limits,\n                           iterations = 1000, evaluations = 120)\nPointing optimization reached the evaluation limit (best design kept): objective -0.9497 → -0.9512\n  13 iterations, 120 evaluations, 240 parameters, none at a bound\n  phase 1: a design within the limits in 2 evaluations\n  3 outer iterations: ended by the budget, the time limit or the callback\n  stationarity: largest |∂L/∂x| inside the box 4.49e-04 (objective units per parameter unit)\n    constraint                       value        limit  violation multiplier  status\n    irradiance                       7.866 ≤      8.273  -4.92e-02          0  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 1]             0.0028272 ≤  0.0034556  -1.82e-01          0  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 2]             0.0039521 ≤  0.0039498   5.89e-04    0.00344  active\n    mode_gate[l = 3]             0.0031078 ≤  0.0031252  -5.56e-03          0  satisfied\n    mode_gate[l = 4]            0.00032155 ≤ 0.00094983  -6.61e-01          0  satisfied\n    clearance[B248]               0.049697 ≥       0.01  -3.97e-02          0  satisfied (the worst of 184: 0 violated, 0 active)\n    light[cap_top+cap_bottom]   4.0414e-10 ≤      1e-05  -1.00e-02          0  satisfied","title":"From quads to beams"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#The-result","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The report of the run above gives the value of each constrained quantity against its limit, with its multiplier and status. pointing_summary puts the polished beams beside the beams of the quads before re-pointing (nif_beams(remaining), matched by name): the aim shifts, the power and cone fraction, the least keep-out clearance against that of the reference, and the light clipped and missed. Together they record the design:\n\npointing_summary(polish, stage2; reference = nif_beams(remaining), keepout = cap_keepout)\nPointingSummary(184 beams: aim shifts mean 0.130 mm, rms 0.171 mm, max 0.305 mm; 364.5 TW, cone fraction 0.345; keep-out clearance 0.050 mm (reference 0.050, margin 0.040); clipped 4.0e-10, missed -2.3e-04)\n\nev2 = evaluate(polish, stage2.x)\n@printf(\"%s after %d evaluations\\n\", stage2.outcome, stage2.evaluations)\ngate_line(\"polished, per beam\", ev2)\n@printf(\"peak wall irradiance %.4f of the ceiling\\n\", peak_irradiance(ev2, :wall) / only(ceiling.limit))\npolished_review = cap_review(stage2.beams)\nreview_line(\"polished\", polished_review)\nheld = filter(r -> r.name != \"irradiance\", stage2.constraints)   # gates, keep-out, light\n@assert stage2.outcome in (:interior, :box, :evaluations) && all(r -> r.violation <= 0.01, held)\n@assert ceiling.f(ev2) <= only(ceiling.limit) * 1.01   # the smooth stand-in of the ceiling\n@assert peak_irradiance(ev2, :wall) <= only(ceiling.limit) * 1.01   # the peak itself\n@assert polished_review.fail == 0 && polished_review.worst > 0\nevaluations after 120 evaluations\npolished, per beam     drive 0.9512, departure / gate for l = 1–4: 0.818 1.001 0.994 0.339\npeak wall irradiance 0.9508 of the ceiling\npolished               nominal  0.149 mm   worst case  0.049 mm   cap power ≤ 2.6e-06   0 fail\n\nThe polish brings the departures back to their gates. It stops at its budget, and a run stopped by its budget with a limit beyond its tolerance returns its design of least violation, here its best design within every limit (least_violation in the result). Six starts were compared: the start of this tutorial and five moved by 10⁻¹² or 10⁻⁹ mm, which stand for another CPU's rounding. Two runs end within the limits and keep the drive they gained, 0.17 and 0.32% over the aims of the first stage on beams. On the other four the last design exceeds a gate by more than the tolerance, and the run returns an earlier one within the limits, at the drive it started from. Every limit then holds: the gates, the keep-out, and the wall peak at 0.93 to 0.995 of the ceiling. The returned design is chosen on the reported rows, which contain the peak itself, while the run constrains the smooth stand-in of the ceiling. The ceiling constrains the peak itself only once a run has converged; before this choice the peak ended 0.966 to 1.020 of the ceiling over thirteen such starts. The budget is kept small here to keep the tutorial short. A design to hand off runs to convergence, which on an earlier first stage took 861 evaluations (five and a half minutes on 8 threads) and met every limit, with the peak at 0.997 of the ceiling and the same drive (research notes, \"Optimization defaults decided\"). The clearance check confirms the keep-out on the worst case: no beam fails, and the closest stays 17 to 49 µm from a cap.\n\nEach stage was one call of optimize_pointing on exported names: a parameterization, its box and the limits, with the scenario of the second stage built by remake on the beams of the first stage. Both results stay at hand for a report or a further stage.","title":"The result"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#A-tolerance-in-physical-units","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The gates above sit at the pointing-noise floor, the strictest case. A study states a tolerance per degree instead, a choice made for the design: 0.5% in P_2P_0 is the modelers' repeat uncertainty for one ignition design (research notes, \"Drive asymmetry inferred from post-shot capsule simulations\"), and degrees 1, 3 and 4 have no published number. Here, as an example, every degree is held within 0.5% of the 48-quad shape. shift_objective, the mean squared aim shift from the start, with the ceiling and the keep-out from the end caps as further constraints, gives the least re-pointing that meets the tolerance, in one call:\n\ntolerance = mode_gate(full, fill(0.005, 4))\nsmallest = optimize_pointing(repoint, shift_objective(repoint); x0, lower, upper,\n                             constraints = (ceiling, tolerance, clearance_floor(cap_keepout)),\n                             iterations = 1000, evaluations = 3000)\nsmallest_summary = pointing_summary(repoint, smallest; keepout = cap_keepout, drive_reference = full)\nPointingSummary(46 beams: aim shifts mean 0.058 mm, rms 0.063 mm, max 0.105 mm; 368.0 TW, cone fraction 0.348; keep-out clearance 0.028 mm (reference 0.066, margin 0.018); clipped 8.0e-12, missed -2.4e-04; drive 0.95936 of the reference, departure (l = 1–4) 0.5000% 0.5000% 0.5000% 0.1583%)\n\n@assert smallest.outcome in (:interior, :box) && all(r -> r.status !== :violated, smallest.constraints)\n@assert sqrt(smallest.value) ≈ smallest_summary.shift_rms\n\nDegrees 1–3 end at the tolerance and l = 4 well inside it; the quads move by 63 µm rms and 0.105 mm at most, and the drive ends 0.3% below that of the start (the objective does not reward drive). The same call with the gates at the noise floor moves the quads by 93 µm rms and up to 0.18 mm; with 1% per degree, which the departures of the start in l = 1 and 2 barely exceed, it moves them by 6 µm. The largest drive within each tolerance, from drive_objective under the same constraints, rises from 0.962 of the 48-quad drive at the floor to 0.967 at 0.5% and 0.971 at 1% (research notes, \"Mode gates in physical units\", measured before phase 1 was the default).","title":"A tolerance in physical units"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/dropped_bundle/#What-this-checks","page":"Freeing a bundle for radiography","text":"The NIF ports have the symmetry D_2 and no more, and every harmonic that D_2 forbids vanishes from the computed drive within round-off.\nBundles in the same D_2 orbit cost the same in every mode, as the symmetry requires.\nThree routes to the drive without a bundle agree within round-off: a scenario on the remaining beams, the full scenario with the bundle at zero power, and the influence matrix.\nFreeing a bundle together with its image under the 180° rotation about the hohlraum axis removes mode 1.\nThe power rebalance from the pseudoinverse and from the automatic-differentiation optimizer agree, as they must for a linear least-squares problem solved from the same start.\nThe worst-case standoff of every candidate bundle from the shields lies beyond the closed-form standoff at which the axes of its quads graze the shield rim (asserted).\nThe reach of the line-of-sight bundle at the far wall with the foil at 8 mm, from the closed form for rays from the corners of the foil through the rim of the capsule, exceeds the radius of the detector-side window, and line_of_sight finds rays stopped there and none at 14 mm. Its closed-form tests (test/backlighter.jl) cover a bundle exactly grazing the rim of a shield, a foil rotated off the line of sight, and imaging rays converging on a point detector.\nThe power per steradian of the backlighter toward the detector equals the isotropic E_x4pi: every emitting element faces the detector and sees it through the windows (asserted). The closed forms of the chain are tested in test/backlighter_signal.jl: point-projection fluence E_x(4pi r^2), the uniform image E_x A_p(pi A q^2) of a Lambertian foil, energy through the pinhole equal to the fluence times the image area, the hemispherical integrals of the angular distributions (E_x2 isotropic, E_x Lambertian), Beer–Lambert filters against the NIST nodes and edges, and the weighted residuals, optimum and hnu scaling of the CE fit against the published (hnu)^-(25text4). The form fitted without Coppari et al.'s 31 points predicts them within their stated 20% and the scatter of the fit, and the fit predicts an OMEGA Ti foil below the fitted lines within 1.5σ (both held out).\nThe worst-case clearance from the keep-out agrees with that from the clearance check, and re-pointing against it passes that check.\nThe re-pointed drive, recomputed by ray tracing and a direct radiosity solve, reproduces the modes from the optimizer.\nRe-pointing within limits meets every gate and the ceiling in one call (asserted, with no constraint violated, the run converged and the peak over the wall elements within 0.1% of the ceiling or below it). In its closed-form test (test/constraints.jl), with the coefficients of the drive linear in the quad powers, a fit under a ceiling on the total power matches the solution of the Karush–Kuhn–Tucker equations to 1e-4 and its multiplier to 1e-3. On a drive-like objective linear in the powers under two active ceilings, the reported multipliers match those of the closed form to 1e-3, also where the estimate of the iteration has zeroed one, and a bound sensitivity matches the closed form and restarts with the bound moved. The same file tests the power-mean ordering of the irradiance norm, its area-mean limit at p = 1, and the gradients of the constraint values against central differences.\nThe keep-out of the study from the end caps agrees with the worst case of the clearance check on the 48-quad pointing to within a micrometer, and the polished beams pass that check with no beam failing (asserted). The budget-stopped polish meets the limits it works on: the normalized violation of every gate, the keep-out and the light ceiling is at most 0.01 (1% of a gate, 10 µm of the keep-out), and the smooth stand-in of the ceiling is at most 1% over its limit (asserted; over 13 starts, perturbed by 10⁻¹² to 10⁻⁹ mm or by rounding, the largest of those violations was 0.002 and the stand-in at most 0.994 of its limit). Only a converged run constrains the wall peak itself, so the peak is not asserted here. In the closed-form tests of the keep-out and light constraints (test/constraints.jl), one free parameter, the aim of the 50° cone, is pulled by the objective into the caps, and a constrained run ends with the constraint active at the aim where its value reaches the limit, found by bisection, to 1e-5 mm. The tests also check the values of the constraints against the quantities that define them (the light on the caps against component_power to 1e-12) and their gradients against central differences to 1e-6.\nThe least re-pointing within a tolerance of 0.5% per degree meets the tolerance, the ceiling and the keep-out, and converges (asserted, with no constraint violated), and the square root of its objective equals the rms aim shift of the summary, computed from the aims directly (asserted). The closed-form test of the objective (test/pointing_summary.jl) checks the least weighted re-pointing for one linear constraint against its solution in closed form, to 1e-5 mm.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#Entrance-hole-size-and-the-power-balance","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"The x rays a hohlraum wall emits have three places to go: back into the wall, into the capsule, or out through the laser entrance holes (LEHs). The holes must be large enough to admit the beams, and radiation that reaches an open hole is lost. The HYBRID-E design that first reached ignition by Lawson's criterion, NIF shot N210808, shrank its LEHs from 3.64 mm to 3.1 mm in diameter (27% less area) and spent the radiation it saved on a lower peak power, 441 TW against the 487 TW of the 3.64 mm shot N210307, at a similar radiation temperature (Kritcher et al., Phys. Rev. E 106, 025201 (2022), Table I and Sec. II A).\n\nThe holes here are their nominal geometric size, open and static. In a shot the hole is not that simple: plasma blown off the LEH rim and the expanding wall (\"gold bubble\") close it down during the pulse and re-emit part of what reaches it, so its effective size, and the loss, are smaller than those of the as-built hole. The fractions below are therefore the open-hole bound for each size, and their variation with size is what a geometric change buys before plasma effects.\n\nThis tutorial sweeps the LEH diameter of the N210808 hohlraum over realistic sizes, 3.0 to 3.6 mm, and computes the fraction of the x-ray source that escapes through the holes and the fraction the capsule absorbs. It checks Briareus's view factors and radiosity solve against an independent Monte Carlo photon walk in the exact geometry, compares both with the closed-form power balance that assumes a uniform flux, and then compares the predicted change in required power between the two shots with the published powers. Laser propagation is not modeled: the x-ray source is prescribed on the wall, so the sweep isolates the radiation power balance from beam clipping and pointing.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nusing Random\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Briareus), \"docs\", \"literate\", \"_plotting.jl\"))   # figure helpers\nset_theme!(fontsize = 15, linewidth = 2.5)","title":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#The-N210808-hohlraum","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"Kritcher et al. (Table I and Sec. II C) give the hohlraum: gold-lined depleted uranium, 6.4 mm in diameter and 11.24 mm long, with 3.1 mm LEHs, and a diamond capsule of inner radius 1.05 mm under an ablator 76–80 µm thick; the outer radius is taken as 1.13 mm.\n\nThe albedos are engineering estimates and do not come from shot data. The wall albedo is the Au-lined depleted-uranium wall loss at 300 eV, 6 ns into the pulse (du_wall_loss, Lindl's gold scaling with the lower loss of uranium). The time that enters the wall loss is the effective age of the wall, the time that the wall would need at the present temperature to store its present energy, t_mathrmeff = int (TT_mathrmnow)^532dt, as drive_history evaluates it. On the measured Dante history of the same design (N221204, Pak et al., Phys. Rev. E 109, 025203 (2024), Fig. 1) that age is about 1 ns at 6 ns, well short of the 6 ns since the pulse began, because the foot stores little. The capsule takes the ablator default, 0.3. The conversion efficiency only scales the source, so the wall converts all of its laser power and every fraction below is a fraction of the x-ray source.\n\nconst N210808 = (radius = 3.2, length = 11.24, leh_diameter = 3.1, capsule_radius = 1.13)\nconst N210307_LEH = 3.64\npak = n221204_pulse()\nmeasured = findall(isfinite, pak.dante1)\nT6 = pak.dante1[findfirst(≈(6.0), pak.t)]\nwt = [t <= 6.0 ? (T / T6)^(3.3 / 0.62) : 0.0 for (t, T) in zip(pak.t[measured], pak.dante1[measured])]\nt_eff = sum(diff(pak.t[measured]) .* (wt[1:end-1] .+ wt[2:end]) ./ 2)\nαw = albedo(du_wall_loss(), t_eff, 300.0)\n@printf(\"effective wall age at 6 ns: %.2f ns\\n\", t_eff)\nαc = 0.3\n@printf(\"wall albedo %.4f, capsule albedo %.2f\\n\", αw, αc)\n\nfunction n210808(leh_diameter; h = 0.4, wall_albedo = αw)\n    return hohlraum(; length = N210808.length, radius = N210808.radius,\n                    leh_radius = leh_diameter / 2, capsule_radius = N210808.capsule_radius,\n                    h, capsule_h = h / 3,\n                    wall = Material(:Au; albedo = wall_albedo, conversion = 1.0),\n                    capsule = Material(:ablator; albedo = αc, conversion = 0.0))\nend\neffective wall age at 6 ns: 1.07 ns\nwall albedo 0.8750, capsule albedo 0.30","title":"The N210808 hohlraum"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#The-x-ray-source","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"The beams heat two bands on each half of the wall: the inner cones (23.5° and 30°) near the waist, the outer cones (44.5° and 50°) near the LEHs. The source is uniform in each band, with the 33% inner-cone fraction of the N210808 peak (Kritcher et al., Sec. II A). The band positions are representative and are not taken from the shot; the fractions change little with them (checked below).\n\nEach wall element gets the source power of its overlap with the bands, so Briareus and the Monte Carlo reference both solve the problem with this piecewise-constant source.\n\nconst BANDS = ((fraction = 0.33, z = (0.6, 1.8)),    # inner cones, |z| (mm)\n               (fraction = 0.67, z = (3.4, 4.6)))    # outer cones\n\nelement_points(t, e) = [p for k in t.elem_tris[e]\n                        for p in (t.tri_v0[k], t.tri_v0[k] + t.tri_e1[k], t.tri_v0[k] + t.tri_e2[k])]\n\n\"Axial and azimuthal extent of a wall element: (z0, z1, φa, φb).\"\nfunction wall_patch(t, e)\n    pts = element_points(t, e)\n    φc = atan(t.elem_centroid[e][2], t.elem_centroid[e][1])\n    δ = [rem2pi(atan(p[2], p[1]) - φc, RoundNearest) for p in pts]\n    return (extrema(p[3] for p in pts)..., φc + minimum(δ), φc + maximum(δ))\nend\n\nfunction wall_source(t; shift = 0.0)\n    P = zeros(nelements(t))\n    for e in elements(t, :wall)\n        z0, z1, φa, φb = wall_patch(t, e)\n        for band in BANDS, s in (-1, 1)\n            a, b = band.z .+ (band === BANDS[2] ? shift : 0.0)\n            lo, hi = s > 0 ? (a, b) : (-b, -a)\n            P[e] += band.fraction / 2 * max(0.0, min(z1, hi) - max(z0, lo)) / (b - a) *\n                    (φb - φa) / 2π\n        end\n    end\n    return P\nend","title":"The x-ray source"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#Briareus","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"RadiationModel computes the view factors over the true cylinder, end-cap annuli, holes and sphere; solve returns the incident flux, and energy_balance splits the source into what each component absorbs, what escapes through each hole, and what the view factors lose (row sums short of one).\n\nfunction briareus_fractions(leh_diameter; h = 0.4, closed = false, shift = 0.0)\n    t = n210808(leh_diameter; h)\n    model = RadiationModel(t; closed)\n    b = energy_balance(solve(model, wall_source(t; shift)))\n    return (; leh = (b.escaped[:leh_top] + b.escaped[:leh_bottom]) / b.source,\n            capsule = b.absorbed[:capsule] / b.source, lost = b.lost / b.source,\n            elements = nelements(t), target = t)\nend","title":"Briareus"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#The-closed-form-power-balance","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"If the incident flux H were the same on every surface, the source would balance the absorption in the wall and capsule and the escape through the holes (Lindl et al., Phys. Plasmas 11, 339 (2004), Sec. IV):\n\nS = Hleft(1-alpha_w)A_w + (1-alpha_c)A_c + A_Lright\nqquad\nf_L = fracA_L(1-alpha_w)A_w + (1-alpha_c)A_c + A_L\n\nwith A_w the wall and end caps, A_c the capsule and A_L both holes. This is the integrating-sphere result of the Integrating sphere tutorial applied to a cylinder, where it is no longer exact: the capsule shadows the wall, and the outer-cone source sits close to the holes.\n\nfunction power_balance(leh_diameter; wall_albedo = αw)\n    R, L, rc, rL = N210808.radius, N210808.length, N210808.capsule_radius, leh_diameter / 2\n    AL, Ac = 2π * rL^2, 4π * rc^2\n    Aw = 2π * R * L + 2π * (R^2 - rL^2)\n    D = (1 - wall_albedo) * Aw + (1 - αc) * Ac + AL\n    return (; leh = AL / D, capsule = (1 - αc) * Ac / D)\nend","title":"The closed-form power balance"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#The-Monte-Carlo-reference","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"Photons start from the source: each element is chosen in proportion to its power, and the start point is uniform over the true curved area of the element. Photons leave each surface in a cosine-distributed direction. Each step intersects the exact cylinder, end planes and sphere analytically, so the walk needs neither a mesh nor view factors. A photon striking the wall or the capsule deposits 1-alpha of its weight there and continues with the rest, and one crossing a hole escapes with its weight; Russian roulette ends photons whose weight falls below 10⁻³ without bias. This is the gray, diffuse radiosity problem solved by a different method, so it is exact up to its sampling error, which is reported with each result.\n\nconst V3 = Briareus.Vec3     # Makie also exports a `Vec3`\n\n\"Cosine-distributed unit vector about the unit normal `n`.\"\nfunction lambertian(rng, n)\n    u, φ = rand(rng), 2π * rand(rng)\n    a = abs(n[1]) < 0.9 ? V3(1, 0, 0) : V3(0, 1, 0)\n    t1 = normalize(cross(n, a))\n    t2 = cross(n, t1)\n    return sqrt(u) * (cos(φ) * t1 + sin(φ) * t2) + sqrt(1 - u) * n\nend\n\n\"Next surface hit from `p` along `d` inside the hohlraum: (kind, point, normal into the cavity).\"\nfunction next_hit(p, d, g)\n    s, kind = Inf, :none\n    b, c = dot(p, d), dot(p, p) - g.rc^2               # capsule\n    if b^2 > c && -b - sqrt(b^2 - c) > 0\n        s, kind = -b - sqrt(b^2 - c), :capsule\n    end\n    a2 = d[1]^2 + d[2]^2                               # cylinder, from inside\n    if a2 > 0\n        bb, cc = p[1] * d[1] + p[2] * d[2], p[1]^2 + p[2]^2 - g.R^2\n        sw = (-bb + sqrt(bb^2 - a2 * cc)) / a2\n        sw < s && abs(p[3] + sw * d[3]) <= g.H && ((s, kind) = (sw, :wall))\n    end\n    if d[3] != 0                                       # end planes: cap or hole\n        se = (copysign(g.H, d[3]) - p[3]) / d[3]\n        if se < s\n            q = p + se * d\n            s, kind = se, hypot(q[1], q[2]) < g.rL ? :leh : :wall\n        end\n    end\n    q = p + s * d\n    n = kind === :capsule ? q / g.rc :\n        abs(abs(q[3]) - g.H) < 1e-9 ? V3(0, 0, -sign(q[3])) : -V3(q[1], q[2], 0) / g.R\n    return kind, q, n\nend\n\nfunction monte_carlo(leh_diameter, histories; h = 0.4, shift = 0.0, seed = 1)\n    t = n210808(leh_diameter; h)\n    g = (; R = N210808.radius, H = N210808.length / 2, rc = N210808.capsule_radius,\n         rL = leh_diameter / 2, αw, αc)\n    wall = elements(t, :wall)\n    P = wall_source(t; shift)[wall]\n    cdf = cumsum(P) ./ sum(P)\n    patches = [wall_patch(t, e) for e in wall]\n    return _monte_carlo(g, cdf, patches, histories, seed)\nend\n\nfunction _monte_carlo(g, cdf, patches, histories, seed)    # function barrier for the threads\n    chunks = 16 * Threads.nthreads()\n    sums = zeros(chunks, 4)              # escaped, escaped², capsule, capsule²\n    Threads.@threads for k in 1:chunks\n        rng = Xoshiro(seed + k)\n        for _ in 1:(histories ÷ chunks)\n            z0, z1, φa, φb = patches[min(searchsortedfirst(cdf, rand(rng)), length(cdf))]\n            φ, z = φa + (φb - φa) * rand(rng), z0 + (z1 - z0) * rand(rng)\n            p, n = V3(g.R * cos(φ), g.R * sin(φ), z), -V3(cos(φ), sin(φ), 0)\n            w, escaped, capsule = 1.0, 0.0, 0.0\n            while true\n                kind, p, n = next_hit(p, lambertian(rng, n), g)\n                kind === :leh && (escaped = w; break)\n                α = kind === :capsule ? g.αc : g.αw\n                kind === :capsule && (capsule += (1 - α) * w)\n                w *= α\n                if w < 1e-3                    # Russian roulette\n                    rand(rng) < 0.1 || break\n                    w *= 10\n                end\n                p += 1e-10 * n\n            end\n            sums[k, 1] += escaped; sums[k, 2] += escaped^2\n            sums[k, 3] += capsule; sums[k, 4] += capsule^2\n        end\n    end\n    total = chunks * (histories ÷ chunks)\n    S = vec(sum(sums; dims = 1)) ./ total\n    return (; leh = S[1], leh_σ = sqrt((S[2] - S[1]^2) / total),\n            capsule = S[3], capsule_σ = sqrt((S[4] - S[3]^2) / total))\nend","title":"The Monte Carlo reference"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#The-nominal-hohlraum","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"At the N210808 hole size, with a wall mesh of 0.4 mm (4,400 elements), 10⁷ photon histories, and the closed form:\n\nnominal = briareus_fractions(N210808.leh_diameter)\nmc_time = @elapsed mc_nominal = monte_carlo(N210808.leh_diameter, 10^7)\npb_nominal = power_balance(N210808.leh_diameter)\n@printf(\"%-14s %10s %18s %10s\\n\", \"\", \"Briareus\", \"Monte Carlo\", \"closed form\")\n@printf(\"%-14s %10.5f %10.5f ± %.5f %10.5f\\n\", \"LEH escape\", nominal.leh, mc_nominal.leh,\n        mc_nominal.leh_σ, pb_nominal.leh)\n@printf(\"%-14s %10.5f %10.5f ± %.5f %10.5f\\n\", \"capsule\", nominal.capsule, mc_nominal.capsule,\n        mc_nominal.capsule_σ, pb_nominal.capsule)\n@printf(\"view factors lose %.1e of the source; Monte Carlo took %.0f s\\n\", nominal.lost, mc_time)\n                 Briareus        Monte Carlo closed form\nLEH escape        0.27262    0.27266 ± 0.00011    0.24855\ncapsule           0.18431    0.18450 ± 0.00008    0.18494\nview factors lose 7.3e-04 of the source; Monte Carlo took 5 s\n\nBriareus and the photon walk agree to 2×10⁻⁴ of the source, within the 6×10⁻⁴ that the view factors lose to their quadrature (rows of the view-factor matrix summing to slightly less than one). The closed form puts the LEH loss 9% low (0.249 against 0.273) and the share of the capsule 0.3% high. Its uniform flux is a poor assumption for the holes: the flux that reaches them comes mostly from the end caps and the wall near the ends, where the outer-cone source sits.","title":"The nominal hohlraum"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#Mesh-convergence","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"The view-factor loss is a discretization error. Refining the wall mesh brings Briareus to within a few 10⁻⁴ of the photon walk; rescaling the view factors so every row sums to one (closed = true, valid here because the holes close the enclosure) brings it within the sampling error. Each mesh has a separate piecewise-constant source, so each gets a separate Monte Carlo run:\n\nconvergence = map([(0.6, false), (0.4, false), (0.25, false), (0.4, true)]) do (h, closed)\n    b = briareus_fractions(N210808.leh_diameter; h, closed)\n    m = monte_carlo(N210808.leh_diameter, 10^7; h, seed = 2)\n    @printf(\"h %.2f mm%-9s %6d elements: LEH %+.1e ± %.0e, capsule %+.1e ± %.0e, lost %.1e\\n\",\n            h, closed ? \", closed\" : \"\", b.elements, b.leh - m.leh, m.leh_σ,\n            b.capsule - m.capsule, m.capsule_σ, b.lost)\n    (; h, closed, b, m)\nend\nh 0.60 mm            2220 elements: LEH +2.2e-03 ± 1e-04, capsule +1.7e-03 ± 8e-05, lost -9.0e-03\nh 0.40 mm            4632 elements: LEH -5.1e-05 ± 1e-04, capsule -2.0e-04 ± 8e-05, lost 7.3e-04\nh 0.25 mm           11544 elements: LEH -2.9e-04 ± 1e-04, capsule -2.7e-04 ± 8e-05, lost 1.2e-03\nh 0.40 mm, closed    4632 elements: LEH +1.7e-05 ± 1e-04, capsule +5.3e-06 ± 8e-05, lost 1.3e-13\n\nOn the coarsest mesh the rows sum to more than one (a negative loss) and both fractions read 2–3×10⁻³ high; the error falls to a few 10⁻⁴ at 0.4 mm and stays there at 0.25 mm (3×10⁻⁴ for the LEH and the capsule), within the 1.1×10⁻³ the view factors lose. Balancing the rows at 0.4 mm brings both within the sampling error.","title":"Mesh convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#The-sweep","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"The sweep covers LEH diameters from 3.0 to 3.6 mm around the 3.1 mm of N210808, and the 3.64 mm of N210307, with the sizes of the two shots marked:\n\ndiameters = sort(unique([collect(3.0:0.1:3.6); N210808.leh_diameter; N210307_LEH]))\nsweep = map(diameters) do d\n    (; d, b = briareus_fractions(d; closed = true), m = monte_carlo(d, 10^7; seed = 3),\n     pb = power_balance(d))\nend\n\nfig = Figure(size = (820, 620))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], ylabel = \"fraction of the x-ray source\",\n          title = \"N210808 hohlraum, wall albedo $(round(αw; digits = 3))\",\n          xticklabelsvisible = false)\nfor (key, label, color) in ((:leh, \"escapes through the LEHs\", Makie.wong_colors()[1]),\n                            (:capsule, \"absorbed by the capsule\", Makie.wong_colors()[2]))\n    lines!(ax, diameters, [getproperty(s.pb, key) for s in sweep]; color, linestyle = :dash,\n           label = \"$label, closed form\")\n    lines!(ax, diameters, [getproperty(s.b, key) for s in sweep]; color, label = \"$label, Briareus\")\n    scatter!(ax, diameters, [getproperty(s.m, key) for s in sweep]; color = :black,\n             markersize = 7)\nend\nscatter!(ax, [NaN], [NaN]; color = :black, markersize = 7, label = \"Monte Carlo\")\nvlines!(ax, [N210808.leh_diameter, N210307_LEH]; color = :gray60, linewidth = 1)\ntext!(ax, [N210808.leh_diameter, N210307_LEH], [0.155, 0.155]; text = [\" N210808\", \"N210307 \"],\n      align = (:center, :bottom), fontsize = 12, color = :gray30)\nylims!(ax, 0.15, 0.5)\naxislegend(ax; position = :lt, framevisible = false, labelsize = 12)\nerr = Axis(fig[2, 1], xlabel = \"LEH diameter (mm)\", ylabel = \"(Briareus − MC)/σ_MC\")\nscatterlines!(err, diameters, [(s.b.leh - s.m.leh) / s.m.leh_σ for s in sweep];\n              color = Makie.wong_colors()[1], label = \"LEH\")\nscatterlines!(err, diameters, [(s.b.capsule - s.m.capsule) / s.m.capsule_σ for s in sweep];\n              color = Makie.wong_colors()[2], label = \"capsule\")\nhspan!(err, -2, 2; color = (:gray, 0.15))\nylims!(err, -5, 5)\naxislegend(err; position = :rb, orientation = :horizontal, framevisible = false, labelsize = 12)\nlinkxaxes!(ax, err)\nrowsize!(fig.layout, 2, Relative(0.3))\nfig\nFigure()\n\nOver the whole sweep Briareus follows the photon walk within its sampling error (the shaded band is ±2σ, with 10⁷ histories per point), while the closed form stays 9% below both on the LEH loss. From 3.0 to 3.6 mm the hole area grows 44% and the loss 33%, because a larger hole also lowers the flux that drives it. Each 0.1 mm of diameter sends another 1.4% of the source out through the holes and takes 0.3% of it from the capsule.\n\nfor s in sweep\n    @printf(\"LEH %.2f mm: escape %.4f (MC %.4f ± %.4f, closed form %.4f), capsule %.4f (MC %.4f)\\n\",\n            s.d, s.b.leh, s.m.leh, s.m.leh_σ, s.pb.leh, s.b.capsule, s.m.capsule)\nend\nLEH 3.00 mm: escape 0.2587 (MC 0.2586 ± 0.0001, closed form 0.2360), capsule 0.1873 (MC 0.1873)\nLEH 3.10 mm: escape 0.2727 (MC 0.2727 ± 0.0001, closed form 0.2486), capsule 0.1845 (MC 0.1845)\nLEH 3.20 mm: escape 0.2869 (MC 0.2868 ± 0.0001, closed form 0.2611), capsule 0.1817 (MC 0.1816)\nLEH 3.30 mm: escape 0.3010 (MC 0.3010 ± 0.0001, closed form 0.2737), capsule 0.1789 (MC 0.1787)\nLEH 3.40 mm: escape 0.3152 (MC 0.3152 ± 0.0001, closed form 0.2863), capsule 0.1760 (MC 0.1759)\nLEH 3.50 mm: escape 0.3294 (MC 0.3296 ± 0.0001, closed form 0.2990), capsule 0.1732 (MC 0.1729)\nLEH 3.60 mm: escape 0.3436 (MC 0.3437 ± 0.0001, closed form 0.3116), capsule 0.1703 (MC 0.1702)\nLEH 3.64 mm: escape 0.3493 (MC 0.3493 ± 0.0001, closed form 0.3166), capsule 0.1692 (MC 0.1691)","title":"The sweep"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#Where-the-source-sits","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"Moving the outer-cone bands 0.5 mm toward the holes or the waist (N210808 moved its 50° beams toward the waist to clear the smaller hole) shifts the fractions by:\n\nat_nominal = sweep[findfirst(s -> s.d == N210808.leh_diameter, sweep)].b\nfor shift in (0.5, -0.5)\n    b = briareus_fractions(N210808.leh_diameter; closed = true, shift)\n    @printf(\"outer bands %+.1f mm: LEH escape %+.4f, capsule %+.4f\\n\", shift,\n            b.leh - at_nominal.leh, b.capsule - at_nominal.capsule)\nend\nouter bands +0.5 mm: LEH escape -0.0047, capsule -0.0029\nouter bands -0.5 mm: LEH escape -0.0012, capsule +0.0047\n\nHalf a millimeter moves either fraction by under 0.5% of the source, about a third of what 0.1 mm of hole diameter does to the LEH loss, so the representative bands do not decide the sweep.","title":"Where the source sits"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#N210307-to-N210808","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"At the same radiation temperature the capsule absorbs the same power, so the source needed scales as the inverse of the capsule fraction, and the laser power with it if conversion and coupling do not change. Shrinking the holes from 3.64 to 3.1 mm then changes the required power by the ratio of the capsule fractions. The published peak powers are 441 TW (N210808) and 487 TW (N210307). Over a range of wall albedos:\n\nfunction power_ratio(wall_albedo; model = :briareus)\n    f(d) = model === :briareus ?\n        begin\n            t = n210808(d; wall_albedo)\n            b = energy_balance(solve(RadiationModel(t; closed = true), wall_source(t)))\n            b.absorbed[:capsule] / b.source\n        end : power_balance(d; wall_albedo).capsule\n    return f(N210307_LEH) / f(N210808.leh_diameter)\nend\nalbedos = [0.8, 0.85, 0.9, αw, 0.95]\nratios = [(a, power_ratio(a), power_ratio(a; model = :closed)) for a in albedos]\n@printf(\"published peak-power ratio 441/487 = %.3f\\n\", 441 / 487)\nfor (a, r, rc) in ratios\n    @printf(\"wall albedo %.3f: Briareus %.3f, closed form %.3f\\n\", a, r, rc)\nend\npublished peak-power ratio 441/487 = 0.906\nwall albedo 0.800: Briareus 0.942, closed form 0.947\nwall albedo 0.850: Briareus 0.927, closed form 0.933\nwall albedo 0.900: Briareus 0.905, closed form 0.913\nwall albedo 0.875: Briareus 0.917, closed form 0.924\nwall albedo 0.950: Briareus 0.871, closed form 0.881\n\nAt the estimated wall albedo the prediction is an 8.3% saving (ratio 0.917), and 9.5% at an albedo of 0.9, against the 9.4% between the peak powers of the two shots. The closed form, whose error largely cancels in the ratio, gives nearly the same. The comparison is approximate. The shots also changed the wavelength separation (1.55 to 1.8 Å), the outer-cone pointing and the pulse duration. \"Similar radiation temperature\" is a qualitative statement in the paper and is not a measured equality. And the holes close down during the pulse, so their effective sizes, and the difference between them, are smaller than the as-built ones. The comparison confirms the size of the effect rather than a number.","title":"N210307 to N210808"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/leh_power_balance/#What-this-checks","page":"Entrance-hole size and the power balance","text":"Code verification. On the N210808 hohlraum, the fraction of the x-ray source escaping through the LEHs and the fraction the capsule absorbs agree with an independent Monte Carlo photon walk in the exact geometry. The difference is 2×10⁻⁴ of the source at a 0.4 mm wall mesh, within the fraction the view factors lose (6×10⁻⁴). With the rows balanced (closed = true) it is within the sampling error (10⁻⁴; 2σ band) at every LEH diameter from 3.0 to 3.64 mm.\nSolution verification. The difference from the reference falls from 3×10⁻³ at a 0.6 mm wall mesh to a few 10⁻⁴ at 0.4 and 0.25 mm, within the fraction the view factors lose.\nThe closed-form power balance with a uniform flux is not a reference for the LEH loss in a hohlraum: it puts that loss 9–9.5% low over 3.0–3.64 mm. It gets the share of the capsule within 0.5%.\nValidation, approximate. For open, static holes, the capsule-coupling gain from shrinking the LEH from 3.64 to 3.1 mm predicts a peak-power saving of 8.3% at the estimated Au-lined DU wall albedo (0.875) and 9.5% at 0.90, against the 9.4% between N210307 and N210808 (Kritcher et al. 2022, Table I). Those shots also changed other parts of the design, and their holes closed down during the pulse; this power balance models neither.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#For-VisRad-users","page":"For VisRad users","text":"VisRad (Prism Computational Sciences; MacFarlane, J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 81, 287 (2003)) is the view-factor code most hohlraum and backlighter pointing work is done in. Briareus covers the same core calculation (laser deposition, 3D view-factor radiosity and capsule drive symmetry), reads and writes VisRad's files, and has been compared with VisRad 21 on NIF hohlraums. This page maps VisRad's concepts to Briareus's, lists the files each reader and writer handles, summarizes how the results of the two codes compare, and states where they differ, including what VisRad does that Briareus does not. It ends with a walkthrough of porting a VisRad study. The Physics background page covers the physics both codes compute, and VisRad interchange documents every reader and writer.","title":"For VisRad users"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#Concepts","page":"For VisRad users","text":"VisRad Briareus Notes\nWorkspace (.vrw) VisRadWorkspace as read; then a Target and a vector of Beams A Briareus study has no workspace object: the target, the beams and the precomputed operators (DepositionOperator, RadiationModel, or both in a Scenario) are separate values.\nTarget component (object), component tree VisRadObject; Component One component per object; a cylindrical hohlraum becomes five (wall, two end caps, two LEH apertures). The folder in the tree is object.folder, for selecting parts.\nSurface elements, object grid (\"Number of mesh pts\") Elements (radiating patch sides) visrad_component meshes primitives on VisRad's grid, or on Briareus's with primitive_h. One-sided surfaces radiate from the front; two_sided radiates from both.\nAlbedo, x-ray conversion efficiency Material: albedo, conversion Constant, tabulated in time, or models: WallLossAlbedo (Marshak wall loss), Conversion.\nLEH (elements of albedo 0) aperture Transparent; its incident flux is the flux that escapes.\nLaser beam, beam cluster Beam, its group NIF beams B111–B468 (nif_beams) or quads (nif_quads); group is the cone.\nPointing Reference Point, focus offset aim, defocus Same sign: positive moves the focus along the propagation direction.\nSpatial profile (super-Gaussian), built-in phase plates SuperGaussianSpot (1/e semi-axes); VISRAD_PHASE_PLATES, visrad_spot VisRad's NIF CPP radii are half widths at half maximum; the readers convert them.\nBeam profile away from focus f-cone sum (default); VisRadProfile See \"The beam away from focus\" below.\nTime-dependent beam powers, pulse shapes PulseShape, with_pulses, drive_history A pulse is piecewise linear in ns and TW.\nDirect laser power source deposit(op, beams).element (deposit) TW absorbed per element, not TW/cm²; visrad_deposition converts VisRad's.\nRadiosity results (Flux In, Flux Out) RadiationSolution: H, B TW/mm²; to_Wcm2 converts.\nSurface element statistics (Legendre and Y_LM fits) legendre_moments, ylm_moments See \"Modes\" below; visrad_legendre_fit and visrad_ylm_fit reproduce VisRad's fits.\nCapture-clearance report clearance, ApertureKeepOut Worst case over pointing and spot tolerances.\n\nVisRad's workspaces and tables are in cm, TW/cm², µm for spot radii and seconds for pulse-shape files; Briareus is in mm, TW/mm², ns and TW. The readers and writers convert.","title":"Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#Files","page":"For VisRad users","text":"File Read Written Notes\nNIF SST pointing CSV read_visrad_sst write_visrad_sst Aim (without defocus) and defocus per beam; beams absent from the file are turned off, as VisRad does. The format to set pointing in VisRad.\nLaser Beam Summary CSV read_visrad_summary write_visrad_summary Ports, pointing, focus, power, spots. The headers after \"Power\" are not shown in the manual, so the written ones are an assumption.\nTime-dependent beam powers CSV read_visrad_powers write_visrad_powers One column per beam, TW against ns; for NIF, VisRad assigns the powers to the 3ω cones.\nPulse-shape library file read_visrad_pulse_shape — W against s; the column order is an assumption.\nWorkspace .vrw (keyed text, VisRad 20–21; JSON, VisRad 21) read_visrad_workspace, visrad_target write_visrad_workspace Components (CAD as generalized meshes; spheres, rectangles, cylindrical hohlraums) and beams, from either format. The writer copies a text template and rewrites the pointing, focus, power and on/off state of the beams; everything else stays VisRad's. A JSON template, or writing over a workspace that holds VisRad's results, is an error.\nComputed power sources in a .vrw visrad_power_sources, visrad_deposition — VisRad's per-element deposition, mapped onto a target built from the same workspace. VisRad 21 saves a computed workspace as JSON; both formats give the same tables.\nSurface Element Properties (.sed), Power Summary (.psd) read_visrad_table — The file layout is not documented; the reader is tolerant.\nBenchmark kit compare_visrad write_visrad_kit Pointing, beam summary, powers and target description for rebuilding a case in VisRad, with Briareus's results named as VisRad's Power Summary columns.\nNIF CMT XML, .bti intersection points, beam-energy files — — Not implemented: the CMT schema is undocumented and no public sample was found.","title":"Files"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#How-the-results-compare","page":"For VisRad users","text":"VisRad 21 computed the laser deposition on two NIF hohlraum workspaces built from Briareus geometry, and Briareus read each one, built the same target and beams, and compared its deposition per element with VisRad's. With VisRadProfile, wall deposition differs from VisRad's by 0.45–0.7% rms per element and peaks agree within 0.1%. With the earlier, tabulated profile, the P2 and P4 of the capsule, computed from the two depositions through the same Briareus radiosity, agreed to 0.014 and 0.004 percentage points. Verification and validation gives the setup and all the numbers. On a foil workspace with four beams, the totals agree to the precision of the table (3.6000 TW), elements above 20 TW/cm² agree within 1.4%, and the faint wings near focus (2–30 TW/cm²) differ by 8–15% (research notes, \"VisRad workspace files (2026-10-17)\").\n\nwrite_visrad_kit and compare_visrad set up the same comparison for any case. The comparison covers deposition only: the VisRad radiosity, absolute drive levels and mode amplitudes have not yet been compared with Briareus's (ROADMAP, \"Benchmark against VisRad\").\n\nModes. VisRad's Surface Element Statistics fit Legendre polynomials and real spherical harmonics by least squares to the element values; the weighting is not documented. Briareus integrates the harmonics over the exact solid angle of each element. The Legendre coefficients mean the same in both (A_l and a_l, with A_2A_0 = a_2a_0 = P2/P0), but the two estimates differ on a coarse mesh; apply visrad_legendre_fit to the element values of both codes for a matched comparison. VisRad's spherical harmonics are normalized so that Y_00 = 1: they are Briareus's orthonormal ylm_moments times sqrt4pi. Whether VisRad includes the Condon–Shortley phase is not stated, so the signs of odd-M coefficients may differ; per-L amplitudes do not.","title":"How the results compare"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#Where-the-codes-differ","page":"For VisRad users","text":"","title":"Where the codes differ"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#The-beam-away-from-focus","page":"For VisRad users","text":"Near best focus both codes deposit the same super-Gaussian. Away from focus they differ. Briareus's default model is the geometric-optics one: the best-focus profile summed over a quadrature of the f-cone directions, which is the focal profile convolved with the disk of focal points. It conserves power at any distance and matches an exact convolution (Laser spot on a foil). VisRad's model is analytic. It is a super-Gaussian whose semi-axes a(z), b(z) and order n(z) grow with the distance z from focus, blended toward a square of growing weight. It is cut off at 10⁻³ of its local peak and renormalized in each transverse plane. The two shapes differ in kind: away from focus VisRad's spot turns square-shouldered, while the f-cone sum softens the focal profile, to about half its focal order at 15 mm.\n\nOn a hohlraum wall, 3–8 mm past focus, the f-cone sum gave 10% rms per element against VisRad's deposition, with peaks 10% higher. VisRadProfile is VisRad's law. The semi-axes, order, square weight and square width follow the parameters VisRad itself exports, and two constants were fitted to its deposition; the docstring gives the forms. visrad_profile takes the envelope ratios from a workspace. Neither model is a measurement of the real beams: the default is the geometric-optics convolution of the focal profile, and VisRadProfile is VisRad's model. Use VisRadProfile to reproduce a VisRad calculation. The law was identified on NIF beams at f/20.755 and VisRad's default lens ratio, and checked at envelope ratios 1.2 and the default.\n\nSmaller differences of the beam model:\n\nSpot rotation. VisRad's rotation 0 is the horizontal of the quad, which is turned from the port frame of the beam by 1.6–5.3° depending on the cone; the readers and writers convert. A positive rotation turns the spot clockwise as seen looking along the beam (rotation_sense = :beam, the default): VisRad's deposition at ±30°, 60° and 90° on ten beams matches it within 0.04°, and the other sense is off by 60°.\nThe beam axis. VisRad's axis runs from the port on the chamber wall through the Reference Point; Briareus's is parallel to the port direction. The difference at focus is under 1 µm for millimeter-scale aims.\nOccultation. Briareus tests each f-cone direction at each point, or, with VisRadProfile, the beam axis through each point. VisRad's shadows are magnified: the shadow ray of a point leans away from the axis in proportion to its position in the spot, as if from a source 12–19 mm upstream of focus, and each beam is then rescaled to its full power. Behind a washer or a sphere 5 mm upstream of a foil, the axis test leaves 30% rms per element against VisRad. VisRad also puts up to 7 × 10⁻⁴ TW/cm² on LEH washers that Briareus leaves dark.\nBeam totals. VisRad rescales the deposited total of each beam: to its full power on surfaces that catch the whole beam, at any incidence, and to a share within ±2.5% of the geometric one when part of the beam hits nothing. VisRadProfile normalizes each transverse plane instead, which puts 0.2% too much power on a foil at 40° incidence and 2.2% at 78°.\nCapture/clearance report. VisRad's report applies the TaLIS margins as fixed widths around its 1% and 10% cones (0.5 mm), with no angular allowance. Its cones match Briareus's envelope (best-focus contour grown by the f-cone) to within 0.05 mm, up to 10 mm from focus. Its incident angle is that of the steepest ray of the f-cone (tilt + 1.38°), as in talis_check. talis_check adds the 0.5° insertion and assembly tolerance to the clearances and the capture margin, where the facility applies it.\nLaser reflectivity. VisRad can reflect laser light from a surface; Briareus absorbs all of it by default and warns when a workspace object reflects.\nUnconverted light. Under VisRad's \"Fraction of Total Beam Power\" model, the power column is the total of the 3ω, 2ω and 1ω cones; the readers take the 3ω share, P(1 - f_G - f_R). Briareus's unconverted-light check (unconverted_light_check) treats the 2ω and 1ω light separately.","title":"The beam away from focus"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#Radiation-on-curved-surfaces","page":"For VisRad users","text":"Briareus's radiation model integrates over the exact surface a component carries: the cylinder and spheres of hohlraum, and the cylinders, cones and spheres that visrad_target fits to remeshed CAD (fit). On flat facets, the exchange between neighboring elements is misstated by an amount of order one, which shows in the flux next to a laser spot; on the curved surface an integrating sphere is uniform within round-off (Integrating sphere). The research notes do not record VisRad's view-factor quadrature, so how much of this difference appears in a VisRad calculation is not known. When rebuilding a VisRad primitive by hand, note that VisRad's grid size of a sphere, cylinder or cylindrical hohlraum is that of its outer wall, with the inner wall inward by the wall thickness.","title":"Radiation on curved surfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#Closure-and-visibility","page":"For VisRad users","text":"A discretized view-factor matrix closes only to its quadrature error, and at high albedo the cavity multiplies that error. For a target declared closed (apertures over every opening), Briareus rescales the view factors so that every row sums to one while reciprocity holds (enforce_closure!). hohlraum declares its targets closed; visrad_target does not, and takes closed = true once the apertures close the cavity (open_fraction checks for leaks). Whether VisRad applies a comparable correction is not recorded in the research notes.\n\nVisibility between two elements is determined from their centroids by default (visibility = :centroid); :sampled traces a grid of rays where the centroid rays and probe rays disagree, and is the default for CAD and VisRad targets, whose LEH washers and rims partly shadow elements.","title":"Closure and visibility"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#Element-values","page":"For VisRad users","text":"VisRad's direct laser power source is an element average: centroid values differ from it by up to 13% next to the peak of a spot. Briareus deposits the integral over each element. visrad_deposition multiplies VisRad's values by the element areas, so the two codes compare in TW per element.","title":"Element values"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#What-VisRad-has-that-Briareus-does-not","page":"For VisRad users","text":"From the import warnings and ROADMAP (\"VisRad import: what is skipped\", \"CAD\", \"GUI requests\"):\n\nSTEP CAD import (Briareus reads STL and the CAD bundled in workspaces), and the NIF CMT XML.\nMost of VisRad's primitives: cylinders, cones, disks, boxes, polygons, halfraums, rugby and slotted hohlraums, surfaces of revolution, multi-element objects, wall thickness and partial spheres. Briareus provides mesh builders for most of these shapes, but read_visrad_workspace skips them with a warning.\nFrom workspaces: unconverted 2ω and 1ω beams, time-dependent beam powers and energy-normalized beams, DPR (split OMEGA beams), pointing toward a diagnostic port or in target-mount frames, and the time-dependent tables of albedo, conversion, lengths and power sources. Briareus provides separate time-dependent models (above), but does not import VisRad's.\nLaser reflection, and power self-emission.\nThe blend toward a square beam far from focus (zero for NIF's beams).\n\nWhat Briareus adds (gradient-based optimization, laser–plasma corrections, the moving wall and closing holes, a synthetic Dante, worst-case clearance and NIF's target-configuration rules) is summarized on the home page.","title":"What VisRad has that Briareus does not"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/visrad/#Porting-a-VisRad-study","page":"For VisRad users","text":"The code below is a sketch of the usual sequence, not run by the documentation build: it needs a workspace. File names and component names are placeholders.\n\n1. Read the workspace. With a template beam set, the pointing, power and spots of the workspace are applied to the NIF beams by name, which keeps their cones as group:\n\nusing Briareus\n\nws = read_visrad_workspace(\"study.vrw\"; template = nif_beams())\nws.objects                  # components: name, kind, folder, albedo, conversion, flags\nbeams = ws.beams            # 3ω power, aim, defocus and spot per beam\nprofile = visrad_profile(ws)   # VisRad's beam model, with this workspace's envelopes\n\n2. Check the deposition against VisRad. On a workspace that VisRad has computed, a target on the VisRad elements (no remeshing) takes VisRad's per-element deposition. visrad_deposition maps only onto components on those elements, so this target is used for the comparison only; step 3 builds the radiation target. VisRad saves a computed workspace as JSON, which read_visrad_workspace reads as it reads the text format. VisRad deposits on a CAD triangle whichever way it is wound, and CAD windings are often inconsistent, so the comparison target takes CAD meshes two-sided (visrad_target warns about inconsistent windings otherwise):\n\nt0 = visrad_target(ws; two_sided = true)\ndep = deposit(DepositionOperator(t0, beams; profile), beams)\nP = visrad_deposition(ws, t0)          # TW per element, from VisRad's table\nwall = elements(t0, :Hohlraum_wall)    # the wall object's name in the workspace\nsum(dep.element[wall]), sum(P[wall])\n\n3. Build the radiation target. CAD can hold far more triangles than a view-factor matrix should. Select the parts that bound the cavity, repair their normals, remesh them and keep what bounds the free space around a point in the gas:\n\nt = visrad_target(ws; select = o -> o.folder == \"Hohlraum\",\n                  orient = true, remesh = 0.5, inside = (0.0, 0.0, 2.0),\n                  materials = Dict(\"Hohlraum_wall\" => nif_gold()))\nopen_fraction(t).fraction    # pass closed = true above only if apertures close the cavity\nmodel = RadiationModel(t)\n\n4. Solve and analyze.\n\nop = DepositionOperator(t, beams; profile)    # or the default f-cone model\nsol = solve(model, deposit(op, beams))\ncapsule = elements(t, :Capsule)\na = legendre_moments(sol.H, t, capsule)\na[3] / a[1]                                   # P2/P0\n\n5. Time-dependent powers. VisRad's power file becomes a pulse per beam, keyed by beam name, and the drive history evaluates the beams at each time (every beam needs a column in the file):\n\npulses = read_visrad_powers(\"powers.csv\"; template = beams)\nbeams_at(time) = with_pulses(beams, pulses, time; by = :beam)\nhistory = drive_history(model, op, beams_at, 0.0:0.25:9.0; observe = :Capsule)\nhistory.moments[3, :] ./ history.moments[1, :]   # P2/P0 against time\n\n6. Optimize and write back. Re-point and re-balance by cone, then write the design into a copy of the original workspace, whose components, spots and settings stay VisRad's, and as an SST file:\n\npointing = GroupPointing(beams; fields = (:z, :power), relative = true)\nscenario = Scenario(t, pointing; observe = :Capsule, deposition_kw = (; profile))\nx0 = initial_parameters(pointing)\ndrive = mean_flux(evaluate(scenario, x0))\nobjective = symmetry_objective(; drive)   # the direct penalty is on `observe`\nlower, upper = bounds(pointing, x0; power = 0.3)\nresult = optimize_pointing(scenario, objective; x0, lower, upper)\ndesign = apply(pointing, result.x)\nwrite_visrad_workspace(\"study_repointed.vrw\", \"study.vrw\", design)\nwrite_visrad_sst(\"study_repointed_sst.csv\", design)\n\n7. Benchmark a case in both codes. For a target built in Briareus (target, here a hohlraum with a component named capsule), the kit holds what VisRad needs to rebuild the case and Briareus's results; VisRad's element and power summary exports are then compared quantity by quantity:\n\nkit = write_visrad_kit(\"kit\", target, design; observe = :capsule)\n# ... build and run the case in VisRad from kit/README.txt, export its tables ...\ncompare_visrad(\"kit\"; elements = \"capsule_elements.txt\",\n               power_summary = \"power_summary.psd\",\n               names = Dict(\"hohlraum_barrel\" => \"wall\"), io = stdout)\n\nThe research notes (\"VisRad interchange formats\" and the sections after it) record how each format detail was established, its confidence, and the items still to confirm on real files (\"To verify with real files at work\").","title":"Porting a VisRad study"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/backlighter_sweep/#Backlighter-placement-sweep","page":"Backlighter placement sweep","text":"An x-ray radiograph needs a backlighter: a foil heated by a few laser beams, placed behind the object on the line of sight of the diagnostic, whose x rays cross the object to the detector. Where the foil goes is a trade. Close to the object, the foil subtends a wide cone, and the x-ray bundle from it can clip hardware near the line of sight; the beams that heat it also pass closer to that hardware. Far from it, the emission from the foil spreads over a larger solid angle before it reaches the object.\n\nThis tutorial designs the pointing of an OMEGA-style backlighter in foil coordinates, then moves the foil along the line of sight with its beams following and checks every step for beam clearance, an unobstructed line of sight and the power the foil captures. It introduces frames (Frame, foil_frame), pointing parameterized in a frame (FramePointing) and placement sweeps (placement_sweep).\n\nThe target is a mock-up with generic dimensions and is not a fielded design. It consists of a 2 mm square gold foil, a 0.43 mm radius plastic sphere at target-chamber center (TCC) as the object, and between them an aperture plate, a gold washer whose bore limits what the detector sees around the foil.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)","title":"Backlighter placement sweep"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/backlighter_sweep/#The-foil-and-its-frame","page":"Backlighter placement sweep","text":"The line of sight runs from TCC toward the detector at polar angle 90° and azimuth 0°, in the frame of omega_ports. backlighter_foil places the foil on the far side, a standoff D from TCC, facing the detector. The same placement as a frame, foil_frame, has its origin at the foil center, its x and y axes along the foil edges and its z axis along the front normal, toward the detector. Local coordinates (u v 0) are points on the foil face.\n\nlos = (90.0, 0.0)\nD0 = 8.0                       # standoff of the design (mm)\nfoil_edge = 2.0\nF = foil_frame(los; standoff = D0)\nfoil_part = backlighter_foil(los; standoff = D0, width = foil_edge, n = 8)\nF\nFrame(origin (-8, 0, 0), x (6.123e-17, 0, -1), y (0, 1, 0), z (1, 0, 0))\n\nThe aperture plate sits 2.5 mm from TCC toward the foil, normal to the line of sight, with a 1.0 mm bore radius and a 1.5 mm outer radius. Its frame is the port_frame of the line of sight moved to that point, so the plate mesh, built about the z axis, lands across the line of sight.\n\nd = port_vector(los...)\nX0, bore, outer = 2.5, 1.0, 1.2\nP = port_frame(los...; origin = -X0 .* d)\nwasher = Component(:washer, transform(annulus_mesh(bore, outer, 0.0; nr = 2, nphi = 64), P.R, P.origin),\n                   gold(); two_sided = true)\ncapsule = Component(:capsule, sphere_mesh(0.43; ntheta = 12, nphi = 24), ablator())\ntarget = Target([foil_part, washer, capsule])\nTarget with 3 components, 672 elements, 912 triangles\n  backlighter    surface  Au           128 elements  area    4.0000 mm²  (two-sided)\n  washer         surface  Au           256 elements  area    1.3801 mm²  (two-sided)\n  capsule        surface  ablator      288 elements  area    2.2841 mm²\n","title":"The foil and its frame"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/backlighter_sweep/#Beams-pointed-in-the-foil-frame","page":"Backlighter placement sweep","text":"The beams come from the ports on the detector side, 20° to 40° from the line of sight: three symmetric pairs, at 25.0°, 33.2° and 38.5°. Each delivers 0.4 TW in an SG4 phase-plate spot (omega_spot); the powers are illustrative. They start aimed at a ring of radius 0.3 mm on the foil, written in the foil frame and mapped to the chamber by to_chamber.\n\nbeams = [b for b in omega_beams(cones = :all, spot = omega_spot(:SG4), power = 0.4)\n         if 20 < acosd(dot(b.port, d)) < 40]\nring(i) = (0.3 * cospi(2i / length(beams)), 0.3 * sinpi(2i / length(beams)), 0.0)\nbeams = [remake(b; aim = to_chamber(F, ring(i))) for (i, b) in enumerate(beams)]\n[(b.name, round(acosd(dot(b.port, d)); digits = 1)) for b in beams]\n6-element Vector{Tuple{String, Float64}}:\n (\"B11\", 38.5)\n (\"B20\", 38.5)\n (\"B31\", 25.0)\n (\"B40\", 25.0)\n (\"B41\", 33.2)\n (\"B50\", 33.2)\n\nA FramePointing makes the aim of each beam a pair of parameters (u v) in the foil frame. Each beam is a group of one (by = :beam), so there are twelve parameters, and each maps to the chamber as R(u v 0) + o: the parameters keep their meaning wherever the foil goes.\n\npointing = FramePointing(beams, F; by = :beam, fields = (:u, :v))\nscenario = Scenario(target, pointing)\nScenario(672 elements, 6 beams, 12 parameters, 0 observed elements)\n\nThe objective is the uniformity of the absorbed flux over the front of the foil, the squared rms nonuniformity, plus the squared fraction of the power of the beams that misses it. It refers only to the foil elements and never to chamber positions, so it serves at any placement.\n\nfunction foil_objective(ev)\n    t = ev.scenario.target\n    front = [e for e in elements(t, :backlighter) if t.elem_side[e] == 1]\n    absorbed = ev.deposition.element[front]\n    area = t.elem_area[front]\n    return rms_nonuniformity(absorbed ./ area, area)^2 +\n           (1 - sum(absorbed) / ev.deposition.delivered)^2\nend\n\nresult = optimize_pointing(scenario, foil_objective; iterations = 100)\nPointing optimization converged: objective 2.088 → 0.1955\n  28 iterations, 34 evaluations, 12 parameters, none at a bound\n\nThe optimizer spreads the spots across the face, each within the default ±1 mm box about its start:\n\nfor (name, u, v) in zip(beams, result.x[1:2:end], result.x[2:2:end])\n    @printf(\"%s  u = %+.3f mm  v = %+.3f mm\\n\", name.name, u, v)\nend\ndesigned = FramePointing(result.beams, F; by = :beam, fields = (:u, :v))\nFramePointing{String}(Beam{Float64}[Beam(B11, θ=58.85°, φ=23.94°, 0.4 TW, aim=(-8, 0.6231, -0.5553), defocus=0 mm), Beam(B20, θ=58.85°, φ=336.06°, 0.4 TW, aim=(-8, 0.6245, 0.5058), defocus=0 mm), Beam(B31, θ=98.75°, φ=23.46°, 0.4 TW, aim=(-8, -0.1958, 0.6834), defocus=0 mm), Beam(B40, θ=98.75°, φ=336.54°, 0.4 TW, aim=(-8, -0.6877, 0.1545), defocus=0 mm), Beam(B41, θ=121.15°, φ=12.06°, 0.4 TW, aim=(-8, -0.5899, -0.6429), defocus=0 mm), Beam(B50, θ=121.15°, φ=347.94°, 0.4 TW, aim=(-8, 0.05307, -0.1559), defocus=0 mm)], [\"B11\", \"B20\", \"B31\", \"B40\", \"B41\", \"B50\"], [[1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [6]], [:u, :v], false, Frame[Frame(origin (-8, 0, 0), x (6.123e-17, 0, -1), y (0, 1, 0), z (1, 0, 0)), Frame(origin (-8, 0, 0), x (6.123e-17, 0, -1), y (0, 1, 0), z (1, 0, 0)), Frame(origin (-8, 0, 0), x (6.123e-17, 0, -1), y (0, 1, 0), z (1, 0, 0)), Frame(origin (-8, 0, 0), x (6.123e-17, 0, -1), y (0, 1, 0), z (1, 0, 0)), Frame(origin (-8, 0, 0), x (6.123e-17, 0, -1), y (0, 1, 0), z (1, 0, 0)), Frame(origin (-8, 0, 0), x (6.123e-17, 0, -1), y (0, 1, 0), z (1, 0, 0))], StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64}[[0.55530720827297, 0.6231052474457531, 0.0], [-0.5058294331751677, 0.624482169779129, 0.0], [-0.6834035503372603, -0.19580616071898407, 0.0], [-0.15445380272587647, -0.6876812831109019, 0.0], [0.6428502512435397, -0.5899250419906713, 0.0], [0.15593492954810964, 0.05307401993342154, 0.0]], [\"B11\", \"B20\", \"B31\", \"B40\", \"B41\", \"B50\"])","title":"Beams pointed in the foil frame"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/backlighter_sweep/#Sweeping-the-standoff","page":"Backlighter placement sweep","text":"placement_sweep moves the foil along the z axis of its frame, toward TCC for a positive shift, with every beam following (follow = true). Each step translates the foil and the aims of the beams by the same vector, rebuilds the target and runs the checks. Here the checks are the beam clearance of clearance to every part but the foil, the line_of_sight check through the disk of the sphere, and the fraction of the power of each beam that the foil absorbs. The clearance runs with its quick settings and without the NIF clearance wings, which are engineering defaults for NIF. Standoffs run from 4 to 9 mm.\n\nstandoffs = 4.0:0.5:9.0\nchecks = (; clearance_kw = (; targets = (:backlighter,), wings = (), nangles = 8, nspot = 31),\n          los, los_kw = (; object = :capsule, radius = 0.43),\n          capture = :backlighter, capture_kw = (; resolution = 0.6))\nsweep = placement_sweep(target, designed, :backlighter, D0 .- standoffs; frame = F, axis = :z,\n                        follow = true, checks...)\nSweepResult: 11 of 11 steps\n     value  clearance mm  worst beam / part        fail review  LOS      blocked  covered  captured  objective\n        +4       -0.8990  B50 / washer               6      0  blocked   0.0073   1.0000   0.70202  –\n      +3.5       -0.7324  B50 / washer               6      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.81816  –\n        +3       -0.9065  B31 / washer               4      2  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.68413  –\n      +2.5       -1.0114  B31 / washer               3      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.67139  –\n        +2       -0.9339  B31 / washer               1      1  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.78081  –\n      +1.5       -0.7731  B31 / washer               1      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.87349  –\n        +1       -0.6074  B31 / washer               1      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n      +0.5       -0.4383  B31 / washer               1      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n        +0       -0.2687  B31 / washer               0      1  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n      -0.5       -0.0963  B31 / washer               0      1  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n        -1       +0.0763  B31 / washer               0      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n\nThe rows list, for each shift D_0 - D, the least worst-case clearance between the envelope of a beam and the hardware (negative where the envelope overlaps it under the error set), the beam and part it belongs to, the failing and review pairs, the line of sight, and the least captured fraction:\n\nsweep\nSweepResult: 11 of 11 steps\n     value  clearance mm  worst beam / part        fail review  LOS      blocked  covered  captured  objective\n        +4       -0.8990  B50 / washer               6      0  blocked   0.0073   1.0000   0.70202  –\n      +3.5       -0.7324  B50 / washer               6      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.81816  –\n        +3       -0.9065  B31 / washer               4      2  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.68413  –\n      +2.5       -1.0114  B31 / washer               3      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.67139  –\n        +2       -0.9339  B31 / washer               1      1  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.78081  –\n      +1.5       -0.7731  B31 / washer               1      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.87349  –\n        +1       -0.6074  B31 / washer               1      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n      +0.5       -0.4383  B31 / washer               1      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n        +0       -0.2687  B31 / washer               0      1  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n      -0.5       -0.0963  B31 / washer               0      1  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n        -1       +0.0763  B31 / washer               0      0  clear     0.0000   1.0000   0.93572  –\n\nTwo limits appear. At 4 mm the line of sight is blocked: the x-ray bundle from the corners of the foil widens past the bore of the aperture plate. The beams set the tighter limit. The axis of a beam crosses the plate plane about (D - X_0) tanalpha from the line of sight, where alpha is the angle of its port from it, shifted by its aim on the foil. A 25° beam aimed 0.68 mm off the foil center (B31) passes closest, and its worst-case envelope clears the plate only beyond about 8.8 mm. At the design standoff of 8 mm it still overlaps under the error set, which makes it a review pair: some light of its displaced envelope reaches the plate. The captured fraction stays the same from 7 to 9 mm, where the nominal beams miss the plate, since the foil and its beams move as one; closer in, the plate takes part of the light.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 360))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"standoff D (mm)\", ylabel = \"least clearance (mm)\",\n           title = \"Beams and the aperture plate\")\nclear = [r.min_clearance for r in sweep.rows]\nhlines!(ax1, [0.0], color = :gray60, linestyle = :dash)\nscatterlines!(ax1, collect(standoffs), clear, color = :black)\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"standoff D (mm)\", ylabel = \"fraction\",\n           title = \"Line of sight and capture\")\nscatterlines!(ax2, collect(standoffs), [r.blocked_fraction for r in sweep.rows],\n              label = \"rays blocked\")\nscatterlines!(ax2, collect(standoffs), [r.min_captured for r in sweep.rows],\n              label = \"least captured\")\naxislegend(ax2, position = :rc)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe rows can be written for a spreadsheet with write_sweep_csv. placement_sweep also re-optimizes the pointing at each step when given an optimize named tuple, starting from the optimum of the previous step; with the beams following the foil rigidly, as here, the design does not change, so that is left off.","title":"Sweeping the standoff"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/backlighter_sweep/#The-line-of-sight-threshold","page":"Backlighter placement sweep","text":"The steps of the sweep bracket the standoff where the line of sight opens; its exact value follows from similar triangles. The widest ray of the bundle runs from a corner of the foil, at wsqrt2 from the line of sight (w the half-width), through the opposite rim of the object disk of radius a. At the aperture plate, a fraction s = X_0D of the way from the disk to the foil, it lies (1 - s)a + swsqrt2 from the axis, and it reaches the rim of the bore, rho, at\n\nD^* = X_0fracwsqrt2 - arho - a\n\nw, a = foil_edge / 2, 0.43\nDstar = X0 * (w * sqrt(2) - a) / (bore - a)\nedge = placement_sweep(target, designed, :backlighter, D0 .- [Dstar + 1e-6, Dstar - 1e-6];\n                       frame = F, axis = :z, follow = true, los, los_kw = checks.los_kw)\n@printf(\"D* = %.6f mm: %s just outside, %s just inside\\n\", Dstar,\n        edge.rows[1].los_clear ? \"clear\" : \"blocked\", edge.rows[2].los_clear ? \"clear\" : \"blocked\")\nD* = 4.316726 mm: clear just outside, blocked just inside\n\nThe followed aims move by exactly the shift along the foil normal:\n\nshifts = [maximum(norm(b.aim - (c.aim + r.value * F.R[:, 3])) for (b, c) in zip(r.beams, result.beams))\n          for r in sweep.rows]\n@printf(\"largest aim error over the sweep: %.1e mm\\n\", maximum(shifts))\nlargest aim error over the sweep: 0.0e+00 mm","title":"The line-of-sight threshold"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/backlighter_sweep/#What-this-checks","page":"Backlighter placement sweep","text":"The line of sight opens at the similar-triangles standoff D^*: the check through the disk of the sphere is clear a nanometer outside it and blocked a nanometer inside, because the extreme ray of the bundle meets a vertex of the bore polygon. That ray runs from a foil corner through the rim of the disk, both of which are sample points.\nThe aims of the followed beams equal the optimized aims plus the shift along the foil normal at every step, exactly.\nThe captured fraction is the same, to the five digits shown, at every step where the nominal beams miss the aperture plate, since the foil and its beams translate together.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/radiation/#Radiation","page":"Radiation","text":"view_factors computes occlusion-aware view factors (the exact point-to-polygon kernel near the emitter, adaptive toward shared edges; about 0.4 s for a 3,000-element hohlraum), and solve the gray diffuse radiosity. For closed enclosures, closed = true removes discretization leakage while keeping reciprocity. Wall albedo can be constant, tabulated in time, or the Marshak wall-loss scaling WallLossAlbedo, iterated to self-consistency with the local radiation temperature. flux_response precomputes the linear map from deposition to capsule flux, so optimizers never re-solve. Large models are never densified: above 10⁴ elements the view factors are a Float32Matrix with iterative solves, above 1.5 × 10⁴ an HMatrix. spectral_solve adds multigroup radiosity about a gray solution, with the laser-produced gold M-band.\n\ndrive_history follows the drive in time from a PulseShape per cone, quad or beam. Each wall element loses energy by its temperature history and re-emits stored energy when the drive falls; the wall moves (WallMotion), the entrance holes close as prescribed (GapClosure) or computed (LipClosure), and the capsule absorbs on a shrinking radius (CapsuleAlbedo). dante1 and dante_unfold record the drive as the Dante instrument would. retune_drive finds the power histories that hold a drive quantity through time under per-key power limits. The quantity is the modes of a capsule (CapsuleModes), the x-ray flux of a flat face (FaceFlux) or the laser irradiance of a backlighter foil (FoilIrradiance), held either to the values of a reference shot or uniform at a prescribed level. With NIF bundle 31 down, retuning the other 46 quads takes the mode-1 drive asymmetry from 2% to 0.02% at the peak.","title":"Radiation"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.Float32Matrix","page":"Radiation","text":"Float32Matrix(A::AbstractMatrix)\nFloat32Matrix(undef, m, n)\n\nA dense matrix stored in Float32 that behaves as a Float64 matrix. Indexing returns the stored value widened, and products with Float64 vectors and matrices (*, mul!, also transposed) accumulate in Float64. A product is therefore exactly the Float64 product with the matrix Float64.(A.data), and the only error against a Float64 original is the rounding of each entry (relative ≤ 2⁻²⁴ ≈ 6e-8). The type halves the memory of a view-factor matrix. It is used by view_factors(target; storage = Float32), and by RadiationModel by default for more than Briareus.LARGE_MODEL[] elements (research notes, \"Large radiation models\").\n\nMatrix–vector products are threaded over row blocks (column blocks when transposed) and are memory-bound. Matrix–matrix products widen column panels of the matrix to Float64 and pass them to BLAS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Float32Matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.closure_error-Tuple{AbstractMatrix}","page":"Radiation","text":"closure_error(F) -> Vector\n\n1 - Σⱼ Fᵢⱼ per element, the fraction of the emission of each element that leaves the geometry entirely. It is zero for every element of a closed enclosure, up to discretization error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.closure_error"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.view_factors-Tuple{Target}","page":"Radiation","text":"view_factors(target; near = 3.0, level = 1, visibility = target.visibility, visibility_grid = 4,\n             storage = Float64, tol, eta, leaf, maxrank) -> Matrix{Float64}, Float32Matrix or HMatrix\n\nDense view-factor matrix F[i, j], the fraction of diffuse radiation leaving element i that arrives at element j. Reciprocity Aᵢ Fᵢⱼ = Aⱼ Fⱼᵢ holds exactly by construction, and rows sum to one for a closed enclosure (counting apertures); see closure_error. storage = Float32 assembles into a Float32Matrix instead, with half the memory, each factor rounded to single precision and products in Float64. storage = :hmatrix assembles a hierarchical HMatrix, with near cluster pairs dense and far ones of uniform visibility low rank (keywords tol, eta, leaf, maxrank; Briareus.HMATRIX_DEFAULTS). On a hohlraum its assembly and storage grow as N^1.3–1.6 instead of N², and at 17–29k elements it is 2–3× faster and 5–8× smaller.\n\nFlat elements use the exact differential-area-to-polygon view factor onto the other element, integrated over the emitter. For pairs closer than near element sizes, the integral uses 4^level sub-triangle points; the element size is √2 × the largest centroid-to-vertex distance, the side of a square element. This rule stays accurate for elements that share an edge, such as at the wall–cap corner of a hohlraum. Beyond that distance the integral uses the centroid, averaged over the two directions. A near pair of flat elements (not on a curved surface) that lie wholly in front of each other takes their exact exchange instead, the double contour integral A_i F_ij = frac12πoint_ioint_j ln r dmathbfl_icdot dmathbfl_j over their outlines, to about 1e-13. For an element folded across several facets, the outlines are those of the facets themselves. Elements that share an edge then cost about 100× less than by the point rule, which also leaves an error of about 5e-4 on them.\n\nElements of a component with a CurvedSurface are integrated over the curved patch, with quadrature points on the true surface with its normals and areas. Between two curved elements the integral is point to point: 4^level sub-triangle points per triangle when near, one per triangle out to three times that distance, and the projected centroid beyond. Onto a planar partner the integral uses the exact polygon. Elements of a DiskSurface are exact polar sectors, planar receivers whose arcs are divided finely. Some near pairs lie about one axis and wholly in front of each other: a sector with another sector or with a chord rectangle of a cylinder seen from inside. Examples are an end cap and the wall at its corner, the annuli and slot of a waist gap, and an LEH disk and its bore. Such a pair takes the double contour above with the arcs exact, to about 1e-12, where the point rule left 1e-3 to 4e-2. A curved concave element also has a view of itself, which gives the diagonal Fᵢᵢ. Flat facets standing in for a curved wall misrepresent the exchange between nearby elements by an amount of order one relative to its size, which matters next to a concentrated source such as a laser spot.\n\nElements of more than two facets (remeshed CAD, remesh_mesh) are not integrated facet by facet. A flat element receives through its outline, which gives exactly the sum over its facets within round-off. Where the tangent plane of the point cuts the element, it receives piece by piece, each piece being its facets in one plane. Near pairs integrate over cells that lump the facets of the emitter, of side size / (2^(level+2) √2) before refining toward the receiver, or 2^(level+1) on a curved surface. A pair then costs a few times a two-facet pair whatever the facet count of the emitter. The facet count of the receiver still matters, since the factor of each point sums its outline, or its pieces when the tangent plane of the point cuts it. An element on a finely faceted curved wall, with hundreds of sliver facets, tens of pieces and a jagged outline of hundreds of edges, therefore costs tens of times more per near pair. Such parts should be given their fitted surface (curved_components), and they are then integrated on the curved surface instead.\n\nVisibility is a single centroid-to-centroid ray between the flat facets. The facets of a curved surface (cylinder, sphere or cone) do not block a ray between two points inside it, since the true surface cannot. On curved elements the centroid facing test is relaxed by the spread of the true normals across the flat facet. Pairs that the curved patches exchange across are therefore kept, since a general triangle on a cylinder has its plane tilted axially by about its size over 2R. The point kernels apply the exact facing per point.\n\nA pair with only a partial view of each other, past the rim of an entrance-hole bore or a gap or around the capsule, is all or nothing by its centroid ray. visibility = :sampled estimates the visible fraction instead; it is the default for targets with rims (cad_target, visrad_target). Up to four probe rays per element, from points near its extremes to the centroid of the other element, test whether the pair straddles an occluder. If they all agree with the centroid ray, the factor is identical to the :centroid one. Otherwise, rays between the visibility_grid² sample points of each element give the kernel-weighted visible fraction, which scales the unobstructed factor and keeps reciprocity. The samples lump the area of the element into a g × g grid of its tangent plane. On an element folded across more than two facets (remeshed CAD), the samples are put on its facets, and its facets do not occlude them. On a hohlraum with 0.1 mm LEH bores and a 20 µm waist gap at 0.5 mm, the sampling brings the largest row-sum error before balancing from 1.0% to 0.19% on the walls and from 5.9% to 0.7% on the LEH apertures, for 2–4× the time. Features narrower than a sample cell, such as the slot of the gap, are not resolved (research notes, \"Partial visibility\"). Against a Monte Carlo radiosity on the same facets, it reduces the balanced capsule a₂/a₀ error there from 0.51 to 0.31 pp at 0.5 mm, and from 0.30 to 0.14 pp at 0.25 mm (research notes, \"Monte Carlo radiosity reference\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.view_factors"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.HMatrix","page":"Radiation","text":"HMatrix\n\nView factors in hierarchical form, from view_factors(target; storage = :hmatrix) and by default from RadiationModel above Briareus.HMATRIX_MODEL[] elements. The form stores the symmetric exchange matrix G = diag(A) F (Gᵢⱼ = Aᵢ Fᵢⱼ), once per unordered pair so that reciprocity is exact, partitioned over a cluster tree of the elements. Then F = diag(A)⁻¹ S G S with a diagonal balancing S, and enforce_closure! updates only S. The type is an AbstractMatrix{Float64}. Products with Float64 vectors and matrices (*, mul!, also transposed, and with dual vectors) accumulate in Float64, which is all that the iterative radiosity path uses. Indexing one entry searches the blocks, for inspection only, and Matrix(H) expands the matrix by products with the identity.\n\nClusters split the elements by the surface they lie on (the curved surface of a component, or the component). They then bisect the centroids along the longest axis of their box down to leaf elements. No cluster therefore mixes capsule and wall, or cap and wall at a corner; such blocks seldom compress, and their low-rank error falls on the small rows. A pair of clusters whose boxes are eta × the larger diameter apart is a candidate for low rank, decided block by block:\n\nRows and columns whose elements face no centroid of the other cluster are zero, by a box test. A block that a convex closed part, such as a capsule, hides wholly is also zero. The test is that the inscribed ball blocks every segment between the boxes, by a cone test from each corner.\nThe shaft between the boxes, their convex hull, is searched for facets that could occlude a segment between them (the shaft culling of Haines & Wallace). The search excludes the facets of a curved surface that both clusters lie inside, of a convex part that one of them lies on, and of a plane that both lie in front of or in. An empty shaft makes every facing pair wholly visible, so its entries need no ray, and the block is cross-approximated. The approximation is ACA with partial pivoting on the exact pair kernel, recompressed by SVD to tol relative Frobenius error and checked on sampled entries. A block that does not compress within maxrank terms is refined.\nA shaft with facets of only one convex part, such as the capsule with both clusters outside its circumscribed ball, resolves each pair exactly by the balls of the part. A segment that misses the circumscribed ball is visible, one through the inscribed ball is hidden, and only the few between take a ray. With the resulting mask the block is zero, low rank without further rays, or refined with the mask down to dense leaf pairs at a shadow edge.\nAny other occupied shaft is refined down to leaf pairs. A leaf pair whose shaft contains facets of up to 8 parts with a certificate is decided in the same way, pair by pair and exactly as the ray would decide it. The certificates are:\nfor planar parts such as washers, LEH caps and discs, the point where the segment crosses the plane of the part, inside or outside its facets with margin;\nfor a cylinder or cone part, its axisymmetric bounds. A prism of facets parallel to its axis, such as a wall of chord rectangles, is also decided by the side lines of its cross-section. A segment qualifies when its part within the axial range of the prism stays inside the half-plane of every side, as a segment through a waist gap onto the wall does;\nfor a convex part, its balls or the plane of an end element lying on it.\nOther leaf pairs take the visibility ray of every pair. Wholly hidden pairs give zero, wholly visible ones a cross approximation, and partly visible ones, such as at a rim or with :sampled fractions, stay dense. With visibility = :sampled the certificates resolve the whole bundle of sample rays of a pair at once, with margins grown by the spread of the samples, and otherwise each of its rays. The fractions are those of view_factors.\nPairs that the facing slack admits, whose rays start farther off curved facets, are judged on their ray ends when the exclusions of the shaft hold with that margin, and take the ray otherwise.\nRows cut by the facing test, at the terminator of a sphere, are refined against the children of the column cluster beside the low-rank rest. They are low rank on the rows that face a child wholly, and dense against a leaf or when few. A small block with only a few facing pairs is stored on those pairs.\n\nNear pairs are dense (Float32), with the same entries as view_factors (_vf_pair). Blocks whose entries are all zero are not stored.\n\nOn the hohlraum presets (10–29k elements) the default tol = 1e-6 changes the capsule moments by ≤ 1e-8 and the incident flux by ≤ 2e-6 relative per element against the Float32 matrix or a tighter HMatrix (research notes, \"Hierarchical view factors\" and, for the surface-split cluster tree and tol, \"The H-matrix's N^1.5 remainder\"; for the zero blocks and cut rows, \"H-matrix near field\"; for the visibility certificates, \"H-matrix visibility certificates\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.HMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.Enclosure","page":"Radiation","text":"Enclosure\n\nResult of enclosure: the reduced target, and how it was cut from source.\n\ntarget: the components of source cut to their kept patches (components left empty are dropped).\nindex[i]: the element of source that element i of target is.\nkept[e]: whether element e of source passed the selection. Both sides of a two-sided patch are kept when either passes, so target can contain sides with kept false.\nflux[e]: the relative flux on element e of source that determined the selection (NaN for elements beyond radius, which cast no rays); see enclosure.\nescape: the fraction of the rays from the point that leave the bounding sphere or reach infinity, which is the solid-angle fraction of the openings seen from the point.\nopen_area: Σ over kept elements of area × the fraction of the rays of the element that escape (mm²). By reciprocity this estimates the open area of the cavity, that is, its entrance holes where no aperture or window closes them.\nleak_area: the same sum over rays that end on dropped elements (mm²), which is the radiation that the kept set sends to surfaces that the reduced target no longer has.\nreturn_area: the radiation that those surfaces would send back, Σ over kept elements of area × the mean over the rays of the element of albedo × flux at the dropped element hit (mm², in units of the reference flux). It is the error of the cut, to compare with open_area, which leaves through the openings, and with the area of the cavity.\nback_area: the sum over rays that end on one-sided surfaces seen from behind (mm²), which also absorb without radiating in the full target.\nback: the fraction of the rays from the point whose first hit was a one-sided surface seen from behind. It is 1 for a point inside the capsule, and otherwise a sign of wrongly oriented surfaces.\nrays: the rays cast per element.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Enclosure"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.cad_target-Tuple{Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"cad_target(parts; orient = true, toward = (0, 0, 0), inside = nothing,\n           remesh = nothing, coarsen = nothing, fit = remesh !== nothing, material = gold(),\n           two_sided = false, visibility = :sampled, closed = false, enclosure_kw = (;)) -> Target\n\nA radiation-ready target from CAD meshes in one call. It does what the Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum tutorial does by hand, and what visrad_target does with the CAD that a VisRad workspace bundles. parts contains name => mesh pairs, the CAD parts (as read_stl_solids returns them), and Components, taken as they are, such as primitives, apertures and parts prepared by hand. The components take part in the cavity selection but are not oriented, remeshed or fitted. The components of the target follow the order of parts.\n\nEach CAD part becomes a component of material radiating from its front (from both sides when two_sided), through these steps:\n\norient = true repairs its windings with orient_mesh: closed solids face out of their material, open sheets face the point toward (mm).\nWith inside, a point (mm) in the cavity, enclosure keeps only the elements that bound the free space around it, among the facets of all the parts as oriented (enclosure_kw are its keywords). The selection needs consistent windings.\nremesh (mm) divides the kept facets into compact, near-planar elements of about that size (remesh_mesh), and coarsen (mm) instead merges whole facets into elements (coarsen_mesh). At most one of them may be given. Only the kept facets are divided, which is much cheaper when most of an assembly lies outside the cavity. With inside, the selection is then repeated on the elements, and an element that straddles the edge of the lit region averages its flux over its area.\nfit splits each remeshed part into its cylinders, cones, spheres and the rest. It is on by default when remesh is given, and it may also be a named tuple of keywords of curved_components and fit_surfaces. An example is minfraction = 0, which also fits the curved faces of under 10⁻³ of the part area that stay flat by default. The fit uses the kept CAD facets, whose vertices lie on the true surfaces, so the radiation model integrates over the curved walls. The split is computational. The pieces, <part>/cyl1, …, <part>/rest, record their part, and a part that is one surface is not renamed. elements, Scenario(...; observe), the keep-outs, the clearance and TaLIS checks and the reports (energy_balance, component_power, write_results, report) take and show the parts (see parts). fit = false keeps flat elements.\n\nThe view factors of the target default to visibility = :sampled (see view_factors). CAD cavities have rims, such as entrance-hole bores and gaps, that partly shadow element pairs, and a single centroid ray would set their visibility to all or nothing.\n\nclosed = true declares the target a closed enclosure, so RadiationModel balances its view factors (enforce_closure!). It applies when apertures (aperture components among the parts) cover every opening of the kept cavity, and open_fraction shows where facets leak. On a remeshed, fitted CAD cavity with 0.1 mm bores and a 20 µm waist gap closed by apertures, balancing brings the LEH and gap loss from 0.7–0.8% above a continuum Monte Carlo to within 0.2%. It brings the capsule drive within 0.06%, against up to 0.16% raw, and moves its a₂/a₀ by under 0.08 pp, toward the reference at 0.5 mm (research notes, \"Closure on bored, windowed and CAD cavities\"). The default is false, with raw view factors, since a cavity selected from CAD is open wherever no aperture was given.\n\nmaterial, two_sided, remesh and coarsen take one value for every CAD part, a function of the part name (a Symbol), or a dictionary keyed by name (Symbol or String). The facets of a part missing from a remesh or coarsen dictionary are kept. A part missing from a two_sided dictionary is one-sided, and one missing from a material dictionary is an error. For example, remesh = n -> n === :capsule ? 0.15 : 0.5 remeshes a capsule finer than the walls. An account of what the cavity selection drops comes from enclosure on the target built without inside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cad_target"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.enclosure","page":"Radiation","text":"enclosure(target, point = (0, 0, 0); threshold = 0.05, albedo = 0.9, rays = 64,\n          points = 8, point_rays = 4096, radius = Inf, seed = 1) -> Enclosure\nenclosure(components, point; kwargs...)\n\nThe elements of target that bound the free-space region containing point (mm). They are returned as a new Target (enc.target) with each component cut to those patches, with diagnostics (Enclosure; show prints the elements and area kept per component). Imported CAD assemblies model a target as closed solids, with outer faces, end faces and mounting structure that cannot exchange radiation with the cavity, while view factors cost O(n²) in the elements. Only element sides that face the free space are found, which are the fronts of one-sided surfaces and either side of two-sided ones. CAD windings must therefore be made consistent first (orient_mesh, visrad_target(...; orient = true)).\n\nThe selection uses the flux that an isotropic source at the point would put on each element, directly and after diffuse re-emission from the surfaces with the uniform albedo albedo. This albedo is a geometric weight and does not come from the materials. Every element within radius of the point casts rays rays from random points on it, in cosine-weighted directions stratified in angle, against the facets of the target. Apertures are transparent, as everywhere. The flux is gathered along the rays:\n\nEⱼ = E⁰ⱼ + albedo × (mean of E over the element sides that the rays of j hit first).\n\nRays that escape, by leaving the sphere of radius about the point or hitting nothing, contribute nothing, and so do rays that meet a one-sided surface from behind. E⁰ⱼ is the direct flux, cos θ/(4π d²) averaged over points random points on the element, and zero where the line of sight to the point is blocked. Lines of sight, and the rays from the point, pass through surfaces seen from behind, so the point may lie inside the capsule. The iteration (Gauss–Seidel in element order, to 10⁻⁶) starts from E⁰. Elements with E ≥ threshold × E_ref are kept. E_ref is the mean flux on the element sides that point_rays rays from the point (a Fibonacci lattice) first hit, which sets the scale of the cavity. enc.flux contains E/E_ref. On a NIF CAD workspace (155k elements) the selection takes a few seconds on a desktop.\n\nEach element gathers through separate rays, which sample its whole view whatever its size or shape. A small patch or a sliver deep in a recess or behind a shield is therefore found through the surfaces around it, which have a view of the cavity. Surfaces outside, such as outer walls and the far sides of mounts, get no flux at all. A surface with a view of the cavity only through an opening, such as mounting structure beyond an entrance hole, gets about albedo × its view factor to the cavity. It also gets the flux that its neighbors outside gather in turn, attenuated by albedo at each step, so structure outside is not selected through its view of itself. The default threshold 0.05 drops a surface facing a hole of radius a from more than about 4a away on its axis. The rim of a hole in a thick wall has about half its view on the cavity and is kept. With threshold = 0, every element with any flux at all is kept, that is, everything connected to the cavity by lines of sight, however weakly, within the bound set by radius. The random numbers come from seed and the element index, so the result does not depend on the thread count. An element whose flux is within the sampling noise of the threshold can fall either way.\n\nRemoving elements outside the view of the cavity does not change the radiosity of the rest. Removing a surface seen through an opening turns the radiation that it would have returned into an escape, which return_area estimates against open_area; a lower threshold reduces it when it matters. Thin films that CAD models as solids, such as a capsule tent or an entrance-hole window, bound the free space like walls. With the point inside a tent, the result is the pocket of the tent around the capsule, with open_area near zero. Such parts should be left out of the target, since they transmit x-rays at their thickness, or the point should be placed on the side wanted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.enclosure"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.SIGMA_SB","page":"Radiation","text":"Stefan–Boltzmann constant in TW mm⁻² eV⁻⁴ (σT⁴ with T in eV gives TW/mm²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SIGMA_SB"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.RadiationModel","page":"Radiation","text":"RadiationModel(target; F = view_factors(target), closed, kwargs...)\n\nTarget plus view factors, which is everything needed to solve for x-ray fluxes given laser deposition. kwargs pass through to view_factors. closed = true, for elements (apertures included) that form a closed enclosure, removes discretization closure error with enforce_closure!. View-factor rows sum to one only to about 1e-3, and multiple bounces at high albedo amplify that leak or excess. At α = 0.925, a 5e-4 shift in the mean row sum of the wall moves the capsule drive by 0.3% and its P2 by about 0.05%. closed defaults to the declaration of the target (which hohlraum makes) when the view factors are computed here, and to false for a given F, which is used as is. closed = false gives the raw view factors of a closed target.\n\nFor a target of several cavities, such as two halfraums across a package or two hohlraums side by side, closed is a collection of cavities. Each cavity is the elements of one closed enclosure, given as a vector of element indices or of component or part names. A part name selects every piece of the part, entrance holes included, which is what the cavity of a hohlraum contains. The rows of each cavity are balanced to sum to one over its elements (enforce_closure!(F, A; cavities)). Elements in no cavity, such as the outer face of a package or free parts in the open, keep their raw rows apart from the reciprocal scaling of their entries with cavity elements. One cavity that contains every element is exactly closed = true. The caller declares which elements form a cavity, and open_fraction(target; cavity) checks the declaration by rays.\n\nThe model caches LU factorizations of the radiosity matrix I - diag(α) F keyed on the albedo vector (the two most recently used), so solve and flux_response with unchanged albedos skip the factorization. The cache is thread-safe. Do not mutate F after the first solve (or call Briareus.empty_cache!(model) after mutating it).\n\nLarge models, with more than Briareus.LARGE_MODEL[] = 10,000 elements by default, store the view factors they compute in a Float32Matrix. Above Briareus.HMATRIX_MODEL[] = 15,000 they use a hierarchical HMatrix (storage = :auto; Float64, Float32 or :hmatrix select a storage). Large models are solved by GMRES without factorizations (solver = :auto; :direct or :iterative select a solver). The storage of a given F is kept unless storage names another, and an HMatrix is solved only iteratively. Each GMRES iteration is one product with F accumulated in Float64, so iterative solves reach the same relative residual (1e-12) as the preconditioned ones. Float32 storage changes the capsule moments by about 1e-8 relative (research notes, \"Large radiation models\"). RadiationModel(target, F; solver = :auto) wraps a given matrix as is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.RadiationModel"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.RadiationSolution","page":"Radiation","text":"RadiationSolution\n\nFields:\n\nlaser: absorbed laser power per element (TW), of every color with the unconverted of solve;\nsource: x-ray source flux ηP/A, plus the emission of wall elements re-emitting stored energy in a drive_history step;\nB (radiosity) and H (incident flux); source, B and H are per element in TW/mm²;\nalbedo: per element, the albedos with which the radiosity was solved, so that B = source + albedo .* H exactly;\nintact: the albedo that each element would have intact, which is the before albedo of a thin-foil element and the albedo elsewhere;\ntransmission: the fraction of the incident flux that each element transmits out of the enclosure, nonzero only on a burned-through BurnthroughAlbedo foil (see energy_balance);\ntime;\nconverted: the part of source converted from laser light (TW/mm²), from every color and without the emission of a re-emitting wall;\nlaser3: the 3ω part of laser (TW per element; laser itself without unconverted), from which spectral_solve forms its default M-band.\n\nWithout converted, the constructor takes it as the 3ω conversion of laser, ηP/A, and without laser3 it takes laser as all 3ω. iterations, residual and converged report the albedo fixed point of a temperature-dependent albedo (solve). They are the number of radiosity solves, the largest albedo change after the last solve (an emission change counted as the albedo change it equals), and whether that change fell below tol. An unconverged solution is the radiosity of the last iterate, with the albedos with which it was solved; it is not self-consistent. A solution without a fixed point (explicit or constant albedos, or one built directly) has iterations = 1, residual = 0 and converged = true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.RadiationSolution"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.albedos","page":"Radiation","text":"albedos(target, time = 0.0, H = nothing; burnthrough = nothing) -> Vector\n\nAlbedo per element at time. Temperature-dependent models use the radiation temperature of each element from incident flux H, or their reference temperature when H is nothing. burnthrough (ns, per element; NaN where not given) sets the burnthrough time of BurnthroughAlbedo elements.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.albedos"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.conversions","page":"Radiation","text":"conversions(target, time = 0.0, laser = nothing) -> Vector\n\nLaser-to-x-ray conversion efficiency per element (0 on apertures) at time (ns), for the absorbed laser power laser (TW per element). Intensity-dependent Conversion models need laser, and they use the absorbed flux laser/area. Generic in the number type of laser.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.conversions"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.energy_balance-Tuple{RadiationSolution}","page":"Radiation","text":"energy_balance(sol; by = :part) -> NamedTuple\n\nGlobal x-ray power accounting (TW). The terms are source (Σ ηPᵢ), absorbed by each component ((1-α)HA), escaped through each aperture, transmitted through each burned-through thin-foil component (BurnthroughAlbedo) and each open or closing gap (GapClosure, (1-s)HA while a fraction s is closed), and lost to open space, which is the power that leaves the geometry without reaching any element. The loss (1-α)HA of a thin-foil component splits into the transmitted part τHA, with τ its sol.transmission, and the absorbed part (1-α-τ)HA. With the default transmission T, τ = sT and α = (1-sT)α_before, with s the progress of the transition, so the foil absorbs (1-sT)(1-α_before)HA. With an explicit after, τ = α_before-α, and the foil absorbs (1-α_before)HA, as if intact. A capsule with a CapsuleAlbedo absorbs (1-α_c)(R/R₀)² HA, the share of its effective sphere, plus its outside share a_out (1-(R/R₀)²) HA. This is the power of E_cap, which energy_budget integrates over a drive_history. source ≈ Σabsorbed + Σescaped + Σtransmitted + lost.\n\nThe dictionaries are keyed by part: the pieces of a part split for computation (curved_components) are summed into it. by = :component keys them by component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.energy_balance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.enforce_closure!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Radiation","text":"enforce_closure!(F, areas; cavities = nothing, tol = 1e-12, maxiter = 1000) -> F\n\nRescale view factors so every row sums to one while keeping reciprocity Aᵢ Fᵢⱼ = Aⱼ Fⱼᵢ. The exchange matrix Gᵢⱼ = Aᵢ Fᵢⱼ becomes sᵢ Gᵢⱼ sⱼ, with s from symmetric (Sinkhorn) balancing. Only valid for closed enclosures. Rows with zero view factors are left untouched.\n\nWith cavities (disjoint vectors of element indices), each cavity is balanced separately. The rows of its elements sum to one over its elements, from the Sinkhorn balancing of its block of G, and elements in no cavity keep sᵢ = 1. The one scaling s applies to the whole matrix, so reciprocity holds everywhere. An entry between a cavity and another element changes only by the factors of the cavity; the other element may belong to another cavity, or lie outside every cavity and be seen through an aperture or a crack. A single cavity of every element gives exactly the whole-matrix balancing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.enforce_closure!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.flux_response-Tuple{RadiationModel, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Radiation","text":"flux_response(model, observed; time = 0.0, albedo = nothing, weights = nothing,\n              laser = nothing, unconverted = nothing) -> Matrix\n\nLinear operator R with H[observed] = R * P, the incident x-ray flux on the observed elements (TW/mm²) per unit absorbed laser power on every element (TW). It is fixed for a given geometry and albedo, so optimizers that only change the laser source apply it as a matrix product instead of solving again. The converged sol.albedo of a reference solution, passed as albedo, linearizes about a temperature-dependent albedo state. The factorization cache of the model is shared with solve.\n\nWith weights (a matrix with one column per observed element, e.g. legendre_weights), weights * R is returned directly, and only its rows are solved for. This is far cheaper than R when only a few moments of the observed flux are wanted. On a large model (model.iterative) the rows are solved by GMRES on the transposed system, in blocks that share each product with F, and the full response costs a solve per observed element.\n\nA time-dependent Conversion is evaluated at time. An intensity-dependent conversion makes the source nonlinear in P. The absorbed laser power laser (TW per element) then sets the state about which the response is linearized: the conversion of each element is fixed at its value for that deposition (the secant, exact at laser itself).\n\nWith unconverted (as in solve, with its colors and, per color, a Deposition or a per-element absorbed power), the result is a Dict of responses keyed by color. :omega3 is the 3ω response above. For each unconverted color c, the response to the absorbed power of that color is the 3ω response with the conversion η(t, P/A) of each element replaced by r_c η(t, P_c/A), where r_c is the conversion of the color. Then H[observed] ≈ R[:omega3] * P + Σ_c R[c] * P_c. The conversion of each color is fixed at its value for the power of that color in unconverted, a secant per color. The sum then equals the per-color result of solve within rounding at those powers, and at any powers for conversions independent of intensity. With an intensity-dependent conversion, other powers P_c′ leave the error of the source change r_c [η(P_c/A) − η(P_c′/A)] P_c′/A, carried through the radiosity solve. On the gold table of Dewald (dη/dlog₁₀I = −0.15 between 10¹⁴ and 10¹⁵ W/cm²), the secant source of the color exceeds the per-color source by 0.065 δ/η of it for a small relative rise δ of the flux of the color. The two are equal where both fluxes lie beyond the same end of the table, which is held at its end values, as for unconverted light below 10¹⁴ W/cm².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.flux_response"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.open_fraction-Tuple{Target}","page":"Radiation","text":"open_fraction(target; cavity = nothing, rays = 64, seed = 1) -> (; fraction, element)\n\nWhether the facets of the target close an enclosure, apertures counted. The result gives the area-weighted fraction of diffuse rays leaving the elements that meet no facet at all, and that fraction per element (zero on apertures). rays cosine-distributed rays leave from uniform points on each radiating side except those of the apertures. Apertures stop the rays here, as walls would. A target is closed, and closed = true balancing (enforce_closure!) applies, when every opening has an aperture. The fraction is then zero up to cracks where meshes meet without sharing vertices, as for a disk aperture of 24 sides in a bore of 20 or 360. Such cracks give 2–7 × 10⁻⁴ on the cavities of \"Closure on bored, windowed and CAD cavities\" in the research notes. The holes of hohlraum meet its caps at shared vertices, and the hohlraum does not leak; the fraction was 2.7 × 10⁻³ at h = 1 when the holes had half the count of the caps. An opening left without an aperture shows as the share of the view that it takes, which may be no larger: 4 × 10⁻³ for a 40 µm waist gap, and 7 × 10⁻² for the two 3.1 mm LEHs of a CAD cavity. The fraction therefore indicates where to look (element), not whether to balance, which follows from the apertures given. Curved surfaces are ignored: cracks between the facets of a curved part and an aperture do not leak in the view factors, which integrate over the true surfaces.\n\ncavity (element indices, or component or part names) checks one cavity of a target of several, among the cavities that RadiationModel(...; closed) balances. The rays leave only from the elements of the cavity. A ray leaks when it misses every facet, or when it first meets an element side outside the cavity or a one-sided surface from behind. An element that the declaration of the cavity misses, or one that belongs to another cavity, therefore shows as a leak where it is seen. element is zero outside the cavity.\n\nEach element draws from a separate stream (from seed and the element), so the result does not depend on the thread count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.open_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.radiation_temperature-Tuple{Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"radiation_temperature(H)\n\nRadiation temperature (eV) of incident flux H (TW/mm²): (H/σ)^(1/4).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.radiation_temperature"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.solve-Tuple{RadiationModel, Deposition}","page":"Radiation","text":"solve(model, dep::Deposition; time = 0.0, albedo = nothing, burnthrough = nothing,\n      tol = 1e-4, maxiter = 50, guess = nothing, unconverted = nothing)\nsolve(model, laser_power::AbstractVector; ...)\n\nSolve the radiosity system for the given absorbed laser power per element. Albedos come from the materials at time unless an explicit per-element albedo vector is passed. Temperature-dependent albedos (WallLossAlbedo) are iterated to self-consistency with the incident-flux temperature of each element (to tol in albedo), in at most maxiter radiosity solves. The first ten iterates use the new albedos in full, and so does every later iterate until the iteration oscillates. An albedo that falls steeply enough with T overshoots, so that the albedo change reverses direction (a negative dot product with the previous change) and shrinks by less than half. Each such iterate halves the step from then on, α ← α + ω (α_new − α) with ω down to 1/64. Results that converge in ten iterates are therefore those of the plain iteration. An albedo that rises steeply with T runs away monotonically toward its range instead, and relaxation does not help there. The iterations, residual and converged of the result report the fixed point (RadiationSolution), and an unconverged solve also warns. burnthrough (ns, per element, NaN where not given) sets the burnthrough times of BurnthroughAlbedo elements. Without it, a thin-foil element uses the explicit burnthrough of its model, or is intact; drive_history tracks the burnthrough from the flux history instead.\n\nA single solve has no history. It iterates a WallLossAlbedo to the present temperature of each element at time, as if the wall had been at that temperature since t = 0, and with no re-emission of stored energy. Partway through a shaped pulse, that wall differs from the wall found by a drive_history of the pulse. On a NIF-like mock-up the wall albedo is 0.74 against 0.67 at the picket, 0.81 against 0.86 in the trough and 0.94 against 0.87 at the peak, and the capsule drive is 15–44% off. A fixed-time scenario of such a phase takes its albedos from the history (history_state; research notes, \"Drive histories: sliver steps and the albedo of a phase (2026-11-18)\").\n\nunconverted adds the x-ray source of unconverted (1ω, 2ω) light absorbed by the target. It is the result of unconverted_deposition, which contains the UnconvertedColors with which it was deposited (its colors). It may also be any collection (a NamedTuple, or a Dict keyed by Symbol) with an entry colors, the UnconvertedColors, and, under the name of each color, a Deposition or a per-element absorbed power (TW). The conversions used are those of these colors, so a deposition and the source made from it share one set of colors. The source flux of element i is then\n\nSᵢ = [ηᵢ(t, Pᵢ/Aᵢ) Pᵢ + Σ_c r_c ηᵢ(t, P_c,ᵢ/Aᵢ) P_c,ᵢ] / Aᵢ,\n\nwith ηᵢ the conversion_efficiency of its material, P the 3ω power, P_c the absorbed power of the color and r_c the conversion of the color relative to 3ω. The conversion of each color is evaluated at the absorbed flux of that color, an engineering default. The relative conversions come from gold disks irradiated at one wavelength at a time, and no measurement of the conversion of overlapping colors was found (research notes, \"Unconverted light: absorption and conversion vs. wavelength\"). With a conversion independent of intensity, this is the 3ω solve of the equivalent power P + Σ_c r_c P_c, within rounding. sol.laser is then the absorbed power of all colors, P + Σ_c P_c, and sol.converted the source above, which spectral_solve takes as the converted share. Without unconverted the source is the unchanged 3ω source. Generic in the number type of the powers of every color.\n\nThe factorization of the system for the initial albedo (that of the materials at time, or the explicit albedo) is cached on the model. Repeated solves with unchanged albedos then cost only a pair of triangular solves and a matrix–vector product. Iterates of a temperature-dependent albedo differ from call to call. They are solved by GMRES preconditioned with the factorization of a nearby system (that of the first iterate, or of the guess model), to a relative residual of 1e-12. They are factored afresh only when GMRES does not converge in a few tens of iterations, and the new factorization then preconditions later iterates. On the fine 12.5k-element hohlraum, a 50-step drive_history ran about nine times faster per step with no fresh factorization (127 GMRES solves, 819 iterations) than with a fresh factorization per iterate (127 in all). On the standard mesh it ran about 2.4 times faster. Results agree with the factored path to about 1e-12 eV in Tr and 1e-11 percentage points in P2/P0.\n\nguess is a previous solution of the same model or of one with the same elements, such as the last step of a history or a keyframe of a moving target. The albedo iteration then starts from its incident-flux temperatures instead of the reference temperatures of the materials, and its radiosity starts the GMRES of the first iterate. The preconditioner of its model is used when this model has none. Fewer iterations are needed when the guess is close. The result then differs from a cold start by less than the tolerance of the iteration. The first albedo is not cached in that case.\n\nA large model (model.iterative, see RadiationModel) is never factored. Every iterate is solved by unpreconditioned GMRES to the same 1e-12 relative residual, from the previous iterate or from guess. Dual-number sources are solved as one block, with the value and partials sharing each product with F.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.solve"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.to_Wcm2-Tuple{Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"Convert a flux in TW/mm² to W/cm².\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.to_Wcm2"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.wall_temperature-Tuple{RadiationSolution, Vararg{Symbol}}","page":"Radiation","text":"wall_temperature(sol::RadiationSolution, names...) -> Float64\n\nThe radiation temperature (eV) of the area-weighted mean incident flux ΣHᵢAᵢ / ΣAᵢ on the elements of the components or parts names. When names are the wall and caps of a cavity, it is the wall temperature of the cavity. With several cavities in one target (two halfraums across a package), the wall pieces of each cavity give a separate temperature. A part name selects every component of the part, entrance holes included, so the wall of a hohlraum is named piece by piece. The cavity_Tr of drive_history records it per step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.wall_temperature"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.PulseShape","page":"Radiation","text":"PulseShape(times, powers)\n\nPiecewise-linear power history (ns, TW per beam), zero outside the table. Callable: pulse(t). Two pulse shapes are equal (==, isequal, hash) when their times and powers are.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PulseShape"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.drive_history-Tuple{RadiationModel, DepositionOperator, Any, Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"drive_history(model, op, beams_at, times; observe = :capsule, lmax = 6,\n              wall = (:wall,), cavities = (), lpi = nothing, holes = nothing, ylm = false,\n              observed_flux = false, transition = nothing, flux_change = nothing, min_step = 1e-3,\n              tol = 1e-4, keep_albedo = false, record = nothing,\n              wall_history = true, wall_release = true, mband = MBandSplit(),\n              spectral = nothing, unconverted = nothing, colors = nif_unconverted(),\n              unconverted_kw = (;), prepulse = nothing, by = :group,\n              per_component = false) -> NamedTuple\n\nQuasi-static drive history. At each time, beams_at(t) is deposited with op after the laser–plasma model lpi, evaluated at that time. The radiosity is solved at that time, with time- and temperature-dependent albedos, and the following records are kept:\n\ntime — the times solved (ns): times, plus any steps inserted by transition or flux_change\ndelivered — laser power (TW)\nflux, Tr — mean incident flux on observe (TW/mm²) and its radiation temperature (eV)\nmoments — (lmax+1) × nt Legendre moments of the observed flux (aₗ/a₀ rows are moments[l+1, :] ./ moments[1, :])\nylm — with ylm = true, the (lmax+1)² × nt real spherical-harmonic coefficients of the observed flux (ylm_weights, in mode_index order; columns work with mode_amplitude), otherwise nothing\nobserved_flux — with observed_flux = true, the n × nt incident flux (TW/mm²) on each of the n observed elements (in the order of elements(target, observe...)), which is the state that the retune of a flat face holds (FaceFlux); otherwise nothing\nwall_Tr, wall_albedo — area-weighted mean incident-flux temperature and albedo of the wall components\ncavity_Tr — per cavity of cavities (pairs name => wall names, e.g. [:upper => (:upper_wall, :upper_cap_top), :lower => (:lower_wall, :lower_cap_bottom)] for two halfraums), the temperature of the area-weighted mean incident flux on the wall components of that cavity (wall_temperature of the solution of each step), keyed by cavity name; empty without cavities (the default). The walls of a cavity are named one by one, since grouping by part would also include the entrance holes of a hohlraum.\nescaped — power (TW) through each aperture, and transmitted through each thin-foil (BurnthroughAlbedo) and closing-gap (GapClosure) component, as in energy_balance (by default the fraction s T of the flux on each burned-through element, and 1 - s on a gap a fraction s closed)\nwindow_loss — power lost through diagnostic windows: transmitted plus escaped through every aperture except the entrance holes. By default the holes are the apertures not on a CylinderSurface, which are the LEHs of a hohlraum.\nfront — per thin-foil component, the area-mean heat-front areal mass as a fraction of the foil areal mass (≥ 1 once burned through), from its flux history even when the model has an explicit burnthrough\nburnthrough — per thin-foil component, the burnthrough time of each element (ns; Inf if it has not burned through)\nalbedo — with keep_albedo = true, the converged albedo per element at each step; otherwise nothing. This is the state about which a flux response is linearized, for example for retune_drive. Where a wall re-emits stored energy, it is the effective albedo that includes the emission, so that B = ηP/A + αH. Alongside it, transmission is the fraction of the incident flux per element transmitted out of the enclosure at each step (from the RadiationSolution), otherwise nothing. history_state interpolates both to a time of the pulse.\nsetup — the power of beams_at(t) (TW, before lpi); laser — absorbed laser power per component (TW, back side included); source — x-ray source power per component (Σ ηP); absorbed — x-ray power absorbed per surface component (and per closing lip), as in energy_balance. absorbed is net of released, the power that wall components re-emit from stored energy (see below). lost — x-ray power lost to open space. energy_budget integrates these records. As in energy_balance, escaped, transmitted, laser, source and absorbed are keyed by part, and the pieces of a part split by curved_components are summed into it. front and burnthrough stay per component. With per_component = true, all of these records, released and the laser power of mband stay per component. This separates one piece of a part from the others, for example the cap of a hohlraum observed while its wall heats.\nlip_radius — per computed entrance-hole lip (LipClosure, hohlraum(; leh_closure = :computed)), the radius of its plasma edge at each step (mm)\nrecord — with record = f, the vector of f(sol) for the RadiationSolution of each step (e.g. sol -> dante(dante1(), sol) for a synthetic Dante, or a spectral_solve), otherwise nothing.\nmband_moments, mband — the (lmax+1) × nt Legendre moments of the observed flux at photon energies above the lower M-band edge, and the laser-produced M-band power per component (TW, keyed by part like laser). The moments are those of band_flux(s, mband.emission.band[1]) for the spectral solution s of the step, which includes laser-produced and recirculating M-band and the thermal tail. Both are nothing with mband = nothing, when the target has no surface of the materials of the model, or, for a per-group model, when no beam of beams_at(times[1]) is in its groups.\nunconverted — with unconverted, (; delivered, skipped): per color, the unconverted power delivered at each step (TW, before absorption), and the beams whose unconverted light cannot be placed (see unconverted_deposition); otherwise nothing.\n\nUnconverted light. unconverted gives the unconverted power per color of colors (UnconvertedColors, the NIF 1ω and 2ω by default) as a fraction of the 3ω power of each beam. The fraction is a number or a function of time (ns), keyed by color name, for example (omega1 = 0.05, omega2 = 0.01) or (omega1 = PulseShape(ts, fs), omega2 = 0.0), as for the ratio of unconverted_history. There is no default, since the fraction is an input. The fraction of a color applies to every beam, or each beam has a separate fraction. A separate fraction is a vector with one entry per beam of beams_at(τ), in its order, which the pointing fixes, or it is keyed by by as prepulse is. A beam without an entry is a KeyError, as in with_pulses. An example for 1ω on the outer cones only is (omega1 = Dict(:outer44 => 0.05, :outer50 => 0.05, :inner23 => 0.0, :inner30 => 0.0), omega2 = 0.01). At each step the absorbed powers of the colors are those of unconverted_deposition for the beams of beams_at(τ), at the fractions of each beam at that step. The beam powers are those before lpi, since unconverted light does not take part in the 3ω laser–plasma model. The step is solved with these powers as the unconverted argument of solve, and each color is converted at its absorbed flux.\n\nprepulse adds light not proportional to the 3ω pulse, as the prepulse of unconverted_history does. Per color it is optional, and it is a power per beam (TW) as a function of time: one function for all beams, or keyed by by (:group, :quad, :beam or a function, as in with_pulses). The power of a beam in color c at step τ is then r_c(τ) · P₃ω(τ) + prepulse[c](τ), with r_c its fraction in color c and P₃ω the power of the beam in beams_at(τ). A prepulse without unconverted fractions uses zero fractions (unconverted = (omega1 = 0.0, omega2 = 0.0)). The history is solved only at times, so a prepulse reaches the drive through the steps in its window. A step where only the prepulse fires (every 3ω power zero, also before the main pulse at negative times) is solved with only the prepulse light. Its x-rays heat the wall, and the memory of the wall (below) carries them into the main pulse. Between a prepulse and the main pulse, a step where nothing fires lets the wall cool and re-emit the energy it stored. Without such a step, the wall-energy integral runs across the gap at the interpolated temperature. unconverted_kw passes the offsets, lens_distance, coarse and resolution of unconverted_deposition.\n\nThe light is linear in the power of each beam, so the history deposits it once per set of beams whose powers are equal at every step and whose fractions and prepulses are the same (equal numbers or the same function). For beams on shared pulses this is once per (pulse, fraction, prepulse) schedule, as in unconverted_history. A set whose light is zero is never deposited, and the deposited patterns are scaled by the powers in time. The pointing must not change in time. laser then includes the absorbed power of each color, and setup and delivered its delivered power, so energy_budget balances. The M-band is still that of the 3ω light. Without unconverted the history is identical to the 3ω history.\n\nM-band. mband is an MBandSplit, which contains the M-band emission model and the spectral split with which it is solved. The default is MBandSplit(), with nif_mband() (the per-cone conversions of Dewald 2020 on gold and Au-lined uranium) in the calibrated two-group split. The laser-produced M-band of each step then comes from the effective beams of that step after lpi (CBET, backscatter, the bubble), as mband_power(mband.emission, op, beams; lpi, time) gives it. It is the share of each beam group (cone) of every element, split off in the deposition pass of that step, so the M-band follows the cone balance in time. An intensity-form MBandSplit(MBandEmission()) converts the deposition of the step directly. The gray solution of the step is then split by spectral_solve with the edges, thermalize and group albedos of the split, and spectral contains the other keywords of spectral_solve (spot, tol, ...). The M-band is a diagnostic, and the gray history (every other record) is identical with or without it. It costs about 6% of a small history (600 elements), and less than the run-to-run spread on N221204 (research notes, \"Per-cone M-band in the drive history (2026-10-27)\"). mband = nothing turns it off.\n\nThe temperature-dependent albedos of each step are iterated to tol (see solve), starting from the temperatures of the previous step.\n\nWall-loss history. With wall_history = true (the default), each element whose albedo is a WallLossAlbedo loses energy as its temperature history requires. The loss is not that of a wall held at the present temperature since t = 0. The constant-T wall energy of Lindl, E_w ∝ T^a t^b (a = 4 + p = 3.3, b = 1 + q = 0.62 for the defaults), is extended to a varying T(t) as E_w ∝ [∫ (T/100 eV)^(a/b) dt]^b. This is the form of the drive integral of HeatFront, exact at constant T. Its rate is the constant-T loss at the effective time\n\nt_eff = ∫₀ᵗ (T(t′)/T(t))^(a/b) dt′,\n\nintegrated trapezoidally between the steps from the incident-flux temperature of each element. The temperature is taken as constant from t = 0 to times[1], and no such interval is added before a first step at or before t = 0, such as that of a prepulse. A wall heated in a low foot holds little energy at the peak temperature. Its loss early in the peak is therefore several times the constant-T value at t (t_eff ≈ the time since the rise), and the drive rises through a flat peak.\n\nA thin-foil element (BurnthroughAlbedo) whose before is a WallLossAlbedo follows the same history for as long as before enters its albedo. While intact it is a wall element with that loss, so an intact window equals the wall around it. Across its transition and after it, α_before comes from the history, and the stored energy that it re-emits (below) is that of the wall element scaled by α/α_before (1 − s f). Its heat front does not depend on this. wall_history = false evaluates the albedo at t, for walls and foils alike. This was the behavior before 2026-10-24, and for foils before 2026-11-19. See the research notes, \"N221204 late-time drive (2026-10-24)\" and \"Intact windows follow the wall's history (2026-11-19)\".\n\nWall re-emission. The self-similar loss never gives energy back: when T falls, t_eff grows and the loss only shrinks. With wall_release = true (the default, with wall_history), a falling drive makes the wall re-emit stored energy. The loss above is taken as the heating response to the highest temperature of the element so far, T_h = max T. A drop below it gives the deficit D = φ(T_h) − φ(T) in the wall-energy potential φ = (T/100 eV)^a, and the response to the deficit is the same self-similar kernel, superposed. This follows the principle of Duhamel for the diffusion in the wall, which is linear in φ (engineering):\n\nF = L(T_h, t_eff) − f_r b C ∫ (t − t′)^(b−1) dD(t′),    C = L t_eff^(1−b) / (b φ(T_h)),\n\nis the net flux into the wall. C, the coefficient of the loss, is constant for the Lindl loss of gold. f_r is the release of WallLossAlbedo, 1.0, calibrated against 1-D radiation hydrodynamics of an ablating gold wall (examples/late_time_wall.jl). A constant-T wall loses C b φ t^(b−1). A drop D at t_d gives back f_r C D (t − t_d)^b by t, quickly at first from the hot surface layer and then as the t^b of the heat wave. The effective albedo 1 − F/H exceeds 1 where the wall gives back more than it takes, and solve includes the release as an emission added to the source of the element. While no element temperature falls below its running maximum, the history is identical to that of the heating loss. At a step where nothing fires, the flux of a heated wall is its re-emission, and the albedo iteration of solve starts it at half its running maximum. An element without flux would otherwise take reference_temperature. A wall heated again after cooling takes back the energy of its surface layer, but never more than the flux on it: its radiosity is kept ≥ 0 (albedo 0, no emission, engineering). released records the re-emitted power per component, and energy_budget integrates it; absorbed is net of it. See the research notes, \"Wall re-emission and the capsule radius (2026-10-24)\" for the closure and \"Capsule absorption, conversion and the ablating-wall release (2026-10-25)\" for release 1.0.\n\nComputed lip closure. Each entrance-hole lip with a LipClosure albedo has a closure depth d, open at times[1]. The depth is advanced over each step from the trapezoidal mean of the free speed v(T) of the lip and of the laser hold. T is the area-mean incident-flux temperature of the lip at the start of the step and, corrected, at its end. The hold is the power of the beams in beams_at, after lpi, whose ports lie on the side of the hole, over the initial area of the hole. The update is exact for the relaxation of the hold. The step is solved with each lip ring closed outside the edge r₀ − d, and solved again until the corrected depth changes by less than 1e-5 mm.\n\nEach thin-foil element without an explicit burnthrough time tracks a separate heat front. The front is the drive integral of heat_front over the incident-flux temperature history of that element, trapezoidal between the steps from times[1], so its burnthrough is consistent with the flux that the windowed hohlraum puts on it. A step is solved with the front extended from the previous step at constant temperature. It is solved again until the temperatures of that step no longer move a burnthrough that affects its albedo.\n\nA burnthrough transition takes the width of the foil (0.4 ns by default), and a step much longer than that jumps across it. Two optional criteria insert steps between times:\n\ntransition — the transition of no thin-foil element (the smooth step s of BurnthroughAlbedo, 0 intact to 1 burned through) and of no closing gap (GapClosure, 0 open to 1 closed) advances by more than this in one step. Steps are planned from the known or predicted burnthrough time of each element. A step is halved if the temperatures of that step move a burnthrough so that the advance exceeds 1.5 transition.\nflux_change — the observed flux relative to its mean, H/⟨H⟩ on each element of observe, changes by no more than this in one step. A step that exceeds it is halved and solved again.\n\nNeither criterion shortens a step below min_step (ns). A planned step that would leave less than min_step before the next of times is extended to that time instead. Without the criteria (the default) the steps are exactly times. With wall_release, the albedo of a cooling wall element in a step of length Δ falls as 1 − c Δ^(b−1), the implicit part of the release kernel. A step of 1e-5 ns gave albedos near −27, which the unpreconditioned GMRES of a large model does not solve, so steps much shorter than min_step are best left out of times as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.drive_history"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.energy_budget-Tuple{Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"energy_budget(history; from = nothing, to = nothing) -> NamedTuple\n\nEnergies (kJ = TW·ns) of a drive_history, the trapezoidal integrals of its power records over the solved times. With [from, to], the integrals run over that interval, and outside the solved times the powers count only through the trapezoids that they bound. The energies are:\n\nsetup — the laser power that beams_at(t) delivers (before the laser–plasma model); coupled — after it (delivered); backscattered — their difference, which is the power that Backscatter and other lossy models remove (CBET conserves power);\nlaser — absorbed laser energy per component (front elements plus back side), unabsorbed — coupled − Σ laser, the light that leaves through the holes;\nsource — x-ray energy converted per component (Σ η P); absorbed — x-ray energy absorbed per surface component ((1 − α) H A, for a CapsuleAlbedo capsule at its effective radius, E_cap); escaped per aperture; transmitted per thin-foil or closing-gap component; lost to open space;\nheating — per surface component, the energy that stays in it, which is unconverted laser plus absorbed x-rays, laser − source + absorbed;\nreleased — per component, the stored wall energy re-emitted under a falling drive (the wall_release of drive_history). absorbed is net of it: a wall component took in absorbed + released and holds absorbed.\n\nsetup ≈ backscattered + unabsorbed + Σ heating + Σ escaped + Σ transmitted + lost, to the closure of the view factors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.energy_budget"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.history_state-Tuple{Target, Any, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"history_state(target, history, time; mean = ()) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe radiation state at time (ns) of a drive_history of target, run with keep_albedo = true. A linear scenario at that time of the pulse, such as a phase of a shaped pulse, needs this state in place of the albedos of the materials.\n\ntime — the time;\nalbedo — the albedo of each element, the converged albedo of the history (history.albedo), interpolated linearly in time between the two steps around time. At the time of a step it is the value of that step. It follows the temperature history of each wall element. It includes the emission of a re-emitting wall (the effective albedo B = ηP/A + αH) and the state of a thin foil ((1 − s𝒯) α_before while it burns through);\ntransmission — the fraction of the incident flux on each element transmitted out of the enclosure (the transmission of the RadiationSolution, from burned-through foils and open gaps), interpolated the same way;\nwindows — per thin-foil (BurnthroughAlbedo) component, (; transition, transmission, burned). These are the area mean of the transition s of its elements at time (0 intact, 1 burned through, from the burnthrough times of the history and the width of the foil), the area mean of their transmitted fraction, and whether every element has finished its transition.\n\nmean (a component or part name, or a collection of them) replaces the albedo of their elements by its area mean, one constant over all of them. mean = :wall gives the wall_albedo of the history on the wall.\n\ntime and albedo are the keywords of Scenario and remake(::Scenario). With st = history_state(target, h, τ), Scenario(target, beams; observe = :capsule, st.time, st.albedo) or remake(scenario; st.time, st.albedo) is the flux response about the state of the history at τ, linear in the laser source as the optimizer requires. At the time of a step and with the history beams, it gives the observed flux of the history. A standalone solve at a time partway through a pulse, or the default albedos of a scenario, evaluate a WallLossAlbedo as if the wall had been at its present (or reference) temperature since t = 0, without the history or the re-emission. On a NIF-like mock-up the capsule drive then differs from that of the history by −15 to +64%, and P2/P0 by up to 3.6 percentage points (research notes, \"Drive histories: sliver steps and the albedo of a phase (2026-11-18)\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.history_state"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.pulse_energy-Tuple{PulseShape}","page":"Radiation","text":"Laser energy (kJ = TW·ns) of one beam following the pulse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pulse_energy"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.with_pulses-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"with_pulses(beams, pulses, t; by = :group) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nBeams with power set to pulses[key](t). key is the group of each beam (pulses given as a Dict or NamedTuple keyed by group), its quad as a Symbol (by = :quad), its name as a Symbol (by = :beam) or by(beam) for a function. pulses may also be a single PulseShape for all beams. Every pulse reader and writer keys by Symbol: read_pulse_table by column header, cone_fraction_pulses by group and read_visrad_powers by beam name (by = :beam).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.with_pulses"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.MBandEmission","page":"Radiation","text":"MBandEmission(; intensity = [1e14, 1e15], fraction = [0.01, 0.20], groups = nothing,\n              band = (1800, 2850), materials = (:Au, :DU))\n\nNon-Planckian gold M-band emission from laser-heated spots, an engineering model. Elements of a material in materials emit part of their absorbed laser power P as M-band x-rays, flat in photon energy over band (eV). The default materials are gold and the Au-lined uranium of a NIF wall (:DU, uranium, nif_uranium), where the laser heats the gold liner. The fraction is\n\nby default fraction(I), with I the absorbed laser flux P/A of the element (W/cm²), interpolated in log–log between the (intensity, fraction) points and held constant beyond them; or\nwith groups (a Dict from beam group to fraction, e.g. nif_mband()), fixed per beam group: an element emits Σ_g fraction_g P_g, P_g the power it absorbs from the beams of group g (mband_power deposits the groups separately).\n\nThe M-band is part of the converted x-rays (it is not added to them): the Planckian source of the element is ηP/A less the M-band (which is capped at ηP/A).\n\nBy default the conversion of laser light to M-band (> 1.8 keV) in gold at normal incidence is ~1% at 10¹⁴ and ~20% at 10¹⁵ W/cm² (Dewald et al., PoP 15, 072706, 2008, as quoted in Dewald et al., PoP 27, 2020, M; sources in the research notes, \"Spectral radiosity, M-band and a synthetic Dante\"). With the upper band edge at 2.85 keV, 1/3 of a flat band lies above 2.5 keV, which is Dewald 2020's share of the hohlraum M-band above the Mo edge (L). The element-averaged intensity of the default form depends on the mesh across a spot (the fraction goes as I^1.3); the per-group form does not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.MBandEmission"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.MBandSplit","page":"Radiation","text":"MBandSplit(emission = nif_mband(); edges = [0, emission.band[1], ∞], thermalize = true,\n           albedo = 0.55)\n\nThe laser-produced M-band of a drive_history and the spectral split it is solved with. emission (an MBandEmission) makes the M-band source of each step. spectral_solve then splits the gray solution of the step with group edges edges (eV), thermalize and the group albedos albedo; the edges must include the lower M-band edge emission.band[1]. albedo is either a number α_M, passed to mband_albedo(target, emission, edges; albedo) (α_M at and above the M-band edge on the emitting surfaces, the gray albedo below), or a Dict from component name to group albedos as spectral_solve takes them (the gray albedo is used for nothing entries).\n\nThe defaults are the calibrated pairing: two groups split at the M-band edge, thermalized, with α_M 0.55 (calibrated, see mband_albedo).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.MBandSplit"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.SpectralSolution","page":"Radiation","text":"SpectralSolution\n\nResult of spectral_solve. Fields: gray (the RadiationSolution it splits), edges (group edges, eV); per element × group (TW/mm²): source, B (radiosity), H (incident flux), albedo; per element: temperature (eV, the wall temperature of the thermal re-emission and of any stored-energy emission, and with spot = :brightness the one temperature of the whole split), spot_temperature (eV, the Planckian of the converted laser x-rays less the M-band), converted (the converted laser source ηP/A, TW/mm²), mband (the laser-produced M-band source, TW/mm²); mband_shares (its split over the groups); thermalize; spot (the spot-spectrum rule, see spectral_solve).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SpectralSolution"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.band_flux","page":"Radiation","text":"band_flux(s::SpectralSolution, lo, hi = Inf; field = :H) -> Vector\n\nPer-element sum of the groups between photon energies lo and hi (eV, both group edges) of field (:H incident flux, :B radiosity, :source).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.band_flux"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.mband_albedo-Tuple{Target, MBandEmission, Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"mband_albedo(t::Target, m::MBandEmission, edges; albedo = 0.55) -> Dict\n\nGroup albedos for spectral_solve's albedo that pair with the laser M-band m: on every surface component of m's materials, albedo (α_M) in each group at or above the lower M-band edge m.band[1] (which must be one of edges) and the gray albedo (nothing) below it. Use with thermalize = true: the rest of the gray albedo is re-emitted thermally, so the group sums stay equal to the gray solution.\n\nThe default α_M = 0.55 is calibrated (research notes, \"Spectral radiosity, M-band and a synthetic Dante\" and \"Laser-spot spectrum\"): with nif_mband(), thermalized and the laser spots thermal at the local radiation temperature (spot = :radiation), it gives Dewald et al. 2020's recirculating-to-laser M-band ratio F_M ≈ 2 on their setup (examples/mband_dewald2020.jl; 0.5 under the :brightness rule). It is fitted to F_M only; Dante's > 1.8 keV share (11.4–12.0% against the measured 12%) is the check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mband_albedo"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.mband_conversion-Tuple{MBandEmission, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"mband_conversion(m::MBandEmission, I) -> Float64\n\nConversion of laser light to M-band x-rays at absorbed laser flux I (W/cm²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mband_conversion"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.mband_power-Tuple{MBandEmission, Target, AbstractVector}","page":"Radiation","text":"mband_power(m::MBandEmission, target, laser) -> Vector\nmband_power(m::MBandEmission, op::DepositionOperator, beams; lpi = nothing, time = 0.0) -> Vector\n\nLaser-produced M-band power per element (TW), computed from the absorbed laser power per element laser (TW; the default intensity form) or by depositing beams with op (either form; the per-group form deposits each group's beams separately). Elements of other materials and apertures emit none. spectral_solve takes the result as mband_power.\n\nWith a laser–plasma model lpi, beams are its effective beams apply_lpi(lpi, b, time), taken after CBET, backscatter and the bubble. Each group is then deposited through lpi_deposit at time, so light that the model deposits off the operator, at the ridge stop of the bubble, converts at the fraction of its group. By default this is done at every step of a drive_history with the effective beams of that step. The groups are split in one deposition pass: each group's share of an element is exactly the deposition of only the beams of that group, with the others at zero power.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mband_power"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.mband_recirculation-Tuple{SpectralSolution, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Radiation","text":"mband_recirculation(s::SpectralSolution, elems; lo = 1800) -> Float64\n\nDewald's F_M on the elements elems, the ratio of recirculating to laser-produced M-band flux (photon energies ≥ lo, eV), where the laser-produced part is the first flight of the non-Planckian M-band from the laser-heated spots (F · S_M) and the recirculating part is everything else incident in the band (re-emission and the thermal tail of the walls). Both parts are area-weighted over elems. Dewald et al., PoP 27 (2020), infer F_M ≈ 2 on the wall through the peak.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mband_recirculation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.ngroups-Tuple{SpectralSolution}","page":"Radiation","text":"Number of photon-energy groups of a SpectralSolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ngroups"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.nif_mband-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"nif_mband(; outer = 1.5e15, inner = 3e14, kwargs...) -> MBandEmission\n\nPer-cone NIF M-band emission. The conversion of laser light to M-band in each cone is the default fraction(I) at its mean wall irradiance, outer 1.5 × 10¹⁵ and inner 3 × 10¹⁴ W/cm² (Dewald et al., PoP 27, 2020, M), which gives 0.20 (held beyond 10¹⁵) and 0.042. Dewald also notes the 2.5× lower turning-point density of the inner beams, which lowers their M-band further ( n_e^2, ~5×). That factor is not applied, so the inner M-band is an upper bound (L). kwargs are passed to MBandEmission. This is the default M-band model of drive_history.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nif_mband"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.planck_fraction-Tuple{Real, Real, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"planck_fraction(T, E1, E2) -> Float64\n\nFraction of a Planckian's power (σT⁴) emitted between photon energies E1 and E2 (eV; E2 = Inf for the whole tail) at temperature T (eV). planck_fraction(T, 0, Inf) is 1 exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.planck_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.planck_fractions-Tuple{Real, AbstractVector}","page":"Radiation","text":"planck_fractions(T, edges) -> Vector\n\nPlanck fractions of the groups [edges[g], edges[g+1]] at temperature T (eV). With edges[1] = 0 and edges[end] = Inf they sum to one (to rounding).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.planck_fractions"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.spectral_edges-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"spectral_edges(; lo = 50, hi = 20_000, n = 40, include = (1800,)) -> Vector\n\nGroup edges (eV) for spectral_solve, made of 0, n log-spaced edges from lo to hi, the energies in include (default the M-band threshold, 1.8 keV) and Inf. The default spans the NIF Dante channels (50 eV – 20 keV; Rubery et al., RSI 2023).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.spectral_edges"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.spectral_solve-Tuple{RadiationSolution}","page":"Radiation","text":"spectral_solve(sol::RadiationSolution; edges = [0, Inf], albedo = nothing,\n               mband = nothing, mband_power = nothing, thermalize = false,\n               spot = :radiation, temperature = nothing, spot_temperature = nothing,\n               tol = 1e-6, maxiter = 20) -> SpectralSolution\n\nMultigroup radiosity about a gray solution sol (same model, laser deposition and time). The groups are [edges[g], edges[g+1]] (eV; edges[1] = 0, edges[end] = Inf, e.g. spectral_edges()).\n\nSources: the gray source of an element is its converted laser x-rays ηP/A (sol.converted, from every color when solve is given unconverted) plus, on a re-emitting wall of a drive_history, the emission of its stored energy. The laser-produced M-band (mband, an MBandEmission) is part of the converted x-rays and is placed in its band. Its power per element mband_power (TW) defaults to mband_power(mband, target, sol.laser3), from only the 3ω light as in drive_history, and must be given for a per-group model. The rest is split by Planck fractions, by spot:\n:radiation (default): the converted x-rays less the M-band are split at the radiation temperature (H/σ)^¼ of the element, which its incident flux sets. The coronal emission of a laser spot is thermal at about the cavity temperature, and its hard part is non-Planckian M-band (research notes, \"Laser-spot spectrum\"). The stored-energy emission, and with thermalize the thermal re-emission, is split at the wall temperature ((B − ηP/A)/σ)^¼, the brightness of the element radiosity without its converted laser part;\n:wall: the converted x-rays also at the wall temperature;\n:brightness: everything at the brightness temperature (B/σ)^¼ of the total radiosity (the rule before 2026-10-24's spot spectrum; a laser spot then emits as a Planckian well above the wall temperature, plus a hard tail).\nThe temperatures follow the group totals when those differ from the gray ones (iterated to tol). A given temperature vector (eV) fixes the wall temperature (with :brightness, the only temperature), and spot_temperature fixes that of the converted x-rays. On elements without laser light the three rules agree.\nGroup albedos α_g: the fraction of the flux incident on an element in group g that it re-emits in group g. The gray converged albedo sol.albedo is used in every group, except for the components in albedo, a Dict (or pairs) from component name to a vector of group albedos (for a nothing entry the gray albedo is used in that group, e.g. Dict(:wall => [nothing, 0.5]); a NaN entry throws).\nthermalize: what happens to the rest of the gray albedo, α − α_g. By default it is lost to the wall (B_g = S_g + α_g H_g, each group a gray-type solve). With thermalize = true it is re-emitted thermally, spread over the groups by the Planck fractions b_g(T) of the element at its wall temperature: B_g = S_g + α_g H_g + b_g(T) Σ_g′ (α − α_g′) H_g′ (group albedos above α are lowered to it). When thermalized, the group sums equal the gray solution whatever the group albedos; with every group albedo 0, all re-emission is thermal.\n\nWith every group albedo equal to the gray one (either way), the group sums of source, B and H equal those of the gray solution (absorbed and emitted power per element too), whatever the split; with one group and no M-band the solution is the gray one exactly. Groups at the gray albedo reuse the cached factorizations of the model. They are solved directly when that albedo is factored and otherwise by GMRES preconditioned with a cached factorization; fresh factorizations are not cached, so the gray solve path is unaffected. Groups with separate albedos are solved by GMRES (jointly when thermalized), which converges fast for the low albedos of hard groups. When the split is thermalized and a single group is at the gray albedo (e.g. a two-group split at the M-band edge, as in drive_history), that group is computed as the gray radiosity less the others, which equals its solve up to the solver tolerance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.spectral_solve"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.TabulatedCurve","page":"Radiation","text":"TabulatedCurve(E, values; extrapolate = :hold, file = \"\")\nTabulatedCurve(file; extrapolate = :hold)\n\nA measured curve, interpolated linearly in its abscissa E (increasing). Curves against photon energy (eV) include filter transmissions, mirror reflectivities, detector sensitivities (V/GW) and the responses of whole channels (V/GW); curves against time (ns) include area histories (DanteView). Outside the table, the end values are held (extrapolate = :hold, for factors and histories), or the curve is zero (:zero, for a whole response). The file form reads a CSV with a header line and two columns, the abscissa and the value.\n\nLinear interpolation is kept for every kind of curve. Henke's constants themselves are interpolated log-log (henke_mass_attenuation). A transmission tabulated from them and read back log-log, or as a power law in the attenuation, is not more accurate where an absorption edge falls between two points, and that case dominates the error of a channel response. On the filters of 17 of the 18 Dante 1 channels tabulated at 167 points per decade, the worst Planck-weighted response error is 4% read linearly and 9% read log-log. Log-log is far better only on smooth, steep curves, such as the 250 µm Al filter of channel 16 (44% read linearly against 0.3% read log-log). Tabulate a curve at the edge energies (research notes, \"Dante channel responses and the unfold\", interpolation of tabulated curves).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.TabulatedCurve"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.henke_mass_attenuation-Tuple{Symbol, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"henke_mass_attenuation(material, E) -> cm²/g\n\nPhotoabsorption mass attenuation coefficient of an element (:Al, :Cr, …) or compound (:CH, :lexan, :parylene, :saran, :polyimide, :mylar, :SiO2, …) at photon energy E (eV, 10–30 000) from Henke's f₂: μ/ρ = 2 r_e λ N_A Σ nᵢ f₂ᵢ / Σ nᵢ Aᵢ. The f₂ values come from the CXRO tables in data/henke/. Scattering is not included; it is < 1% of μ below ~10 keV for the filter materials used here. Absorption edges are tabulated as point pairs 0.2 eV apart, so they stay sharp under log–log interpolation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.henke_mass_attenuation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.mirror_reflectivity-Tuple{Symbol, Real, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"mirror_reflectivity(material, angle, E; roughness = 0, density = nothing) -> Float64\n\nSpecular reflectivity of a thick, flat material mirror (an element or compound, e.g. :SiO2) at grazing angle (degrees from the surface) and photon energy E (eV), for unpolarized light. The reflectivity is the mean of the s and p Fresnel reflectivities, with the refractive index n = 1 − δ − iβ from Henke's f₁, f₂ (data/henke/). For an rms roughness σ (nm), a Névot–Croce factor exp(−2 k_z0 k_z1 σ²) multiplies the amplitudes. density (g/cm³) defaults to the tabulated density of the material (SiO2 2.2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mirror_reflectivity"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.AbstractDanteChannel","page":"Radiation","text":"AbstractDanteChannel\n\nThe spectral response of a Dante channel: a DanteChannel (Gaussian approximation) or a DanteResponse (built from filters, mirror and XRD).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.AbstractDanteChannel"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.DanteChannel","page":"Radiation","text":"DanteChannel(energy, fwhm)\n\nThe spectral response of one Dante channel, approximated by a unit-peak Gaussian centered on energy (eV) with full width fwhm (eV): the fallback of dante1_channels(model = :gaussian).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DanteChannel"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.DanteView","page":"Radiation","text":"DanteView(theta, phi; through = :leh_bottom, pixels = 400, channels = dante1_channels(),\n          area = nothing, field_of_view = nothing, center = (0, 0, 0))\n\nA broadband spectrometer at port (θ, φ) (degrees). The visible surface is found by casting pixels × pixels parallel rays toward the target, since the detector is distant. Every element then counts with its visible projected area, and partial views and occlusion by any part are included. A ray through a transmitting aperture, such as a closing lip, is weighted by its open fraction (sol.transmission). The rays cover one of two regions:\n\nfield_of_view = nothing (default) gives the view through one entrance hole. The rays cover a square just enclosing the aperture component through and its closing lip <through>_lip, if the target has one.\nfield_of_view = D (mm) gives the collimated view. The rays cover a disk of diameter D normal to the line of sight about center, cut to the target extent, with pixels rays across it. center is a point (mm) or a part name, which stands for the area-weighted centroid of the part. The field of view of a NIF Dante is ±30 mm at TCC in the 2004 design (research notes, \"Dante-family hardware and geometry\"). Every surface of a target that faces the detector therefore lies inside it, such as walls seen through windows, packages and shields.\n\nA channel with a separate field_of_view (DanteResponse) records the signal over that field in place of the view field. That field is a disk of its diameter about center; in a view through a hole, it is the square about the hole cut to that disk. The signal of the channel comes from the footprint of that field, which is cast once per distinct field and target. Channels that share the field of the view therefore cost nothing more and record exactly what they record without a separate field. The intensity, Tr and the visible areas belong to the view.\n\nThe brightness temperature is\n\nT = (π I / (σ A cos θ_v))^¼,\n\nwith I the power per steradian toward the detector and A cos θ_v the reference area seen from the detector. By default A is the full initial area of through, aperture plus lip, as in the Dante convention of the pre-shot LEH size. θ_v is the angle between the line of sight and the normal of through. The keyword area sets A otherwise:\n\narea = A (mm²) replaces A, with the aperture θ_v, or cos θ_v = 1 without through.\narea = :part takes the visible projected area of the part as A cos θ_v. This is the convention of Fournier et al. 2010, who used the projected wall and cap.\narea = :open takes the open area of the hole at the solution time. That area is the aperture plus the area of each closing-lip element times its open fraction. It comes from the entrance-hole closure of the model, so Tr then equals Tr_open.\narea = TabulatedCurve(t, A) takes a measured area history, with time in ns, area in mm² and the end values held outside. Glenzer et al. 2010 used the hole area from SXI images.\n\nWith a field of view, through = nothing and no area give Tr = NaN, though the power is still computed. The footprint is computed once per target, or once per keyframe of a moving target. dante1() and dante2() give the two NIF Dantes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DanteView"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.dante-Tuple{DanteView, RadiationSolution}","page":"Radiation","text":"dante(view::DanteView, sol::RadiationSolution) -> NamedTuple\ndante(view::DanteView, s::SpectralSolution; unfold = :traditional, kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\nComputes the synthetic Dante signal. The result holds:\n\nintensity (TW/sr toward the detector);\nTr (eV), the brightness temperature over the reference area (see DanteView);\narea (mm²) and cos of the reference area, so that area × cos is its projection toward the detector;\nTr_open, the brightness temperature over the open area of the hole, which is the aperture plus the area of each closing-lip element times its open fraction. It is the radiation temperature behind the hole that the view samples. It equals Tr without lips and is NaN without through.\n\nWithout a reference area Tr is NaN, and the in-group spectrum and the unfold take the visible projected area instead, which gives a brightness temperature over what is seen. For a SpectralSolution the result also holds:\n\nspectrum (TW/sr per group) and edges;\nchannels, the signal of each channel over its field of view where it has one, with nothing from the open-ended top group;\ncolor_temperature (eV), the Planckian whose channel signals best match, in log, those of the channels below 1.5 keV, as the Dante unfold starts;\nmband_fraction, the fraction of the intensity above 1.8 keV, NaN without that edge.\n\nWith DanteResponse channels, the default of dante1_channels(), channels are the voltages Ω ∫ (dI/dE) R dE (V). The spectrum within each group is taken as a Planckian at Tr_open, the temperature behind the hole. The voltages are then unfolded as data are, by dante_unfold with method = unfold and kwargs over the area and angle of the view. The result then also holds unfold, the result of the unfold, and Tr_unfold, the unfolded radiation temperature to compare with Tr. unfold = nothing skips the unfold. With Gaussian DanteChannels, channels is ∫ (dI/dE) R(E) dE (TW/sr) with the spectrum flat within each group, and there is no unfold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.dante"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.dante1-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"dante1(; kwargs...)\n\nReturns NIF Dante 1, at port P143-274: θ 143.25°, φ 274°, 36.75° from the hohlraum axis (NIF User Guide 2024-09, Table 5-1; NIF_DIAGNOSTIC_PORTS). The papers round the angle to 37° (Rubery et al., RSI 2023; Rubery et al., PRL 132, 065104, 2024). The whole target is viewed over the collimated field of 60 mm at TCC, which was ±30 mm in the 2004 design (Dewald et al., RSI 75, 3759, 2004). Tr is taken over the initial area of the lower entrance hole, and the channels are those of dante1_responses. The view comes from the built-in definition nif_dante1, and field_of_view = nothing gives only the view through the hole. Definitions on the search path do not change it; diagnostic(\"nif_dante1\") takes them (diagnostic). kwargs replace the values of the definition with keywords of DanteView.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.dante1"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.dante1_channels-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"dante1_channels(; model = :hardware, resolution = 0.3) -> Vector\n\nThe 18 NIF Dante 1 channels (Rubery et al., RSI 2023, Fig. 4). model = :hardware (default): dante1_responses, each response built from the channel filters, grid, mirror and XRD cathode. model = :gaussian: the fallback approximation, a DanteChannel Gaussian at each published band energy (0.25–3.66 keV and one at 14.4 keV) of FWHM resolution × energy; it has no K- or L-edge structure, mirror cutoffs or high-energy tails, and resolution is an engineering choice.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.dante1_channels"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.dante2-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"dante2(; kwargs...)\n\nReturns NIF Dante 2, at port P64-350: θ 64°, φ 350° (NIF User Guide 2024-09, Table 5-1; Rubery et al., RSI 2023). It is a shallow view through the upper entrance hole, from the built-in definition nif_dante2. The channels and fields of view of Dante 2 are not public, so the definition has none. The view therefore looks through the hole with the channels of Dante 1 as an assumption (dante1_channels), without their per-channel fields. kwargs replace these defaults with keywords of DanteView, such as the channels of a group. diagnostic(\"nif_dante2\") takes definitions from the search path, where the channels can be given in a file that extends nif_dante2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.dante2"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.sentinel","page":"Radiation","text":"sentinel(dim = :polar; through, kwargs...)\n\nReturns the NIF Sentinel filtered-XRD array (Kemp et al., RSI 2020, LLNL-JRNL-813698), a DIM-mounted broadband spectrometer. It can be fielded on the polar DIM or on either equatorial DIM (NIF User Guide 2024-09, Table 5-1):\n\n:polar is port 000-000 at θ 0°, on the hohlraum axis, with the view straight down through the upper entrance hole.\n:equatorial_78 is P90-78 at φ 78.75°, and :equatorial_315 is P90-315. From either port a hohlraum is seen only through a diagnostic window, so through must name it.\n\nThe Sentinel filter set of 16 Al, Ni and Cr MiniXRDs behind Dante filters is not tabulated in Briareus. The channels default to those of Dante 1 (dante1_channels(), assumed) without their per-channel fields of view, since the Sentinel fields are not public. The channels of the shot configuration should replace them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.sentinel"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.visible_area-Tuple{DanteView, Target}","page":"Radiation","text":"visible_area(view::DanteView, target) -> Dict{Symbol,Float64}\n\nProjected area (mm², normal to the line of sight) of each part that the view sees, through its hole or within its field of view (closing lips counted fully open); the pieces of a part split for computation are summed into it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visible_area"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.DanteResponse","page":"Radiation","text":"DanteResponse(number, energy; filters = [], grid = 1, mirror = nothing, cathode,\n              solid_angle, tables = [], field_of_view = nothing)\nDanteResponse(number, energy; solid_angle, response::TabulatedCurve, field_of_view = nothing)\n\nOne Dante channel built from its hardware. filters are material => thickness pairs (thickness in mm) or material => (thickness, density), with transmission from Henke's tables (filter_transmission with table = :henke); tables are measured filter transmissions (TabulatedCurves); grid is the open fraction of a 25 µm tantalum mesh (opaque wires). The mirror is (:SiO2, angle°) for grazing incidence, calibrated to Rubery 2023 Fig. 3a; (:mlm, 1.9) for the Cr/Sc multilayer of channel 2; a GrazingMirror of another material; or a measured reflectivity as a TabulatedCurve. The cathode is an XRD, :Al, :Cr or :Ni (xrd_sensitivity), or a measured sensitivity as a TabulatedCurve in V/GW. The second form takes a measured whole-channel response (V/GW). energy (eV) is the nominal band energy and solid_angle (sr) that of the channel. Fields E (eV) and R (V/GW) tabulate the response on 50 eV – 30 keV; a spectrum dI/dE (GW/sr/eV) gives the voltage V = Ω ∫ (dI/dE) R dE (Harris et al., RSI 92, 033505, 2021, eq. 1).\n\nfield_of_view (mm) is the collimated field of the channel, the diameter of the disk seen at the view center in place of the field of the view (DanteView). With nothing, the default, the channel takes the field of the view.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DanteResponse"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.GrazingMirror","page":"Radiation","text":"GrazingMirror(material, angle; roughness = 0, density = nothing)\n\nA grazing-incidence mirror of material at angle (degrees from the surface). The material is an element or compound of henke_mass_attenuation, such as :C, :Be and :B4C. Its reflectivity is mirror_reflectivity with roughness (nm rms) and density (g/cm³), which defaults to that of the material. The reflectivity is Fresnel from Henke's constants and is uncalibrated. The SiO₂ mirrors of NIF Dante take (:SiO2, angle) instead, the model calibrated to Rubery et al. 2023 Fig. 3a (DanteResponse).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.GrazingMirror"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.dante1_responses-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"dante1_responses() -> Vector{DanteResponse}\n\nReturns the 18 NIF Dante 1 channels as configured for ICF experiments from October 2021 (Rubery et al., RSI 2023, Fig. 4). The configuration covers the filters, the 8.8% grids, two SiO₂ mirrors and the 2–4 keV multilayer, the Al, Cr and Ni XRDs, and the solid angles. Each channel is built as a DanteResponse from the built-in definition nif_dante1_2021 (diagnostic). The transcription and its assumptions are recorded in data/diagnostics/nif_dante1_2021.toml. The channels are built once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.dante1_responses"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.dante_unfold-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:DanteResponse}, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Radiation","text":"dante_unfold(channels, voltages; area, cos = 1, method = :traditional,\n             below = 1500, mband = (1.0, 2500.0, 600.0), λ = 1e-3, knots = 48,\n             tol = 1e-4, maxiter = 50) -> NamedTuple\n\nUnfolds Dante channel voltages (V) into a spectrum, as the data are processed. The channels are of type DanteResponse, and nonpositive or non-finite entries are dropped. area (mm²) and cos, the cosine of the angle between the line of sight and the emitter normal, turn the power per steradian into a radiation temperature, Tr = (π I / (σ A cos))^¼.\n\nBoth methods start as the Dante analysis does (Rubery et al., RSI 2023 §II.C; Harris et al., RSI 92, 033505, 2021 §II). Each channel below below eV in nominal energy is matched by a separate Planckian, which gives its channel_temperatures entry, and the start temperature is their mean.\n\nmethod = :traditional is Harris's algorithm. The start spectrum is that Planckian plus a Gaussian M-band mband = (amplitude GW/sr/eV, center eV, FWHM eV). Harris uses amplitude 1 at 2.5 keV and a \"width\" of 600 eV, which dante_unfold takes as the FWHM. The response of each channel is replaced by a unit-peak Gaussian, centered between its half-maximum points with their distance as the FWHM, and the Gaussian amplitudes are adjusted until the computed voltage of every channel equals the measured one. In Harris's sweeps the amplitudes are set from the highest-energy channel down, each to match its channel, for up to maxiter sweeps and to tol. Duplicate channels (e.g. Dante 1's 1/8, 10/14 and 7/15) have identical Gaussians, and the sweep then diverges; where the sweeps do not converge, the same equations are solved directly by least squares in the relative voltages, with minimum-norm amplitudes. The solver field of the result is the method that ran. The spectrum may go negative locally, as in the original.\nmethod = :smooth gives a positive, smooth unfold. The spectrum is the start Planckian, without an M-band, times a piecewise-linear factor on knots log-spaced knots over 50 eV – 20 keV. The factor is fitted by nonnegative least squares to the relative voltages with a second-difference penalty λ, so a Planckian is recovered exactly. The cubic-spline unfold of Barnak et al. (RSI 91, 073102, 2020) is similar but does not use a prior.\n\nReturns a named tuple with fields:\n\nenergy (eV grid), spectrum (GW/sr/eV) and intensity (TW/sr, 50 eV – 20 keV);\nTr (eV) and start_temperature;\nchannel_temperatures, per channel, NaN above below;\nfit (V), the voltages of the unfolded spectrum;\nmisfit, the largest |fit/V − 1| over the channels used;\nsolver, which is :sweep, :direct or :nnls;\niterations, the number of sweeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.dante_unfold"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.xrd_sensitivity-Tuple{Symbol, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"xrd_sensitivity(cathode, E) -> V/GW\n\nReturns the sensitivity of a NIF Dante XRD-31 x-ray diode with an :Al, :Cr or :Ni cathode at photon energy E (eV). The sensitivity is in volts into 50 Ω per GW of x-rays on the cathode, where 1 MV/GW = 2 × 10⁻⁵ A/W. The model is a Henke total-yield model fitted to the curves digitized from Rubery et al., RSI 2023. Cr is fitted to Fig. 3b over 0.075–1 keV and Al to Fig. 8b over 1–4.5 keV. Ni takes the Cr parameters as an assumption, since no curve is published for it. See data/rubery2023_dante.csv and the research notes, \"Dante channel responses and the unfold\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.xrd_sensitivity"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.Keyframes","page":"Radiation","text":"Keyframes(build, times, beams; closed = nothing, keep = 2, deposition_kw = (;),\n          viewfactor_kw = (;))\n\nA target that changes through the pulse. build(τ) makes its Target at time τ (ns), with the same elements at every time (e.g. moving_hohlraum). The view factors and the deposition operator of beams are computed at the keyframe times. Between two keyframes, model_at interpolates the exchange matrix Aᵢ Fᵢⱼ linearly in time and divides by the areas of build(τ), which preserves reciprocity. It rebalances the matrix when closed is set. By default closed is the declaration of the target, and it may be true, false or cavities of the elements of build(times[1]), as in RadiationModel. Deposition between keyframes uses an operator built at that time (deposition_at). Before the first keyframe and after the last, the nearest keyframe is used. The error against rebuilding at every time falls as the square of the spacing. On a NIF hohlraum whose wall moves at 0.1 mm/ns, keyframes 2, 1 and 0.5 ns apart keep P2/P0 within 0.035, 0.012 and 0.009 percentage points (research notes, \"Moving wall\").\n\nKeyframes are built when first needed, and only the keep most recently used models are held, since the view factors of a fine hohlraum take over 1 GB each. Deposition operators are kept. A history advances forward in time, so each keyframe is built once.\n\ndrive_history(kf, beams_at, times; ...) runs a drive history on the moving target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Keyframes"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.WallMotion","page":"Radiation","text":"WallMotion(; speed = 0.185, start = 0.8, saturation = 0.45, knee = 0.05)\n\nInward motion of the radiating wall of a hohlraum. The wall cylinder lies at radius R₀ - d(t) (mm, ns), where R₀ is the initial wall radius and the displacement\n\nd(t) = stagnate(speed · max(0, t − start), saturation, knee)\n\nis ballistic from start and stops smoothly at saturation. It follows the straight line up to saturation − knee, then approaches saturation as saturation − knee · exp(−(x − saturation + knee)/knee), which is C¹ at the knee and has the form of bubble_depth. With saturation = Inf the wall keeps moving at speed.\n\nThe radius stands for that of the LLNL dynamic view-factor model (Young et al., PoP 27, 082702, 2020), which is the average of the laser-absorption and x-ray-emission surfaces of a 2D HYDRA simulation, as one cylinder. The defaults are fitted to the hohlraum radius of that model for N170827 (Young Fig. 3a, digitized as young2020_radii). The wall starts moving at the end of the picket, 0.8 ns after the turn-on (fit 0.78, range 0.68–0.85). It moves in at 0.185 mm/ns (0.175–0.195) and stops 0.45 mm in (0.445–0.455, ±0.03 reading), where it stays through the peak. start is therefore the turn-on of the outer cones plus 0.8 ns, and the default assumes a turn-on at 0. For HYBRID-E (N221204) the HYDRA laser-absorption edge of the wall at the waist is 0.38 ± 0.04 mm in at 6 ns (PRE 109, 025204 Fig. 1a, digitized as pre109_fig1a_wall). The emission surface lies outside it, so this value is an upper bound on the saturation of that platform. Chen et al. 2020 (G-LEH) observed the emitting wall 0.5 mm in by 5.5 ns at 0.3 mg/cc and 0.4 mm in at 0.45 mg/cc. See the research notes, \"Hohlraum dynamics calibrated to published data (2026-10-24)\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.WallMotion"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.chen2020_leh-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"chen2020_leh() -> NamedTuple\n\nMeasured effective LEH radius against time on five HDC basecamp shots (5.75 × 10.13 mm Au hohlraum, 3.37 mm LEHs, r₀ = 1.685 mm, about 400 TW from about 4.2 to 6 ns), from H. Chen et al., Phys. Plasmas 27, 022702 (2020), Fig. 27. The values were taken from the vector graphics of the accepted manuscript (data/chen2020_fig27.csv, sources in its header). The columns are:\n\nshot: N171010, N171212, N180102, N180103, N180117;\nkind: \"gleh\" for the edge from the gated LEH imager, with an analysis error of 10–20 µm; \"lasnex_f003_regional\", \"lasnex_f003_global\" and \"lasnex_f015_global\" for LASNEX images analyzed the same way; \"laser\" for the total delivered power;\nt (ns) and value (radius in mm, power in TW).\n\nThe table is the calibration data of LipClosure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.chen2020_leh"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.deposit-Tuple{Keyframes, Any, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"deposit(kf::Keyframes, beams, τ) -> Deposition\n\nLaser deposition of beams (on the ports of the keyframes, in order) on the moving target at time τ, with deposition_at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.deposit"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.deposition_at-Tuple{Keyframes, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"deposition_at(kf::Keyframes, τ) -> DepositionOperator\n\nThe deposition operator of the beams of the keyframes on the moving target at time τ. It is the operator of a keyframe, or between two keyframes an operator built on build(τ); the last one is kept. Unlike the view factors, it is not interpolated. An operator costs a tenth of the view factors, and interpolating depositions blurs footprints that slide with the wall. On a NIF hohlraum at 0.1 mm/ns with six keyframes, interpolation put P2/P0 off by 0.24 points, against 0.035 for the rebuilt operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.deposition_at"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.drive_history-Tuple{Keyframes, Any, Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"drive_history(kf::Keyframes, beams_at, times; kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\ndrive_history on a moving target: each time is solved on model_at(kf, τ) with the deposition deposit(kf, beams_at(τ), τ). Keywords and results are those of the fixed-target history, and areas, moments and spherical harmonics are taken on the target of each time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.drive_history"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.kritcher2024_capsule-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"kritcher2024_capsule() -> NamedTuple\n\nThe scaled capsule radius R/R(0) from HYDRA against time on the 2.05 MJ drive of N221204, from Kritcher et al., PoP 31, 070502 (2024) Fig. 1, digitized (data/kritcher2024_fig1.csv, sources in its header). The columns are t (ns, 0–9.5 every 0.25), n221204 (the as-shot, thicker capsule), n210808 (the thinner N210808 capsule on the same drive, NaN after its minimum at 9.18 ns) and u (reading error of n221204). The paper does not state which radius is plotted. The radius stays constant until about 3.6 ns, near the breakout of the first shock from the ablator into the fuel (3.86–3.91 ns in keyhole N230110, the N221204 design). That is about 2 ns before the ice–gas surface can move. The curve therefore follows the ablator–fuel interface (1.050 mm initially) or the center of mass of the fuel, and the ablation front is ruled out. It reaches its minimum at bang time (9.53 ns). It is the only published radius history of the N221204 capsule. See the research notes, \"Capsule radius schedules: published histories and the API (2026-10-26)\"; kritcher2024_radius gives it as a schedule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.kritcher2024_capsule"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.kritcher2024_radius","page":"Radiation","text":"kritcher2024_radius(capsule = :n221204) -> TabulatedRadius\n\nkritcher2024_capsule as a RadiusSchedule. :n221204 gives the R/R(0) of the as-shot capsule on the drive of N221204 (0–9.5 ns). :n210808 gives the thinner N210808 capsule on the same drive, digitized to 9.0 ns and closed at its minimum, 0.055 at 9.18 ns; the curve after the minimum is not digitized. These are published HYDRA histories (Kritcher et al., PoP 31, 070502 (2024) Fig. 1, digitized). Applied to the outer radius of the capsule, they are the upper bound on the ablation front, for a shell of constant relative thickness. For another shot, ScaledRadius moves the minimum to its bang time (derived).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.kritcher2024_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.model_at-Tuple{Keyframes, Real}","page":"Radiation","text":"model_at(kf::Keyframes, τ) -> RadiationModel\n\nThe radiation model of the moving target at time τ. It is the model of a keyframe, or between two keyframes the target build(τ) with interpolated exchange factors (see Keyframes). The last interpolated model is kept, so repeated calls at one time build it once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.model_at"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.moving_hohlraum-Tuple{WallMotion}","page":"Radiation","text":"moving_hohlraum(motion::WallMotion; radius = 2.875, kwargs...) -> (τ -> Target)\n\nThe hohlraum(; radius, kwargs...) at time τ with its wall moved in by motion. The cylinder has radius wall_radius(motion, radius, τ). The caps stay in their planes and the entrance holes keep their radius, so the annuli of the caps shrink with the wall. Rounded corners follow the wall, and the capsule stays in place. The mesh counts are those of the initial radius (from h when given), so every time has the same elements, as Keyframes requires.\n\ncapsule_shrink shrinks the capsule mesh itself to R₀ R/R₀ at τ, with the mesh counts of the initial radius (the frequency of the :compact mesh, or the lat-long counts). It is nothing, a RadiusSchedule, a (times, R/R₀) table (linear and held at its ends), or a function τ -> R/R₀. The flux is then evaluated on the shrinking sphere instead of the initial one. The albedo of the capsule should then not shrink it again, so a CapsuleAlbedo is given without a radius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.moving_hohlraum"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.pre109_fig1a_wall-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"pre109_fig1a_wall() -> NamedTuple\n\nThe inner edge of the wall material at 6 ns in the HYDRA simulations of N210808 and N221204 drawn in PRE 109, 025204 (2024) Fig. 1a, digitized (data/pre109_fig1a_wall.csv, sources in its header). Columns z (mm from the waist, every 0.1 mm), n210808, n221204 (edge radius, mm; initial wall 3.2 mm) and region (\"waist\", \"inner\", \"collision\", \"outer\", \"bubble\", \"lip\"). Laser rays are drawn over the plasma, so the edge is where they end (the laser-absorption surface) or, under crossing rays, an upper bound on the radius. The waist rows (z < 0.9 mm) give the usable number, which puts the wall 0.38 ± 0.04 mm in at 6 ns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pre109_fig1a_wall"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.wall_displacement-Tuple{WallMotion, Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"Wall displacement (mm) of w at time t (ns): ballistic, then stopping at saturation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.wall_displacement"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.wall_radius-Tuple{WallMotion, Any, Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"Wall radius (mm) at time t (ns) of a wall that starts at radius R0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.wall_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.young2020_albedos-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"young2020_albedos() -> NamedTuple\n\nThe albedos of the LLNL dynamic view-factor model for N170827 against time, from Young et al., PoP 27, 082702 (2020), Fig. 3a top panel, digitized (data/young2020_fig3a_albedo.csv, sources in its header). The columns are t (ns, 0–8 every 0.25, plus 0.55–0.65 on the drop of the wall), wall and capsule (quasi-1D HYDRA albedos of gold and the HDC ablator) and their reading errors u_wall, u_capsule (0.03; 0.05 where the static line hides the curve or on the drop). The static model uses 0.8 and 0.3. With young2020_radii the table defines the dynamic model of Young, which TabulatedAlbedo builds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.young2020_albedos"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.young2020_radii-Tuple{}","page":"Radiation","text":"young2020_radii() -> NamedTuple\n\nThe radii of the LLNL dynamic view-factor model for N170827 against time, from Young et al., PoP 27, 082702 (2020), Fig. 3a, digitized (data/young2020_fig3a.csv, sources in its header). The columns are t (ns, 0–8 every 0.25), wall (the hohlraum radius, the average of the HYDRA laser-absorption and x-ray-emission surfaces), leh (from SXI, of which only the end value is measured) and capsule (ablation front), all in mm, and their reading errors u_wall, u_leh, u_capsule (0.03 mm, 0.05 where the static line hides the curve). The plotted initial wall radius is 3.0 mm, so displacements should be used. The table is the calibration target of the defaults of WallMotion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.young2020_radii"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.CapsuleModes","page":"Radiation","text":"CapsuleModes(model; observe = :capsule, modes = (1 => 1.0, 2 => 1.0, 3 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0),\n             components = ())\n\nThe drive of a capsule for retune_drive. The quantity is the real spherical-harmonic coefficients c_lm of the incident x-ray flux on observe in the radiation model model (ylm_weights about the center of the observed elements, in mode_index order), with level c_00. The shape terms weight each degree l => w of modes over its 2l + 1 coefficients, and each (l, m) => w of components separately, for example the l = 1, m = ±1 drift of a dropped bundle. The reference is a drive_history run with ylm = true (through the highest degree weighted) and keep_albedo = true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CapsuleModes"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.FaceFlux","page":"Radiation","text":"FaceFlux(model, face; uniformity = 1.0)\n\nThe x-ray drive of a face for retune_drive. The quantity is the incident flux H_e (TW/mm²) on each element of face (a part or component name, or a tuple of them) in the radiation model model, and the level is the area mean H̄ = Σ A_e H_e / Σ A_e. The shape terms are uniformity × Σ_e (A_e/ΣA) (H_e/H̄ - ŝ_e)², times (H̄/L_t)² (see retune_drive). For the uniform target (ŝ = 1) this is the square of the RMS nonuniformity of the face, rms_nonuniformity(H, A). The quantity is meant for a flat face, such as the face of a package in a halfraum or an opacity sample, whose flux has no modes to restore. The reference is a drive_history run with observe = face, observed_flux = true and keep_albedo = true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.FaceFlux"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.FoilIrradiance","page":"Radiation","text":"FoilIrradiance(foil; center = nothing, radius = Inf, side = 1, uniformity = 1.0)\n\nThe laser drive of a foil, such as a backlighter, for retune_drive. The quantity is the absorbed laser irradiance I_e = P_e/A_e (TW/mm²) of each element of the part or component foil on side side (1 front, -1 back) whose centroid lies within radius (mm) of center. By default center is the area centroid of the foil elements on that side. The level is the mean Σ P_e / Σ A_e, and the shape terms are those of FaceFlux. These are the uniformity and intensity terms of irradiance_objective. Its spill term has no counterpart here, since the pointing is fixed. No radiation model is used, since the deposition is linear in the powers. The reference is (; time, deposition), the absorbed laser Deposition of the reference shot at each time, with every color that it fires (e.g. [deposit(op, with_pulses(beams, pulses, τ)) for τ in times]). With target = :uniform and a level, the reference is only (; time).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.FoilIrradiance"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.RetuneQuantity","page":"Radiation","text":"RetuneQuantity\n\nThe quantity that retune_drive holds through time. It is a vector y of the drive, linear in the powers of the beam groups at fixed albedos, with a level L = μ'y and a shape y/L. The kinds are CapsuleModes (the x-ray flux on a capsule, by its real spherical-harmonic coefficients), FaceFlux (the x-ray flux on each element of a face, such as the face of a package in a halfraum) and FoilIrradiance (the absorbed laser irradiance on the spot of a foil, without a radiation model).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.RetuneQuantity"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.cone_fraction_pulses-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"cone_fraction_pulses(beams, times, total, fraction; inner = (:inner23, :inner30))\n    -> Dict{Symbol,PulseShape}\n\nPer-group pulses (power per beam, TW) from a total laser power history total (TW, at times ns) and a cone fraction history fraction (the inner-cone share of the total), in the way NIF pulses are specified. The beams of the inner groups share fraction × total equally, and the beams of the other groups share the rest. The pulses are keyed by the group of each beam, for with_pulses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.cone_fraction_pulses"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.read_pulse_table-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Radiation","text":"read_pulse_table(path) -> Dict{Symbol,PulseShape}\n\nPulses per group, quad or beam from a CSV table in the layout of write_pulse_table, with a time_ns column and one column per key (power per beam, TW). The measured or requested powers of a reference shot are read this way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_pulse_table"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.retune_drive-Tuple{RetuneQuantity, DepositionOperator, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any}","page":"Radiation","text":"retune_drive(quantity, op, beams, reference; target = :reference, level = nothing,\n             by = :group, limits = nothing, smooth = 0.0, nominal = nothing, stay = 0.0,\n             unconverted = nothing, prepulse = nothing, colors = nif_unconverted(),\n             unconverted_kw = (;)) -> NamedTuple\n\nPower histories for beams that hold a drive quantity through the times of reference, for example after a bundle is dropped. Beams are grouped by :group, :quad, :beam or a function beam -> key, with one power per beam of a group at each time. The quantity is the modes of a capsule (CapsuleModes), the x-ray flux on a face (FaceFlux) or the laser irradiance on a foil (FoilIrradiance); their docstrings list what reference must contain. op is the deposition operator of beams, for example that of the reference restricted to the remaining beams, op[keep]. Its pointing is fixed through the pulse.\n\nTarget. The vector y of the quantity (the c_lm of a capsule, the H_e of a face, the I_e of a foil) has a level L = μ'y (c_00, or the area mean) and a shape y/L. At each time the target is a shape ŝ and a level L_t. The shape is set by target:\n\ntarget = :reference (the default): the full shape of the reference, ŝ = y_ref/L_ref. This is the modes of a capsule, or the flux distribution of a face or a foil, and so also its RMS nonuniformity and peak-to-mean ratio;\ntarget = :uniform: a uniform drive (c_lm = 0 for l ≥ 1, or a flat flux), which is the least nonuniformity that the powers allow.\n\nL_t is level (TW/mm², a number or a function of time in ns) when given, and otherwise the level L_ref of the reference. target = :uniform, level = I₀ thus gives the most uniform irradiance of a foil at the mean I₀.\n\nSolve. At each reference time y is linear in the powers P of the groups, y = M P + y₀, where y₀ is the part of the prepulse (below). For the x-ray quantities the albedos are fixed at the converged albedos of the reference (flux_response(...; albedo)). The powers minimize\n\nΣ_i w_i ((y_i - ŝ_i L)/L_t)² + smooth Σ_g ((P_g - P_g,prev)/P_scale)²\n    + stay Σ_g ((P_g - P_g,nominal)/P_scale)²\n\nat the level L = L_t, with the weights w_i of the quantity. For a capsule they are its modes and components. For a face or a foil they are uniformity × A_e/ΣA, and the first sum is then the square of the RMS departure from ŝ. The powers lie within the bounds limits (TW per beam), which are nothing (from 0, unbounded above), a Dict key => (lo, hi) (with (0, Inf) for missing keys), or a function (key, t) -> (lo, hi). Examples are the power capability of a quad, or (0, 0) for a dropped quad. smooth ties the powers of each step to those of the previous step, and stay ties them to nominal powers, such as those of the reference shot. nominal is a Dict key => PulseShape or a function (key, t) -> P. Among the many power sets that restore the drive, stay selects the one closest to the nominal powers, instead of one that runs many quads to their limits. The problem is a box-bounded linear least-squares problem per time. The level is held by a penalty row whose target is shifted until the level of the solution is L_t, by the secant method on that level. The level is piecewise linear in the shift, so one step suffices while the active bounds are unchanged. A penalty without the shift would pull the level down by about the square of the nonuniformity (L/L_t ≈ 1/(1 + RMS²)), a percent at an RMS of 10%. Where the bounds cannot reach L_t, the level is the nearest they allow, and drive reports it.\n\nUnconverted light. For a reference run with unconverted (1ω, 2ω) light, the unconverted, prepulse, colors and unconverted_kw of that history are passed here (see drive_history), so that the retune uses the same per-color sources:\n\ny = (R₃ E + Σ_c R_c E_c(t)) P + Σ_c R_c Q_c p_c(t).\n\nR₃ and R_c are the 3ω and per-color responses, from flux_response(...; unconverted) for the x-ray quantities; for a foil, the absorbed power of each color adds to the irradiance. E is the 3ω absorbed power of each group at 1 TW per beam. E_c(t) is its absorbed power in color c at 1 TW per beam, times the fraction r_c(t) of each beam. It is the sum, over the beams of the group on each fraction schedule, of unconverted_deposition of those beams at that fraction. p_c(t) is the prepulse of the color, fixed powers deposited by Q_c. The fractions and prepulses are keyed by by, as the retuned groups are. The fraction of a color may also be a vector with one entry per beam of beams, so the beams of a retuned group may have different fractions (e.g. 1ω only on some beams of a quad). The prepulse is not retuned, and it shifts the target of the least squares. As for the 3ω response, the conversions must not depend on intensity. A reference history with unconverted light and no unconverted here is an error.\n\nReturns (; time, keys, power, pulses, shape, target_shape, level, drive, rms, peak, residual). These are the per-beam powers (length(keys) × nt), the same powers as PulseShapes for with_pulses (with by the same key), the predicted shape y/L and the target ŝ (ny × nt), and the predicted level L (TW/mm²) and L/L_t. For a face or a foil they include the predicted RMS nonuniformity and peak-to-mean ratio max(y)/L (nothing for a capsule), and finally the weighted residual norm per time. A time whose limits fix every power (lo == hi) is not solved. An example is (0, 0) where the reference has no 3ω light, as with only a prepulse or in a gap between pulses. The powers at such a time are the limits, the target level may be zero, and the residual is NaN. For the x-ray quantities the prediction is linear about the albedos of the reference, and a drive_history of the pulses, where the albedos follow the new drive, confirms it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.retune_drive"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.write_pulse_table-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractDict}","page":"Radiation","text":"write_pulse_table(path, pulses) -> path\n\nWrite pulses (power per beam, TW, against time, ns) per group, quad or beam as CSV. The table has a time_ns column and one column per key, on the union of the times of the pulses. read_pulse_table reads the table back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_pulse_table"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/radiation/#Briareus.montecarlo_incident-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Radiation","text":"montecarlo_incident(target, laser_power, receivers; paths = 10_000, batches = 32,\n                    reemit = :hit, albedo = albedos(target), seed = 1)\n    -> (H, batch)\n\nElement-averaged incident x-ray flux (TW/mm²) on the receivers by Monte Carlo transport, with no view factors, for checking view_factors and solve against independent ray tracing. Sources are those of solve, ηPᵢ/Aᵢ uniform over each element, from the absorbed laser power laser_power (per element).\n\nPaths are traced backward from the receivers, by reciprocity of the Lambertian kernel. Each path starts at a uniform point on the facets of the receiver, in a cosine-weighted direction, and finds the first facet hit (closest_hit over every triangle, apertures included), whose element contributes its source. The path continues diffusely with its weight multiplied by the albedo of the element, with Russian roulette below 1/20. It ends on an aperture (escape), on the back of a one-sided surface, or on a miss. A closing lip (aperture(...; closure), a GapClosure) is the exception among apertures. Like a surface, it re-emits diffusely the fraction albedo of the radiation that reaches it, its α(t) = s α_after from albedos(target, time) as solve uses it, and the rest escapes. The lip has no source. Apertures stay transparent to laser rays and lines of sight elsewhere, and here the lip plane is the boundary of the enclosure. albedo defaults to the albedos at time 0. albedos(target, time) (or the albedo of a solution) gives a comparison with a solve at a later time. The sum of each path is an unbiased estimate of the incident flux, as a forward photon tally would be, but every sample is scored on a receiver.\n\nreemit = :hit continues from the hit point. This is the transport of the continuum, with radiosity varying within elements, which is the limit to which the element model converges. reemit = :element continues from a uniform point on the hit element. This is the element model with exact view factors, visibility included, which solve should reproduce up to its view-factor error.\n\nGeometry is the facets throughout. A CurvedSurface is ignored, so the comparison should use a target with flat elements (the same meshes without surfaces). H is the mean over batches independent batches of paths ÷ batches paths per receiver. batch, with one row per batch, gives the statistical errors of any functional of H. Batch b draws from a separate Xoshiro stream (from seed and b), and batches run on threads, so the results do not depend on the thread count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.montecarlo_incident"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/frames/#Frames-and-placement","page":"Frames and placement","text":"A Frame is a rigid frame in the chamber: its axes as the columns of a rotation and its origin, so that local coordinates map to the chamber by to_chamber and back by to_frame. compose places one frame in another, and inv inverts one. Frames come from closed-form constructors: euler_rotation and euler_frame (VisRad's polar, azimuthal and rotation angles), port_frame (the axis of a port), foil_frame (the frame in which backlighter_foil places its foil) and component_frame (a part of a scripted target, about its part_centroid). coords_of and point_of convert between chamber points and Cartesian, cylindrical or spherical coordinates in a frame.\n\nFrameDef places a named frame in a parent frame by an offset and three angles, and resolve_frames resolves a set of them, depth first, from base frames such as target-chamber center. The PlacementSteps of a definition then move it relative to other frames, in order, each in closed form: relations (Coincident, OnAxis, Concentric, Distance, Facing, Parallel, Roll) fix degrees of freedom, and relative moves (Offset, Rotate) fix none. A relation that undoes an earlier one is an error naming both; placement_report gives the degrees of freedom each step fixes, redundant steps, what stays free, and the residuals left by relative moves. The steps run in sequence, without a simultaneous solver: every relation has a closed form, the result is deterministic, and an error names one step.\n\nFramePointing parameterizes beam aims as local coordinates (u, v, w) of a frame per beam, with the non-aim fields of GroupPointing. Examples are the beams of a backlighter optimized across the foil face, and the cones of a hohlraum in the frames of their entrance holes, where one local aim gives mirrored chamber aims. It works with Scenario, subset and optimize_pointing.\n\nplacement_sweep steps a design through a sequence of placements, such as a foil moved along its line of sight with its beams following, and checks each step for beam clearance, an unobstructed line of sight and beam capture, optionally re-optimizing the pointing with a warm start. SweepResult contains one SweepRow per step, and write_sweep_csv writes them. The Backlighter placement sweep tutorial runs the whole sequence.","title":"Frames and placement"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.CHAMBER","page":"Frames and placement","text":"CHAMBER\n\nThe chamber frame: identity axes at target-chamber center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CHAMBER"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Frame","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Frame(R, origin)\nFrame(origin; z, x = θ̂ of z)\n\nA right-handed rigid frame in the chamber. The columns of R are the unit axes of the frame, and origin is its origin (mm). A point with local coordinates x therefore lies at R * x + origin in the chamber (to_chamber), and a chamber point p has local coordinates R' * (p - origin) (to_frame). CHAMBER is the identity frame at target-chamber center (TCC).\n\nThe keyword form builds the frame from its z axis, of any length, and a vector x that sets the direction of its x axis. The x axis is x minus its component along z, normalized. By default x is the polar unit vector θ̂ of z (port_basis), which lies along chamber x when z is the pole. y = z × x completes the right-handed triad. An x parallel to z throws an ArgumentError.\n\nA frame is Float64 and constant; the points mapped through it may carry ForwardDiff duals (the aims of FramePointing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Frame"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.FrameDef","page":"Frames and placement","text":"FrameDef(parent = :tcc; offset = (0, 0, 0), rotation = (0, 0, 0), steps = [])\n\nA frame placed in another frame. Its origin lies at offset (mm) in the frame named parent, and its axes are turned by rotation = (polar, azimuth, rotation) in degrees in the parent (euler_rotation). Its chamber frame is therefore compose(parent's frame, euler_frame(offset, rotation...)). The PlacementSteps steps then apply in order, as relations to other frames and relative moves. Degrees of freedom that no step fixes keep the values of offset and rotation. The frame of a port is FrameDef(:tcc; rotation = (θ, φ, roll)) (port_frame). Resolve a set of named definitions with resolve_frames.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.FrameDef"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.component_frame-Tuple{Target, Symbol}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"component_frame(target, part) -> Frame\n\nReturns a frame for a part of a target built in a script. Parts made in the GUI have separate placement frames. The origin is at the part_centroid of the part, and the z axis is the first of these that applies:\n\nThe axis of its exact surface, such as the axis of a CylinderSurface, ConeSurface or TorusSurface or the normal of a DiskSurface. This rule applies when every piece with such a surface has a parallel one, and the axis of the first piece sets the sign.\nThe area-weighted mean front normal of its triangles, when that mean is at least 0.1 in length. For a flat foil this is its front normal; for a hemispherical cup the mean is 0.5 long.\nThe principal axis of its triangle area moments whose moment differs most from the other two, which is the symmetry axis of a body of revolution. The axis is signed toward chamber +z, else +x, else +y. When the three moments agree within 1e-6, as for spheres and cubes, the axis is chamber z.\n\nThe x axis is θ̂ of z (see Frame). The choice is ambiguous for shapes with no axis, such as spheres and closed boxes, and for the sign of a principal axis. The 0.1 threshold of rule 2 is an engineering default. Give the frame explicitly when it matters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.component_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.compose-Tuple{Frame, Frame}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"compose(parent, child) -> Frame\n\nReturns the chamber frame of child, a frame given in the local coordinates of parent, so that to_chamber(compose(parent, child), x) == to_chamber(parent, to_chamber(child, x)) within round-off.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.compose"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.coords_of","page":"Frames and placement","text":"coords_of(p, system = :cartesian; frame = CHAMBER) -> NTuple{3,Float64}\n\nReturns the coordinates of the chamber point p in frame, in one of three systems:\n\n:cartesian (the default) gives (x, y, z).\n:cylindrical gives (r, φ, z): the distance from the frame z axis, the azimuth in degrees in [0, 360) from x toward y, and the height.\n:spherical gives (r, θ, φ): the distance from the origin, the polar angle from z and the azimuth, in degrees.\n\nAt r = 0 the angles are 0. point_of is the inverse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.coords_of"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.euler_frame","page":"Frames and placement","text":"euler_frame(origin, polar, azimuth, rotation = 0) -> Frame\n\nReturns the frame at origin with axes euler_rotation(polar, azimuth, rotation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.euler_frame"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.euler_rotation","page":"Frames and placement","text":"euler_rotation(polar, azimuth, rotation = 0) -> SMatrix{3,3}\n\nReturns the rotation Rz(azimuth) · Ry(polar) · Rz(rotation) (degrees, right-handed). The rotation is a turn by rotation about z, a tilt by polar about y, and then a turn by azimuth about z. Its z column is port_vector(polar, azimuth). At rotation = 0 its x and y columns are the polar and azimuthal unit vectors θ̂ and φ̂ at that direction (port_basis). A frame with this rotation therefore has its z axis along the direction (polar, azimuth), and rotation turns its x axis from θ̂ toward φ̂. This is the order of the VisRad orientation angles Polar, Azimuthal and Rotation, which part placement in the GUI also uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.euler_rotation"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.foil_frame-Tuple{Any}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"foil_frame(los; standoff, tilt = 0.0, tilt_toward = 0.0, rotation = 0.0,\n           center = (0, 0, 0)) -> Frame\n\nReturns the frame of a backlighter foil on the line of sight los = (θ, φ) in degrees, as backlighter_foil places it:\n\nThe origin is at the foil center, center − standoff · port_vector(θ, φ).\nx lies along the width edge of the foil, y along its height edge, and z along its front normal, toward the detector.\n\nBefore the tilt, x is θ̂ of the line of sight turned by rotation toward φ̂. tilt then turns the whole frame off the line of sight toward cos(ψ) θ̂ + sin(ψ) φ̂, with ψ = tilt_toward in degrees. Local (u, v, 0) is a point on the foil face, with u across its width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.foil_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.part_centroid-Tuple{Target, Symbol}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"part_centroid(target, part) -> Vec3\npart_centroid(target) -> Dict{Symbol,Vec3}\n\nReturns the area-weighted centroid of the triangles of part, over all its pieces (see part_pieces). For parts such as foils, disks and entrance holes the centroid is the center, a reference point to aim beams at. The second form gives the centroid of every part of the target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.part_centroid"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.point_of","page":"Frames and placement","text":"point_of(c, system = :cartesian; frame = CHAMBER) -> Vec3\n\nReturns the chamber point with coordinates c in frame, in the order of coords_of: :cartesian (x, y, z), :cylindrical (r, φ, z) or :spherical (r, θ, φ), with angles in degrees. A negative radius throws an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.point_of"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.port_angles-Tuple{Symbol, Any}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"port_angles(facility::Symbol, port) -> (theta, phi)\n\nReturns the polar angle and azimuth (degrees) of a facility port from the facility table:\n\n:nif: an indirect-drive quad port, by its number (NIF_PORTS, NIF User Guide Table 4-1) or its quad name such as \"Q31B\" (nif_port), or a diagnostic port by name such as \"P143-274\" (NIF_DIAGNOSTIC_PORTS, Table 5-1);\n:omega: a beam port by name, \"B01\"–\"B60\", in the hohlraum frame of omega_ports.\n\nAn unknown port throws a KeyError, and another facility throws an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.port_angles"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.port_frame-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"port_frame(theta, phi; roll = 0, origin = (0, 0, 0)) -> Frame\n\nport_frame(beam::Beam; roll = 0, origin = (0, 0, 0)) -> Frame\nport_frame(facility::Symbol, port; roll = 0, origin = (0, 0, 0)) -> Frame\n\nReturns the frame of the port at polar angle theta and azimuth phi (degrees), with its origin at origin (TCC by default):\n\nz lies along port_vector(theta, phi), toward the port. A beam from the port therefore travels along -z, as the beam convention k = -port requires.\nx is the port θ̂ turned by roll (degrees) toward φ̂.\n\nThe frame equals euler_frame(origin, theta, phi, roll). The second form takes the port of a beam (port_angles), and the third takes a facility port by name (port_angles(facility, port)). According to the documentation of the VisRad coordinate transform tool, its diagnostic-port frames share the origin and z. The x axis along θ̂ is an assumption at low confidence, because VisRad port frames have not been seen in a sample workspace (research notes, \"Frames and placement (2026-10-28)\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.port_frame"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.resolve_frames","page":"Frames and placement","text":"resolve_frames(defs, base = Dict(:tcc => CHAMBER)) -> Dict{Symbol,Frame}\n\nReturns the chamber frames of the named definitions defs, given as a dictionary or a collection of name => FrameDef pairs. Each frame is placed in its parent and then moved by its placement steps. The parent is another definition or one of the base frames, which are analytic frames such as TCC, the entrance holes of a template and foils. :tcc is added as CHAMBER when absent, and the result also contains the base frames too. Definitions are resolved depth first, and the frames named by the steps of a definition are resolved before it. A frame therefore moves exactly with every ancestor and with every frame that its steps refer to. Each of these is an ArgumentError:\n\na definition that reuses the name of a base frame, or a name given twice;\nan unknown parent or step frame;\nframes that depend on each other in a loop, where the error names the loop, e.g. a → b → a;\na step that cannot apply, such as a facing point at the origin;\na step that undoes an earlier one (see placement_report).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.resolve_frames"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.to_chamber-Tuple{Frame, AbstractVector}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"to_chamber(frame, x; direction = false) -> SVector{3}\n\nReturns the chamber coordinates R * x + origin of the local point x of frame. With direction = true, it returns those of the local vector x, R * x, without translation. The element type follows x, so duals pass through.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.to_chamber"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.to_frame-Tuple{Frame, AbstractVector}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"to_frame(frame, p; direction = false) -> SVector{3}\n\nReturns the local coordinates R' * (p - origin) in frame of the chamber point p. With direction = true, it returns those of the chamber vector p, R' * p. It is the inverse of to_chamber.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.to_frame"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.transform_point-Tuple{Frame, Frame, Any}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"transform_point(from::Frame, to::Frame, c; input = :cartesian, output = :cartesian,\n                direction = false) -> NTuple{3,Float64}\ntransform_point(frames, from::Symbol, to::Symbol, c; …)\n\nReturns the coordinates in frame to of the point with coordinates c in frame from, after the VisRad coordinate transform tool. c is given in the system input and the result in output, each :cartesian, :cylindrical or :spherical (coords_of, point_of), in mm and degrees. With direction the three numbers are a vector, which is turned by the frame rotations and not moved by the frame origins. Its cylindrical or spherical coordinates are those of the vector tip from the origin. The second form looks the frames up by name in frames, a dictionary such as resolve_frames returns, and a missing name throws an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.transform_point"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Coincident","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Coincident(ref; point = (0, 0, 0))\n\nMove the origin of the frame onto the local point of the frame ref (mm). Fixes the three translations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Coincident"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Concentric","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Concentric(ref; axis = :z, flip = false)\n\nTurn the frame by the smallest rotation so that its z axis lies along the axis of ref (against it with flip), and move its origin onto that axis line at the nearest point. Fixes the two translations and the two rotations across the axis. The position along the axis and the roll about it stay free.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Concentric"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Distance","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Distance(ref, distance; point = (0, 0, 0))\n\nMove the origin of the frame along the line from the local point of ref through the origin, to distance (mm, ≥ 0) from the point. Fixes the translation along that line. An origin at the point is an ArgumentError, since the line is then undefined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Distance"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Facing","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Facing(ref; point = (0, 0, 0), side = :front)\n\nTurn the frame by the smallest rotation so that its z axis points at the local point of ref (side = :front) or away from it (side = :back). Fixes the two rotations across that direction, and the roll about it stays free. A point at the origin of the frame is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Facing"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Offset","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Offset(dx, dy, dz; frame = nothing)\n\nMove the frame by (dx, dy, dz) (mm) along its axes, or along the axes of the frame named frame. This is a relative move and does not fix a degree of freedom.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Offset"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.OnAxis","page":"Frames and placement","text":"OnAxis(ref; axis = :z, distance = nothing)\n\nMove the origin of the frame onto the line through the origin of ref along its axis (:x, :y, :z or a vector in the coordinates of ref). With distance (mm), the origin moves to that signed distance from the origin of ref along the axis, which fixes the three translations. Without it, the origin moves to the nearest point of the line, which fixes the two translations across it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.OnAxis"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Parallel","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Parallel(ref; axis = :z, flip = false)\n\nTurn the frame by the smallest rotation so that its z axis is parallel to the axis of ref (antiparallel with flip). Fixes the two rotations across the axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Parallel"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.PlacementReport","page":"Frames and placement","text":"PlacementReport\n\nThe degrees of freedom that the placement steps of a frame fix (placement_report). Fields:\n\nframe: the name of the frame;\nsteps;\nfixed: per step, the translations and rotations that it fixes and no earlier step fixed, as (translations, rotations);\nredundant: indices of relations that fix nothing new;\nfree_translation and free_rotation: orthonormal chamber directions and rotation axes that no step fixes. They keep the values of the offset and rotation of the frame;\nresiduals: per step, how far the final frame is from it, as (mm, degrees). The residual is zero for a relation that no later relative move disturbs, and for every relative move.\n\nThe rotations that a step fixes are counted at the orientation of the frame after the step. They are the axes across a z axis set by Facing, Parallel or Concentric, and that z axis for Roll.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PlacementReport"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.PlacementStep","page":"Frames and placement","text":"PlacementStep\n\nA step of the placement of a frame (the steps of a FrameDef), applied in order after the offset and rotation of the frame in its parent. Relations fix degrees of freedom: Coincident, OnAxis, Concentric, Distance, Facing, Parallel and Roll with a reference direction. Relative moves do not fix any: Offset, Rotate and Roll without a reference direction. Each step has a closed form, and a rotation turns the frame about its origin. Frames named by steps (ref) are resolved first, so a frame follows them as it follows its parent. placement_report gives the degrees of freedom that each step fixes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.PlacementStep"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Roll","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Roll(angle; from = nothing)\n\nTurn the frame about its z axis. With from (a frame name for its x axis, or (name, axis)), the x axis of the frame is set to angle degrees from the projection of that direction across z, turning toward y, which fixes the roll. A direction along z has no projection and is an ArgumentError. Without from, the frame turns by angle from the current roll, a relative move.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Roll"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.Rotate","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Rotate(axis, angle)\n\nTurn the frame by angle degrees (right-handed) about its axis (:x, :y, :z or a vector in its coordinates) through its origin. This is a relative move and does not fix a degree of freedom.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Rotate"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.placement_report","page":"Frames and placement","text":"placement_report(defs, base = Dict(:tcc => CHAMBER)) -> Dict{Symbol,PlacementReport}\n\nA PlacementReport for every definition in defs, as in resolve_frames, which raises the same errors. The report gives the degrees of freedom that each placement step fixes, the redundant steps, the free degrees of freedom and the residuals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.placement_report"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.FramePointing","page":"Frames and placement","text":"FramePointing(beams, frames; by = :group, fields = (:u, :v, :defocus),\n              relative = false)\n\nMaps a parameter vector to beams whose aims are local coordinates of a Frame. Beam i aims at to_chamber(frame_i, (u, v, w)), which is R * SVector(u, v, w) + origin. frames is one Frame for every beam, a vector of frames (one per beam), or a function beam -> Frame. Beams are grouped by by (:group, :quad, :beam, or a function beam -> key), and each group gets one value per field:\n\n:u, :v, :w — the local coordinates of the aim (mm). On the foil_frame of a foil, u and v run across its face and w along its front normal.\n:defocus, :power, :spot, :dlambda — as for GroupPointing.\n\nA group shares local coordinates. Beams of one group in different frames aim at the same local point of each frame, so their chamber aims differ. With two entrance-hole frames, the bottom one the top one mirrored through the equator, a mirrored pointing is given without a mirror flag. For aim coordinates that are not parameters, the value of each beam is kept, which is its template aim in its frame. With no aim field at all, the template aims are kept exactly.\n\nrelative = true makes the aim fields shifts added to the local coordinates of each beam, so a group moves rigidly in its frame; its initial aim parameters are then zero. The other fields stay absolute.\n\napply is generic in the number type (frames are Float64, parameters may be ForwardDiff duals), so the parameterization can be used with Scenario, optimize_pointing and subset in the same way as a GroupPointing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.FramePointing"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.apply-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{FramePointing, AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Frames and placement","text":"apply(p::FramePointing, x) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nBeams for parameter vector x. Each aim is R * SVector(u, v, w) + origin in the frame of its beam. The element type is that of x, so the map is differentiable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.apply"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.bounds","page":"Frames and placement","text":"bounds(p::FramePointing, x0; u = 1.0, v = 1.0, w = 1.0, defocus = 2.0,\n       power = 0.5, spot = 0.5, dlambda = 2.0, power_min = 0.0, power_max = Inf)\n       -> (lower, upper)\n\nBox bounds around x0: ±u, ±v, ±w, ±defocus mm, relative ±power and ±spot fractions and ±dlambda Å (engineering defaults that are not facility limits), with power clipped to power_min ≤ P ≤ power_max (TW per beam). A group at zero power gets [0, power · P̄], P̄ the mean nonzero power, as for a GroupPointing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.initial_parameters-Tuple{FramePointing}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"initial_parameters(p::FramePointing) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nParameters that reproduce the template beams. They are the group means of the local aim coordinates and of the other fields. The aim fields of a relative parameterization are zero shifts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.initial_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.nparams-Tuple{FramePointing}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Number of parameters of a FramePointing (groups × fields).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.nparams"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.parameter_names-Tuple{FramePointing}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"Names \"<group>.<field>\" for each parameter of a FramePointing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.parameters-Tuple{FramePointing, Vararg{Pair}}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"parameters(p::FramePointing, values::Pair...; group = (field = value, ...), ...)\n    -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe parameter vector of p with named values, as for a GroupPointing. Fields and groups not named are set to their initial_parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.parameters"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.SweepResult","page":"Frames and placement","text":"SweepResult\n\nThe result of placement_sweep, with fields:\n\nvalues, the requested steps;\nrows, one SweepRow per completed step, in order;\ncanceled, true when a callback stopped the sweep. rows then hold the steps completed before the stop.\n\nwrite_sweep_csv writes the rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SweepResult"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.SweepRow","page":"Frames and placement","text":"SweepRow\n\nOne step of a placement_sweep:\n\nvalue — the swept quantity, which for the convenience method is the shift along the axis in mm;\nbeams — the beams of the step, after re-optimization when it is on;\nmin_clearance, worst_beam, worst_component, fails, reviews — the least worst-case clearance of the clearance check (mm), its beam and hardware part, and the numbers of :fail and :review pairs (NaN, \"\", :none, 0 when not checked);\nlos_clear, blocked_fraction, covered — the results of the line_of_sight check, which are whether it is clear, the fraction of its rays obstructed and the fraction of the object covered (missing, NaN when not checked);\nmin_captured — the least fraction of the power of a beam absorbed by the capture parts (NaN when not checked);\nobjective_before, objective_after, x — the objective at the starting parameters of the step and after re-optimization, and the optimized parameters (NaN, empty when not optimized);\nclearance, los — the full reports (nothing when not checked).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SweepRow"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.placement_sweep-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"placement_sweep(design, values; clearance_kw = nothing, los = nothing, los_kw = (;),\n                capture = nothing, capture_kw = (;), optimize = nothing,\n                callback = nothing) -> SweepResult\nplacement_sweep(target, beams, parts, values; frame = CHAMBER, axis = :z,\n                follow = false, kwargs...) -> SweepResult\n\nSteps a design through values and checks each step. design(value) returns a named tuple with the target and beams of the step. When the step is re-optimized, the tuple also holds pointing, the parameterization whose initial parameters give those beams. It may add scenario_kw, keywords of Scenario for only that step, which are applied after optimize.scenario_kw. For instance, viewfactor_kw = (; closed = cavities) balances the cavities of the step, whose elements move with its target.\n\nEach check runs when its keyword is given:\n\nclearance_kw, the keywords of clearance (targets among them): the least worst-case clearance, its beam and part, and the numbers of failing and review pairs;\nlos, a line of sight (θ, φ in degrees or a direction) for line_of_sight with keywords los_kw: whether it is clear, the fraction of its rays blocked and the coverage of the object;\ncapture, a part name or a tuple of them, such as a backlighter foil: the least fraction of the power of a beam that those parts absorb, from beam_component_power on a DepositionOperator built for the step with keywords capture_kw.\n\nWith optimize, a named tuple, the pointing is re-optimized at each step before the checks. Its fields are:\n\nobjective (required);\nscenario_kw, keywords of Scenario;\nbounds_kw, keywords of bounds, a box about the initial parameters of the step;\nwarm, which defaults to true and starts from the optimum of the previous step when it has the same number of parameters;\nany keywords of optimize_pointing.\n\nWith a FramePointing in a frame that moves with the part, the parameters keep their meaning from step to step, which is what makes the warm start useful.\n\ncallback((; done, total, row)) is called once with done = 0 and row = nothing before any work, and then after each step with the row of that step. Returning true cancels the sweep, and the result contains the completed rows with canceled = true. A true returned after the last step is ignored.\n\nMoving parts along a frame axis. The second form moves the named parts, a Symbol or a collection, of target by value · a at each step. The vector a is the unit vector of axis in frame, expressed in the chamber; axis is :x, :y, :z or a local vector. The beams selected by follow move with the parts, and their aims shift by the same vector. follow is true for all beams, false for none, beam indices, or a predicate beam -> Bool. beams may be a FramePointing instead: its template beams are swept, the frames of followed beams move with them, and it is the pointing that optimize re-optimizes. value is then the shift (mm) and every other keyword passes to the first form.\n\nEach step costs a target rebuild, the clearance check, a deposition operator for the capture check, and the optimization when it is on. For long sweeps, give these checks quick settings, such as clearance_kw = (; nangles = 8, nspot = 31, ...) and a coarse capture_kw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.placement_sweep"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/frames/#Briareus.write_sweep_csv-Tuple{AbstractString, SweepResult}","page":"Frames and placement","text":"write_sweep_csv(path, result) -> path\n\nWrites the rows of a SweepResult as CSV, one line per step. Each line contains the value, the least clearance (mm) with its beam and part, and the failing and review pairs. It also contains the line-of-sight results, which are the clear flag and the blocked and covered fractions, the least captured fraction, and the objective before and after re-optimization. A check that did not run leaves its cells empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_sweep_csv"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/laser_spot/#Laser-spot-on-a-foil","page":"Laser spot on a foil","text":"A phase plate placed before the focusing lens of a high-power laser breaks the beam into many small, mutually phased elements. Each element illuminates the whole focal region, and their sum, averaged over the speckle, is a smooth spot whose shape is set by the plate; the aberrations of the beam do not shape it. The spots of the continuous phase plates on NIF and the distributed phase plates on OMEGA are flat-topped and elliptical or round, and an elliptical super-Gaussian describes them well. A foil placed in such a beam, as in an area backlighter, absorbs the power distribution of the spot as it is projected onto the foil surface.\n\nThree properties of that distribution have exact forms: the total power, the growth of the spot as the foil moves away from best focus, and the stretching of the footprint at oblique incidence. This tutorial computes the deposition on a foil with both of Briareus's methods and compares each property with its exact value. It introduces beams, spot profiles, the deposition operator, the reference ray trace and rendered images.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Briareus), \"docs\", \"literate\", \"_plotting.jl\"))   # figure helpers\ncutaway! (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Laser spot on a foil"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/laser_spot/#The-spot-and-its-defocus","page":"Laser spot on a foil","text":"A spot with semi-axes a and b and super-Gaussian order n has the best-focus intensity\n\nI(u v) = fracPN_na bexpleft-left(fracu^2a^2 + fracv^2b^2right)^n2right\nqquad N_n = frac2piGamma(2n)n\n\nwhere u and v are transverse coordinates and P is the beam power. Its second moment along u is langle u^2rangle = a^2 m_n with m_n = Gamma(4n)2Gamma(2n).\n\nBecause every point of the lens illuminates the whole spot, the light that reaches a point a distance s from best focus arrives from a disk of focal points of radius s2F, where F is the f-number. In geometric optics the defocused profile is therefore the focal profile convolved with a uniform disk. Second moments of a convolution add, and a uniform disk of radius rho has langle u^2 rangle = rho^24, so for a round spot the mean squared radius grows as\n\nlangle r^2 rangle(s) = 2 a^2 m_n + fracs^28F^2\n\nBriareus evaluates deposition in two ways, both built on a quadrature of the f-cone: a set of directions, equally weighted over the lens, whose spread reproduces the second moment of the disk exactly. A DepositionOperator samples every lit surface at points fixed by the target and the beam directions; each point receives, from every cone direction that reaches it, the focal profile where the ray along that direction crosses the best-focus plane. This sum carries out the convolution and is differentiable in pointing, focus and power. raytrace_deposition launches rays from a grid of focal points along the same directions and follows them to the first surface.\n\nThe beam here comes from directly above, through OMEGA's SG4 phase plate, which gives a round spot with a 1/e radius of 0.36 mm and order 4.2 at f/6.7.\n\nspot = omega_spot(:SG4)\nF = 6.7\nP = 0.5                                  # TW\nm_n = Briareus._sg_moment(spot.order)\nmoment(s) = 2 * spot.a^2 * m_n + s^2 / (8F^2)\n\nThe foil is a 6 mm square in the plane z = 0, facing up, divided into 120 times 120 patches. foil builds the Component, and Target assembles components into the geometry the calculations use.\n\nW, N = 6.0, 120\ntarget = Target(foil(:foil, (0, 0, 0), (0, 0, 1); width = W, n = N, two_sided = false))\nTarget with 1 components, 14400 elements, 28800 triangles\n  foil           surface  Au         14400 elements  area   36.0000 mm²\n","title":"The spot and its defocus"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/laser_spot/#Power-and-centroid","page":"Laser spot on a foil","text":"A Beam is given by its port angles, power, spot, f-number and aim point. Here it is aimed off center, at (03 -02 0) mm.\n\nbeam = Beam(\"B\"; theta = 0, phi = 0, power = P, spot, fnumber = F, aim = (0.3, -0.2, 0.0))\nop = DepositionOperator(target, [beam])\nfast = deposit(op, [beam])\nray = raytrace_deposition(target, [beam]; nspot = 61, ncone = 61)\n\nThe operator's deposition is a vector of absorbed power per element, and its sum is the power that reached the foil. The ray trace conserves power exactly; the operator integrates the profile by quadrature.\n\ncentroid(d) = sum(d.element .* target.elem_centroid) / sum(d.element)\nfor (label, d) in ((\"operator\", fast), (\"ray trace\", ray))\n    c = centroid(d)\n    @printf(\"%-9s  power %.6f TW   centroid (%.4f, %.4f) mm\\n\", label, sum(d.element), c[1], c[2])\nend\noperator   power 0.500000 TW   centroid (0.3000, -0.2000) mm\nray trace  power 0.500000 TW   centroid (0.3002, -0.1997) mm","title":"Power and centroid"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/laser_spot/#Spot-size-through-focus","page":"Laser spot on a foil","text":"Moving the focus a distance s along the beam leaves the foil at distance s from best focus. The second moment of the deposited power is computed from the patch centroids. Assigning the power of each patch to its centroid adds the variance of a uniform distribution over the patch, Delta^212 per axis for a patch of side Delta, which is removed (Sheppard's correction).\n\nΔ = W / N\nfunction spread(d)\n    c = centroid(d)\n    return sum(d.element .* [sum(abs2, x - c) for x in target.elem_centroid]) /\n           sum(d.element) - 2Δ^2 / 12\nend\n\nshifts = range(-4.0, 4.0; length = 9)\nfast_spread, ray_spread = Float64[], Float64[]\nfor s in shifts\n    b = Beam(\"B\"; theta = 0, phi = 0, power = P, spot, fnumber = F, defocus = -s)\n    o = DepositionOperator(target, [b])\n    push!(fast_spread, spread(deposit(o, [b])))\n    push!(ray_spread, spread(raytrace_deposition(target, [b]; nspot = 61, ncone = 61)))\nend\n\nThe upper panel compares the second moments with the exact growth; the lower panel shows their relative errors, langle r^2ranglelangle r^2rangle_rm exact - 1.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 520))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], ylabel = \"⟨r²⟩ (mm²)\", title = \"Spot growth through focus\",\n          xticklabelsvisible = false)\nss = range(-4.4, 4.4; length = 200)\nlines!(ax, ss, moment.(ss), color = :black, label = \"exact: 2a²mₙ + s²/8F²\")\nscatter!(ax, shifts, fast_spread, marker = :rect, markersize = 12, label = \"deposition operator\")\nscatter!(ax, shifts, ray_spread, marker = :circle, markersize = 7, label = \"ray trace\")\naxislegend(ax, position = :ct)\nerr = Axis(fig[2, 1], xlabel = \"distance from best focus s (mm)\", ylabel = \"relative error (10⁻³)\")\nhlines!(err, [0.0], color = :black)\nscatterlines!(err, shifts, 1e3 .* (fast_spread ./ moment.(shifts) .- 1), marker = :rect,\n              markersize = 10)\nscatterlines!(err, shifts, 1e3 .* (ray_spread ./ moment.(shifts) .- 1), marker = :circle,\n              markersize = 8)\nlinkxaxes!(ax, err)\nxlims!(ax, -4.5, 4.5)\nrowsize!(fig.layout, 2, Relative(0.3))\nrowgap!(fig.layout, 8)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe largest relative errors over the scan:\n\n@printf(\"operator  %.1e\\nray trace %.1e\\n\",\n        maximum(abs.(fast_spread ./ moment.(shifts) .- 1)),\n        maximum(abs.(ray_spread ./ moment.(shifts) .- 1)))\noperator  9.7e-04\nray trace 2.7e-03\n\nBoth follow the exact growth, because the cone quadrature reproduces the second moment of the disk exactly. The residuals come from integrating a flat-topped profile over patches, from the approximation behind Sheppard's correction, and for the ray trace from sampling the focal plane on a grid.","title":"Spot size through focus"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/laser_spot/#Shape-far-from-focus","page":"Laser spot on a foil","text":"Second moments do not fix the shape. Four millimeters from focus the f-cone disk has a radius of 0.3 mm, comparable to the spot, and the convolution rounds the flat top into a lower, broader peak with soft shoulders. The exact convolution is evaluated here directly, by averaging the focal profile over 3000 points of the disk of focal points at each of 16 points in each patch near the spot.\n\nfunction exact_deposition(s; ndisk = 3000, nsub = 4)\n    golden = π * (3 - sqrt(5))\n    disk = [sqrt((i - 0.5) / ndisk) .* (cos(i * golden), sin(i * golden)) for i in 1:ndisk]\n    ρ = abs(s) / 2F\n    E = zeros(nelements(target))\n    Threads.@threads for e in 1:nelements(target)\n        c = target.elem_centroid[e]\n        hypot(c[1], c[2]) < 1.5 || continue\n        acc = 0.0\n        for i in 1:nsub, j in 1:nsub, (u, v) in disk\n            x = c[1] + Δ * ((i - 0.5) / nsub - 0.5) - ρ * u\n            y = c[2] + Δ * ((j - 0.5) / nsub - 0.5) - ρ * v\n            acc += focal_profile(spot, P, hypot(x, y) / spot.a)\n        end\n        E[e] = acc / (nsub^2 * ndisk) * target.elem_area[e]\n    end\n    return E\nend\ns_far = 4.0\nfar = Beam(\"B\"; theta = 0, phi = 0, power = P, spot, fnumber = F, defocus = -s_far)\nexact_far = exact_deposition(s_far)\n@printf(\"exact convolution: power %.6f TW\n\", sum(exact_far))\nexact convolution: power 0.500000 TW\n\nThe error of the operator, as the fraction of the beam power deposited in the wrong place, falls as cone directions are added; 13 is the default:\n\nmisplaced(E) = sum(abs.(E .- exact_far)) / P\nfor ncone in (7, 13, 16)\n    E = deposit(DepositionOperator(target, [far]; ncone), [far]).element\n    @printf(\"operator, %2d directions: misplaced %.4f, peak/exact %.3f\\n\", ncone,\n            misplaced(E), maximum(E) / maximum(exact_far))\nend\noperator,  7 directions: misplaced 0.0178, peak/exact 1.012\noperator, 13 directions: misplaced 0.0092, peak/exact 1.004\noperator, 16 directions: misplaced 0.0070, peak/exact 1.005\n\nA super-Gaussian of the same order whose axes grow to reproduce the second moments, which intensity evaluates, is the obvious smooth model of a defocused spot. It has the right size but the wrong shape:\n\nmoment_matched = [intensity(far, x) * a for (x, a) in zip(target.elem_centroid, target.elem_area)]\n@printf(\"moment-matched super-Gaussian: misplaced %.4f, peak/exact %.3f\\n\",\n        misplaced(moment_matched), maximum(moment_matched) / maximum(exact_far))\nmoment-matched super-Gaussian: misplaced 0.1596, peak/exact 0.717\n\nA cut through the spot shows the difference. Every curve gives, for each patch of the row just above y = 0, the absorbed power divided by the patch area; the best-focus profile is dashed for reference:\n\noperator_far = deposit(DepositionOperator(target, [far]), [far]).element\nfocus = Beam(\"B\"; theta = 0, phi = 0, power = P, spot, fnumber = F)\nfocus_dep = deposit(DepositionOperator(target, [focus]), [focus]).element\ncut = sort([e for e in 1:nelements(target) if abs(target.elem_centroid[e][2] - Δ / 2) < Δ / 4];\n           by = e -> target.elem_centroid[e][1])\nx_cut = [target.elem_centroid[e][1] for e in cut]\nflux_cut(E) = to_Wcm2.(E[cut] ./ target.elem_area[cut]) ./ 1e14\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"x (mm)\", ylabel = \"absorbed flux (10¹⁴ W/cm²)\",\n          title = \"Cut through the spot, 4 mm from focus\", limits = ((-1.2, 1.2), (0, 1.5)))\nlines!(ax, x_cut, flux_cut(focus_dep), color = :gray55, linestyle = :dash, label = \"best focus\")\nlines!(ax, x_cut, flux_cut(exact_far), color = :black, linewidth = 2, label = \"exact convolution\")\nscatter!(ax, x_cut, flux_cut(operator_far), color = Makie.wong_colors()[1], markersize = 8,\n         label = \"operator, 13 directions\")\nlines!(ax, x_cut, flux_cut(moment_matched), color = Makie.wong_colors()[2], linewidth = 2,\n       label = \"moment-matched\\nsuper-Gaussian\")\nLegend(fig[1, 2], ax; framevisible = false)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe moment-matched spot has a flat top more than a quarter below the exact peak, and a sixth of its power is misplaced, which is why deposition sums over cone directions. In a hohlraum the walls lie several millimeters from focus, often at grazing incidence, and a moment-matched spot on those walls deposits up to 2% more power than the beam carries.","title":"Shape far from focus"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/laser_spot/#Oblique-incidence","page":"Laser spot on a foil","text":"A beam arriving at polar angle theta onto the same horizontal foil projects its spot onto the surface. Along the plane of incidence the footprint is stretched by 1costheta, across it the footprint is unchanged, and the absorbed power per unit area is the beam intensity times costheta. The second moments of the footprint at best focus are therefore langle x^2 rangle = a^2 m_ncos^2theta and langle y^2 rangle = a^2 m_n, apart from one geometric correction: a tilted foil lies above the focal plane on one side of the aim point and below it on the other, where the spot is wider. A ray leaving the focal plane at transverse offset u with slope t in the plane of incidence meets the foil at x = u(costheta - tsintheta); averaging over the f-cone, where langle t^2 rangle = 116F^2, gives to leading order\n\nfraclangle x^2 rangle cos^2thetaa^2 m_n = 1 + frac3tan^2theta16F^2\n\nThe ratio is 1.0059 at f/6.7 and 1.0001 for a nearly collimated f/50 beam. In this check the ray trace samples the spot on a 241-point grid, a sixth of a patch apart. The rays of a nearly collimated beam fall on a regular lattice, and a lattice comparable to the patch spacing beats against the patches and biases the second moment by about 2%.\n\nθ = 50.0\nfunction footprint(d)\n    c = centroid(d)\n    w = d.element ./ sum(d.element)\n    x2 = sum(w .* [(x[1] - c[1])^2 for x in target.elem_centroid]) - Δ^2 / 12\n    y2 = sum(w .* [(x[2] - c[2])^2 for x in target.elem_centroid]) - Δ^2 / 12\n    return x2 * cosd(θ)^2 / (spot.a^2 * m_n), y2 / (spot.a^2 * m_n)\nend\nprintln(\"                     ⟨x²⟩cos²θ/a²mₙ   ⟨y²⟩/a²mₙ\")\nfor fnum in (F, 50.0)\n    b = Beam(\"B\"; theta = θ, phi = 0, power = P, spot, fnumber = fnum)\n    for (label, d) in ((\"operator\", deposit(DepositionOperator(target, [b]), [b])),\n                       (\"ray trace\", raytrace_deposition(target, [b]; nspot = 241, ncone = 61)))\n        x, y = footprint(d)\n        @printf(\"f/%-4.1f %-10s      %.4f          %.4f\\n\", fnum, label, x, y)\n    end\nend\n                     ⟨x²⟩cos²θ/a²mₙ   ⟨y²⟩/a²mₙ\nf/6.7  operator        1.0056          1.0010\nf/6.7  ray trace       1.0075          1.0018\nf/50.0 operator        0.9997          0.9991\nf/50.0 ray trace       1.0002          0.9998\n\nThe transverse moment matches the projection, and the in-plane moment follows the correction, to 0.05% for the operator and to the 0.2% of its sampling for the ray trace.\n\ntilted = Beam(\"B\"; theta = θ, phi = 0, power = P, spot, fnumber = F)\ntilted_dep = deposit(DepositionOperator(target, [tilted]), [tilted])\n\nThe peak absorbed flux is the peak beam intensity times costheta:\n\nflux_peak(d) = maximum(laser_flux(d, target))\nI0 = focal_profile(spot, P, 0.0)\n@printf(\"peak absorbed flux: normal %.4f, oblique/cos θ %.4f, exact %.4f TW/mm²\\n\",\n        flux_peak(fast), flux_peak(tilted_dep) / cosd(θ), I0)\npeak absorbed flux: normal 1.3864, oblique/cos θ 1.3864, exact 1.3865 TW/mm²","title":"Oblique incidence"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/laser_spot/#Images","page":"Laser spot on a foil","text":"render draws any per-element field as seen by an OrthoCamera. Here the camera is aimed down at the central 2.5 mm of a foil, and the field is the absorbed laser flux from laser_flux, converted to W/cm² by to_Wcm2. For the images the foil covers only that central 2.5 mm and is divided into 20 µm patches, finer than the 50 µm patches of the calculations. Each patch shows the flux it absorbs. The three images share one color scale, from zero to the peak at best focus. The beam at best focus is aimed at (03 -02) mm, the other two at the origin.\n\ncamera = OrthoCamera((0, 0, -1); up = (0, 1, 0), width = 2.5, nx = 500)\nfine = Target(foil(:foil, (0, 0, 0), (0, 0, 1); width = 2.5, n = 125, two_sided = false))\ndefocused = Beam(\"B\"; theta = 0, phi = 0, power = P, spot, fnumber = F, defocus = -4.0)\ncases = (\"best focus\" => beam, \"4 mm from focus\" => defocused, \"θ = 50°\" => tilted)\nfine_flux(b) = laser_flux(deposit(DepositionOperator(fine, [b]), [b]), fine)\nimages = [render(fine, to_Wcm2.(fine_flux(b)) ./ 1e14, camera) for (_, b) in cases]\npeak = maximum(maximum(filter(isfinite, img)) for img in images)\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 300))\nfor (i, (label, _)) in enumerate(cases)\n    panel = Axis(fig[1, i], title = label, aspect = DataAspect(), xlabel = \"x (mm)\",\n                 ylabel = i == 1 ? \"y (mm)\" : \"\", yticklabelsvisible = i == 1)\n    camera_image!(panel, images[i], camera; colormap = :inferno, colorrange = (0, peak))\nend\nColorbar(fig[1, 4], colormap = :inferno, colorrange = (0, peak),\n         label = \"absorbed flux (10¹⁴ W/cm²)\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Images"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/laser_spot/#What-this-checks","page":"Laser spot on a foil","text":"The deposition operator and the ray trace both deliver the power of the beam to the foil and center it at the aim point.\nThrough focus, the second moment of the spot follows the exact geometric-optics growth 2a^2m_n + s^28F^2 with both methods. This checks the cone quadrature.\nFour millimeters from focus, the footprint of the operator matches the exact convolution of the focal spot with the f-cone in every patch, to within about 1% of the beam power with the default 13 directions, and converges as directions are added. A moment-matched super-Gaussian of the right size has the wrong shape.\nAt oblique incidence the footprint is stretched by 1costheta along the plane of incidence, the transverse width is unchanged, and the absorbed flux is reduced by costheta. The defocus across the tilted footprint adds 3tan^2theta16F^2 to the in-plane moment, which the operator reproduces to 0.05%; this checks its incidence factor for each cone direction.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/materials/#Material-defaults","page":"Material defaults","text":"A Material contains the two surface properties the radiation model uses: the x-ray albedo (re-emitted over incident flux, a number or an albedo model) and the laser-to-x-ray conversion of the absorbed laser power (a number or a Conversion). The constructors gold, uranium, nif_gold, nif_uranium, ablator and BLACK build the package defaults.\n\nmaterial_library lists named material defaults whose every quantity has a published source or a derivation recorded in the research notes (\"Material defaults and their sources (2026-11-18)\"). Each entry, a MaterialDefault, contains the Material it builds and, per quantity, a SourcedValue: the value, how it was obtained (one of MATERIAL_BASES), its source and its uncertainty. Displaying an entry prints that record:\n\nd = material_default(:gold)\ndisplay(d)                       # albedo and conversion, each with basis, source, uncertainty\nt = hohlraum(; wall = d.material)\n\nThe entries named after a constructor build exactly the material that constructor builds, identical in every field, so a model built from the library gives the same results. The foil entries are the materials of foil_material, the default of foil and backlighter_foil: gold from published disk and sphere data, the mid-Z backlighter elements with the basis :suggested (a suggested range that no measurement backs). Package defaults with no source found, such as the capsule conversion 0.3 in ablator, are not in the library.","title":"Material defaults"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/materials/#Briareus.FOIL_ELEMENTS","page":"Material defaults","text":"FOIL_ELEMENTS\n\nThe foil elements of foil_material, as (symbol, name, Z). Gold comes first, then the backlighter elements of kshell_ce that are solid as pure elements. Ga, Br and Kr are left out, since they are a metal that melts at 30 °C, a liquid and a gas.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.FOIL_ELEMENTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/materials/#Briareus.MATERIAL_BASES","page":"Material defaults","text":"MATERIAL_BASES\n\nHow a SourcedValue was obtained:\n\n:published: a measured value, a published scaling, or the input of a published model, as published;\n:range: a round value inside a published range, which is not itself a published number;\n:calibrated: fitted in Briareus to the published data that the source names;\n:derived: arithmetic in Briareus on published inputs;\n:definition: true by construction (a perfect absorber);\n:suggested: no published measurement exists. The value is a stated point (the midpoint) of a suggested range bracketed by published values of related materials or conditions, which source names and uncertainty gives. It is not a measurement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.MATERIAL_BASES"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/materials/#Briareus.MaterialDefault","page":"Material defaults","text":"MaterialDefault\n\nA named entry of material_library. The fields are name, a one-line description, the material that it builds (a Material), the SourcedValues albedo and conversion from which the material is built, and other. other contains recorded quantities that the radiation model does not use, such as laser absorption and cold reflectance, as quantity => SourcedValue pairs. The display (display(entry)) lists every quantity with its basis, source and uncertainty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.MaterialDefault"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/materials/#Briareus.SourcedValue","page":"Material defaults","text":"SourcedValue(value, basis, source, uncertainty)\n\nOne quantity of a MaterialDefault with its sources. The fields are value (a number, an AlbedoModel or a Conversion), basis (how the value was obtained, one of MATERIAL_BASES), source (the citation, and the research-notes section that records it) and uncertainty (the stated or estimated spread, or the published values around it). source must not be empty, since a value without a source does not enter the library.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.SourcedValue"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/materials/#Briareus.foil_material","page":"Material defaults","text":"foil_material(element::Symbol = :Au) -> Material\n\nThe material of a directly irradiated foil (a backlighter) of element, one of FOIL_ELEMENTS. It is the material of the material_library entry :<name>_foil (:gold_foil, :titanium_foil, …), built without the library. The default of foil and backlighter_foil is foil_material().\n\nelement albedo conversion basis\n:Au 0.8 0.8 gold disks and spheres, static equivalent (below)\n:Ti … :Ge, :Zr … :Ag 0.55 (range 0.3–0.8) 0.35 (range 0.1–0.6) suggested range\n\nGold: the conversion is the backscatter-corrected plateau of Dewald et al. at 10¹⁵ W/cm² (PoP 15, 072706 (2008); 0.95 at 10¹⁴), where backlighter foils are driven (He-α optima 1–5 × 10¹⁵ W/cm²). Open 3ω gold disks gave 0.51–0.59 of the absorbed energy at 2 × 10¹³–4 × 10¹⁴ W/cm² (Mead et al., LA-UR-86-300 (1985)). The albedo is the wall loss of Lindl at 1 ns, 0.76–0.85 over 150–300 eV. The intensity table is kept in material_default(:gold_disk), for solves that pass the laser power.\nMid-Z: no published 3ω broadband conversion was found for flat foils of these elements. The conversion range lies below the open-disk value of gold, 0.51–0.59, and below the 0.35–0.60 total conversion of laser-heated Fe-oxide aerogels at 351 nm (Pérez et al., PoP 19, 083101 (2012); steel-lined cavities 0.60–0.75). Its floor of 0.1 lies under the 0.13–0.26 obtained from the results of Eidmann & Kishimoto at 0.53 µm (APL 49, 377 (1986)), 2% (Be) to about 50% (heaviest), interpolated linearly in Z over Z 22–47. The measured dependence oscillates with Z. The albedo range lies between the static albedos of a low-Z ablator (0.3) and gold (0.8) (Young 2020). The value is the midpoint of each range, since the x-ray emission of a foil is a wanted source in some designs and an unwanted background in others, so neither end is conservative.\n\nThe conversion here is the broadband laser-to-x-ray conversion, the radiosity source. The He-α line signal comes from kshell_ce, fitted to separate data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.foil_material"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/materials/#Briareus.material_default-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Material defaults","text":"material_default(name::Symbol) -> MaterialDefault\n\nThe entry name of material_library, whose material field is the Material. An unknown name throws an ArgumentError that lists the names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.material_default"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/materials/#Briareus.material_library-Tuple{}","page":"Material defaults","text":"material_library() -> Vector{MaterialDefault}\n\nThe named material defaults for which every quantity has a published source, a derivation recorded in the research notes (\"Material defaults and their sources (2026-11-18)\") or, flagged with the basis :suggested, a suggested range bracketed by published values. The defaults are built afresh on each call. Each entry is a MaterialDefault. material_default(name) returns one, and its material is the Material to use. The entries named after a constructor build exactly the material that the constructor builds:\n\n:gold: gold(), albedo 0.8 and conversion 0.9 (static solves), the gold wall of a published static VisRad model of a NIF hohlraum (Young et al., PoP 27, 082702 (2020));\n:uranium: uranium(), the Au-lined DU wall, conversion 0.9;\n:nif_gold, :nif_uranium: nif_gold(), nif_uranium(), the drive-history walls, with the wall loss of Lindl and the rising nif_conversion();\n:gold_foil: foil_material(), the default of foil and backlighter_foil. It is directly irradiated gold, the static equivalent of :gold_disk at backlighter intensities (albedo 0.8, conversion 0.8);\n:titanium_foil, :iron_foil, … (one per mid-Z element of FOIL_ELEMENTS): foil_material(element), with both quantities :suggested. They are suggested ranges, and no measurement backs them;\n:black: BLACK, the perfect absorber.\n\nThe others are published alternatives:\n\n:gold_swadling2025: the only published VisRad energetics calibration, albedo 0.77 and conversion 0.95 on a 5.75 mm gold hohlraum at 400 TW (Swadling et al., PoP 32, 052707 (2025));\n:gold_disk: directly irradiated gold in open geometry (a foil or disk). It uses the wall loss of Lindl and the conversion plateau of Dewald et al. (PoP 15, 072706 (2008)), 0.95 at 10¹⁴ and 0.80 at 10¹⁵ W/cm² (an intensity table), and it records the 3ω laser absorption and the 1ω cold reflectance.\n\nPackage defaults without a source are not in the library. These are the laser-to-x-ray conversion 0.3 of the capsule (ablator) and the 0/0 of Material. The albedo 0.3 of ablator has a source (the static model of Young 2020), but its conversion has none, so the whole material is left out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.material_library"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/integrating_sphere/#Integrating-sphere","page":"Integrating sphere","text":"An integrating sphere is a hollow sphere with a diffusely reflecting interior and a small port. Light admitted through the port and scattered from any point of the wall illuminates every other point of the wall equally: for two points on a sphere of radius R a distance r apart, costheta_i = costheta_j = r2R, so the view-factor kernel costheta_icostheta_jpi r^2 is the constant 14pi R^2. Ulbricht built photometers on this property in 1900, and it makes the sphere an exact test of a radiosity calculation.\n\nA hohlraum is a cavity of the same kind: its gold wall re-emits most of the x rays it absorbs, and its laser entrance holes let radiation escape. This tutorial heats a spot on the wall of a spherical cavity with a laser admitted through a port, solves for the x-ray flux, and compares it with the integrating-sphere solution. The comparison shows why Briareus integrates radiation over the true curved surface: the flat facets of the mesh misstate the exchange between nearby elements. The tutorial introduces materials, apertures, curved surfaces, view factors, the radiosity solve and the energy balance.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Briareus), \"docs\", \"literate\", \"_plotting.jl\"))   # figure helpers\ncutaway! (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Integrating sphere"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/integrating_sphere/#The-sphere-multiplier","page":"Integrating sphere","text":"Let the wall absorb laser power P and convert a fraction eta of it into x rays, and let it re-emit a fraction alpha (the albedo) of the x rays that reach it. The whole interior is seen from every point of the wall with the same kernel, so the incident flux H is the same everywhere: the total power radiated by the wall, Phi, divided by the area A of the whole sphere, port included. The wall radiates its source and re-emits what it absorbs, Phi = eta P + alpha H A_w, where A_w = (1 - f)A is the wall area and f the fraction of the sphere taken by the port. Hence\n\nH = fraceta PAfrac11 - alpha (1 - f) \n\nthe direct flux eta PA times the sphere multiplier 11 - alpha(1 - f), independent of where the laser deposits its power; H f A escapes through the port.","title":"The sphere multiplier"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/integrating_sphere/#Geometry","page":"Integrating sphere","text":"The sphere has a radius of 2 mm. cubed_sphere_mesh divides it into triangles projected from the faces of a cube, with inward normals because the interior radiates. split_mesh cuts out the patches within 30° of +z, and aperture makes them a transparent port whose incident flux is the flux that escapes. The wall is a Material with albedo 0.5 and conversion efficiency 0.7.\n\nA component can carry the exact surface its mesh approximates. With a SphereSurface, the radiation model integrates over the curved patches (true points, normals and areas); without one, it integrates over the flat triangles. Ray casting uses the triangles in both cases. Both versions are built here.\n\nR = 2.0\nα, η = 0.5, 0.7\nfunction cavity(n; curved = true)\n    sphere = cubed_sphere_mesh(R; n, outward = false)\n    port, wall = split_mesh(sphere, c -> c[3] > R * cosd(30))\n    surface = curved ? SphereSurface(R) : nothing\n    return Target(Component(:wall, wall, Material(:wall; albedo = α, conversion = η); surface),\n                  aperture(:port, port; surface))\nend\ncurved_target = cavity(16)\nflat_target = cavity(16; curved = false)\nTarget with 2 components, 3072 elements, 3072 triangles\n  wall           surface  wall        2896 elements  area   46.9870 mm²\n  port           aperture black        176 elements  area    3.1749 mm²\n","title":"Geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/integrating_sphere/#Heating-a-spot","page":"Integrating sphere","text":"A beam enters through the port from directly above and heats a spot on the lower hemisphere. The reference ray trace delivers it to the wall, apart from a few rays of the far wings that graze the rim of the port and strike the outside of the sphere.\n\nbeam = Beam(\"B\"; theta = 0, phi = 0, power = 1.0, spot = omega_spot(:SG4), fnumber = 6.7,\n            aim = (0.3, 0.0, -1.0))\n\nfunction sphere_solution(target)\n    dep = raytrace_deposition(target, [beam]; nspot = 61)\n    wall = elements(target, :wall)\n    A = sum(target.elem_area)\n    f = sum(target.elem_area[elements(target, :port)]) / A\n    H_exact = η * sum(dep.element[wall]) / A / (1 - α * (1 - f))\n    model = RadiationModel(target)\n    return (; target, dep, wall, A, f, H_exact, model, solution = solve(model, dep))\nend\ncurved = sphere_solution(curved_target)\nflat = sphere_solution(flat_target)\n@printf(\"laser power on the wall %.6f TW\\n\", sum(curved.dep.element[curved.wall]))\nlaser power on the wall 0.999825 TW","title":"Heating a spot"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/integrating_sphere/#Uniformity","page":"Integrating sphere","text":"RadiationModel computes the view factors between all elements, and solve returns the radiosity B and the incident flux H of every element. The plot shows the relative error of the incident flux on each element of the wall against its angular distance from the center of the heated spot, on a logarithmic scale that covers both calculations. Elements where the curved calculation is exact to the last bit are drawn at 10^-17:\n\nfunction angle_from_spot(s)\n    center = normalize(sum(s.dep.element .* s.target.elem_centroid))\n    return [acosd(clamp(dot(normalize(s.target.elem_centroid[e]), center), -1, 1)) for e in s.wall]\nend\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 400))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"angle from the heated spot (degrees)\",\n          ylabel = \"|H/H_exact − 1|\", title = \"Incident flux on the wall\", yscale = log10,\n          yticks = LogTicks(-17:2:-1), xticks = 0:30:180, limits = ((0, 180), (4e-18, 0.4)))\nfor (s, label, marker, markersize) in ((flat, \"flat facets\", :circle, 4),\n                                       (curved, \"curved surface\", :diamond, 5))\n    rel_error = abs.(s.solution.H[s.wall] ./ s.H_exact .- 1)\n    scatter!(ax, angle_from_spot(s), max.(rel_error, 1e-17); marker, markersize, label)\nend\nhlines!(ax, [eps()], color = :gray40, linestyle = :dash, label = \"machine ε\")\nLegend(fig[1, 2], ax; framevisible = false)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nOn the flat facets the flux is uniform far from the spot but scattered by several percent near it. Next to a flat facet, a neighboring facet is tilted by about half the angular size of an element, while on the sphere the cosines of nearby points are only of order r2R. The facets therefore misstate the exchange between nearby elements by an amount of order one, and the spot, which contains most of the source in a few elements, makes that error visible in the flux of its neighbors. Refining the facets reduces the scatter only in proportion to the element size. On the curved surface the flux is uniform within round-off, about 10⁻¹⁴:\n\nfor (label, s) in ((\"flat facets\", flat), (\"curved surface\", curved))\n    h = s.solution.H[s.wall] ./ s.H_exact\n    @printf(\"%-15s H/H_exact %.6f – %.6f\\n\", label, extrema(h)...)\nend\nflat facets     H/H_exact 0.872560 – 1.077488\ncurved surface  H/H_exact 1.000000 – 1.000000","title":"Uniformity"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/integrating_sphere/#Global-quantities","page":"Integrating sphere","text":"The mean flux, the power escaping through the port, and the energy balance from energy_balance are:\n\nfor (label, s) in ((\"flat facets\", flat), (\"curved surface\", curved))\n    Aw = s.target.elem_area[s.wall]\n    b = energy_balance(s.solution)\n    @printf(\"%-15s mean H/H_exact %.6f   escape/(H f A) %.6f   source − sinks %.1e TW\\n\", label,\n            sum(s.solution.H[s.wall] .* Aw) / sum(Aw) / s.H_exact,\n            b.escaped[:port] / (s.H_exact * s.f * s.A),\n            b.source - sum(values(b.absorbed)) - sum(values(b.escaped)) - b.lost)\nend\nflat facets     mean H/H_exact 1.000608   escape/(H f A) 1.001721   source − sinks -1.2e-16 TW\ncurved surface  mean H/H_exact 1.000000   escape/(H f A) 1.000000   source − sinks -6.7e-15 TW\n\nThe faceted cavity has the right mean to 0.1% because the misstated local exchanges nearly cancel in sums. The sphere multiplier grows rapidly as the albedo approaches one, and so does the amplification of any radiation the view factors lose. The multiplier is computed below over a range of albedos:\n\nfunction mean_ratio(s, a)\n    sol = solve(s.model, s.dep; albedo = [e in s.wall ? a : 0.0 for e in 1:nelements(s.target)])\n    Aw = s.target.elem_area[s.wall]\n    exact = η * sum(s.dep.element[s.wall]) / s.A / (1 - a * (1 - s.f))\n    return sum(sol.H[s.wall] .* Aw) / sum(Aw) / exact\nend\nalbedos = 0.0:0.05:0.95\nratios = [[mean_ratio(s, a) for a in albedos] for s in (flat, curved)]\nmultiplier(a) = 1 / (1 - a * (1 - curved.f))\n\nThe upper panel shows the computed multiplier, the mean wall flux over the direct flux eta PA, against the exact one; the lower panel shows the relative error of the mean flux, with exact results drawn at 10^-17:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 520))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], ylabel = \"⟨H⟩ / (ηP/A)\", title = \"Sphere multiplier\",\n          xticklabelsvisible = false)\nαs = range(0, 0.96; length = 200)\nlines!(ax, αs, multiplier.(αs), color = :black, label = \"exact 1/[1 − α(1 − f)]\")\nscatter!(ax, albedos, ratios[1] .* multiplier.(albedos), marker = :rect, markersize = 11,\n         label = \"flat facets\")\nscatter!(ax, albedos, ratios[2] .* multiplier.(albedos), marker = :circle, markersize = 6,\n         label = \"curved surface\")\naxislegend(ax, position = :lt)\nerr = Axis(fig[2, 1], xlabel = \"albedo α\", ylabel = \"|⟨H⟩/⟨H⟩_exact − 1|\", yscale = log10,\n           yticks = LogTicks(-16:4:-2), limits = (nothing, (4e-18, 1e-2)))\nscatterlines!(err, albedos, max.(abs.(ratios[1] .- 1), 1e-17), marker = :rect, markersize = 9)\nscatterlines!(err, albedos, max.(abs.(ratios[2] .- 1), 1e-17), marker = :circle, markersize = 7)\nhlines!(err, [eps()], color = :gray40, linestyle = :dash)\ntext!(err, 0.5, eps(); text = \"machine ε\", align = (:center, :top), fontsize = 12,\n      color = :gray30)\nlinkxaxes!(ax, err)\nrowsize!(fig.layout, 2, Relative(0.4))\nrowgap!(fig.layout, 8)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe multiplier reaches 11 - 095(1-f) approx 9 at the right of the plot, with the port taking f = 63 of the sphere. The error of the faceted cavity grows with it, tenfold over these albedos. The curved calculation follows the multiplier within round-off, and the rows of its view-factor matrix sum to one within round-off, because the kernel of the sphere is integrated exactly:\n\nfor (label, s) in ((\"flat facets\", flat), (\"curved surface\", curved))\n    @printf(\"%-15s largest |closure error| %.1e\\n\", label, maximum(abs.(closure_error(s.model.F))))\nend\nflat facets     largest |closure error| 5.0e-04\ncurved surface  largest |closure error| 7.7e-15","title":"Global quantities"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/integrating_sphere/#Images","page":"Integrating sphere","text":"Looking into the sphere from above through the port, an imager views the lower hemisphere of the wall. radiance converts radiosity to the Lambertian radiance it records, and render draws any per-element field as seen by an OrthoCamera. The port is cut from the mesh along element edges, so its rim is a staircase; the images are cropped to the largest circle inside it.\n\nThe radiance shows the heated spot, about four elements across. The radiance is computed per element, and the left image smooths the element values with a Gaussian 90 µm wide, about half an element, so that the facets of this coarse mesh do not hide the shape of the spot; the smoothing lowers the peak by 7%. The other two images show the magnitude of the error of the incident flux, per element and unsmoothed, on one logarithmic scale, as in the plot of the wall flux above. With flat facets it is 10^-3 to 10^-1 and varies from triangle to triangle; on the curved surface it is round-off, near 10^-15 everywhere.\n\ncamera = OrthoCamera((0, 0, -1); up = (0, 1, 0), width = 2.1, nx = 420)\nB = render_smoothed(curved.target, to_Wcm2.(radiance(curved.solution)) ./ 1e12, :wall, camera;\n                    σ = 0.09)\nlog_error(s) = log10.(max.(abs.(s.solution.H ./ s.H_exact .- 1), 1e-17))\nimages = [B, render(flat.target, log_error(flat), camera), render(curved.target, log_error(curved), camera)]\nrim = minimum(inscribed_radius(img, camera) for img in images)\nforeach(img -> crop_to_circle!(img, camera, rim), images)\n@printf(\"images cropped to a radius of %.2f mm; the port's radius is %.2f mm\\n\", rim, R * sind(30))\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 360))\npanels = [Axis(fig[1, j]; title, aspect = DataAspect(), limits = (-1.05, 1.05, -1.05, 1.05))\n          for (j, title) in enumerate((\"radiance B/π\", \"H error, flat facets\", \"H error, curved surface\"))]\nforeach(hidedecorations!, panels)\nforeach(hidespines!, panels)\nhm = camera_image!(panels[1], images[1], camera; colormap = :inferno,\n                   colorrange = (0, maximum(filter(isfinite, images[1]))))\nColorbar(fig[2, 1], hm; vertical = false, flipaxis = false, width = Relative(0.85),\n         label = \"B/π (10¹² W/cm²/sr)\")\nfor j in 2:3\n    camera_image!(panels[j], images[j], camera; colormap = :viridis, colorrange = (-16, -1))\nend\nColorbar(fig[2, 2:3]; colormap = :viridis, colorrange = (-16, -1), vertical = false,\n         flipaxis = false, width = Relative(0.6), ticks = -16:3:-1,\n         label = \"log₁₀ |H/H_exact − 1|\")\nfor ax in panels\n    lines!(ax, Circle(Point2f(0, 0), rim); color = :black, linewidth = 1)\nend\nrowgap!(fig.layout, 6)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Images"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/integrating_sphere/#What-this-checks","page":"Integrating sphere","text":"On the curved surface, the radiosity solve reproduces the integrating-sphere flux on every element within round-off, wherever the laser deposits its power, and the sphere multiplier over albedos from 0 to 0.95.\nEnergy is conserved within round-off: the x-ray source equals the power absorbed by the wall, escaping through the port, and lost.\nFlat facets standing in for the sphere get the mean right to 0.1% but misstate the local exchange near a concentrated source by several percent. This is why hohlraum walls, end caps, entrance holes and capsules carry their exact surfaces.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Desktop-application","page":"Desktop application","text":"The Briareus window (the BriareusGUI package and its briareus command) puts the design loop of the library on screen in the manner of a CAD tool such as VisRad: a target is built or imported, the beams are pointed, and the laser deposition, the x-ray drive and the capsule drive symmetry follow every edit. The same window optimizes the pointing, checks beam clearance from the target hardware and the NIF TaLIS target-configuration rules, solves the drive through a pulse, and reads and writes VisRad pointing, beam summary and workspace files. It serves target designers who would otherwise iterate in VisRad, and anyone who wants the results of the library without writing a script; every action in the window is a call into the same functions the scripts and tutorials use.\n\n(Image: The Briareus window shows the NIF hohlraum starter after a pointing optimization, cut away and colored by the incident x-ray flux, with the NIF quads drawn as rays colored by cone. The model tree is on the left, the drive properties are on the right, and the capsule modes and laser power accounting are in the results panel below.)\n\nThe window is laid out as a CAD tool: everything is reachable at any time, and each analysis reports when its result is out of date.","title":"Desktop application"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Install","page":"Desktop application","text":"","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#The-briareus-command","page":"Desktop application","text":"The command needs Julia 1.12.7 or later and no further Julia knowledge.\n\nInstall Julia through juliaup: on Windows winget install --id 9NJNWW8PVKMN -e (Julia from the Microsoft Store), on macOS or Linux curl -fsSL https://install.julialang.org | sh. Open a new terminal afterward.\nInstall the app and compile it (about ten minutes, once):\njulia -e \"using Pkg; Pkg.Apps.add(\\\"BriareusGUI\\\")\"\nor, in the package mode of the Julia prompt (]), pkg> app add BriareusGUI.\nPut Julia's app folder on the PATH (the install prints a warning when it is missing). Windows, in PowerShell:\n$p = [Environment]::GetEnvironmentVariable(\"Path\", \"User\")\n[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable(\"Path\", \"$p;$HOME\\.julia\\bin\", \"User\")\nmacOS (zsh): echo 'export PATH=\"$HOME/.julia/bin:$PATH\"' >> ~/.zprofile; Linux (bash): the same line appended to ~/.bashrc. Open a new terminal afterward.\nAdd Briareus to the desktop and the applications menu: briareus --shortcut. On Windows this makes Start-menu and desktop shortcuts, on macOS ~/Applications/Briareus.app (listed by Launchpad and Spotlight), on Linux a menu entry.\n\nThe shortcut, or briareus in a terminal, then opens the window on a fresh design, and briareus design.briar opens a saved session. The window takes about 20 s to open (the first start after an update compiles for a few minutes). A shortcut shows a splash within about a second meanwhile; briareus in a terminal shows nothing until the window, since the code of the command runs only once BriareusGUI and its graphics libraries have loaded. The command runs Julia on all threads (--threads=auto) and returns when the window closes. A shortcut has no terminal: its output goes to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Briareus\\briareus.log (on macOS, ~/Library/Logs/Briareus/), and a failure to start shows in the splash with the location of the log.\n\npkg> app update BriareusGUI updates the app, and pkg> app rm BriareusGUI removes it (the shortcuts are deleted by hand). A shortcut runs a script that names the Qt libraries of the installed app for its splash, so briareus --shortcut is run again after an update.","title":"The briareus command"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#From-Julia","page":"Desktop application","text":"The window opens from any Julia session started with several threads (julia -t auto), so that background jobs leave it responsive:\n\npkg> add BriareusGUI\n\njulia> using BriareusGUI\n\njulia> sess = briareus()                   # or briareus(resolution = :fine)\n\nbriareus blocks while the window is open (Qt runs its event loop on the calling thread) and returns the session when it closes. The window needs a graphical session, and it cannot share a process with a GLMakie window or an offscreen GLMakie render.","title":"From Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#From-a-development-checkout","page":"Desktop application","text":"BriareusGUI lives in the BriareusGUI/ directory of the Briareus repository; its environment takes Briareus from the checkout once that is developed into it:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/stillyslalom/Briareus.jl\njulia --project=Briareus.jl/BriareusGUI -e \"using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\\\"Briareus.jl\\\")\"\ngit -C Briareus.jl checkout BriareusGUI/Project.toml\njulia --project=Briareus.jl/BriareusGUI -t auto -e \"using BriareusGUI; briareus()\"\n\nPkg.develop also writes a [sources] entry into BriareusGUI/Project.toml, which the git checkout discards (the developed copy stays in the manifest). The package has no [sources] entry: an app installed from the registry would keep the path .. of the entry, which exists in the installed tree but has no package in it, and the install would fail. The briareus command installs from the checkout with julia -e \"using Pkg; Pkg.Apps.develop(path=\\\"Briareus.jl/BriareusGUI\\\")\".","title":"From a development checkout"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#A-first-session","page":"Desktop application","text":"The steps below design the drive of a NIF hohlraum from the Hohlraum with capsule starter. Each step names the menu entries, and the panels are described in The window.\n\nOpen. briareus opens on the Hohlraum with capsule starter, a hohlraum part and a capsule sphere, with the 48 NIF quads at illustrative example cone aims (nif_quads). The model tree lists the hohlraum part with its pieces under it (wall, caps and entrance holes), the capsule, the beams by cone and quad, and the analyses.\nThe target. Selecting the hohlraum part in the tree shows its dimensions and wall material in Properties, and Target shows the mesh. Target ▸ Mesh sets the element sizes. Its presets come from a work-precision study on the NIF hohlraum (examples/mesh_convergence.jl) and give the capsule P2/P0 and P4/P0 to about 0.5 (coarse), 0.1 (standard) and 0.02 (fine, converged) percentage points.\nThe beams. Selecting a cone (a group) in the tree shows its aim, defocus and power, which apply to all its quads. A quad or a beam is edited the same way, and the Beam table tab lists every one of them. View ▸ Beams ▸ Volumes draws the 5% contour of each beam along its f-cone, cut where it meets the target, so a beam clipping an entrance-hole lip or the capsule shows at a glance.\nCompute the drive. Compute ▸ Drive (Ctrl + R), or coloring the target by a drive field (View ▸ Color target by ▸ Incident x-ray flux), computes the view factors on a worker thread in seconds on the standard mesh. From then on a beam edit re-evaluates the drive at once, because the view factors depend only on the target and the beam ports.\nRead P2/P0. The Drive tab lists the capsule mode amplitudes ε₁…ε₄, the Legendre ratios P_lP_0, and the laser power accounting as report prints it. The accounting gives the power absorbed in the wall, on the outer faces of the caps and on the capsule, the parts below 0.1% of the delivered power on one line, and the light that misses the target. For a foil the tab lists the spot statistics instead. Capsule θ–φ map in the drive properties (or View ▸ Capsule θ–φ map) puts the capsule flux map in place of the 3D view. On the starter at equal power per quad, an inner-cone fraction of 1/3, P2/P0 starts near −15%. A static solve without cross-beam energy transfer or the hohlraum dynamics is not balanced at a fielded cone fraction: the static P2/P0 of the N221204 benchmark at 5.5 ns is −26%, and the shot is balanced by wavelength-detuned transfer, wall motion, the gold bubble and closing entrance holes.\nOptimize. Compute ▸ Optimization selects the optimization task, which has three parts:\nthe free parameters, which are the aim, defocus and power per cone, quad or beam within ± bounds;\nthe objective, which is axisymmetric P_l or all-m 3D modes (optionally with the drive level held), the RMS nonuniformity of a flat observed face (face_flux_objective) for a halfraum package or an opacity sample, or the nonuniformity of a foil spot; the face objective is relative to its start, so that a weight of 1 balances it against the penalties on any face;\nthe keep-outs.\nOptimize starts optimize_pointing in the background with progress and Cancel. The tab then compares the modes, the clipped power and the capsule clearance before and after, and lists every parameter against its bounds, with a mark on each parameter at a bound. Apply result writes the optimized pointing to the beam table, and the model tree marks the result out of date once the beam table changes.\n(Image: The Optimization tab after a run on the four cone aims, with z within ±2 mm and r within ±1 mm and the capsule and entrance-hole keep-outs on. The tab shows the modes before and after and the parameters against their bounds, and the 3D view is replaced by the capsule θ–φ flux map of the applied result.)\nCheck clearance. Compute ▸ Clearance computes the worst-case beam-to-hardware clearance over pointing, positioning and focus errors. Compute ▸ TaLIS checks the NIF target-configuration rules, on NIF beams only. Both run in the background and color the beams by their worst status, and a result is marked out of date in the tree when the beams or the target change afterward.\n(Image: The TaLIS rules checked on every NIF quad of the starter hohlraum, with the rule-tolerance note, the per-rule summary and every check with its margin; the beams are colored by their worst status. On the optimized pointing the eight 23.5° quads fail 3ω incidence on the wall. Their axes meet the wall at 69.9°, and the steepest ray of the f/7.9 cone of a quad is 3.6° steeper; VisRad's report measures incidence on that ray. The 48 reviews are 3ω clearance at the entrance-hole edges.)\nSave. File ▸ Save session (Ctrl + S) writes the whole design, from the target to the settings of the analyses and the view, to a .briar file. The file is a zip archive of the design as TOML and a copy of every CAD file the design reads (STL parts, VisRad workspaces), so it opens on another computer. File ▸ Save session without CAD files writes only the TOML (.briar.toml, with CAD files by path), for CAD that is not to travel with the design. File ▸ Open session (Ctrl + O), or briareus design.briar, reads either back, and results are computed again on load. The design reads the CAD copies saved with it. A source file that changed on disk since the save is offered in its place, and the properties of a part can relink its file or read it from the source again.\nSessions saved before frames (version 2 and older) are converted on load. A part placed in the old foil frame, with z along the back normal of the foil, is re-expressed in the frame of the backlighter foil at the same place. Beams that followed a component aim are given aims in the frame of that component, with their saved aims unchanged. In sessions before version 5 the part with the foil role was named backlighter; it loads as the active foil under the same name.\nA session also contains the definition of each diagnostic it records (see Diagnostics below), CSV files included, and its histories use those definitions wherever the session is opened. A saved definition that differs from the definition of the same id in the diagnostic directories is marked * on its chip. The tooltip of the chip names the differing keys, and its menu switches to the definition in the directories. After a save, the status line names the definitions from outside the built-ins and those that are not public, which are then stored in the file.\nEdit ▸ Undo (Ctrl + Z) and Redo (Ctrl + Y) step through every edit of what a session saves, in one history. The history covers design edits, whether typed, chosen from a menu, read from a file or applied from an optimization, as well as loaded pulses and changes of the view: display modes, the cutaway, the beam drawing, hidden beams and frame axes. An undo therefore takes back the last thing done, and the view state of a part follows the undo of its rename. The camera, the selection and the diagnostics added for a drive history are not steps, and an undo of a view change leaves the target as it is.\nA session also saves the window as it was left, so it reopens the same way. The saved state includes the camera pose and projection, whether the drive shows the 3D view, the map or the x-ray image (with the image camera), and the chart of the Time tab. A session from before version 13 opens on the default camera, the 3D view and the Pulses chart.\n\nTwo further steps use the view factors of the drive without computing them again: an x-ray image of the target (Compute ▸ X-ray image along the view) and the drive through a pulse (the Time tab).\n\n(Image: An x-ray image of the hohlraum seen through its top entrance hole, 20° off the axis, beside the 3D view with the beams drawn as volumes.)\n\n(Image: The Time tab after a drive history on the N221204 pulse shape. The chart plots the capsule radiation temperature, a synthetic Dante 1, and a₂/a₀ and a₄/a₀ against time, and the 3D view is colored by the history step at peak temperature.)\n\nA backlighter design starts from File ▸ New target ▸ Backlighter foil, which loads the OMEGA backlighter beams. The Backlighter foil starter is a foil part with the foil role, 6 mm from TCC and facing it, and an absorbing sphere at TCC that stands in for the main target. The drive then reports the mean and peak intensity of the foil spot, its nonuniformity and the fraction of the power inside the spot, and the optimization offers the foil-uniformity objective.\n\n(Image: A backlighter foil 6 mm from the target chamber center with the OMEGA backlighter beams, colored by the absorbed laser intensity. The 3D view faces the front of the foil, the foil face is shown beside it, and the spot statistics are in the Drive tab.)","title":"A first session"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#The-window","page":"Desktop application","text":"","title":"The window"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Model-tree","page":"Desktop application","text":"The left panel lists the parts of the target with a swatch of their color in the 3D view, so the tree is the color key. A hohlraum part lists its pieces under it (wall, caps, entrance holes and diagnostic windows), followed by the capsule and other parts. The tree also lists the beams by group, quad and beam, and the analyses (drive, optimization, clearance and TaLIS) with their state. Selecting a node selects it in the 3D view and shows it in Properties. Ctrl + click adds components or beams to the selection and Shift + click adds a range, which Properties then edits together and the 3D view highlights together. Esc clears the selection, and a double click fits the view to it. A right click on a row opens its commands, only those that apply to it. Remove takes out every selected part, assembly, frame and window in one edit (Controllers.remove_nodes!). An assembly goes with its contents. What was placed in a removed frame, such as a capsule in the frame of its hohlraum, stays where it is and is placed in the nearest frame above that remains, and beams aimed in a removed frame keep their aim points. Removing every part leaves an empty target.\n\nTaking part in the calculations and being drawn are separate settings. Each beam row shows VisRad's 2 × 3 matrix of dots. The top row shows whether the 1ω (red), 2ω (green) and 3ω (blue) light of the beams takes part in the calculations, and the bottom row whether it is drawn. A filled dot is on, a ring is off, and a pale dot is on for some of the beams. A click switches the dot for the beams of the row, or for the whole selection when the row is in it, and the beam properties show the same matrix as check boxes. 3ω participation switches a beam on or off, and only beams that are on enter the drive, the clearance check and the optimization. A beam can be hidden to show the surface it lights, or its unconverted light can be drawn while the beam stays out of the way, without changing any result. The unconverted 1ω and 2ω light is drawn for NIF quads and beams as unconverted_deposition deposits it. For each beamlet the drawing shows the nif_unconverted footprint square at the 3ω best-focus plane, displaced toward the position of the beamlet in its quad, and the square beam converging to its focus. Outlines and center lines are drawn as rays and the converging beam as a volume. The participation dots of a color switch once the drive has a power ratio for that color (below).\n\n(Image: The beam matrix in the model tree. Each beam row shows 2 × 3 dots for 1ω, 2ω and 3ω, with the top row for participation in the calculations and the bottom row for drawing, and the properties of the inner quad Q15T show the same matrix as check boxes. With illustrative 1ω and 2ω power ratios of 5% each, the unconverted light of Q15T is drawn, with the outlines and center lines of each color as rays, while only its 3ω light takes part in the calculations. The rows of its group and of all the beams show the 1ω and 2ω drawing dots pale, on for one of their beams.)\n\nEach component has a box in its color that shows or hides it. The fill of the box shows the display mode: solid for opaque, pale for semi-transparent, an outline for wireframe, and empty when hidden. The context menu and Properties ▸ Display set the mode to opaque, semi-transparent, wireframe or invisible. A click selects through semi-transparent and wireframe components to an opaque one behind them. Include in calculations is a separate setting. A component left out is drawn as a gray ghost and removed from the target, which becomes an open enclosure, and beams left out are switched off.","title":"Model tree"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Properties","page":"Desktop application","text":"The right panel edits the selection. A value the geometry or a check rejects is refused with the reason in the status line. The pages contain controls and short status lines, and tooltips are one-line hints. An ⓘ beside a page title, a section heading or a control shows such a hint on hover and, when clicked, opens its explanation in the Help window (Help ▸ Help, F1). The Help window is separate, resizes and stays open beside the design, and contains the explanations by page, a search field, and the mouse and key bindings. Help ▸ Documentation opens this page.\n\n(Image: The Help window at the page of the placement sweep, with the explanations behind the ⓘ marks listed by page and a link to this documentation.)\n\nTarget: the mesh, set by a preset (above) or by element sizes in mm for the walls and parts and for the capsule. A target is made of parts. File ▸ New target starts from a ready-made target, listed under the facility it is a mock-up for, and loads the beams of that facility; imported beams are kept. The NIF starters are Hohlraum with capsule, Frustraum with capsule (a hohlraum part with a frustraum profile), Halfraum pair with shock tube and Opacity halfraum. In the halfraum pair, two halfraums face each other across a cylinder package whose end faces close them, and the top face is observed; the opacity halfraum has an observed sample foil in its open end. The OMEGA starter is Backlighter foil, and Empty has no parts at all. The dimensions are illustrative mock-ups, and a session saved with the earlier hohlraum or foil template loads as these parts. The Insert menu (and the target properties) adds spheres, disks, washers, rectangles, cylinders, cones, bare hohlraum parts and STL meshes, placed at TCC. It also adds diagnostic windows in the wall of the selected hohlraum part, assemblies of the selected parts and frames. (insert_starter! still adds the parts of a starter to a design from a script.)\nHohlraum parts: a cylindrical hohlraum (hohlraum) along the z axis of the part, in pieces named after the part (hohlraum_wall, hohlraum_cap_top, …). The properties are its length, radius, entrance-hole radius (the bottom hole radius may differ), wall albedo and conversion, the closing entrance holes and its windows. Each end is an entrance hole, closed by its cap or open. An open end has neither cap nor hole, as in a halfraum, and is closed or not by whatever is placed there. The cavity of each hohlraum part consists of the part pieces and the elements of other parts inside it that face into it, such as a capsule or a package face closing an open end. Its view factors are balanced separately when under 1% of its rays leave it, and elements in no cavity keep their raw view factors. The wall and caps have the Wall role, which gives the wall temperature of the drive history. The clearance and TaLIS checks treat the caps, which form the edges of the entrance holes, as hardware. The row of a piece in the model tree (its properties, or the right-click menu) gives the piece another role. A cap can be Observed, which gives it a face map and flux statistics and takes it out of the walls of its cavity; a wall section or a cap can be None, and a closed window Wall. The entrance holes, their lips and open windows are apertures and take no role. Each end has a frame (hohlraum.leh_top, hohlraum.leh_bottom) with its origin at the center of the end and z pointing out of the hohlraum, so one placement or aim mirrors between the two. Each window has a frame at its center on the wall (hohlraum.window_1). The Profile of the part is a cylinder, a frustraum or sections. A frustraum is two cones meeting at the waist, set by the waist radius and the radius of each end (frustraum_profile). Sections are a table of breaks, each a height and a wall radius, with the end breaks at ±length/2 (HohlraumProfile). The wall is then one piece per section (hohlraum_wall_1, …), each with the Wall role, and windows go in cylindrical sections only.\nWindows (WallWindow) in the wall of a hohlraum part (Insert ▸ Diagnostic window, or its properties ▸ Add window): azimuth, height, round (the default, with the exact disk area) or rectangular shape, and open or closed with their own albedo and conversion efficiency. Overlapping windows, or a window past the end of the wall, are refused.\nParts: spheres, disks, washers, rectangles, cylinders and cones placed anywhere, with their own albedo, conversion, sidedness, aperture flag and element size, for hardware such as shields, stalks, detector apertures and second foils. A part is placed in a named frame (Frame, resolved with resolve_frames). The frame is the chamber frame, the frame of another part, or the end or window frame of a hohlraum part. The frame of a part has its origin at the part center and z along its axis; on a foil, z is the front normal. A part moves and turns with its frame when the part that defines the frame changes. Its position is given in Cartesian, cylindrical or spherical coordinates of that frame (coords_of), and its orientation by the axis polar and azimuth angles and a rotation, in VisRad's order (euler_rotation). Changing the frame leaves the part where it is.\nThe Move along row steps the part by ± a distance along the x, y or z axis of any frame, one edit per click; z of the part frame is the axis of the part. Parts placed in its frame and beams aimed in that frame move with it. Sweep along this axis… sets up a placement sweep of the part along that axis (below).\nPlacement steps then place the part relative to other frames, in order (PlacementStep). The steps are coincidence with a point, lying on or concentric with an axis, a distance from a point, facing a point, being parallel to an axis, a roll from a direction, and relative offsets and rotations. The heading of each step names what it fixes. A step that undoes an earlier one is refused, with both named (placement_report), and the degrees of freedom that no step fixes take the values of the placement fields. A new step starts with the part where it is, at the current point, distance and roll. The part follows the frames its steps refer to, as it follows the frame it is placed in.\nA mesh part reads an STL file (Insert ▸ Mesh from STL file), scaled to mm, with its windings repaired (orient_mesh) and its surface remeshed into elements of the part element size (remesh_mesh). A multi-body ASCII STL of named solids gives one part per solid. A VisRad object part is a component of an imported VisRad workspace; a VisRad rectangle, sphere or cylindrical hohlraum retains the VisRad element grid unless the part is given an element size.\nThe Role of a part marks it for the analyses, and the part name is unchanged. The roles are:\nObserved, the surface whose x-ray drive is analyzed, one at a time. A sphere gives the capsule modes, θ–φ map and keep-out that the drive and optimization take. A flat face, such as a rectangle or disk for a package face or an opacity sample, gets a map of its incident flux over the face, its mean, range and RMS nonuniformity, and no capsule modes. A CAD capsule is replaced in the calculations by a sphere fitted to it (fit_sphere), so a fill tube does not count.\nBacklighter foil, a flat part or a mesh.\nWall, a re-emitting cavity surface. It enters the wall temperature of the drive history, and the clearance and TaLIS checks treat it as a surface the beams land on, as for the wall of a hohlraum part.\nThin film, which is left out of the drive cavity (below).\nAny number of parts can be foils or walls. The active foil is chosen with Drive ▸ Active foil, or with the check in the part properties shown when there are two or more foils. Its spot statistics, map and objective are used by the drive and the optimization, and its line of sight and captured power are checked by the sweep. The clearance and TaLIS checks treat every foil as a surface the beams land on. The model tree marks the foils, and the active one among several.\nFrames: the Frames folder of the model tree contains user frames, named frames without geometry that are placed like parts by origin, axis angles, rotation and placement steps, in any frame. Insert ▸ Frame or Add frame in the folder places one in the selected frame. Parts are placed in a user frame, beams aim in it and a sweep steps it, as for the frame of a part. When a frame is removed, what was placed in it stays where it is. A port frame, set on the page of the folder for a port of the beam table or a NIF quad at the angles of port_angles(:nif, quad), has its origin at TCC and z toward the port (port_frame, VisRad's diagnostic-port axis); only its roll is edited. Tools ▸ Coordinate transform gives the coordinates of a point or direction in any frame from those in another (transform_point), Cartesian, cylindrical or spherical, and Create frame makes a user frame there. The 3D view draws the axes of the selected frame (x red, y green, z blue, with its name) and those of the frames switched on in their page or menu.\nBeams (a group, quad, beam, or any Ctrl-selected set): the aim, defocus, power and on/off state, and where the axis lands. The aim is given relative to the ports (z, r and a tangential offset), or as a common aim point (x, y, z) in the Aim frame of the beams, as VisRad points a cluster at a reference point. The aim frame is the chamber, a user frame, a part, or the end (its entrance hole and cap) or a window of a hohlraum part, listed after their part. The beams keep their coordinates in that frame when it moves or turns, so an aim 0.3 mm off the center of a foil stays 0.3 mm off it, and when the part is renamed or given a role. Aim at the frame's center points them at the center of the frame, which for a part is its centroid. The OMEGA backlighter beams aim in the frame of the active foil. Look along beam views the target from the port of the beam.\nBeams belong to groups, as VisRad's clusters do: the cone by default, or any named set (\"backlighter\") made with Move to group. A group is edited, switched and optimized together, and its members keep their cone for the laser–plasma models. A NIF quad held as one beam splits into its four beams, each with a separate port and a quarter of the power, so that the laser deposition is computed per beam. It merges back from the right-click menu of the row or the Beams menu, for one quad, the quads of a group or every quad at once. The quad stays the unit of pointing and power either way. Its row edits its beams together, and the radial and tangential aim offsets are measured in the port azimuth of the quad (GroupPointing's unit = :quad). The beams of a quad with the same values therefore share one aim point, the point that the quad held as one beam aims at, to under a micron; the mean of the four ports of a quad is the quad port to 0.004°. Measured in the azimuth of each beam, the aims of a 23.5° quad would land 0.2 mm apart per mm of aim r. The row of a cone or a quad, the 192-beam preset and the optimizer per cone or per quad keep one aim point per quad, and the beams of a quad are pointed apart only from their individual rows. A click on one of the beams of a quad in the 3D view selects the quad while its row is closed in the tree. The power of rows of whole quads is given per quad (Controllers.power_unit), so a split leaves the numbers in the Beam table unchanged.\nFacility (Beams ▸ Facility, or the Beams properties): NIF, OMEGA, OMEGA EP or Other. The facility sets the beams, the presets offered, the instruments the Diagnostics tab lists and the checks; the TaLIS rules are NIF's. The first preset of a facility replaces the preset beams of another. OMEGA EP and Other have no built-in set, so the preset beams of another facility are taken out and a beam table is imported; imported beams are kept. The NIF presets are quads or beams at illustrative example cone aims; in the holes of a frustraum, the aims are derived from them to keep the spot of each cone at the same fraction of the wall height where the hole allows. The OMEGA presets are the hohlraum and backlighter sets.\nAnalyses: the settings and Run of the drive, the optimization, the clearance check, the placement sweep and the TaLIS rules.","title":"Properties"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#3D-view","page":"Desktop application","text":"The center canvas navigates as a CAD tool:\n\nthe wheel zooms toward the point under the pointer;\na left or middle drag orbits about the selection or the point under the pointer;\na right drag, Shift + drag or Ctrl + middle drag pans;\na click selects a component or beam, and a double click fits it;\nf fits everything, and the arrow keys orbit by 15°.\n\nThe near and far planes follow the eye, so nothing is clipped however close the view. The View menu contains the standard views, a perspective or orthographic projection, turntable (z up) or free rotation, the cutaway, Look from port (the port of a beam or any θ, φ) and Save view as image. Switching the turntable back on levels a view that a free rotation rolled. Help ▸ Mouse and keys lists the bindings.\n\nA placement triad is drawn on the selected part, piece or assembly, in the axes of the frame it is placed in (Properties ▸ Placed in). A drag on an arrow moves the part along that axis in whole multiples of the Move along step. A drag on a square, one between each pair of arrows, moves it in that plane, each component in those steps, as one move. A drag on a ring turns it about that axis, through the origin of the part, in 15° steps, and Ctrl held at the press snaps to a tenth of either. The handle under the pointer is drawn brighter and thicker. While dragging, the view previews the moved parts and the beams aimed in their frames. The release makes one edit (nudge_part! or turn_part!, the edits of the Move along buttons), undone as one, so the placement stays parametric and the beams aimed in the moved frames follow. A press on a handle released without moving is a click, and the triad is hidden when nothing, several things or a beam is selected.\n\nThe cutaway is a pie slice or a section plane through the chosen components. The context menu of the Cutaway button offers presets, the components cut, and Cut settings…. A slice has a center azimuth and a width about the x, y or z axis, optionally bounded along it. A section plane removes the side its normal points to. Its normal is a preset (x, y, z, toward the view), typed port angles θ and φ, or a typed vector; the port angles follow the Briareus convention, with θ from +z and φ from +x toward +y. The plane passes through a typed point (TCC by default), moved along the normal by an offset slider, and Flip selects the other side of the same plane.\n\nA plane can also come from the geometry:\n\nThrough the selected component puts the point at its centroid and, for a flat component such as a window, foil or disk, takes its mean normal.\nFacing it turns the normal toward a direction, a beam port picked from the table or typed θ, φ.\nContaining the line of sight lays the plane along the line from TCC or the centroid of the selected component toward that direction, turned about the line by a rotation slider. At 0° the plane is vertical, and at 90° it contains the horizontal perpendicular to the line. This is the section through a diagnostic window along its view, and the cut halves the window along the line of sight of the diagnostic.\n\nThe plane, its point and offset, and the line and rotation are saved with the session. Cut components are clipped exactly at the cut, without whole elements removed: each triangle is split by the planes that bound the removed region, the pieces outside it are kept and colored as their element, and the edges of the cut are outlined. A slice narrower than 180° removes the intersection of two half-spaces, so what remains is a union that one set of GPU clip planes cannot express; splitting the triangles also keeps picking and the outline exact.\n\n(Image: Display modes and a bounded sector cut on the starter hohlraum, colored by the incident x-ray flux. The top cap is drawn as a wireframe (its Display setting in Properties) and the bottom cap semi-transparent, and a 120° slice of the wall toward the view is removed between z = −3 mm and z = 3 mm, clipped exactly at the cut.)\n\n(Image: A round diagnostic window 2 mm across in the wall at φ = 90°, z = 0, cut by the plane that contains its line of sight from the centroid of the window along θ = 90°, φ = 90°. At a rotation of 0° this is the vertical plane with normal −x, so the cut halves the window along the line. The drive, computed again with the window, puts 4.47 TW (1.17%) of the 384 TW delivered on it.)\n\n(Image: The Cut settings popup for that plane, with the normal θ = 90°, φ = 180° (the vector −1, 0, 0) through the centroid of the window at (0, 2.83, 0) mm, the offset slider, and the direction θ = 90°, φ = 90° whose line from the selected component the plane contains.)\n\nBeams are drawn as rays or as volumes. A ray runs along each axis from the lens side to the surface it reaches, with the aim point marked. Beams are colored by group or by their worst clearance or TaLIS status, in the colors of the tree. The target is colored by component or by the absorbed laser intensity, the x-ray radiosity or the incident x-ray flux (W/cm²); a drive coloring computes the drive when it is missing. Two further views share the canvas. One is the capsule θ–φ flux map, or the foil face for a foil. The other is an x-ray image (Compute ▸ X-ray image along the view, xray_image!), the radiance of the surfaces seen along the direction of the 3D view, framed as the view frames them and seen through transparent apertures, so a hohlraum is imaged inside through its entrance holes. The image follows the drive as beams are edited. View ▸ Map or image beside the 3D view shows either view in a second canvas next to the target.","title":"3D view"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Results","page":"Desktop application","text":"The bottom panel has the following tabs.\n\nBeam table: every cone, quad or beam with its pointing, defocus, power, on/off state and the frame its aims are held in, edited in place. A click on a column heading sorts the rows; a second click sorts them in descending order, and a third restores the order of the table. The sorting is a display order only, and the order of the beams in the design and the session is unchanged.\nDrive: the view factors are computed once per target and beam ports, on a worker, and beam edits then re-evaluate the drive at once. The tab shows the capsule mode amplitudes and P_lP_0, the foil spot statistics and the laser power accounting. A VisRad benchmark kit for the same case is written from a script with write_visrad_kit.\nThe Drive properties take the unconverted 1ω and 2ω power as fractions of the 3ω power of each beam. An empty field is off, and there is no default, because the NIF User Guide does not give a fraction. The beams whose 1ω or 2ω dot is on in the calculations row then add the absorbed power of unconverted_deposition to the 3ω deposition wherever that deposition enters, which covers the absorbed-laser coloring, the power accounting, the back-side losses and the x-ray source. The unconverted totals are listed separately. The absorption and x-ray conversion of the unconverted light are scaled by the wavelength factors absorption and conversion of nif_unconverted, relative to 3ω (see UnconvertedColor). Its x-ray source is the conversion efficiency of the material times the conversion of the color. The drive history and the retune take the unconverted light of the same beams (drive_history's unconverted, a fraction per beam where the light of only some beams takes part), and the pointing optimization uses only the 3ω light. - Time: the drive through a pulse. Pulses ▾ sets the pulses the beams fire. They come from a pulse table (read_pulse_table) of power against time per cone group, quad or beam, in which disabled beams stay off, or from a published shape (Controllers.PULSE_SHAPES). The published shapes are the delivered total power of N221204 (n221204_pulse) and of N191118 and N210601 (zylstra2022_fig2), with each beam reaching its set power at the peak. A table column for a cone or a quad is the power per quad, shared by the beams of that quad in the table, so the pulses fire the same total whether quads are split or not. The box beside Pulses ▾ switches the columns to the power per beam (Controllers.PULSE_UNITS; with_pulses itself takes a table per beam), and the retune reads its reference table and writes its result in the same unit. A session saves the pulses, including the table data, so it opens without the CSV file.\nRun history solves the drive at each time of the pulses on the view factors of the drive (drive_history), and every 0.5 ns over the prepulse window (below). The x-rays of the prepulse thus heat the wall and capsule, and the wall carries that heat into the main pulse. A time between two times that fire, where nothing fires, is solved as a zero-power step, in which the wall re-emits what it stored. The diagnostics added in the Diagnostics tab (below) are recorded with the history.\nFive charts are drawn in the colors of the tree:\nPulses, the power of each group and the total, in TW;\nDrive history, the Tᵣ of the capsule and of each recorded diagnostic (dante) in eV, with a₂/a₀ and a₄/a₀ in % on the right axis; for a flat observed face it plots its Tᵣ from the area-mean flux, and its RMS nonuniformity and peak and lowest flux relative to the mean on the right axis;\nEnergy, the laser energy delivered by each time and where it went, in kJ (energy_budget);\nIrradiance and Retune (both below).\nOn the Irradiance chart, Run checks computes the irradiance histories of the unconverted 1ω and 2ω light on the target of the drive (unconverted_history). The histories use the ratios set in the Drive properties, on the beams whose light takes part, plus the prepulse. The chart plots the peak on each part against the plasma threshold on a log axis, with the 3ω peak on the active foil. Above the chart, one line per check reports pass or fail. Specular (specular_check) passes when every surface part stays below the threshold, and otherwise reports the first crossing (threshold_crossing). On a target with a foil, Pre-plasma (preplasma_check) compares the onset of the unconverted light on the foil with the arrival of the 3ω light at the foil, from irradiance_history.\nThe Drive properties set the prepulse and the threshold. The prepulse is a 1ω and a 2ω power per beam, flat over a window from -5 to 0 ns by default (drive_history's prepulse, also taken by the drive history and the retune). The threshold is 10⁸ W/cm², the NIF User Guide's guideline for the onset of plasma on a metal, used as an engineering default. The row under the chart sets what the 3D view shows. Beam powers shows the drive at the powers set on the beams, and Drive history shows a step of the history, chosen with the slider or a click or drag on the chart, on one color scale for the whole history; scrubbing computes nothing. A beam, pulse or target edit marks the history out of date. File ▸ Export results writes a current history with the drive (Controllers.export_history): the drive, the energy and each recorded diagnostic against time, the fields per element at every step, and the images from the imagers. - Diagnostics: the synthetic diagnostics a drive history records, by category (x-ray power, x-ray imagers and radiography). Add ▾ lists the instruments of the category at the facility of the session. They come from the diagnostic directories (Diagnostic definitions), which are the built-ins followed by the directories set in Diagnostics ▸ Diagnostic directories… and kept for later sessions, and from the definitions saved with the session. An instrument is listed once, whatever the number of its definitions; the definitions share an instrument key, as the Dante 1 channel sets of several years and shots do. Each instrument is recorded in one configuration, which is changed from the menu of its chip (right click or double click). NIF Dante 1 is recorded in a new session, and an instrument that cannot be built cannot be added; Dante 2 is an example, because its channels are not public. The dialog of the directories lists the problems found in the definitions (check_diagnostics), and Diagnostics ▸ Refresh reads them again. X-ray power charts the Tᵣ of each recorded diagnostic, with the intensity toward it in TW/sr on the right axis. X-ray imagers shows the images of each imager in the history, one at a time, at the time cursor or for the recording (time-integrated, or a frame per gate), and opens one in the main image view. Radiography definitions are listed and do not record anything yet.\n(Image: The Diagnostics tab on its x-ray imagers after a drive history. The tab shows the categories with the number recorded in each, the recorded instrument as a chip, the history images of the lower SXI in a list, and the image chosen beside it.)\nOptimization: the free parameters, objective and limits described in the first session. A capsule keep-out (SphereKeepOut, on by default) penalizes a worst-case beam envelope that reaches the capsule, and an entrance-hole keep-out (ApertureKeepOut) penalizes one that reaches the hole rims. The aims are free either in the chamber, as z, r and the tangential offset of GroupPointing, or in the aim frame of each beam, as u, v and w of FramePointing. In the foil frame, u and v run across the face and w along the normal. A group shares one point of its frame, so the common point of a group converged on a foil moves across the foil face. The run stops at the iteration or the evaluation limit, whichever comes first, and after a cancel, the best design found before it is kept.\nClearance: the worst-case beam-to-hardware clearance (clearance) over pointing, positioning, hardware and focus errors on the chosen components, with progress per batch of beams and Cancel, after which the beams already checked are kept. The rows are colored pass, review or fail, the closest beam per component is listed, and the rows are exported as CSV (write_clearance_csv).\nSweep: a placement sweep (placement_sweep) steps a part, or the foil of a backlighter target, through a range of shifts along an axis of any frame. The parts placed in its frame and the beams aimed in those frames move with it. Each step is checked on a worker for:\nthe clearance (with the components, envelope and errors of the clearance check);\nthe line of sight from the foil through an object, by default from the center of the foil through the center of the object (line_of_sight);\nthe least fraction of the power of a beam that the foil captures;\noptionally, the pointing re-optimized with the optimization settings, warm-started from the previous step when the aims are free in their frames.\nThe quick sampling, the default, makes each of the many steps of a sweep short. The table lists the least clearance of each step and where it occurs, the failing and review pairs, the line of sight and the captured power. The chart plots the least clearance and the blocked fraction of the line of sight against the shift. Instead of a shift, the sweep can step a number in one of the placement steps of the part, such as a distance, an angle or a coordinate, set with Sweep… on the heading of the step. Every part whose frame changes, including the parts facing it, then moves rigidly with it at each value. A click on the chart or a row selects a step, drawn as an orange ghost of the moved parts beside the design. Apply step (or a double click) moves the part there as one edit, with the re-optimized pointing when the sweep re-optimized. A sweep is marked out of date when the design or a setting it used changes.\n(Image: A placement sweep of the backlighter foil along its front normal, in 1 mm steps from 2 mm farther from the target chamber center to 2 mm nearer it, which puts the foil 8 mm to 4 mm from it. The sweep runs the clearance check, the line of sight from the foil through the main target, and the power the foil captures. The least clearance falls from −1.130 mm to −1.901 mm (beams B35 and B36 on the main target), the line of sight stays clear, and the least fraction of the power of a beam on the foil drops from 95.62% at no shift to 63.50% at +2 mm. The step at +1 mm is selected.)\nTaLIS: the NIF target-configuration checks (talis_check). They cover 3ω capture, incidence and clearance, 1ω clearance and unconverted-light management, and the TAS stay-in zone. The checks run on the chosen capture components, checked components and diagnostics; diagnostics are checked for 3ω clearance and kept 10–25 mm from 1ω light. The settings include the rule tolerance and how it becomes a distance: measured from the target chamber center, from the aim point of the beam, the larger of the two, or from a given point. The error stack and sampling are those of the clearance check. The report shows the notes and a per-rule summary beside every check, colored by status with its margin, with a draggable split between the two. Each note takes one line, and the whole note shows on hover. The notes give what the tolerance stands for at the top, then the per-rule summary, the error set, the checks not evaluated and the requirements out of scope (TALIS_OUT_OF_SCOPE). A report is marked out of date when the target, the beams or any setting it used changes. The report exports as CSV with write_talis_csv, and the header of the file lists the settings, the checks not evaluated and the requirements out of scope.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Files-in-and-out","page":"Desktop application","text":"Beams: beam tables (read_beam_table, write_beam_table), a NIF VisRad SST pointing CSV with aim points and defocus (read_visrad_sst), and a Laser Beam Summary CSV with pointing, defocus, power and spot (read_visrad_summary). Beams off or absent in a file are switched off at their power, and the warnings of the readers are listed.\nVisRad workspaces: File ▸ Import VisRad workspace reads the components of a workspace as VisRad object parts, and its beams, on worker threads with progress. File ▸ Export VisRad workspace writes the beams back into a copy of the imported workspace (write_visrad_workspace).\nResults: File ▸ Export target as STL (write_stl, mm) and File ▸ Export results (write_results). The results are the per-element laser, radiosity and flux of the drive, the power per component and beam, and the capsule modes, as CSV, with the session that produced them.\nRetune to a reference drive (Compute ▸): the pulse table of a reference shot that fired every beam of the table sets the target. The powers of the enabled beams are retuned at each step of the drive history to restore the drive of the reference (retune_drive), within a limit relative to the reference powers and optionally staying near them. The steps are the times of the table, the prepulse window and the zero-power steps between; the power is zero where the reference does not fire 3ω light. The disabled beams are the ones the shot loses. What is restored depends on the target:\nfor an observed sphere, its level and modes through l = 4 (CapsuleModes);\nfor an observed flat face, the incident x-ray flux over its elements, and so its mean, RMS nonuniformity and peak-to-mean (FaceFlux);\nwithout a radiation model, the absorbed 3ω irradiance of the active backlighter foil within the spot radius of the drive (FoilIrradiance, laser only).\nThe new pulses are written as a pulse table. For a capsule, the dialog reports the worst-time change of mode 1 and Tᵣ before and after. For a face or a foil, it reports the worst RMS and the worst departure of the mean from the reference mean, before and after. The Time tab plots the power of each group on the reference and retuned pulses, with ε₁ before and after for a capsule. For a face or a spot it plots the RMS (before, after and reference) and the departure of the mean from the reference mean (before and after).","title":"Files in and out"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#CAD-workspaces-and-large-targets","page":"Desktop application","text":"A CAD workspace can bring hundreds of thousands of facets, most of them mounting hardware far from the target. On import, a CAD part that reaches farther than twice the farthest aim point of the beams the workspace fires is only drawn, from the facets of its file without remeshing, and is left out of the calculations. The status line names these parts, and Include in calculations puts one back. Beams that the workspace has switched off are not drawn when most of the beams are off (View ▸ Beams ▸ Show beams that are off).\n\nThe drive of a target with CAD parts runs on its cavity around the target chamber center (Drive ▸ Cavity; Briareus.enclosure). The cavity is made of the elements that bound the free space around the point, without the outer faces of the assembly. A thin film modeled as a solid that seals the point into a pocket, such as a capsule tent, is found and left out, and the drive properties list what the cavity kept of each part. The view factors of the cavity are balanced once aperture parts facing into the cavity close its entrance holes, with under 1% of its rays leaking (CAVITY_MAX_LEAK). A target whose x-ray view factors would not fit in half the memory (radiation_size, about 160,000 elements on 32 GB) gets only the laser deposition, with the reason in the status line.\n\nRemeshing a CAD part and building the ray-tracing hierarchy of a large target run on worker threads. An edit takes effect at once: the part is drawn from the facets of its file, or the previous target is shown, until the new one is ready, and a drive requested meanwhile starts when the target is ready. The sphere fit of a CAD capsule runs the same way. A CAD part that is one cylinder or sphere as a whole, such as a tube or a shell (fit_surfaces), is integrated on that surface in the view factors. Parts of several faces stay flat, and the drive status names flat parts with more than 50 facets per element, a sign of a curved face tessellated in slivers, which slows the view factors.","title":"CAD workspaces and large targets"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Scripting-the-window","page":"Desktop application","text":"briareus returns its session (a DesignSession) when the window closes, and the controllers in BriareusGUI.Controllers run the steps of the window from a script without a window. Text-field setters (edit_*) take the text of the field and return false, with the reason in the error of the controller, when the geometry or a check rejects the value.\n\nusing BriareusGUI, BriareusGUI.Controllers\nsess = DesignSession(resolution = :coarse)       # hohlraum + NIF quads\nadd_window!(sess.target; phi = 60.0, open = true) # a 1 × 1 mm hole in the wall\nadd_part!(sess.target, :disk; z = 7.5, r1 = 2.5, theta = 180)   # a shield above the top LEH\nedit_mesh!(sess.target, :capsule_h, \"0.05\")      # element sizes in mm\nedit_beam_node!(sess.beams, :group, \"inner23\", :z, \"3.2\")   # inner 23.5° cone aim z (mm)\nimport_visrad_sst!(sess.beams, \"pointing.csv\")   # or apply a VisRad pointing file\nbuild_scenario!(sess)                            # view factors + evaluation\ndrive_summary(sess.drive.result[])\nstart_optimize!(sess; spawn = false)             # blocks; spawn = true runs on a worker\naccept_optimum!(sess)\nstart_clearance!(sess; spawn = false)\nworst_rows(sess.review)\nstart_talis!(sess; spawn = false)                # TaLIS rules, same error stack\ntalis_rule_rows(sess.talis)\ntalis_notes(sess.talis)                          # the rule-tolerance note first\nexport_results(sess, \"results\")                  # per-element and per-mode CSV\nretune_to_reference!(sess, \"reference.csv\", \"retuned.csv\")  # disabled beams are the dropped ones\nload_pulses!(sess, \"reference.csv\")              # or use_pulse_shape!(sess): N221204's shape\nstart_history!(sess)                             # drive_history on the drive's view factors\nset_diagnostic!(sess, \"nif_dante1_2012\")          # also record Dante 1 as fielded in 2012–14\ntime_chart(sess, :drive)                         # what the Time tab plots (also :pulses, :diagnostics, :energy, :irradiance, :retune)\nset_time_cursor!(sess, 6.0)                      # the 3D view at the history step nearest 6 ns\nset_prepulse!(sess.time, 2e-3, 0.0; start = -4.0, stop = 0.0)  # 1ω prepulse, TW per beam\nstart_irradiance!(sess)                          # unconverted irradiance histories and checks\nirradiance_checks(sess)                          # specular and pre-plasma: pass, first crossing\nnudge_part!(sess.target, 1, 0.5; frame = :leh_top, axis = :z)   # part 1 up 0.5 mm, one edit\nturn_part!(sess.target, 1, 15.0; axis = :x)      # 15° about x of the frame it is placed in (the triad's rings)\nset_aim_frame!(sess, 1:4, :disk_1; keep = false)     # four beams at the disk's center, following it\nset_sweep!(sess.sweep; subject = :disk_1, frame = :tcc, axis = :x)\nedit_sweep!(sess.sweep, :step, \"0.25\")           # shifts from start to stop, mm\nstart_sweep!(sess; spawn = false)                # placement_sweep with the window's checks\napply_sweep_step!(sess, 3)                       # the third step into the design, one edit\nsave_session(\"design.briar\", sess)               # the design and its CAD files; load_session! reads it\nsave_session(\"design.briar.toml\", sess)          # the design alone as TOML, CAD files by path\nrecord!(sess); undo!(sess)                       # undo/redo steps (a window records them itself)\n\nThe screenshots on this page are produced by dev/gui_screenshots.jl in the repository, which drives the real window through the same callbacks as its menus.","title":"Scripting the window"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/retune/#Retuning-a-drive-to-a-reference-shot","page":"Retuning a drive to a reference shot","text":"A shot that worked is the reference for the next one. The next one may not repeat it exactly: a bundle may be down, the amplifiers of a quad may not reach the power they gave before, or a backlighter may take a bundle from the drive. The question is then how to set the powers of the remaining beams, at every moment of the pulse, so that the capsule receives the drive it received before: the same level and the same low-mode shape through time.\n\nThis tutorial builds a reference shot from a total laser power and a cone fraction against time (the way NIF pulses are written), drops a bundle, and retunes the power histories of the other 46 quads to match the capsule drive of the reference in every mode through l = 4, within the power limit of each quad. It introduces cone_fraction_pulses, pulse tables (write_pulse_table, read_pulse_table), drive histories that keep their albedos, and retune_drive.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing Printf\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\nset_theme!(fontsize = 15, linewidth = 2.5)","title":"Retuning a drive to a reference shot"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/retune/#The-reference-shot","page":"Retuning a drive to a reference shot","text":"The target is the NIF-like hohlraum of the other tutorials (10.1 × 5.75 mm, with 3.1 mm entrance holes and a 0.9 mm capsule). Its gold wall loses energy into the Marshak wave (WallLossAlbedo, with an albedo that rises through the pulse), and the mesh is coarse to keep the tutorial quick. The 48 quads are pointed as in Freeing a bundle for radiography.\n\ntarget = hohlraum(length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9,\n                  wall = Material(:Au; albedo = WallLossAlbedo(), conversion = 0.8),\n                  h = 0.45, capsule_h = 0.12)\npointing = Dict(:outer50 => (0.0, 5.01), :outer44 => (0.0, 5.20),\n                :inner30 => (0.60, 3.63), :inner23 => (1.09, 3.09))\nfunction aimed(b)\n    r, z = pointing[b.group]\n    φ = port_angles(b)[2]\n    return remake(b; aim = (-r * cosd(φ), -r * sind(φ), sign(b.port[3]) * z))\nend\nquads = aimed.(nif_quads())\n48-element Vector{Beam{Float64}}:\n Beam(Q15T, θ=23.50°, φ=78.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.2126, -1.069, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q42T, θ=23.50°, φ=168.75°, 8 TW, aim=(1.069, -0.2126, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q33T, θ=23.50°, φ=258.75°, 8 TW, aim=(0.2126, 1.069, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q24T, θ=23.50°, φ=348.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-1.069, 0.2126, 3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q13T, θ=30.58°, φ=34.33°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.4955, -0.3384, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q41T, θ=30.00°, φ=123.75°, 8 TW, aim=(0.3333, -0.4989, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q36T, θ=30.58°, φ=214.33°, 8 TW, aim=(0.4955, 0.3384, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q26T, θ=30.00°, φ=303.75°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.3333, 0.4989, 3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q14T, θ=44.50°, φ=16.29°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, -0, 5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q12T, θ=44.50°, φ=62.46°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, -0, 5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n ⋮\n Beam(Q22B, θ=135.50°, φ=343.71°, 8 TW, aim=(-0, 0, -5.2), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q16B, θ=150.00°, φ=56.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.3333, -0.4989, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q44B, θ=149.42°, φ=145.67°, 8 TW, aim=(0.4955, -0.3384, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q31B, θ=150.00°, φ=236.25°, 8 TW, aim=(0.3333, 0.4989, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q21B, θ=149.42°, φ=325.67°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.4955, 0.3384, -3.63), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q15B, θ=156.50°, φ=11.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-1.069, -0.2126, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q42B, θ=156.50°, φ=101.25°, 8 TW, aim=(0.2126, -1.069, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q33B, θ=156.50°, φ=191.25°, 8 TW, aim=(1.069, 0.2126, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(Q24B, θ=156.50°, φ=281.25°, 8 TW, aim=(-0.2126, 1.069, -3.09), defocus=0 mm)\n\nThe reference pulse is a total power with a picket, foot, second step and 420 TW peak, and a cone fraction, the share of the inner cones, that falls from 0.35 in the foot to 0.25 at the peak. cone_fraction_pulses turns them into the power per quad of each cone; write_pulse_table writes them as CSV, the form in which the requested or measured powers of a reference shot come:\n\ntt = [0.0, 0.3, 1.0, 1.4, 4.5, 5.0, 6.0, 7.5, 9.5, 10.0]\ntotal = [0.0, 60.0, 60.0, 15.0, 15.0, 50.0, 50.0, 420.0, 420.0, 0.0]\nfraction = [0.35, 0.35, 0.35, 0.35, 0.33, 0.30, 0.30, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25]\nreference_pulses = cone_fraction_pulses(quads, tt, total, fraction)\npulse_file = write_pulse_table(joinpath(mktempdir(), \"reference.csv\"), reference_pulses)\nprint(read(pulse_file, String)[1:300], \"…\\n\")\ntime_ns,inner23,inner30,outer44,outer50\n0,0,0,0,0\n0.3,1.3125,1.3125,1.21875,1.21875\n1,1.3125,1.3125,1.21875,1.21875\n1.4,0.328125,0.328125,0.3046875,0.3046875\n4.5,0.309375,0.309375,0.3140625,0.3140625\n5,0.9375,0.9375,1.09375,1.09375\n6,0.9375,0.9375,1.09375,1.09375\n7.5,6.5625,6.5625,9.84375,9.84375\n9.…\n\nThe reference drive is a drive_history of the shot, which solves the radiosity at each time with the albedos the wall has then. With ylm = true it stores the real spherical harmonics of the capsule flux, and with keep_albedo = true the converged albedos of each step. Together these are the target drive and the state to linearize about.\n\nmodel = RadiationModel(target; closed = true)\nop = DepositionOperator(target, quads)\ntimes = 0.4:0.4:9.6\nreference = drive_history(model, op, τ -> with_pulses(quads, reference_pulses, τ), times;\n                          lmax = 4, ylm = true, keep_albedo = true)\n@printf(\"capsule Tᵣ at the peak: %.0f eV; wall albedo from %.2f to %.2f\\n\", maximum(reference.Tr),\n        extrema(reference.wall_albedo)...)\ncapsule Tᵣ at the peak: 293 eV; wall albedo from 0.65 to 0.94","title":"The reference shot"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/retune/#A-bundle-down","page":"Retuning a drive to a reference shot","text":"Bundle 31 (quads Q31T and Q31B, one in each hemisphere) is not available. With the same pulses on the other 46 quads, the capsule loses some drive and gains a mode-1 asymmetry, a flux difference between its sides toward and away from the missing ports. The deposition operator of the remaining quads is that of the reference, restricted to them:\n\nkeep = findall(b -> nif_bundle(b) != \"31\", quads)\nkept = quads[keep]\nop_kept = op[keep]\ndropped = drive_history(model, op_kept, τ -> with_pulses(kept, reference_pulses, τ),\n                        reference.time; lmax = 4, ylm = true)\nε(h, l) = [100 * mode_amplitude(h.ylm[:, k], l) for k in eachindex(h.time)]\npeak(h, x) = (k = findall(t -> 7.6 <= t <= 9.4, h.time); sum(x[k]) / length(k))\n@printf(\"bundle down: ε₁ %.2f%% at the peak (%.2f%% at most), Tᵣ %+.1f eV\\n\",\n        peak(dropped, ε(dropped, 1)), maximum(ε(dropped, 1)), peak(dropped, dropped.Tr .- reference.Tr))\nbundle down: ε₁ 2.70% at the peak (5.17% at most), Tᵣ -3.2 eV","title":"A bundle down"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/retune/#Retuning-the-quads","page":"Retuning a drive to a reference shot","text":"At any moment the capsule flux is linear in the powers of the beams once the albedos are fixed, and the reference history gives the albedos. So at each reference time retune_drive finds the power of each quad that brings the shape of the capsule flux, c_lmc_00, back to that of the reference for l = 1–4 at the reference level c_00 (the quantity CapsuleModes). That is a least-squares problem with the power of each quad between zero and its limit, here 1.3 times the power per quad of its cone in the reference at that time. The same retune constrains the x-ray flux of a flat face (FaceFlux, such as the package face in a halfraum) or the laser irradiance of a backlighter foil (FoilIrradiance). It matches either the distribution of the reference or, with target = :uniform and a level, as uniform as the powers allow at a prescribed mean.\n\nnominal(k, τ) = reference_pulses[first(b.group for b in kept if Symbol(b.quad) == k)](τ)\nlimit(k, τ) = (0.0, 1.3 * nominal(k, τ) + 1e-9)\nretuned = retune_drive(CapsuleModes(model), op_kept, kept, reference; by = :quad, limits = limit)\n@printf(\"linear prediction: shape matched to %.1e, drive to %.1e (worst time)\\n\",\n        maximum(retuned.residual), maximum(abs.(retuned.drive .- 1)))\nlinear prediction: shape matched to 4.7e-03, drive to 6.7e-16 (worst time)\n\nThe prediction fixes the albedos at those of the reference; a drive history of the new pulses, where the albedos follow the new drive, is the check:\n\ntuned = drive_history(model, op_kept, τ -> with_pulses(kept, retuned.pulses, τ; by = :quad),\n                      reference.time; lmax = 4, ylm = true)\n@printf(\"history against the prediction: c_lm/c₀₀ within %.1e\\n\",\n        maximum(abs.(retuned.shape .- tuned.ylm[1:25, :] ./ tuned.ylm[1:1, :])))\nhistory against the prediction: c_lm/c₀₀ within 8.4e-05\n\nThe two agree to better than 1e-4: the drive stays close enough to that of the reference that the wall albedos do not move. The retune takes the mode 1 out at every time and gives back the drive the bundle took:\n\nprintln(\"               ε₁ peak   ε₁ max   ε₂ peak   ε₄ peak   ΔTᵣ peak\")\nfor (label, h) in ((\"reference\", reference), (\"bundle down\", dropped), (\"retuned\", tuned))\n    @printf(\"  %-12s  %.3f%%   %.3f%%   %.3f%%   %.3f%%   %+.2f eV\\n\", label, peak(h, ε(h, 1)),\n            maximum(ε(h, 1)), peak(h, ε(h, 2)), peak(h, ε(h, 4)), peak(h, h.Tr .- reference.Tr))\nend\n               ε₁ peak   ε₁ max   ε₂ peak   ε₄ peak   ΔTᵣ peak\n  reference     0.000%   0.000%   10.199%   0.417%   +0.00 eV\n  bundle down   2.698%   5.165%   9.994%   0.419%   -3.24 eV\n  retuned       0.031%   0.179%   10.156%   0.472%   +0.00 eV\n\nBut it gets there by using the limits: at the peak many quads run at 1.3 times their reference power and a few almost off. Many power sets restore the drive; with nominal (the powers of the reference) and a weight stay, the retune selects the one closest to the reference, trading a little of the match for smaller departures:\n\nk_peak = findfirst(≈(8.8), reference.time)\nprintln(\"  stay      ε₁ peak   ε₁ max   powers / reference at 8.8 ns   at the limit\")\nrows = Any[(\"unconstrained\", 0.0, retuned, tuned)]\nfor stay in (1e-5, 1e-4, 1e-3)\n    r = retune_drive(CapsuleModes(model), op_kept, kept, reference; by = :quad, limits = limit, nominal, stay)\n    h = drive_history(model, op_kept, τ -> with_pulses(kept, r.pulses, τ; by = :quad),\n                      reference.time; lmax = 4, ylm = true)\n    push!(rows, (@sprintf(\"%.0e\", stay), stay, r, h))\nend\nfor (label, stay, r, h) in rows\n    ratio = [r.power[g, k_peak] / nominal(key, r.time[k_peak]) for (g, key) in enumerate(r.keys)]\n    @printf(\"  %-12s  %.3f%%   %.3f%%   %.2f–%.2f                      %d of %d\\n\", label,\n            peak(h, ε(h, 1)), maximum(ε(h, 1)), extrema(ratio)...,\n            count(>=(1.3 - 1e-6), ratio), length(ratio))\nend\n  stay      ε₁ peak   ε₁ max   powers / reference at 8.8 ns   at the limit\n  unconstrained  0.031%   0.179%   0.00–1.30                      25 of 46\n  1e-05         0.130%   0.255%   0.78–1.30                      7 of 46\n  1e-04         0.340%   0.456%   0.93–1.30                      3 of 46\n  1e-03         1.314%   1.574%   1.01–1.19                      0 of 46\n\nA small stay keeps most of the correction with only a few quads at their limit. Larger ones give back mode 1 for powers within about 20% of those of the reference. The power histories of one design are plotted below as the power of each quad over its reference power, for the other quads in the cluster of the dropped bundle (quads Q3…):\n\nr_stay = rows[3][3]\nfig = Figure(size = (820, 420))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (ns)\", ylabel = \"power / reference power\",\n           title = \"Cluster 3 quads, stay = 1e-4\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"time (ns)\", ylabel = \"ε₁ (%)\", title = \"Mode 1 of the capsule drive\")\ncluster3 = sort(filter(g -> startswith(String(r_stay.keys[g]), \"Q3\"), eachindex(r_stay.keys));\n                by = g -> String(r_stay.keys[g]))\nfor (i, g) in enumerate(cluster3)\n    key = r_stay.keys[g]\n    lines!(ax1, r_stay.time, r_stay.power[g, :] ./ nominal.(key, r_stay.time); label = String(key),\n           color = Makie.to_colormap(:tab10)[i])\nend\nhlines!(ax1, [1.0, 1.3]; color = :black, linestyle = :dash, linewidth = 1.5)\nfor (label, h, c) in ((\"bundle down\", dropped, :firebrick), (\"retuned\", tuned, :seagreen),\n                      (\"retuned, stay = 1e-4\", rows[3][4], :steelblue))\n    lines!(ax2, h.time, ε(h, 1); label, color = c)\nend\nLegend(fig[2, 2], ax2; orientation = :vertical, framevisible = false, labelsize = 12, tellheight = false, valign = :top)\nLegend(fig[2, 1], ax1; orientation = :horizontal, nbanks = 3, framevisible = false, labelsize = 11)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Retuning the quads"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/retune/#What-this-checks","page":"Retuning a drive to a reference shot","text":"The per-time linear model is that of the drive history: the history of the retuned pulses, with albedos that follow the drive, reproduces the predicted shape to better than 1e-4 (the test suite checks 1e-4 on a separate mesh).\nEach quad stays within its limits and the drive level within 0.1% of the reference level at every time (test suite), and the mode 1 of the dropped bundle falls by more than an order of magnitude.\nWeighting the departure from the reference powers trades the match for smaller departures monotonically; in the test suite a larger stay gives a smaller departure and a larger residual.\nA face and a foil (test suite, test/retune.jl) are tested with two beams symmetric by a half turn, one at 0.6 of the power of the other in the reference. The reference target recovers the powers to 1e-7. The uniform target gives equal powers at the requested level (1e-10), with the RMS below its value at power ratios of 0.9–1.1 on that level. The reported mean and RMS equal those recomputed from the depositions (foil) and from the flux of the drive history on the face elements (halfraum face) to 1e-12.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#Polar-direct-drive:-a-published-NIF-pointing","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"NIF's beams enter through ports within 50° of the poles, built for indirect drive. To drive a bare capsule directly, polar direct drive (PDD) re-points the beams toward the equator and balances the powers of the cones, so that the illumination of the sphere is as uniform as the port layout allows. Barlow et al. (PRL 133, 175101, 2024; Ba24 below) optimized such a pointing with a genetic algorithm and published it as their \"Optimized Config\" (OC). The OC gives, per cone, the surface point the beams aim at, a defocus and a power (their Table I). One term of their fitness is σ₁, the rms of the vacuum illumination of the 1.1 mm sphere relative to its mean over the spherical-harmonic degrees l ≤ 30, which is 6.03% for the OC (their Table II).\n\nThis tutorial builds the OC on a bare sphere, computes σ₁, its mode spectrum and its m = 0 / m ≠ 0 split with Briareus, and compares them with Ba24. The comparison is a documented example and is not used as a test, for three reasons. Ba24's best-focus spots are unpublished internal models of their ray tracer (Ifriit), and Briareus uses the public LMF-measured fits instead. The defocus model of Briareus differs from theirs. And the illumination is CBET-free, as σ₁ is. The tutorial settles how Ba24's Table I angles are read, measures how much the spot model moves σ₁, and checks Briareus on this facility case against an independent quadrature. It introduces the spherical fields of GroupPointing and the capsule-frame metrics mode_rms and mode_power. A pointing optimization from the OC is the example examples/ba24_pdd_optimization.jl.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Briareus), \"docs\", \"literate\", \"_plotting.jl\"))   # figure helpers\ncutaway! (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#The-target-and-the-metric","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"A bare sphere is a one-component target. An icosphere of subdivision n has 20n² triangles; at n = 30 (18 000 elements, edges about 40 µm on R = 1.1 mm) the facet averaging of the element representation lowers σ by about 1.7%, a second-order error that a Richardson extrapolation from n = 20 removes (the research notes, \"Hard-sphere direct-drive illumination\"). ylm_weights projects any per-element field onto real spherical harmonics to l = 30 with exact facet solid angles; it is built once per mesh.\n\nR, L = 1.1, 30\nsphere(n) = Target(Component(:capsule, icosphere_mesh(R; n), Material(:CH);\n                             surface = SphereSurface(R)))\nmeshes = map((20, 30)) do n\n    t = sphere(n)\n    (; n, t, W = ylm_weights(t, collect(1:nelements(t)); lmax = L))\nend\ntarget = meshes[2].t\n@printf(\"%d and %d elements\\n\", nelements(meshes[1].t), nelements(target))\n8000 and 18000 elements\n\nA deposition on the sphere is hard-sphere illumination: rays travel straight, and the incident intensity times the cosine of the incidence angle, Icospsi, is absorbed where each ray first meets the sphere. Its rms relative to the mean over 1 le l le 30 is mode_rms of the flux coefficients, and mode_power is the spectrum σ_l. Both split into the zonal part m = 0, the azimuthal average set by the polar angles and powers of the cones, and the rest, m ≠ 0, which comes from the discreteness of the beams in azimuth.\n\nfunction modes(m, op, beams; kw...)\n    dep = deposit(op, beams; kw...)\n    c = m.W * (dep.element ./ m.t.elem_area)\n    (; σ = mode_rms(c, L), zero = mode_rms(c, L; m = :zero), nonzero = mode_rms(c, L; m = :nonzero),\n       σl = mode_power(ylm_matrix(c, L)), absorbed = sum(dep.element) / dep.delivered,\n       field = dep.element ./ m.t.elem_area)\nend\nrichardson(a, b) = ((30 / 20)^2 * b - a) / ((30 / 20)^2 - 1)\nrichardson (generic function with 1 method)","title":"The target and the metric"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#The-Optimized-Config","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"Ba24's Table I gives, per cone pair, a polar angle and an azimuthal offset of the aim point on the 1.1 mm sphere, a defocus and a power in percent of 4 TW per quad. The caption measures the polar angle from the pole and the offset from the azimuth of the port. That is GroupPointing's :theta_s and :dphi_s with radius = R: the aim is the point of the sphere at those angles, mirrored to 180 - theta_s in the lower hemisphere with the offset in the same sense (Ba24 chose offsets that turn the same way in both hemispheres).\n\noc = Dict(:inner23 => (6.36, 52.02, 8.22, 66.26), :inner30 => (14.95, 160.75, 9.07, 66.26),\n          :outer44 => (52.48, -12.14, 4.67, 95.05), :outer50 => (85.62, 6.90, 6.83, 82.88))\np = GroupPointing(nif_quads(); fields = (:theta_s, :dphi_s, :defocus, :power), radius = R)\nx_oc = reduce(vcat, [[oc[k][1], oc[k][2], oc[k][3], 4 * oc[k][4] / 100] for k in p.keys])\nquads = apply(p, x_oc)\nfor k in p.keys\n    q = quads[findfirst(b -> b.group == k && b.port[3] > 0, quads)]\n    θ, φ = port_angles(q)\n    @printf(\"%-8s port (%5.1f°, %6.2f°) -> aim (%5.2f°, %6.2f°), defocus %.2f mm, %.2f TW/quad\\n\",\n            k, θ, φ, acosd(q.aim[3] / R), mod(atand(q.aim[2], q.aim[1]), 360), q.defocus, q.power)\nend\ninner23  port ( 23.5°,  78.75°) -> aim ( 6.36°, 130.77°), defocus 8.22 mm, 2.65 TW/quad\ninner30  port ( 30.6°,  34.33°) -> aim (14.95°, 195.08°), defocus 9.07 mm, 2.65 TW/quad\nouter44  port ( 44.5°,  16.29°) -> aim (52.48°,   4.15°), defocus 4.67 mm, 3.80 TW/quad\nouter50  port ( 50.0°,  39.67°) -> aim (85.62°,  46.57°), defocus 6.83 mm, 3.32 TW/quad\n\nBa24 assume that the four beams of a quad overlap exactly at the aim point, repointed after defocusing. Briareus therefore uses NIF's 192 beams, each from its port direction, with the aim and defocus of its quad and a quarter of its power:\n\nquad_of = Dict(q.quad => q for q in quads)\nfunction oc_beams(; spot = b -> b.spot, defocus = q -> q.defocus, aims = Dict(q.quad => q.aim for q in quads))\n    map(nif_beams()) do b\n        q = quad_of[b.quad]\n        remake(b; aim = aims[b.quad], defocus = defocus(q), power = q.power / 4, spot = spot(b))\n    end\nend\nbeams = oc_beams()\nlength(beams)\n192","title":"The Optimized Config"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#Two-spot-models","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"The Briareus beams. The best-focus spots are the LMF-measured CPP fits of nif_spot (super-Gaussians of orders 4.5–8, elliptical), and a defocus blurs the best-focus profile over the f/20.7 cone of each beam (geometric optics, Cok et al. 2008's model in substance), with the focus the defocus beyond the aim.\n\nBa24's beams, emulated. Ba24 treat each beam as a plane wave and grow the super-Gaussian 1/e radii linearly with defocus, w(d) = w_0 (1 + kappa d) with κ = 0.30, 0.40, 0.80, 0.95 per 50 mm for the 23.5°, 30°, 44.5° and 50° cones (fitted to pF3D; their appendix). Here that is a zero-defocus spot with the radii scaled explicitly and collimated beams (ncone = 1), with the same LMF fits as w_0.\n\nκ = Dict(:inner23 => 0.30 / 50, :inner30 => 0.40 / 50, :outer44 => 0.80 / 50, :outer50 => 0.95 / 50)\ngrown(b) = (g = 1 + κ[b.group] * quad_of[b.quad].defocus; with_axes(b.spot, g * b.spot.a, g * b.spot.b))\nemulated = oc_beams(; spot = grown, defocus = q -> 0.0)\n192-element Vector{Beam{Float64}}:\n Beam(B111, θ=52.38°, φ=42.28°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(0.754, 0.7965, 0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B112, θ=47.68°, φ=42.47°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(0.754, 0.7965, 0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B113, θ=52.38°, φ=37.05°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(0.754, 0.7965, 0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B114, θ=47.68°, φ=36.86°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(0.754, 0.7965, 0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B115, θ=127.62°, φ=53.24°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(0.589, 0.9252, -0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B116, θ=132.32°, φ=53.43°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(0.589, 0.9252, -0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B117, θ=127.62°, φ=48.01°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(0.589, 0.9252, -0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B118, θ=132.32°, φ=47.82°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(0.589, 0.9252, -0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B121, θ=46.89°, φ=59.62°, 0.9505 TW, aim=(0.5571, 0.6715, 0.6699), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B122, θ=42.19°, φ=59.37°, 0.9505 TW, aim=(0.5571, 0.6715, 0.6699), defocus=0 mm)\n ⋮\n Beam(B458, θ=133.11°, φ=160.87°, 0.9505 TW, aim=(-0.7672, 0.4154, -0.6699), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B461, θ=47.68°, φ=132.18°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(-0.7927, 0.758, 0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B462, θ=52.38°, φ=131.99°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(-0.7927, 0.758, 0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B463, θ=47.68°, φ=126.57°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(-0.7927, 0.758, 0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B464, θ=52.38°, φ=126.76°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(-0.7927, 0.758, 0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B465, θ=132.32°, φ=143.14°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(-0.9222, 0.5937, -0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B466, θ=127.62°, φ=142.95°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(-0.9222, 0.5937, -0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B467, θ=132.32°, φ=137.53°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(-0.9222, 0.5937, -0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n Beam(B468, θ=127.62°, φ=137.72°, 0.8288 TW, aim=(-0.9222, 0.5937, -0.08401), defocus=0 mm)\n\nA DepositionOperator depends on the target and the ports only, so one operator per mesh and beam model is used for every pointing below.\n\nops = [(; native = DepositionOperator(m.t, beams), plane = DepositionOperator(m.t, beams; ncone = 1))\n       for m in meshes]\nnative = [modes(m, o.native, beams) for (m, o) in zip(meshes, ops)]\nplane = [modes(m, o.plane, emulated) for (m, o) in zip(meshes, ops)]\nfor (name, r) in ((\"Briareus beams\", native), (\"Ba24 emulated\", plane))\n    @printf(\"%-15s σ₁ = %.3f%% (n = 20), %.3f%% (n = 30), %.3f%% extrapolated; m = 0 %.3f%%, m ≠ 0 %.3f%%; absorbed %.3f\\n\",\n            name, 100r[1].σ, 100r[2].σ, 100richardson(r[1].σ, r[2].σ),\n            100richardson(r[1].zero, r[2].zero), 100richardson(r[1].nonzero, r[2].nonzero), r[2].absorbed)\nend\nBriareus beams  σ₁ = 6.492% (n = 20), 6.534% (n = 30), 6.569% extrapolated; m = 0 5.266%, m ≠ 0 3.926%; absorbed 0.936\nBa24 emulated   σ₁ = 7.781% (n = 20), 7.829% (n = 30), 7.867% extrapolated; m = 0 6.936%, m ≠ 0 3.712%; absorbed 0.924\n\nThe two models give σ₁ ≈ 6.6% and 7.9% against Ba24's 6.03%. Both are dominated by the zonal part. The absorbed fraction, 0.92–0.94, is the light that meets the sphere; the rest passes beside it, because no plasma is modeled.","title":"Two spot models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#An-independent-check","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"For collimated beams the illumination has a closed form at every point of the sphere. A point hat s is lit by each beam whose port it faces, with the super-Gaussian of the beam evaluated at the distance of the point from the beam axis and multiplied by hat s cdot hat U for the projected intensity. The coefficients to l = 30 follow from a Gauss–Legendre rule in mu = costheta (256 nodes) and the trapezoid rule in φ (512 points), with the normalized associated Legendre functions by their recurrence. This code shares nothing with the operator or the mode weights. It also gives the incident intensity I (no cosine), which deposit does not.\n\nfunction gauss_legendre(n)\n    x, w = zeros(n), zeros(n)\n    for i in 1:n\n        z, dp = cos(π * (i - 0.25) / (n + 0.5)), 0.0\n        for _ in 1:100\n            p0, p1 = 1.0, z\n            for k in 2:n\n                p0, p1 = p1, ((2k - 1) * z * p1 - (k - 1) * p0) / k\n            end\n            dp = n * (z * p1 - p0) / (z^2 - 1)\n            δ = p1 / dp\n            z -= δ\n            abs(δ) < 1e-15 && break\n        end\n        x[i], w[i] = z, 2 / ((1 - z^2) * dp^2)\n    end\n    return x, w\nend\n\nfunction legendre_normalized!(P, μ)          # P[l+1, m+1] = P̄_lm(μ), ∫ P̄² dμ = 1\n    s = sqrt(max(0.0, 1 - μ^2))\n    P[1, 1] = sqrt(0.5)\n    for m in 0:L\n        m > 0 && (P[m+1, m+1] = -sqrt((2m + 1) / (2m)) * s * P[m, m])\n        m < L && (P[m+2, m+1] = sqrt(2m + 3) * μ * P[m+1, m+1])\n        for l in m+2:L\n            P[l+1, m+1] = sqrt((4l^2 - 1) / (l^2 - m^2)) *\n                          (μ * P[l, m+1] - P[l-1, m+1] / sqrt((4(l - 1)^2 - 1) / ((l - 1)^2 - m^2)))\n        end\n    end\n    return P\nend\n\nfunction plane_wave_modes(beams; incident = false, nμ = 256, nφ = 512)\n    spots = map(beams) do b\n        θ, φ = port_angles(b)\n        U = [sind(θ) * cosd(φ), sind(θ) * sind(φ), cosd(θ)]\n        eθ, eφ = [cosd(θ) * cosd(φ), cosd(θ) * sind(φ), -sind(θ)], [-sind(φ), cosd(φ), 0.0]\n        sp = b.spot\n        ea = cos(sp.angle) * eθ + sin(sp.angle) * eφ\n        n = sp.order\n        (; U, aim = collect(b.aim), ea, eb = cross(U, ea), sp.a, sp.b, n,\n           w = b.power * n / (2π * sp.a * sp.b * Briareus.gamma(2 / n)))\n    end\n    function I(s)\n        v = 0.0\n        for g in spots\n            c = dot(g.U, s)\n            c > 0 || continue\n            d = R * s - g.aim\n            v += g.w * exp(-((dot(d, g.ea) / g.a)^2 + (dot(d, g.eb) / g.b)^2)^(g.n / 2)) * (incident ? 1 : c)\n        end\n        return v\n    end\n    xg, wg = gauss_legendre(nμ)\n    P = zeros(L + 1, L + 1)\n    A = zeros(ComplexF64, L + 1, L + 1)\n    vals = zeros(nφ)\n    for (μ, wμ) in zip(xg, wg)\n        st = sqrt(1 - μ^2)\n        Threads.@threads for j in 1:nφ\n            vals[j] = I([st * cospi(2(j - 1) / nφ), st * sinpi(2(j - 1) / nφ), μ])\n        end\n        legendre_normalized!(P, μ)\n        for m in 0:L\n            F = sum(vals[j] * cispi(-2m * (j - 1) / nφ) for j in 1:nφ) / nφ\n            for l in m:L\n                A[l+1, m+1] += wμ * sqrt(2π) * F * P[l+1, m+1]\n            end\n        end\n    end\n    zonal = [abs(A[l+1, 1]) / abs(A[1, 1]) for l in 0:L]\n    rest = [sqrt(2sum(abs2, A[l+1, 2:l+1]; init = 0.0)) / abs(A[1, 1]) for l in 0:L]\n    rms(v) = sqrt(sum(abs2, v[2:end]))\n    return (; σ = rms(hypot.(zonal, rest)), zero = rms(zonal), nonzero = rms(rest), σl = hypot.(zonal, rest))\nend\n\nreference = plane_wave_modes(emulated)\nincident = plane_wave_modes(emulated; incident = true)\n@printf(\"plane-wave quadrature: σ₁ = %.4f%% (m = 0 %.4f%%, m ≠ 0 %.4f%%); Briareus extrapolated %.4f%%\\n\",\n        100reference.σ, 100reference.zero, 100reference.nonzero, 100richardson(plane[1].σ, plane[2].σ))\n@printf(\"max |Δσ_l|: n = 20 %.1e, n = 30 %.1e, extrapolated %.1e (units of the mean)\\n\",\n        maximum(abs.(plane[1].σl .- reference.σl)), maximum(abs.(plane[2].σl .- reference.σl)),\n        maximum(abs.(richardson.(plane[1].σl, plane[2].σl) .- reference.σl)))\n@printf(\"incident intensity I instead of I cos ψ: σ₁ = %.2f%%\\n\", 100incident.σ)\nplane-wave quadrature: σ₁ = 7.8674% (m = 0 6.9365%, m ≠ 0 3.7123%); Briareus extrapolated 7.8669%\nmax |Δσ_l|: n = 20 7.3e-04, n = 30 3.3e-04, extrapolated 4.0e-06 (units of the mean)\nincident intensity I instead of I cos ψ: σ₁ = 14.25%\n\nBriareus's spectrum converges to the quadrature at second order in the element size (the error falls by 2.2 from n = 20 to 30, against (30/20)² = 2.25), and the extrapolated σ₁ agrees to 6 parts in 10⁵ of itself. The incident intensity, 14%, is far from Ba24's 6.03%: σ₁ is the projected (absorbed) intensity, as the hard-sphere deposition computes it.","title":"An independent check"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#Which-azimuth-reading","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"Ba24's method text describes the pointing differently from the caption. It measures the pointing relative to the point of the sphere nearest the port of the quad, as an angle away from that point and a direction about it. Their code, Polaris, rotates the surface point of the port by the angle and then about the port axis by the offset; the rotation is defined for the lower hemisphere and mirrored to the upper. Read that way, the 85.62° of Table I for the 50° cone would aim those beams 85.6° from their ports, near the edge of the sphere as seen from those ports.\n\nfunction about_port(q)\n    θp, φp = port_angles(q)\n    U = q.port\n    eθ, eφ = Briareus.port_basis(U)\n    toward_equator = U[3] > 0 ? eθ : -eθ\n    θo, ψ = oc[q.group][1], oc[q.group][2]\n    d = -cosd(ψ) * toward_equator + sind(ψ) * eφ     # Polaris's lower-hemisphere frame, mirrored\n    return R * (cosd(θo) * U + sind(θo) * d)\nend\naims_b = Dict(q.quad => about_port(q) for q in quads)\nreading_b = (; native = modes(meshes[2], ops[2].native, oc_beams(; aims = aims_b)),\n             plane = modes(meshes[2], ops[2].plane, oc_beams(; aims = aims_b, spot = grown, defocus = q -> 0.0)))\n@printf(\"about the port: σ₁ = %.0f%% (Briareus beams), %.0f%% (Ba24 emulated), n = 30\\n\",\n        100reading_b.native.σ, 100reading_b.plane.σ)\nabout the port: σ₁ = 150% (Briareus beams), 145% (Ba24 emulated), n = 30\n\nThe outer beams then graze the sphere and σ₁ exceeds 100%. Two variants of that reading (Polaris's other hemisphere convention, or the direction counted from the toward-equator side) give 145% and 30% with the emulated spots (research notes). The reading of the caption, with the polar angle from the pole and the azimuth offset from the port, is the only one within reach of 6.03%. It places the four cones where a PDD design places them: near the pole, at mid-latitude and near the equator. It is therefore the published design, and it is the reading that :theta_s and :dphi_s implement. Reversing the sense of the offset in the lower hemisphere moves σ₁ by 0.3 points, all of it in the m ≠ 0 part, so σ₁ does not settle that detail; Ba24's statement that the offsets turn the same way in both hemispheres settles it.","title":"Which azimuth reading"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#How-much-the-spots-matter","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"Ba24's best-focus spots are not published, so the comparison rests on how far σ₁ moves when the spots change within what could reasonably be substituted. Below, all the emulated spots are scaled by ±10%, or their orders are replaced by a common one with the same 1/e radii:\n\nfor s in (0.9, 1.1)\n    r = modes(meshes[2], ops[2].plane, oc_beams(; spot = b -> (g = grown(b); with_axes(g, s * g.a, s * g.b)),\n                                                defocus = q -> 0.0))\n    @printf(\"spots × %.1f: σ₁ = %.2f%% (m = 0 %.2f%%, m ≠ 0 %.2f%%)\\n\", s, 100r.σ, 100r.zero, 100r.nonzero)\nend\nfor n in (3.0, 4.0)\n    r = modes(meshes[2], ops[2].plane, oc_beams(; spot = b -> (g = grown(b);\n                                                                SuperGaussianSpot(g.a, g.b; order = n, angle = g.angle)),\n                                                defocus = q -> 0.0))\n    @printf(\"order %.0f: σ₁ = %.2f%% (m = 0 %.2f%%, m ≠ 0 %.2f%%)\\n\", n, 100r.σ, 100r.zero, 100r.nonzero)\nend\nspots × 0.9: σ₁ = 8.72% (m = 0 6.69%, m ≠ 0 5.59%)\nspots × 1.1: σ₁ = 9.23% (m = 0 8.89%, m ≠ 0 2.50%)\norder 3: σ₁ = 5.99% (m = 0 5.83%, m ≠ 0 1.39%)\norder 4: σ₁ = 7.17% (m = 0 6.61%, m ≠ 0 2.79%)\n\nTen percent on the spot size raises σ₁ by 0.9 to 1.4 points either way: the LMF spot size is near the minimum of σ₁ for the OC, which suggests Ba24's spots are of about that size. The steepness of the profile matters more: order-3 spots with the same 1/e radii bring the emulation to Ba24's value. The OC was optimized for the Ba24 spots, so their σ₁ is at an optimum that other spots need not share; the 6.6–7.9% of Briareus is within the spread the unpublished spots allow.\n\nThe convention of absorption matters more still. With the thin-corona absorption weight eta_a(psi) = 1 - (1 - eta_perp)^cos^3psi of the absorption keyword of deposit (Murakami and Nishi 2017; η⊥ = 0.9 is their value and does not come from Ba24), the oblique light near the equator counts for less:\n\nηa = modes(meshes[2], ops[2].native, beams; absorption = 0.9)\n@printf(\"absorbed with η⊥ = 0.9: σ₁ = %.2f%%, absorbed fraction %.3f\\n\", 100ηa.σ, ηa.absorbed)\nabsorbed with η⊥ = 0.9: σ₁ = 14.36%, absorbed fraction 0.620\n\nσ₁ more than doubles: the OC drives the equator with grazing light, which the weight discounts. Ba24's σ₁ is the vacuum intensity without such a weight (and the thin-corona weight itself underestimates grazing absorption in real coronas; research notes, \"Corona model prototype\").","title":"How much the spots matter"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#The-illumination","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"The two models are shown side by side on equal-area maps, with the field relative to its mean and the north pole at the top, together with their spectra against the plane-wave quadrature. The discrete spots of the outer cones near the equator are the m ≠ 0 part; the f-cone blur of the Briareus defocus smooths them more than the linear growth of Ba24 does.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 640))\nfor (j, (title, r)) in enumerate(((\"Briareus beams (f-cone defocus)\", native[2]),\n                                 (\"Ba24 spots emulated (plane waves)\", plane[2])))\n    ax = sphere_axis(fig[1, j]; title)\n    hm = sphere_heatmap!(ax, sphere_map(target, r.field ./ (sum(r.field .* target.elem_area) / sum(target.elem_area)),\n                                        :capsule); colormap = :inferno, colorrange = (0.7, 1.3))\n    j == 2 && Colorbar(fig[1, 3], hm; label = \"I cos ψ / mean\")\nend\nax = Axis(fig[2, 1:3]; xlabel = \"degree l\", ylabel = \"σ_l (% of the mean)\", yscale = log10,\n          xticks = 0:2:30, limits = (0.5, 30.5, 1e-2, 10))\nscatterlines!(ax, 1:L, 100 .* max.(native[2].σl[2:end], 1e-4); label = \"Briareus beams\")\nscatterlines!(ax, 1:L, 100 .* max.(plane[2].σl[2:end], 1e-4); label = \"Ba24 emulated\")\nscatter!(ax, 1:L, 100 .* max.(reference.σl[2:end], 1e-4); marker = :xcross, color = :black,\n         label = \"plane-wave quadrature (emulated)\")\naxislegend(ax; position = :rt)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe zonal part of the odd degrees vanishes: for every cone, the polar angles and powers are mirrored about the equator. The odd degrees that remain (l = 9, 11, 13 near 1%) are m ≠ 0. NIF's lower ports are the upper ones turned about a horizontal axis, and they are not mirrored, so the spots of the two hemispheres are not at the same azimuths.","title":"The illumination"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#Optimizing-from-the-OC","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"The OC does not minimize σ₁ by itself: Ba24's fitness also rewards a uniform ablation pressure in a 4-ns plasma with CBET and a high mean pressure, which Briareus cannot yet compute. The example examples/ba24_pdd_optimization.jl minimizes only σ₁ over Ba24's 16 parameters and bounds (θ_s, Δφ_s, defocus 0–10 mm, power 2–4 TW/quad per cone), with the Briareus beams on the n = 20 sphere. L-BFGS-B with ForwardDiff gradients through deposit (one gradient costs about four evaluations) takes σ₁ from 6.49% to 2.61% in 50 evaluations and to 2.11% in 229 (four minutes on 8 threads). Nelder–Mead needs about 700 evaluations to reach 2.1% and a particle swarm reaches 2.5% in 1 200; four random starts reach 2.4–5.3% in 75 evaluations each, so σ₁ has several optima. Ba24 report that their genetic algorithm with coordinate descent needs more than 10 000 evaluations to converge with σ₁ as the only objective. The research notes have the table (\"Ba24 σ₁ comparison and optimization\").","title":"Optimizing from the OC"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#Briareus-against-Ba24","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"quantity Ba24 Briareus beams Ba24 spots emulated likely cause of the difference\nσ₁ (l ≤ 30) 6.03% 6.57% 7.87% best-focus spots (Ifriit internal vs LMF fits): ±10% in size adds ~1 point, order 3 gives 6.0%\nσ₁, m = 0 n/a 5.27% 6.94% the cone balance at the poles and equator, through the spot widths\nσ₁, m ≠ 0 n/a 3.93% 3.71% discrete quads near the equator\ndominant modes Fig. 2a (figure only) l = 8, 6, 4 l = 4, 10, 6 defocus convention: f-cone blur vs linear radius growth\nabsorbed fraction 77% (3-D, with plasma and CBET) 93.6% (vacuum, light meeting the sphere) 92.4% no plasma, no CBET here\n\nThe Briareus values are extrapolated from n = 20 and 30. With σ₁ within 0.5 points (Briareus beams) and 1.8 points (emulated) and the measured spot sensitivity larger than both, the comparison supports reading Table I as the caption states, and the hard-sphere deposition of Briareus. It cannot calibrate either model and is not used to.","title":"Briareus against Ba24"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/polar_direct_drive/#What-this-checks","page":"Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing","text":"Ba24's Table I, read as the caption states with the polar angle from the pole and the azimuth offset from that of the port, gives σ₁ = 6.57% with the Briareus beams and 7.87% with Ba24's defocus emulated, against their 6.03%. Read about the port, as their method text and code describe the search parameters, it gives 30–150%. The reading of the caption is the published design; GroupPointing's :theta_s/:dphi_s implement it.\nOn the emulated (collimated) beams, Briareus's σ_l (l ≤ 30) matches an independent plane-wave quadrature to 3.3e-4 of the mean at n = 30 and to 4e-6 extrapolated, at second order in the element size, as on the analytic cases of the test suite (Skupsky–Lee, Temporal et al. 2014, Murakami and Nishi 2017); σ₁ to 6e-5 relative.\nσ₁ is the projected intensity Icospsi: the incident intensity gives 14.2%, and the thin-corona absorption weight at η⊥ = 0.9 gives 14.4%.\nThe difference from Ba24 is within the spot-model uncertainty: ±10% in spot size adds about one point to σ₁, and order-3 spots give 6.0%. Ba24's numbers are a comparison, never a fit target (decision 1 of the polar-direct-drive review).\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/deposition/#Laser-deposition","page":"Laser deposition","text":"Two methods deposit laser power on a target. DepositionOperator with deposit is a precomputed quadrature over the f-cone directions with per-direction visibility, refined where each beam can land: each surface point receives, from each cone direction that reaches it unobstructed, the focal profile where that ray crosses the best-focus plane, weighted by the incidence angle of that ray. The result is smooth in pointing, defocus, spot size and power, so ForwardDiff gradients flow through it; 48 quads on a 3,000-element hohlraum take about 45 ms on one thread, and the power of each beam is conserved to 1e-4. raytrace_deposition, an energy-conserving geometric ray trace, is the reference.\n\nPower that clips the outside of a target, misses it or strikes the capsule is accounted for separately. The operator samples the outside of a target only for its total, with Gauss rules graded toward the entrance-hole rims, where clipped light concentrates; clipped power agrees with converged ray traces to 1% from 1e-4 to 5% of a beam. render draws orthographic images of any per-element field from any direction or port, and line_of_sight_power gives the x-ray power toward a distant detector.","title":"Laser deposition"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.Deposition","page":"Laser deposition","text":"Deposition\n\nThe absorbed laser power, with fields:\n\nelement (TW), the power absorbed by each element;\nbackside (TW), the power on the unmodeled back side of each component, such as the outside of a hohlraum, which receives the LEH clipping;\nper_beam (TW), the total of each beam deposited on any opaque surface.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Deposition"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.DepositionOperator","page":"Laser deposition","text":"DepositionOperator(target, beams; level = 1, ncone = 13, resolution = 0.3,\n                   reach = 2.0, maxlevel = 5, shadow = 0,\n                   backside_resolution = 1.0, edge_resolution = 0.01,\n                   profile = nothing)\n\nPrecomputed laser-deposition quadrature. For each beam, every opaque triangle side that faces the beam and belongs to an element is sampled at 4^level points. More points are used where the beam can land:\n\nA triangle within reach (mm) of the beam footprint is divided until its sub-triangles are no longer than resolution times the smaller semi-axis of the spot, up to 4^maxlevel points. The footprint is 2.5 spot semi-axes, grown by the f-cone along the beam axis through the aim.\nWith shadow > 0, such a triangle is divided shadow times more where a shadow edge crosses it, so that its points see different cone directions.\n\nreach is the aim motion over which the operator stays accurate. Beyond it, deposition is resolved only at level. The defaults conserve the power of each beam to 1e-4 when nothing clips and to 2e-3 when an entrance-hole lip clips several percent of it, on fine and coarse hohlraum meshes alike.\n\nUnmodeled back sides, such as the outside of a hohlraum, carry the clipped backside loss. Only their total per component is needed, and the open edges that the light passes determine it. When an entrance-hole lip clips 1e-4 of a beam, the clipped light falls within ~1/50 of a spot semi-axis of the rim. Back sides away from the beam are sampled at one point per triangle. Within reach they are sampled as follows:\n\nA triangle on an open edge, or touching one at a vertex, gets a Gauss rule graded geometrically toward the edge, from resolution down to edge_resolution spot semi-axes. An open edge is an edge that no other opaque triangle shares, such as an entrance-hole rim.\nA triangle within one larger spot semi-axis of an open edge, where the shoulder of a clipped spot is deposited, is divided down to resolution.\nThe rest are divided down to backside_resolution.\n\nOn the NIF hohlraum, the clipped power then matches ray traces, averaged over nspot 241–361, to 1% from 1e-4 to 5% of a beam, 2.5% at 1e-6, and 0.6% up to 50%. It is also smooth in the aim. Element values do not depend on these two keywords.\n\nEach point stores its area weight, the normal of the lit side and a bitmask of which of the ncone f-cone directions reach it from the lens without hitting another surface. These depend only on the target and the beam directions, so the operator stays valid as pointing, defocus, spot size and power change. Deposition is then a smooth function of those parameters (see deposit).\n\nThe beam is the superposition of ncone collimated sub-beams, one per f-cone direction dⱼ (cone_directions), each carrying the best-focus profile. A point receives from each visible direction the focal profile where the ray along dⱼ through it crosses the best-focus plane, times the incidence factor of that ray |n·dⱼ|/(k·dⱼ). This is the geometric-optics convolution of the focal spot with the f-cone, sampled at the cone directions as the reference ray trace samples it. The power on any surface that intercepts the whole beam is therefore the beam power, at any distance from focus and any incidence. Shadows cast by objects near focus, such as capsules at TCC and LEH edges, are correlated with the part of the spot that they block.\n\nprofile = VisRadProfile(...) replaces this with VisRad's beam model, so that a design can be checked in VisRad and its deposition reproduced. The model is a super-Gaussian whose semi-axes and exponent grow away from focus and which blends toward a square. It is cut off at 10⁻³ of its local peak, or tapered to the cut for optimization with VisRadProfile(; taper). Points are then sampled only along the beam axis, ncone is ignored, and the beam axis through each point determines its shadowing. The two models agree near focus. Away from focus the f-cone convolution softens the edges, while VisRad's spot turns square and grows faster; on NIF hohlraum walls the peaks of the f-cone model are 10% higher than VisRad's. Power on a surface that intercepts the whole beam equals the beam power in planes normal to the beam and only approximately on tilted ones. This follows from the per-plane normalization, which is 0.2% high at 40° incidence and 2.2% at 78°, where VisRad's totals are exact.\n\nThe beam ports must not change after construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DepositionOperator"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.component_power-Tuple{Deposition, Target}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"component_power(dep, target; by = :part) -> Dict{Symbol,Float64}\n\nReturns the absorbed laser power (TW) per part, plus back-side (outside) losses keyed as Symbol(name, \"_outside\") and :missed for power that strikes nothing. The pieces of a part split for computation (curved_components) are summed into the part; by = :component keys by component instead. The two agree on targets of unsplit parts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.component_power"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.deposit-Tuple{DepositionOperator, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"deposit(op, beams; absorption = nothing) -> Deposition\n\nEvaluates laser deposition for beams, with the same ports and order as at construction. The result is differentiable in the aim, power, defocus and spot axes of every beam. Power that does not strike an opaque surface, for instance power that passes through a hohlraum and out the other LEH, is delivered - sum(per_beam). Threads share the work dynamically, and every sum is taken in a fixed order: the result is identical on any number of threads.\n\nBy default all the power of every ray is deposited where the ray strikes. With absorption = η⊥ (0 < η⊥ ≤ 1), only the fraction\n\nη_a(ψ) = 1 - (1 - η⊥)^(cos³ψ)\n\nof each ray is kept, with ψ its angle of incidence. That angle is the angle of each f-cone direction, or of the beam axis with a VisRad profile.\n\nThis is inverse-bremsstrahlung absorption in a thin corona whose density falls exponentially with radius. The absorption at normal incidence is η⊥ = 1 - exp(-8ν_ei L/3c), with ν_ei the collision frequency at critical density and L the scale length (Murakami & Nishi, Matter Radiat. Extremes 2, 55 (2017), Eq. 29, after Kruer's text). It is a model for hard-sphere direct-drive illumination, derived for that profile, and it is neither measured nor calibrated. η⊥ therefore has no default and is the user's estimate.\n\nThe model holds only for thin coronas, with scale length L ≪ the critical radius r_c. Against ray tracing through a spherical exponential corona, the absorption of an oblique ray approaches η_a only as Lr_c → 0. At sin ψ = 0.9 it is 4% off at Lr_c = 0.0025 and 13% off at 0.01, and at 0.1 it is 0.33 against η_a = 0.18. Rays that miss the critical sphere also still absorb. On a 40-beam OMEGA PDD pointing with Lr_c = 0.2, η_a on a hard sphere at r_c gives σ = 5.7% (l ≤ 30), where the corona gives 10.4% (research notes, \"Corona model prototype: impact-parameter kernels vs 3-D ray tracing\"). Polar direct drive gets its equatorial drive from exactly those grazing rays.\n\nThe unabsorbed light is dropped, and no reflection is followed. element, backside and per_beam are therefore absorbed powers, and delivered - sum(per_beam) includes the unabsorbed light. η⊥ may be a dual number, and the result is then differentiable in it too. η⊥ = 1 gives the model of the default (η_a  1) and equals it to rounding; the default itself is unchanged by this option.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.deposit"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.laser_flux-Tuple{Deposition, Target}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"laser_flux(dep, target) -> Vector\n\nReturns the absorbed laser intensity per element (TW/mm²; multiply by 1e14 for W/cm²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.laser_flux"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.raytrace_component_power-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"raytrace_component_power(target, beams; nspot = 41, ncone = 13, extent = nothing) -> Matrix\n\nReturns the power (TW) that each beam (rows) deposits on each component (columns, both sides), from the reference ray trace. The power accounting is exact and independent of mesh resolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.raytrace_component_power"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.raytrace_deposition-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"raytrace_deposition(target, beams; nspot = 41, ncone = 13, extent = nothing) -> Deposition\n\nComputes the reference deposition by geometric ray tracing. Each spot component (core, and halo if present) is sampled with an nspot × nspot grid of best-focus points spanning ±extent 1/e semi-axes (by default out to 1e-12 of the component peak), each launched along ncone f-cone directions and weighted by the focal intensity. Rays travel straight (no refraction) to the first opaque surface. Power is conserved exactly, and defocus is exact within the sampling. The result is not differentiable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.raytrace_deposition"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.CoronaOperator","page":"Laser deposition","text":"CoronaOperator(profile, beams; center = (0, 0, 0), lmax = 30, ncone = 13,\n               spacing = 0.1 * smallest spot semi-axis, panels = 200, shells = nothing)\n\nPrecomputed laser absorption in the static, spherically symmetric corona profile (CoronaProfile) about center, for polar and symmetric direct drive on a bare sphere: deposit(op, beams) gives the absorbed power per steradian on the sphere about center as real spherical harmonics up to degree lmax, the primary output of direct-drive designs (reference/litreview/polar_direct_drive.md, decision 4).\n\nEach beam is ncone collimated sub-beams along its f-cone directions (cone_directions), each carrying the best-focus profile, as in DepositionOperator. Their rays are refracted by the corona (cold-plasma geometric optics, n^2 = 1 - n_en_c) and absorbed by inverse bremsstrahlung along their paths (Kruer, The Physics of Laser Plasma Interactions, ch. 5); light that is not absorbed leaves the corona. Absorbed power is assigned to the direction from center of the point where it is absorbed (projected radially). In a spherical corona every ray stays in its plane and is fixed by its impact parameter, so the paths are integrated once, by radial quadrature (panels per pass), into harmonic kernels at the impact parameters of a square lattice of rays (spacing, mm) across each sub-beam, out to the outer radius of the profile. These depend on the profile, the lattice and the ports only, so one operator is valid for every aim, defocus, spot and power; the beam ports and f-numbers must not change after construction.\n\nshells, increasing radii (mm) from at most the first radius of the profile to at least its outer radius, resolves the absorption in radius as well: deposit then also gives the absorbed power per steradian in each shell shells[s] ≤ r < shells[s+1] and the absorbed power per shell (a radial deposition profile), which sum to the totals. Each shell edge is then also a panel boundary of every ray, so every panel lies in one shell and the partition is exact; the totals then come from these refined panels (on an OMEGA case the absorbed power equals that of the operator without shells within round-off, and the harmonics differ by 1e-7 to 1e-6 of the mean, from the angular placement of the panels). The harmonics of a shell cost as much as those of the totals in each evaluation, so the evaluation time grows in proportion to the number of shells. ablation_pressure_proxy sums the shells with a density weight.\n\nScope and limits:\n\nSpherical corona only. A non-spherical corona is outside the model: a P₂ of 4% in the critical radius moves σ₂ by about 3% of its value and the absorption by 1 point (measured by 3-D ray tracing; research notes, \"Corona model prototype\").\nBare sphere only. Nothing but the corona interacts with the light: no other target parts, stalks or shields, which would need separate shadowing; the profile must reach n_c (CoronaProfile), so no light reaches the surface inside.\nNo cross-beam energy transfer (decision 8 of the review; deferred). The results are CBET-free, and optimistic where CBET matters (the PDD equator).\n\ndeposit(op, beams; absorption = η⊥) on a hard sphere is the limit of this model for a thin corona; oblique and grazing rays depart from it at finite scale lengths (research notes, \"Corona model prototype\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CoronaOperator"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.CoronaProfile","page":"Laser deposition","text":"CoronaProfile(r, ne_over_nc, Te; Z, lnΛ, wavelength = 0.351)\nCoronaProfile(; critical_radius, scale_length, absorption,\n              outer_radius = critical_radius + 14scale_length)\n\nA static, spherically symmetric laser corona for CoronaOperator. It is given by the electron density as a fraction n_en_c of the critical density of the light (not in cm⁻³) and by the inverse-bremsstrahlung absorption rate, both as functions of the radius from the target center.\n\nThe first form tabulates a snapshot, such as a 1-D hydrodynamics output: radii r (mm, increasing, not necessarily uniform), ne_over_nc (n_en_c, positive), electron temperature Te (eV), and the mean ionization Z and Coulomb logarithm lnΛ (each a number or a vector over r; no defaults). wavelength is the vacuum laser wavelength (µm), which sets n_c. The absorption rate per unit length of a ray, divided by its refractive index, is (ν_ei/c)(n_e/n_c) with ν_ei the electron–ion collision frequency of Kruer (The Physics of Laser Plasma Interactions, ch. 5), 2.91e-6 Z n_e lnΛ / T_e^{3/2} s⁻¹ (n_e in cm⁻³, T_e in eV). Both ln(n_e/n_c) and the logarithm of that rate are interpolated by cubic Hermite polynomials (three-point slopes), so an exponential profile is represented exactly; the profile is zero beyond r[end]. ne_over_nc[1] ≥ 1 is required: every ray must turn before the innermost radius (the light never reaches a hard surface, whose absorption the corona model does not include).\n\nThe second form is the analytic isothermal corona n_e/n_c = exp(-(r - r_c)/L) with critical_radius r_c and scale_length L (mm), truncated at outer_radius, whose absorption rate is set by absorption, the fraction η⊥ that a planar profile of the same scale length absorbs at normal incidence: η⊥ = 1 - exp(-8ν_c L/3c) (Kruer; the η⊥ of deposit(op, beams; absorption), Murakami & Nishi 2017, Eq. 29). It is also the exact absorption of a radial ray when the truncation is far out (e⁻¹⁴ of n_c by default).\n\nThe model is not calibrated: the profile is a user input (a snapshot or an engineering estimate), and Z, lnΛ and absorption have no defaults.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CoronaProfile"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.ablation_pressure_proxy-Tuple{CoronaOperator, Any}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"ablation_pressure_proxy(op::CoronaOperator, dep; lmax = op.lmax, exponent = 2/3,\n                        density_exponent = 2/3) -> Vector\n\nThe ablation-pressure proxy of Barlow et al. (PRL 133, 175101, 2024, Eqs. 2a–2b of the published Letter's appendix; Eqs. 1a–1b of arXiv:2311.18342v2) from a corona deposition dep = deposit(op, beams) of an operator with shells. The result is the flat real-harmonic coefficients (mode_index order, l ≤ lmax) of the pressure map (Mbar)\n\nP_abl(Ω) = 247mathrmMbar (F(Ω)  10^14mathrmWcm^-2)^23, F(Ω) = r_c^-2 Σ_s (n_sn_c)^23 dP_sdΩ,\n\nwith dP_sdΩ the absorbed power per steradian in shell s (dep.shell_ylm), n_s the electron density of the profile at the middle of the part of the shell inside it, and r_c the critical radius of the profile (outermost n_e = n_c). Ba24 write the sum over their hydrodynamic cells, Σ_r r^2 n_r^23 I_r  (R_351^2 n_351^23) with r^2 I_r the absorbed power per steradian of a cell, so with the cell edges of a snapshot as shells this is their sum; finer shells converge to the radial integral, with an error of second order in the shell width (on an OMEGA case, shells of an eighth of the density scale length L out to 4L past the critical radius put σ₂ within 0.1% of its converged value, a quarter of L within 1%; research notes). n_c is the critical density at the wavelength of the profile, Ba24's n_351 at 351 nm. exponent replaces the outer 2/3 and density_exponent the 2/3 of the density weight.\n\nThe angular mean, Ba24's bar P_abl, is c[1] / √(4π) and their σ_2 is mode_rms(c, 30). F is formed at the full degree op.lmax of the deposition, the map is formed on a Gauss–Legendre product grid for that degree, and the result is projected back to degree lmax. The result is therefore F^23 filtered to l ≤ lmax, as Ba24's σ₂ is; with a smaller lmax the leading coefficients of the full-degree result are returned. F must be positive everywhere (an ArgumentError otherwise).\n\nThe proxy is an engineering estimate and is not a facility criterion. The 2/3 power follows Manheimer et al. (Phys. Fluids 25, 1644, 1982; Ba24's Ref. 33). The density weight is empirical, chosen by Ba24 to match pressures and asymmetries of their 3-D radiation-hydrodynamics runs, and the prefactor 24.7 Mbar is Ba24's. The proxy is CBET-free, as the deposition is, and generic in the number type (for ForwardDiff through deposit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ablation_pressure_proxy"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.deposit-Tuple{CoronaOperator, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"deposit(op::CoronaOperator, beams) -> (; ylm, per_beam, delivered, shell_ylm, radial)\n\nLaser absorption in the corona of op for beams, which have the same ports and order as at construction. The result contains ylm, the flat real-harmonic coefficients (mode_index order, l ≤ op.lmax, polar axis ẑ, about the operator's center) of the absorbed power per steradian F(Ω) = Σ ylm[mode_index(l, m)] Y_lm(Ω) (TW/sr); per_beam, the absorbed power of each beam (TW); and delivered, the total power of the beams. The absorbed fraction is sum(per_beam) / delivered (equal to √(4π) ylm[1] / delivered), the nonuniformity mode_rms(ylm, lmax) and its spectrum mode_power(ylm_matrix(ylm, lmax)). Cross-beam energy transfer is not modeled, so the results are CBET-free.\n\nFor an operator with shells, shell_ylm[:, s] contains the same coefficients for the power absorbed in shell s (TW/sr; a (lmax + 1)² × nshells matrix, the absorbed power per steradian and radius) and radial[s] the power absorbed in it (TW, the radial deposition profile); sum(eachcol(shell_ylm)) ≈ ylm and sum(radial) ≈ sum(per_beam) within round-off, and √(4π) shell_ylm[1, s] ≈ radial[s]. Without shells both are empty.\n\nThe function is generic in the number type of the beams (aims, powers, defocus and spot axes may be ForwardDiff duals); threads share the beams and the sums are taken in a fixed order, so the result does not depend on the number of threads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.deposit"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.shell_displacement_proxy-Tuple{AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"shell_displacement_proxy(ylm, lmax; exponent = 0.72, mean_displacement = nothing) -> Vector\n\nThe azimuthal shell-displacement model of Yeamans et al. (Nucl. Fusion 61, 046031, 2021, Eq. 3) from the absorbed power per steradian ylm (flat real-harmonic coefficients, mode_index order, polar axis ẑ; e.g. deposit(op::CoronaOperator, beams).ylm). The result is the coefficients (l ≤ lmax) of\n\nd(θ φ) = bar d(θ) (E(θ φ)  bar E(θ))^072,\n\nwith E the deposited energy per steradian (in a static deposition proportional to the absorbed power per steradian), bar E(θ) its mean over φ at each polar angle θ, and bar d(θ) the mean distance the shell has moved at θ, mean_displacement(θ) (θ in degrees) or 1 when nothing. Ye21 take bar d(θ) from a 2-D (axisymmetric) hydrodynamics run; with bar d = 1 the m ≠ 0 part, mode_rms(d, lmax; m = :nonzero), is the azimuthal nonuniformity of the shell velocity (their ΔV_m0, which adds in quadrature to the ΔV_m=0 of the 2-D run). exponent replaces 0.72.\n\nEq. 3 as published sets the factor 0.72 beside Ebar E without marking it as an exponent; the text defines it as the power with which the shell velocity scales with laser energy, which this function follows. The linear reading bar d(1 + 072 (Ebar E - 1)) agrees with it to first order in Ebar E - 1, and Ye21's statement that their velocity modes are 0.72 times those of the energy holds exactly for that reading and to first order for this one (research notes, \"Corona deposition per radius and drive proxies\"). E is the field of all of ylm (its full degree; length(ylm) must be a square), the map is formed on a Gauss–Legendre product grid for that degree, and the result is projected back to degree lmax, so it is d filtered to l ≤ lmax; with a smaller lmax the leading coefficients of the full-degree result are returned. E must be positive everywhere (an ArgumentError otherwise).\n\nThe proxy is an engineering estimate and is not a facility criterion. The 0.72 power comes from implosion simulations (Ye21's Ref. 47, an LLE report) and is close to an experimental 0.71 that Ye21 cite. The function is generic in the number type of ylm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.shell_displacement_proxy"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.OrthoCamera","page":"Laser deposition","text":"OrthoCamera(direction; up = ẑ, center = 0, width, height = width, nx = 256, ny = nx)\n\nOrthographic camera looking along direction (from the camera toward the scene) at center, imaging a width × height mm field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.OrthoCamera"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.line_of_sight_power-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"line_of_sight_power(target, B, direction; elems = all, nsub = 4, refine = 8) -> Real\n\nX-ray power per steradian (TW/sr) radiated toward a distant detector in direction (unit vector from the target toward the detector) by the given elements with radiosity B (TW/mm²), each counted with its unobstructed fraction fᵢ: Σ Bᵢ/π · fᵢ Aᵢ cosθᵢ.\n\nfᵢ is the area-weighted fraction of points on the element from which a ray toward the detector does not meet an opaque surface; apertures are transparent. The points are the centroids of the nsub² equal sub-triangles of each of its triangles. On an element whose first points give different results, such as an element partly hidden behind an edge, (refine·nsub)² points are used. The error is first order in the sub-triangle size, on partly hidden elements only. An occluder that misses every first point of an element is not detected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.line_of_sight_power"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.radiance-Tuple{RadiationSolution}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"radiance(sol) -> Vector\n\nLambertian x-ray radiance B/π per element (TW mm⁻² sr⁻¹).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.radiance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/deposition/#Briareus.render-Tuple{Target, AbstractVector, OrthoCamera}","page":"Laser deposition","text":"render(target, values, camera; background = NaN) -> Matrix\n\nImage of a per-element field. Each pixel shows values[e] for the element first met along its line of sight, or background where there is none; apertures are transparent. Row 1 is the top of the image. Pass radiance(sol) for an x-ray image or laser_flux(dep, target) for a deposition map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.render"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/background/#Physics-background","page":"Physics background","text":"This page collects the physics a Briareus calculation rests on, at the level needed to set one up and to judge its results. It is written for a reader who knows electromagnetism and thermal radiation but not inertial-confinement fusion. Each section ends with what Briareus computes for that piece of physics and where the tutorial and the reference documentation for it are. The tutorials derive and check the formulas used here; the reference texts at the end treat each subject in full.","title":"Physics background"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/background/#Indirect-drive-and-the-hohlraum","page":"Physics background","text":"In indirect drive, a fuel capsule is imploded by x rays, and the laser light does not reach it directly. The capsule sits at the center of a hohlraum, a high-Z cylinder (gold, or depleted uranium lined with gold) a few millimeters across. Laser beams enter through a laser entrance hole (LEH) at each end and strike the inner wall. The laser-heated plasma of the wall converts the absorbed light into soft x rays, with a conversion efficiency of roughly 0.7–0.95 depending on intensity and time (research notes, \"Literature review against Kritcher et al. 2024\"). The x rays fill the cavity, are partly absorbed by the wall and partly re-emitted, and bathe the capsule. The ablator of the capsule absorbs them, ablates outward, and the reaction drives the shell and fuel inward. NIF's ignition-era hohlraums reach radiation temperatures near 300 eV: Dante measured about 307–313 eV on the HYBRID-E shots (Abu-Shawareb et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 132, 065102 (2024), Table I).\n\nThe cavity is what makes the drive uniform. The wall is seen from the capsule at a distance comparable to the hohlraum radius, so each point of the capsule receives flux from a wide patch of wall, and the fine structure of the laser spots averages out. What remains are the low-order variations set by where on the wall the laser power lands, which the beam layout controls. The price is efficiency: most of the x-ray energy is absorbed by the wall or escapes through the LEHs, and the rest reaches the capsule (Lindl 1995; Lindl et al. 2004, Sec. IV).\n\nNIF's 192 beams are grouped into 48 quads of four. The indirect-drive ports lie on four cones in each hemisphere, at polar angles of 23.5° and 30° (the inner cones) and 44.5° and 50° (the outer cones). Outer-cone beams strike the wall near the LEHs; inner-cone beams cross the hohlraum at a shallower angle and strike it near the waist. The share of the laser power carried by the inner cones, the cone fraction, is the main control of the pole-to-waist balance of the drive. The HYBRID-E designs run a peak cone fraction near 33% (research notes, \"Wavelength detuning (NIF)\").\n\nIn Briareus. A Target is a list of Components: meshed surfaces with a Material (albedo and conversion efficiency) and, for curved walls, the exact surface the mesh approximates. LEHs are apertures, transparent elements that record the flux escaping through them. hohlraum builds the standard cylinder with LEHs and capsule, and nif_quads and nif_beams give the NIF beams with their cones as group. The tutorial Freeing a bundle for radiography works through a NIF hohlraum; the reference pages are Geometry and targets and Beams and facilities.","title":"Indirect drive and the hohlraum"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/background/#Laser-spots","page":"Physics background","text":"A high-power laser beam focused by a lens alone has a spot shaped by the aberrations of the beam. A phase plate before the lens breaks the beam into many small, mutually phased elements, each of which illuminates the whole focal region. Averaged over the resulting speckle, the spot is smooth, with a shape set by the plate: elliptical and flat-topped for NIF's continuous phase plates (CPPs), round for most of OMEGA's distributed phase plates (DPPs). A super-Gaussian of order n with semi-axes a and b describes these spots well,\n\nI(u v) propto expleft-left(fracu^2a^2 + fracv^2b^2right)^n2right\n\nwhere u and v are transverse coordinates. Briareus quotes a and b at 1/e of the peak. Facility tables quote radii at other levels: VisRad's NIF CPP table gives half widths at half maximum, and a radius r_f at the fraction f of the peak converts as a = r_f(ln 1f)^1n.\n\nThe f-number F is the ratio of the focal length to the lens diameter; light converges onto the focus from a cone of half-angle arctan(12F). A NIF beam has F approx 20, and a quad of four beams acts as a beam of F approx 8. Away from best focus each point receives light from a disk of focal points, of radius s2F at a distance s along the beam, so in geometric optics the defocused spot is the focal profile convolved with that disk. Defocus moves best focus along the beam on purpose, enlarging the spot on the wall to lower its intensity. In Briareus and in VisRad a positive defocus moves the focus along the propagation direction.\n\nA beam striking a surface at incidence angle iota spreads its power over a footprint stretched by 1cosiota, so the absorbed power per unit area is the beam intensity times cosiota. In a hohlraum, inner-cone beams strike the wall at grazing incidence, and their footprints are long and faint.\n\nIn Briareus. A Beam has a port (which fixes its direction), an aim, a defocus, a power, an f-number and a SuperGaussianSpot. A DepositionOperator carries out the f-cone convolution by summing the focal profile over a quadrature of cone directions at points fixed on the target; it conserves the power of each beam and is differentiable in aim, defocus and power. By default each lit surface absorbs all the light that reaches it; no reflection is followed, and backscatter is a laser–plasma correction (below). raytrace_deposition is the reference. The tutorial Laser spot on a foil checks the power of the spot, its growth through focus and its footprint at oblique incidence against exact results; the reference page is Laser deposition.","title":"Laser spots"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/background/#View-factors-and-radiosity","page":"Physics background","text":"Hohlraum walls emit diffusely, with a radiance independent of direction (Lambertian emission), and Briareus treats the radiation as gray, a single energy group, unless a multigroup solve is requested. The exchange of diffuse radiation between surfaces is then purely geometric. The view factor F_ij is the fraction of the radiation leaving surface element i that arrives at element j:\n\nF_ij = frac1A_iint_A_iint_A_j fraccostheta_icostheta_jpi r^2\nV(mathbfx_i mathbfx_j) dA_j dA_i \n\nwhere theta_i and theta_j are the angles between the line joining the two points and the surface normals, r its length, and V is 1 where the two points see each other and 0 where another surface blocks the line. Two identities follow from the definition. Reciprocity, A_i F_ij = A_j F_ji, holds because the integrand is symmetric. Closure, sum_j F_ij = 1, is true in a closed enclosure, where all radiation leaving an element lands somewhere; a hohlraum is closed once its LEHs are counted as elements. A discretized view-factor matrix satisfies closure only to its quadrature error, and that error is a useful measure of its accuracy.\n\nThe radiosity B_i is the power per unit area leaving element i, and the incident flux H_i the power per unit area arriving there. An element that absorbs laser power P_i over area A_i, converts a fraction eta_i of it to x rays, and re-emits a fraction alpha_i (the albedo) of the x rays it receives obeys\n\nB_i = fraceta_i P_iA_i + alpha_i H_i qquad H_i = sum_j F_ij B_j \n\nThe two equations close into the linear system (I - operatornamediag(alpha)F)B = S for the source S_i = eta_i P_i  A_i. An LEH has alpha = eta = 0: the flux it receives is the flux that escapes. The radiation temperature of a flux is T_r = (Hsigma)^14.\n\nThe albedo of a hohlraum wall is not a surface property in the ordinary sense. X rays absorbed by the wall drive a radiative heat wave (a Marshak wave) into it, and the energy that wave stores is the wall loss; what the wall does not store, it re-emits. The loss falls as the wall heats and as time goes on, so the albedo rises through the pulse, to about 0.8–0.9 at peak drive for gold. Briareus's default for gold is Lindl's power-law fit, 1 - alpha = 032(T_r100 mathrmeV)^-07(t1 mathrmns)^-038 (Lindl et al. 2004, Sec. IV.A; Hammer and Rosen, Phys. Plasmas 10, 1829 (2003), for the self-similar wave).\n\nA sphere is the one cavity with a closed-form answer. Every point inside a sphere is seen from every other with the same kernel, so the incident flux is uniform wherever the source lies, and with a port taking a fraction f of its area,\n\nH = fraceta PAfrac11 - alpha(1 - f) \n\nThe factor 11 - alpha(1 - f) is the cavity multiplier: about 9 at alpha = 095 and f = 6. It also multiplies any radiation a discretization loses or creates, so a closure error of 10⁻³ per row matters at high albedo. At alpha = 0925, a 5 × 10⁻⁴ shift of the mean row sum of the wall moves the capsule drive of a NIF hohlraum by 0.3% and its P2 by about 0.05% (RadiationModel).\n\nThe flux on the capsule is linear in the laser deposition, as long as the albedos are fixed: H_textcapsule = RP for a response matrix R that depends on the geometry and the albedos alone. A pointing study that changes only where the laser power lands therefore needs R once. A temperature-dependent albedo makes the problem nonlinear, and the solve then iterates the albedos to self-consistency with the flux; R is the linearization about one albedo state.\n\nIn Briareus. view_factors computes occluded view factors with the exact point-to-polygon kernel near the emitter, on the exact curved surface where a component carries one: on a sphere, flat facets misstate the exchange between neighboring elements by an amount of order one, and the curved calculation is exact to round-off. closed = true removes the closure error while keeping reciprocity (enforce_closure!; closure_error reports it). solve solves the radiosity system, WallLossAlbedo is the Marshak wall loss, and flux_response is the matrix R. The tutorials Integrating sphere and Entrance-hole size and the power balance check these against the closed form for the sphere, the uniform-flux power balance and a Monte Carlo photon walk; the reference page is Radiation.","title":"View factors and radiosity"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/background/#Drive-symmetry","page":"Physics background","text":"A capsule converges to a small fraction of its initial radius, and a small variation of the drive around it grows into a large distortion of the compressed fuel. The tolerance is of order 1% in the time-integrated flux for the lowest modes (Lindl 1995; Lindl et al. 2004). Variations of the flux over the capsule are measured by its expansion about the capsule center. For a hohlraum with azimuthal symmetry, a Legendre expansion in the polar angle theta from the hohlraum axis suffices,\n\nH(theta) = sum_l a_l P_l(costheta) qquad\na_l = frac2l + 14piint HP_l(costheta)dOmega \n\nand the asymmetry of mode l is the ratio a_la_0, written P2/P0 for l = 2. Because P_2(pm 1) = 1 and P_2(0) = -12, a positive P2/P0 means the poles are hotter than the waist (pole-hot, an oblate implosion) and a negative one that the waist is hotter (waist-hot, a prolate implosion). Real spherical harmonics Y_lm extend the expansion to flux that varies in azimuth as well, such as the mode 1 left by a missing bundle or a power imbalance between hemispheres.\n\nThe geometry filters the modes. Each capsule point integrates the emission of the wall over a wide solid angle, which suppresses high orders; what reaches the capsule is dominated by l = 2 and l = 4, with P6 smaller still. A drive that is symmetric between the two hemispheres has no odd Legendre modes. The low modes have different consequences. Mode 1 pushes the hot spot sideways: about 100 km/s of hot-spot velocity per percent of mode-1 flux over the rise and peak (Casey et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 126, 025002 (2021); MacGowan et al., High Energy Density Phys. 40, 100944 (2021); Young et al., Phys. Plasmas 27, 082702 (2020)), a velocity that carries kinetic energy the hot spot no longer converts to heat. P2 and P4 distort the hot spot and shell, with the same loss of stagnation pressure and yield.\n\nPointing moves P2 because the power of each cone lands at a latitude of the wall as seen from the capsule. Raising the cone fraction puts more power near the waist and drives P2 negative; moving the aim of a ring along the axis moves its spots in latitude. With two rings per hemisphere, the cone fraction and the axial aims of the two rings are enough to null P2 and P4 together, as the first calculation on the home page does. On NIF, about 2.7 µm of hot-spot P2 follows from each percent of cone fraction (Callahan et al., Phys. Plasmas 25, 056305 (2018)).\n\nIn Briareus. legendre_moments and ylm_moments integrate the harmonics over the exact solid angle of each facet as seen from the capsule center (a centroid rule leaks P0 into P2 at the 0.5% level); mode_amplitude gives the rotation-invariant size of each degree, and flux_moments the moments of the capsule flux of a scenario. symmetry_objective and optimize_pointing null chosen modes at a held drive, and hotspot_velocity converts mode 1 to a velocity. The tutorials are Freeing a bundle for radiography (the port symmetry, mode 1, rebalancing), Pointing and power errors and From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics; the reference pages are Mode analysis, Optimization and Implosion metrics.","title":"Drive symmetry"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/background/#Time-dependence","page":"Physics background","text":"The laser pulse of a NIF hohlraum shot lasts several nanoseconds (5.5–14.1 ns over the 39 shots of Callahan et al. 2018). A low-power picket launches the first shock, the drive is held steady through a trough, and a rise to peak power sets the final implosion velocity. Each cone has a separate pulse, so the cone fraction varies through the shot. Light crosses a hohlraum in tens of picoseconds, so the radiation field follows the drive at each instant; the slow parts of the problem are the energy stored in the wall and the plasma in the hohlraum.\n\nThe hohlraum changes during the pulse in ways that move the drive symmetry:\n\nWall motion. The ablated wall plasma moves the x-ray emission surface inward, at about 50–100 µm/ns (research notes, \"Literature review against Kritcher et al. 2024\").\nLEH closure. Gold blown off the LEH rims shrinks the holes, lowering the loss through them and moving the outer-cone footprints.\nThe gold bubble. The picket of the outer cones blows off a bubble of high-Z plasma under their footprint. Its tip moves inward at 300–460 µm/ns, and it absorbs inner-cone light before that light reaches the waist, so the inner-cone deposition moves toward the LEHs and the drive turns pole-hot (Callahan et al. 2018). On N221204 the P2/P0 of the capsule flux swings from waist-hot to pole-hot during peak power (Kritcher et al., Phys. Rev. E 109, 025204 (2024)).\nCross-beam energy transfer (CBET). Where beams cross in the LEH plasma, the beat of two beams drives an ion-acoustic wave that scatters power from one into the other: from the higher frequency to the lower, in the frame of the flowing plasma. A wavelength detuning Δλ between cones sets the direction and size of the transfer. With Δλ = λ(inner) − λ(outer) positive, power moves from the outer cones to the inner ones; on NIF it is quoted at 1ω, three times its 3ω value. N221204 ran 2.75 Å at 1ω (research notes, \"Wavelength detuning (NIF)\"; Michel et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 102, 025004 (2009)).\nBackscatter. Stimulated Brillouin and Raman scattering return part of the power of a beam out of the LEH before it reaches the wall (Kruer 1988).\n\nWhat Briareus models, and what it does not. The drive is quasi-static: at each time the radiosity is solved for the deposition, wall albedos and geometry at that time. The loss of each wall element follows its temperature history. Wall motion, LEH closure, the bubble, CBET and backscatter are reduced models, calibrated to published simulations and measurements that the research notes record. Briareus does not solve hydrodynamics, trace refraction or store energy in the hohlraum plasma. After the laser turns off, the synthetic Dante therefore comes out low, by 37–54 eV at 9 ns on three shots (Verification and validation). The CBET model assumes a uniform LEH plasma and must be calibrated before its magnitudes are trusted. The implosion itself is outside the code: capsule_drive hands the flux history of the capsule to a hydrodynamics code.\n\nIn Briareus. PulseShape and with_pulses set powers in time, and drive_history runs the quasi-static history. WallMotion, LipClosure and GapClosure, BubbleAbsorption, CBET and TwoZoneCBET, and Backscatter are the reduced models; Beam.dlambda is the detuning at the (3ω) wavelength of the beam. dante1 records the drive as the Dante instrument does. The tutorial Retuning a drive to a reference shot runs a history; the reference pages are Radiation and Laser–plasma models, and examples/n221204.jl is the benchmark against N221204.","title":"Time dependence"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/background/#Further-reading","page":"Physics background","text":"J. D. Lindl, \"Development of the indirect-drive approach to inertial confinement fusion and the target physics basis for ignition and gain\", Phys. Plasmas 2, 3933 (1995). The physics basis of indirect drive: hohlraum energetics, symmetry and capsule stability.\nJ. D. Lindl et al., \"The physics basis for ignition using indirect-drive targets on the National Ignition Facility\", Phys. Plasmas 11, 339 (2004). The NIF-era update; Sec. IV covers hohlraum energetics and wall loss.\nS. Atzeni and J. Meyer-ter-Vehn, The Physics of Inertial Fusion (Oxford University Press, 2004). A textbook treatment of implosions, ignition and indirect drive.\nM. F. Modest, Radiative Heat Transfer, 3rd ed. (Academic Press, 2013), and J. R. Howell, M. P. Mengüç and R. Siegel, Thermal Radiation Heat Transfer, 6th ed. (CRC Press, 2015). View factors, their identities and the radiosity method.\nW. L. Kruer, The Physics of Laser Plasma Interactions (Addison-Wesley, 1988). Laser absorption, stimulated scattering and the ion-acoustic coupling behind CBET.\nJ. J. MacFarlane, \"VISRAD—A 3-D view factor code and design tool for high-intensity laser and particle beam target experiments\", J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 81, 287 (2003). The view-factor method as VisRad implements it; see also For VisRad users.\nA. L. Kritcher et al., Phys. Rev. E 109, 025204 (2024). The modeling chain behind N221204's symmetry tuning, and the benchmark data Briareus checks against.\n\nreference/research-notes.md in the repository gives the source and confidence of every number Briareus uses, and reference/litreview/ reviews the literature on hohlraum dynamics, CBET calibration, energetics and asymmetry transfer.","title":"Further reading"},{"category":"section","location":"#Briareus.jl","page":"Home","text":"Briareus computes laser deposition, view-factor radiation transport and drive symmetry for laser-driven high-energy-density experiments: indirect-drive hohlraums, backlighter foils and other laser-irradiated targets. It covers the calculations that a view-factor code such as VisRad is used for and exchanges files with VisRad. It adds gradient-based optimization of pointing, defocus, power balance and wavelength detuning; reduced laser–plasma corrections (backscatter, cross-beam energy transfer, inner-beam absorption); a time-dependent drive with a moving wall, closing entrance holes and a synthetic Dante; and shot-review checks of worst-case beam clearance, NIF's target-configuration rules and unconverted light.\n\npkg> add Briareus\n\nA first calculation evaluates a NIF hohlraum at an illustrative cone pointing (aim height z and radial offset r per cone, mm); the pointing is an example and was not fielded on a shot. The calculation then moves the inner and outer rings of cones along the axis and re-balances their power to null the P2 and P4 of the capsule flux at the same mean flux, with the beams kept clear of the entrance-hole rims and the capsule. Once the package has loaded, the scenario builds in a few seconds on a 16-thread desktop, and the optimization takes about 5 s on its first call (mostly compilation) and 1 s after:\n\nusing Briareus\n\ntarget = hohlraum(length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9)\ncones = GroupPointing(nif_quads(power = 8.0); fields = (:z, :r))\nquads = apply(cones, parameters(cones; inner23 = (z = 3.09, r = -1.09),\n                                inner30 = (z = 3.63, r = -0.60),\n                                outer44 = (z = 5.20,), outer50 = (z = 5.01,)))\n\nring(b) = b.group in (:inner23, :inner30) ? :inner : :outer\npointing = GroupPointing(quads; by = ring, fields = (:z, :power), relative = true)\nscenario = Scenario(target, pointing; observe = :capsule)\n\nx0 = initial_parameters(pointing)    # no aim shift, 8 TW per quad\nreport(evaluate(scenario, x0))       # 384 TW, Tr = 262.7 eV, P2/P0 = -15.737%\n\ndrive = mean_flux(evaluate(scenario, x0))    # mean capsule flux, held fixed\nkeepout = (ApertureKeepOut(target), SphereKeepOut(target))\nobjective = symmetry_objective(modes = (2 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0); drive, keepout)\nlower, upper = bounds(pointing, x0; power = 0.6)     # ±1 mm of aim, ±60% of power\nresult = optimize_pointing(scenario, objective; x0, lower, upper)  # 20 iterations\nreport(evaluate(scenario, result.x)) # 422.1 TW, P2/P0 = -0.000%, P4/P0 = +0.000%\n\nThe starting P2/P0 of −15.7% is expected. Equal power per quad puts 1/3 of it on the inner cones, near HYBRID-E's peak cone fraction, and a static solve without cross-beam energy transfer or the hohlraum dynamics is not balanced there. The N221204 benchmark has a static P2/P0 of −26% at 5.5 ns; the shot is balanced by wavelength-detuned transfer, wall motion, the gold bubble and closing entrance holes, which drive_history models. The optimum lowers the inner-cone fraction to 0.15 and stops where the 30° beams reach the entrance-hole keep-out.\n\nThe same workflow runs in a desktop window: see Desktop application.","title":"Briareus.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Start-here","page":"Home","text":"Evaluating Briareus against VisRad or a design workflow. For VisRad users maps VisRad's concepts and files to Briareus's, states where the two codes differ, and walks through porting a VisRad study. Verification and validation lists every check by kind (code verification, solution verification, validation, calibration), including the comparison of laser deposition with VisRad 21 and the N221204 benchmark against published data. Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing reproduces a published pointing against an independent quadrature. Retuning a drive to a reference shot restores the capsule drive of a reference shot through time after a bundle is lost. Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum takes tessellated CAD solids to a radiation model. VisRad interchange documents the workspace, pointing and power files read and written.\n\nLearning the physics with the code. Physics background introduces indirect drive, laser spots, view factors, drive symmetry and the time dependence of the hohlraum, with pointers into the code and further reading. The tutorials build up in order, and each ends with \"What this checks\", the exact references it is held to.\n\nLaser spot on a foil: a phase-plate spot, defocus and oblique incidence.\nIntegrating sphere: view factors and radiosity in a cavity with an exact solution.\nFreeing a bundle for radiography: capsule drive symmetry in a NIF hohlraum, the port symmetry, and power rebalancing within beam clearance.\nOne topic each: Entrance-hole size and the power balance, Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall, Pointing and power errors, From drive asymmetry to implosion metrics.\n\nThe reference pages document every exported name.","title":"Start here"},{"category":"section","location":"#The-model-in-brief","page":"Home","text":"Targets are assembled from meshed surfaces (cylinders, annuli, spheres, rectangles, frusta) or imported CAD. Each patch side that radiates is an element of the radiation model. Curved components carry the exact surface their mesh approximates, and the radiation model integrates over it. Laser entrance holes are transparent apertures that record the flux escaping through them.\nBeams enter from facility ports with elliptical super-Gaussian phase-plate spots, an f-number cone, pointing and defocus. A precomputed quadrature over the cone directions makes deposition a smooth function of those parameters, so derivatives come from automatic differentiation. An energy-conserving ray trace is the reference.\nRadiation is diffuse, gray or multigroup. View factors account for occlusion, and a radiosity solve gives the incident flux on every element. Wall albedo can depend on time and on the local radiation temperature. Because the system is linear in the laser source, the response of the capsule flux to deposition is computed once and reused by optimizers.\nSymmetry is measured by Legendre and real spherical-harmonic moments, integrated exactly over the solid angle of each facet.\nTime dependence is quasi-static: at each time, the radiosity is solved for the deposition, the wall state and the geometry of that time.","title":"The model in brief"},{"category":"section","location":"#Glossary","page":"Home","text":"View factor F_ij: the fraction of the diffuse radiation leaving element i that arrives at element j, set only by geometry and occlusion.\nRadiosity: the power per unit area leaving a surface, emission plus reflection (re-emission, for a hohlraum wall). With the view factors it closes a linear system for the flux on every element; Integrating sphere solves the case with an exact answer.\nAlbedo: the fraction of the incident x-ray flux a wall re-emits. Gold hohlraum walls reach 0.8–0.9 at peak drive.\nLEH: the laser entrance hole at each end of a hohlraum. It admits the beams and loses the x rays that reach it.\nP2/P0 (and Pₗ/P0): the ratio of the Legendre moment of degree l of the capsule flux to its mean. P2 > 0 is pole-hot, P2 < 0 waist-hot. Real spherical harmonics Y_lm extend this to three-dimensional asymmetry, such as mode 1 from a missing bundle.\nCone fraction: the share of the inner cones in the total laser power on NIF. It is the main control of P2 through the pulse.\nCBET: cross-beam energy transfer, the exchange of power between crossing beams through ion-acoustic waves in the LEH plasma. A wavelength detuning Δλ between cones sets its direction and size. See Laser–plasma models.\nDante: the absolutely calibrated, filtered x-ray diode array that measures the radiation temperature seen through an LEH.\n\nLindl et al., Phys. Plasmas 11, 339 (2004) review indirect-drive hohlraum physics; the tutorials cite the primary sources for each topic.","title":"Glossary"},{"category":"section","location":"#Units-and-conventions","page":"Home","text":"Units are mm, ns, TW (TW·ns = kJ), TW/mm² for flux (to_Wcm2 multiplies by 10¹⁴) and eV for temperature. Port angles are in degrees: polar angle from +z, azimuth counterclockwise from +x, which on NIF points to Switchyard 1.\n\nThe direction of a beam is fixed by its port (mathbfk = -hatmathbfp), and pointing translates its axis through aim. Best focus is at aim + defocus·k: positive defocus moves the focus along the beam, as in VisRad and the NIF CMT. Spot axes are given in the (hattheta, hatphi) frame of the port.\n\nAway from focus, the spot is the focal profile convolved with the f-cone. The deposition operator samples that convolution at the cone directions: each surface point receives, from each cone direction that reaches it unobstructed, the focal profile where that ray crosses the best-focus plane, weighted by the incidence angle of that ray. Power is conserved at any distance from focus and incidence, and shadows near focus are correlated with the part of the spot they block.","title":"Units and conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Sources","page":"Home","text":"Facility data and physics defaults are sourced in reference/research-notes.md in the repository, with a confidence level for each number and the data each calibration was fitted to. The HYBRID-E pointing in the examples is an illustrative mock-up (hybride_mock_pointing); it is not a facility pointing.","title":"Sources"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/implosion/#Implosion-metrics","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"Reduced models connect drive asymmetry to implosion observables: mode 1 as a hot-spot velocity, the yield factors of the piston model, P2 swing metrics, and beam-error budgets in velocity. Sources and confidence are in reference/research-notes.md, \"Implosion metrics\".","title":"Implosion metrics"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/implosion/#Capsule-drive-for-a-hydrodynamics-code","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"capsule_drive turns a drive_history into the drive a capsule-only hydrodynamics run takes (LLNL's two-step method): Tr(t), the capsule-averaged incident flux, Legendre ratios aₗ/a₀ (P2 > 0 pole-hot) on a uniform ≤ 50 ps grid, the radius the flux was evaluated on, and its sources. CompactLES takes it in memory through its CompactLESBriareusExt extension (radiation_drive(drive), loaded when both packages are). The sources are reference/litreview/hydro_coupling.md and ROADMAP, \"Briareus → CompactLES\".","title":"Capsule drive for a hydrodynamics code"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT = 100.0\n\nHot-spot velocity (km/s, burn-off) per percent of capsule flux mode 1 (a₁/a₀, the norm of the l = 1 vector, mode_amplitude(c, 1)), applied over the rise and peak. It is an engineering default, not set by any facility criterion. Young et al., Phys. Plasmas 27, 082702 (2020) find 37.6 km/s for 0.44% on N170601 in 3D HYDRA, and 44.7 km/s from view factors and their Green's function. MacGowan et al., HEDP 40, 100944 (2021) give ~100 km/s per %, and Kritcher et al. 2024 give 70–75 km/s burn-off for ~0.7% on N230729. The value is platform specific and linear only below about 0.75% (Young); Spears et al. 2014 find 120 km/s at 2%, saturated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.ResponseKernel","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"ResponseKernel(times, values)\n\nA response (Green's) function of time, piecewise linear through the table and zero outside it, callable as k(t). A time may repeat once to make a step. Units are the observable per percent of drive mode per ns (km/s per % per ns for the hot-spot velocity, Young et al. 2020; µm per % per ns for a hot-spot P2 kernel, Masse et al. 2019). Briareus does not include the published kernels: they are platform specific and appear only as figures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ResponseKernel"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.VelocityPenalty","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"VelocityPenalty(; scale = 10, weight = 1, per_percent, amplification = 1,\n                uncertainty = nothing)\n\nAn objective term on the hot-spot velocity of the capsule flux of an evaluation (hotspot_velocity), equal to weight × |v|²/scale² (scale in km/s). Mode 1 is thus weighted by its velocity sensitivity; in an asymmetry objective it has the same weight as the other modes. It is callable on an Evaluation and is added to an objective, e.g. obj = let J = asymmetry_objective(; modes = (2 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0)), P = VelocityPenalty(); ev -> J(ev) + P(ev) end. With uncertainty (mode_uncertainty's error keywords, as for the objectives) it adds the expected |δv|² of the errors. It is generic in the number type (ForwardDiff).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.VelocityPenalty"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.alpha_factor-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"alpha_factor(f, chi; a = 3.3, q = a - 1) -> Real\n\nThe α-heating part of the yield degradation, Y_amp,3D/Y_amp,1D = exp(−q χ^1.2 f²), with χ the 1D generalized Lawson parameter (Y_amp ≈ exp(χ^1.2)). q = a − 1 is Hurricane et al. 2020 (Eq. 21 and the tolerance Eq. 26, from which their Table I comes); Casey et al., PRE 108, L053203 (2023), Eq. 5, has q = 1.2(a − 1). It is generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.alpha_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.burn_amplification-Tuple{Real}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"burn_amplification(Yamp) -> Float64\n\nFactor by which α heating raises the measured (burn-on) hot-spot speed over the burn-off one, Vhs_amp^burn, as a function of the yield amplification Y_amp (burn-on over burn-off yield), from Nora et al., Phys. Plasmas 30, 092705 (2023), Eq. 4, fitted to 2D HYDRA simulations perturbed about N210808:\n\n0.324 ln(Y_amp + 2.239) + 0.653 for 1 ≤ Y_amp ≤ 40;\n13.105 ln(Y_amp + 1585.225) − 95.539 for 80 ≤ Y_amp ≤ 500, where a significant part of the burn is in the expanding dense fuel (burn fraction above ~6%). These are the coefficients of the fit drawn in Fig. 6 of the paper; Eq. 4 gives them rounded to 13, 1585 and −95, which moves the result by −0.24 (the logarithm is ~7.4), e.g. 1.43 instead of 1.67 at Y_amp = 80.\n\nThe two regimes do not join (the low branch is 2.08 at 80) and the paper fits nothing between 40 and 80, so that range is refused, as is anything outside the fits.\n\nTable IV of the paper lists the inverse, 1/Vhs_amp^burn, for NIF shots; for N210808, Y_amp 27.6 gives 0.570, i.e. 1.75. Burn-on/burn-off ratios in Kritcher et al. 2024 (Table II, 1.27–1.39 at comparable yields) are lower: treat it as an upper estimate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.burn_amplification"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.flat_kernel-Tuple{Real, Real}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"flat_kernel(t0, t1; total = HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT) -> ResponseKernel\n\nA constant kernel on [t0, t1] whose integral is total: a constant 1% mode over the window gives total. It is a placeholder for Young's mode-1 Green's function and is derived. The published static sensitivity is kept, and all weight is put on the window, which should be the rise and peak.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.flat_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.hotspot_velocity-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"hotspot_velocity(c; per_percent = HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT, amplification = 1) -> SVector{3}\nhotspot_velocity(ev::Evaluation; ...)\nhotspot_velocity(history; kernel = nothing, window = nothing, per_percent, amplification)\n\nHot-spot velocity (km/s, chamber x, y, z) that mode 1 of the capsule flux drives: the hot spot moves away from the high-flux side (Casey et al., PRL 126, 025002, 2021), at per_percent km/s per percent of mode1_vector (an engineering default, see HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT). amplification multiplies the burn-off velocity, e.g. by burn_amplification to compare with a burn-on nToF measurement. The function is generic in the number type (usable in objectives).\n\nFrom a drive_history result (run with ylm = true; without it only the z component, from the Legendre moments), the velocity is the convolution of Young et al. (Phys. Plasmas 27, 082702, 2020, Eq. 9), v = −∫ G₁(t) · 100 p₁(t) dt, with G₁ (km/s per % per ns) given by kernel (a ResponseKernel or any function of time). The default is flat_kernel(window...; total = per_percent), a flat weight over window (by default the whole history) that gives per_percent for a constant 1%; Young's published kernels are negative in the foot and dominated by the rise and peak, so pass window as the rise and peak, or a custom kernel. The mode 1 is taken as linear between the steps of the history and integrated exactly against a tabulated kernel (two-point Gauss on each interval between their breakpoints; a function kernel is split into 16 pieces per interval); the kernel weight outside the span of the history is dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.hotspot_velocity"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.implosion_metrics-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"implosion_metrics(v; vimp = 380, a = 3.3, c = 0.66, chi = nothing, q = a - 1, ke = nothing)\nimplosion_metrics(ev::Evaluation; per_percent, amplification, kwargs...)\nimplosion_metrics(history; kernel, window, per_percent, amplification, kwargs...)\n\nThe piston-model metrics for a hot-spot velocity v (km/s, a 3-vector; or computed with hotspot_velocity from an evaluation or a drive history). Returns (; velocity, speed, f, rke_fraction, rke, yield_factor, alpha_factor, total):\n\nf = |v|/vimp, the shell asymmetry (ρR_max − ρR_min)/(ρR_max + ρR_min) (Hurricane et al. 2020, Eq. 23); vimp defaults to Hurricane's Table I value, 380 km/s, an engineering default to be replaced by the implosion velocity of the design;\nrke_fraction = c f² (rke_fraction), and rke in the units of ke when given;\nyield_factor, the no-α yield over 1D, (1 − f²)ᵃ;\nalpha_factor, with chi (the 1D generalized Lawson parameter) given, otherwise 1;\ntotal = yield_factor × alpha_factor.\n\nThe function is generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.implosion_metrics"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.infer_velocity_errors-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"infer_velocity_errors(scenario, beams, v; sigma = ntof_covariance(v),\n             per_percent, amplification = 1, thickness = 0.002,\n             thickness_per_percent = 110, kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe most probable beam errors behind a measured hot-spot velocity v (km/s, chamber x, y, z), computed by infer_errors on the mode-1 components. The measurement is mapped to these components through hotspot_velocity with per_percent and amplification, and amplification divides out a burn amplification. Its covariance sigma is a 3 × 3 matrix (km²/s²) or one rms number, by default ntof_covariance(v). The priors are infer_errors's keywords.\n\nA capsule-thickness mode 1 competes with the drive (Casey et al., PRL 126, 025002, 2021): the hot spot moves toward the thicker side, at thickness_per_percent km/s per % of the shell thickness (110 simulated, 140 ± 30 measured), which is the opposite sign of a flux mode 1 on that side. It enters as a nuisance error, a vector t (fractions of the thickness, toward the thicker side) with rms thickness per axis, and is inferred with the beam errors. The default 0.2% corresponds to about 0.15 µm metrology on a 78 µm shell (derived), and thickness = 0 leaves the error out.\n\nReturns infer_errors's fields, with measured, predicted and residual still in the mode-1 components, plus thickness (the inferred t) and velocity = (; measured, nominal, predicted, residual, chi2, chi2_prior) in km/s. predicted is the nominal plus the velocity of the inferred errors and thickness. chi2 is MacGowan's χ^2_ν of the nominal (the forward prediction) against the measurement, (v − v₀)ᵀ Σₘ⁻¹ (v − v₀)/3 (Casey et al. 2023), and chi2_prior is the same with the prior spread of the errors added to Σₘ; it is about 1 when the measurement lies within the spread the priors allow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.infer_velocity_errors"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.mode1_tolerance-Tuple{Real}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"mode1_tolerance(loss; vimp = 380, a = 3.3, chi = 0.0, q = a - 1) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe largest mode-1 asymmetry for which the yield loss 1 − (1 − f²)ᵃ exp(−q χ^1.2 f²) (Hurricane et al. 2020, Eq. 26) equals loss. It returns (; f, velocity), the shell asymmetry and the hot-spot speed (km/s) at vimp. It is the inverse of implosion_metrics's total.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode1_tolerance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.mode1_vector-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"mode1_vector(c) -> SVector{3}\n\nThe mode-1 vector of the flux from real spherical-harmonic coefficients c (mode_index order, e.g. flux_ylm). It is √3 (c₁₁, c₁,₋₁, c₁₀)/c₀₀ along (x, y, z) and points toward the high-flux side. Its norm is mode_amplitude(c, 1), the fractional amplitude ε of a dipole drive 1 + ε cos γ (not a percentage). It is generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.mode1_vector"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.ntof_covariance-Tuple{Any}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"ntof_covariance(v; speed = 14, theta = 13, phi = 29) -> Matrix\n\nCovariance (km²/s²) of an nToF hot-spot velocity measurement v (km/s): rms speed in magnitude and theta, phi degrees in direction (±14 km/s, 13° and 29° at NIF; Young et al. 2020), along the local radial, polar and azimuthal directions of v.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.ntof_covariance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.reactivity_exponent-Tuple{Real}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"reactivity_exponent(T) -> Float64\n\nThe exponent a of ⟨σv⟩ ∝ Tᵃ at ion temperature T (keV): 6 − 1.5 ln T (Casey et al., PRE 108, L053203, 2023). It is 3.3 at 6 keV, the value Hurricane uses for 4–7 keV.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.reactivity_exponent"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.rke_fraction-Tuple{Any}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"rke_fraction(f; c = 0.66) -> Real\n\nResidual kinetic energy over the implosion kinetic energy of the shell, RKE/KE = c f², from Hurricane et al. 2020, Eq. 23, where f = v_HS/v_imp = √(RKE/(c KE)). The constant c is ≈ 0.66 in NIF-like simulations and 1 for incompressible pistons.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.rke_fraction"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.shape_amplification-Tuple{Real}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"shape_amplification(P2) -> Float64\nshape_amplification(P2, theta) -> Float64\n\nFactor by which the stagnated hot-spot shape changes the burn-off hot-spot speed driven by a mode-1 flux asymmetry, Vhs_amp^shape (speed over the speed without a flux P2), as a function of the equatorial neutron-image shape P2 (P2/P0 of the hot spot as a fraction, so −0.2 is 20% oblate), from Nora et al., Phys. Plasmas 30, 092705 (2023):\n\nshape_amplification(P2): Eq. 2, a fit to their 2D database (mode 1 along the pole, ~1% flux mode 1, time-dependent flux P2): 1.31 P2 + 0.71 below 0.35, −0.65 P2 + 1.4 above. It is 0.71 for a round hot spot: the 2D points sit shifted from the 3D ones (Fig. 5 of the paper), and a time-integrated P2 swing does not order them well. Use it for trends.\nshape_amplification(P2, theta): their Table I (3D HYDRA, constant asymmetries), bilinear in P2 (−0.5656 to 0.6448, clamped outside) and the polar angle theta (degrees from the north pole) of the mode-1 flux asymmetry, folded onto 0–90° by the equatorial symmetry. The factor is 0.8–1.2 for |P2| < 0.4 at most angles; mode 1 along the pole of an oblate hot spot is suppressed (0.05 at P2 = −0.57, a toroidal hot spot).\n\nTable IV of the paper lists 1/Vhs_amp^shape per shot. Its equatorial rows follow Table I (0.965 for N130530); its polar rows could not be reproduced from the published angles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.shape_amplification"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.swing_metrics-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"swing_metrics(history; l = 2, window = nothing, picket = nothing, limit = 0.075,\n              kernels = (;)) -> NamedTuple\n\nTime metrics of aₗ/a₀ of the capsule flux over a drive_history result (taken as linear between its steps). Returns (; mean, max, min, swing, deviation, picket_mean, picket_max, picket_ok, response):\n\nover window (by default the whole history): the time average mean, the extremes and swing = max − min, and deviation, the time average of |aₗ/a₀ − mean|;\nover picket (by default the first 2 ns of the history), the time average and the largest |aₗ/a₀|. picket_ok is true when the magnitude of the average is at most limit. The default limit is the 7.5% early-time P2/P0 of Milovich et al. (Phys. Plasmas 23, 032701, 2016) for under 10% margin loss. It is derived, since the paper does not state whether the limit is on the average or the peak;\nresponse: for each name => kernel in kernels (ResponseKernels or functions, per % per ns), ∫ K(t) · 100 aₗ/a₀ dt in the units of the kernel, e.g. a hot-spot or ρR P2 Green's function (Masse et al., Phys. Plasmas 26, 2019). Without kernels it is an empty NamedTuple: the flux history alone does not give a hot-spot shape. Masse's hot-spot kernel changes sign before about 4.5 ns while the ρR one does not, so a time-integrated flux P2 determines neither, and by default Briareus does not report a value in µm.\n\nAll values are fractions (not percent).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.swing_metrics"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.velocity_uncertainty-Tuple{Scenario, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"velocity_uncertainty(scenario, beams; per_percent, amplification = 1, vimp = 380,\n                     a = 3.3, chi = nothing, q = a - 1, kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\nvelocity_uncertainty(u; ...)\n\nThe beam errors of mode_uncertainty (through its keywords pointing, power, group_pointing, group_power, groups, positioning), or its result u, carried to the hot-spot velocity at per_percent km/s per % of mode 1 (hotspot_velocity). Returns (; velocity, covariance, sigma, rms, rms_by, speed_rms, yield_factor, expected_yield, total, expected_total):\n\nvelocity, the nominal (km/s), covariance (3 × 3, km²/s²) and sigma its per-axis rms; rms = √tr Σ, split by source in rms_by (beam, group, positioning; they add in quadrature) and speed_rms = √E|v|²;\nyield_factor, the no-α factor at the nominal velocity, and expected_yield, its expectation over the errors, from the Gaussian closed form of E[exp(−a f²)] ((1 − f²)ᵃ ≈ exp(−a f²), within 1% at f = 0.25); total and expected_total include the α factor (chi given) the same way, exp(−(a + q χ^1.2) f²).\n\nThe result is linear in the errors, like mode_uncertainty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.velocity_uncertainty"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.yield_factor-Tuple{Any}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"yield_factor(f; a = 3.3) -> Real\n\nNo-α yield relative to 1D for a mode-1 (piston) asymmetry f = v_HS/v_imp: (1 − f²)ᵃ (Hurricane et al., Phys. Plasmas 27, 062704, 2020, Eq. 20), with a the temperature exponent of ⟨σv⟩ (3.3 for 4–7 keV; reactivity_exponent). It is generic in the number type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.yield_factor"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.young2020_dropped_quads-Tuple{}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"young2020_dropped_quads() -> NamedTuple\n\nFlux mode 1 (%, the norm of the l = 1 vector) on the capsule of N170827 with one quad dropped from each cone and the requested pulses, in the LLNL static view-factor model (radii at 85%, albedos 0.8/0.3), from Young et al., Phys. Plasmas 27, 082702 (2020), Fig. 10a, digitized (data/young2020_fig10a.csv, sources in its header). The columns are t (ns, 0.25–7.75 every 0.125), inner23, inner30, outer44, outer50 (NaN where hidden). Fig. 10c's hot-spot velocities (km/s, read off the markers ±2) are in velocity (requested pulses) and velocity_delivered, in the same cone order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.young2020_dropped_quads"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.young2020_green-Tuple{}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"young2020_green() -> NamedTuple\nyoung2020_kernel() -> ResponseKernel\n\nThe mode-1 Green's function of NIF shot N170827 (km/s of final hot-spot velocity per % of flux mode 1 per ns) from 59 2D capsule-only HYDRA impulse runs in Young et al., Phys. Plasmas 27, 082702 (2020), Fig. 5, digitized twice (data/young2020_fig5.csv, sources and reading errors in its header). young2020_green() has columns t (ns, 0.25–7.875 every 0.125), panel_b and panel_d (NaN where the curve is hidden under another stroke); young2020_kernel() is their mean, or either one where the other is missing, as a ResponseKernel for hotspot_velocity(history; kernel). The kernel is negative in the foot and dominated by the rise and peak. Its integral is ~117 km/s per %, the constant-mode-1 sensitivity on this platform, against 100 for HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT. The kernel is platform specific: that of N170601 is almost identical, and that of N180128 puts more weight on the rise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.young2020_green"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.young2020_kernel-Tuple{}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"young2020_green() -> NamedTuple\nyoung2020_kernel() -> ResponseKernel\n\nThe mode-1 Green's function of NIF shot N170827 (km/s of final hot-spot velocity per % of flux mode 1 per ns) from 59 2D capsule-only HYDRA impulse runs in Young et al., Phys. Plasmas 27, 082702 (2020), Fig. 5, digitized twice (data/young2020_fig5.csv, sources and reading errors in its header). young2020_green() has columns t (ns, 0.25–7.875 every 0.125), panel_b and panel_d (NaN where the curve is hidden under another stroke); young2020_kernel() is their mean, or either one where the other is missing, as a ResponseKernel for hotspot_velocity(history; kernel). The kernel is negative in the foot and dominated by the rise and peak. Its integral is ~117 km/s per %, the constant-mode-1 sensitivity on this platform, against 100 for HOTSPOT_VELOCITY_PER_PERCENT. The kernel is platform specific: that of N170601 is almost identical, and that of N180128 puts more weight on the rise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.young2020_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.young2020_pulses-Tuple{}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"young2020_pulses() -> NamedTuple\n\nThe requested laser power per beam of NIF shot N170827 (TW), (; inner, outer) as PulseShapes (inner for 23.5° and 30°, outer for 44.5° and 50°), from Young et al., Phys. Plasmas 27, 082702 (2020), Fig. 2e, digitized (data/young2020_fig2e.csv, 0.25–7.75 ns every 0.125, reading error ~0.03 TW). The pulses are passed to with_pulses(beams, pulses, t; by) with by = b -> startswith(String(b.group), \"inner\") ? :inner : :outer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.young2020_pulses"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.CapsuleDrive","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"CapsuleDrive\n\nThe radiation drive on the capsule over time, for a capsule-only hydrodynamics run. LLNL's two-step method runs an integrated hohlraum step first and then drives capsule-only runs with its capsule flux (Clark et al., PoP 26, 050601, 2019; Masse et al., PoP 26, 2019). Build one with capsule_drive. Fields:\n\ntime — uniform time grid (ns)\nTr — radiation temperature of the capsule-averaged incident flux (eV), radiation_temperature(flux)\nflux — capsule-averaged incident flux a₀ (TW/mm²; ×1e14 → W/cm²)\nmodes — (lmax+1) × nt Legendre ratios aₗ/a₀ (row l+1, so row 1 is 1), of the incident flux F(θ,t) = a₀(t) Σₗ (aₗ/a₀)(t) Pₗ(cos θ), θ from the hohlraum axis (+z) about the capsule center. P2 > 0 is pole-hot (Masse 2019's convention).\nabsorbed — x-ray power absorbed by the capsule (TW), as in energy_balance (NaN where the history does not record it)\nradius — the radius (mm) of the sphere the flux was evaluated on (the capsule mesh)\narea — the area of the observed elements (mm²)\nprovenance — Dict{Symbol,Any}: code, version, git commit, the source times of the history, observed components, capsule material, mesh size, and whatever the caller adds\nylm — nothing (axisymmetric), or the drive with every m: the (lmax+1)² × nt ratios bₗₘ (rows in mode_index order, so row 1 is 1) of F(θ,φ,t) = a₀(t) Σₗₘ bₗₘ(t) Ŷₗₘ(θ,φ), where Ŷₗₘ are the real Schmidt semi-normalized harmonics, Ŷₗ₀ = Pₗ(cos θ) and Ŷₗ,±ₘ = √(2(l−m)!/(l+m)!) Pₗᵐ(cos θ) (cos mφ, sin mφ) for m > 0, without the Condon–Shortley phase, φ from +x toward +y (Switchyard 1 on NIF). That is the normalization of modes: the m = 0 rows are modes (bₗ₀ = aₗ/a₀), and √(Σₘ bₗₘ²) is mode_amplitude, the rotation-invariant |aₗ/a₀| of degree l. From the orthonormal coefficients cₗₘ of ylm_moments (or drive_history(...; ylm = true)), bₗₘ = √((2l+1)/4π) cₗₘ / a₀.\n\nThe angular structure is that of the flux on the initial capsule radius; applying it at a moving ablation front far inside that radius changes the geometric smoothing (hydro_coupling.md §2, pitfall 2). The drive is gray, without a spectral (M-band) split (ROADMAP \"Spectral radiosity\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CapsuleDrive"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.CapsuleDrive-Tuple{Real}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"(d::CapsuleDrive)(t) -> (; Tr, flux, modes, ylm)\n\nThe drive at time t (ns), linear in the moments aₗ (and a₀ bₗₘ) between grid times and held at the end values outside them; ylm is nothing for an axisymmetric drive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.CapsuleDrive"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/implosion/#Briareus.capsule_drive-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Implosion metrics","text":"capsule_drive(history, target; observe = :capsule, dt = 0.05, times = nothing,\n              lmax = size(history.moments, 1) - 1, radius = nothing,\n              ylm = history.ylm !== nothing, provenance = (;)) -> CapsuleDrive\n\nThe capsule drive of a drive_history result for a capsule-only hydrodynamics run. The drive is sampled on a uniform grid of spacing ≤ dt ns that spans the solved times of the history, or on the given times, which must lie within them. The default dt is 50 ps (hydro_coupling.md §(c)). The Legendre moments aₗ are interpolated linearly between the history steps and divided by the interpolated a₀. flux is a₀ and Tr its radiation temperature; at the history times both equal the flux and Tr of the history exactly. Run drive_history(...; lmax = 8) for the moments through l = 8 that the hand-over requires; the lmax of capsule_drive may only truncate.\n\nWith ylm, the drive contains every m as well. ylm is the default when the harmonics were recorded in the history with drive_history(...; ylm = true). The orthonormal coefficients cₗₘ of the history are interpolated linearly like aₗ and become the Schmidt ratios bₗₘ = √((2l+1)/4π) cₗₘ / a₀ of the ylm field. The m = 0 rows of that field are then set to modes, so the two agree exactly. The history integrates its Legendre moments one sub-triangle level finer than its Y_lm, and the replaced rows differ by less than 1e-3 of a₀ on the coarse test hohlraum.\n\ntarget is a Target or RadiationModel; for a moving hohlraum it is the reference target of the keyframes. The radius of the observed sphere is taken from its SphereSurface. The observed area, and the capsule material and mesh recorded in provenance, are taken from the target. For any other observed surface, pass radius. observe must name the components observed in the history. provenance, a NamedTuple or Dict with entries such as the hohlraum, pulse and albedo model, is merged into the recorded provenance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.capsule_drive"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#Radiation-in-an-imported-CAD-hohlraum","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"Target designers draw hohlraums in CAD, and the assembly that reaches a view-factor code is a set of tessellated solids: two hohlraum halves with thick walls, entrance hole washers, the capsule, the tent films that hold it, and the structure that mounts the target. As a radiation model it has three problems. Every part is a closed solid, so most of its surface (outer walls, back faces, the mount) never exchanges radiation with the cavity, while view factors cost the square of the element count. The tessellation is made for drawing, which does not suit radiosity: a cylindrical wall comes as strips running its full length and flat rings as radial slivers, and the windings of the facets (which side is the front) are mixed. And the curved walls are polygons: flat facets standing in for a cylinder or a sphere misstate the exchange between nearby elements (see the Integrating sphere tutorial).\n\nThis tutorial builds such an assembly, with the flaws of real exports, writes it as a multi-solid STL file and takes it to a radiation calculation: orient_mesh repairs the windings, enclosure selects the surfaces that bound the cavity around the capsule, remesh_mesh divides them into compact elements, fit_surfaces and curved_components find the cylinders and spheres and attach their exact surfaces; cad_target then takes these steps in one call. The radiosity solution is checked against the same hohlraum built from Briareus's primitives and against closed forms.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nusing Random\nusing Briareus: Vec3                     # Makie exports a Vec3 too\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Briareus), \"docs\", \"literate\", \"_plotting.jl\"))   # figure helpers\ncutaway! (generic function with 1 method)","title":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#A-CAD-like-assembly","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"The hohlraum has a cavity of radius 2.875 mm and length 9.4 mm with 1.55 mm entrance holes (LEHs), as the primitive hohlraum has by default. In CAD it is two halves with 50 µm walls that meet at the waist across a 20 µm gap; each end wall is 50 µm thick, and a 50 µm washer on it bears the LEH, so the holes are 0.1 mm deep bores. The capsule has a radius of 0.9 mm, and the tent is a 15 µm film wrapped around it, 30 µm off its surface, made of two cups that meet at the equator. A mounting block sits beside the waist. All lengths are in millimeters.\n\nR, Rout, Rleh, h = 2.875, 2.925, 1.55, 4.7   # cavity and outer radius, LEH radius, half length\nte, tw, Rw = 0.05, 0.05, 2.4                 # end wall and washer thickness, washer radius\ng2 = 0.01                                    # half the waist gap\nrc, ri, ro = 0.9, 0.93, 0.945                # capsule, tent film inside and outside\nzL = h + te + tw                             # the outer end of the LEH bores\n\nEvery part is a solid of revolution, tessellated the way CAD exports cylinders: one facet pair per azimuthal step and profile segment, whatever the length of the segment. A wall segment becomes strips running its full length, a flat ring radial slivers, and a sphere latitude bands with fans of slivers at the poles. revolve turns a closed profile of (r z) points into such facets; the bottom half is the top half mirrored.\n\npt(r, φ, z) = Vec3(r * cos(φ), r * sin(φ), z)\nfunction revolve(profile; nphi)\n    facets = NTuple{3,Vec3}[]\n    φs = range(0, 2π; length = nphi + 1)\n    for (i, (r1, z1)) in enumerate(profile)\n        r2, z2 = profile[mod1(i + 1, length(profile))]\n        r1 == r2 == 0 && continue                               # along the axis\n        for j in 1:nphi\n            a, b = pt(r1, φs[j], z1), pt(r1, φs[j+1], z1)\n            c, d = pt(r2, φs[j+1], z2), pt(r2, φs[j], z2)\n            r1 == 0 ? push!(facets, (a, c, d)) :                # fans at the poles\n            r2 == 0 ? push!(facets, (a, b, c)) : push!(facets, (a, b, c), (a, c, d))\n        end\n    end\n    return facets\nend\narc(r, a, b, n) = [(r * sinpi(x), r * cospi(x)) for x in range(a, b; length = n + 1)]  # polar angles in units of π\nmirror(facets) = [map(v -> v .* Vec3(1, 1, -1), f) for f in facets]\nfunction block(lo, hi)\n    c = [Vec3(x, y, z) for x in (lo[1], hi[1]), y in (lo[2], hi[2]), z in (lo[3], hi[3])]\n    quads = ((1, 3, 7, 5), (2, 6, 8, 4), (1, 5, 6, 2), (3, 4, 8, 7), (1, 2, 4, 3), (5, 7, 8, 6))\n    return [f for q in quads for f in ((c[q[1]], c[q[2]], c[q[3]]), (c[q[1]], c[q[3]], c[q[4]]))]\nend\n\nhalf = revolve([(R, g2), (Rout, g2), (Rout, h + te), (Rleh, h + te), (Rleh, h), (R, h)]; nphi = 120)\nwasher = revolve([(Rleh, h + te), (Rw, h + te), (Rw, zL), (Rleh, zL)]; nphi = 120)\ncapsule = revolve(arc(rc, 0, 1, 40); nphi = 80)\ncup = revolve([arc(ro, 0, 1 / 2, 20); reverse(arc(ri, 0, 1 / 2, 20))]; nphi = 80)\nassembly = [\"hohlraum_top\" => half, \"hohlraum_bottom\" => mirror(half),\n         \"washer_top\" => washer, \"washer_bottom\" => mirror(washer),\n         \"capsule\" => capsule, \"tent_top\" => cup, \"tent_bottom\" => mirror(cup),\n         \"mount\" => block((Rout + 0.3, -1.0, -1.5), (Rout + 2.3, 1.0, 1.5))]\n\nCAD faces are stitched with either orientation: here 30% of the facets, chosen at random, are wound the other way, and the facets are shuffled. write_stl with binary = false writes one solid … endsolid block per part, the way CAD exporters write assemblies, and read_stl_solids reads them back as named meshes. STL lists three separate vertices for every facet, so the file does not record which facets are neighbors.\n\nrng = Xoshiro(2026)\nfunction cad_mesh(facets)\n    m = Briareus.Mesh()\n    for (a, b, c) in shuffle(rng, facets)\n        add_polygon!(m, rand(rng) < 0.3 ? [a, c, b] : [a, b, c])\n    end\n    return m\nend\npath = joinpath(mktempdir(), \"assembly.stl\")\nwrite_stl(path, [name => cad_mesh(f) for (name, f) in assembly]; binary = false)\nsolids = read_stl_solids(path)\n8-element Vector{Pair{String, Mesh}}:\n    \"hohlraum_top\" => Mesh(1440 patches, 1440 triangles)\n \"hohlraum_bottom\" => Mesh(1440 patches, 1440 triangles)\n      \"washer_top\" => Mesh(960 patches, 960 triangles)\n   \"washer_bottom\" => Mesh(960 patches, 960 triangles)\n         \"capsule\" => Mesh(6240 patches, 6240 triangles)\n        \"tent_top\" => Mesh(6400 patches, 6400 triangles)\n     \"tent_bottom\" => Mesh(6400 patches, 6400 triangles)\n           \"mount\" => Mesh(12 patches, 12 triangles)\n\nThe shapes of the facets, as the longest edge over the height across it (1.15 for an equilateral triangle), and the signed volumes of the solids are listed below before and after orient_mesh. The signed volume, sum acdot(btimes c)6 over the facets, is the volume of the material for a closed solid wound to face out of it:\n\nfunction aspect(m, k)\n    a, b, c = (m.vertices[i] for i in m.triangles[k])\n    L = maximum(norm, (b - a, c - b, a - c))\n    return L^2 / norm(cross(b - a, c - a))\nend\nsigned_volume(m) = sum(dot(m.vertices[a], cross(m.vertices[b], m.vertices[c])) for (a, b, c) in m.triangles) / 6\nvolume(name) = name == \"capsule\" ? 4π / 3 * rc^3 : startswith(name, \"hohlraum\") ?\n               π * (Rout^2 - R^2) * (h - g2) + π * (Rout^2 - Rleh^2) * te :\n               startswith(name, \"washer\") ? π * (Rw^2 - Rleh^2) * tw :\n               startswith(name, \"tent\") ? 2π / 3 * (ro^3 - ri^3) : 2 * 2 * 3\noriented = [name => orient_mesh(m) for (name, m) in solids]\nprintln(\"part              facets  aspect: median   max    volume (mm³): as read  oriented   exact\")\nfor ((name, m), (_, o)) in zip(solids, oriented)\n    as = sort([aspect(m, k) for k in eachindex(m.triangles)])\n    @printf(\"%-16s %7d %13.1f %7.0f %19.4f %9.4f %8.4f\\n\", name, length(m.triangles), as[end ÷ 2],\n            as[end], signed_volume(m), signed_volume(o), volume(name))\nend\npart              facets  aspect: median   max    volume (mm³): as read  oriented   exact\nhohlraum_top        1440           9.0      31              9.1329    5.2370   5.2394\nhohlraum_bottom     1440           9.0      31             -6.5550    5.2370   5.2394\nwasher_top           960           2.9      11             -1.2464    0.5272   0.5274\nwasher_bottom        960           2.9      11              1.6148    0.5272   0.5274\ncapsule             6240           2.1      13             -1.2071    3.0458   3.0536\ntent_top            6400           2.1      13             -0.0509    0.0826   0.0828\ntent_bottom         6400           2.1      13              0.0405    0.0826   0.0828\nmount                 12           2.2       2             14.9000   12.0000  12.0000\n\nThe wall strips are 4.7 mm long and 0.15 mm wide. As read, the signed volumes are meaningless. orient_mesh welds vertices by position, makes the windings consistent across every shared edge, and turns each closed surface to face out of the volume it encloses. Every part here is closed, and its oriented volume is the exact volume of the solid less the faceting (a 120-gon has 1 - (2pi120)^26, 0.05% less area than its circle). The inner wall of a hohlraum half is the same surface as its outer wall, so it now faces the axis: the cavity side.\n\nEach closed surface is oriented as a whole, so a hollow part whose inner surface is a separate closed surface (a shell modeled with its gas volume) gets that inner surface facing into the material as well. None of these parts has one; the tent cups and the washers are each a single closed surface.","title":"A CAD-like assembly"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#The-cavity","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"The radiation problem is the free space around the capsule. enclosure finds the element sides that bound it: each element casts 64 diffuse rays and gathers the flux that an isotropic source at the point would put on it, directly and after re-emission, and the elements that receive at least 5% of the typical flux in the cavity are kept. The point is the capsule center; lines of sight from it pass through surfaces seen from behind, such as the capsule surface itself.\n\npart_target(ps) = Target([Component(Symbol(n), m, n == \"capsule\" ? ablator() : gold()) for (n, m) in ps])\nwith_tent = enclosure(part_target(oriented))\nEnclosure of (0.0, 0.0, 0.0): 12480 of 23852 elements (12480 selected), 3 of 8 components\n  escape 0.0000 of the rays from the point; open area 0 mm², leak area 0 mm² (returning 0 mm²), back sides 0 mm² (64 rays per element)\n  hohlraum_top                0 / 1440     elements  area          0 / 210.9      mm²\n  hohlraum_bottom             0 / 1440     elements  area          0 / 210.9      mm²\n  washer_top                  0 / 960      elements  area          0 / 22.33      mm²\n  washer_bottom               0 / 960      elements  area          0 / 22.33      mm²\n  capsule                  6240 / 6240     elements  area      10.17 / 10.17      mm²\n  tent_top                 3120 / 6400     elements  area      5.427 / 11.12      mm²\n  tent_bottom              3120 / 6400     elements  area      5.427 / 11.12      mm²\n  mount                       0 / 12       elements  area          0 / 32         mm²\n\n\nThe two cups of the tent are closed solids, and their inner faces enclose the capsule completely: from the center, the free space is the 30 µm pocket between the capsule and the film, and nothing escapes it. CAD gives the film a thickness to make it a solid. The real tent is a polymer film thin enough that the x rays of the drive cross it, so it is left out of the radiation model, as an entrance-hole window would be. That opens the cavity:\n\nno_tent = filter(p -> !startswith(p[1], \"tent\"), oriented)\ncavity = enclosure(part_target(no_tent))\nEnclosure of (0.0, 0.0, 0.0): 8640 of 11052 elements (8640 selected), 5 of 6 components\n  escape 0.0513 of the rays from the point; open area 14.99 mm², leak area 0.03588 mm² (returning 7.67e-05 mm²), back sides 0 mm² (64 rays per element)\n  hohlraum_top              960 / 1440     elements  area      104.5 / 210.9      mm²\n  hohlraum_bottom           960 / 1440     elements  area      104.5 / 210.9      mm²\n  washer_top                240 / 960      elements  area     0.4869 / 22.33      mm²\n  washer_bottom             240 / 960      elements  area     0.4869 / 22.33      mm²\n  capsule                  6240 / 6240     elements  area      10.17 / 10.17      mm²\n  mount                       0 / 12       elements  area          0 / 32         mm²\n\n\nThe kept surfaces are the inner wall of each half, its end cap, the bores of the end wall and of the washer, and the two faces of the waist gap; the outer walls, the faces of the washers and the mount are dropped. By reciprocity, the rays that escape estimate the open area of the cavity: the two LEHs (120-gons of radius 1.55 mm) and the outer end of the waist gap, 2pi r g, less what the mount intercepts beyond the gap (leak_area). The estimate is a Monte Carlo sum, with a spread of about 2% at 64 rays per element and 0.4% at 512:\n\nleh_area = 2 * 60 * Rleh^2 * sin(2π / 120)\ngap_exit = 2π * Rout * 2g2\n@printf(\"LEHs %.3f mm² + gap exit %.3f mm² - leak to the mount %.3f mm² = %.3f mm²\\n\",\n        leh_area, gap_exit, cavity.leak_area, leh_area + gap_exit - cavity.leak_area)\nfor rays in (64, 512)\n    e = rays == 64 ? cavity : enclosure(part_target(no_tent); rays)\n    @printf(\"%3d rays per element: open area %.3f mm² (%+.1f%%), %d elements chosen otherwise\\n\", rays,\n            e.open_area, 100 * (e.open_area / (leh_area + gap_exit - cavity.leak_area) - 1),\n            count(e.kept .!= cavity.kept))\nend\nLEHs 15.088 mm² + gap exit 0.368 mm² - leak to the mount 0.036 mm² = 15.420 mm²\n 64 rays per element: open area 14.988 mm² (-2.8%), 0 elements chosen otherwise\n512 rays per element: open area 15.328 mm² (-0.6%), 0 elements chosen otherwise\n\nThe cutaways show the far half of the assembly from the front and a little above. What each selection keeps is drawn in color, and what it drops in gray. The pocket is drawn close up, with the upper half of the capsule left out to show the inner face of the tent behind it.\n\ncolors = Dict{String,Any}(zip(first.(assembly), Makie.wong_colors()[[1, 1, 3, 3, 6, 4, 4, 2]]))\nfunction cutaway_panel(pos, enc, title; limits, show = (name, poly) -> true)\n    ax = Axis(pos; title, aspect = DataAspect(), limits)\n    hidedecorations!(ax); hidespines!(ax)\n    t = enc.source\n    polys, cols = Vector{Vec3}[], Any[]\n    for (i, c) in enumerate(t.components), (p, e) in zip(eachindex(c.mesh.patches), t.comp_elems[i])\n        poly = patch_outline(c.mesh, p)\n        show(c.name, poly) || continue\n        push!(polys, poly)\n        push!(cols, enc.kept[e] ? colors[String(c.name)] : :gray88)\n    end\n    cutaway!(ax, polys, cols; strokewidth = 0.1)\n    return ax\nend\nfar_half(name, poly) = name in (:capsule, :mount) || sum(v[2] for v in poly) > 0\nlower_capsule(name, poly) = name === :capsule ? sum(v[3] for v in poly) < 0 : far_half(name, poly)\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 500))\ncutaway_panel(fig[1, 1], with_tent, \"with the tent: a pocket\";\n              limits = ((-1.6, 1.6), (-1.2, 1.6)), show = lower_capsule)\ncutaway_panel(fig[1, 2], cavity, \"tent left out: the cavity\"; limits = ((-3.3, 5.4), (-5.0, 5.7)),\n              show = far_half)\ncolsize!(fig.layout, 1, Relative(0.42))\nfig\nFigure()","title":"The cavity"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#Elements","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"The kept facets are still the CAD's: 960 per half, a quarter of them the wall strips. Radiosity elements must be compact, because the radiosity is constant over an element and the view factor of a pair is integrated over both. coarsen_mesh only groups whole facets into elements, which cannot shorten a strip; remesh_mesh divides the surface itself into near-planar elements of about the given size. The element diameter over the square root of its area is 1.4 for a square and grows with elongation:\n\nkept = cavity.target.components\nfacets = Dict(c.name => c.mesh for c in kept)\nsize_of(name) = name === :capsule ? 0.15 : 0.5\nfunction shape(m)\n    d = map(m.patches) do r\n        vs = unique(v for k in r for v in m.triangles[k])\n        A = sum(norm(cross(m.vertices[b] - m.vertices[a], m.vertices[c] - m.vertices[a])) / 2 for (a, b, c) in m.triangles[r])\n        maximum(norm(m.vertices[u] - m.vertices[v]) for u in vs, v in vs) / sqrt(A)\n    end\n    sort!(d)\n    return d[end ÷ 2], d[end]\nend\nprintln(\"part              facets   size | remesh_mesh  diam/√A: median  max | coarsen_mesh  median   max\")\nfor c in kept\n    s = size_of(c.name)\n    rm, cm = remesh_mesh(c.mesh; size = s), coarsen_mesh(c.mesh; size = s)\n    @printf(\"%-16s %7d %6.2f | %8d %20.2f %5.1f | %9d %10.2f %5.1f\\n\", c.name, npatches(c.mesh), s,\n            npatches(rm), shape(rm)..., npatches(cm), shape(cm)...)\nend\npart              facets   size | remesh_mesh  diam/√A: median  max | coarsen_mesh  median   max\nhohlraum_top         960   0.50 |      457                 1.44   4.7 |       594       5.74   7.9\nhohlraum_bottom      960   0.50 |      465                 1.44   4.7 |       594       5.74   7.9\nwasher_top           240   0.50 |       21                 2.86   3.4 |        35       2.25   2.9\nwasher_bottom        240   0.50 |       22                 2.86   3.4 |        35       2.25   2.9\ncapsule             6240   0.15 |      406                 1.39   1.6 |      2234       1.42   2.7\n\nOn the walls and the capsule the remeshed elements are as compact as squares, about one per size² (the 104.5 mm² cavity side of a half in about 470 elements of 0.5 mm). Faces narrower than the size, the 50 µm bores and gap faces, give elements as narrow: the geometry sets that scale. Coarsening leaves the strips whole, and five times as many elements on the capsule. The capsule is remeshed finer (0.15 mm) than the walls (0.5 mm), as the capsule of a primitive hohlraum is.\n\nThe selection is then repeated on the elements, since an element straddling the edge of the lit region averages its flux over its whole area. Selecting on the facets first and remeshing only the kept ones saves remeshing the mount and the outsides. The remeshed surface has the area of the facets within round-off.\n\nremesh_cavity(wall, capsule) =\n    enclosure([Component(c.name, remesh_mesh(c.mesh; size = c.name === :capsule ? capsule : wall), c.material)\n               for c in kept])\nremeshed = remesh_cavity(0.5, 0.15)\n@printf(\"%d elements, %.2f mm² (the kept facets: %.2f mm²), all kept: %s\\n\", nelements(remeshed.target),\n        sum(remeshed.target.elem_area), sum(cavity.target.elem_area), all(remeshed.kept))\n1371 elements, 220.18 mm² (the kept facets: 220.18 mm²), all kept: true","title":"Elements"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#Curved-surfaces","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"fit_surfaces finds the planes, cylinders and spheres in a mesh by region growing with least-squares fits. Remeshing puts new points inside the CAD facets, which lie inside a curved wall by up to the sagitta of the facets, so the fits are made on the CAD facets (reference) and carried over to the elements. For the top half:\n\nregions = Dict(c.name => fit_surfaces(c.mesh; reference = facets[c.name]) for c in remeshed.target.components)\nforeach(println, regions[:hohlraum_top])\nSurfaceRegion(:cylinder, radius 2.875 mm, axis (-7.539e-34, 9.233e-34, 1) through (-1.419e-16, -8.486e-17, 2.355), 334 patches, 84.71 mm², rms 0.00036 mm, max 0.00098 mm, facing in)\nSurfaceRegion(:plane, normal (-0, -0, -1), 78 patches, 18.41 mm², rms 1.3e-14 mm, max 1.3e-14 mm)\nSurfaceRegion(:plane, normal (-0, -0, -1), 25 patches, 0.9106 mm², rms 6.9e-18 mm, max 6.9e-18 mm)\nSurfaceRegion(:cylinder, radius 1.55 mm, axis (-4.312e-32, -7.225e-32, 1) through (5.817e-17, 4.194e-18, 4.725), 20 patches, 0.4869 mm², rms 7e-17 mm, max 2.2e-16 mm, facing in)\n\nThe inner wall (2.875 mm, facing in), the LEH bore of the end wall (1.55 mm) and two planes (the end cap and the gap face) are found. The CAD vertices lie on the true surfaces, so the radii come out exact; the residuals measure the facets, whose middles lie inside the wall by up to their sagitta, 2875 (2pi120)^28 = 0.98 µm. curved_components splits each part into its curved regions, each carrying the fitted CylinderSurface or SphereSurface, and the rest; planar faces stay flat elements, which are exact. So do curved regions of under 10⁻³ of the part area (minfraction): in real CAD those are fillet pieces, small holes and edge faces, each of which would otherwise become a separate component (the bore here is about 5×10⁻³ of the kept area of its half).\n\nfitted = reduce(vcat, [curved_components(c; regions = regions[c.name]) for c in remeshed.target.components])\n\nFitted radii, axes and centers:\n\nfor c in fitted\n    @printf(\"%-22s %4d elements  %s\\n\", c.name, npatches(c.mesh),\n            c.surface isa CylinderSurface ? @sprintf(\"cylinder r = %.9f mm, axis %s\", c.surface.radius, round.(c.surface.axis; digits = 9)) :\n            c.surface isa SphereSurface ? @sprintf(\"sphere r = %.9f mm at %s\", c.surface.radius, round.(c.surface.center; digits = 9)) : \"flat\")\nend\nhohlraum_top/cyl1       334 elements  cylinder r = 2.875000000 mm, axis [-0.0, 0.0, 1.0]\nhohlraum_top/cyl2        20 elements  cylinder r = 1.550000000 mm, axis [-0.0, -0.0, 1.0]\nhohlraum_top/rest       103 elements  flat\nhohlraum_bottom/cyl1    337 elements  cylinder r = 2.875000000 mm, axis [-0.0, 0.0, 1.0]\nhohlraum_bottom/cyl2     22 elements  cylinder r = 1.550000000 mm, axis [0.0, 0.0, 1.0]\nhohlraum_bottom/rest    106 elements  flat\nwasher_top               21 elements  cylinder r = 1.550000000 mm, axis [0.0, -0.0, 1.0]\nwasher_bottom            22 elements  cylinder r = 1.550000000 mm, axis [-0.0, -0.0, 1.0]\ncapsule                 406 elements  sphere r = 0.900000000 mm at [0.0, -0.0, -0.0]\n\nThe three stages of one half are cut away as before: the CAD facets as read (dark where the front of a facet faces away from the viewer, which shows the mixed windings), the remeshed elements, and the fitted components.\n\nfunction stage_panel(pos, meshes, colorof, title; strokewidth = 0.25)\n    ax = Axis(pos; title, aspect = DataAspect(), limits = ((-3.1, 3.1), (-0.4, 5.6)))\n    hidedecorations!(ax); hidespines!(ax)\n    polys, cols = Vector{Vec3}[], Any[]\n    for (key, m) in meshes, p in eachindex(m.patches)\n        poly = patch_outline(m, p)\n        (key === :capsule || sum(v[2] for v in poly) > 0) && sum(v[3] for v in poly) > 0 || continue\n        push!(polys, poly)\n        push!(cols, colorof(key, m, p))\n    end\n    cutaway!(ax, polys, cols; strokewidth)\nend\nfunction facing(m, p)\n    a, b, c = (m.vertices[i] for i in m.triangles[first(m.patches[p])])\n    return dot(cross(b - a, c - a), Vec3(0, 1, -0.3)) < 0          # toward the viewer\nend\ntop = (:hohlraum_top, :washer_top, :capsule)\nraw = [Symbol(n) => m for (n, m) in solids if Symbol(n) in top]\nfig = Figure(size = (820, 330))\nstage_panel(fig[1, 1], raw, (k, m, p) -> facing(m, p) ? colors[String(k)] : :gray20, \"CAD facets as read\";\n            strokewidth = 0.05)\nstage_panel(fig[1, 2], [c.name => c.mesh for c in remeshed.target.components if c.name in top],\n            (k, m, p) -> colors[String(k)], \"remeshed elements\")\nkind_color(c) = c.surface isa CylinderSurface ? Makie.wong_colors()[c.surface.radius > 2 ? 1 : 3] :\n                c.surface isa SphereSurface ? Makie.wong_colors()[6] : :gray70\nfitted_top = [c for c in fitted if c.part in top]      # the pieces record their part\nstage_panel(fig[1, 3], [c.name => c.mesh for c in fitted_top],\n            (k, m, p) -> kind_color(fitted_top[findfirst(c -> c.name === k, fitted_top)]),\n            \"fitted: cylinders, sphere, flat\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Curved surfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#In-one-call","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"cad_target takes the parts as read and runs the same steps: it orients each part, selects the cavity around the point inside on the facets, remeshes the kept facets (sizes and materials can be given per part, as functions of the part name), selects again on the elements, and, since it remeshes, fits the curved surfaces on the kept facets (fit = false keeps flat elements). visrad_target makes this call for the CAD that a VisRad workspace bundles; components built otherwise, such as primitives, can be passed with the parts and take part in the selection as they are. The target it builds is the one built by hand above:\n\none_call = cad_target(filter(p -> !startswith(p[1], \"tent\"), solids); inside = (0, 0, 0),\n                      remesh = size_of, material = n -> n === :capsule ? ablator() : gold())\nby_hand = Target(fitted)\n@printf(\"%d elements in %d components (by hand: %d in %d); same names, areas and normals: %s\\n\",\n        nelements(one_call), length(one_call.components), nelements(by_hand), length(by_hand.components),\n        [c.name for c in one_call.components] == [c.name for c in by_hand.components] &&\n        one_call.elem_area == by_hand.elem_area && one_call.elem_normal == by_hand.elem_normal)\n1371 elements in 9 components (by hand: 1371 in 9); same names, areas and normals: true\n\nThe split is computational. Each piece records its part, and parts, elements(target, :hohlraum_top), the keep-outs, the clearance checks and the reports (energy_balance, component_power, write_results) take and show the parts as read; each piece stays reachable by name. A part that is one surface, as the capsule is, retains its name:\n\nprintln(parts(one_call))\none_call\nTarget with 5 parts (9 components), 1371 elements, 21070 triangles\n  hohlraum_top   surface  Au           457 elements  area  104.5296 mm²  (3 pieces)\n  hohlraum_bottom surface  Au           465 elements  area  104.5296 mm²  (3 pieces)\n  washer_top     surface  Au            21 elements  area    0.4869 mm²\n  washer_bottom  surface  Au            22 elements  area    0.4869 mm²\n  capsule        surface  ablator      406 elements  area   10.1788 mm²\n","title":"In one call"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#Openings-and-references","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"The cavity is closed with apertures where the radiation leaves: the two LEHs at the outer ends of the bores and the outer end of the waist gap. Apertures are transparent and record the flux through them. With them, the view factors of every element should sum to one, so the view factors can be balanced by Sinkhorn scaling.\n\nopenings = [aperture(:leh_top, disk_mesh(Rleh, zL; nr = 3, nphi = 24, up = false); surface = DiskSurface(center = (0, 0, zL))),\n            aperture(:leh_bottom, disk_mesh(Rleh, -zL; nr = 3, nphi = 24, up = true); surface = DiskSurface(center = (0, 0, -zL))),\n            aperture(:gap, cylinder_mesh(Rout, -g2, g2; nz = 1, nphi = 180, inward = true); surface = CylinderSurface(Rout))]\nflat = Target([remeshed.target.components; openings])\ncurved = Target([fitted; openings])\n\nA second CAD model takes the same steps at half the element size on the walls, 0.25 mm, and 0.1 mm on the capsule:\n\nfine = remesh_cavity(0.25, 0.1).target\ncurved_fine = Target([reduce(vcat, [curved_components(c; reference = facets[c.name]) for c in fine.components]);\n                      openings])\n@printf(\"%d elements\\n\", nelements(curved_fine))\n4725 elements\n\nThe reference is the same cavity built from Briareus's primitives on structured grids, every piece on its exact surface: the walls of the two halves, the end caps, the bores, the two faces of the waist gap and the capsule. The primitive hohlraum with the same dimensions is the thin-walled idealization of it, with flush LEHs and no gap.\n\nfunction primitives(; hw, hc)\n    nphi = 8 * ceil(Int, 2π * R / hw / 8)\n    nz, nr = ceil(Int, (h - g2) / hw), ceil(Int, (R - Rleh) / hw)\n    (; capsule_ntheta, capsule_nphi) = capsule_counts(rc, hc)\n    au = gold()\n    wall(z0, z1) = cylinder_mesh(R, z0, z1; nz, nphi, inward = true)\n    disk(z) = DiskSurface(center = (0, 0, z))\n    parts = [Component(:wall_top, wall(g2, h), au; surface = CylinderSurface(R)),\n             Component(:wall_bottom, wall(-h, -g2), au; surface = CylinderSurface(R)),\n             Component(:cap_top, annulus_mesh(Rleh, R, h; nr, nphi, up = false), au; surface = disk(h)),\n             Component(:cap_bottom, annulus_mesh(Rleh, R, -h; nr, nphi, up = true), au; surface = disk(-h)),\n             Component(:bore_top, cylinder_mesh(Rleh, h, zL; nz = 1, nphi = nphi ÷ 2), au; surface = CylinderSurface(Rleh)),\n             Component(:bore_bottom, cylinder_mesh(Rleh, -zL, -h; nz = 1, nphi = nphi ÷ 2), au; surface = CylinderSurface(Rleh)),\n             Component(:gap_top, annulus_mesh(R, Rout, g2; nr = 1, nphi, up = false), au; surface = disk(g2)),\n             Component(:gap_bottom, annulus_mesh(R, Rout, -g2; nr = 1, nphi, up = true), au; surface = disk(-g2)),\n             Component(:capsule, sphere_mesh(rc; ntheta = capsule_ntheta, nphi = capsule_nphi), ablator();\n                       surface = SphereSurface(rc))]\n    return Target([parts; openings])\nend\nmodels = [\"flat 0.5\" => flat, \"fitted 0.5\" => curved, \"prim. 0.5\" => primitives(hw = 0.5, hc = 0.15),\n          \"fitted 0.25\" => curved_fine, \"prim. 0.25\" => primitives(hw = 0.25, hc = 0.05),\n          \"hohlraum()\" => hohlraum(; length = 2h, radius = R, leh_radius = Rleh, capsule_radius = rc,\n                                   h = 0.25, capsule_h = 0.05)]","title":"Openings and references"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#Radiation-from-a-prescribed-wall-source","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"A diffusely emitting sphere sends its radiation to every surface that sees all of it as a point source at its center would, so the view factors of the capsule to the LEHs, the bores, the caps and the wall are the solid angles they subtend from the center over 4π: per end, (1 - costheta_L)2 for the LEH, with costheta_L = z_Lsqrtz_L^2 + r_L^2, and differences of such cosines for the bore, the cap and the wall. (The faces and the exit of the waist gap see the capsule through a 20 µm slot, partly hidden, so the identity does not hold for them.) Elements are grouped by where they are, so that the components of the CAD model and those of the primitives are compared alike.\n\nfunction group(t, e)\n    c = t.components[t.elem_comp[e]]\n    c.kind === :aperture && return startswith(String(c.name), \"leh\") ? :leh : :gap_exit\n    startswith(String(c.name), \"capsule\") && return :capsule\n    p, n = t.elem_centroid[e], t.elem_normal[e]\n    abs(n[3]) > 0.5 && return abs(p[3]) > h - 0.01 ? :cap : :gap_faces\n    return hypot(p[1], p[2]) < (Rleh + R) / 2 ? :bore : :wall\nend\ncosine(z, r) = z / hypot(z, r)\nsolid_angle_fraction = Dict(:leh => 1 - cosine(zL, Rleh), :bore => cosine(zL, Rleh) - cosine(h, Rleh),\n                            :cap => cosine(h, Rleh) - cosine(h, R), :wall => cosine(h, R) - cosine(g2, R))\n\nIn place of the laser, a prescribed source is put on the inner wall: two Gaussian rings per half, at z = 39 mm and 2.2 mm, standing in for the outer and inner cones' spots. Each element gets the source integrated over it by quadrature, so every model receives the same power wherever its elements are, and the gold converts 90% of it into x rays. A laser drive is handled the same way, with DepositionOperator tracing the beams onto the CAD elements, washers and bores included.\n\nsource(z) = exp(-((abs(z) - 3.9) / 0.8)^2) + 0.6 * exp(-((abs(z) - 2.2) / 0.8)^2)   # TW/mm²\nfunction drive(t)\n    P = zeros(nelements(t))\n    for e in 1:nelements(t)\n        group(t, e) === :wall || continue\n        points, weights, _ = element_quadrature(t, e, 2)\n        P[e] = sum(w * source(p[3]) for (p, w) in zip(points, weights))\n    end\n    return P\nend\n\nThe table below lists, for each model, the view factors and the time they take, the largest error of a row sum before balancing, and the view factors of the capsule to the LEHs, bores, caps and wall against the solid angles (the largest relative error). After Sinkhorn balancing it gives the radiosity solution: the mean incident flux on the capsule, its Legendre moments a_2a_0 and a_4a_0 (legendre_moments), and the fractions of the x-ray source lost through the LEHs and the gap (energy_balance).\n\nfunction analyze(t)\n    seconds = @elapsed F = view_factors(t)\n    groups = [group(t, e) for e in 1:nelements(t)]\n    ce = closure_error(F)\n    closure(gs) = maximum(abs(ce[e]) for e in eachindex(ce) if groups[e] in gs)\n    cap = findall(==(:capsule), groups)\n    A = t.elem_area\n    row(g) = sum(A[i] * F[i, j] for i in cap for j in findall(==(g), groups); init = 0.0) / sum(A[cap])\n    rows = :bore in groups ? maximum(abs(row(g) / f - 1) for (g, f) in solid_angle_fraction) : NaN\n    enforce_closure!(F, A)\n    sol = solve(RadiationModel(t; F), drive(t))\n    a = legendre_moments(sol.H, t, cap; lmax = 4)\n    b = energy_balance(sol)\n    lost(prefix) = sum((v for (k, v) in b.escaped if startswith(String(k), prefix)); init = 0.0) / b.source\n    return (; N = nelements(t), seconds, closure = closure((:wall, :cap, :capsule)),\n            closure_narrow = closure((:bore, :gap_faces, :leh)), rows, area = sum(A[cap]) / (4π * rc^2) - 1,\n            H = sum(sol.H[cap] .* A[cap]) / sum(A[cap]), P2 = a[3] / a[1], P4 = a[5] / a[1],\n            leh = lost(\"leh\"), gap = lost(\"gap\"), sol, cap, t)\nend\nresults = [name => analyze(t) for (name, t) in models]\nlet ref = results[5][2]\n    function rowfmt(label, f, fmt)\n        print(rpad(label, 34))\n        foreach(r -> print(lpad(Printf.format(Printf.Format(fmt), f(r[2])), 12)), results)\n        println()\n    end\n    print(rpad(\"\", 34)); foreach(r -> print(lpad(r[1], 12)), results); println()\n    rowfmt(\"elements\", r -> r.N, \"%d\")\n    rowfmt(\"view factors (s)\", r -> r.seconds, \"%.1f\")\n    rowfmt(\"|row sum - 1|: wall, caps, capsule\", r -> r.closure, \"%.1e\")\n    rowfmt(\"  bores, gap faces, LEHs\", r -> r.closure_narrow, \"%.1e\")\n    rowfmt(\"capsule rows / solid angles - 1\", r -> r.rows, \"%.1e\")\n    rowfmt(\"capsule area / 4πr² - 1\", r -> r.area, \"%.1e\")\n    rowfmt(\"capsule flux H (TW/mm²)\", r -> r.H, \"%.5f\")\n    rowfmt(\"H / H(prim. 0.25) - 1\", r -> r.H / ref.H - 1, \"%+.1e\")\n    rowfmt(\"a₂/a₀ (%)\", r -> 100r.P2, \"%+.3f\")\n    rowfmt(\"a₄/a₀ (%)\", r -> 100r.P4, \"%+.3f\")\n    rowfmt(\"LEH loss / source\", r -> r.leh, \"%.5f\")\n    rowfmt(\"gap loss / source\", r -> r.gap, \"%.5f\")\nend\n                                      flat 0.5  fitted 0.5   prim. 0.5 fitted 0.25  prim. 0.25  hohlraum()\nelements                                  1695        1695        2244        4725       11176       13120\nview factors (s)                           3.3         3.8         1.1         9.8        11.3        15.3\n|row sum - 1|: wall, caps, capsule     8.6e-03     8.6e-03     9.8e-03     9.6e-03     9.0e-03     1.3e-03\n  bores, gap faces, LEHs               7.0e-02     6.9e-02     6.0e-02     6.8e-02     6.1e-02     2.5e-04\ncapsule rows / solid angles - 1        1.2e-03     1.0e-03     3.7e-04     8.2e-04     4.3e-04         NaN\ncapsule area / 4πr² - 1               -1.3e-03    -2.2e-16     0.0e+00     4.4e-16    -2.2e-16    -2.2e-16\ncapsule flux H (TW/mm²)                1.01714     1.01731     1.01776     1.01607     1.01639     1.01457\nH / H(prim. 0.25) - 1                 +7.3e-04    +9.1e-04    +1.3e-03    -3.2e-04    +0.0e+00    -1.8e-03\na₂/a₀ (%)                              +10.574     +10.447     +10.541     +10.825     +10.880     +10.115\na₄/a₀ (%)                               -0.781      -0.793      -1.006      -0.807      -0.822      -0.631\nLEH loss / source                      0.25304     0.25288     0.25316     0.25396     0.25389     0.26235\ngap loss / source                      0.00207     0.00207     0.00159     0.00175     0.00161     0.00000\n\nThe columns are the CAD model with flat and with fitted elements at 0.5 mm (0.15 mm on the capsule), the primitives at the same sizes, the fitted CAD model and the primitives at 0.25 mm (0.1 and 0.05 mm on the capsule), and the thin-walled hohlraum at 0.25 mm.\n\nThe view factors of the capsule match the solid angles within 1.2e-3 in every model. The row sums of the wall, caps and capsule miss one by up to 1%, in the primitives as in the CAD, against 0.14% in hohlraum(); the narrow faces and the LEHs miss by up to 7%. These are the elements that see others partly, past the rim of a bore or into the gap, where a single line of sight determines visibility; balancing restores the rows.\nAt the same element size, the CAD models and the primitives agree on the capsule flux within 0.06%, on a_2a_0 within 0.1 percentage points, and on the LEH loss within 0.11%; at 0.25 mm within 0.03%, 0.05 points and 0.02%. Both move by more than that between 0.5 and 0.25 mm: a_2a_0 rises by 0.35 points (the primitives at 0.2 mm give 10.92%, so 0.25 mm is not yet converged in it).\na_4a_0 is set by the capsule mesh. The remeshed CAD capsule, with compact elements, gives −0.78% to −0.81% at every size tried (0.15 and 0.1 mm); the latitude-longitude capsule of the primitives gives −1.01% at 0.15 mm and −0.82% at 0.05 mm.\nFlat and fitted CAD agree on these integrated quantities within the same margins (the flat capsule is 0.13% short of its area). Flat facets misstate the exchange between neighbors, which sums out over a cavity, as the Integrating sphere tutorial shows. The view factors of the two models cost about the same: neighboring flat elements, folded across the wall strips, exchange by the exact double contour over their outlines (14 edges on average), and curved ones exchange point to point on their surface.\nThe thin-walled hohlraum() loses 3.3% more through its flush LEHs, and its a_2a_0 is 0.77 points lower: the 0.1 mm bores and the gap matter at this level, and the CAD model keeps them.\n\nThe incident flux on the capsule is plotted below against the cosine of its polar angle, per element for the CAD models. The capsule of the primitives is divided into latitude bands, on which the flux is the same at every azimuth: their band means, joined by straight lines, are the reference curves, and the right panel shows the difference of each model from the curve of the primitives at 0.25 mm.\n\nfunction band_curve(r)\n    μ = [round(normalize(r.t.elem_centroid[e])[3]; digits = 6) for e in r.cap]\n    bands = sort(unique(μ))\n    mean_H = [sum(r.sol.H[r.cap][μ .== b]) / count(==(b), μ) / r.H for b in bands]\n    return function (x)\n        bands[1] <= x <= bands[end] || return NaN                 # beyond the polar bands\n        k = min(searchsortedlast(bands, x), length(bands) - 1)\n        w = (x - bands[k]) / (bands[k+1] - bands[k])\n        return (1 - w) * mean_H[k] + w * mean_H[k+1]\n    end\nend\nreference = band_curve(results[5][2])\nfig = Figure(size = (820, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"cos θ on the capsule\", ylabel = \"H / ⟨H⟩\", title = \"Incident flux on the capsule\")\ndx = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"cos θ on the capsule\", ylabel = \"difference (%)\",\n          title = \"Difference from prim. 0.25\")\nfor (name, marker, markersize) in ((\"flat 0.5\", :rect, 5), (\"fitted 0.5\", :circle, 5), (\"fitted 0.25\", :circle, 3))\n    r = Dict(results)[name]\n    μ = [normalize(r.t.elem_centroid[e])[3] for e in r.cap]\n    y = r.sol.H[r.cap] ./ r.H\n    scatter!(ax, μ, y; marker, markersize, label = \"CAD, $name\")\n    scatter!(dx, μ, 100 .* (y .- reference.(μ)); marker, markersize)\nend\nμs = range(-1, 1; length = 401)\nfor (k, linestyle) in ((3, :dash), (5, :solid))\n    name, r = results[k]\n    curve = band_curve(r)\n    lines!(ax, μs, curve.(μs); color = :black, linestyle, label = name)\n    lines!(dx, μs, 100 .* (curve.(μs) .- reference.(μs)); color = :black, linestyle)\nend\naxislegend(ax; position = :ct)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nPer element, the CAD model at 0.25 mm follows the primitives at 0.25 mm within a few hundredths of a percent of the mean flux. At 0.5 mm the CAD models and the primitives depart from that curve alike, by up to 0.3% toward the poles. That departure comes from the resolution of the walls, which the CAD model shares with the primitives. Flat and fitted elements differ by less than that.","title":"Radiation from a prescribed wall source"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/cad_hohlraum/#What-this-checks","page":"Radiation in an imported CAD hohlraum","text":"orient_mesh repairs the windings: every part, read with 30% of its facets reversed, becomes a closed solid whose signed volume is its exact volume less the faceting (to 5e-4, the area deficit of a 120-gon).\nThe cavity selection is checked twice. From the capsule center, the cups of the tent seal a pocket with no open area. Without them, enclosure keeps exactly the surfaces of the cavity (inner walls, caps, bores, gap faces and capsule), and its open-area estimate matches the area of the LEHs plus the exit of the gap, less what the mount intercepts, within its sampling noise (0.6% at 512 rays per element).\nremesh_mesh turns 4.7 mm strips into elements as compact as squares where coarsen_mesh cannot, and conserves the area within round-off.\nfit_surfaces recovers the radii of the wall (2.875 mm), the bores (1.55 mm) and the capsule (0.9 mm) to nine digits from the CAD facets, and the fitted capsule has the area 4πr² within round-off.\ncad_target on the parts as read builds the target built by hand in one call, with the same components, element areas and normals, exactly; its parts are the parts as read, in order.\nIn the radiation solution, the view factors of the capsule to the LEHs, bores, caps and wall match the solid angles they subtend from its center to 1e-3. The capsule flux, its a_2a_0 and a_4a_0, and the LEH and gap losses of the CAD models, flat or fitted, match the same cavity built from Briareus's primitives at the same element size, within less than the change that halving the element size makes. The view factors of the flat and fitted models cost about the same. The thin-walled hohlraum differs from both by more: its LEH loss by 3.3% and its a_2a_0 by 0.8 points.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/verification-validation/#Verification-and-validation","page":"Verification and validation","text":"The tutorials and the test suite carry out four different activities, and their results mean different things.\n\nCode verification checks whether the implementation solves the intended discrete equations. Examples are exact view factors of catalog geometries, power conservation of the ray trace, symmetry selection rules that must hold within round-off, and derivatives checked against finite differences.\nSolution verification measures how large the discretization error of a given calculation is. Examples are the closure error of a view-factor matrix and its convergence with mesh refinement, and the agreement of the fast deposition operator with the ray trace.\nValidation tests whether the model reproduces an independent reference, either physical or analytic, for the intended use. Examples are the integrating-sphere multiplier, the exact convolution of a laser spot with its f-cone, the comparison of laser deposition with VisRad calculations, and the N221204 benchmark against published shot data.\nCalibration fits model parameters to reference data. The cross-beam energy transfer model is calibrated, not validated: its multiplier, flow and saturation clamp are fitted to measured transfer.\n\nThe closing section of each tutorial, \"What this checks\", lists which of these it carries out.","title":"Verification and validation"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/verification-validation/#Where-each-check-lives","page":"Verification and validation","text":"Check Activity Location\nBVH ray casting against brute force code verification test/runtests.jl, \"ray casting\"\nCoaxial-disk view factor, occlusion, point-to-polygon kernel code verification \"view factors\"\nIntegrating sphere, energy balance, closure enforcement validation, code verification \"radiosity\"; Integrating sphere\nLEH escape and capsule fractions against a Monte Carlo photon walk over LEH size (N210808 hohlraum); peak-power saving from 3.64 to 3.1 mm against N210307/N210808 code verification, approximate validation test/leh_power_balance.jl; Entrance-hole size and the power balance\nBeam power at any defocus; defocus second moments; oblique-incidence correction code verification \"beam profile\"; Laser spot on a foil\nDefocused footprint against the exact f-cone convolution validation Laser spot on a foil\nFast deposition against the ray trace, with shadowing and at grazing incidence far from focus solution verification \"deposition vs ray trace\"; Laser spot on a foil; Freeing a bundle for radiography\nLegendre and Y_lm recovery; facet integration code verification \"mode analysis\", \"spherical harmonics and 3D symmetry\"\nNIF D2 selection rule; superposition; influence matrix code verification \"spherical harmonics and 3D symmetry\"; Freeing a bundle for radiography\nGradients against finite differences code verification \"hohlraum + gradients\", \"CBET\"\nOptimizer against the closed-form least-squares solution code verification Freeing a bundle for radiography\nCBET coupling against an independent Im Γ computation code verification \"CBET\"\nCBET transfer against published points calibration calibrate_cbet\nTwo-zone CBET against the N221204 HYDRA transfer (PRE 109 Fig. 9, digitized): cone fraction over the pulse, per-cone outer ratios not fitted calibration test/cbet_zones.jl; examples/n221204_cbet_calibration.jl\nHard-sphere direct-drive illumination against Skupsky–Lee separability, Temporal et al. 2014 (one ring; LMJ), Murakami and Nishi 2017 validation, solution verification test/direct_drive.jl\nNIF polar-direct-drive pointing (Barlow et al. 2024's Optimized Config) against an independent plane-wave quadrature; σ₁ compared with Barlow's, not fitted solution verification, approximate validation Polar direct drive: a published NIF pointing\nClearance envelopes, wings, worst-case formulas code verification \"clearance\"\nDifferentiable entrance-hole keep-out against the clearance check code verification \"clearance\"; Freeing a bundle for radiography\nLaser deposition against VisRad 21 on NIF hohlraum workspaces calibration (beam profile), validation (deposition) research notes, \"VisRad deposition parity: hohlraum runs\"\nN221204 drive history against PRE 109 Fig. 10 and Dante (blind) approximate validation examples/n221204.jl; research notes, \"N221204 time-dependent benchmark\" and later sections","title":"Where each check lives"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/verification-validation/#Comparison-with-VisRad","page":"Verification and validation","text":"VisRad 21 computed the laser deposition on workspaces built from Briareus geometry: an N210808-like hohlraum (gold wall of radius 3.2 mm and length 11.24 mm, open LEHs, 2388 wall triangles of about 0.6 mm), its capsule, two window patches, a foil tilted 20° and LEH shields, with 192 NIF beams and VisRad's built-in phase plates. One workspace is a reference pointing, the other a re-pointing of it. Briareus read each workspace, built the same target and beams, and compared the Briareus deposition per element with the direct laser source VisRad wrote into the file.\n\nThe beam profile away from focus is the one piece taken from VisRad. VisRadProfile is the law VisRad uses. Its semi-axes, exponent, square blend and square half-width follow the parameters VisRad exports at each beam–target hit. Two constants, the exponent of the square and the cut-off, were fitted to VisRad's deposition on 48 plates normal to single beams, each plate within 0.22% rms per element. On surfaces that played no part in the fit, the law matches VisRad's deposition per element (elements above 1% of VisRad's peak) as follows:\n\non 32 plates tilted up to 53° and normal plates up to 15 mm past focus, to 0.12–0.27% rms;\non hohlraum walls, to 0.62% rms (N210808-like, reference pointing), 0.69% (re-pointing) and 0.45% on a 38 000-element wall; on window patches to 0.20%, and on a foil to 0.55%;\nin total wall power, which is 0.07% below VisRad's.\n\nWith the tabulated profile this law replaced, the absolute capsule P2 and P4 from the depositions of the two codes, through the same Briareus radiosity, agreed to 0.014 and 0.004 percentage points. The change of P2 from the re-pointing was 0.104% against VisRad's 0.092%.\n\nThe numbers, the setup and what remains open (VisRad's per-beam renormalization, and its shadowing, which is not along the beam axis) are in reference/research-notes.md. write_visrad_kit and compare_visrad set up the same kind of comparison for any case.","title":"Comparison with VisRad"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/verification-validation/#The-N221204-benchmark","page":"Verification and validation","text":"examples/n221204.jl runs the time-dependent drive of NIF shot N221204 (Kritcher et al., Phys. Rev. E 109, 025204 (2024)) on a mock-up of the HYBRID-E pointing, with the two-zone CBET model calibrated to Fig. 9 of that paper, and the wall motion, LEH closure and inner-beam bubble calibrated to other published data. Neither Fig. 10 of the paper (the capsule flux P2/P0 against time) nor the measured Dante history is a fit target. On the default settings (coarse mesh, measured backscatter), the P2 swing reaches −0.181 at 5.25 ns and turns positive at 6.19 ns, against −0.170 at 5.6 ns and 6.67 ns in Fig. 10. The synthetic Dante 1 at 8 ns gives 290.9 and 314.2 eV over the initial and open LEH areas, against the measured 310.1 eV. After the laser falls, the synthetic Dante is 37–54 eV low at 9 ns on three shots, because the quasi-static radiosity has no plasma energy store. The research notes record each run and the inputs that remain unconstrained.","title":"The N221204 benchmark"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/io/#Input-and-output","page":"Input and output","text":"Beam tables in a canonical CSV (write_beam_table, read_beam_table), with column and unit mapping for foreign tables; STL (read_stl, read_stl_solids, write_stl); and write_results, which exports per-element laser, radiosity and flux, per-component and per-beam power, and the Legendre and Y_lm modes of the capsule as CSV for postprocessing. VisRad files are on VisRad interchange.","title":"Input and output"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.BEAM_COLUMNS","page":"Input and output","text":"Column names of the canonical beam table (see write_beam_table).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.BEAM_COLUMNS"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.read_beam_table-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Input and output","text":"read_beam_table(path; columns = Dict(), scale = Dict(), defaults = (;), template = nothing)\n    -> Vector{Beam}\n\nRead beams from a CSV. Canonical column names (see write_beam_table) are recognized case-insensitively. For foreign tables, columns maps a canonical field to the column name in the file, e.g. Dict(:aim_x => \"X (cm)\", :theta => \"Theta\"), and scale multiplies a field after reading, e.g. Dict(:aim_x => 10, :aim_y => 10, :aim_z => 10) for cm → mm. Fields missing from the file come from defaults (a NamedTuple of canonical fields). When template is a beam vector, they come instead from the template beam with the same name, so that a pointing table can update a facility beam set. Otherwise they take the Beam defaults. A field that is not a number throws an ArgumentError that names the line and column of the file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_beam_table"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.read_csv-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Input and output","text":"read_csv(path; comment = '#') -> (header::Vector{String}, rows::Vector{Vector{String}})\n\nCSV reader without dependencies (RFC 4180 quoting). A field in double quotes may contain the delimiter, line breaks and the comment character, with \"\" for a quote. Its text is kept as quoted, while unquoted fields are stripped of surrounding blanks. Lines that are blank or start with comment (after blanks, outside a quoted field) are skipped. Tab- or semicolon-separated files are detected from the header line. A row with fewer fields than the header, a row with more (other than empty trailing fields), and a quoted field left open at the end of the file throw an ArgumentError with the line number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_csv"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.read_stl-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Input and output","text":"read_stl(path; scale = 1.0) -> Mesh\n\nTriangle mesh from an STL file (binary or ASCII), one patch per facet, with vertex coordinates multiplied by scale. STL files do not specify units, so scale is 1 for millimeters, 10 for centimeters and 25.4 for inches. Facet normals in the file are ignored, and the winding determines the front side, as STL specifies (counterclockwise seen from outside). Exported CAD often mixes windings, so the mesh should be passed through orient_mesh, and through coarsen_mesh before view factors.\n\nAll facets of the file go into one mesh, also when an ASCII file contains several solid … endsolid blocks, one per CAD body, as many exporters write them. read_stl_solids keeps them apart. A binary file is recognized by its size, 84 + 50n bytes for the n facets that its header announces. Binary files whose header begins with \"solid\", as SolidWorks writes them, are therefore read as binary. ASCII keywords are read in any case, with any whitespace and line endings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_stl"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.read_stl_solids-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Input and output","text":"read_stl_solids(path; scale = 1.0) -> Vector{Pair{String,Mesh}}\n\nThe solids of an STL file as name => mesh pairs in file order, each mesh as read_stl reads it (one patch per facet, coordinates multiplied by scale). An ASCII file gives one pair per solid <name> … endsolid block, named by the rest of its solid line. The k-th solid is named \"solid<k>\" when that line is blank, and repeated names are kept as they are. An empty solid gives an empty mesh. A binary file does not have solids, and it gives one pair named after the file. A target is built from the parts with, for example, Target([Component(n, orient_mesh(m), gold()) for (n, m) in read_stl_solids(path)]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_stl_solids"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.write_beam_table-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"Input and output","text":"write_beam_table(path, beams) -> path\n\nWrite beams as CSV with the canonical columns name, quad, group, theta, phi, power, aim_x, aim_y, aim_z, defocus, spot_a, spot_b, spot_order, spot_angle, spot_halo, spot_halo_scale, spot_halo_order, fnumber, wavelength, dlambda (mm, TW, degrees, µm, Å). The hohlraum-cylindrical aim coordinates aim_r (toward the port azimuth) and aim_phi_offset (tangential) are appended for reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_beam_table"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.write_element_table-Tuple{AbstractString, Target, Vararg{Pair}}","page":"Input and output","text":"write_element_table(path, target, fields...; elems = all) -> path\n\nPer-element CSV: index, component, side, centroid (mm), normal, area (mm²), polar and azimuthal angle of the centroid (degrees, about TCC), then one column per name => values pair in fields (e.g. :H => sol.H, :laser => laser_flux(dep, t)). On a target with parts split for computation (curved_components), a part column follows component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_element_table"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.write_moments-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector}","page":"Input and output","text":"write_moments(path, moments; label = \"a\") -> path\n\nWrite Legendre moments aₗ and aₗ/a₀ (vector) or a time history (matrix (lmax+1) × nt with times) as CSV.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_moments"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.write_results-Tuple{AbstractString, Target, Deposition}","page":"Input and output","text":"write_results(dir, target, dep; sol = nothing, beams = nothing, observe = nothing,\n              lmax = 8) -> Vector{String}\n\nWrite the results of a calculation to the folder dir as CSV files, for postprocessing or comparison with another code, and return their paths:\n\nelements.csv — per element (write_element_table): absorbed laser power (TW) and intensity (W/cm²), and with a radiosity solution sol the x-ray source, radiosity and incident flux (W/cm²), albedo and transmitted fraction;\ncomponents.csv — per part: absorbed laser power (TW, front and the unmodeled back side), and with sol the x-ray power absorbed, escaped (apertures) and transmitted (thin foils, gaps) from energy_balance. A part is a component unless it was split for computation by curved_components, in which case its pieces are summed;\nbeams.csv — with beams (in the order of the deposition): the name, port angles, and delivered and landed power (TW) of each beam;\nlegendre.csv and ylm.csv — with observe (part or component names) and sol: the Legendre moments aₗ (write_moments) and the real spherical-harmonic coefficients c_lm of the observed incident flux, with their ratios to the mean term;\nsummary.txt — totals (delivered, landed, missed; x-ray source, lost).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_results"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/io/#Briareus.write_stl-Tuple{AbstractString, Any}","page":"Input and output","text":"write_stl(path, x; binary = true, scale = 1.0, apertures = true, name = \"briareus\") -> path\n\nWrite x as an STL file for CAD or another code. x is a Mesh, a Component, a Target, or a vector of components or of name => mesh pairs. Every triangle becomes a facet whose normal is computed from its winding, toward the front side. Vertex coordinates are divided by scale, since STL files do not specify units: 1 writes millimeters, 10 centimeters and 25.4 inches. read_stl(path; scale) then reads the same geometry back.\n\nbinary = true writes binary STL with Float32 coordinates, so a coordinate of size L moves by up to about 6e-8 L. Binary STL has no solid names, so the header lists them. binary = false writes ASCII STL with one solid <name> … endsolid <name> block per component (or pair), in order, at full Float64 precision. read_stl_solids reads them back as named parts, and whitespace in a name is written as _. A single mesh is named name.\n\nA two-sided component is written once, wound toward its front side. Apertures, which are laser entrance holes and other open windows transparent to light, are written as separate solids so that a CAD user sees them. apertures = false leaves them out, e.g. for a code that would take every facet for a wall. A closing lip (aperture(...; closure), hohlraum(; leh_closure)) is a plasma model and is not a part. A lip named <hole>_lip is written in the solid of its aperture <hole>, so the entrance hole comes out whole, at its full radius. A lip without its hole is written as a separate solid. The exact CurvedSurface of a component is not written, only its facets, and the patch structure is lost, since read_stl returns one patch per triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_stl"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beam_errors/#Pointing-and-power-errors","page":"Pointing and power errors","text":"No two shots point their beams the same way. Each NIF beam lands within about 50 µm (rms) of where it was aimed, and the powers of the beams differ from their requested values by a few percent. These errors are independent from beam to beam, so they do not cancel the way the symmetric design does. They leave a random low-mode asymmetry in the drive, and the mode-1 part of it, a flux difference between opposite sides of the capsule, displaces the hot spot by about 100 km/s per percent of mode-1 flux (MacGowan et al., High Energy Density Physics 40, 100944, 2021).\n\nThis tutorial computes how much asymmetry such errors leave in the drive of a NIF hohlraum, checks the linear estimate against a Monte Carlo of full evaluations, and uses it two ways: to infer the errors behind a measured asymmetry, and to design a pointing that is less sensitive to them. It introduces mode_sensitivity, mode_uncertainty, sample_modes, infer_errors and the uncertainty keyword of the symmetry objectives.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing Printf\nusing Random\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\nset_theme!(fontsize = 15, linewidth = 2.5)","title":"Pointing and power errors"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beam_errors/#The-hohlraum","page":"Pointing and power errors","text":"The hohlraum and pointing are those of the Freeing a bundle for radiography tutorial: 10.1 × 5.75 mm with 3.1 mm entrance holes and a 0.9 mm capsule, and 48 quads at 8 TW with their phase-plate spots. The outer cones are aimed on the axis near the entrance-hole planes and the inner cones across the hohlraum. The mesh is coarser than the default to keep the tutorial quick.\n\ntarget = hohlraum(length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9,\n                  wall = gold(albedo = 0.85, conversion = 0.8), h = 0.45, capsule_h = 0.12)\np = GroupPointing(nif_quads(power = 8.0); fields = (:z, :r))\nexample = Dict(:outer50 => (5.01, 0.0), :outer44 => (5.20, 0.0),\n               :inner30 => (3.63, -0.60), :inner23 => (3.09, -1.09))\nx0 = reduce(vcat, [[example[k]...] for k in p.keys])\nquads = apply(p, x0)\nscenario = Scenario(target, quads; observe = :capsule, viewfactor_kw = (; closed = true))\n@printf(\"%d elements, %d quads\\n\", nelements(target), length(quads))\n3396 elements, 48 quads","title":"The hohlraum"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beam_errors/#How-the-errors-of-each-beam-move-the-drive","page":"Pointing and power errors","text":"The incident flux on the capsule is written in real spherical harmonics, c_lm (see the radiography tutorial), and its shape as c_lmc_00. The errors of a beam are its displacement transverse to the beam, along the two axes of its elliptical spot, and a fractional power error. The deposition of a beam depends on the errors of that beam only, so mode_sensitivity differentiates the contribution of every beam with three dual numbers in one pass, exactly. The sensitivities are compared below against central differences of full evaluations:\n\nlmax = 4\nsens = mode_sensitivity(scenario, quads; lmax)\nshape(bs) = (c = flux_ylm(evaluate(scenario, bs); lmax); c ./ c[1])\nworst = 0.0\nfor (bi, k) in ((3, 1), (17, 2), (30, 3), (44, 1))\n    b = quads[bi]\n    e1, e2 = spot_axes(b)\n    move(δ) = k == 3 ? remake(b; power = b.power * (1 + δ)) :\n              remake(b; aim = b.aim + δ * (k == 1 ? e1 : e2))\n    h = 1e-4\n    fd = (shape([j == bi ? move(h) : quads[j] for j in eachindex(quads)]) .-\n          shape([j == bi ? move(-h) : quads[j] for j in eachindex(quads)])) ./ 2h\n    global worst = max(worst, maximum(abs, fd .- sens.J[:, 3(bi-1)+k]) / maximum(abs, fd))\nend\n@printf(\"largest relative difference from central differences: %.1e\\n\", worst)\nlargest relative difference from central differences: 2.2e-09\n\nThe columns are the response of the drive to each error. For mode 1, the pointing columns of the inner quads, whose spots fall on the wall across from their ports near the waist, are the largest:\n\nl1 = 2:4                                      # c_1,-1, c_10, c_11\nper_beam(k) = [norm(sens.J[l1, 3(b-1)+k]) for b in eachindex(quads)]\nfor g in (:inner23, :inner30, :outer44, :outer50)\n    idx = findall(b -> b.group === g, quads)\n    @printf(\"%-8s |∂c₁/∂(pointing)| %.3f per mm, |∂c₁/∂(power)| %.3f per unit fraction\\n\", g,\n            sum(per_beam(1)[idx] .+ per_beam(2)[idx]) / 2length(idx), sum(per_beam(3)[idx]) / length(idx))\nend\ninner23  |∂c₁/∂(pointing)| 0.008 per mm, |∂c₁/∂(power)| 0.011 per unit fraction\ninner30  |∂c₁/∂(pointing)| 0.008 per mm, |∂c₁/∂(power)| 0.012 per unit fraction\nouter44  |∂c₁/∂(pointing)| 0.003 per mm, |∂c₁/∂(power)| 0.009 per unit fraction\nouter50  |∂c₁/∂(pointing)| 0.003 per mm, |∂c₁/∂(power)| 0.008 per unit fraction","title":"How the errors of each beam move the drive"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beam_errors/#The-asymmetry-the-errors-leave","page":"Pointing and power errors","text":"With independent errors of rms size sigma_j, the covariance of the shape is Jmathrmdiag(sigma_j^2)J^mathsfT to first order. mode_uncertainty takes the rms pointing as the length of the displacement (sigmasqrt 2 along each axis) and the rms fractional power, and returns, for each degree l, the rms of sqrtsum_m (delta c_lmc_00)^2, the share of mode l the errors use up. For NIF's 50 µm pointing and a 3% power imbalance:\n\nerrors = (; pointing = 0.05, power = 0.03)\nu = mode_uncertainty(scenario, quads; errors..., lmax)\nfor l in 1:lmax\n    @printf(\"l = %d: rms %.3f%% of the mean flux\\n\", l, 100 * u.rms_mode[l])\nend\npointing_only = mode_uncertainty(scenario, quads; pointing = 0.05, power = 0.0, lmax)\n@printf(\"mode 1 from pointing alone %.3f%%, from power alone %.3f%%\\n\", 100 * pointing_only.rms_mode[1],\n        100 * mode_uncertainty(scenario, quads; pointing = 0.0, power = 0.03, lmax).rms_mode[1])\nl = 1: rms 0.279% of the mean flux\nl = 2: rms 0.212% of the mean flux\nl = 3: rms 0.132% of the mean flux\nl = 4: rms 0.033% of the mean flux\nmode 1 from pointing alone 0.199%, from power alone 0.196%\n\nThe linear estimate holds while the errors are small against the spot scale. A Monte Carlo of full evaluations, each with fresh random errors on every beam (sample_modes), is the independent check:\n\nsamples = sample_modes(scenario, quads; errors..., n = 300, lmax, rng = MersenneTwister(2026))\nmc = [sqrt(sum(abs2, samples[l^2+1:(l+1)^2, :] .- u.c[l^2+1:(l+1)^2]) / size(samples, 2))\n      for l in 1:lmax]\nfor l in 1:lmax\n    @printf(\"l = %d: linear %.3f%%, Monte Carlo %.3f%% (ratio %.2f)\\n\", l, 100u.rms_mode[l],\n            100mc[l], mc[l] / u.rms_mode[l])\nend\nl = 1: linear 0.279%, Monte Carlo 0.277% (ratio 0.99)\nl = 2: linear 0.212%, Monte Carlo 0.208% (ratio 0.98)\nl = 3: linear 0.132%, Monte Carlo 0.131% (ratio 0.99)\nl = 4: linear 0.033%, Monte Carlo 0.033% (ratio 0.99)\n\nThey agree to the sampling error of 300 shots, a few percent. The mode-1 amplitude over the samples is shown below as a hot-spot velocity budget at 100 km/s per percent:\n\nε1 = [sqrt(3 * sum(abs2, samples[2:4, k])) for k in axes(samples, 2)]   # mode_amplitude\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 330))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"mode-1 amplitude ε₁ (%)\", ylabel = \"shots\",\n          title = \"Mode 1 from 50 µm pointing and 3% power errors\")\nhist!(ax, 100 .* ε1; bins = 30, color = (:steelblue, 0.8))\nvlines!(ax, [100 * sqrt(3) * u.rms_mode[1]]; color = :black, linestyle = :dash)\nfig\nFigure()\n\n@printf(\"ε₁: median %.2f%%, 90th percentile %.2f%% (≈ %.0f km/s); linear rms √3 × %.3f%% = %.2f%%\\n\",\n        100 * sort(ε1)[end÷2], 100 * sort(ε1)[9end÷10], 100 * 100 * sort(ε1)[9end÷10],\n        100 * u.rms_mode[1], 100 * sqrt(3) * u.rms_mode[1])\nε₁: median 0.43%, 90th percentile 0.67% (≈ 67 km/s); linear rms √3 × 0.279% = 0.48%","title":"The asymmetry the errors leave"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beam_errors/#Correlated-errors","page":"Pointing and power errors","text":"Some errors are shared. The beams of a cone can all be off the same way, every beam of a quad can carry the same power error, and a target displaced from chamber center moves every aim by the same vector relative to the target. mode_uncertainty takes these as group_pointing and group_power, with groups a beam field such as :quad or a function of the beam, and positioning, an rms length. A shared displacement is transverse to the mean beam direction of the group, so a group must have one. The beams of a cone in one hemisphere have one; the whole cone (groups = :group) does not. A shared displacement also moves beams along themselves (their focus moves with the aim), so these errors propagate through a fourth per-beam sensitivity. rms_by splits the budget by source. The errors here are 50 µm pointing per beam, 20 µm shared per cone in each hemisphere and 30 µm target positioning:\n\ncone(b) = (b.group, b.port[3] > 0)\nshared = (; pointing = 0.05, group_pointing = 0.02, groups = cone, positioning = 0.03)\nus = mode_uncertainty(scenario, quads; shared..., lmax)\nfor l in 1:lmax\n    @printf(\"l = %d: total %.3f%% = beams %.3f, cones %.3f, positioning %.3f (in quadrature)\\n\", l,\n            100us.rms_mode[l], 100us.rms_by.beam[l], 100us.rms_by.group[l], 100us.rms_by.positioning[l])\nend\nss = sample_modes(scenario, quads; shared..., n = 200, lmax, rng = MersenneTwister(7))\n@printf(\"Monte Carlo / linear, l = 1..4: %s\\n\",\n        join((@sprintf(\"%.2f\", sqrt(sum(abs2, ss[l^2+1:(l+1)^2, :] .- us.c[l^2+1:(l+1)^2]) / size(ss, 2)) /\n                                us.rms_mode[l]) for l in 1:lmax), \" \"))\nl = 1: total 0.250% = beams 0.199, cones 0.077, positioning 0.131 (in quadrature)\nl = 2: total 0.183% = beams 0.175, cones 0.053, positioning 0.002 (in quadrature)\nl = 3: total 0.130% = beams 0.117, cones 0.032, positioning 0.047 (in quadrature)\nl = 4: total 0.033% = beams 0.031, cones 0.010, positioning 0.003 (in quadrature)\nMonte Carlo / linear, l = 1..4: 1.01 1.01 1.02 1.00\n\nA shared displacement adds coherently over the beams it moves: 20 µm shared by each half-cone gives about 40% of the l = 1 budget of 50 µm scattered independently over every beam. The two halves of a cone move independently, so they also feed l = 2. The target offset is a translation of the whole drive pattern: it feeds the odd modes (l = 1 3) and leaves l = 2 unchanged.","title":"Correlated errors"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beam_errors/#Reading-the-errors-back-from-a-measured-mode","page":"Pointing and power errors","text":"The inverse question comes after a shot: the implosion showed a mode-1 asymmetry; which errors could have caused it? Trending measured low modes against beam and target data over many shots, as MacGowan et al. (2021) did for NIF's mode 1, starts from this question for each shot. infer_errors answers it linearly: with the measured departure Delta c (measured minus nominal c_lmc_00), its measurement error, and the same error priors as mode_uncertainty, it returns the most probable errors and their posterior spread.\n\nIn a synthetic measurement the target is 30 µm off center along x, so every aim moves 30 µm along +x relative to it. The \"measured\" l = 1 comes from a full evaluation, with an assumed measurement error of 0.01 percentage points per component. The prior allows every error source of the previous section:\n\nprior = shared\noffset = [0.03, 0.0, 0.0]\nmoved = [remake(b; aim = b.aim + offset) for b in quads]\nc_nom, c_meas = shape(quads), shape(moved)\nΔc(l) = c_meas[l^2+1:(l+1)^2] .- c_nom[l^2+1:(l+1)^2]\ninv1 = infer_errors(scenario, quads, (1 => Δc(1),); sigma = 1e-4, prior...)\npos(r) = join((@sprintf(\"%+.1f ± %.1f\", 1000r.positioning[i], 1000r.sigma[end-3+i]) for i in 1:3), \", \")\n@printf(\"measured l = 1: positioning (µm) %s; %.2f of 3 combinations resolved\\n\", pos(inv1), inv1.resolved)\nfor t in inv1.sources[1:4]\n    @printf(\"  %-28s explains %5.1f%%\\n\", t.source, 100t.fraction)\nend\nmeasured l = 1: positioning (µm) +6.9 ± 15.2, -0.0 ± 15.1, -0.0 ± 14.3; 2.98 of 3 combinations resolved\n  positioning                  explains  23.0%\n  (:inner23, false) pointing   explains   5.6%\n  (:inner23, true) pointing    explains   5.6%\n  Q24T pointing                explains   4.3%\n\nThe direction is right, and the positioning is the largest single source, but it accounts for only a quarter of the measurement and its estimate falls well short of 30 µm. Three numbers do not separate a target offset from the same mode-1 pattern made by the 96 independent beam displacements and the shared displacements of the cones. The prior allows all of them, so the most probable explanation spreads the blame over them. l = 3, which a translation also drives, separates them a little further:\n\ninv13 = infer_errors(scenario, quads, (1 => Δc(1), 3 => Δc(3)); sigma = 1e-4, prior...)\n@printf(\"measured l = 1, 3: positioning (µm) %s; %.2f of 10 resolved\\n\", pos(inv13), inv13.resolved)\nfor t in inv13.sources[1:3]\n    @printf(\"  %-28s explains %5.1f%%\\n\", t.source, 100t.fraction)\nend\n@printf(\"posterior/prior rms: positioning %.2f %.2f %.2f; median over all %d errors %.3f\\n\",\n        (inv13.sigma[end-2:end] ./ inv13.prior_sigma[end-2:end])..., length(inv13.x),\n        sort(inv13.sigma ./ inv13.prior_sigma)[end÷2])\nmeasured l = 1, 3: positioning (µm) +10.7 ± 13.9, +0.0 ± 13.7, -0.0 ± 8.2; 9.50 of 10 resolved\n  positioning                  explains  35.6%\n  Q33B pointing                explains   7.6%\n  Q42T pointing                explains   7.6%\nposterior/prior rms: positioning 0.80 0.79 0.47; median over all 163 errors 0.989\n\nTen numbers determine at most ten combinations of the errors, and most of the errors keep their prior spread. The answer is only as specific as the prior. If the beams are known to have pointed well (their errors measured, say), a prior on only the positioning recovers the offset from l = 1 by itself:\n\ninv_pos = infer_errors(scenario, quads, (1 => Δc(1),); sigma = 1e-4, pointing = 0.0,\n                       positioning = 0.03)\n@printf(\"positioning-only prior: positioning (µm) %s; residual %.4f%% of |Δc| %.4f%%\\n\",\n        pos(inv_pos), 100norm(inv_pos.residual), 100norm(inv_pos.measured))\npositioning-only prior: positioning (µm) +29.2 ± 2.8, -0.0 ± 2.8, -0.0 ± 1.7; residual 0.0028% of |Δc| 0.1051%\n\nThe estimate is 29 ± 3 µm against the true 30. With the broad prior, the posterior rms of the positioning fell only to 0.8 of its prior along x, while the median error kept 99% of its prior spread. The measurement constrains any one error only weakly unless the rest are held small by the prior. For the broad-prior case, the inferred errors applied to the beams (beams) reproduce the measurement in a full evaluation:\n\nc_back = shape(inv13.beams)\nrows13 = [mode_index(l, m) for (l, m) in inv13.components]\n@printf(\"|Δc| %.4f%%, |prediction J x̂ − Δc| %.4f%%, full evaluation − prediction %.4f%%\\n\",\n        100norm(inv13.measured), 100norm(inv13.residual),\n        100norm(c_back[rows13] .- c_nom[rows13] .- inv13.predicted))\n|Δc| 0.1166%, |prediction J x̂ − Δc| 0.0021%, full evaluation − prediction 0.0003%","title":"Reading the errors back from a measured mode"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beam_errors/#Does-the-pointing-change-the-budget?","page":"Pointing and power errors","text":"The share of each mode taken by the errors depends on where the spots are placed: a spot on a steep part of the drive response moves the shape more for the same displacement. First the cones are tuned to null l = 1–4 at fixed drive, with aim and power per cone, aims within 1.5 mm axially and 1 mm radially, and powers within 40%. Then the budgets of the example and the tuned designs are compared:\n\np3 = GroupPointing(quads; fields = (:z, :r, :power))\ns3 = Scenario(target, p3; observe = :capsule, viewfactor_kw = (; closed = true))\nx3 = initial_parameters(p3)\na0 = mean_flux(evaluate(s3, x3))\nlo, hi = bounds(p3, x3; z = 1.5, r = 1.0, power = 0.4)\nkeepout = (ApertureKeepOut(target), SphereKeepOut(target))\nmodes = (1 => 1.0, 2 => 1.0, 3 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0)\ntuned = optimize_pointing(s3, symmetry_objective(; modes, odd = 0.0, drive = a0, keepout);\n                          x0 = x3, lower = lo, upper = hi, iterations = 200)\nfunction budget_row(label, x)\n    ev = evaluate(s3, x)\n    a = flux_moments(ev; lmax = 4)\n    ur = mode_uncertainty(s3, ev.beams; errors..., lmax)\n    @printf(\"  %-10s %+7.3f%%  %+7.3f%%          %s\n\", label, 100a[3] / a[1], 100a[5] / a[1],\n            join((@sprintf(\"%.3f%%\", 100x) for x in ur.rms_mode), \"  \"))\n    return ur.rms_mode\nend\nprintln(\"  design      P₂/P₀     P₄/P₀    rms from the errors: l = 1     2      3      4\")\nbudget_row(\"example\", x3)\ntuned_budget = budget_row(\"tuned\", tuned.x);\n  design      P₂/P₀     P₄/P₀    rms from the errors: l = 1     2      3      4\n  example    -14.578%   +0.136%          0.279%  0.212%  0.132%  0.033%\n  tuned       -0.044%   +0.107%          0.234%  0.159%  0.092%  0.022%\n\nThe tuned design leaves less to the errors: l = 1 falls from 0.28% to 0.23–0.24%, with the third digit depending on rounding in the tuning run. It has also moved power from the inner cones, whose errors weigh most per beam (above), to the outer ones. With uncertainty, symmetry_objective instead minimizes the expected value of the square of each mode over the errors, the nominal value plus the variance they add, at about three times the cost per evaluation through nested dual numbers. The run below starts from the tuned design:\n\nrobust = optimize_pointing(s3, symmetry_objective(; modes, odd = 0.0, drive = a0, keepout,\n                                                  uncertainty = errors);\n                           x0 = tuned.x, lower = lo, upper = hi, iterations = 30)\nrobust_budget = budget_row(\"robust\", robust.x)\n@printf(\"largest change of a cone parameter: %.3f; of the l = 1 budget: %+.1f%%\n\",\n        maximum(abs, robust.x .- tuned.x), 100 * (robust_budget[1] / tuned_budget[1] - 1))\n  robust      -0.026%   +0.092%          0.234%  0.159%  0.092%  0.022%\nlargest change of a cone parameter: 0.020; of the l = 1 budget: +0.0%\n\nThe robust objective barely moves the tuned design: within these bounds, where the cones point hardly changes how much asymmetry independent beam errors leave. These errors set a floor, about a quarter of a percent of mode 1 in this hohlraum, that only better pointing accuracy and power balance can lower.","title":"Does the pointing change the budget?"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/beam_errors/#What-this-checks","page":"Pointing and power errors","text":"The per-beam sensitivities from dual numbers match central differences of full evaluations (printed above; the test suite checks them to 1e-6).\nThe linear propagation of independent errors matches a Monte Carlo of full evaluations to its sampling error in every mode, and so does the propagation of the shared (cone and target-positioning) errors. The test suite also checks that a group of one beam reproduces the independent errors exactly, and that a shared target displacement matches central differences.\nWith a positioning-only prior, the inverse recovers a known 30 µm target offset from the mode 1 of a full evaluation (29 ± 3 µm, printed above), and a full evaluation of the inferred errors departs from their linear prediction by 0.0003 percentage points. The test suite also recovers the shared displacement of a cone in one hemisphere, checks the data-space posterior against the dense solve in error space, (Sigma_p^-1 + J^mathsfTSigma_m^-1J)^-1, to 1e-8, and checks that full evaluations of the inferred beams depart from Jhat x only at second order.\nThe robust objective adds exactly the propagated variance to the nominal one (test suite), and its gradients, through nested dual numbers, match finite differences.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Diagnostic-definitions","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"A synthetic diagnostic is described by a definition file: one TOML file per instrument, holding its line of sight, its hardware and its recording, each value with its source. The public definitions ship with the package (data/diagnostics); a user group keeps separate definitions, including details that are not public, in directories on a search path, and modifies a public definition by extending it; the public file is not copied. diagnostic builds the instrument of a definition (for the soft x-ray power kind, a DanteView for dante); check_diagnostics reports every problem in the files on the search path, by file and key.","title":"Diagnostic definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/diagnostics/#The-search-path","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostic_paths lists the directories read, in order of precedence: the built-in definitions, the directories saved in the user configuration file (diagnostic_config_path, key diagnostics.paths), those of the environment variable BRIAREUS_DIAGNOSTICS (for batch jobs; ;-separated on Windows, :-separated elsewhere) and those added for the current session. Directories are read recursively. A definition later on the path shadows an earlier one with the same id, with a warning naming both files. A directory is added once and kept for later sessions with\n\nadd_diagnostic_path(\"//groupserver/briareus/diagnostics\")\n\nand remove_diagnostic_path removes it. The built-in constructors (dante1, dante1_responses) read the built-in definitions only, so user files do not change them; diagnostic(\"nif_dante1\") takes the search path.","title":"The search path"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/diagnostics/#A-definition-file","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"Every file has a header: schema (the format version, DIAGNOSTIC_SCHEMA), id, and either kind or extends; optionally name, facility, instrument, public (false for non-public detail), valid_from and valid_to (dates), shots, and the source keys source, confidence (H, M or L, as in the research notes) and note, which every table may also carry. instrument names the instrument a definition is one configuration of: the Dante 1 channel sets of several years and shots all carry instrument = \"NIF Dante 1\", and a file that extends one of them inherits it (diagnostic_instrument). A list of diagnostics, such as the Diagnostics tab of the desktop application, shows each instrument once for the facility of the session and offers its definitions as configurations. Keys are checked: an unknown key or a value of the wrong type is an error naming the file and the key. Units are in the key names (theta_deg, energy_eV, thickness_um, solid_angle_sr).\n\nThe kind xray_power (Dante, Sentinel) takes:\n\nTable Keys\n[line_of_sight] (required) port, a NIF diagnostic port by name (\"P143-274\", with facility = \"NIF\"; the angles of NIF_DIAGNOSTIC_PORTS, the User Guide's Table 5-1), or theta_deg and phi_deg: the direction of the instrument from TCC\n[view] field_of_view_mm (the collimated field, a disk of this diameter normal to the line of sight; without it, only the aperture through is viewed), center_part or center_mm (the field center, at the centroid of a part or a point; default TCC), through (the aperture looked through, and the reference for Tr; default leh_bottom without a field of view, none with one), pixels (rays per side; default 400), area_mm2 (the area Tr is quoted over; default the initial area of the aperture), area_part (Tr over the visible projected area of that part), area_open = true (Tr over the open area of the hole at each time, from the entrance-hole closure of the model) or area_history_csv (a measured area history, time in ns and area in mm²)\n[recording] mode = \"history\"\n[[channel]] (one per channel) number and solid_angle_sr (required); energy_eV (nominal band energy; default the peak of the response); filters (an array of { material, thickness_um }, optionally density_g_cm3, or { transmission_csv }); grid (open fraction; default 1); mirror ({ kind = \"SiO2\", angle_deg }, calibrated; { kind = \"mlm\", angle_deg = 1.9 }; { kind = \"fresnel\", material, angle_deg }, optionally roughness_nm and density_g_cm3, an uncalibrated GrazingMirror for C, Be, B₄C and other coatings; or { reflectivity_csv }); cathode (\"Al\", \"Cr\" or \"Ni\", xrd_sensitivity) or sensitivity_csv; or a measured response_csv in place of all of these; field_of_view_mm (a per-channel field in place of the view field, such as the 12 mm of Dante 1's multilayer channel)\ntop level remove_channels (numbers dropped from the definition extended)\n\nThe kind xray_imager (the SXI, G-LEH and gated imagers) builds an XrayImager and takes:\n\nTable Keys\n[line_of_sight] (required) port, or theta_deg and phi_deg (as above)\n[optic] (required) kind = \"pinhole\", diameter_um, distance_mm (the pinhole from the object), magnification; energy_eV (the photon energy of the diffraction blur)\ntop level filters (required; as for a channel, an empty array for none), mirror (as for a channel)\n[detector] (required) absorber and absorber_um (the absorbing layer, e.g. Si 15 µm) or efficiency_csv; kind, pixel_um, pixels (across, down)\n[view] width_mm (the object-plane field; default the extent of the detector over the magnification), pixels (samples per side; default 256), center_part or center_mm, up (the up direction of the image, a chamber direction)\n[recording] mode = \"integrated\" (default) or \"gated\" with times_ns, gate_ns (FWHM) and gate_shape (\"gaussian\" or \"square\")\n\ndetected_radiance weighs the radiance of each element by the response of the imager over its spectrum, imager_image renders it on the object plane and blurs it by the pinhole, and imager_record integrates the images of a history over the gates of the recording.\n\nThe kind radiography, for backlit radiographs of an area backlighter imaged through a pinhole or by point projection, builds a Radiograph for radiograph, which takes the target with its backlighter foil, the laser deposition on the foil and the parts of the object with their materials. It takes:\n\nTable Keys\n[line_of_sight] (required) port, or theta_deg and phi_deg: from the object toward the detector\n[backlighter] (required) line (the He-α element, e.g. \"Ge\") or line_keV; pulse_ns (the flat-top pulse) and start_ns; foil_part (default backlighter); angular (\"isotropic\" or \"lambertian\"); prepulse (the conversion-efficiency class); ce (in place of kshell_ce)\n[optic] (required) kind = \"pinhole\" with diameter_um, distance_mm (from the object) and magnification (or the distance_mm of the detector), or kind = \"projection\"\ntop level filters (required; as for a channel)\n[detector] (required) distance_mm (from the object), absorber and absorber_um or efficiency_csv; kind, pixel_um, pixels\n[view] width_mm (the object-plane field), pixels (default 128), center_part or center_mm, up\n[recording] as for an imager\n\nThe images are of detected photons per mm² of detector, with the open field (no object) and the transmission that a radiograph normalized by it measures; the frames of a gated radiograph are the emission recorded through each gate.\n\nA field of view takes in every surface of the target facing the instrument, occluded by any part: a NIF Dante's is ±30 mm at TCC (the 2004 design), wider than any hohlraum, so windows, packages and shields enter the signal as they would on the shot. Within an extended definition, giving one of area_mm2 / area_part, center_part / center_mm or port / theta_deg and phi_deg drops the other inherited from the base.\n\nThe response of a channel is the product of its parts (DanteResponse). Measured curves are CSV files with a header line and two columns, photon energy (eV) and value (a fraction, or V/GW for a sensitivity or a response), at a path relative to the definition file (TabulatedCurve); outside a table a factor keeps its end values and a whole response is zero, and check_diagnostics warns when a table does not cover 50 eV – 20 keV.","title":"A definition file"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Built-in-definitions","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"The package contains the public configurations that the literature documents (research notes, \"Dante-family hardware and geometry: what is public\"), each read from the table of its source and checked against the energies the source tabulates:\n\nid Instrument and configuration Not public (reported as missing)\nnif_dante1 NIF Dante 1, latest public configuration, viewing the whole target: a 60 mm field of view at TCC, ±30 mm as in the 2004 design (Dewald et al. 2004), Tr over the lower entrance hole (extends nif_dante1_2021) the collimators as fielded\nnif_dante1_2021 from October 2021 (Rubery et al. 2023, Fig. 4), the same field and Tr's reference —\nnif_dante1_N180604, _N180129, _N170924 single shots, 2017–18 (Kemp et al. 2020, Tables I–III) —\nnif_dante1_2012 2012–14 (May et al. 2015, Table IV) —\nnif_dante1_2009 N091104 (Fournier et al. 2010, Table II) —\nnif_dante2 NIF Dante 2: port P64-350, through the upper entrance hole (dante2 supplies Dante 1's channels, assumed) the channels, the field of view\nnif_sentinel_N170924 NIF Sentinel on N170924-003 (Kemp et al. 2020, Table II) the equatorial DIM (phi_deg); the MiniXRD responses\nomega_dante Omega Dante (Rubery et al. 2023, Fig. 13) the solid angles (the standoff)\nnif_sxi_lower NIF SXI lower, hard channel: 25 µm pinhole at 2×, Be 75 + Ti 50 µm, Si CCD (Schneider et al. 2010) —\nnif_sxi_upper NIF SXI upper, hard channel (extends nif_sxi_lower; Schneider et al. 2012, a draft) —\nnif_gleh2 NIF G-LEH-2, gated, 1× (Chen et al. 2021, Table I) the Ti filter, the absorbing layer of the sensor, frame times and gate\nnif_inflight_radiography NIF in-flight radiography: Ge He-α area backlighter, pinholes at 8×, two gated MCP strips of ≈ 90 ps (Rygg et al. 2014) the equatorial port, the diameter and distance of the pinholes, the filters, the detector efficiency, the pulse, the frame times, the field\n\nA user group completes one by extending it with the values it holds, e.g. Omega's solid angles, one [[channel]] table per channel with number and solid_angle_sr.","title":"Built-in definitions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Extending-a-definition","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"A file with extends = \"<id>\" takes that definition and replaces what it gives: tables per key, channels by number (the keys listed for a channel replace those of the base channel; a new number adds a channel), and remove_channels drops channels. A campaign that replaced Dante 1's channel 6 filter, removed channel 16 and added a calibrated channel is, in full:\n\nschema = 1\nid = \"group_dante1_campaign\"\nextends = \"nif_dante1\"\nname = \"Dante 1, campaign configuration\"\npublic = false\nremove_channels = [16]\n\n[[channel]]\nnumber = 6\nfilters = [{ material = \"Cu\", thickness_um = 1.1 }]\nsource = \"campaign shot sheet\"\n\n[[channel]]\nnumber = 19\nenergy_eV = 1000\nsolid_angle_sr = 6.5e-7\nresponse_csv = \"calibrations/ch19.csv\"\n\nDiagnosticDefinition.chain lists the files a definition was resolved from, and public is false when any of them sets public = false. Values a definition lacks to be built (Dante 2's channels are not public, for example) are reported as :missing by check_diagnostics, with the key to supply.","title":"Extending a definition"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Definitions-in-sessions","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"A saved GUI session contains the definitions of the diagnostics it records, so that it records the same instruments on any computer. diagnostic_table writes a resolved definition as one standalone table, without extends and with each CSV path mapped, for example to a copy stored beside it. diagnostic_definition reads such a table back and checks it as a file on the search path is checked. diagnostic_diff lists the keys in which two definitions differ, with CSV files compared by content. When a session is opened, a saved definition that differs from the definition of the same id on the search path is reported with those keys, and the saved one is used unless the one on the search path is chosen.","title":"Definitions in sessions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/diagnostics/#New-kinds","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"register_diagnostic_kind adds a kind: a schema of DiagnosticFields and a builder. The format already reserves the recording modes of the kinds planned next (time-integrated, gated and streaked imagers, radiography, streaked optical diagnostics); see reference/diagnostics-design.md in the repository.","title":"New kinds"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.DIAGNOSTIC_SCHEMA","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"DIAGNOSTIC_SCHEMA\n\nThe version of the diagnostic definition format that this release reads (the schema key of every definition file).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DIAGNOSTIC_SCHEMA"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.DiagnosticDefinition","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"DiagnosticDefinition\n\nA diagnostic definition read from a file and resolved through extends. The fields are id, name, kind, file (the file that defines it), chain (the files from which it was resolved, the base first), public and data. public is false when any file in the chain sets public = false, and data is the resolved table with CSV paths made absolute. diagnostic builds the instrument.\n\ndata[\"instrument\"], when a file of the chain gives it, names the instrument of which the definition is one configuration. For example, the channel sets of NIF Dante 1 for several years and shots share instrument = \"NIF Dante 1\". A definition that extends another inherits the key, and a definition without it is a separate instrument (diagnostic_instrument).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DiagnosticDefinition"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.DiagnosticField","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"DiagnosticField(type; required = false, doc = \"\")\n\nOne key of the schema of a diagnostic kind (register_diagnostic_kind). type is Real, Integer, String, Bool, :date, Vector{String}, Vector{Real}, a schema table (a Dict{String,DiagnosticField}) or a one-element vector that contains a schema table (an array of tables). Keys ending in _csv name a CSV file, as a path relative to the definition file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DiagnosticField"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.DiagnosticIssue","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"DiagnosticIssue\n\nA problem found in a definition file (check_diagnostics), with fields file, key (the path to the value, e.g. channel[6].filters[1].thickness_um), message and level. level is :error when the file cannot be used, :missing for a value that the definition lacks to be built (e.g. one that is not public), or :warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.DiagnosticIssue"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.add_diagnostic_path-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"add_diagnostic_path(dir; save = true) -> Vector{String}\n\nAdd the directory dir to the diagnostic search path, after those already on it. With save = true, the directory is stored in the configuration file (diagnostic_config_path), so later sessions find it. With save = false, it is added only for this Julia session. Returns the search path (diagnostic_paths).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.add_diagnostic_path"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.check_diagnostics-Tuple{}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"check_diagnostics(; paths = diagnostic_paths()) -> Vector{DiagnosticIssue}\n\nRead every definition on paths, resolve extends, check each definition against the schema of its kind and build it, and return the problems found (DiagnosticIssue). The problems are errors (unknown keys, wrong types, unreadable CSV files, unknown materials), values that a definition lacks to be built (:missing) and warnings (shadowed ids, CSV tables that do not cover the energy range of a channel). An empty result means that every definition builds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.check_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.diagnostic-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostic(id; paths = diagnostic_paths(), kwargs...)\ndiagnostic(def::DiagnosticDefinition; kwargs...)\n\nBuild the instrument of definition id found on paths (diagnostics), or of def, such as a DanteView for kind = \"xray_power\". kwargs go to the builder of the kind, for xray_power those of DanteView such as pixels, and replace the values of the definition. A definition that lacks a value that the instrument needs, such as a channel set that is not public, throws an error that names the key to supply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diagnostic"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.diagnostic_config_path-Tuple{}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostic_config_path() -> String\n\nThe user configuration file that stores the diagnostic search path, under the key diagnostics.paths. It is BRIAREUS_CONFIG if set, and otherwise %APPDATA%\\Briareus\\config.toml on Windows, ~/Library/Application Support/Briareus/config.toml on macOS and $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/briareus/config.toml (default ~/.config) elsewhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diagnostic_config_path"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.diagnostic_definition-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostic_definition(table; file = \"\", dir = pwd()) -> DiagnosticDefinition\n\nThe definition of a standalone table, which is the table of a definition file with kind and no extends (diagnostic_table). The table is checked as a file on the search path is, and CSV paths are relative to dir. file names the table in the definition and in errors. Throws an ArgumentError that lists the problems when the table has errors or lacks values that it needs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diagnostic_definition"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.diagnostic_diff-Tuple{DiagnosticDefinition, DiagnosticDefinition}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostic_diff(a::DiagnosticDefinition, b::DiagnosticDefinition) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe keys whose values differ between two definitions (as in DiagnosticIssue, e.g. channel[6].filters), sorted. The result is empty when the definitions define the same instrument and sources. CSV files are compared by content, and their paths are ignored. name and public count as differences.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diagnostic_diff"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.diagnostic_instrument-Tuple{DiagnosticDefinition}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostic_instrument(d::DiagnosticDefinition) -> String\n\nThe instrument of which d is a configuration. It is the instrument key of d (set directly, or inherited through extends), or else its name. A list of diagnostics shows each instrument once and offers its definitions as configurations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diagnostic_instrument"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.diagnostic_paths-Tuple{}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostic_paths(; builtin = true) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe diagnostic search path, in order of precedence, where a later definition of an id shadows an earlier one. The path consists of the built-in definitions (data/diagnostics, unless builtin = false), the directories saved in the configuration file (diagnostic_config_path), those of the environment variable BRIAREUS_DIAGNOSTICS (separated by ; on Windows, : elsewhere), and those added for this Julia session (add_diagnostic_path with save = false).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diagnostic_paths"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.diagnostic_table-Tuple{DiagnosticDefinition}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostic_table(def::DiagnosticDefinition; csv = identity) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe resolved definition as one standalone table, with no extends. It is the table of a definition file that diagnostic_definition converts back to the same definition. A session embeds its diagnostics this way, and TOML.print writes the table as a file. Each CSV path (absolute in def) is replaced by csv(path), e.g. the name of a copy stored beside the table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diagnostic_table"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.diagnostics-Tuple{}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"diagnostics(; paths = diagnostic_paths()) -> Dict{String,DiagnosticDefinition}\n\nEvery diagnostic definition on the search path (directories, read recursively, or single files), resolved through extends, by id. A later definition of an id shadows an earlier one, with a warning that names both files. A file with errors is left out with a warning, and check_diagnostics reports all the errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.register_diagnostic_kind-Tuple{Symbol, Dict{String, DiagnosticField}, Any}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"register_diagnostic_kind(kind::Symbol, schema::Dict{String,DiagnosticField}, build)\n\nMake kind a diagnostic kind. Definition files with kind = \"<kind>\" are checked against schema and built by build(def::DiagnosticDefinition; kwargs...). The schema covers the keys besides the header keys, which are schema, id, kind, name, facility, instrument, extends, public, valid_from, valid_to, shots, and source, confidence and note in every table. A kind registered again replaces the earlier one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.register_diagnostic_kind"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.remove_diagnostic_path-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"remove_diagnostic_path(dir; save = true) -> Vector{String}\n\nRemove dir from the saved search path (save = true) or from the search path of this session (add_diagnostic_path). Returns the search path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.remove_diagnostic_path"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.XrayImager","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"XrayImager(theta, phi; pinhole, distance, magnification, width, pixels = 256,\n           center = 0, up = ẑ, filters = (), tables = (), mirror = nothing,\n           detector = nothing, detector_pixel = 0.0, energy = nothing,\n           mode = :integrated, times = Float64[], gate = 0.0, gate_shape = :gaussian)\n\nA pinhole x-ray imager on the line of sight (θ, φ), in degrees, from target chamber center toward the instrument. A pinhole of diameter pinhole (mm) at distance (mm) from the object images at magnification onto a detector of pixel detector_pixel (mm). The object plane is sampled over a width × width mm field about center (a point, or the name of a part) on pixels × pixels rays. up (a chamber direction) is the up direction of the image.\n\nThe spectral response R(E) is the product of the filters, an optional mirror and the absorption of the detector. The filters are filter_transmission materials with thicknesses in mm, as for DanteResponse, and measured tables, and the mirror is as for DanteResponse. detector = (:Si, 0.015) absorbs 1 − e^{−μρt} of the photons in 15 µm of Si (no dead layer), a TabulatedCurve is a measured efficiency, and nothing absorbs every photon. R is a fraction, so images are of detected x-ray energy.\n\nThe point-spread function on the object plane is the geometric penumbra of the pinhole, a uniform disk of diameter penumbra = d(1 + 1/M). It is convolved with a Gaussian of the summed variances of the Fraunhofer diffraction of the pinhole (FWHM diffraction = 1.03 λ distance / d at photon energy, eV) and of the detector pixel (a box of detector_pixel / M). The Gaussian is an engineering approximation, since the Airy pattern and the box are not Gaussian, and a pinhole whose Fresnel number d² / λz is near 1 is not in the far field. energy defaults to the mean photon energy of the light that the imager detects from a 300 eV Planckian, an engineering default; a definition file states the value.\n\nmode is :integrated for one image of the time integral, or :gated for one frame per time of times (ns). Each gated frame is the integral of the image against a gate of FWHM gate ns, :gaussian or :square, of peak 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.XrayImager"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.detected_radiance-Tuple{XrayImager, AbstractVector}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"detected_radiance(im::XrayImager, B::AbstractVector) -> Vector{Float64}\ndetected_radiance(im::XrayImager, sol::RadiationSolution) -> Vector{Float64}\ndetected_radiance(im::XrayImager, s::SpectralSolution) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe radiance of each element that the imager detects (TW mm⁻² sr⁻¹ of detected x-ray energy), B ⟨R⟩ / π. It is the Lambertian radiance of the element times the response of the imager averaged over its spectrum. For a gray radiosity B (TW/mm²), the spectrum is the Planckian at the brightness temperature (B/σ)^{1/4} of the element. This is an assumption, since the reflected part of the radiosity has the incident spectrum. For a SpectralSolution, the radiosity of each group is used with the Planckian of the wall temperature of the element within the group.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.detected_radiance"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.imager_image-Tuple{XrayImager, Target, AbstractVector}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"imager_image(im::XrayImager, t::Target, L::AbstractVector) -> Matrix{Float64}\nimager_image(im::XrayImager, sol) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nThe image on the object plane (row 1 at the top, up) of the per-element detected radiance L (detected_radiance), blurred by the resolution of the imager, in TW mm⁻² sr⁻¹ of detected x-ray energy. For a solution, the target of the solution and detected_radiance(im, sol) are used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.imager_image"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.imager_record-Tuple{XrayImager, Target, AbstractVector, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"imager_record(im::XrayImager, t::Target, time, L::AbstractMatrix) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe record of a history by the imager. L is the detected radiance per element (rows) at each time of time (ns, columns), the detected_radiance of each step, taken as linear between the times. The result is (; images, times). For mode = :integrated it holds one image of the time integral (kJ mm⁻² sr⁻¹, as TW·ns), with times empty. For :gated it holds one frame per gate time, the integral against the gate (peak 1; kJ mm⁻² sr⁻¹), with times the gate times.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.imager_record"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.pinhole_solid_angle-Tuple{XrayImager}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"pinhole_solid_angle(im::XrayImager) -> Float64\n\nThe solid angle (sr) of the pinhole seen from the object, π d² / 4 distance². A detector pixel of side p records L × (p/M)² × Ω of an image of detected radiance L.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.pinhole_solid_angle"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.Radiograph","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"Radiograph(theta, phi; line, distance, pinhole = nothing, pinhole_distance = nothing,\n           width, pixels = 128, center = 0, up = ẑ, filters = (), tables = (),\n           detector = nothing, detector_pixel = 0.0, foil = :backlighter,\n           angular = :isotropic, prepulse = false, ce = nothing, irradiance = :mean,\n           pulse, start = 0.0, mode = :integrated, times = Float64[], gate = 0.0,\n           gate_shape = :gaussian)\n\nA backlit radiograph on the line of sight (θ, φ), in degrees, from the object toward the detector. It records the He-α line (an element Symbol of kshell_line_energy, or keV) of the backlighter foil, a part of the target behind the object. The detector lies distance mm from center, which is a point or the name of a part. The object plane is a width × width mm field about center normal to the line of sight, sampled on pixels × pixels points, and it is the coordinate system of the image. up (a chamber direction) is the up direction of the image.\n\nThe optic is one of:\n\nPinhole imaging (pinhole, diameter mm, at pinhole_distance p mm from center): each image point views the foil through the pinhole and the object point, at magnification M = (distance − p)/p. The point-spread function is that of the x-ray imager (XrayImager). It combines the penumbra d(1 + 1/M) and a Gaussian of the diffraction at the line energy and the detector pixel, on the object plane.\nPoint projection (pinhole = nothing): each detector point receives every foil element as a point source at its centroid, along its path through the object. The extent of the source therefore blurs the image, by the source width × L/(s + L) on the object plane, with s the distance of the foil behind the object and L = distance. The image point of a detector point is where its line to the centroid of the foil crosses the object plane, and M = (s + L)/s. Only the detector pixel adds further blur.\n\nThe detector is flat and normal to the line of sight, and images are per mm² of it. A pinhole is taken as a thin disk normal to the line of sight, of area A = π(d/2)². An image point at ρ from center views the foil along a ray at the angle α to the line of sight, tan α = ρ/p, with radiance R. The étendue between the pinhole and a detector area dA_d at the end of the ray is A cos α · dA_d cos α / D², with D = (L − p)/cos α the pinhole–detector distance along the ray. The fluence is therefore R A cos⁴α/(L − p)², the on-axis value times cos⁴α. The factor combines the projected pinhole area, the inverse square of the longer distance and the incidence cosine on the detector. In point projection, the fluence E_e f(cos θ)/(4π r²) of each foil element, at its distance r from the detector point, is multiplied by the incidence cosine of the ray on the detector, r̂ · d̂. On the axis the pinhole factor is exactly 1. In point projection the incidence cosines differ from 1 by the angular extent of the source, at most (w/2)²/(2(s + L)²) for a foil of width w. Fluence normal to each ray, as backlighter_signal gives it, has none of these factors.\n\nThe spectral response at the line is the product of the transmission of the filters, the measured tables (TabulatedCurves, eV) and the efficiency of the detector. The filters are filter_transmission materials with thicknesses in mm, with the NIST tables where they cover the material and energy, as for backlighter_signal. detector = (:Si, 0.015) absorbs 1 − e^{−μρt} (Henke photoabsorption, no dead layer), a TabulatedCurve is a measured efficiency, and nothing absorbs every photon. rg.filter_transmission and rg.efficiency contain the two values at the line. Images are of detected photons.\n\nThe source is that of backlighter_signal, with prepulse, ce, irradiance and angular as there. It is the He-α energy of each foil element, emitted uniformly over the flat-top laser pulse of pulse ns from start ns, on the assumption that the emission follows the laser. mode is :integrated for the whole emission, or :gated for one frame per time of times (ns). Each gated frame records the emission against a gate of FWHM gate ns, :gaussian or :square, of peak 1, as in imager_record. The object is static over the emission, so a moving object needs one radiograph per frame of its geometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.Radiograph"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/diagnostics/#Briareus.radiograph-Tuple{Radiograph, Target, Deposition}","page":"Diagnostic definitions","text":"radiograph(rg::Radiograph, target, deposition; object = (),\n           model = kshell_ce_model()) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe radiograph that rg records of target, with its backlighter foil driven by deposition, the laser power on each element in TW, as backlighter_signal takes it.\n\nobject lists the parts that attenuate, as part => material (with the density of the material) or part => (material, density) (g/cm³), with materials as in mass_attenuation. Each part is a closed surface filled with its material, and along each ray the transmission is exp(−Σ μ/ρ · ρ · L), with L the length of the ray inside each part. Where parts overlap their attenuations add, so a hollow shell is its outer surface with the material of the shell and its inner surface with the negative density. The parts of the object do not obstruct, and every other surface part except the foil does, as hardware does in backlighter_signal.\n\nReturns (; images, open, transmission, weights, times, magnification, line, ce, sigma, extrapolated):\n\nimages: one matrix per frame (one for :integrated) of detected photons per mm² of detector at each object-plane point (row 1 at the top, up). Per detector pixel, the values are multiplied by its area.\nopen: the same over the whole emission without the object (the open field).\ntransmission: the image of the whole emission over open (NaN where open is 0), which is the transmission of the object as the radiograph measures it, blurred.\nweights: the fraction of the emission that each frame records (1 for :integrated), so images[k] = weights[k] × transmission × open; times: the times of the frames.\nmagnification, line (keV), and from the source ce (the He-α energy over the laser energy), sigma (1σ of ln CE, by which every image is uncertain) and extrapolated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.radiograph"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/visrad/#VisRad-interchange","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"This page documents readers and writers for the files VisRad (Prism Computational Sciences, VISRAD 21.1.1) imports and exports: NIF SST pointing, the Laser Beam Summary table, time-dependent beam powers, element and power-summary tables, and workspaces (.vrw, with target components, bundled CAD and beams). It also documents a benchmark kit that writes everything needed to rebuild a Briareus case in VisRad, together with Briareus's results for comparison. Formats follow the VisRad manual; the sources and confidence of each detail are in reference/research-notes.md (\"VisRad interchange formats\").","title":"VisRad interchange"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.VISRAD_NIF_PLATES","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"VISRAD_NIF_PLATES\n\nThe VisRad phase plate (a VISRAD_PHASE_PLATES name) for each NIF cone: Rev3 Inner on the 23.5° cone, 1.0 Rev1a Inner on the 30° cone, Rev3 Outer on the 44.5° cone and Rev1 Outer on the 50° cone. The assignment is that of the measured CPP profiles behind nif_spot (LMF phase-plate table, 2020-10-15). VisRad lists the four plates among its NIF CPPs (VR/power_sources/import_nif_cmt_data_file.html) and tabulates their radii and exponents (VR/power_sources/phase_plate_params.html). Other campaigns field other plates. This is a default for VisRad parity and is not a facility record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.VISRAD_NIF_PLATES"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.VISRAD_PHASE_PLATES","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"VISRAD_PHASE_PLATES\n\nThe built-in phase-plate profiles of VisRad (VR/power_sources/phase_plate_params.html), keyed by the names in the manual, as (facility, major, minor, order, level). The radii are in µm; the \"(mm)\" header in the manual is a µ rendered in the Symbol font. They are measured where the intensity is level × peak: exp(-1) (1/e) for the OMEGA, OMEGA EP and GEKKO tables, 0.5 (HWHM) for NIF CPPs and 0.03 for LMJ. Circular plates have minor == major. order is the super-Gaussian exponent n of exp(-[(x/a)²+(y/b)²]^(n/2)) (VR/power_sources/set_laser_profile_props.html). The NIF entries with n = 2.0 are placeholders in the manual where no data were available. The GEKKO table does not state its radius definition, and 1/e is an assumption. See visrad_spot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.VISRAD_PHASE_PLATES"},{"category":"constant","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.VISRAD_SST_COLUMNS","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"VISRAD_SST_COLUMNS\n\nColumn headers of the NIF SST beam-pointing CSV, as VisRad maps them when it imports an SST file (screenshot in VR/intro/revision_history.html, v21.0.0, and VR/power_sources/import_laser_beam_data_csv.html). The columns are the beam name, the aim point x, y, z (mm, target-chamber Cartesian about TCC) and the defocus (mm).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.VISRAD_SST_COLUMNS"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.compare_visrad-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"compare_visrad(kit_dir; elements = nothing, power_summary = nothing, observe = :capsule,\n               names = Dict(), terms = (0, 2, 4), lmax = 4, io = nothing)\n    -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nCompare a kit written by write_visrad_kit with the VisRad results for the same case.\n\nelements: the VisRad Surface Element Properties export for the observed component (read_visrad_table). The polar and azimuthal columns are those whose headers contain \"polar\"/\"theta\" and \"azim\"/\"phi\", in degrees and in the component frame, which for an unrotated sphere is the chamber frame. They and \"Flux In\" (TW/cm²; a TW/mm² header is converted) are fitted exactly as the Briareus elements were (visrad_legendre_fit over terms, visrad_ylm_fit to lmax).\npower_summary: the VisRad Power Summary, as a file (read_visrad_table, with object names as row labels) or a Dict(object => Dict(column => value)) typed from the dialog. The columns are as in VisRad (\"Laser Pwr Dep\", \"Incident Rad Pwr\", \"Emitted Rad Pwr\", \"Pwr Src Term\", \"Surface Area\"; TW and cm²). names maps VisRad object names to Briareus component names, e.g. Dict(\"hohlraum_barrel\" => \"wall\").\n\nReturns rows (; quantity, unit, briareus, visrad, difference, relative) and prints them to io when given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.compare_visrad"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.read_visrad_powers-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"read_visrad_powers(path; template = nothing) -> Dict{Symbol,PulseShape}\n\nRead a VisRad time-dependent beam-power CSV. The first column is time in ns, and a \"(s)\"/\"(sec)\" or \"(ps)\" unit in its header is converted. One power column (TW) per beam follows, headed by the beam name (VR/files/laser_beam_data_files.html). Negative powers are set to zero, as VisRad does on import (VR/power_sources/import_tdep_beam_powers.html).\n\nWithout a template the keys are the normalized beam names as Symbols, so that BL115 becomes :B115, and OMEGA beams become keys such as :Beam_10. With a template beam set the keys are the names of the template. Beams match by name, and a NIF quad gets the sum of the pulses of its four beams, on the union of their knots. Template beams absent from the file have no key. The beams at time t are with_pulses(beams, P, t; by = :beam) (with_pulses).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_visrad_powers"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.read_visrad_pulse_shape-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"read_visrad_pulse_shape(path) -> PulseShape\n\nRead one of the standard laser pulse-shape files of VisRad (data/laser_pulse_shapes of the installation), a two-column ASCII table of power (W) versus time (s) (VR/files/laser_pulse_shape_files.html), and return it in ns and TW. The column order is not stated. Time first is an assumption, and the columns are swapped when the first column is not sorted while the second is. Non-numeric lines are skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_visrad_pulse_shape"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.read_visrad_sst-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"read_visrad_sst(path; template = nif_beams(), absent = :off, units = nothing)\n    -> Vector{Beam}\n\nRead a NIF SST pointing CSV and apply it to template. The file may come from File | Export Laser Beam Data | NIF SST Beam Data in VisRad, from the SST, or from write_visrad_sst. Each beam found in the file gets its aim (mm, chamber Cartesian about TCC, defocus not applied) and defocus (mm), and power and spot are kept. As in the VisRad import (VR/power_sources/import_laser_beam_data_csv.html), template beams absent from the file are turned off (absent = :off, power 0). :keep leaves them unchanged, and :error throws.\n\nBeam names match case-insensitively, with BL115 read as B115. With a quad template (nif_quads()), each quad takes the mean aim and defocus of its four beams, with a warning if they differ by more than 1 µm. The header row is found by its \"Beam Aim Point\" columns. Lines before it are skipped, and extra columns are ignored. Units come from the headers (\"(mm)\", \"(cm)\", \"(um)\"), and units (mm per file unit) overrides them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_visrad_sst"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.read_visrad_summary-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"read_visrad_summary(path; template = nothing, absent = :off, spot_definition = :auto,\n                    rotation_sense = :beam, angle_tol = 0.05) -> Vector{Beam}\n\nRead a VisRad Laser Beam Summary CSV (Export Data in the Laser Beams Summary dialog, VR/power_sources/laser_summary_table.html; also written by write_visrad_summary). Columns are recognized by header, so older layouts and extra columns are accepted.\n\nConversions:\n\nPointing. \"TC Pt Pos-x/y/z\" (cm) is the focus of the beam in the target-chamber frame, which is the Reference Point plus the focus offset along the beam. This follows from the example table in the manual (VR/power_sources/images/laser_summary.jpg). Beams pointed at the same foil-frame Reference Point with focus offset -0.215 cm have TC positions that differ by exactly 0.215 cm × the difference of their port vectors. So aim = 10·TC + defocus·port (mm) with defocus = 10·focus offset (cm → mm). When the TC columns are absent, the Reference Point is taken from \"Pt Param-1/2/3\" (cm, as Cartesian, cylindrical (r, z, φ) or spherical (r, θ, φ) coordinates, with angles in degrees). This works only for references in the target-chamber frame (\"Target Chamber\" or \"TCC\"). When both are present they are cross-checked.\nPower. \"Power\" is in TW, and for NIF it is the sum of the 3ω, 2ω and 1ω cones (VR/power_sources/nif_beam_params.html). Time-dependent beams show their peak as \"T-<peak>\", which is read as the peak with a warning. Off beams get power 0.\nSpot. \"R0\" major/minor are radii (µm) at the spot-size definition level, \"36.8% (1/e)\", \"50%\" or \"5%\" of peak (VR/power_sources/set_laser_profile_props.html). They become the 1/e semi-axes a (major) and b (minor) of the same super-Gaussian exp(-[(x/a)²+(y/b)²]^(n/2)), which is the focal profile of VisRad. The definition comes from an \"R0 Def\" column if present, and otherwise from spot_definition. With :auto, the level of a recognized built-in phase plate is used (VISRAD_PHASE_PLATES; NIF CPP radii are HWHM), and 1/e otherwise. 1/e is the VisRad default for Custom Supergaussian and an assumption for other models. :e, :hwhm, :pct5 or a number set the level explicitly. The exponent n comes from an \"SG Exponent\" column, else from the phase-plate table, else from the template beam. A \"Uniform\" profile (flat across R0 at focus) is approximated by n = 50 with 1/e radius R0.\nRotation. \"Rot Angle\" is the minor-axis rotation ρ (degrees, clockwise from vertical; ρ = 0 puts the major axis \"nearly horizontal\"). The Briareus angle of the major axis from θ̂ toward φ̂ is 90° − ρ for rotation_sense = :beam (the spot seen along the beam) and 90° + ρ for :target (seen from TCC). The manual does not state which. The deposition of VisRad 21 settles it as :beam: at ρ = ±30°, 60° and 90° on ten NIF beams, its spot axes match :beam within 0.04° and differ from :target by 60°. Horizontal is φ̂ of the NIF quad port of the beam (quad of the template beam), which is turned 1.6–5.3° from φ̂ of the beam, as measured in VisRad 21 to 0.02° on 48 beams. Beams of no NIF quad use their φ̂.\nDPR, halo, f-number and wavelength are not in the file. DPR \"On\" is ignored with a warning; VisRad splits such OMEGA beams into two cones 85 µm apart. The other values come from the template.\n\nWith a template, beams are matched by name, case-insensitively, with \"BL115\" read as \"B115\" and \"10\" and \"BL-10\" as \"Beam_10\". Port angles are checked against the \"Polar Ang\"/\"Azm Ang\" of the file, with a warning beyond angle_tol degrees, which indicates a frame or naming mismatch. Template beams absent from the file follow absent (:off, :keep, :error), since VisRad omits hidden unused beams. NIF quads aggregate their four beams, with the mean aim and defocus and the summed power. Without a template, beams are built from the port angles of the file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_visrad_summary"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.read_visrad_table-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"read_visrad_table(path; header = nothing) -> (; header, labels, data)\n\nRead a multi-column ASCII table exported by VisRad. The table is the Surface Element Properties table (File | Save Data for Object / All Objects / Selected Rows, and the *.sed files of a run; VR/results/display_surf_elem_table.html, VR/simulation_times/config_sim_params.html) or a Power Summary (*.psd). The manual shows the columns, for example \"Z (cm)\", \"Azim. ang (deg)\", \"Radius (cm)\", \"Surf Index\", \"Emis T (eV)\", \"Rad T (eV)\", \"Flux In (TW/cm2)\" and \"Flux Out (TW/cm2)\" (VR/results/images/surf_elem_table.jpg), but not the file layout. The reader is therefore tolerant (assumption throughout):\n\nthe table may be comma-, tab- or whitespace-separated;\ndata rows are the most common shape of lines ending in numbers, and any leading non-numeric text is kept as the label of the row (an object name);\nthe header is the last non-data line before the first data row. It is split on the delimiter, on runs of 2+ spaces, or by attaching \"(unit)\" tokens to the words before them.\n\nWhen that fails, header (a vector of names for the numeric columns) sets the names.\n\nReturns the numeric column names, row labels (empty strings when none) and an nrows × ncols matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_visrad_table"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_legendre_fit-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_legendre_fit(theta, values; terms = 0:4, weights = nothing) -> Vector\n\nLeast-squares fit f(θ) = Σₗ Aₗ Pₗ(cos θ) over the given terms, as in the Surface Element Statistics of VisRad, with coefficients up to A4 and each term selected by a check box (VR/results/display_surf_elem_table.html). theta is in degrees. The result A is indexed l+1, with zeros for unused terms, so A[3]/A[1] is P2/P0. The fit is unweighted by default, since the VisRad weighting is not documented (assumption); element areas passed as weights weight it. The same fit applied to Briareus and VisRad element values gives a like-for-like comparison.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_legendre_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_nif_spot-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_nif_spot(cone) -> SuperGaussianSpot\n\nThe best-focus spot of the VisRad phase plate for a NIF cone (VISRAD_NIF_PLATES, visrad_spot at rotation 0), with the major axis along φ̂ of the port as for nif_spot. VisRad measures the rotation of the spot of a NIF beam from the horizontal of its quad, so the spots of the VisRad beams are nif_beams(; spot = visrad_nif_spot, spot_frame = :quad). With deposition_kw = (; profile = VisRadProfile()), the deposition follows the VisRad beam model (research notes, \"VisRad deposition parity\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_nif_spot"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_spot-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_spot(plate; rotation = 0.0, rotation_sense = :beam) -> SuperGaussianSpot\n\nBriareus spot equivalent to a VisRad built-in phase plate, given by its VISRAD_PHASE_PLATES name or a CMT or summary-table spelling such as \"Rev3 Inner Cone 2W CPP\" or \"OMEGA E-IDI-300 DPP\". The tabulated radii, defined at the intensity level of the table, are converted to 1/e semi-axes (mm) of the same super-Gaussian, r_1/e = r_f / (ln(1/f))^(1/n). rotation is the minor-axis rotation of VisRad (degrees, clockwise from vertical, 0 with the major axis horizontal); see read_visrad_summary for rotation_sense.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_spot"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_ylm_fit-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_ylm_fit(theta, phi, values; lmax = 4, weights = nothing) -> Vector\n\nLeast-squares fit to the real spherical harmonics of VisRad (VR/results/display_surf_elem_table.html). These are Y_LM = N_LM P_L^M(cos θ) for M = 0 and √2 N_LM P_L^|M|(cos θ) × cos Mφ (M > 0) or sin |M|φ (M < 0), with N_LM = √((2L+1)(L−M)!/(L+M)!). Then Y_00 = 1, and A_00 is the mean; the example in the manual gives a mean of 9.736 and A_00 = 9.739. A_L0 = A_L/√(2L+1) for the Legendre A_L, and the same example gives 1.143·√5 = 2.556 against A_2 = 2.563. Angles are in degrees, and the coefficients are in the flat mode_index order of Briareus. They are the orthonormal ylm_moments of Briareus times √(4π). The manual does not state whether its P_L^M includes the Condon–Shortley phase. No phase is assumed, so the signs of odd-M coefficients may flip; √(Σ_M A_LM²)/A_00 is unaffected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_ylm_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.write_visrad_kit-Tuple{AbstractString, Target, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"write_visrad_kit(dir, target, beams; model = nothing, op = nothing, pulses = nothing, by = :group,\n                 time = 0.0, observe = :capsule, terms = (0, 2, 4), lmax = 4,\n                 rotation_sense = :beam, notes = \"\") -> NamedTuple\n\nWrite everything needed to rebuild a Briareus case in VisRad, together with the Briareus results for comparison (compare_visrad), into directory dir:\n\npointing_sst.csv — per-beam pointing in the NIF SST format (write_visrad_sst), imported with File | Import Laser Beam Data | Pointing Data, Custom CSV, units mm.\nbeam_summary.csv — the full beam set in Laser Beam Summary layout (write_visrad_summary): port angles, pointing, focus, power, 1/e spot radii, exponents and rotations.\npowers.csv — per-beam time-dependent powers (write_visrad_powers), when pulses is given (keyed by, as in write_visrad_powers); beam powers are then evaluated at time.\nbriareus_beams.csv — the canonical Briareus beam table (write_beam_table).\nREADME.txt — target geometry (component shapes and dimensions in mm, cm and µm, materials, albedos and conversion efficiencies), beam settings, and the VisRad steps.\nbriareus_results.csv — quantity,value,unit rows: per-component \"Laser Pwr Dep\", \"Pwr Src Term\" (conversion × laser), \"Incident Rad Pwr\" (Σ H·A), \"Emitted Rad Pwr\" (Σ B·A), all TW, and \"Surface Area\" (cm²), named as the Power Summary columns of VisRad; the laser power that misses or strikes outer surfaces; and for observe the mean incident flux (TW/cm²), the VisRad-style Legendre fit (visrad_legendre_fit over terms) and per-L amplitudes of the VisRad spherical-harmonic fit (visrad_ylm_fit).\nbriareus_<observe>_elements.csv — per-element polar and azimuthal angle about the center of the component (degrees), area (cm²), incident flux and absorbed laser flux (TW/cm²).\n\nThe deposition uses op (default DepositionOperator(target, beams)) and the radiation model (default RadiationModel(target)). Returns (; dir, files, results) with results a Dict of the quantities in briareus_results.csv.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_visrad_kit"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.write_visrad_powers-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:Beam}, Any}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"write_visrad_powers(path, beams, pulses; by = :group, times = nothing) -> path\nwrite_visrad_powers(path, pulses::AbstractDict{Symbol,PulseShape}; times = nothing) -> path\n\nWrite a VisRad time-dependent beam-power CSV (VR/files/laser_beam_data_files.html). The header is time (ns), followed by one column per beam named as VisRad names beams (B115 for NIF, Beam_10 for OMEGA), and the rows hold time (ns) and power (TW per beam). The file is imported with File | Import Laser Beam Data | Time-Dependent Powers (VR/power_sources/import_tdep_beam_powers.html). For NIF, VisRad assigns the powers to the 3ω (blue) cones.\n\npulses takes the forms of with_pulses: a PulseShape for every beam, or a Dict/NamedTuple keyed by group (default), quad, beam name (by = :beam, as read_visrad_powers returns them) or a function of the beam. The second form writes a Dict keyed by beam name as it is. NIF quads expand to their four beams at a quarter of the quad power each. Times default to the union of all pulse knots, so piecewise-linear pulses are reproduced exactly when each starts and ends at zero power. A PulseShape is zero outside its table, so a pulse that starts or ends at a finite power gets a ramp to the knots of its neighbors instead. Briareus beam names that are not facility names, such as the hohlraum-frame B01… of omega_beams(), are written as they are and should be renamed first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_visrad_powers"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.write_visrad_sst-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"write_visrad_sst(path, beams; include_off = false) -> path\n\nWrite beam pointing as a NIF SST CSV (VISRAD_SST_COLUMNS). The file has one row per NIF beam (\"B111\"–\"B468\"), with the aim point in target-chamber Cartesian coordinates about TCC (mm) without the defocus applied, and the defocus (mm; a positive defocus moves the focus along the propagation direction). This is the export format of VisRad (VR/files/laser_beam_data_files.html, \"NIF SST Data Files\"). It imports into VisRad through File | Import Laser Beam Data | Pointing Data, \"Custom\" CSV, which recognizes these headers (VR/power_sources/import_laser_beam_data_csv.html).\n\nNIF quads (names \"Q11T\"…, e.g. nif_quads()) are written as their four beams with the pointing of the quad. VisRad turns on exactly the beams that have pointing data, so beams with zero power are left out unless include_off.\n\nThe file does not contain powers or spots. It is paired with write_visrad_powers, and the spot model is set in VisRad (see write_visrad_kit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_visrad_sst"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.write_visrad_summary-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"write_visrad_summary(path, beams; rotation_sense = :beam, cluster = b -> string(b.group),\n                     profile = \"Custom Supergaussian\", extras = true) -> path\n\nWrite beams in the layout of the VisRad Laser Beam Summary CSV, one row per VisRad beam. NIF quads expand to their four beams with a quarter of the power each. The columns are the cluster, beam ID, on/off (power > 0), port polar angle and azimuth (degrees), the pointing reference \"Target Chamber\"/\"Cartesian\" with the aim as Pt Param-1/2/3 (cm), the focus offset (cm), the focus position \"TC Pt Pos\" (cm, aim + defocus·k), the power (TW), DPR \"Off\", the spatial profile model, R0 major/minor as 1/e radii (µm), and the minor-axis rotation (degrees; see read_visrad_summary for rotation_sense). extras appends \"SG Exponent\" and \"R0 Def\" (\"36.8% (1/e)\") so that the file is self-contained.\n\nColumns through \"TC Pt Pos-z\" reproduce the headers of the v21 dialog. The names of the later columns are not shown in the manual (assumption), and halo parameters are not written, since VisRad has none. The file serves for records and for read_visrad_summary. Pointing is set in VisRad by importing the output of write_visrad_sst, whose format is documented.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_visrad_summary"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.VisRadObject","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"VisRadObject\n\nOne target component of a VisRad workspace, as read by read_visrad_workspace.\n\nFields: name; VisRad's object type code and the kind Briareus recognizes (:mesh for CAD/generalized meshes, :sphere, :rectangle, :cylindrical_hohlraum, :other); the folder path in VisRad's component tree (\"\" at the top); params, every key = value line of the object verbatim; rotation and position (mm), the object frame in the target-chamber frame (x_chamber = rotation * x_object + position); mesh, the surface in the chamber frame (mm) for meshes (one patch per VisRad surface element) and nothing otherwise; albedo, conversion (x-ray conversion efficiency) and reflectivity (laser); the flags radiosity, laser (direct laser deposition) and visible; and color (RGB in [0, 1]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.VisRadObject"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.VisRadWorkspace","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"VisRadWorkspace\n\nContents of a VisRad workspace file (read_visrad_workspace): path, VisRad version string, objects (VisRadObjects in file order), beams (Beams), clusters (beam cluster names in file order), and rows, the raw beam records (one NamedTuple per beam with every column of the beam table, the reference frame name and any power tables).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.VisRadWorkspace"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.read_visrad_workspace-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"read_visrad_workspace(path; template = nothing, absent = :off, spot_definition = :auto,\n                      rotation_sense = :beam, angle_tol = 0.05) -> VisRadWorkspace\n\nRead a VisRad workspace (.vrw) with its target components, including CAD imported as generalized meshes, and its laser beams. Both of VisRad's formats are read: the text format of VisRad 20–21 (\"Workspace Format ID = 11\") and the JSON format VisRad 21 writes when it saves a computed workspace (\"Workspace Format ID\": 1001). The two formats contain the same objects, meshes, beam rows and beams, except that the JSON file stores the beam port angles and f-numbers to full precision (the text table rounds them to 3 decimals) and colors to 6 digits. Lengths are converted from cm to mm; every object and beam frame is resolved to the target-chamber frame. Build a Briareus target from the components with visrad_target.\n\nComponents become VisRadObjects. CAD meshes keep VisRad's surface elements as patches (one per triangle). Spheres, rectangles and cylindrical hohlraums are meshed on VisRad's grid when converted by visrad_target. Objects are placed by VisRad's orientation angles and position (VR/grid/specifying_orient_angles.html), relative to the chamber or to another component. The parameters that set the geometry and material of an object (its position, coordinate type and orientation angles, the lengths and mesh counts its primitive uses, the albedo, conversion and reflectivity values, and the radiosity and laser-deposition flags) are required: a missing or malformed one throws an ArgumentError naming the key and the object. Flags that only modify (normals flipped, wall thickness, time-dependent models, the key-point reference) default to 0, visibility to on and the color to gray; a malformed value of any key throws.\n\nBeams. Port angles and f-number come from the file. Pointing at TCC or at a point given in the chamber frame or a component frame is supported; the point becomes the aim and the focus offset (cm) becomes the defocus (mm), as in read_visrad_summary. Power is the 3ω (blue cone) power in TW for beams with 3ω on, and 0 otherwise. It is the power column times the beam power multiplier when enabled and, under VisRad's \"Fraction of Total Beam Power\" red/green model, where that column is the total, also times 1 - f_G - f_R. Spots are VisRad's super-Gaussian radii (µm), exponent and minor-axis rotation, converted as in read_visrad_summary. Radii that match a built-in phase plate (VISRAD_PHASE_PLATES) are taken at the level of that plate (HWHM for NIF CPPs) and other radii at spot_definition (:auto = 1/e). The rotation of a NIF beam is relative to the horizontal of its quad; the quad comes from the template or, without one, from the beam name. Unconverted light, time-dependent powers, energy normalization and DPR are warned about and not imported.\n\nWith a template (e.g. nif_beams()), the pointing, power and spots in the file are applied to the template beams by name, and their other properties (halo, wavelength, quad, group) are kept; port angles are checked to angle_tol degrees and absent handles template beams missing from the file, as in read_visrad_summary. Without one, beams are built from the file, grouped by VisRad cluster.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.read_visrad_workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_component-Tuple{VisRadObject}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_component(object; material = nothing, two_sided = false, orient = false,\n                 toward = (0, 0, 0), coarsen = nothing, remesh = nothing,\n                 fit = remesh !== nothing, primitive_h = nothing) -> Vector{Component}\n\nBriareus components for one VisRadObject, in the chamber frame (mm). The material defaults to the albedo and x-ray conversion efficiency of the object. A CAD mesh becomes one component radiating from the front of VisRad's surface elements (counterclockwise winding, assumption), or from both sides when two_sided. VisRad does not let users edit the normals of imported CAD (VR/grid/import_export_cad_files.html), and tessellated CAD often faces the wrong way: in the NIF workspace this reader was built from, the inner hohlraum wall faces away from the cavity. orient = true repairs the winding with orient_mesh, so that it is consistent across shared edges, closed solids face out of their material and open sheets face the point toward (mm, chamber frame; by default the target chamber center). With two_sided = true the surfaces instead radiate from both sides, and thin shells are then modeled as two radiating sides. CAD needs fewer, compact elements before view factors: remesh (mm) divides the surface into near-planar elements of about that size whatever the facet shapes (remesh_mesh; long slivers are cut), while coarsen (mm) only merges whole near-coplanar facets up to that size (coarsen_mesh; slivers longer than the size stay). fit is on by default with remesh, or is a named tuple of curved_components and fit_surfaces keywords. It then splits a remeshed CAD part into its cylinders, cones and spheres and the rest (curved_components). Each curved piece is given its exact surface fitted to the CAD facets, and curved faces of under 10⁻³ of the part area stay flat unless minfraction = 0. The radiation model then integrates on the curved walls as it does for a primitive hohlraum. The pieces, <name>/cyl1, …, <name>/rest, keep the object name as their part, which the name-taking functions and reports use (see cad_target); fit = false keeps one flat component.\n\nA rectangle becomes one component (normal +z before orientation) and a sphere one with a SphereSurface. A cylindrical hohlraum becomes five components, <name>_wall, _cap_top, _cap_bottom and the apertures _leh_top and _leh_bottom, with the curved surfaces hohlraum uses. All are meshed on VisRad's grid (Number of mesh pts, which reproduces VisRad's element table), or with primitive_h (mm) on Briareus's grid for elements of about that size (hohlraum_counts, capsule_counts), e.g. to resolve a spot on a foil that VisRad meshes coarsely. No components are made for other object types, and a warning is issued.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_component"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_deposition","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_deposition(workspace, target = visrad_target(workspace);\n                  sources = visrad_power_sources(workspace)) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nVisRad's absorbed laser power per element of target (TW), from the direct laser power source of a computed workspace (visrad_power_sources). Each value is the source of a VisRad surface element (TW/cm²) times its area. This is the convention of deposit(...).element, so VisRad's and Briareus's depositions on the same target compare per element and per component (sum(P[elements(target, :wall)])).\n\ntarget must hold the workspace objects as visrad_target(workspace) or visrad_component build them, one component per object (named after it) on the VisRad elements. Each component is checked against the elements of its object, and a component remeshed, coarsened, fitted or cut to a cavity is refused. The target compared with VisRad is therefore not the remeshed target of a radiation study (see visrad_target): build both from the workspace, and compare depositions on this one. Elements are matched as follows.\n\nA CAD mesh (generalized mesh) has one Briareus patch per VisRad surface element, in the connectivity order of the file. The order is that of VisRad. It is confirmed by Briareus's deposition matching VisRad's at every element (correlation 1.0000, 0.9% rms with VisRadProfile) on 2388 wall elements of a NIF workspace.\nOn a rectangle on VisRad's grid, VisRad's elements run the local x fastest (measured on a foil workspace).\nOn spheres and cylindrical hohlraums, VisRad's element order is not established; only the counts are. An object of these kinds with any laser power is refused; leave it out of the target (visrad_target(ws; select)) to map the rest.\n\nOn a two-sided component the power is assigned to its front elements (VisRad's table has one value per surface element). Objects with laser power but no component in target are warned about; components without a VisRad table stay zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_deposition"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_power_sources-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_power_sources(workspace; stale = :error) -> (; time, units, tables)\n\nVisRad's power-source results saved in a computed workspace (a .vrw path or a VisRadWorkspace, re-read from its path), taken from the [Power Source Data] section that VisRad writes after computing the power sources. Per object it lists one row per surface element: index (0-based), direct laser power source (TW/cm², the laser power absorbed per area averaged over the element), uniform source, albedo, x-ray conversion efficiency, laser reflectivity, pre-reflection laser power, numbers of red and blue cones hitting the element, and minimum overlap angles at 1ω and 3ω.\n\ntables maps each object name to a NamedTuple of column vectors, named from the section header (:surface_index, :direct_laser_pwr_source, :uniform_source, :albedo, :x_ray_conv_efficiency, :laser_reflectivity, :pre_reflect_laser_pwr, :n_red_cones_hitting_surf, :n_blue_cones_hitting_surf, :min_angle_overlap_1w, :min_angle_overlap_3w in VisRad 20–21); integer columns are Int. units maps column names to the header units (\"TW/cm2\", \"deg\", \"\"), and time is the simulation time (ns) the sources were computed for.\n\nA workspace saved before computing, or changed since, shows \"Power sources are not up to date\" and has no tables: that is an error, or nothing with stale = :nothing. Tables of the same layout in other sections (beam capture and clearance data) are not read. Map the direct laser source onto a Briareus target with visrad_deposition.\n\nJSON workspaces (VisRad 21's format for a computed workspace) contain the same tables. Their \"Power source data\" lists the objects in the order of the target components without names, with values at full precision (the text table rounds the direct source to 5 decimals, 5 × 10⁻⁷ relative on a NIF wall).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_power_sources"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_profile-Tuple{VisRadWorkspace}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_profile(workspace) -> VisRadProfile\n\nVisRad's beam model with the envelope ratio from the workspace for each beam (by name; VisRad's default ratio differs by phase plate, 1.27–1.54 for the NIF plates). Pass it to DepositionOperator(target, beams; profile), or to a Scenario through deposition_kw = (; profile).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_profile"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_target-Tuple{VisRadWorkspace}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_target(workspace; select = o -> o.radiosity || o.laser, materials = Dict(),\n              two_sided = false, orient = false, toward = (0, 0, 0),\n              coarsen = nothing, remesh = nothing, fit = remesh !== nothing,\n              inside = nothing, visibility = :sampled, closed = false) -> Target\n\nTarget built from the components of a VisRad workspace (read_visrad_workspace) for which select(object) is true — by default those that VisRad includes in its radiosity or direct-laser calculation. materials maps object names (Strings or Symbols) to a Material overriding the albedo and conversion efficiency in the file; a name that is not an object of the workspace is an error. two_sided (a Bool, or a function of the object) makes surfaces radiate from both sides; see visrad_component for why CAD meshes often need it, and for orient, toward, remesh, coarsen and fit. These apply to CAD meshes and give repaired windings, elements of about remesh or coarsen mm, and exact cylinders, cones and spheres for the radiation model.\n\nCAD meshes can hold hundreds of thousands of triangles, and each becomes a radiating element. That is fine for ray tracing, as in deposition, clearance and imaging, but too many for view factors; select the parts that matter for the radiation problem, e.g. select = o -> o.folder == \"Hohlraum\" or by name, and remesh them. inside, a point (mm, chamber frame) in the cavity, keeps only the elements that bound the free space around it, as enclosure does with its defaults; enclosure called on the full target gives the options and a per-component account of what was dropped. It needs consistent windings (orient = true). The selection runs on the CAD facets as oriented. remesh or coarsen then divides only the facets kept (much cheaper when most of the assembly lies outside the cavity), and a second selection on the resulting elements drops those that average too little flux. fit then applies to the elements kept and fits them on the kept facets. The CAD meshes go through these steps in cad_target, which takes the other components as they are (into the selection, but not remeshed or fitted); it does the same for CAD read from STL files. visibility is the default view_factors visibility rule of the target. It is :sampled, as for cad_target, because workspaces contain entrance-hole washers and CAD with rims. closed = true declares the target a closed enclosure (balanced view factors), as for cad_target; it applies only when apertures close every opening (see open_fraction).\n\nTwo targets from the same workspace are usually built, one for a comparison with VisRad and one for a radiation study. visrad_deposition maps VisRad's per-element deposition only onto components on the VisRad elements, so it takes a target built without remesh, coarsen, fit and inside (e.g. visrad_target(ws), with the same select). On that target a DepositionOperator reproduces VisRad's calculation per element, and the view factors are computed on the remeshed, cut target. The same beams are used for both, with one DepositionOperator per target.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_target"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.write_visrad_workspace-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:Beam}}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"write_visrad_workspace(path, template, beams; angle_tol = 0.05, overwrite = false) -> path\n\nA VisRad workspace with the pointing, focus, power and on/off of beams written into a copy of the workspace template (a .vrw file, e.g. the one a design was imported from with read_visrad_workspace). Each beam row of the template whose name matches a beam is given the aim of that beam, as a Cartesian position in the reference frame of the row (cm), its focus offset defocus (cm), its power as the 3ω power column (divided by the power multipliers of the row and, under the \"Fraction of Total Beam Power\" red/green model, by its blue fraction, so that the beam power is in VisRad's blue cone) and on (power > 0) or off. Everything else is copied verbatim: the target components and their frames, the spots, pulse shapes and VisRad's settings stay as the template has them. Numbers are written in place at the column widths and decimals of the template (0.1 µm for positions).\n\nThe results of a computed template (power sources, radiosities, beam capture and clearance) are reset to \"not up to date\" when any row is written: they belong to the old beams. The template must be a text workspace: VisRad 21 saves computed workspaces as JSON, which is read (read_visrad_workspace) but not patched.\n\nA path that already contains VisRad's results — a JSON workspace, or a text one with power sources or radiosities up to date, including the template itself — is refused unless overwrite = true: those results exist only in that file.\n\nRows without a matching beam are left as they were, and beams without a row are reported in a warning: the template defines which beams exist. A beam whose port differs from the port of its row by more than angle_tol degrees is refused (ports cannot be changed). Time-dependent power tables and energy normalization in the template are left as they are, so a row that uses them does not take the constant power (with a warning).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.write_visrad_workspace"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.compare_visrad-Tuple{VisRadWorkspace, RadiationSolution}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"compare_visrad(workspace, sol; elements = Dict(), sources = visrad_power_sources(workspace; stale = :nothing),\n               observe = :capsule, lmax = 4, center = nothing, index_base = nothing, io = nothing)\n    -> (; rows, elements)\n\nCompares a Briareus radiation solution sol with VisRad's results for the same workspace, per element. sol is solved on a target with the VisRad elements, from visrad_target(workspace) or a selection of its objects (see visrad_deposition). The solve can start from the VisRad deposition, as in solve(RadiationModel(t), visrad_deposition(workspace, t)), to isolate the radiation solve, or from the Briareus deposition. Three comparisons are made:\n\nLaser deposition comes from the power sources of the workspace, sources, and nothing skips it. Per object, the rows give the absorbed power (\"Laser Pwr Dep\", TW) as Σ source × area against sol.laser. They also give the per-element agreement of the absorbed flux (TW/cm²) on the elements that either side lights.\nelements holds VisRad's Surface Element Properties export per object, as a Dict from object names to paths or read_visrad_table tables, mapped as in visrad_element_values. Each column of the table among \"Flux In\" (sol.H), \"Flux Out\" (sol.B), \"Rad T\" and \"Emis T\" is compared per element on the elements that the table lists; \"Rad T\" and \"Emis T\" are the radiation temperatures of H and B. Per object, rows are added for the incident and emitted powers (\"Incident Rad Pwr\", \"Emitted Rad Pwr\"), Σ flux × area in TW. A row is also added for \"Rad T\", the radiation temperature (eV) of the area-mean incident flux. Each of these rows uses the elements that both sides have.\nobserve names the component whose incident flux is analyzed in spherical harmonics, such as the capsule. Both sides use the same exact solid-angle weights (ylm_weights to lmax, about center). center defaults to the object position, the origin of the VisRad angles for that object; for a CAD mesh it is the bounding-box center. The rows are \"P2/P0\" and so on, the m = 0 coefficient as a Legendre ratio aₗ/a₀. Rows \"mode amplitude l=2\" and so on give √((2l+1) Σₘ cₗₘ²) / c₀₀ for the orthonormal coefficients cₗₘ, which is |aₗ/a₀| for an axisymmetric field. All modes for l = 1, …, lmax are in percent. The analysis needs the \"Flux In\" of that object over all its elements. A table that VisRad fits itself (Analysis | Statistics) fits element centroids by least squares (visrad_ylm_fit). The weights in compare_visrad integrate the solid angle of each element exactly, so both sides are analyzed alike.\n\nReturns rows and elements. rows holds (; quantity, unit, briareus, visrad, difference, relative) entries, as the kit form of compare_visrad gives them, with modes in percent. elements holds one (; object, quantity, unit, n, briareus, visrad, rms, max, correlation) per object and quantity. Its fields are the area-weighted means of both sides, the area-weighted rms and the largest absolute difference as fractions of VisRad's mean, and the area-weighted correlation. Both are printed to io when given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.compare_visrad"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/visrad/#Briareus.visrad_element_values-Tuple{VisRadWorkspace, Target, AbstractDict, Regex}","page":"VisRad interchange","text":"visrad_element_values(workspace, target, tables, column; index_base = nothing) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nMaps one column of VisRad's per-element results onto the elements of target. The target must be built on the VisRad elements, from visrad_target(workspace) or a selection of its objects without remesh, coarsen, fit or inside. visrad_deposition describes the element orders that result. tables maps object names to VisRad's Surface Element Properties export of that object, which is saved with File | Save Data for Object or as the *.sed file of a run. Each entry is a path, or a table as read_visrad_table returns it. column is a Regex matched against the table header (e.g. r\"flux\\s*out\"i, r\"rad\\s*t\"i).\n\nRows are matched to VisRad's surface elements by the \"Surf Index\" column of the table, which may list a subset in any order. Without that column the rows must be the elements of the object in order. VisRad does not document whether the index counts from 0, as in the power-source tables of the workspace, or from 1 (assumption). With index_base = nothing, the base is 0 when an index is 0 and 1 when the largest index equals the element count; otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown. Pass index_base = 0 or 1 to fix it. Fluxes, with headers in TW/cm², TW/mm² or W/cm², are converted to the Briareus unit TW/mm², the unit of H and B in a RadiationSolution. Other columns, such as temperatures in eV, are returned as they are. A two-sided component takes the values on its front elements. Elements without a value are NaN. An object named in tables that is not in the workspace throws an error, and one without a component in target is skipped with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Briareus.visrad_element_values"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#Diagnostic-windows-in-the-hohlraum-wall","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"To see the capsule from the side during the implosion, a diagnostic needs a line of sight through the hohlraum wall. A hole would lose too much drive, so a thin-foil window is set in the wall instead: a disk of gold about 2 mm across and a couple of micrometers thick, backed by a low-Z material that x rays above a few keV pass through. Early in the pulse the thin gold re-emits like the rest of the wall. The Marshak heat front that drives the x rays into the wall burns through it during the drive, and from then on part of the radiation that reaches the window passes through it and leaves: the waist of the capsule loses drive, and the poles, relatively, gain it. Two windows facing each other on the waist give a positive P_2 and an m = 2 asymmetry, and they cost drive. Assembly leaves a thin annular gap around each window that leaks from the start.\n\nThis tutorial meshes circular windows with their gaps into the wall, checks the heat-front model that predicts when a window burns through against published solutions and measurements, and then follows a pair of windows through a NIF-like drive for three window thicknesses, with and without a gap. It then lets the transmission follow the drive and the gaps close, and restores the drive the windows take, with quad powers or quad pointing. It introduces WallWindow disks and gaps, the BurnthroughAlbedo thin-foil model with its HeatFront and its transmission after burnthrough, GapClosure, the window accounting of drive_history and energy_balance, and a Scenario linearized about a solution.\n\nusing Briareus\nusing CairoMakie\nusing Printf\nCairoMakie.activate!(type = \"png\", px_per_unit = 2)\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(Briareus), \"docs\", \"literate\", \"_plotting.jl\"))   # figure helpers\nset_theme!(fontsize = 15, linewidth = 2.5)","title":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#A-circular-window-on-the-wall","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"A WallWindow with a diameter is a disk drawn on the unrolled wall: arc length s = Rphi along the wall by z along the axis. Unrolling a cylinder is an isometry, so the window area is pi d^24. A gap adds a ring of that width around it, a separate component (here the default, an open aperture).\n\nhohlraum cuts a square box around the window and its gap out of the conforming wall grid, half a wall cell wider than the gap, and re-meshes it with triangles: polar rings inside the window, one band of triangles across the gap, and a band joining the outer edge of the gap to the vertices the box shares with the grid around it, so the mesh has no T-junctions. Every vertex lies on the cylinder. A triangle with its vertices on the cylinder has a curved area of exactly R times its area in (phi z); the radiation model already uses that exact area (see the Integrating sphere tutorial for why curved areas matter). The window edge is a regular polygon whose radius is enlarged to equal_area_radius, so the polygon area is exactly that of the disk. The window and the gap therefore have exact areas, while their edges stay within a few micrometers of the circles.\n\nd, gap = 2.0, 0.05\npair = [WallWindow(phi = φ, diameter = d, gap = gap) for φ in (78, 258)]\ntarget = hohlraum(length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55, capsule_radius = 0.9,\n                  windows = pair)\narea(name) = sum(target.elem_area[elements(target, name)])\nexact = (window = π * d^2 / 4, gap = π * ((d / 2 + gap)^2 - (d / 2)^2))\nfor (name, ref) in ((:window_1, exact.window), (:window_1_gap, exact.gap),\n                    (:window_2, exact.window), (:window_2_gap, exact.gap))\n    @printf(\"%-14s %4d elements  area %.12f mm²  exact %.12f  relative error %.1e\\n\", name,\n            length(elements(target, name)), area(name), ref, area(name) / ref - 1)\nend\nR, L = 2.875, 10.1\n@printf(\"wall + windows + gaps: %.12f mm², 2πRL = %.12f\\n\",\n        area(:wall) + area(:window_1) + area(:window_1_gap) + area(:window_2) + area(:window_2_gap),\n        2π * R * L)\nwindow_1        112 elements  area 3.141592653590 mm²  exact 3.141592653590  relative error 2.2e-16\nwindow_1_gap     64 elements  area 0.322013246993 mm²  exact 0.322013246993  relative error 3.1e-15\nwindow_2        112 elements  area 3.141592653590 mm²  exact 3.141592653590  relative error -4.4e-16\nwindow_2_gap     64 elements  area 0.322013246993 mm²  exact 0.322013246993  relative error 7.1e-15\nwall + windows + gaps: 182.447993357227 mm², 2πRL = 182.447993357227\n\nThe unrolled mesh around the first window is plotted below. The outer vertices of the box lie on the grid, the gap is one band of triangles 50 µm wide, and the window elements are about the size of the wall elements:\n\nfunction unrolled(target, φc)\n    fig = Figure(size = (800, 640))\n    ax = Axis(fig[1, 1], aspect = DataAspect(), xlabel = \"arc length from the window center (mm)\",\n              ylabel = \"z (mm)\", title = \"Window, gap and wall near φ = $(φc)°\")\n    colors = Dict(:wall => (:gray85, \"wall\"), :window_1 => (:goldenrod1, \"window\"),\n                  :window_1_gap => (:dodgerblue3, \"gap\"))\n    uv(p) = Point2f(R * (mod(atan(p[2], p[1]) - deg2rad(φc) + π, 2π) - π), p[3])\n    for k in eachindex(target.tri_area)\n        name = target.components[target.tri_comp[k]].name\n        haskey(colors, name) || continue\n        v = target.tri_v0[k]\n        pts = uv.((v, v + target.tri_e1[k], v + target.tri_e2[k]))\n        all(p -> abs(p[1]) < 1.6 && abs(p[2]) < 1.6, pts) || continue\n        poly!(ax, collect(pts); color = colors[name][1], strokecolor = :gray20, strokewidth = 0.6)\n    end\n    limits!(ax, -1.6, 1.6, -1.6, 1.6)\n    Legend(fig[1, 2], [PolyElement(color = c, strokecolor = :gray20, strokewidth = 0.6)\n                       for (c, _) in values(colors)], [l for (_, l) in values(colors)])\n    return fig\nend\nunrolled(target, 78)\nFigure()","title":"A circular window on the wall"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#When-a-thin-gold-foil-burns-through","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"Radiation of temperature T drives a subsonic, ablative heat front into a gold wall. Hammer and Rosen solved this Marshak wave self-similarly with power-law fits to the opacity and equation of state of gold (Phys. Plasmas 10, 1829, 2003). At constant T the areal mass behind the front is\n\nm_F = 951times10^-4left(fracT100 mathrmeVright)^1914\n      left(fract1 mathrmnsright)^05156 mathrmgcm^2\n\n(their eq. 112 for a constant drive), the default HeatFront. A window burns through when m_F reaches the window areal mass, 193 mathrmgcm^3 times its thickness, 3.86 mg/cm² for 2 µm. The temperature of a hohlraum is far from constant, so the front is integrated over the drive history:\n\nm_F(t) = cleftint_0^t left(fracT(t)100 mathrmeVright)^ab dtright^b \nqquad c = 951times10^-4 a = 1914 b = 05156 \n\nThis depends only on the drive history and is exact at constant T. For a ramp T = T_0 t^k it gives m_F = c(1 + kab)^-bT(t)^a t^b. The exact self-similar solutions for ramps are independent references for it.\n\nheat_front integrates the drive by the trapezoidal rule. At constant temperature on an uneven grid it reproduces the closed form:\n\nfront = HeatFront()\n(; coefficient, temperature_exponent, time_exponent) = front\nts = [0.0, 0.05, 0.3, 0.31, 1.0, 2.2, 4.0]\nm = heat_front(front, ts, fill(300.0, length(ts)))\n@printf(\"constant 300 eV: largest relative error %.1e\\n\",\n        maximum(abs.(m[2:end] ./ (coefficient * 3.0^temperature_exponent .* ts[2:end] .^ time_exponent) .- 1)))\nconstant 300 eV: largest relative error 2.2e-16\n\nFor ramps, compare with two exact solutions:\n\nHammer and Rosen give 951times10^-4 at constant temperature and 713times10^-4 for T propto t^0182 (their eq. 112, q_0 = 1).\nShussman and Heizler's exact solution of the same problem (Phys. Plasmas 22, 082109, 2015, eqs. 35–36) gives 1017times10^-4 at constant temperature and 832times10^-4 at constant absorbed flux, T propto t^0123.\n\nThe two solutions differ by 7% at constant temperature, so the test is the ratio of each ramp to the matching constant-temperature coefficient. Hammer and Rosen also plot the ablated mass at 4 ns for T_s = (t1mathrmns)^0182 hectovolts: about 2.2–2.4 mg/cm² from both HYDRA and the analytic curve (their Fig. 4b).\n\nramp_mass(k, T0, t) = heat_front(front, range(0, t; length = 40001),\n                                 T0 .* range(0, t; length = 40001) .^ k)[end]\nfor (k, exact_ratio, source) in ((0.182, 7.13 / 9.51, \"Hammer & Rosen\"),\n                                 (0.123, 8.32 / 10.17, \"Shussman & Heizler\"))\n    ratio = ramp_mass(k, 100.0, 1.0) / ramp_mass(0.0, 100.0, 1.0)\n    @printf(\"T ∝ t^%.3f: m(k)/m(0) = %.4f, exact %.4f (%s): %+.1f%%\\n\", k, ratio, exact_ratio,\n            source, 100 * (ratio / exact_ratio - 1))\nend\n@printf(\"ablated mass at 4 ns, T = t^0.182 heV: %.2f mg/cm² (HYDRA and analytic: 2.2–2.4)\\n\",\n        1e3 * ramp_mass(0.182, 100.0, 4.0))\nT ∝ t^0.182: m(k)/m(0) = 0.7663, exact 0.7497 (Hammer & Rosen): +2.2%\nT ∝ t^0.123: m(k)/m(0) = 0.8237, exact 0.8181 (Shussman & Heizler): +0.7%\nablated mass at 4 ns, T = t^0.182 heV: 2.41 mg/cm² (HYDRA and analytic: 2.2–2.4)\n\nThe integrated front is 2.2% too heavy for Hammer and Rosen's ramp and 0.7% for Shussman and Heizler's. The residual has a known cause: the shape of the self-similar profile depends on the drive history, which a single integral of the temperature cannot carry. Two percent in mass is four percent in burnthrough time.","title":"When a thin gold foil burns through"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#Against-measured-burnthrough","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"The Nova experiments measured burnthrough of gold foils of 0.8–3 µm on the wall of a hohlraum near 260 eV. In those experiments the radiation temperature rose as about t^015 under a flat laser pulse (Lindl et al., Phys. Plasmas 11, 339, 2004, Fig. 4-6, which compares the data with a heat-front curve for that drive). The times below are the half-signal times read from that figure, to about ±0.03 ns. burnthrough_time gives the arrival of the heat front for the same drive, and BurnthroughAlbedo starts its transition there, reaching half transmission width/2 (0.2 ns by default) later:\n\nnova = [(0.77, 0.33), (1.07, 0.565), (1.55, 0.78), (1.93, 0.86), (2.05, 1.03), (2.27, 1.14),\n        (3.0, 1.52)]\ntn = range(0, 2.5; length = 2501)\nTnova = 260 .* tn .^ 0.15\nprintln(\"  Au (µm)   measured (ns)   front arrival   + width/2   constant 260 eV\")\noffsets = Float64[]\nfor (x, measured) in nova\n    model = BurnthroughAlbedo(thickness = x)\n    tb = burnthrough_time(model, tn, Tnova)\n    push!(offsets, measured - tb)\n    @printf(\"  %5.2f     %5.2f           %5.2f           %5.2f       %5.2f\\n\", x, measured, tb,\n            tb + model.width / 2, burnthrough_time(model, [0.0, 2.5], [260.0, 260.0]))\nend\n@printf(\"measured − front arrival: %.2f ns on average (%.2f–%.2f)\\n\",\n        sum(offsets) / length(offsets), extrema(offsets)...)\n  Au (µm)   measured (ns)   front arrival   + width/2   constant 260 eV\n   0.77      0.33            0.24            0.44        0.07\n   1.07      0.56            0.36            0.56        0.13\n   1.55      0.78            0.57            0.77        0.27\n   1.93      0.86            0.75            0.95        0.41\n   2.05      1.03            0.80            1.00        0.46\n   2.27      1.14            0.91            1.11        0.56\n   3.00      1.52            1.29            1.49        0.96\nmeasured − front arrival: 0.19 ns on average (0.09–0.23)\n\nThe front tracks the thickness dependence of the data: about 0.8 ns from 0.8 to 2.3 µm, and 1.2 ns out to 3 µm. The measured half signal comes 0.1–0.25 ns after the front arrives, and the default transition width puts half transmission there. A constant-temperature estimate would burn the foils through three times too early.\n\nThat timing is calibrated on bare foils under Nova's slowly rising drive. Under an ignition-scale drive a 1-D gold foil (examples/tamped_foil.jl) starts transmitting about 0.6 ns after the front arrives and passes about half the energy the default transition and transmission give it; BurnthroughAlbedo lists the numbers, and a study of such a drive should bracket the start time and transmission of a window.\n\nBefore the front arrives, the foil absorbs exactly what a thick wall does: in the diffusion picture the cold gold ahead of a nonlinear front does not change the flux into it, and the measurements show no early transmission. So the thin-foil albedo follows the same Marshak scaling as the wall, WallLossAlbedo, until burnthrough.","title":"Against measured burnthrough"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#After-burnthrough","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"A burned-through foil does not vanish. It is hot, held in place by its backing, and still optically thick. A gray, planar foil of optical depth tau in the Eddington approximation with a Marshak boundary condition transmits\n\nfracF_mathrmtransF_mathrminc = frac43tau + 23 = frac43tau + 2\n\nof the flux on it (R. S. Lester et al., Phys. Rev. E 114, L023201, 2026, eq. 2; eddington_transmission). Their eq. 1 sets tau = Llambda_mathrmAu(T_h), with lambda_mathrmAu the Rosseland mean free path of gold at the radiation temperature of the hohlraum, and gives tau sim 5 for L = 25 µm (gold at an assumed density of about 1 g/cm³, T_h typically 300–350 eV). BurnthroughAlbedo keeps that mean free path and scales tau with the areal mass of the foil (optical_depth). A fraction mathcalT of the flux on a burned-through window leaves the hohlraum. The rest meets the foil as before and is re-emitted with the Marshak albedo of the wall, so across the transition the albedo falls smoothly from alpha_mathrmbefore to (1 - mathcalT)alpha_mathrmbefore. For the windows below:\n\nfor x in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6)\n    model = BurnthroughAlbedo(thickness = x)\n    @printf(\"%.1f µm: τ = %.1f, transmission %.3f\\n\", x, optical_depth(model),\n            model.transmission)\nend\n1.7 µm: τ = 3.4, transmission 0.328\n2.5 µm: τ = 5.0, transmission 0.235\n3.6 µm: τ = 7.2, transmission 0.169\n\nThis is a gray estimate with the mean free path held at one temperature. The limit it replaces, after = 0, makes a burned-through window an open hole: everything that reaches it leaves, an upper bound on the loss.\n\nGold's Rosseland mean free path grows with temperature. Hammer and Rosen's power-law fit to its opacity, 1K = gT^15rho^-02 with g = 17200 g/cm² and T in hectovolts (their eq. 31, fitted over 100–200 eV), puts the as-built areal mass of a 2.5 µm foil, at the expanded density of 1 g/cm³ that Lester et al. assume, at tau = 59 for 325 eV: within 20% of their tau sim 5, from independent physics. transmission = RosselandTransmission() keeps their point at 325 eV, the middle of their 300–350 eV, and scales tau as T^-15 with the radiation temperature of the flux on the foil (foil_transmission):\n\n@printf(\"Hammer & Rosen, 2.5 µm at 1 g/cm³ and 325 eV: τ = %.2f\\n\", 19.3 * 2.5e-4 * 7200 * 3.25^-1.5)\nprintln(\"  Au (µm)   transmission at 250, 300, 325, 350 eV\")\nfor x in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6)\n    model = BurnthroughAlbedo(thickness = x, transmission = RosselandTransmission())\n    @printf(\"  %.1f       %s\\n\", x, join((@sprintf(\"%.3f\", foil_transmission(model, T))\n                                         for T in (250, 300, 325, 350)), \"  \"))\nend\nHammer & Rosen, 2.5 µm at 1 g/cm³ and 325 eV: τ = 5.93\n  Au (µm)   transmission at 250, 300, 325, 350 eV\n  1.7       0.234  0.296  0.328  0.359\n  2.5       0.165  0.211  0.235  0.259\n  3.6       0.118  0.152  0.169  0.188\n\nA foil that burns through while the hohlraum is cooler than 325 eV transmits less than the constant estimate, and one in a hotter hohlraum more.","title":"After burnthrough"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#A-pair-of-windows-in-a-NIF-like-hohlraum","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"The hohlraum of the Freeing a bundle for radiography tutorial (10.1 × 5.75 mm, with 3.1 mm entrance holes and a 0.9 mm capsule) has a gold wall with the Marshak albedo of WallLossAlbedo and a laser-to-x-ray conversion of 0.8. Its 48 quads are pointed as there. Each quad follows a pulse with a picket, long foot, second step and 2 ns peak at 9.5 TW per quad (456 TW, 1.6 MJ in all). Two windows 2 mm across sit at the waist at φ = 78° and 258°, a pair related by the 180° rotation about the axis, which the NIF ports share; their foils are 1.7, 2.5 or 3.6 µm of gold. The mesh is coarser than the default to keep the tutorial quick.\n\nwall = Material(:Au; albedo = WallLossAlbedo(), conversion = 0.8)\nresolution = (; nz = 24, nphi = 32, capsule_ntheta = 12, capsule_nphi = 24)\nbuild(windows) = hohlraum(; length = 10.1, radius = 2.875, leh_radius = 1.55,\n                          capsule_radius = 0.9, wall, windows, resolution...)\nfoil(thickness; kw...) = Material(:Au; albedo = BurnthroughAlbedo(; thickness, kw...), conversion = 0.8)\nwindows(thickness; gap = 0.0, kw...) =\n    [WallWindow(; phi, diameter = 2.0, gap, material = foil(thickness; kw...)) for phi in (78, 258)]\n\npointing = Dict(:outer50 => (0.0, 5.01), :outer44 => (0.0, 5.20),\n                :inner30 => (0.60, 3.63), :inner23 => (1.09, 3.09))\nfunction aimed(b)\n    r, z = pointing[b.group]\n    φ = port_angles(b)[2]\n    return remake(b; aim = (-r * cosd(φ), -r * sind(φ), sign(b.port[3]) * z))\nend\nquads = aimed.(nif_quads())\npulse = PulseShape([0.0, 0.3, 1.0, 1.4, 4.5, 5.0, 6.0, 7.5, 9.5, 10.0],\n                   [0.0, 1.2, 1.2, 0.3, 0.3, 1.0, 1.0, 9.5, 9.5, 0.0])\n@printf(\"laser energy %.2f MJ, peak power %.0f TW\\n\", 48 * pulse_energy(pulse) / 1e3, 48 * 9.5)\nlaser energy 1.58 MJ, peak power 456 TW\n\ndrive_history marches the radiosity through the pulse quasi-statically. Each window element tracks a separate heat front from its incident flux, so the windows burn through at the times that flux implies. With ylm = true it also records the real spherical harmonics of the capsule flux. The three thicknesses share one geometry, so they share its view factors and its deposition operator.\n\nThe steps are 0.2 ns, but the albedo of a window falls to (1 - mathcalT)alpha_mathrmbefore over the 0.4 ns width of its transition, which two steps would jump across. With transition = 0.05 the march inserts steps wherever the transition of an element would advance by more than a twentieth of the way in one step, planned from the known or predicted burnthrough time of each element. It returns the times it took in time.\n\nThe open-hole bound and the linearized scenarios below are standalone solves, which evaluate the WallLossAlbedo of the wall at the time since the pulse start. The histories do the same here (wall_history = false), so that every comparison is like with like; by default a drive history follows the temperature history of each wall element instead, which lowers the wall albedo early in the peak.\n\ntimes = 0.2:0.2:9.8\nbeams_at(τ) = with_pulses(quads, pulse, τ)\nhistory(model, op, times) = drive_history(model, op, beams_at, times; lmax = 6, ylm = true,\n                                          transition = 0.05, wall_history = false)\nruns = Dict{Tuple{Float64,Float64},Any}()\nshared = Dict{Float64,Any}()                       # view factors and operator per gap\nfor g in (0.0, 0.05), thickness in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6)\n    t = build(windows(thickness; gap = g))\n    if !haskey(shared, g)\n        shared[g] = (RadiationModel(t; closed = true).F, DepositionOperator(t, quads))\n    end\n    F, op = shared[g]\n    runs[(thickness, g)] = history(RadiationModel(t; F), op, times)\nend\n@printf(\"steps: %d given, %d–%d taken\\n\", length(times),\n        extrema(length(h.time) for h in values(runs))...)\nsteps: 49 given, 67–71 taken\n\nThe hohlraum without windows is the reference. It is solved at every time any of the windowed runs took, so the differences below compare like with like.\n\nbase_target = build(())\nbase_model, base_op = RadiationModel(base_target; closed = true), DepositionOperator(base_target, quads)\nrebase(hs) = history(base_model, base_op, sort(unique(reduce(vcat, [h.time for h in hs]))))\nbase = rebase(values(runs))\n@printf(\"reference steps: %d\\n\", length(base.time))\nreference steps: 167\n\nThe table below lists the change in the radiation temperature of the capsule and in P_2 from the hohlraum without windows, the m = 2 amplitude sqrtc_22^2 + c_2-2^2c_00 of the capsule flux, and the heat front of each window as a fraction of its foil. Without windows the radiation temperature of the capsule peaks at the value printed below:\n\non(h, x) = x[indexin(h.time, base.time)]              # base values at the times of run h\nP2(h) = h.moments[3, :] ./ h.moments[1, :]\nP4(h) = h.moments[5, :] ./ h.moments[1, :]\nm2(h) = hypot.(h.ylm[mode_index(2, 2), :], h.ylm[mode_index(2, -2), :]) ./ h.ylm[1, :]\npalette = Makie.wong_colors()\ncolors = Dict(1.7 => palette[6], 2.5 => palette[2], 3.6 => palette[1])\nfig = Figure(size = (820, 700))\naxT = Axis(fig[1, 1], ylabel = \"ΔTᵣ (eV)\", title = \"Change in capsule Tᵣ\")\naxP = Axis(fig[1, 2], ylabel = \"ΔP₂/P₀ (%)\", title = \"Change in P₂ from the windows\")\naxM = Axis(fig[2, 1], xlabel = \"time (ns)\", ylabel = \"|c₂,±₂|/c₀₀ (%)\", title = \"m = 2 amplitude\")\naxF = Axis(fig[2, 2], xlabel = \"time (ns)\", ylabel = \"heat front / foil areal mass\",\n           title = \"Heat front in window 1\")\nhlines!(axT, [0.0], color = :black)\nhlines!(axP, [0.0], color = :black)\nfor thickness in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6), g in (0.0, 0.05)\n    h = runs[(thickness, g)]\n    kw = (; color = colors[thickness], linestyle = g == 0 ? :solid : :dash)\n    lines!(axT, h.time, h.Tr .- on(h, base.Tr); kw...)\n    lines!(axP, h.time, 100 .* (P2(h) .- on(h, P2(base))); kw...)\n    lines!(axM, h.time, 100 .* m2(h); kw...)\n    g == 0 && lines!(axF, h.time, h.front[:window_1]; kw...)\nend\nhlines!(axF, [1.0], color = :gray50, linestyle = :dot)\nylims!(axF, 0, 2)\nLegend(fig[3, 1:2],\n       [[LineElement(color = :black)];\n        [LineElement(color = colors[x]) for x in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6)];\n        [LineElement(color = :gray30), LineElement(color = :gray30, linestyle = :dash)]],\n       [\"no windows (reference)\", \"1.7 µm\", \"2.5 µm\", \"3.6 µm\", \"no gap\", \"50 µm open gap\"],\n       orientation = :horizontal, framevisible = false, nbanks = 1)\n@printf(\"capsule Tᵣ without windows: %.0f eV at the peak\\n\", maximum(base.Tr))\nfig\nFigure()\n\nWithout a gap the windows cost nothing until they burn through: an intact window re-emits like the wall, and its Delta P_2 and m = 2 stay below 0.05%, the size of the remeshing. Then the waist goes cold. Thinner foils burn through earlier (1.7 µm during the second step of the pulse, 2.5 µm on the rise, and 3.6 µm just before the peak) and transmit more. At the peak, P_2 is up by 0.9–1.7%, the m = 2 amplitude reaches 0.7–1.3%, and the radiation temperature of the capsule is down by 2.5–4.7 eV, from 3.6 µm to 1.7 µm. An open 50 µm gap leaks from the start. Before burnthrough the gaps alone give about 0.5% of P_2, 0.4% of m = 2 and 1.3 eV; at the peak they add 0.58% and 1.7–1.8 eV to the cost of the windows.\n\nThe shape of a capsule integrates the drive asymmetry over the pulse. The table therefore gives the change in P_2, P_4 and m = 2 weighted by the capsule flux from 5 ns on, as well as at the peak. It also gives the drive cost: the radiation temperature at the peak, the energy the capsule absorbs over the pulse, and the energy that leaves through the windows and gaps (window_loss). Each history is summed over its time steps, each step weighted by half the time to its neighbors (the first and last by a whole step), which on the uniform times is the plain sum times the step.\n\nfunction weights(τ)\n    w = [(τ[min(k + 1, end)] - τ[max(k - 1, 1)]) / 2 for k in eachindex(τ)]\n    w[1] *= 2\n    w[end] *= 2\n    return w\nend\nintegral(h, x) = sum(weights(h.time) .* x)\nfunction flux_weighted(h, x; from = 5.0)\n    w = weights(h.time) .* (h.time .>= from) .* h.moments[1, :]\n    return sum(w .* x) / sum(w)\nend\nfunction peak_mean(h, x)\n    w = weights(h.time) .* (7.6 .<= h.time .<= 9.4)\n    return sum(w .* x) / sum(w)\nend\nprintln(\"  Au    gap   burnthrough  ΔTᵣ peak  capsule energy  window loss   ΔP₂     ΔP₂     ΔP₄      m=2\")\nprintln(\" (µm)  (µm)     (ns)        (eV)       (%)           (kJ)       peak   weighted weighted weighted\")\nfor g in (0.0, 0.05), thickness in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6)\n    h = runs[(thickness, g)]\n    tb = h.burnthrough[:window_1]\n    @printf(\" %.1f   %3.0f     %5.2f      %+6.1f      %+6.1f        %6.0f       %+5.2f%%  %+5.2f%%  %+5.2f%%   %4.2f%%\\n\",\n            thickness, 1e3g, sum(tb) / length(tb), peak_mean(h, h.Tr) - peak_mean(base, base.Tr),\n            100 * (integral(h, h.flux) / integral(base, base.flux) - 1), integral(h, h.window_loss),\n            100 * (peak_mean(h, P2(h)) - peak_mean(base, P2(base))),\n            100 * (flux_weighted(h, P2(h)) - flux_weighted(base, P2(base))),\n            100 * (flux_weighted(h, P4(h)) - flux_weighted(base, P4(base))),\n            100 * flux_weighted(h, m2(h)))\nend\n  Au    gap   burnthrough  ΔTᵣ peak  capsule energy  window loss   ΔP₂     ΔP₂     ΔP₄      m=2\n (µm)  (µm)     (ns)        (eV)       (%)           (kJ)       peak   weighted weighted weighted\n 1.7     0      4.77        -4.7        -5.5            58       +1.72%  +1.70%  -0.22%   1.30%\n 2.5     0      6.34        -3.4        -3.8            40       +1.22%  +1.12%  -0.14%   0.86%\n 3.6     0      7.05        -2.5        -2.4            24       +0.87%  +0.67%  -0.08%   0.52%\n 1.7    50      4.79        -6.5        -7.4            76       +2.30%  +2.28%  -0.28%   1.73%\n 2.5    50      6.35        -5.2        -5.8            58       +1.80%  +1.69%  -0.20%   1.28%\n 3.6    50      7.06        -4.2        -4.4            43       +1.44%  +1.24%  -0.14%   0.93%\n\nA thinner foil burns through earlier and transmits more, so the thin foils cost the most. The flux-weighted Delta P_2 rises from 0.7% for 3.6 µm to 1.7% for 1.7 µm (1.2–2.3% with the gaps), and the capsule loses 2.3–5.4% of its energy (4.4–7.4% with the gaps).\n\nThe open hole bounds these losses from above. At the peak every window has burned through, so the albedos no longer depend on when each element did: a solve at each peak step with after = 0 and every window element burned through gives the open-hole history there exactly. It reuses the view factors and the deposition operator of the runs above.\n\npeak_times = filter(τ -> 7.6 <= τ <= 9.4, collect(times))\nat_peak(h, x) = (w = weights(peak_times); sum(w .* x[indexin(peak_times, h.time)]) / sum(w))\nfunction open_hole(g)\n    hole = Material(:Au; albedo = BurnthroughAlbedo(thickness = 2.5, after = 0), conversion = 0.8)\n    t = build([WallWindow(; phi, diameter = 2.0, gap = g, material = hole) for phi in (78, 258)])\n    F, op = shared[g]\n    model = RadiationModel(t; F)\n    obs = elements(t, :capsule)\n    W = ylm_weights(t, obs; lmax = 6)\n    burned = zeros(nelements(t))                   # burnthrough times: all at 0 ns\n    sols = [solve(model, deposit(op, beams_at(τ)); time = τ, burnthrough = burned)\n            for τ in peak_times]\n    moments = reduce(hcat, [legendre_moments(s.H, t, obs; lmax = 6) for s in sols])\n    return (; time = peak_times, moments, Tr = radiation_temperature.(moments[1, :]),\n            ylm = reduce(hcat, [W * s.H[obs] for s in sols]))\nend\nprintln(\"  peak, 7.6–9.4 ns              gap    ΔTᵣ     ΔP₂      m=2\")\nprintln(\"                                (µm)   (eV)\")\nfor g in (0.0, 0.05)\n    transmitting = [(@sprintf(\"transmission %.3f (%.1f µm)\",\n                              BurnthroughAlbedo(thickness = x).transmission, x), runs[(x, g)])\n                    for x in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6)]\n    for (label, r) in [(\"open hole (after = 0)\", open_hole(g)); transmitting]\n        @printf(\"  %-28s  %3.0f   %+5.1f   %+5.2f%%   %4.2f%%\\n\", label, 1e3g,\n                at_peak(r, r.Tr) - at_peak(base, base.Tr),\n                100 * (at_peak(r, P2(r)) - at_peak(base, P2(base))), 100 * at_peak(r, m2(r)))\n    end\nend\n  peak, 7.6–9.4 ns              gap    ΔTᵣ     ΔP₂      m=2\n                                (µm)   (eV)\n  open hole (after = 0)           0   -13.7   +5.41%   4.13%\n  transmission 0.328 (1.7 µm)     0    -4.7   +1.72%   1.31%\n  transmission 0.235 (2.5 µm)     0    -3.4   +1.22%   0.93%\n  transmission 0.169 (3.6 µm)     0    -2.5   +0.87%   0.66%\n  open hole (after = 0)          50   -15.2   +6.02%   4.59%\n  transmission 0.328 (1.7 µm)    50    -6.4   +2.30%   1.74%\n  transmission 0.235 (2.5 µm)    50    -5.2   +1.80%   1.35%\n  transmission 0.169 (3.6 µm)    50    -4.2   +1.44%   1.08%\n\nAt the peak the open hole costs three to six times as much as the transmitting foils: 13.6 eV of radiation temperature and 5.4% of P_2 without gaps. The gaps add about the same in either model, 0.58–0.62% of P_2 and 1.6–1.8 eV.\n\nThe pair of windows does not add odd-m harmonics, because it shares the 180° symmetry of the ports:\n\nh = runs[(2.5, 0.05)]\nodd = maximum(abs(h.ylm[mode_index(l, m), k]) / h.ylm[1, k]\n              for l in 1:4 for m in -l:l if isodd(m) for k in eachindex(h.time))\n@printf(\"largest odd-m |c_lm|/c₀₀ over the pulse: %.1e\\n\", odd)\nlargest odd-m |c_lm|/c₀₀ over the pulse: 2.5e-16\n\nThe capsule flux at the end of the peak is mapped below in equal-area (Hammer) projections seen from outside, centered between the windows, which face the waist of the capsule at φ = 78° and 258° (circles). The maps are drawn from the spherical harmonics through l = 6, which contain the low modes a capsule responds to; the coarse capsule mesh is not used for them. The top row shows the flux relative to the mean flux, without windows and with the 2.5 µm pair and its gaps. Without windows this untuned pointing already drives the waist hot, so the bottom row subtracts that map and shows what the windows change: on the left without gaps, on the right with them.\n\nfunction ylm_field(c; lmax = 6)\n    C = ylm_matrix(c, lmax)\n    Y = zeros(lmax + 1, 2lmax + 1)\n    mean = c[1] / sqrt(4π)                          # c₀₀ Y₀₀\n    return (θ, φ) -> (Briareus._real_ylm!(Y, lmax, cosd(θ), deg2rad(φ)); 100 * (sum(C .* Y) / mean - 1))\nend\nφ0 = 168.0                                         # map center, between the windows\ndeviation_map(h) = sphere_map(ylm_field(h.ylm[:, findfirst(≈(9.4), h.time)]); φ0)\nbase_map = deviation_map(base)\nchange_map(h) = (m = deviation_map(h); (m[1], m[2], m[3] .- base_map[3]))\nrows = ([\"no windows\" => base_map, \"2.5 µm windows, 50 µm gaps\" => deviation_map(h)],\n        [\"2.5 µm windows, no gap\" => change_map(runs[(2.5, 0.0)]),\n         \"2.5 µm windows, 50 µm gaps\" => change_map(h)])\nlimits = [ceil(maximum(maximum(abs, filter(isfinite, m[3])) for (_, m) in row)) for row in rows]\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 470))\nfor (i, row) in enumerate(rows), (j, (title, m)) in enumerate(row)\n    ax = sphere_axis(fig[i, j]; title, φ0)\n    sphere_heatmap!(ax, m; colormap = :balance, colorrange = (-limits[i], limits[i]))\n    mark_directions!(ax, [(90, 78), (90, 258)]; φ0, marker = :circle, markersize = 14,\n                     color = :transparent, strokecolor = :black, strokewidth = 2)\nend\nColorbar(fig[1, 3]; colormap = :balance, colorrange = (-limits[1], limits[1]),\n         height = Relative(0.8), label = \"H/⟨H⟩ − 1 (%)\")\nColorbar(fig[2, 3]; colormap = :balance, colorrange = (-limits[2], limits[2]),\n         height = Relative(0.8), label = \"change from no windows (%)\")\nrowgap!(fig.layout, 6)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nWithout windows this untuned pointing drives the waist hot (P_2P_0 approx -12, which cone tuning would remove). With the windows open, the waist facing them cools to about the mean while the waist between them gains, relative to the mean, and the poles gain too. The mean excess at the waist drops, which is the positive Delta P_2, and the pattern cos 2(phi - 78) is the m = 2 asymmetry. The gaps add about half again to it.","title":"A pair of windows in a NIF-like hohlraum"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#The-window-energy-balance","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"At any time, energy_balance splits the loss of a thin-foil window, (1 - alpha) H A, into what it transmits, smathcalT H A with s the progress of its transition (0 intact, 1 burned through), and what it absorbs like the wall, (1 - smathcalT)(1 - alpha_mathrmbefore) H A. The transmitted part plus the escape through the open gap is window_loss. At the end of the peak, for 2.5 µm windows with a 50 µm gap:\n\nt = build(windows(2.5; gap = 0.05))\nF, op = shared[0.05]\nmodel = RadiationModel(t; F)\ntb = fill(NaN, nelements(t))\nfor n in (:window_1, :window_2)\n    tb[elements(t, n)] .= h.burnthrough[n]\nend\nsol = solve(model, deposit(op, beams_at(9.4)); time = 9.4, burnthrough = tb)\nbal = energy_balance(sol)\n@printf(\"source %.1f TW: absorbed %.1f, entrance holes %.1f, windows transmit %.1f, gaps %.1f, lost %.1e\\n\",\n        bal.source, sum(values(bal.absorbed)), bal.escaped[:leh_top] + bal.escaped[:leh_bottom],\n        bal.transmitted[:window_1] + bal.transmitted[:window_2],\n        bal.escaped[:window_1_gap] + bal.escaped[:window_2_gap],\n        bal.source - sum(values(bal.absorbed)) - sum(values(bal.escaped)) - sum(values(bal.transmitted)))\n@printf(\"window loss from the history at 9.4 ns: %.1f TW\\n\", h.window_loss[findfirst(≈(9.4), h.time)])\nincident = sum(sol.H[e] * t.elem_area[e] for n in (:window_1, :window_2) for e in elements(t, n))\n@printf(\"incident on the windows %.1f TW, transmitted fraction %.6f (transmission %.6f)\\n\",\n        incident, (bal.transmitted[:window_1] + bal.transmitted[:window_2]) / incident,\n        BurnthroughAlbedo(thickness = 2.5).transmission)\nsource 363.6 TW: absorbed 193.6, entrance holes 150.5, windows transmit 13.6, gaps 5.9, lost 1.6e-11\nwindow loss from the history at 9.4 ns: 19.6 TW\nincident on the windows 57.9 TW, transmitted fraction 0.235294 (transmission 0.235294)\n\nThe accounting closes within round-off, and the burned-through windows transmit the fraction mathcalT of the power on them.","title":"The window energy balance"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#A-transmission-that-follows-the-drive","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"The same pairs of windows without gaps, with the transmission following the temperature of the flux on each window element, against the constant transmission above. Each history is compared with the hohlraum without windows solved at the same times, with the measures of the table above:\n\nfunction costs(h, b)\n    on_b(x) = x[indexin(h.time, b.time)]\n    return (; burnthrough = sum(h.burnthrough[:window_1]) / length(h.burnthrough[:window_1]),\n            energy = 100 * (integral(h, h.flux) / integral(b, b.flux) - 1),\n            loss = integral(h, h.window_loss),\n            P2 = 100 * (flux_weighted(h, P2(h)) - flux_weighted(b, P2(b))),\n            m2 = 100 * flux_weighted(h, m2(h)), Tr = peak_mean(h, h.Tr) - peak_mean(h, on_b(b.Tr)))\nend\nF0, op0 = shared[0.0]\nfollows = Dict(x => history(RadiationModel(build(windows(x; transmission = RosselandTransmission())); F = F0),\n                            op0, times) for x in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6))\nbase_follows = rebase([collect(values(follows)); [runs[(x, 0.0)] for x in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6)]])\nprintln(\"  Au    transmission   burnthrough  ΔTᵣ peak  capsule energy  window loss  ΔP₂ weighted  m=2 weighted\")\nprintln(\" (µm)                    (ns)        (eV)       (%)            (kJ)\")\nfor x in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6), (label, h) in ((\"constant\", runs[(x, 0.0)]), (\"follows Tᵣ\", follows[x]))\n    c = costs(h, base_follows)\n    @printf(\" %.1f   %-12s    %5.2f      %+5.1f      %+5.1f         %5.0f        %+5.2f%%        %4.2f%%\\n\",\n            x, label, c.burnthrough, c.Tr, c.energy, c.loss, c.P2, c.m2)\nend\n@printf(\"wall Tᵣ at burnthrough: %s eV; at the peak %.0f eV\\n\",\n        join((@sprintf(\"%.0f\", runs[(x, 0.0)].wall_Tr[findfirst(≥(costs(runs[(x, 0.0)], base).burnthrough),\n                                                                 runs[(x, 0.0)].time)])\n              for x in (1.7, 2.5, 3.6)), \", \"), peak_mean(base, base.wall_Tr))\n  Au    transmission   burnthrough  ΔTᵣ peak  capsule energy  window loss  ΔP₂ weighted  m=2 weighted\n (µm)                    (ns)        (eV)       (%)            (kJ)\n 1.7   constant         4.77       -4.7       -5.4            58        +1.70%        1.30%\n 1.7   follows Tᵣ       4.77       -4.4       -4.8            51        +1.49%        1.13%\n 2.5   constant         6.34       -3.4       -3.7            40        +1.12%        0.86%\n 2.5   follows Tᵣ       6.34       -3.2       -3.3            36        +1.01%        0.77%\n 3.6   constant         7.05       -2.5       -2.3            24        +0.67%        0.52%\n 3.6   follows Tᵣ       7.05       -2.3       -2.1            22        +0.62%        0.48%\nwall Tᵣ at burnthrough: 149, 227, 285 eV; at the peak 309 eV\n\nThese windows burn through while the wall is at 150–285 eV, and even at the peak it stays near 310 eV, below the 325 eV the constant transmission is pinned at. The transmission that follows the drive therefore loses about a tenth less. For the 1.7 µm pair the flux-weighted Delta P_2 drops from 1.71% to 1.49% and the capsule-energy cost from 5.4% to 4.8%; for the 3.6 µm pair they drop from 0.68% to 0.62% and from 2.3% to 2.1%. The burnthrough times do not change: the transmission starts only once the front is through.","title":"A transmission that follows the drive"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#Gaps-that-close","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"The 50 µm gaps above stay open through the pulse, but plasma blown off their gold walls fills them. GapClosure makes a gap open (all the flux on it leaves) until a closure time and wall-like after it, across a 0.4 ns step; the closure time is an input this model does not compute. For the 2.5 µm pair with gaps closing at 3 ns (before the windows burn through) and at 7 ns (after):\n\nclosing_gap(tc) = Material(:Au; albedo = GapClosure(closure = tc), conversion = 0.8)\ngapped(tc) = build([WallWindow(; phi, diameter = 2.0, gap = 0.05, gap_material = closing_gap(tc),\n                               material = foil(2.5)) for phi in (78, 258)])\nFg = RadiationModel(gapped(3.0); closed = true).F          # one geometry for both closures\nopg = DepositionOperator(gapped(3.0), quads)\ncloses = Dict(tc => history(RadiationModel(gapped(tc); F = Fg), opg, times) for tc in (3.0, 7.0))\nbase_closes = rebase([collect(values(closes)); runs[(2.5, 0.0)]; runs[(2.5, 0.05)]])\nprintln(\"  2.5 µm windows        ΔTᵣ peak  capsule energy  window loss  ΔP₂ weighted  m=2 weighted\")\nfor (label, h) in ((\"no gap\", runs[(2.5, 0.0)]), (\"gap closing at 3 ns\", closes[3.0]),\n                   (\"gap closing at 7 ns\", closes[7.0]), (\"gap open throughout\", runs[(2.5, 0.05)]))\n    c = costs(h, base_closes)\n    @printf(\"  %-20s   %+5.1f      %+5.1f          %5.0f        %+5.2f%%        %4.2f%%\\n\",\n            label, c.Tr, c.energy, c.loss, c.P2, c.m2)\nend\n  2.5 µm windows        ΔTᵣ peak  capsule energy  window loss  ΔP₂ weighted  m=2 weighted\n  no gap                  -3.4       -3.7             40        +1.13%        0.86%\n  gap closing at 3 ns     -3.4       -3.8             40        +1.14%        0.85%\n  gap closing at 7 ns     -3.4       -4.1             44        +1.23%        0.92%\n  gap open throughout     -5.2       -5.8             58        +1.70%        1.28%\n\nA gap that closes at 3 ns costs nothing measurable beyond the windows: it leaks only during the picket and the foot. Closing at 7 ns, after the windows have burned through but before the peak, it adds 0.4% of capsule energy and 0.10% of P_2, a fifth of what a gap open throughout adds (2.0% and 0.57%). How long a gap stays open thus matters more than its width, and the open-gap rows of the first table are the bound.","title":"Gaps that close"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#Restoring-the-drive-around-the-windows","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"The windows take drive from the waist facing them: at the peak the 2.5 µm pair raises P_2 by 1.2% and adds an m = 2 of 0.9%. Cone pointing and power can restore P_2, but no cone setting can remove m = 2, which needs quads set apart. Two ways to set them: per-quad powers (NIF quads take their own pulse shapes), and per-quad pointing.\n\nThe optimizer needs a linear model of the capsule flux at the peak, with the windows burned through and the wall at its peak albedo. A Scenario with albedo set to the converged albedos of a solution is exactly that: its flux response uses fixed albedos, so it reproduces the solution it was taken from.\n\ntpeak = 8.6\nw25 = runs[(2.5, 0.0)]\ntwin = build(windows(2.5))\nburned = fill(NaN, nelements(twin))\nfor n in (:window_1, :window_2)\n    burned[elements(twin, n)] .= w25.burnthrough[n]\nend\nwin_model = RadiationModel(twin; F = F0)\nsol_win = solve(win_model, deposit(op0, beams_at(tpeak)); time = tpeak, burnthrough = burned)\nsol_base = solve(base_model, deposit(base_op, beams_at(tpeak)); time = tpeak)\npeak = beams_at(tpeak)\nquad_power = GroupPointing(peak; by = :quad, fields = (:power,))\nS_win = Scenario(twin, quad_power; observe = :capsule, time = tpeak, albedo = sol_win.albedo,\n                 viewfactor_kw = (; F = F0))\nS_base = Scenario(base_target, quad_power; observe = :capsule, time = tpeak,\n                  albedo = sol_base.albedo, viewfactor_kw = (; F = base_model.F))\nx0 = initial_parameters(quad_power)\ncapsule = elements(twin, :capsule)\na = legendre_moments(sol_win.H, twin, capsule; lmax = 2)\n@printf(\"P₂/P₀ at %.1f ns with windows: solve %+.6f%%, linearized scenario %+.6f%%\\n\", tpeak,\n        100 * a[3] / a[1], 100 * (b = flux_moments(evaluate(S_win, x0); lmax = 2); b[3] / b[1]))\nP₂/P₀ at 8.6 ns with windows: solve -10.879234%, linearized scenario -10.879234%\n\nThe design without windows is the reference shape: the coefficients c_lmc_00 of its capsule flux at the peak. asymmetry_objective with reference measures the departure from them for every (l m) through l = 4, and drive constrains the mean flux to that of the reference. The power of each quad may change by 20%:\n\nref = flux_ylm(evaluate(S_base, x0))\nrestore = asymmetry_objective(; modes = (1 => 1.0, 2 => 1.0, 3 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0), reference = ref,\n                              drive = ref[1] / sqrt(4π))\nlo, hi = bounds(quad_power, x0; power = 0.2)\ntrim = optimize_pointing(S_win, restore; x0, lower = lo, upper = hi, iterations = 300)\nfunction departure(ev)\n    c = flux_ylm(ev; lmax = 4)\n    d = c ./ c[1] .- ref[1:length(c)] ./ ref[1]\n    return [100 * sqrt(sum(abs2, d[l^2+1:(l+1)^2])) for l in 1:4]\nend\nfactors = trim.x ./ x0\n@printf(\"%s after %d iterations; quad powers ×%.2f–%.2f (%d at a limit), total ×%.3f\\n\",\n        trim.reason, trim.iterations, extrema(factors)..., count(f -> abs(abs(f - 1) - 0.2) < 1e-6, factors),\n        sum(trim.x) / sum(x0))\nfor (label, ev) in ((\"as designed\", evaluate(S_win, x0)), (\"quad powers\", evaluate(S_win, trim.x)))\n    @printf(\"  %-12s  departure from the reference shape, l = 1–4: %s\\n\", label,\n            join((@sprintf(\"%.3f%%\", d) for d in departure(ev)), \", \"))\nend\nfor g in (:inner23, :inner30, :outer44, :outer50)\n    f = factors[[b.group === g for b in peak]]\n    @printf(\"  %-8s quads ×%.3f on average (%.2f–%.2f)\\n\", g, sum(f) / length(f), extrema(f)...)\nend\nconverged after 96 iterations; quad powers ×0.80–1.20 (32 at a limit), total ×1.039\n  as designed   departure from the reference shape, l = 1–4: 0.001%, 1.102%, 0.003%, 0.149%\n  quad powers   departure from the reference shape, l = 1–4: 0.002%, 0.074%, 0.013%, 0.192%\n  inner23  quads ×1.076 on average (0.87–1.20)\n  inner30  quads ×1.170 on average (1.08–1.20)\n  outer44  quads ×1.012 on average (0.80–1.20)\n  outer50  quads ×0.983 on average (0.80–1.20)\n\nThe trim moves power to the inner cones, as the cold waist needs (the 30° cone by 17%), and spreads the quads within each cone over the whole ±20%. It brings the l = 2 departure from 1.10% to 0.07% and keeps l = 1 and l = 3 below 0.02%, but not l = 4, which stays near 0.15–0.19%: the l = 4 pattern of these windows is out of reach of quad powers within the limits. Pointing can do the same: below, the quads are re-pointed at fixed power, up to 0.3 mm in z and along the wall (phi). The windows and the NIF ports share the 180° rotation about the axis, so each quad moves with its partner across the axis, and the entrance-hole and capsule keep-outs hold.\n\npartner(b) = (b.group, sign(b.port[3]), round(Int, mod(port_angles(b)[2], 180)))\npairs = GroupPointing(peak; by = partner, fields = (:z, :phi), mirror = false)\nS_pairs = subset(S_win, eachindex(peak); parameterization = pairs)\nxp = initial_parameters(pairs)\nlo, hi = bounds(pairs, xp; z = 0.3, phi = 0.3)\nshape_only = asymmetry_objective(; modes = (1 => 1.0, 2 => 1.0, 3 => 1.0, 4 => 1.0), reference = ref,\n                                 keepout = (ApertureKeepOut(twin), SphereKeepOut(twin)))\nrepoint = optimize_pointing(S_pairs, shape_only; x0 = xp, lower = lo, upper = hi, iterations = 200)\nmoves = reshape(repoint.x .- xp, 2, :)\n@printf(\"%d quad pairs re-pointed: rms %.0f µm in z, %.0f µm along the wall (largest %.0f, %.0f)\\n\",\n        size(moves, 2), 1e3 * sqrt(sum(abs2, moves[1, :]) / size(moves, 2)),\n        1e3 * sqrt(sum(abs2, moves[2, :]) / size(moves, 2)), 1e3 .* maximum(abs, moves; dims = 2)...)\nev = evaluate(S_pairs, repoint.x)\n@printf(\"  re-pointed    departure from the reference shape, l = 1–4: %s; mean flux %+.1f%%\\n\",\n        join((@sprintf(\"%.3f%%\", d) for d in departure(ev)), \", \"), 100 * (flux_ylm(ev)[1] / ref[1] - 1))\n24 quad pairs re-pointed: rms 137 µm in z, 97 µm along the wall (largest 226, 195)\n  re-pointed    departure from the reference shape, l = 1–4: 0.003%, 0.019%, 0.009%, 0.168%; mean flux -3.4%\n\nMoves of 0.09–0.14 mm rms (two to three times NIF's 50 µm rms pointing specification, the largest 0.25–0.27 mm; the run ends elsewhere on a flat family of designs as rounding takes it) restore l = 1–3 to 0.02%, better than the powers, and leave the same l = 4; pointing alone cannot give back the 3.4% of drive the windows take.\n\nBoth settings were found at the peak, with the windows burned through. Before they burn through, the windows cost nothing, and a trim that is on throughout the pulse then makes the asymmetry it was meant to remove. NIF can set the pulse shape of each quad, so the power trim can instead come on with the burnthrough, across the 0.4 ns transition of the windows; pointing cannot change during the shot. The four designs are compared below through the pulse, against the hohlraum without windows:\n\ntb_mean = sum(w25.burnthrough[:window_1]) / length(w25.burnthrough[:window_1])\nswitch(τ) = (x = clamp((τ - tb_mean) / 0.4, 0, 1); x^2 * (3 - 2x))\nrepointed = apply(pairs, repoint.x)\ndesigns = [\"as designed\" => beams_at,\n           \"quad powers throughout\" => τ -> [remake(b; power = b.power * f)\n                                             for (b, f) in zip(beams_at(τ), factors)],\n           \"quad powers at burnthrough\" => τ -> [remake(b; power = b.power * (1 + switch(τ) * (f - 1)))\n                                                 for (b, f) in zip(beams_at(τ), factors)],\n           \"re-pointed\" => τ -> with_pulses(repointed, pulse, τ)]\nrestored = Dict(label => drive_history(win_model, op0, f, times; lmax = 6, ylm = true, transition = 0.05,\n                                               wall_history = false)\n                for (label, f) in designs)\nbase_restored = rebase(values(restored))\nfig = Figure(size = (820, 380))\naxP = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (ns)\", ylabel = \"ΔP₂/P₀ (%)\", title = \"Change in P₂ from no windows\")\naxM = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"time (ns)\", ylabel = \"|c₂,±₂|/c₀₀ (%)\", title = \"m = 2 amplitude\")\nhlines!(axP, [0.0], color = :black)\nvspan!(axP, tb_mean, tb_mean + 0.4, color = (:gray, 0.2))\nvspan!(axM, tb_mean, tb_mean + 0.4, color = (:gray, 0.2))\nfor ((label, _), color) in zip(designs, palette)\n    h = restored[label]\n    lines!(axP, h.time, 100 .* (P2(h) .- P2(base_restored)[indexin(h.time, base_restored.time)]); color, label)\n    lines!(axM, h.time, 100 .* m2(h); color, label)\nend\nxlims!(axP, 4, 10); xlims!(axM, 4, 10)\nLegend(fig[2, 1:2], axP, orientation = :horizontal, framevisible = false, nbanks = 2)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nspan_mean(h, x, a, b) = (w = weights(h.time) .* (a .<= h.time .<= b); sum(w .* x) / sum(w))\nprintln(\"  design                      laser    ΔTᵣ      ΔP₂ (%)                    m=2 (%)\")\nprintln(\"                              energy   peak     before  peak  weighted    before  peak  weighted\")\nfor (label, f) in designs\n    h = restored[label]\n    c = costs(h, base_restored)\n    energy = integral(h, h.delivered) / integral(base_restored, base_restored.delivered) - 1\n    dP2 = 100 .* (P2(h) .- P2(base_restored)[indexin(h.time, base_restored.time)])\n    @printf(\"  %-26s  %+5.1f%%   %+5.1f    %+5.2f  %+5.2f   %+5.2f       %4.2f  %4.2f   %4.2f\\n\",\n            label, 100 * energy, c.Tr, span_mean(h, dP2, 5.0, tb_mean), peak_mean(h, dP2), c.P2,\n            100 * span_mean(h, m2(h), 5.0, tb_mean), 100 * peak_mean(h, m2(h)), c.m2)\nend\n  design                      laser    ΔTᵣ      ΔP₂ (%)                    m=2 (%)\n                              energy   peak     before  peak  weighted    before  peak  weighted\n\u001b[33m\u001b[1m┌ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[33m\u001b[1mWarning: \u001b[22m\u001b[39mAssignment to `h` in soft scope is ambiguous because a global variable by the same name exists: `h` will be treated as a new local. Disambiguate by using `local h` to suppress this warning or `global h` to assign to the existing global variable.\n\u001b[33m\u001b[1m└ \u001b[22m\u001b[39m\u001b[90m@ diagnostic_windows.md:802\u001b[39m\n  as designed                  +0.0%    -3.4    -0.02  +1.22   +1.13       0.04  0.93   0.86\n  quad powers throughout       +3.9%    +0.2    -1.32  +0.13   +0.02       1.13  0.03   0.10\n  quad powers at burnthrough   +3.3%    +0.2    -0.02  +0.13   +0.11       0.04  0.03   0.03\n  re-pointed                   +0.0%    -2.7    -1.45  +0.00   -0.12       1.18  0.04   0.13\n\n(\"Before\" is from 5 ns to the burnthrough.) The settings that act throughout overshoot before burnthrough: the trimmed powers and the re-pointed quads drive the waist the windows will cool while they are still intact, so P_2 falls by 1.3–1.5% and an m = 2 of 1.1–1.2% in the opposite phase appears until the windows open, which the flux-weighted averages largely hide. Switched on with the burnthrough, the trimmed powers keep P_2 and m = 2 at the no-window values before it (−0.02% and 0.04%) and hold m = 2 at 0.03% after it; the 0.13% of P_2 left at the peak is what the ±20% limits leave (the l = 2 departure above). The trims cost 3.3–3.9% more laser energy, the drive the windows take. The switch time comes from the heat front of this model, so it inherits the uncertainty of the front (about 0.2 ns against the Nova data, above).","title":"Restoring the drive around the windows"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#What-a-gray-view-factor-model-leaves-out","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"The model gives the geometry of the loss exactly, but its physics after burnthrough is the gray estimate above. The foil is planar and steady. Its mean free path follows the temperature only through the T^15 of the opacity fit, at a density held at the assumed expansion, and the fit is extrapolated from 100–200 eV. The open hole, after = 0, bounds the loss from above. The heated backing absorbs part of what the gold transmits and radiates part back; here all of it is lost. The closure time of a closing gap comes from elsewhere (a hydrodynamic estimate or a measurement). The restored designs are linear about the peak; they hold the no-window shape in the quasi-static histories, which use the full temperature-dependent albedos. The view-factor model also has no capsule hydrodynamics: it gives flux asymmetries but not the shape they imprint.","title":"What a gray view-factor model leaves out"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/diagnostic_windows/#What-this-checks","page":"Diagnostic windows in the hohlraum wall","text":"A circular window and its gap have the areas pi d^24 and pi(d2 + g)^2 - (d2)^2 within round-off. The equal-area edge polygon and the exact curved areas of triangles on the cylinder make them exact, and wall, windows and gaps tile the cylinder exactly.\nThe integrated heat front reproduces the constant-temperature closed form within round-off. For ramps it agrees with the exact self-similar solutions of Hammer and Rosen to 2.2% and of Shussman and Heizler to 0.7%, and with Hammer and Rosen's simulated ablated mass. The cause of the residual is identified.\nOver 0.8–3 µm, the heat front and the transition width reproduce the measured burnthrough times of gold foils on a 260 eV hohlraum wall to within the scatter of the data. A constant-temperature estimate does not.\nThe thin-foil energy accounting closes within round-off at a single time, the burned-through windows transmit the fraction mathcalT = 4(3tau + 2) of the power on them within round-off, and the accounting agrees with the window loss the drive history tracks.\nA pair of windows related by the 180° rotation about the axis does not add odd-m harmonics.\nThe temperature-following transmission equals the constant one at its 325 eV reference, and Hammer and Rosen's gold opacity fit puts the 2.5 µm foil at tau = 59 there, within 20% of the tau sim 5 of Lester et al.\nA scenario linearized about the albedos of the peak solution reproduces the P_2 of that solution within round-off.\n\nThe test suite (test/windows.jl, test/burnthrough.jl) adds seven more checks. The view-factor rows of a windowed hohlraum close as well as those without windows. Reciprocity holds. The burnthrough time of each window element in the drive history matches the standalone estimate from the temperature history of that element. Once a window has burned through, the drive history sends exactly the fraction mathcalT of the power on it to transmitted. A history refined through the transitions follows a uniformly fine one five times more closely than the unrefined steps do. The temperature-following transmission of each burned-through element is the one at the converged temperature of that element. An open GapClosure gap matches the open (aperture) gap, a closed one the wall, and halfway closed it transmits half the power on it.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this checks"}]
}
